          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fd783ca8d2e1a97fdee68fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is remarkable! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from the bench and paced the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is only a source of inspiration for me. You might find nothing special in him. I find everything in him. He is most present in my work when no image of him appears. He is, as I said, a hint of a fresh style. I see him in the sweep of certain lines, in the beauty and fine shades of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why won\u2019t you show his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I put into it an expression of this strange artistic devotion. I never cared to speak of it to him. He knows nothing about it, and never shall. But the world might suspect it, and I will not expose my soul to their shallow, prying eyes. They will not put my heart under their microscope. There is too much of me in that piece, Harry\u2014far too much!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are not as careful as you. They know passion sells. Today a broken heart brings many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI despise them for it,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist should create beautiful things but put nothing of his own life into them. We live in an age when men treat art as if it were a diary. We have lost the sense of beauty in itself. One day I will show the world what it means; and for that reason the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you are wrong, Basil, but I won\u2019t argue. Only the intellectually lost argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter pondered a moment. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he said at last. \u201cI know he likes me. Of course, I flatter him terribly. I take a strange pleasure in saying things to him that I know I will regret. Usually he is delightful to me, and we sit in the studio and talk about a thousand topics. Now and then, though, he is terribly careless and seems to enjoy hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I have given my whole soul to someone who treats it like a flower for his buttonhole, a bit of decoration to gratify his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
