          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b49fd4eb0990da5d672e014a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Clay\u2019s feelings had overruled her ambition, and she gave up the chance to keep scheming for Sir Walter for the young man\u2019s sake. Yet she has brains as well as emotions; and now it is uncertain whether his shrewdness or hers will win in the end\u2014whether, after stopping her from becoming Lady Elliott, he might not end up coaxed and charmed into making her Lady William instead.\\n\\nThere is no doubt Sir Walter and Elizabeth were appalled and humiliated by losing their companion and discovering her deceit. True, they could turn to their grand cousins for solace, but they would long feel the sting of flattering and fawning on others without being flattered and followed in return\u2014only half a pleasure.\\n\\nAnne, satisfied early on that Lady Russell meant to love Captain Wentworth as she ought, had no real shadow on her happiness except the knowledge that she had no relatives to offer him whom any sensible man could appreciate. It was there she felt her own inferiority most. The difference in their fortunes meant nothing; it never troubled her for a moment. But to have no family ready to welcome and value him, nothing respectable, harmonious, or affectionate to offer in return for all the merit and warm reception she found in his brothers and sisters, caused as sharp a pain as her mind could feel amid otherwise strong happiness. She had only two friends to add to his circle, Lady Russell and Mrs. Smith. He was, however, very ready to attach himself to both. Lady Russell, despite her past faults, he now admired wholeheartedly. Though he did not have to say he thought she was right to separate them originally, he could say nearly everything else in her favor. As for Mrs. Smith, she had many claims that quickly and firmly recommended her.\\n\\nHer recent kindness to Anne was reason enough, and their marriage, instead of costing her a friend, gave her two. She was their first visitor once they were settled, and Captain Wentworth, by helping her recover her husband\u2019s West Indian property\u2014writing letters for her, handling business, and carrying her through every small difficulty with the energy of a brave man and devoted friend\u2014fully repaid every service she had given, or ever intended to give, his wife.\"}"
        }
      ],
