          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e892bc5bda66a86c9e974826\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I rushed over and scooped her into my arms, covering her with kisses while I begged, \u201cTalk to me, darling, please! What\u2019s wrong?\u201d\\n\\nHer head sagged against my chest, and she whispered, barely audible, \u201cHello, Central.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I shouted to Clarence. \u201cCall the king\u2019s homeopath, now!\u201d\\n\\nTwo minutes later I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, and Sandy was sending servants racing in every direction throughout the palace. I saw the situation almost instantly\u2014membranous croup. I bent close and murmured, \u201cWake up, sweetheart! Hello, Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe opened those soft eyes, slow and cloudy, and managed to say, \u201cPapa.\u201d That was a relief\u2014she still had fight left in her. I sent someone for sulphur, and I grabbed the croup kettle myself; I don\u2019t sit around waiting for doctors when Sandy or the child is sick. I knew how to care for both of them, and I\u2019d done it before. This little one had spent half its short life in my arms, and many times I could hush its troubles and coax a laugh through those tear-sprinkled lashes when even its mother couldn\u2019t.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, decked out in his finest armor, came striding down the great hall on his way to the stock exchange; he served as president, holding the Siege Perilous, which he\u2019d bought off Sir Galahad, since the Round Table now doubled as their trading floor. Seats at it cost so much you wouldn\u2019t believe me if I told you. Sir Launcelot was playing the bear and had cornered one of the new ventures, about to squeeze the shorts that day\u2014but so what? He remained the same Launcelot, and the moment he glanced in while passing the door and realized his favorite was ill, that settled it. Bulls and bears could claw each other all they wanted; he\u2019d come right in and stand guard over little Hello-Central with everything he had. And that\u2019s exactly what he did. He pitched his helmet into a corner, and within thirty seconds he\u2019d fitted a fresh wick into the alcohol lamp and had the croup kettle heating. By then Sandy had draped a blanket canopy over the crib, and we were set.\\n\\nSir Launcelot stoked the heat, and together we loaded the kettle with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a dash of lactic acid, then topped it off with water and fed the steam pipe under the canopy. Everything was squared away, and we settled down on either side of the crib to stand our watch.\"}"
