          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"734bbf7198dd8da6c8cba65b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cTake heed to what I say; I have mastered the dialect of despair: it is engraven in me, for I am Despair itself, a being at once exultant and melancholy. Yet those words deceive, for though the wave runs dark it is not bitter. We sink down, whisper a kindly good night with closed lids, and when we rouse again, we are liberated. Come, tarry no further, thou loitering one! See the gracious draught! Behold, it is a spirit of kindness, not a mortal maiden, who calls thee, and with beseeching tones (would they might draw thee!) entreats, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke I fastened my gaze upon his face, and his surpassing loveliness, the celestial pity shining in his eyes, the gentle yet earnest protest and amazement that sat on him even before he uttered a word, altered the tension of my mood, stripping me of desolate defiance and leaving only the tenderest sorrow. I noted his eyes moist as well while he gathered both my hands into his; and seating himself beside me, he spoke:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a mournful act to which you would guide me, beloved friend, and your misery must be profound indeed to instill such gloomy purposes. You yearn for death, yet tremble before it, and wish me to share the passage. But I possess less daring than you, and even at your side I shrink from dying. Hear me, and then decide whether you ought to enlist me in your scheme, even if with that overwhelming rhetoric of despair you could render grim death so alluring that fair heaven would seem obscured. Listen, I beseech you, to one who has himself fostered desperate designs, and burned with restless longing for the grave, yet at last has trodden the phantom beneath his feet and dulled its sting. Come, since you have personated Despair to me, I will now become Una to you and draw you safely from his murky cave. Hear me, and be softened by words from which all selfish fire has fled.\\n\\n\u201cWe comprehend not the meaning of this vast world; its baffling intermingling of good with ill. Nonetheless we are set here and commanded to live and to hope. What it is we should hope for I know not; but some good lies before us which we must pursue; that is the charge laid upon us in this life. Should misfortune assail, we must struggle with her; we must thrust her aside and still advance to seek that which our very nature craves. Whether this hope of future good is but a schooling for another life I cannot tell; or whether it is only that we, as laborers in God\u2019s vineyard, must lend our efforts to level the road for those who follow.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
