          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b6d7cf6b1b5d07275723219\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost finished their cycle. It was now late May; the woods wore their freshest green, and the sweet scent of newly cut grass filled the fields. I hoped that the gentle air and the lovely face of nature might help me inspire him with calm feelings, and stir in him a sense of peace and love to prepare him for the trust I was determined to win from him.\\n\\nI therefore chose one of those evenings for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me and led him to a nearby beech wood whose light shade shielded us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After walking in silence for some time, I sat down with him on a mossy mound. It is strange, but even now I can see the place: the slender, smooth trunks were wrapped in ivy, whose glossy dark-green leaves contrasted with the white bark and the pale leaves of the young beech shoots growing from their parent trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss and partly covered by the dead leaves of the previous autumn that the wind had gathered into little heaps here and there. A few moss-covered stumps were scattered about. The leaves were gently stirred by the breeze, and through their green canopy you could glimpse the bright blue sky. As evening advanced, the distant trunks were reddened by the sun, the wind fell completely still, and a few birds flew past us to their evening rest.\\n\\nSo there we sat, and when you hear all that happened\u2014all that tore our souls even in this peaceful place, which, without those strange passions, might have been a paradise for us\u2014you will not wonder that I remember it as I looked upon it then, hoping its calm might give me calm and inspire me not only with courage but with persuasive words. I noticed all these details almost absent-mindedly while I tried to arrange my thoughts for what I meant to do. My heart pounded as I worked myself up to speak to him, for I was resolved not to be turned away, yet I trembled to imagine how my words might affect him. At last, with much hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014you showed me when you first returned, I hope, will excuse me in your eyes for daring to speak to you, though with the tender love of a daughter, yet also with the freedom of a friend and equal.\u201d\"}"
