          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b8d9b5481dcde0111074242f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m going, Master Will\u2014really I am,\u201d the servant answered quickly. \u201cAlmost to the end now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOut to the end!\u201d Legrand almost screamed. \u201cYou\u2019re saying you\u2019re at the end of that branch?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSoon be at de end, massa\u2014o-o-o-oh! Lord have mercy! What\u2019s this on the tree?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d cried Legrand, delighted. \u201cWhat is it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s nothing but a skull. Somebody left his head up here, and the crows ate every bit of the meat.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA skull, you say! Fine. How is it fastened to the branch? What holds it there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSure enough, massa; let me look. Why, this is a curious thing, I swear\u2014there\u2019s a big nail in the skull that fastens it to the tree.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood. Now, Jupiter, do exactly what I tell you\u2014understand?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, massa.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPay attention, then. Find the left eye of the skull.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHum! hoo! that\u2019s funny! There isn\u2019t any eye left at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDamn your stupidity! Do you know your right hand from your left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I know that\u2014know all about it. I chop wood with my left hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! You\u2019re left-handed, and your left eye is on the same side as your left hand. Now I suppose you can find the left eye of the skull, or where it used to be. Have you found it?\u201d\\n\\nThere was a long silence. Finally the servant asked, \u201cIs the left eye of the skull on the same side as the skull\u2019s left hand too? Because the skull doesn\u2019t have any hands. Never mind! I\u2019ve got the left eye now\u2014here it is! What should I do with it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet the beetle drop through it, as far as the string will reach\u2014but be careful not to let go of the string.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll done, Master Will; easy to put the bug through the hole. Look out down below!\u201d\\n\\nDuring this talk none of Jupiter\u2019s body could be seen, but the beetle he had lowered now hung at the end of the string and shone like a globe of polished gold in the last rays of sunset, a few of which still lit the hill where we stood. The scarab hung clear of every branch, and if dropped would have fallen at our feet. Legrand at once grabbed the scythe and cleared a circular space, three or four yards wide, just beneath the insect. When he finished, he told Jupiter to let go the string and come down from the tree.\"}"
