          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dbec9775e59f115fece0a346\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If you refuse and prefer to rely on my patience and the mercy of those you have wronged, sit in that chair without a word. It has been waiting for you for two whole days.\u201d\\n\\nMonks muttered something unintelligible but still wavered.\\n\\n\u201cYou will act quickly,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow. \u201cOne word from me, and your other choice is gone forever.\u201d\\n\\nStill the man hesitated.\\n\\n\u201cI have no desire to bargain,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, \u201cand because I defend the dearest interests of others, I have no right to.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs there\u2014\u201d asked Monks, his tongue faltering, \u201cis there no middle course?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNone.\u201d\\n\\nMonks looked anxiously at the old gentleman, but read only severity and resolve in his face, so he walked into the room and, shrugging his shoulders, sat down.\\n\\n\u201cLock the door from the outside,\u201d Mr. Brownlow told the attendants, \u201cand return when I ring.\u201d\\n\\nThe men obeyed, leaving the two alone.\\n\\n\u201cThis is fine treatment, sir,\u201d said Monks, throwing down his hat and cloak, \u201cfrom my father\u2019s oldest friend.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is because I was your father\u2019s oldest friend, young man,\u201d replied Mr. Brownlow. \u201cIt is because the hopes and wishes of our youth were bound up with him and that fair girl of his blood and kin who rejoined her God while still young, leaving me solitary and lonely. It is because he knelt with me beside his only sister\u2019s deathbed when he was still a boy, on the morning that\u2014though Heaven decided otherwise\u2014would have made her my young wife. It is because my scarred heart clung to him from then on, through all his trials and faults, until he died. It is because old memories fill my heart, and even the sight of you brings back thoughts of him. It is because of all this that I am moved to treat you gently even now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford, even now\u2014and blush for your unworthiness to bear the name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat has the name to do with it?\u201d the other asked after studying his agitated companion in sullen wonder. \u201cWhat is the name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, \u201cnothing to you. But it was hers, and even after all these years it still brings back to me, an old man, the glow and thrill I once felt when I heard it from a stranger. I am very glad you changed it\u2014very, very.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very grand,\u201d said Monks (to keep his assumed name) after a long silence, during which he jerked himself to and fro in stubborn defiance while Mr. Brownlow sat shading his face with his hand.\"}"
