          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5efe560aa0b3a6c0a0f23334\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you speaking of\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, of course I am. I could never have imagined any one else\u2014and I thought you understood that. When we discussed him, it seemed the plainest thing in the world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d Emma replied with constrained composure, \u201cfor everything you said then appeared to concern another person. I might almost declare that you actually mentioned Mr. Frank Churchill. I am certain you spoke of the favor Mr. Frank Churchill conferred on you in defending you from the gipsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how could you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly what I said at the time. I told you I could not wonder at your affection; that, considering the assistance he provided, it was entirely natural\u2014and you agreed, expressing yourself fervently about how deeply you felt that obligation, even detailing your sensations as you saw him step forward to your aid. The impression remains vivid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I recall what you mean, but I was thinking of something quite different then. It was not the gipsies\u2014it was not Mr. Frank Churchill I intended. No!\u201d (with a touch of pride) \u201cI was thinking of a far dearer moment\u2014of Mr. Knightley\u2019s approaching me to dance when Mr. Elton refused to stand up with me, and when there was not another partner available. That was kindness itself; that was true compassion and generosity; that was the service that first made me feel how far he surpasses every other creature on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens!\u201d exclaimed Emma. \u201cThis has been a most unhappy\u2014most lamentable error!\u2014What can be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have supported me, then, had you understood me? At least, I cannot be worse off now than I should have been if the other had been the person; and now\u2014it _is_ possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe paused for a few seconds. Emma remained silent.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she continued, \u201cthat you perceive a vast difference between the two, in relation to me or to anybody. You must esteem one five hundred million times higher than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing\u2014that if\u2014strange as it may sound\u2014yet they were your own words, that _more_ astonishing things have occurred, that matches of _greater_ inequality have been made than between Mr. Frank Churchill and me; and so it seems as if even such a thing as this may have taken place before\u2014and if I should be so unspeakably fortunate as to\u2014if Mr. Knightley should actually\u2014if _he_ ignores the disparity, I trust, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not oppose it or try to create obstacles.\u201d\"}"
