          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b1cb6ef31abce314fb5ac3d5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm howled all night, but nothing noteworthy happened. The next morning, when they came down to breakfast, the dreadful bloodstain was back on the floor. \u201cI don\u2019t think it\u2019s the Paragon Detergent\u2019s fault,\u201d said Washington. \u201cI\u2019ve tried everything. It must be the ghost.\u201d He scrubbed the stain away again, but the following morning it returned. On the third morning it was there too, even though Mr. Otis had locked the library himself and taken the key upstairs. Now the whole family was intrigued. Mr. Otis began to think he had been too firm in denying ghosts existed, Mrs. Otis announced she wanted to join the Psychical Society, and Washington started drafting a long letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore about the Persistence of Bloodstains Connected with Crime. That night, any remaining doubts about the reality of apparitions vanished forever.\\n\\nThe day had been warm and sunny, and in the cool evening the family went out for a drive. They did not return until nine o\u2019clock, when they had a light supper. Their conversation never touched on ghosts, so there were none of those basic conditions of expectant belief that often come before sightings of supernatural events. The topics they discussed, as I later learned from Mr. Otis, were just the usual subjects for cultured Americans of the better sort: the great advantage of Miss Fanny Davenport over Sarah Bernhardt as an actress; the difficulty of getting green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy even in the best English homes; Boston\u2019s importance in developing the world-soul; the convenience of the baggage-check system on trains; and the pleasant sound of the New York accent compared with the London drawl. They never mentioned anything supernatural, nor did Sir Simon de Canterville come up. At eleven o\u2019clock the family went to bed, and by half past eleven all the lights were out. Some time later, Mr. Otis woke up because he heard a strange noise in the hallway outside his room. It sounded like clanking metal that drew closer every moment. He got up at once, struck a match, and checked the time. It was exactly one o\u2019clock. He stayed perfectly calm and felt his pulse; it was steady. The odd noise continued, and he clearly heard footsteps with it. He put on his slippers, took a small oblong bottle from his dressing case, and opened the door.\"}"
