          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"722ff6ab3967f9012b88a853\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I told myself I didn\u2019t want to get mixed up in that kind of business.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning the parlor was closed, the watchers were gone, and only the household, widow Bartley, and our group were around. I studied their faces to see if anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t tell.\\n\\nAround midday the undertaker arrived with his helper. They set the coffin in the middle of the room on two chairs, lined up our chairs in rows, and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were full. I saw the coffin lid looked the same as before, but I didn\u2019t dare peek under it with people nearby.\\n\\nThen folks started flocking in, and the cousins and the girls took the front row at the head of the coffin. For half an hour the visitors filed past slowly in single file, looked at the dead man\u2019s face for a moment, and some shed a tear. Everything stayed quiet and solemn, except for the girls and cousins holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, heads bowed, sobbing a little. The only other sounds were shoes scraping the floor and noses being blown\u2014because people always blow their noses more at funerals, unless it\u2019s church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker glided around in his black gloves and soothing ways, adding the last touches, settling people and things, making no more sound than a cat. He never spoke. He moved folks, squeezed in latecomers, opened a path, and did it all with nods and hand signs. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the softest, smoothest, stealthiest man I ever saw, and he didn\u2019t have any more smile to him than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeon\u2014a weak one\u2014and when all was ready a young woman sat down to play it. It was pretty squeaky and colicky, and everyone joined in singing, and Peter was the only one who had anything good out of it, if you asked me. Then Reverend Hobson began slow and solemn, and started to speak, when the wildest uproar broke out in the cellar that anyone ever heard; it was just one dog, but he made a fierce racket and kept it up without stopping. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
