{"request_id":"ce69101f07de9f84189c5a38","rewritten_text":"Few could boast a heart so spotless, a humility so genuine, joined to unwavering trust in her own integrity and in that of others. She had lived in seclusion since birth. Her mother died when she was very young, yet her father devoted himself to guiding her education. He held many singular notions that shaped his plan for her upbringing. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, as well as the English worthies of centuries past, yet remained nearly ignorant of current happenings: she had read scarcely any author of the last fifty years, though her reading otherwise was extensive. So, while she might seem less versed in society’s enigmas than he, her knowledge ran deeper and rested on firmer ground; and even had her beauty and sweetness not captivated him, her intellect would have forever enthralled his. He looked to her as a guide, and his adoration was such that he took pleasure in heightening, in his own eyes, the inferiority she sometimes made him feel.

At nineteen his mother died. He left college, and, casting off his old friends for a time, withdrew to the neighborhood of his Diana, finding all solace in her gentle voice and dearer embraces. This brief separation from his companions gave him courage to claim his independence. He felt that, however they might mock his intended marriage, they would not dare do so once it was accomplished; therefore, seeking first his guardian’s consent—which he gained with difficulty—and then her father’s, which came far more readily, he informed no one else of his plan, and by his twentieth birthday he had become Diana’s husband.

He loved her with ardor, and her tenderness possessed a charm that excluded all thoughts but of her. He invited some college friends to visit him, yet their frivolity repelled him. Diana had lifted the veil that had kept him in boyhood: he was now a man and wondered how he could ever have shared the catchphrases and notions of his fellow students, or how he could have feared their censure even briefly. He cast off his former friendships, not through inconstancy, but because they were truly unworthy of him."}

