          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b4a823a1f9822dec7e0d87bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her situation was truly one of uncommon trial and perplexity. Though she felt the keenest longing to unravel the mystery that hid Oliver\u2019s past, she could not betray the trust that the wretched woman she had just spoken with had placed in her, as a young, innocent girl. The stranger\u2019s words and demeanour had struck Rose Maylie deeply; mingled with her affection for Oliver, and nearly equal in strength and warmth, was her earnest wish to lead the forsaken creature back to repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to remain in London only three days before setting off for several weeks to a distant part of the coast. It was already midnight of the first day. What could she plan that might be carried out within eight-and-forty hours? And how might she delay the trip without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was with them, and would stay for the next two days; yet Rose knew too well the excellent gentleman\u2019s impetuous temper, and could foresee the anger with which, in the first burst of his indignation, he would regard the agent of Oliver\u2019s capture. She could not trust him with the secret, when no experienced person could back up her plea for the girl. These were ample grounds for the utmost caution and discretion when telling Mrs. Maylie; the lady\u2019s first impulse would surely be to consult the worthy doctor in the matter. Seeking any legal adviser, even had she known how, was hardly to be considered for the same reason. She did, for a moment, think of turning to Harry; but this brought back the memory of their last parting, and it seemed an unworthy thing to summon him home when\u2014tears sprang to her eyes as she followed this line of thought\u2014he might already have learned to forget her, and to be happier apart.\\n\\nTossed by these conflicting thoughts; leaning now toward one plan, then another, and again recoiling from all as each new argument rose before her; Rose spent a wakeful, troubled night. After more self-communing the following day, she reached the desperate resolve to seek Harry\u2019s counsel.\\n\\n\u201cIf it grieves him,\u201d she pondered, \u201cto return here, how grievous must it be for me! Yet perhaps he will not come; he may send a letter, or he may come in person and carefully avoid seeing me\u2014he did when he left. I hardly expected it, yet it was better for us both.\u201d With that, Rose set down the pen and turned aside, as if even the paper that was to bear her message should not witness her tears.\"}"
