{"request_id":"92b5379a0f4994823fe63a48","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered at breakfast, they spoke at some length about the ghost. The United States Minister was naturally rather vexed that his gift had been refused. “I have no desire,” he remarked, “to inflict any personal harm on the ghost, yet, considering how long he has occupied the house, I do not consider it at all courteous to throw pillows at him”—a thoroughly sensible observation, at which, I regret to report, the twins burst out laughing. “On the other hand,” he went on, “if he truly refuses to make use of the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall be obliged to deprive him of his chains. It is quite impossible to sleep with such a racket outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, however, they experienced no disturbance, the only incident provoking any notice being the constant reappearance of the blood-stain on the library floor. This was particularly odd, as Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and kept the windows tightly barred. The chameleon-like color of the stain also stirred a great deal of discussion. Some mornings it appeared a dull, almost Indian red; at other times it gleamed vermilion; then again a deep purple; and once, when they gathered for family prayers according to the plain rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they discovered it a vivid emerald green. These shifting hues naturally entertained the family, and they placed wagers on the subject each evening. The only person who refused to join the fun was little Virginia, who for some unexplained cause was always quite upset at the sight of the stain, and very nearly wept the morning it shone bright emerald.

The ghost’s second manifestation occurred on Sunday night. Shortly after they had gone to bed, they were startled by a terrible crash in the hall. Racing down the stairs, they found that a large suit of ancient armor had slipped from its stand and collapsed upon the stone floor, while the Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees with a look of extreme anguish. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters, instantly fired two pellets at him, displaying that precision of aim attainable only by long and careful practice against a writing-master, while the United States Minister leveled his revolver and, in accordance with Californian etiquette, ordered him to hold up his hands! With a wild scream of fury, the ghost leapt up and swept through them like a mist, snuffing out Washington Otis’s candle as he passed, and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

