          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bd4214c7f8af77a4d2033e7a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This marriage is entirely right. I didn\u2019t think so at first, but I admit it now. The gods created Sibyl Vane for you. Without her you would have been unfinished.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThanks, Basil,\u201d replied Dorian Gray, squeezing his hand. \u201cI knew you\u2019d understand me. Harry is so cynical he frightens me. But here comes the orchestra. It\u2019s dreadful, but it lasts only five minutes. Then the curtain goes up, and you\u2019ll see the girl to whom I\u2019m going to devote my whole life, the one to whom I\u2019ve already given everything good in me.\u201d\\n\\nFifteen minutes later, amid a wild storm of applause, Sibyl Vane stepped onto the stage. Yes, she was undeniably beautiful\u2014one of the loveliest creatures Lord Henry thought he had ever seen. There was something fawn-like in her shy grace and startled eyes. A faint blush, like the reflection of a rose in a silver mirror, touched her cheeks as she glanced at the packed, enthusiastic house. She retreated a few steps, and her lips seemed to tremble. Basil Hallward sprang to his feet and began applauding. Dorian Gray sat motionless, as if in a dream, staring at her. Lord Henry peered through his glasses, murmuring, \u201cCharming! Charming!\u201d\\n\\nThe scene was the hall of Capulet\u2019s house, and Romeo in his pilgrim costume had entered with Mercutio and the others. The orchestra, such as it was, struck up a few bars, and the dance began. Through the crowd of awkward, shabby actors, Sibyl Vane moved like a being from a more refined world. Her body swayed as she danced, like a plant drifting in water. The curves of her neck were like those of a white lily. Her hands seemed carved from cool ivory.\\n\\nYet she was strangely lifeless. No joy appeared when her eyes met Romeo\u2019s. The few lines she had to speak\u2014\\n\\nGood pilgrim, you do wrong your hand too much,\\nWhich mannerly devotion shows in this;\\nFor saints have hands that pilgrims\u2019 hands do touch,\\nand palm to palm is holy palmers\u2019 kiss\u2014\\n\\nwith the brief exchange that followed, were delivered in a completely artificial way. Her voice was lovely, but the tone was entirely wrong. It was off in color. It drained all vitality from the verse. It made the passion seem unreal.\\n\\nDorian Gray turned pale as he watched her. He felt confused and worried. Neither of his friends dared say anything to him. To them she now seemed utterly incapable.\"}"
