          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5456ee5b7f1cc5cb0b360208\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou\u2019ve reared an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am, utterly unfitting for someone of his station: as the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, who are practical philosophers, would tell you. What have paupers to do with soul or spirit? It\u2019s ample that we let \u2019em keep living bodies. Had you kept the boy on gruel, ma\u2019am, none of this would have occurred.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Sowerberry, lifting her eyes devoutly toward the kitchen ceiling. \u201cThis is what comes of being generous!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s generosity to Oliver had consisted of a lavish measure of all the grimy scraps nobody else would eat; so there was much meekness and self-sacrifice in her standing, of her own free will, beneath Mr. Bumble\u2019s heavy charge, though, to be fair, she was entirely guiltless in thought, word, or deed.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, when the lady\u2019s eyes dropped back to earth, \u201cthe only plan now, that I know of, is to leave him in the cellar a day or two, until he\u2019s a bit starved down; then take him out and keep him on gruel through his apprenticeship. He comes of bad stock. Excitable temperaments, Mrs. Sowerberry! Both nurse and doctor said that mother of his forced her way here through difficulties and agony that would have killed any well-disposed woman, weeks beforehand.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point in Mr. Bumble\u2019s speech, Oliver, hearing merely enough to realize some mention was being made of his mother, began kicking again with such fury that every other sound was drowned. Sowerberry reappeared just then. Oliver\u2019s offence, with those exaggerations the ladies felt best calculated to inflame his wrath, was explained, whereupon he unlocked the cellar door in a flash and hauled his mutinous apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes were ripped from the beating he\u2019d taken; his face mottled with bruises and scratches; his hair hanging over his brow. The hot flush had not faded, however; and as he was hauled from his confinement, he glowered courageously at Noah and looked entirely undaunted.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a fine young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, giving Oliver a shake and a cuff on the ear.\\n\\n\u201cHe insulted my mother,\u201d Oliver answered.\\n\\n\u201cWell, suppose he did, you ungrateful little wretch?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe earned what he said, and more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe didn\u2019t,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry broke into a torrent of tears.\\n\\nThis deluge of weeping left Mr. Sowerberry no real choice. Had he hesitated for a single instant to punish Oliver severely, it must be plain to every knowledgeable reader that he would have been, by all precedents in marital quarrels, a brute, an unnatural husband, an insulting creature, a base imitation of a man, and various other delightful characters too numerous to list within the confines of this chapter.\"}"
