          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d04f630d7de095160a6f00b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried to the skies on soft, flowery beds of ease while others fight to win the prize and sail through bloody seas?\\n\\nPeople thought he was a marvelous reader. At church sociables he was always asked to read poetry; when he finished, the ladies would raise their hands, let them fall into their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads as if to say, \u201cWords fail us; it is too beautiful, far too beautiful for this earthly world.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn, the Reverend Mr. Sprague turned himself into a bulletin board and read notices of meetings, societies, and other matters until it seemed the list would run on to the crack of doom\u2014a strange custom still kept up in America, even in cities, in this era of plenty of newspapers. Often, the less reason there is to keep a tradition, the harder it is to discard it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a generous, detailed prayer: it pleaded for the church and its little children; for the other churches in the village; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the State officers; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for the officers of the Government; for poor sailors tossed by storms; for the oppressed millions under European monarchies and Eastern despotisms; for those who have the light and good news yet lack eyes to see or ears to hear; for the heathen on faraway islands; and it ended by asking that the words he was about to speak find grace and favor, like seed planted in fertile soil, yielding a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nDresses rustled, and the standing congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if he even managed that. He was restless throughout. His mind kept count of every element of the prayer without meaning to\u2014he was not listening, but he knew the territory well and the minister\u2019s usual route through it. Whenever a bit of new material was added, his ear caught it and his whole being objected; he thought additions were unfair and underhanded. In the middle of the prayer a fly settled on the back of the pew before him and tormented his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands, wrapping its arms around its head and polishing it so vigorously it seemed almost to come off, exposing the thin thread of its neck; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them down as if they were coat-tails; going through its whole toilette with perfect calm, as though it knew it was completely safe.\"}"
