          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"169ae8e566d3ea200272ed70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no cheer in the little town that same calm Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s family were putting on mourning clothes, full of grief and tears. An unusual stillness held the village, though it was usually quiet enough. The townspeople went about their business absent-mindedly and spoke little, though they often sighed. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children; their games held no joy, and they gave them up one by one.\\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher found herself drifting around the empty schoolyard, feeling very low. Nothing there soothed her. She murmured to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if I only had a brass andiron knob again! But I haven\u2019t got anything now to remember him by.\u201d She swallowed a sob.\\n\\nAfter a moment she muttered:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014not for the whole world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThe thought broke her, and she wandered off with tears running down her cheeks. Soon a bunch of Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s friends came by, stood looking over the picket fence, and spoke softly about how Tom did this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe said something that\u2014now that they thought about it\u2014seemed like a sign of doom. Each one pointed out the exact spot where the boys had stood, then added something like, \u201cand I was standing just here\u2014just like I am now, suppose you were him\u2014I was this close\u2014and he smiled just like this\u2014and then something ran all over me, you know\u2014awful\u2014and I never thought what it meant, but now I can see!\u201d\\n\\nThey argued over who saw the dead boys last, each claiming that gloomy honor and supplying proof, though the stories were bent a bit. When they finally agreed who actually saw the boys last and spoke the final words, those lucky ones adopted a kind of sacred importance, and the others gaped and envied them. One poor fellow, who had nothing else to brag about, said with obvious pride:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer he thrashed me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that bid for glory fell flat. Almost all the boys could say the same, which ruined the distinction. The group wandered off, still sharing memories of the lost heroes in hushed voices.\"}"
