          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2efffcfa33d2d5454552797\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must know, Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been taken by a young man of large fortune from the north of England. He came down on Monday in a chaise-and-four to see the place, liked it so much that he made an agreement with Mr. Morris at once, plans to move in before Michaelmas, and some of his servants will be in the house by the end of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, my dear, of course! A single man with four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful opportunity for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? How can it matter to them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife answered, \u201chow can you be so aggravating? You must know I expect him to marry one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that his purpose in settling here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurpose? Nonsense, how can you say that! But it is very likely that he may fall in love with one of them, so you must visit him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see no need for that. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be better; since you are as handsome as any of them, Mr. Bingley might like you best of all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you flatter me. I certainly had my share of beauty, but I do not claim anything extraordinary now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she should stop thinking about her own beauty.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn those cases, a woman usually has little beauty left to think about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you must go to see Mr. Bingley when he comes to the neighborhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is more than I promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Imagine what a good match it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are determined to go for that reason alone; normally, you know, they never visit new arrivals. Indeed, you must go, for it will be impossible for us to call on him if you do not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are overly particular, surely. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be happy to see you; and I will send a few lines with you to assure him of my full consent to his marrying whichever of the girls he prefers\u2014though I must give a special recommendation for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI insist you do no such thing. Lizzy is no better than the others; I am sure she is not half as pretty as Jane, nor half as good-humored as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
        }
