          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4454fe307dd23fb9fdef6b0f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had never suspected his own influence. He followed her into the shrubbery without any plan of testing it. He came only from the anxiety to see how she was taking Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, with no selfish aim, only the wish, if she gave him the chance, to comfort or advise her. Everything else sprang from the moment, from what he heard and how it moved him. Hearing that she cared nothing for Frank Churchill\u2014that her heart was free\u2014sparked the hope that, eventually, he might win her love; but it was never a hope for the present. In a brief surge of eagerness over judgment, he had only dared to ask that she would not forbid him to try. The greater hopes that soon unfolded were all the more enchanting. The love he had hoped to earn, if possible, was already his. In half an hour he had gone from deep distress to a happiness so complete it could go by no other name.\\n\\nHer change matched his. This single half hour had given both of them the same treasured certainty of being loved, and had cleared away ignorance, jealousy, and distrust on both sides. He had long felt jealousy, dating back to the arrival, or even the expectation, of Frank Churchill. He had been in love with Emma and jealous of Frank Churchill from almost the same moment; one feeling had probably opened his eyes to the other. That jealousy drove him from the country. The Box Hill outing pushed him to leave; he meant to spare himself the sight of such permitted, even encouraged, attentions again. He left to learn to be indifferent, but he chose the wrong place. His brother\u2019s house held too much domestic happiness; woman there appeared in too amiable a light; Isabella was too much like Emma\u2014differing only in the few, obvious inferiorities that made Emma shine brighter\u2014for him to accomplish much, even if he had stayed longer. Yet he persisted, day after day, until this very morning\u2019s mail brought the news about Jane Fairfax. Then, with the gladness he could not help feeling, since he had never thought Frank Churchill worthy of Emma, and with so much tender concern and anxious care for her, he could stay away no longer. He rode home through the rain and walked over right after dinner to see how this sweetest and best of creatures, flawless in spite of all her faults, bore the discovery.\"}"
