          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"19be3eb8fd214f9b4ab741ae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very distinguished-looking old gentleman, and another younger one who had his right arm in a sling. And, my goodness, how the crowd yelled and laughed, keeping it up nonstop. But I didn\u2019t see anything funny about it, and I figured it would take some effort for the duke and the king to find it funny, either. I expected they\u2019d go pale. But no, neither of them turned a shade. The duke never even hinted he suspected what was going on, just strolled around cooing, pleased and contented, like a jug gently glugging out buttermilk; and the king gazed down so sorrowfully on the newcomers it looked like it hurt his heart to imagine there could be such crooks and scoundrels in the world. Oh, he did it beautifully. Lots of the leading townspeople crowded around the king to show they were on his side. The old gentleman who\u2019d just arrived looked utterly confused. Before long he started to speak, and I could tell right away he sounded like an Englishman\u2014not exactly like the king, though the king\u2019s version was a pretty fair imitation. I can\u2019t repeat the old gentleman\u2019s exact words, or copy his voice, but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I never expected, and I\u2019ll admit straight out I\u2019m not in the best position to respond to it; because my brother and I have been unlucky\u2014he\u2019s broken his arm, and our luggage got set off at a town upriver last night by mistake. I am Peter Wilks\u2019s brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can neither hear nor speak\u2014and right now can\u2019t even make decent signs, seeing as he has only one hand to do it with. We are who we say we are, and in a day or two, when my luggage catches up, I can prove it. But until then I\u2019ll say nothing more, and go over to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new mute started off; and the king laughed and blustered:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014very likely, isn\u2019t it?\u2014and mighty convenient, too, for a fraud who has to use signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their luggage! That\u2019s rich!\u2014and mighty clever\u2014under the circumstances!\u201d\\n\\nHe laughed again, and so did everyone else, except three or four, or maybe half a dozen. One of them was that doctor; another was a sharp-faced fellow carrying an old-fashioned carpetbag made of real carpet material, who had just come off the steamboat and was speaking to the doctor in a low voice, glancing toward the king once in a while and nodding\u2014they were Levi Bell, the lawyer who\u2019d gone up to Louisville; and another was a big rough fellow who came over and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
