          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5ce154808d4fe0e2e19e93e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have to go, unsure of what awaits me; but I will come back here, or trail your group, as soon as I can. A single word, a single look, will decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house tonight or never again.\u201d\\n\\nA letter like that could not easily be shaken off. Half an hour alone to think might have calmed her; but the ten minutes she had before being interrupted, burdened by her present constraints, did nothing for her composure. Each moment only stirred her more. It was happiness so intense it overwhelmed her. Before she even got through the first wave of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all walked in.\\n\\nThe urgent need to appear like herself sparked an immediate battle, but after a short while she couldn\u2019t keep it up. She stopped understanding a word they were saying and had to plead illness as an excuse. They could see she looked very unwell, were alarmed and sympathetic, and insisted they would not leave her for anything. It was awful. If only they would go away and leave her to the quiet of that room, it would have been her cure; but to have them standing or hovering around her drove her to desperation, and she declared she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs. Musgrove, \u201cgo home this instant and take care of yourself so you\u2019ll be ready for tonight. I wish Sarah were here to tend to you, but I\u2019m no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut a chair was out of the question. Worse than anything! To give up the chance of even a brief word with Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town (and she was almost sure she would meet him) was unbearable. She objected strongly to the chair, and Mrs. Musgrove, whose mind ran only to one kind of illness, after confirming with some worry that there had been no fall, that Anne had not recently slipped and struck her head, that she was absolutely certain she had not fallen, could cheerfully let her go and count on finding her better that night.\\n\\nDetermined to leave no possible precaution undone, Anne forced herself and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI fear, ma\u2019am, it hasn\u2019t been made completely clear. Please be kind enough to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your entire party this evening. I think there was some misunderstanding, and I especially want you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope they will both come.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear, it\u2019s perfectly clear, I assure you.\u201d\"}"
