{"request_id":"1ae1ed2475cedf6cd743e7fb","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless; she no longer found joy in her usual occupations. Every pleasure seemed to her like a crime against the dead, and she believed endless grief and tears were the only tribute she could give to innocence so cruelly destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who once walked with me along the lake shore, speaking with delight about our future. The first sorrow meant to loosen our hold on the world had touched her, dimming her brightest smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “about Justine Moritz’s wretched death, I no longer see the world as I used to. Previously, when I heard or read about vice and injustice, they felt like stories from the distant past or imagined wrongs; they seemed remote and easier to grasp with reason than with the heart. But now misery has struck close to home, and people seem like monsters thirsting for one another’s blood. Yet I know I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl was guilty; and if she really could have committed the crime for which she died—killing the son of her benefactor and friend, a child she had nursed from birth and appeared to love as her own—she would have been the vilest of humans. For a handful of jewels to murder that child! I could never approve of any execution, but I would have thought such a person unfit to live among others. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel it; and you agree, which confirms my belief. Alas, Victor, when lies can so closely mimic truth, who can feel secure in their own happiness? I feel as if I am walking along the edge of a cliff, with crowds pushing me toward the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer is free—perhaps even respected. Yet even if I were condemned to die for those same crimes, I would not trade places with that villain.”

I listened with unbearable anguish. I was not the murderer in deed, but in consequence I was. Elizabeth saw my misery on my face, and taking my hand gently, said, “My dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have wounded me deeply, God knows; but I am not as wretched as you are.”"}

