          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b9a0699d00176c1d4c5e730\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr Elliot looked up with such marked respect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuite delightful!\u201d cried Mrs Clay, though she dared not glance at Anne. \u201cExactly like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, may I not call them father and son?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I place no restriction on anyone\u2019s words. If you choose to have such ideas! But honestly, I hardly notice his attentions as anything beyond what other men show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d exclaimed Mrs Clay, raising her hands and eyes, and letting the rest of her surprise sink into a convenient silence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear Penelope, you need not be so anxious about him. Remember, I invited him. I sent him away smiling. When I learned he truly meant to spend all of tomorrow with his friends at Thornberry Park, I took pity on him.\u201d\\n\\nAnne admired the skill of her friend, who could show such pleasure in anticipating and greeting the very person whose presence must interfere with her main aim. Mrs Clay must inevitably hate the sight of Mr Elliot; yet she could wear the most obliging, calm expression, and seem perfectly content with the shortened chance of devoting only half as much time to Sir Walter as she would have otherwise.\\n\\nTo Anne, it was extremely distressing to see Mr Elliot enter the room, and quite painful to have him approach and speak. She had already suspected he could not always be sincere, but now she saw deceit in everything. His respectful attention to her father, compared with his earlier comments, was disgusting; and when she remembered his cruel conduct towards Mrs Smith, she could barely endure his present smiles, gentleness, and polished sentiments.\\n\\nShe was determined to avoid any change in behavior that might provoke a protest from him. She was keen to escape any questioning or attention. Still, she meant to be as decidedly cool to him as their relationship allowed, and to quietly step back from the needless intimacy she had been led into. So she was more cautious and more distant than the night before.\\n\\nHe tried to stir her curiosity again about where and how he might have once heard her praised; he very much wished to be encouraged by more questioning. But the magic was gone. He realized that the bustle and excitement of a public room were needed to ignite his modest cousin\u2019s vanity; he discovered, at least, that it could not be managed now, amid the competing claims of everyone else.\"}"
        }
