          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"902db7cc906171c4e9cdfae9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cPlease think, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, tears forcing their way despite his efforts to hold them back. \u201cOh, remember how young and good she is, and how much comfort she brings everyone. I am sure\u2014certain\u2014quite certain\u2014that, for your sake, because you are so good; for her own sake; and for the sake of all she makes happy, she will not die. Heaven will never let her die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, resting her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou think like a child, poor boy. Still, you remind me of my duty. I forgot it for a moment, Oliver, but I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old and have seen enough of illness and death to know the pain of parting from those we love. I have seen enough to know it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them. Yet this should comfort us even in sorrow, for Heaven is just, and such things teach us clearly that there is a brighter world than this, and the passage to it is quick. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was amazed to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke, she checked her tears by one effort; straightening her posture and becoming calm and steady. He was even more surprised that this steadiness lasted, and that during all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie stayed ready and composed, carrying out all her duties with steadiness and, outwardly, even cheerfulness. But he was young, and did not yet know what strong minds can do in such trials. How could he, when even their owners seldom see their own strength?\\n\\nA tense night followed. Morning brought the fulfillment of Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears. Rose had entered the first stage of a high and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must be active, Oliver, and not give in to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, pressing her finger to her lips as she looked into his face. \u201cThis letter must reach Mr. Losberne with all speed. It has to go to the market town, no more than four miles away by the footpath across the field, and from there be sent by express on horseback straight to Chertsey. The innkeepers will see to it, and I trust you to make sure it is done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not speak; his face showed nothing but the urgent wish to start at once.\"}"
