          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"18dd7548260209c7fc5018b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day the specter felt faint and spent. The dreadful suspense of the past four weeks had begun to tell on him. His nerves were utterly wrecked, and he flinched at the lightest rustle. For five days he stayed shut in his chamber, and finally resolved to abandon the issue of the blood-stain on the library floor. If the Otises did not care for it, plainly they did not merit it. They were clearly persons of a coarse, material nature, quite unable to grasp the emblematic value of sense-bound manifestations. The subject of spectral appearances, and the evolution of astral forms, was naturally a separate affair, beyond his power to regulate. It remained his solemn duty to show himself in the corridor every week, and to gibber from the broad oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of each month, and he could not perceive any honourable means of shirking his obligations. That his life had been exceedingly wicked was beyond dispute, yet he was scrupulous in every matter touching the supernatural. Thus, for the ensuing three Saturdays he paced the corridor between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every conceivable step to avoid detection. He took off his boots, moved as softly as possible over the worm-eaten boards, donned a sweeping black velvet cloak, and carefully used the Rising Sun Lubricator to oil his chains. I must admit he found it extremely hard to bring himself to employ this last precaution. Nevertheless, one evening while the household were at dinner, he stole into Mr. Otis\u2019s room and purloined the bottle. At first he felt somewhat abased, but later was sensible enough to acknowledge that the contrivance had many advantages and, to some extent, served his ends. Still, despite all his efforts, they would not let him alone. Strings were repeatedly stretched across the corridor, and he stumbled over them in the darkness, and once, when dressed as \u201cBlack Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u201d he received a nasty fall after stepping on a butter-slide the twins had constructed from the doorway of the Tapestry Chamber to the top of the oak staircase. This final insult so infuriated him that he decided to make one last attempt to uphold his dignity and station, resolving to confront the insolent young Etonians the following night in his famed guise of \u201cReckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u201d\\n\\nHe had not assumed that disguise for over seventy years; indeed, not since he had so terrified pretty Lady Barbara Modish with it that she abruptly ended her betrothal to the reigning Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather and fled to Gretna Green with dashing Jack Castleton, vowing that nothing on earth would persuade her to wed into a house that allowed such a hideous specter to pace the terrace at dusk.\"}"
        }
      ],
