          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4f511519d71ae99ebcce4ffd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was within Warwick Castle that I met the curious stranger I am about to describe. Three traits drew me to him: his frank simplicity, his astonishing mastery of ancient armor, and the restful ease of his company\u2014inasmuch as he supplied all the conversation. As modest folk tend to do, we drifted together at the rear of the group being guided through the halls, and he immediately began voicing observations that caught my interest. Speaking in a soft, pleasant, unhurried fashion, he seemed by degrees to slip out of this age and world into some distant era and forgotten realm; sooner or later he bound me in such a spell that I thought myself moving through the ghosts, shadows, dust, and decay of a hoary antiquity, conversing with one of its relics. Precisely as I might mention my closest associates or bitterest foes, or the neighbors most familiar to me, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and every other illustrious name of the Round Table\u2014and how incredibly old, indescribably old and faded and withered and ancient he appeared as he proceeded! Presently he turned and addressed me, as casually as another might refer to the weather or some routine concern\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know of transmigration of souls; are you acquainted with transposition of epochs\u2014and of bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI replied that I had never heard of such a thing. He cared so little\u2014equally as when one talks of the weather\u2014that he did not even observe whether I answered or not. A brief pause followed, instantly cut off by the monotonous drone of the paid guide:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, dating from the sixth century, era of King Arthur and the Round Table; reputed to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the circular opening in the chain-mail over the left breast; no explanation; thought to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014possibly by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers from spite.\u201d\\n\\nMy companion smiled\u2014not a modern expression, but one that surely vanished from general use ages upon ages ago\u2014and murmured, apparently to himself:\\n\\n\u201cWit ye well, I saw it done.\u201d Then, after a short pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBefore I had recovered from the electric shock of that assertion, he had disappeared.\\n\\nThe whole evening I sat by the fire at the Warwick Arms, steeped in visions of bygone days, while the rain rattled against the panes and the wind howled round the eaves and corners. At intervals I dipped into old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s bewitching volume, feasting on its rich wonders and exploits, inhaling the perfume of its archaic names, and dreaming anew.\"}"
        }
      ],
