          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"65a9b94168524ea84ab96bda\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe and the children might do perfectly well here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Papa\u2014that\u2019s something you\u2019ve never managed yet, and I doubt you ever will. Isabella cannot stand staying behind when her husband goes.\u201d\\n\\nSadly, this was too true to argue. Unwelcome as it was, Mr. Woodhouse could only give a resigned sigh; and since Emma saw how the thought of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband dampened his spirits, she immediately steered the conversation to something that would lift them.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must spend as much time with us as she can while my brother and sister are here. I\u2019m sure she\u2019ll enjoy the children. We are very proud of them, aren\u2019t we, Papa? I wonder which one she\u2019ll think is the handsomest, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I wonder which she\u2019ll choose. Poor little darlings, they\u2019ll be so happy to come. They love being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they do, sir. I\u2019m sure I can\u2019t think of anyone who doesn\u2019t.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John is very like his mother. Henry is the eldest; he was named after me, not his father. John, the second, is named for his father. Some people are surprised, I believe, that the eldest wasn\u2019t named after his father, but Isabella insisted on Henry, which I thought very sweet of her. And he\u2019s a remarkably clever boy. They\u2019re all remarkably clever, and they have so many charming little ways. They\u2019ll come stand by my chair and say, \u2018Grandpapa, could you give me a piece of string?\u2019 And once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives are only made for grandpapas. I think their father is far too rough with them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you\u2019re so very gentle yourself; but if you could compare him to other fathers, you wouldn\u2019t find him rough. He wants his boys to be active and sturdy; and if they misbehave, he\u2019ll give them a sharp word now and then; but he\u2019s an affectionate father\u2014certainly Mr. John Knightley is affectionate. The children are all devoted to him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and tosses them up to the ceiling in such a terrifying way!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they love it, Papa; there is nothing they enjoy more. It\u2019s such fun for them that if their uncle didn\u2019t make them take turns, whichever one started would never let the other have a chance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I can\u2019t understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s true of all of us, Papa. Half the world can\u2019t fathom what delights the other half.\u201d\"}"
