          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"26c38d783267e1131706e4a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered and went back, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He did not even look at the murdered man. He knew the only way to survive was to refuse to face what had happened. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait causing all his misery was gone from his life now. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was an unusual Moorish piece, made of dull silver with steel arabesques and set with rough turquoises. His servant might notice it missing and start asking questions. After a brief hesitation, he went back and took it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the corpse. How still it was! How terribly white the long hands seemed! It looked like a hideous wax figure.\\n\\nWith the door locked behind him, he crept quietly downstairs. The wood creaked and sounded as if it were crying out in pain. He stopped several times and listened. No, everything was silent. It was only the echo of his own steps.\\n\\nWhen he reached the library, he spotted the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden. He unlocked a secret cupboard in the wainscoting, the one where he kept his disguises, and put the items inside. He could burn them later. He checked his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down to think. Every year\u2014nearly every month\u2014men were hanged in England for what he had done. There had been a madness for murder everywhere. Some red star must have come too close to Earth. And yet, what evidence pointed to him? Basil Hallward had left at eleven. No one had seen him return. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet was asleep. Paris! Yes, Basil had left for Paris on the midnight train, just as planned. With his strange, private habits, it would be months before anyone was suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nSuddenly an idea struck him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went out into the hall. He stopped, listening to the slow heavy steps of the policeman on the pavement outside and seeing the flash of the lantern in the window. He waited, holding his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments, he lifted the latch and slipped out, closing the door gently behind him. Then he began to ring the bell.\"}"
