{"request_id":"8c34df70808aa8d5ded16c76","rewritten_text":"You’ll be glad to hear that no disaster has accompanied the launch of the venture you viewed with such grim forebodings. I arrived here yesterday, and my first task is to reassure my dear sister about my health and my growing confidence in the success of my mission.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk the streets of Petersburg, I feel a cold northern breeze on my cheeks, bracing my nerves and filling me with delight. Do you understand that feeling? This breeze, which has traveled from the regions I’m heading toward, gives me a preview of those icy realms. Stirred by this promising wind, my daydreams grow more intense and vivid. I try in vain to believe that the pole is just a place of frost and desolation; it always appears to my imagination as a region of beauty and wonder. There, Margaret, the sun is forever visible, its wide disk barely touching the horizon while bathing everything in constant light. There—if you’ll allow me to trust the reports of earlier explorers—snow and ice are banished, and, sailing over a calm sea, we may be carried to a land surpassing every other region on the inhabited globe in marvels and beauty. Its products and features may be without equal, just as the phenomena of the heavenly bodies surely differ in those unknown solitudes. What might we not expect in a land of eternal light? I might discover the mysterious power that draws the compass needle and coordinate a thousand celestial observations whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to become forever consistent. I will satisfy my burning curiosity with the sight of a part of the world never before visited and may tread ground never before touched by human feet. These temptations are enough to overcome any fear of danger or death and to make me begin this arduous voyage with the same joy a child feels when he and his schoolmates sail a small boat on an adventure up their home river. But even if all these guesses prove false, you cannot deny the immeasurable benefit I would give to all humanity, for generations to come, by discovering a passage near the pole to those countries that currently take so many months to reach, or by uncovering the secret of the magnet, which—if possible at all—can only be achieved by an effort like mine.

These thoughts have calmed the agitation with which I began this letter, and my heart burns with an enthusiasm that lifts me to heaven, for nothing brings peace to the mind like a steady purpose—a point on which the soul can fix its gaze."}

