          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1644e11489bf6f2eedeb8db\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a gentleman, plain and simple. He was a gentleman through and through, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and the Widow Douglas always claimed a man\u2019s breeding counted as much as a horse\u2019s; no one argued with her, because she was top aristocracy in our town. Pap repeated it too, though he was no more high-class than a mudcat. Colonel Grangerford stood tall and thin, with a dark, pale complexion and not a touch of red anywhere. He shaved his narrow face clean every morning, had the thinnest lips and nostrils, a sharp nose, heavy eyebrows, and eyes so black and deep-set they looked like they stared out from caves. His forehead was high, his hair black and straight and hanging to his shoulders. His hands were long and slim. Every day he put on a clean shirt and a full linen suit so white it almost hurt to look at. On Sundays he added a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane topped with silver. There was never any foolishness around him, not a bit, and he was never loud. He was as kind as any man could be\u2014you could feel it, and that gave you confidence. Now and then he smiled, and it was a fine sight; but if he drew himself up like a liberty pole and lightning started flashing under his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree and only later find out what was wrong. He never had to tell anyone to mind their manners\u2014everyone behaved when he was around. Folks loved his company too; he was like sunshine most of the time\u2014he made everything seem like fair weather. And if he turned into a storm cloud, it was dark for only half a minute, and that was enough; nothing else would go wrong for a week afterward.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came downstairs in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs, greeted them, and wouldn\u2019t sit again until they did. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard where the decanter stood, mixed him a glass of bitters, and handed it over. He held it while Tom and Bob mixed their own, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d The colonel and his wife bowed just a touch and said thank you, and all three drank. After that, Bob and Tom dripped a spoon of water over the sugar and the bit of whiskey or apple brandy left in their tumblers, passed them to me and Buck, and we drank to the old folks too.\"}"
