          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"673dc63be6ab70956fcb9c0f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my early years discontent never entered my thoughts, and whenever ennui threatened, the sight of nature\u2019s beauties or the study of humanity\u2019s excellent and sublime works could always engage my heart and restore buoyancy to my spirits. Now I am a blasted tree, lightning seared into my soul; I felt then that I would live on merely to display what I would soon cease to be\u2014a wretched wreck of humanity, pitied by others and insufferable to myself.\\n\\nWe lingered at Oxford for some time, wandering through its surroundings and striving to recognize every spot linked with the most stirring period of English history. Our small exploratory excursions were frequently extended by the succession of objects that appeared. We went to the tomb of the illustrious Hampden and to the field where that patriot fell. For a moment my soul rose above its degrading, miserable terrors to reflect on the divine ideals of liberty and self-sacrifice of which these sights were monuments and reminders. For an instant I dared shake off my chains and gaze about me with a liberated and exalted spirit, but the iron had already bitten into my flesh, and I relapsed, trembling and despairing, into my wretched self.\\n\\nWith reluctance we left Oxford and traveled to Matlock, our next resting place. The countryside near this village recalled far more vividly the scenery of Switzerland; yet every feature was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the coronet of distant snowy Alps that constantly accompany the pine-clad mountains of my homeland. We toured the marvelous cavern and the small natural history cabinets, where curiosities are arranged just as in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. Henry\u2019s utterance of the latter name made me tremble, and I hurried to leave Matlock, which was now linked with that dreadful scene.\\n\\nContinuing north from Derby, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine myself back among the Swiss mountains. The patches of snow still lingering on the northern slopes, the lakes, and the rushing rocky streams were all beloved, familiar sights. We also formed some acquaintances who nearly managed to trick me into feeling happiness. Clerval\u2019s joy was proportionately greater than mine; his mind unfolded amid men of talent, and he discovered within himself greater faculties and resources than he had ever thought possible while keeping the company of his inferiors.\"}"
