          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52d3a24c08b01c0e3e8305d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to calm you quite a bit. But what ran through your mind? What did you think we\u2019d found?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was cornered\u2014the man\u2019s searching gaze was on him\u2014he\u2019d have given anything for a believable answer\u2014nothing came\u2014those eyes were drilling deeper and deeper\u2014one foolish reply popped up\u2014no time to judge it, so he went with it. Weakly he said, \u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was too miserable to smile, but the old man laughed loud and delightedly, shaking his whole frame from head to toe, and said that a laugh like that was money in a man\u2019s pocket because it slashed the doctor\u2019s bill like nothing else. Then he added, \u201cYou poor kid, you\u2019re pale and worn out\u2014you\u2019re not well at all\u2014no wonder you\u2019re a little flighty and off balance. But you\u2019ll be fine. Rest and sleep will pull you through, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed with himself for being such a fool and showing so much suspicious excitement, since he had abandoned the idea that the bundle from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the talk at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only thought it probably wasn\u2019t the treasure\u2014he hadn\u2019t known for sure\u2014and so the hint of a captured bundle rattled him beyond control. Still, he was glad the little scene had occurred, because now he knew without question that that package wasn\u2019t the package, and his mind was at ease and very comfortable. Everything seemed to be falling into place now; the treasure had to still be in No. 2, the men would be caught and jailed that day, and he and Tom could grab the gold that night without trouble or fear of interruption.\\n\\nRight as breakfast ended there was a knock at the door. Huck leaped for a hiding place\u2014he had no desire to be linked even remotely to the previous night. The Welshman let in several ladies and gentlemen, including Widow Douglas, and noticed groups of townspeople climbing the hill to stare at the stile. Word had spread. The Welshman had to tell the night\u2019s story to the guests. The widow expressed her gratitude loudly for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t mention it, ma\u2019am. There\u2019s someone you owe more to than me and my boys, maybe, but he won\u2019t let me tell his name. We wouldn\u2019t have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
        }
