          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"76f849fb5e4b4b43f5c49821\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I will soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d said Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, eager to take this as a special compliment, smiled very graciously.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I assume,\u201d he went on, \u201cand know that he is my son, though he does not share my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, and I shall be very happy to meet him. I am sure Mr. Elton will call on him right away, and we will both be delighted to have him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind. Frank will be extremely pleased, I\u2019m certain. He is to be in town next week, if not sooner. We had notice of it in a letter today. I met the mail as I was out this morning, and seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, I took the liberty of opening it, though it was not addressed to me\u2014it was to Mrs. Weston. She is the one he writes to most; I hardly ever get a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually opened what was meant for her! Oh, Mr. Weston,\u201d said Mrs. Elton, giving an affected laugh, \u201cI must protest. That sets a very dangerous example! Please do not let your neighbors follow it. Upon my word, if this is what we are to expect, we married women must fight back! Oh, Mr. Weston, I never would have believed it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, we men are sad creatures. You must guard yourself, Mrs. Elton. This letter tells us\u2014it is a short letter, written in haste just to alert us\u2014that they are all coming up to town at once, because of Mrs. Churchill. She has been ill all winter and thinks Enscombe is too cold, so they are all moving south immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed\u2014from Yorkshire, I believe. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, about a hundred and ninety miles from London, a long journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, very long. Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance to people with great fortunes, Mr. Weston? You would be amazed at how my brother, Mr. Suckling, travels. You will hardly believe it, but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back again with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe trouble with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d said Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand, has not been able to leave the sofa for a full week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter, he said she complained of being too weak to enter her conservatory unless she had both his arm and his uncle\u2019s.\u201d\"}"
