{"request_id":"d1503221e3bb47e56d37f04b","rewritten_text":"When my tears stopped, reason and memory returned, and I set myself, with increased composure, to consider what had happened and what course I ought to follow—Only a handful of hours had elapsed, yet for me an immense transformation had taken place—the natural labor of years had been accomplished since morning: my father was as though dead to me, and for a moment it seemed that he, white-haired, already lay in his coffin while I—my youth departing into premature age—stood mourning over his seasonable death. But it was not so; I was still young—alas, much too young—and he was not dead to others; only I, most desolate, must never see or speak with him again. I must flee him more earnestly than an enemy: whether in solitude or a bustling city, I must never gaze on him again. That thought made me gasp with agony, and stamping itself upon my imagination, it prevented me for a while from pursuing any train of thought. Ever after this, I vowed, I would dwell in the most barren seclusion. I would retire to the Continent and take the veil; not for the sake of religion, for I was no Catholic, but so that I might forever be closed off from mankind. There I should find solitude where tears might fall unchecked, and the sounds of life would never reach my ears.

But my father—my treasured and most afflicted father? Would he die? Would he never subdue the merciless passion now ruling him? Might he not, many years hence, when age had quenched the burning feelings possessing him, might he not then become a father to me once more? This thought smoothed my brow, and I felt (and wept to feel it) a half-melancholy smile driving from my lips their expression of sorrow: I dared to entertain brighter hopes for the life before me; years must pass, yet they would seem to fly quickly, borne on the wings of hope—or if they moved heavily, still they would move, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him endure another sixteen years of lonely wandering: let him again pour forth his wild laments before the vast forests and thunderous cataracts of another land: let him once more face peril and spirit-crushing hardships: let the scorching southern sun once again beat upon his passion-worn cheeks, and the icy night rains descend to chill his blood.

To that life, wretched father, I consign thee!—Go!—Spend thy days among savages and thy nights beneath the open heavens! Let thine limbs grow weary and thy heart congeal, and let all youth die within thee!"}

