          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abd280e1f1352dce788f83d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d I said, handing the slip back to him, \u201cI\u2019m still completely in the dark. Even if all the riches of Golconda were waiting for me as a reward, I\u2019m certain I couldn\u2019t earn them by solving this puzzle.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYet,\u201d said Legrand, \u201cthe solution isn\u2019t nearly as difficult as you might think from first glancing at the symbols. These characters, as anyone could easily guess, form a cipher\u2014that is, they carry a meaning. But from what we know of Kidd, I couldn\u2019t imagine him capable of constructing one of the more complex cryptographs. I decided at once that this was a simple kind\u2014one that would seem, to a sailor\u2019s crude mind, utterly impossible without the key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually solved it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEasily; I\u2019ve solved others that were ten thousand times more complex. Circumstances, and a certain inclination of mind, have led me to take an interest in such riddles, and it\u2019s questionable whether human ingenuity can devise a cipher that human ingenuity cannot, with proper effort, unravel. Once I had established connected, legible characters, I scarcely spared a thought for the difficulty of discovering their meaning.\\n\\n\u201cIn this case\u2014as in every case of secret writing\u2014the first question concerns the language of the cipher, because the methods of solution, especially for the simpler types, depend on and vary with the nature of the language. Generally, there\u2019s no option but to test (guided by probabilities) every language known to the solver until the right one appears. But with this cipher, the signature made everything easy. The pun on the word \u2018Kidd\u2019 works in no language but English. If not for that clue, I would have begun with Spanish and French, as the languages most likely for a pirate of the Spanish Main to use for such a secret. As it was, I assumed the cipher was English.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll notice there are no spaces between the words. If there had been, the task would have been relatively easy. I would have started by comparing and analyzing the shorter words, and if a single-letter word had appeared, as would be likely (a or I, for example), I would have considered the solution certain. But with no separations, my first step was to identify the most common letters and the rarest ones.\u201d\"}"
