          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5db4a4396cc3ac80173de858\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those dignified and frail old men were always seen bowing, and people believed that after bowing one customer out, they kept bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in.\\n\\nThe barrister shrewdly guessed that the banker would not speak so strongly without being morally certain. Though unprepared for the bitter truth he had to swallow, he managed it. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, wagging his courtroom forefinger at the Temple once he had done so, \u201cmy way out of this is to make all of you the ones in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a trick from an Old Bailey strategist, and it gave him great comfort. \u201cYou will not make me the one in the wrong, young lady,\u201d said Mr. Stryver; \u201cI\u2019ll do that for you.\u201d\\n\\nSo when Mr. Lorry visited that night as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver, surrounded by books and papers deliberately scattered around him, seemed to have nothing on his mind less than the morning\u2019s subject. He even looked surprised to see Mr. Lorry and appeared absent and distracted.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said the kindly envoy, after half an hour of useless attempts to bring him to the matter. \u201cI have been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d repeated Mr. Stryver, coolly. \u201cOh, of course! What was I thinking of?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat our talk was correct. My opinion is confirmed, and I repeat my advice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d replied Mr. Stryver, very amiably, \u201cthat I\u2019m sorry for it for your sake, and sorry for it for the poor father\u2019s sake. I know it must always be painful for the family; let\u2019s drop it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t follow you,\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI expect not,\u201d Stryver answered, nodding in a soothing and final way; \u201cno matter, no matter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d Mr. Lorry insisted.\\n\\n\u201cNo it doesn\u2019t; I assure you it doesn\u2019t. I assumed there was sense where there isn\u2019t, and worthy ambition where there isn\u2019t, and I\u2019m relieved of my mistake, with no harm done. Young women have made similar foolish choices often before, and often paid for them in poverty and obscurity. From an unselfish view, I\u2019m sorry it ended, because it would have been bad for me in worldly terms; from a selfish view, I\u2019m glad it ended, because it would have been bad for me in worldly terms\u2014it hardly needs saying I could have gained nothing from it.\u201d\"}"
