          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42ce7ef7dd8d4c2da23c3873\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Otis, who, I should add, is no insignificant authority on Art\u2014having enjoyed the privilege of several Boston winters during her girlhood\u2014assures me these jewels have considerable monetary value and would command a high price if brought to market. Given that, Lord Canterville, I am certain you will understand how utterly impossible it would be for me to permit them to remain in any member of my household\u2019s possession; in fact, such frivolous baubles, however fitting or even essential they may be for the dignity of the British nobility, would be utterly incongruous among those raised on the austere, and I believe enduring, doctrines of republican plainness. I ought perhaps to mention that Virginia is very keen you should allow her to keep the box as a keepsake of your unfortunate yet misdirected forebear. As it is exceedingly old and consequently somewhat shabby, you may feel inclined to grant her request. For my own part, I must admit it surprises me to find one of my children displaying any sympathy for medievalism in any shape, and I can only explain it by remembering that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs shortly after Mrs. Otis returned from a journey to Athens.\\n\\nLord Canterville listened very seriously to the worthy Minister\u2019s discourse, occasionally tugging at his grey moustache to conceal an unbidden smile, and when Mr. Otis concluded, he grasped his hand warmly and said, \u201cMy dear sir, your delightful little daughter rendered my unfortunate ancestor, Sir Simon, an immense service, and my family and I owe her a great debt for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels are plainly hers, and, by Jove, I dare say that if I were cruel enough to take them, the mischievous old knight would be up from his grave in a fortnight, giving me the very deuce of a time. As for calling them heirlooms, nothing is an heirloom unless the fact 