          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6de2b089740811ad5bbbeb73\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s wrong?\u201d Oliver asked. \u201cHush!\u201d the Dodger whispered. \u201cDo you see that old fellow at the bookstall?\u201d \u201cThe gentleman across the way?\u201d Oliver answered. \u201cI do.\u201d \u201cHe\u2019ll answer,\u201d the Dodger said. \u201cA first-rate plant,\u201d added Master Charley Bates. Oliver glanced between them in sheer astonishment; but questions were out of the question, for the two boys slipped quietly across the street and crept up behind the old gentleman to whom they had pointed him. Oliver followed a few steps, unable to decide whether to go forward or back, and watched in silent wonder. The old gentleman looked thoroughly respectable, with powdered hair and gold spectacles. He wore a bottle-green coat with a velvet collar, white trousers, and tucked a neat bamboo cane beneath his arm. He had picked up a book at the stall and stood there reading with as much intentness as if seated in his own study armchair. Very likely he fancied himself so, for it was quite clear from his absorption that he noticed neither stall, street, boys, nor anything but the volume before him. He read straight on, turning a page when he reached the bottom, starting again at the top line, and proceeding with the utmost eagerness. Imagine Oliver\u2019s horror as he lingered a few steps away, eyes stretched as wide as they could open, to see the Dodger slip a hand into the old gentleman\u2019s pocket and pull out a handkerchief! To see him pass it to Charley Bates, and then watch both boys dart around the corner at full speed! In a flash, the entire mystery of the handkerchiefs, watches, jewels, and the Jew burst upon the boy\u2019s mind. For a moment he stood, terror making his blood tingle as if he burned; then, dazed and frightened, he ran, scarcely aware of what he was doing, flying as fast as his legs would carry him. All this happened within a minute. The very instant Oliver started, the old gentleman felt for his pocket, missed his handkerchief, and spun around. Catching sight of the boy tearing away, he naturally assumed he was the thief; shouting \u201cStop thief!\u201d with all his strength, he set off after him, book in hand.\"}"
