          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"03c373a0effa762e7308878e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had not in the least suspected the sway he possessed. He followed her into the shrubbery without any intention of exerting it. His only object, in his worried desire to observe how she met Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, was to be at hand, should she permit, to comfort or advise her. All that followed sprang from the instant, the direct response of his feelings to what he heard. The exquisite assurance that she was wholly indifferent to Frank Churchill, that her heart lay entirely free of him, had birthed a hope that, eventually, he might win her own affection; but it was no immediate hope\u2014only a momentary yielding of eagerness over prudence, craving merely to be told she did not forbid his efforts to gain her. The loftier hopes so gradually revealed were all the more entrancing. The love he had wished to be allowed to create, if he were able, was already his! In the course of half an hour, he had moved from utter distress to something so near perfect felicity that no other term would fit.\\n\\nHer transformation matched his. This same half-hour had given each the priceless certainty of being cherished, stripping away, for both, the same measure of ignorance, jealousy, or mistrust. On his side, jealousy had long been present, dating back to the arrival\u2014or even the anticipated arrival\u2014of Frank Churchill. He had loved Emma and envied Frank Churchill from nearly the same moment, one feeling perhaps revealing the other. Jealousy of Frank Churchill had drawn him from the neighbourhood. The Box Hill expedition had fixed his resolve to depart. He would spare himself the sight of such authorized, encouraged attentions again. He had gone to learn indifference. Yet he chose badly. His brother\u2019s home contained too much domestic bliss; woman there showed too attractive a form; Isabella resembled Emma too strongly\u2014differing only by those marked inferiorities that continually set Emma\u2019s brilliance before him\u2014for much progress to be possible, even had he stayed longer. Nevertheless, he remained resolutely, day after day, until this very morning\u2019s post brought Jane Fairfax\u2019s story. Then, filled with the gladness he could not help feeling\u2014never having thought Frank Churchill worthy of Emma\u2014there arose so much tender concern, so much urgent anxiety for her, that he could stay away no more. He rode home through the rain, and walked up immediately after dinner to learn how this dearest and best of all beings, perfect despite all her imperfections, sustained the revelation.\"}"
