          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5590c20000adcd5f5158c366\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! The soul had its own animal nature, and the body sometimes felt spiritual. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could sink low. Who could say where bodily impulse ended or the mental began? The neat definitions from ordinary psychologists felt shallow. Yet how hard it was to choose between the different schools of thought! Was the soul a shadow sitting inside the house of sin? Or was the body contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? Separating spirit from matter was a mystery, and so was their union.\\n\\nHe wondered if psychology could ever become a precise enough science to reveal each small spring of life. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood others. Experience had no moral value. It was merely the name men gave to their mistakes. Moralists usually treated experience as a warning, claimed it had ethical power in shaping character, and praised it for teaching us what to follow and what to avoid. But experience held no motivating force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly showed was that our future would mirror our past, and that the sin we once committed with disgust we would commit many times with delight.\\n\\nTo him, the experimental method was clearly the only way to reach any scientific understanding of the passions. Dorian Gray was a subject ready to hand and seemed likely to yield rich and fruitful results. His sudden, wild love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon worth studying. Curiosity surely played a large role in it, along with the desire for new experiences, yet it was not simple but a complex passion. Whatever boys\u2019 crude sense-desire lay in it had been transformed by the imagination into something that seemed to the young man far removed from the senses, and for that very reason it became more dangerous. It was always the passions whose origins we lied about that ruled us most. Our weakest motives were the ones we knew and admitted to ourselves. Often when we thought we were experimenting on others, we were really experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nWhile Lord Henry sat lost in these thoughts, a knock sounded at the door, and his valet came in to remind him it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
        }
