          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8c491b5132d4cbd9df7e91d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At noon on Saturday I strolled to the well and watched for a bit. Merlin was still burning smoke powders, flailing the air, and mumbling nonsense as fiercely as ever, though he seemed rather crestfallen, for he hadn\u2019t coaxed a single bead of sweat from that well yet. At last I asked, \u201cHow does it look now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am even now engaged upon the mightiest enchantment known to the princes of the occult arts in the Eastern lands; if this fail me, nothing can serve. Hold thy peace until I am done.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that blacked out the entire landscape, surely making life miserable for the hermits, for the wind was blowing their way and rolling over their dens in a thick, heaving fog. He poured forth torrents of speech to match, contorted himself, and slashed the air with his hands in astounding fashion. After twenty minutes he sank down panting, about spent. Then came the abbot with several hundred monks and nuns, and behind them a throng of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the immense smoke and all wildly excited. The abbot anxiously asked for the results. Merlin replied:\\n\\n\u201cIf any labor of mortal strength could break the spell upon these waters, this last assay had wrought it. It has failed; from which I do now know that what I feared is truth established; the proof of this failure is that the most potent spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name none may utter and live, has cast his charm upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever shall, who can pierce the secret of that spell, and without that secret none may shatter it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done what man might. Let me depart.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally this threw the abbot into no small dismay. He turned to me with alarm written on his face and said, \u201cYe have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then\u2014pray, not all! Which part holds?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has laid a spell on the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are undone!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not surely? Ye mean, not surely?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWherefore ye would say that when he declares none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he says what is not necessarily so. There are conditions under which an attempt to break it might stand some chance\u2014some small, some trifling chance\u2014of success.\u201d\"}"
