          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4ade73d1b542203283d6609c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s wrong?\u201d Oliver asked. \u201cQuiet!\u201d whispered the Dodger. \u201cSee that old fellow at the bookstall?\u201d \u201cThe gentleman across the way?\u201d said Oliver. \u201cYes, I see him.\u201d \u201cHe\u2019ll do,\u201d the Dodger said. \u201cA perfect setup,\u201d added Master Charley Bates. Oliver stared at them in shock, but he had no chance to ask questions. The two boys slipped across the street, creeping close behind the old gentleman who had caught their attention. Oliver followed a few steps, unsure whether to approach or retreat, and gazed on in silent wonder. The old gentleman looked very respectable, with powdered hair and gold spectacles. He wore a bottle-green coat with a black velvet collar, white trousers, and carried a neat bamboo cane under his arm. He had picked up a book from the stall and stood there reading as earnestly as if he were in his own study. He might well have imagined himself at home, for his focus was such that he noticed neither the stall, the street, nor the boys. He read straight through, turning a page when he finished a line and starting the next, absorbed with lively interest. Oliver watched from a few steps away, horror widening his eyes, as the Dodger slipped a hand into the gentleman\u2019s pocket and pulled out a handkerchief. He saw him hand it to Charley Bates, and then both boys bolted around the corner at full speed. In a flash, the whole mystery of the handkerchiefs, the watches, the jewels, and the Jew came to the boy\u2019s mind. He stood there for a heartbeat, his blood burning through his veins with terror, then, confused and scared, he ran as fast as his legs could carry him. All this happened in a minute. As soon as Oliver began to run, the old gentleman felt in his pocket, missed his handkerchief, and spun around. Seeing Oliver speeding off, he naturally assumed the boy was the thief, and, shouting \u201cStop thief!\u201d with all his strength, chased after him, book still in hand.\"}"
