          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"603322dccb3c71c33db78848\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\nNo answer.\\n\\n\u201cTOM!\u201d\\n\\nStill no answer.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s happened to that boy, I wonder? You, TOM!\u201d\\n\\nSilence.\\n\\nThe old lady pulled her spectacles down to look over them around the room. Then she pushed them back up and peered under them. She almost never looked through them for small things like the boy; they were her fancy pair, the pride of her heart, meant for show, not practical use\u2014she could see just as well through a pair of stove lids. She looked puzzled for a moment and then said, not exactly furious, but loud enough for the furniture to hear:\\n\\n\u201cWell, I swear, if I catch you I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe did not finish, because she was already bending down and poking under the bed with the broom, and she needed her breath for that. She dragged out nothing but the cat.\\n\\n\u201cI never did see the beat of that boy!\u201d\\n\\nShe went to the open door, stood there, and looked out at the tomato vines and jimpson weeds that made up the garden. No Tom. So she lifted her voice at an angle made for distance and shouted:\\n\\n\u201cY-o-u-u, TOM!\u201d\\n\\nA slight noise sounded behind her and she turned just in time to grab a small boy by the back of his jacket and stop his escape.\\n\\n\u201cThere! I might have thought of that closet. What were you doing in there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing! Look at your hands. And look at your mouth. What is that stuff?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know, aunt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I know. It\u2019s jam\u2014that\u2019s what it is. Forty times I\u2019ve said if you didn\u2019t leave that jam alone I\u2019d skin you. Hand me that switch.\u201d\\n\\nThe switch hovered in the air\u2014the danger was serious\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy! Look behind you, aunt!\u201d\\n\\nThe old lady spun around and snatched her skirts out of harm\u2019s way. The boy took off instantly, scrambled up the high board fence, and disappeared over it.\\n\\nHis aunt Polly stood surprised for a moment, then broke into a gentle laugh.\\n\\n\u201cHang the boy, can\u2019t I ever learn anything? Hasn\u2019t he played enough tricks on me for me to be watching for them by now? But old fools are the biggest fools. Can\u2019t teach an old dog new tricks, so they say. But goodness me, he never plays the same trick two days in a row, so how\u2019s anyone supposed to know what\u2019s coming? He seems to know just how long he can tease me before I lose my temper, and he knows if he can stall me for a minute or make me laugh, it\u2019s all over and I can\u2019t hit him a lick.\u201d\"}"
