          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d4f19b5aa7f7f246cd2f39a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You wouldn\u2019t know me unless you read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but that doesn\u2019t matter. Mr. Mark Twain made that book, and he mostly told the truth. Sometimes he stretched things, but mostly it was true. That\u2019s nothing. I never saw anyone who didn\u2019t lie now and then, except maybe Aunt Polly, the widow, or Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly\u2014Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all in that book, which is mostly true, with some stretches, like I said.\\n\\nHere\u2019s how that book ends: Tom and I found the money the robbers hid in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars each\u2014all gold. It was a heap of money when it was piled up. Judge Thatcher took it and put it out at interest, and it brought us a dollar a day each all year long\u2014more than anyone could figure what to do with. Widow Douglas took me in as her son and said she would civilize me; but it was hard living in her house, with how steady and proper she was in everything. When I couldn\u2019t take it anymore, I ran off. I got back into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead and felt free and happy. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he was starting a band of robbers, and I could join if I would go back to the widow and be respectable. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, along with a bunch of other names, though she didn\u2019t mean any harm. She put me in those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and feel squeezed in. Then the old routine started up again. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to show up on time. At the table you couldn\u2019t start eating right away; you had to wait for the widow to bow her head and mutter over the food, even though nothing was wrong with it\u2014except that everything was cooked separate. In a barrel of odds and ends it\u2019s different; things mix up, share their juices, and taste better.\\n\\nAfter supper she brought out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was eager to know all about him; but after a while she let slip that Moses had been dead a long time. Then I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t pay attention to dead folks.\"}"
