          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf6a0220523289f0d67a39b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Indeed, he had arrayed himself as \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d one of his most notable portrayals, and one the Cantervilles remembered only too well, for it truly sparked their feud with their neighbour, Lord Rufford. It was about quarter past two in the morning, and, so far as he could tell, the household slumbered. Yet as he sauntered toward the library to examine whether any vestige of the bloodstain remained, two figures suddenly sprang from a shadowy nook, flung their arms frantically above their heads, and screeched \u201cBOO!\u201d right in his ear.\\n\\nGripped by a panic quite excusable under such conditions, he bolted for the staircase, only to find Washington Otis stationed there with the large garden syringe; thus trapped by foes on every side and nearly brought to bay, he fled into the vast iron stove, which, luckily for him, lay unlit, and had to crawl home through flues and chimneys, reaching his own room in a dreadful state of grime, disorder, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that night he ventured on no further nocturnal raids. The twins lay in ambush for him more than once and scattered the corridors with nutshells nightly, much to the irritation of their parents and the servants, but their efforts proved futile. Plainly his feelings were so hurt he refused to show himself. Mr. Otis therefore returned to his great treatise on the history of the Democratic Party, which had occupied him for years; Mrs. Otis arranged a splendid clam-bake that astonished the entire county; the boys devoted themselves to lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other national American pastimes; while Virginia rode the lanes on her pony, with the young Duke of Cheshire\u2014who was spending the last week of his holidays at Canterville Chase\u2014at her side. People generally concluded the ghost had departed, and Mr. Otis even wrote as much to Lord Canterville, who replied with delight at the tidings and sent his warmest compliments to the Minister\u2019s estimable wife.\\n\\nThe Otis family, however, was mistaken, for the ghost still haunted the place and, though now nearly an invalid, had no intention of letting things end there, especially after learning that the young Duke of Cheshire was among the guests. The Duke\u2019s great-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost, and was discovered next morning sprawled on the card-room floor in such a powerless paralytic condition that, though he lived to advanced years, he could utter nothing ever again but \u201cDouble Sixes.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
