{"request_id":"076954c71af16dee890f6afe","rewritten_text":"Four days after those peculiar events, a funeral procession set out from Canterville Chase around eleven o’clock at night. Eight black horses drew the hearse, each beast bearing a great spray of swaying ostrich plumes, and the heavy coffin lay beneath a rich purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville arms. Servants walked beside the hearse and the coaches with torches blazing, making the cortege strikingly solemn. Lord Canterville, who had journeyed up from Wales expressly to attend, led the mourners and shared the first carriage with little Virginia. Following them rode the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington with the three boys, and Mrs. Umney occupied the final carriage, since everyone felt that after more than fifty years of the ghost alarming her, she deserved the privilege of witnessing his end. A deep grave had been opened in the churchyard corner beneath the ancient yew, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier conducted the service with great solemnity. Once the rites concluded, the servants quenched their torches according to an old Canterville tradition, and as the coffin descended, Virginia stepped forward to place upon it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment, the moon slipped out from behind a cloud and poured silent silver over the little churchyard, while a nightingale began singing from a distant thicket. Thinking of the ghost’s tale about the Garden of Death, Virginia’s eyes filled with tears, and she scarcely spoke on the ride home.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville returned to town, Mr. Otis met with him concerning the jewels the ghost had bestowed on Virginia. They were exquisite, most notably a ruby necklace with antique Venetian setting, truly a splendid piece of sixteenth-century craftsmanship, and their worth was so great that Mr. Otis felt uneasy about letting his daughter keep them.

“My lord,” he said, “I understand that here mortmain is considered applicable to trinkets as well as land, and it is evident to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, family heirlooms of yours. I must therefore entreat you to take them with you to London and view them merely as part of your property, returned under curious circumstances. As for my daughter, she is still merely a child, and, I am happy to say, cares little for such tokens of idle luxury.”"}

