{"request_id":"fb19872392fff64c99be1345","rewritten_text":"By eight o’clock I had actually climbed to a height of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It seemed clear to me that my rate of ascent was not only increasing, but would have showed some progress even if I hadn’t thrown out the ballast. The pains in my head and ears returned in violent bursts, and I still bled from the nose now and then; yet overall I suffered less than one might expect. Breathing grew harder with every moment, each inhalation accompanied by a harsh spasmodic pull in my chest. I unpacked the condensing apparatus and readied it for immediate use.

The view of the earth at this point in my rise was truly beautiful. To the west, the north, and the south, as far as I could see, stretched an endless sheet of seemingly unruffled ocean, deepening each moment into a darker blue and already taking on a faint curve. Far to the east, yet perfectly visible, were the islands of Great Britain, the entire Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small portion of northern Africa. No individual buildings could be distinguished; the proudest cities of humankind had vanished from the landscape. From the Rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a dim speck, the dark Mediterranean, sprinkled with shining islands as the sky is with stars, spread eastward as far as I could see, until the whole body of water seemed to spill headlong over the edge of the horizon, and I found myself listening on tiptoe for the roar of a colossal waterfall. Overhead, the sky was pitch black, and the stars shone brilliantly.

The pigeons were clearly suffering now, so I decided to release them. I untied one—a beautiful gray-mottled bird—and set him on the rim of the wickerwork. He looked extremely uneasy, glanced nervously around, fluttered his wings, and cooed loudly, yet refused to leave the car. Finally I picked him up and tossed him about six yards from the balloon. Contrary to my expectations, he made no move to descend but fought desperately to return, crying out with shrill, piercing calls. He finally regained his place on the rim, but almost at once his head drooped to his breast, and he died, falling inside the car."}

