{"request_id":"e23ea01cfa42f6bb88697d95","rewritten_text":"The stars still burned bright when I woke, and Taurus high in the southern sky told me it was midnight. I had been roused from troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s final feast; I arrived hungry, the dishes were uncovered, the hot water sent up empty steam, while I fled the host’s anger as he took Raymond’s form. To my fevered mind the vessels he hurled after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s face twisted into a giant phantom, the sign of plague upon its brow. The swelling shadow grew higher and higher, filling the adamantine vault above, threatening to burst the roof that held the world. The nightmare became agony; with effort I cast off sleep and forced my reason back to its usual seat. My first thought was Perdita; I had to return to her, support her, draw strength from despair to sustain her broken heart, and call her back from wild grief with duty’s stern rule and gentle regret.

The stars were my only guide. I turned from the dreadful ruin of the Golden City and, after hard work, escaped its walls. Outside I met a group of soldiers; I borrowed a horse and rushed to my sister. In the short time I had been away, the plain had changed; the camp was gone; scattered remnants of the dispersed army gathered in small groups, each face dark with shock and fear.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, afraid to move, speak, or even look. Perdita sat on the hall’s marble floor, her head sunk to her chest, hair loose, fingers twisting together. She was pale as the stone beneath her, every feature drawn in pain. She saw me and looked up, searching; her half-formed glance of hope became misery. Words failed on my tongue; a ghastly smile creased my lips. She understood my sign; her head dropped again, fingers working in restless knots. At last I found my voice, but the sound frightened her; the poor girl had read my face, and for nothing in the world would she have her sorrow fixed and confirmed by cold, final words. She even tried to turn my mind from it: rising from the floor, she whispered, “Hush! After much weeping, Clara sleeps; we must not wake her.”"}

