          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f95acf3e3cf52246fae8ad02\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must leave, ignorant of what awaits me; yet I shall either return here or catch up with your party the instant I am able. A single word or glance will suffice to settle whether I enter your father\u2019s house tonight or never again.\u201d\\n\\nIt was not the sort of letter to recover from quickly. Half an hour alone to think might have calmed her, but the mere ten minutes that elapsed before she was disturbed, coupled with all the constraints of her circumstances, did nothing for her composure. Each moment seemed only to heighten the tumult. It was joy too intense to bear. And while she was still in the first rush of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta entered together.\\n\\nThe sheer obligation to appear her usual self prompted an immediate effort, yet after a time she could manage no more. The conversation passed her by completely; she could not follow a word and was forced to plead illness and withdraw. They immediately noticed how unwell she looked, were appalled and full of concern, and insisted they would not budge without her for anything. This was dreadful. If only they had departed and left her to the quiet of that room, it would have soothed her; but to have them all clustered around her in anxious attendance was maddening, and in desperation she declared she would return home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Musgrove, \u201cgo straight home and take care, so you may be ready for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to tend you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nYet the chair was out of the question. Worse than anything! To sacrifice the least chance of exchanging two words with Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk through town (and she felt almost sure she would meet him) was impossible. She vehemently opposed the chair, and Mrs. Musgrove, who considered only a single type of ailment, having anxiously assured herself there had been no fall involved; that Anne had not recently slipped and struck her head; that she was thoroughly certain no fall had occurred; could then let her go with a relieved heart, relying on finding her improved by evening.\\n\\nResolute not to overlook any precaution, Anne exerted herself and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI fear, ma\u2019am, that everything is not entirely settled. Please be so kind as to inform the other gentlemen that we expect your whole party tonight. I am afraid there may have been some misapprehension; and I especially wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is perfectly clear, I give you my word.\u201d\"}"
