{"request_id":"c692a561a9965540b1b63398","rewritten_text":"In fact, once you rise to a certain altitude, the weight of air above you does not decrease in direct proportion to each extra height you climb (as I have already shown). Instead, it lessens at a steadily declining rate. So it is clear that no matter how high we go, we cannot honestly say we reach a boundary where no atmosphere exists. I concluded that air must still be present, even if only as an infinitely thin gas.

At the same time, I knew there were arguments claiming a real and fixed edge to the atmosphere, beyond which there is absolutely no air. Yet these arguments ignored one factor that, though not a complete refutation, seemed to deserve close attention. When we compare the time between Encke’s comet’s arrivals at perihelion, and carefully account for every disturbance caused by planetary attraction, we find the period slowly but steadily shrinking. In other words, the major axis of the comet’s orbit is becoming shorter at a regular rate. This is exactly what we would expect if the comet faces resistance from an extremely thin ether filling the region of its orbit. Such a medium would slow the comet’s speed, which would weaken its outward, or centrifugal, force and thereby strengthen the sun’s pull on it. The sun’s attraction would thus increase, drawing the comet closer with each orbit. There seems no other explanation for this change.

Furthermore, the actual diameter of the comet’s coma contracts quickly as it nears the sun, then expands just as quickly as it moves toward aphelion. Was I not justified in agreeing with M. Valz that this visible shrinking comes from the compression of the same ether, denser nearer the sun? The lenticular phenomenon called the zodiacal light also deserved attention. This glow, so clear in the tropics and unlike any meteoric flash, rises obliquely from the horizon and generally follows the sun’s equator. It seemed clearly a rare atmosphere stretching outward from the sun beyond the orbit of Venus, and I thought it likely extended far farther. I could not believe this medium was limited to the comet’s path or to the sun’s immediate vicinity."}

