content.text:
{"request_id":"f086bffb8abdef486e92ecef","rewritten_text":"My father had lived, and his remembrance informed the very essence of my life. I could feel gratitude for another, yet never again could I love or hope as once; everything became suffering; even joys were borne, not truly felt. I was like a lone clearing among mountains, hemmed in on all sides by sheer black cliffs, where no warm ray could reach and from which there was no passage to sunnier meadows. Thus, though the spirit of friendship soothed me briefly, it could not restore me. It passed like some gentle visitation; it departed and I scarcely sensed the want. The spirit of being was extinct within me; be not astonished then that when it arrived I greeted not more cheerfully, and when it left I mourned not more deeply, the best boon of heaven—a friend.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I shall relate his history in brief, that you may fathom how icy my heart was, not to be thawed by his fervent speech and tender compassion; and how he, being likewise grievously unhappy, might have been a perfect solace for me, had I not been petrified by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville’s calamities were not embedded in his heart’s core as mine were; his sorrow was natural, meant not to destroy but to refine the heart, and from which, once its shadow lifted, he might shine forth more radiant and content than before.

Woodville was the son of an impoverished clergyman and had been classically educated. He belonged to that rare few whom fortune favours at birth; on whom she lavishes every gift of mind and form with boundless profusion, and under whose peculiar guardianship no flaw, however slight, nor disappointment, however fleeting, is permitted to alight. She seemed to have fashioned his intellect of such purity that no dross could stain it, and endowed his understanding so that no error could mislead it. His genius was transcendent; when it climbed like a bright star in the east, every gaze turned upon it in admiration. He was a Poet. That title, so often degraded, scarcely conveys what he was. He resembled some ancient bard whom the muses crowned in his cradle and on whose lips the bees had fed. Moving among other men, he appeared wrapped in a celestial halo that separated and elevated him above them. It was his matchless beauty, the dazzling flame in his eyes, and his words whose rich cadences held the hearer in silent, ecstatic awe, that set him apart, so that beside him others seemed made merely to minister to his superior excellence."}

