          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d5929a02098d8574e7bbce1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis marriage is exactly as it ought to be. I did not believe it in the beginning, yet now I concede it. For you the gods fashioned Sibyl Vane. Without her you would have been unfinished.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, Basil,\u201d Dorian Gray replied, clasping his hand. \u201cI knew you would understand me. Harry is so cynical; he frightens me. But here comes the orchestra. It is dreadful, though it goes on only five minutes. Once the curtain goes up, you will behold the girl to whom I mean to give my entire life, to whom I have already surrendered whatever good is within me.\u201d\\n\\nFifteen minutes later, amid a storm of applause, Sibyl Vane came onto the stage. She was indeed beautiful to behold\u2014one of the most exquisite beings Lord Henry thought he had ever encountered. Something of a fawn lingered in her timid grace and startled gaze. A delicate flush, like the reflection of a rose in silver, touched her cheeks as she looked at the packed, eager audience. She retreated a step or two; her lips seemed to quiver. Basil Hallward sprang up and began clapping. Dorian Gray sat motionless, as if dreaming, fixed upon her. Lord Henry peered through his glasses, whispering, \u201cCharming! charming!\u201d\\n\\nThe setting was the hall of Capulet\u2019s home; Romeo, in his pilgrim\u2019s attire, had arrived with Mercutio and the rest. The orchestra, scant as it was, played a few bars, and the dance commenced. Amid a throng of awkward, poorly attired performers, Sibyl Vane moved like a being from some finer realm. As she danced, her body swayed like a water plant. The curves of her throat were those of a white lily. Her hands appeared wrought of cool ivory.\\n\\nStill, she was strangely apathetic. No happiness appeared when her eyes fell upon Romeo. The few lines allotted to her\u2014\\n\\nGood pilgrim, you do wrong your hand too much,\\nWhich mannerly devotion shows in this;\\nFor saints have hands that pilgrims\u2019 hands do touch,\\nAnd palm to palm is holy palmers\u2019 kiss\u2014\\n\\nwith the short exchange that followed, she delivered in a wholly artificial style. The voice remained exquisite, yet its tone rang entirely false. The colouring was wrong. It drained the verse of vitality. It rendered the passion unreal.\\n\\nDorian Gray turned pale as he followed her. He was both bewildered and uneasy. Neither friend dared speak to him. To them she appeared completely inept.\"}"
