{"request_id":"851ffc1a93f2ff65c9ac8636","rewritten_text":"The instruments now showed I had reached an altitude of 132,000 feet—twenty-five miles up—and I was surveying nothing less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of the earth’s entire surface. By nine o’clock I had again lost sight of land to the east, but not before realizing that the balloon was drifting rapidly to the north-northwest. The curvature of the ocean below was unmistakable, though bands of cloud often interrupted my view, and I noticed that even the lightest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers through the valve. Instead of floating as I expected, they plunged straight down like a musket ball, staying together and moving with incredible speed, out of sight in seconds. I was initially baffled by so strange a sight, unwilling to believe my ascent had all at once accelerated so dramatically. Soon, however, I realized the atmosphere had grown too thin to support even feathers; they had indeed fallen with astonishing speed, and I was simply taken aback by the combined velocity of their descent and my rise.

“By ten o’clock I had little left to occupy me. Everything went smoothly, and I felt sure the balloon was climbing faster every moment, though I no longer had a way to measure the increase. I felt no pain or discomfort of any kind and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I alternated between checking the condition of my various instruments and renewing the air inside the cabin. I resolved to do this every forty minutes, more for the sake of my health than because the air truly needed such frequent refreshment. Meanwhile, I could not stop myself from indulging in expectations. My fancy roamed through the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. Imagination, at last unfettered, wandered freely among the ever-shifting marvels of a shadowy, unstable land. At one moment I envisioned ancient, venerable forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls plunging with a thunderous roar into bottomless pits. Then I was suddenly transported into still, noonday solitudes where no winds from heaven intruded, and where vast fields of poppies and slender, lily-like blooms stretched endlessly, all perfectly silent and motionless forever.”"}

