          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a0cac1f9cbca198122cb4663\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 7th. I rose early and, to my great joy, finally saw what could only be the northern Pole itself. There was no doubt about it. It lay directly below me; yet I had climbed so high that nothing could be seen clearly. From the progress recorded in my altitude numbers between six in the morning on April second and twenty minutes to nine that same day (when the barometer fell), it could be reasonably inferred that, at four in the morning of April seventh, the balloon had reached a height of no less than 7,254 miles above the sea. This elevation may seem immense, but the calculations I used probably produced a figure far below the truth. In any case I unquestionably saw the earth\u2019s whole major diameter; the entire northern hemisphere spread beneath me like a flat map, and the equator formed the horizon around me. Your Excellencies may readily imagine that the narrow tracts within the Arctic circle, though directly beneath me and therefore not foreshortened, were still too small and too distant from my viewpoint to allow very precise study. Still, what I could make out was striking and unusual. Northward from that vast rim already mentioned\u2014the practical limit of human discovery in these regions\u2014stretched an unbroken, or nearly unbroken, sheet of ice. In the first few degrees of its extent the surface was fairly flat, farther on it settled into a plane, and finally, becoming noticeably concave, it ended at the Pole in a circular center, sharply defined, whose apparent diameter subtended an angle of about sixty-five seconds. Its dusky color, shifting in intensity, was always darker than any other spot on the visible hemisphere, at times deepening into the blackest and most impenetrable shade. Beyond this little could be learned. By noon the circular center had shrunk considerably, and by seven in the evening I lost sight of it entirely, the balloon crossing the western edge of the ice and racing off toward the equator.\\n\\nApril 8th. I noticed a clear decrease in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a marked change in its general color and appearance.\"}"
