          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0b591b4fe79f7abdd19c3ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I call on you, spirits of the dead, and you wandering agents of vengeance, to help and guide me in what I must do. Let the cursed, hellish monster drink deep of agony; let him feel the despair that torments me now. \\n\\nI began my invocation with such solemnity and awe that I almost believed the shades of my murdered friends heard me and approved my devotion, but fury seized me by the end, and rage choked my voice. \\n\\nThrough the stillness of night, a loud and demonic laugh answered me. It rang in my ears long and heavy; the mountains echoed it, and I felt as if all of hell surrounded me with mockery and laughter. Surely in that moment I would have gone mad and ended my miserable life, except that my vow had been heard and I was meant for vengeance. The laughter died away, and then a familiar, hateful voice, seemingly right at my ear, whispered audibly, \u201cI\u2019m satisfied, miserable wretch! You\u2019ve chosen to live, and I\u2019m satisfied.\u201d \\n\\nI lunged toward the spot where the sound came from, but the fiend escaped my grasp. Suddenly the broad disk of the moon rose and lit up his ghastly, twisted figure as he fled with more-than-human speed. \\n\\nI chased him, and for many months this has been my sole task. Guided by a faint clue, I followed the twists of the Rh\u00f4ne in vain. The blue Mediterranean appeared, and by strange chance I saw the fiend slip aboard a vessel bound for the Black Sea under cover of night. I booked a berth on the same ship, but he slipped away\u2014I still have no idea how. \\n\\nThrough the wilds of Tartary and Russia, even though he always slipped away, I never stopped tracking him. Sometimes peasants, terrified by this horrific apparition, showed me the direction he took; sometimes he himself, afraid that if I lost his trail I would give up and die, left signs to guide me. Snow fell on my head, and I saw the imprint of his huge footsteps in the white plain. You who are just starting life, to whom care is new and agony unknown, how could you understand what I have felt and still feel? Cold, hunger, and exhaustion were the least of my torments; some devil\u2019s curse clung to me, and I carried my own eternal hell. Yet a benevolent spirit still followed me, guiding my steps, and when I complained the most it suddenly pulled me out of obstacles that seemed impossible. Sometimes, when nature, overwhelmed by hunger, nearly collapsed, a meal appeared for me in the wilderness that restored my strength and courage.\"}"
