          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a90a73ed6ef272fcbf74475c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to ease your mind a good deal. But what gave you that shock? What were you expecting we\u2019d turned up?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was in a tight corner\u2014the questioning eye fixed on him\u2014he would have bartered anything for a believable answer\u2014nothing came\u2014those probing eyes drove deeper and deeper\u2014a foolish reply presented itself\u2014there was no time to weigh it, so he tried it\u2014weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too miserable to grin, while the old man roared with delight, shaking every joint in his frame, and concluded by remarking that a laugh like that was cash in a man\u2019s pocket since it cut a doctor\u2019s bill down like everything. Then he went on:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old fellow, you\u2019re pale and overdone\u2014you\u2019re not well at all\u2014no wonder you\u2019re a bit light-headed and off balance. But you\u2019ll pull through. Rest and sleep will bring you round all right, I\u2019m hoping.\u201d\\n\\nHuck smarted to think he had been such a fool and shown such a suspicious excitement, for he had abandoned the notion that the bundle from the tavern contained the treasure as soon as he\u2019d overheard the talk at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only supposed it wasn\u2019t the treasure\u2014he hadn\u2019t known\u2014and so the revelation of a captured parcel upset his self-control. Still, he was glad the fuss had taken place, since now he knew beyond doubt that this bundle wasn\u2019t the bundle, and his mind was at ease and remarkably comfortable. In truth, everything seemed to be moving exactly as they wanted now; the treasure had to be still in No. 2, the men would be seized and locked up that day, and he and Tom could secure the gold that night without effort or any fear of interference.\\n\\nThey had barely finished breakfast when there was a knock at the door. Huck darted for a hiding spot, unwilling to be tied even in rumor to the previous night\u2019s affair. The Welshman let in several ladies and gentlemen, the Widow Douglas among them, and noticed clusters of townsfolk climbing the hill to stare at the stile. So the story had got around. The Welshman had to recount the night\u2019s events to the visitors. The widow voiced her gratitude for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t mention it, madam. There\u2019s someone you owe more to than to me and my lads, likely, but he won\u2019t let me speak his name. We\u2019d never have been there except for him.\u201d\"}"
