          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b2bb3719045233fb15e52d0b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams to fade like this? Our name had been written \u201ca little lower than the angels,\u201d yet we proved no better than mayflies. We had called ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and lo, we were a \u201cquintessence of dust.\u201d We complained that the pyramids still stood while the embalmed body of their builder decayed. Alas, even the shepherd\u2019s straw hut we passed on the road held more promise of longevity than the whole human race. How could we reconcile this dismal change with our past hopes and apparent powers?\\n\\nSuddenly an inner voice, clear and articulate, seemed to say: Thus from eternity it was decreed; the steeds that carry Time forward had this hour and this end chained to them from the moment the void produced its burden. Would you try to read backwards the unchangeable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Omnipotent! eternal, changeless Necessity! You sit with tireless fingers weaving the indissoluble chain of events\u2014I will not complain of your acts. If my human mind cannot accept that all that exists is right, still, since what is must be, I will sit among the ruins and smile. Truly we were not born to enjoy, but to submit and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow tired if I describe in detail our long journey from Paris to Geneva? If, day by day, I were to record in journal form the pressing miseries of our lot, could my hand write, or language provide words, equal to the variety of our woe, the crowding of one lamentable event upon another? Have patience, reader! Whoever you are, wherever you live, whether of a spiritual race or descended from some surviving pair, your nature will be human, your home the earth; here you will read of the acts of the extinct race and ask in wonder if those who suffered what you find recorded were made of frail flesh and soft bodies like yourself. Indeed they were\u2014weep, then; for surely, solitary being, you will be tender-hearted. Shed compassionate tears; yet listen carefully to the tale, and learn the deeds and sufferings of your predecessors.\\n\\nStill, the last events that marked our progress through France were so full of strange horror and heavy misery that I dare not linger over them. If I dissected each incident, every moment would contain a harrowing tale whose smallest detail would chill your young blood. It is right that I raise, for your instruction, this memorial to the lost race; but not that I drag you through the wards of a hospital, nor the hidden chambers of the charnel house.\"}"
        }
