{"request_id":"be5add63c522557eb4cdd68f","rewritten_text":"I was born in a little corner of land ringed by the sea and cloaked in cloud. When I picture the globe with its endless oceans and uncharted continents, my homeland appears only as a tiny speck in the vast whole; yet weighed on the scale of intellect, it surpasses far larger, more populated countries. It proves that human mind alone created everything good or great for humankind, with Nature merely his first minister. England, set far to the north amid the turbid sea, now visits me in dreams as a vast, well-crewed ship that mastered the winds and rode triumphantly over the waves. In my boyhood she was my entire universe. When I stood on my native hills and saw plains and mountains stretching to the furthest limits of my sight, dotted with my countrymen’s dwellings and cultivated by their labor, I believed the earth’s center lay there, and the rest of the world was like a fable whose loss would cost my imagination or understanding no effort.

My fortunes have always demonstrated how change can reshape a man’s life. In my case this was almost inherited. My father was one of those men on whom nature lavishly bestowed the prized gifts of wit and imagination, then left his life’s bark to be driven by those winds without reason for a rudder or judgment for a pilot. His origins were obscure, but circumstances brought him early to public attention, and his small paternal property was quickly squandered in the glittering world of fashion and luxury where he played his part. In the brief years of carefree youth he was adored by the highborn idlers of the day, not least by the young sovereign, who fled the intrigues of party and the painful duties of kingship to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in his company. My father’s impulses, which he never controlled, constantly led him into scrapes from which only his ingenuity could free him; the growing heap of debts of honor and commerce that would have crushed anyone else was borne by him with a light heart and untamable cheerfulness. His presence was so essential at the tables and gatherings of the rich that his transgressions were deemed pardonable, and he was greeted with intoxicating flattery.

Such popularity, like every other, is fleeting; and the troubles pressing on him multiplied at a terrifying rate compared with his limited means of escape."}

