          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3b24cb3281e04bceb0fe87c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself to visit the Coles. The trip gave her plenty of pleasant thoughts the next day, and whatever she might have sacrificed in dignified solitude was more than compensated by the glow of popularity. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014good people who deserved to be made happy\u2014and left a reputation that would linger for a long time.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is rare, and there were two points that still bothered her. She wondered whether she had betrayed one woman\u2019s duty to another by sharing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings with Frank Churchill. It had hardly been right, but the idea had taken hold of her so strongly that it slipped out, and his readiness to accept everything she told him felt like a compliment to her insight\u2014one that made it hard to be sure she should have kept quiet.\\n\\nHer other regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and in that she had no doubts. She sincerely and unquestionably lamented that her own playing and singing were inferior. She deeply regretted the idle hours of her childhood\u2014and sat down to practice with determination for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was interrupted when Harriet arrived; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might have been comforted quickly.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t lump us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunlight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh dear\u2014I think you play better than she does. I\u2019m sure I prefer hearing you. Everyone last night said how beautifully you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything about it must have felt the difference. The truth is, Harriet, my playing is just good enough to earn praise, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far beyond it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I will always think you play just as well as she does, or that if there is any difference, nobody would notice it. Mr. Cole said you had excellent taste, and Mr. Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, saying he valued taste more than technique.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! But Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw she had technique, but I didn\u2019t know she had any taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing\u2014there\u2019s no understanding a word. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know it\u2019s only what she\u2019s obliged to do, since she\u2019ll have to teach.\u201d\"}"
