          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"11638e07d61441cb89ba3320\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am delighted to hear such a favourable report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cPray tell her from me that she must not expect to excel unless she practises continually.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he answered, \u201cthat she does not require that counsel. She practises habitually.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practise too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist upon her never neglecting it. I often remind young ladies that no proficiency in music can be acquired without steady diligence. I have repeatedly told Miss Bennet she will never play truly well unless she exercises more; and though Mrs. Collins has no instrument of her own, she is perfectly welcome\u2014as I have frequently assured her\u2014to come daily to Rosings and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would inconvenience no one in that quarter of the house.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked slightly mortified by his aunt\u2019s want of manners, and held his peace.\\n\\nWhen the coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam recalled Elizabeth\u2019s promise to play for him, and she immediately took her seat at the instrument. He drew a chair close beside her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then resumed talking to her other nephew, until he walked away and, moving with his customary deliberateness toward the pianoforte, placed himself where he could fully observe the lovely performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed his design, and at the first convenient pause turned toward him with a mischievous smile, saying\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou intend to awe me, Mr. Darcy, by approaching with such solemnity to hear me. But I shall not be intimidated, even though your sister plays so admirably. There is a stubborn spirit in me that cannot endure being frightened at another\u2019s pleasure. My courage always mounts with every attempt to cow me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not tell you that you err,\u201d he replied, \u201cfor you cannot seriously suppose I have any wish to alarm you; and I have known you long enough to understand that you take great delight in sometimes professing sentiments that are not, in truth, your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this sketch of herself, and turned to Colonel Fitzwilliam: \u201cYour cousin is giving you a very pretty illustration of me, and teaching you never to believe a word I utter. I am particularly unfortunate in meeting someone so well qualified to expose my true character, in a neighbourhood where I hoped to pass with some slight degree of credit.\u201d\"}"
        }
