          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b74cbab85a0a850f549949a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Back in my day, in my own country, this money came from tariffs, and the citizen liked to imagine the foreign importer paid it. That belief comforted him, though in truth the American people paid it, and the cost was so evenly spread that the yearly charge to a man worth a hundred million dollars and to a day laborer\u2019s nursing infant was exactly the same\u2014each paid six dollars. Nothing could be fairer than that, I guess. Now, Scotland and Ireland paid tribute to Arthur, and all the British Islands together held fewer than a million people. A mechanic\u2019s average wage was three cents a day when he supported himself. By that measure the national government\u2019s expenses were ninety thousand dollars a year, or about two hundred fifty a day. So, by using nickels instead of gold on a king\u2019s-evil day, I hurt no one, displeased no one, made everyone happy, and saved four-fifths of that day\u2019s national expense besides\u2014a saving equal to eight hundred thousand dollars in my America. When I made the change, I drew on wisdom from a very distant source\u2014the wisdom of my boyhood\u2014for a truly wise statesman never scorns any wisdom, no matter how humble its origin. In my boyhood I always saved my pennies and donated buttons to the foreign missionary cause. The buttons satisfied the ignorant native as well as the coin, the coin served me better than the buttons; everyone was pleased and nobody harmed.\\n\\nMarinel took the patients in order. He examined each applicant; if the person failed, he was turned away; if he passed, he went on to the king. A priest spoke the words, \u201cThey shall lay their hands on the sick, and they shall recover.\u201d Then the king stroked the ulcers as the reading went on; at last the patient graduated, received his nickel\u2014the king hung it around his neck himself\u2014and was dismissed. Would you expect that to heal? It certainly did. Any ritual will heal if the patient\u2019s faith in it is strong. Near Astolat there stood a chapel where the Virgin had once appeared, so a girl who herded geese there said, and they built the chapel on that spot and hung inside a picture showing the event\u2014a picture you would think dangerous for a sick person to see. Yet thousands of the lame and sick came there, prayed before it every year, and left healthy and whole; even the well could gaze on it and live.\"}"
