          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"999fb566924873a229c09161\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, bother my hat! I want Virginia!\u201d cried the little Duke, laughing, and they galloped on to the railway station. There Mr. Otis asked the station-master if anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but he could learn nothing. The station-master sent messages up and down the line and promised to keep a close watch for her, and after Mr. Otis bought a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper closing his shop, he rode off to Bexley, a village about four miles away that was said to be a regular haunt of the gypsies because of a large common nearby. They woke the village policeman but got no news from him, and after riding all over the common, they turned back home and reached the Chase around eleven o\u2019clock, exhausted and almost heartbroken. They found Washington and the twins waiting for them at the gate-house with lanterns, as the avenue was very dark. Not a trace of Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been caught on Brockley Meadows, but she was not with them, and they explained their sudden departure by saying they had mistaken the date of Chorton Fair and had left in a rush so they wouldn\u2019t be late. They were even distressed to hear of Virginia\u2019s disappearance, as they were grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of them had stayed behind to help search. The carp pond had been dragged, and the entire Chase had been carefully checked, but nothing was discovered. It was clear that, for that night at least, Virginia was lost to them, and Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house in deep despair, the groom following with the two horses and the pony. In the hall they found a cluster of frightened servants, and in the library poor Mrs. Otis lay on a sofa, almost maddened by fear and anxiety while the old housekeeper bathed her forehead with eau-de-cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she eat something and ordered supper for everyone. It was a sad meal, as hardly anyone spoke, and even the twins were awed and quiet, being devoted to their sister. When they were done, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s pleas, sent them all to bed, saying nothing more could be done that night and that he would telegraph Scotland Yard in the morning to send detectives at once.\"}"
