{"request_id":"bdc4999492a3498884822831","rewritten_text":"During his brief visit, Emma had scarcely encountered him; yet sufficiently to feel the initial meeting was behind them, and to note the blend of pique and pretension now colouring his manner. She was, indeed, growing amazed she had ever found him agreeable; his presence was so tightly linked to unpleasant memories that, unless as a moral exercise—a punishment, a lesson, a humbling benefit to herself—she would gladly have been promised she need never meet him again. She wished him no ill; still, he brought her discomfort, and knowing him prosperous twenty miles away would be most gratifying.

Yet the pain of his lingering in Highbury must surely be eased by his marriage. Many pointless anxieties would disappear, many embarrassments be softened. A Mrs. Elton would excuse any alteration in their dealings; former closeness might fade without notice. It would be little less than restarting their civil acquaintance.

Of the lady herself, Emma scarcely formed an opinion. She was suitable for Mr. Elton, certainly; sufficiently accomplished for Highbury—handsome enough—to appear plain, perhaps, beside Harriet. Regarding connection, Emma felt entirely at ease; convinced that, despite his puffed-up pretensions and contempt of Harriet, he had achieved nothing. On that point, truth seemed within reach. What she was might be doubtful; but who she was could be investigated; and apart from the ten thousand pounds, she did not seem in the least Harriet’s superior. She brought no name, no lineage, no alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, one must call him; yet, since the whole proceeds of his trading life seemed so modest, it was reasonable to suspect the rank of his trade had been modest likewise. Part of each winter she habitually spent in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very core of Bristol; for though her parents had been dead some years, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more precisely respectable being ventured than that he was in the law line; and with him the daughter resided. Emma imagined him the drudge of some attorney, and too dull to advance. And all the magnificence of the connection appeared to rest on the elder sister, who was very well married to a gentleman in a great way near Bristol, who kept two carriages! That concluded the tale; that was the proud boast of Miss Hawkins."}

