          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d8214cdca750f6e9ad86d68d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He rose and left the room with my nurse, and in another moment my father stepped inside. Nothing could have pleased me more just then than his arrival. I reached out to him and cried, \u201cAre you safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d My father soothed me with assurances of their safety and tried, by lingering on these subjects so dear to me, to lift my spirits. Yet he soon realized that a prison is no place for cheerfulness. \u201cWhat sort of place is this where you\u2019re living, my son?\u201d he said, looking mournfully at the barred windows and the squalid room. \u201cYou set out to find happiness, but misfortune keeps hounding you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d The name of my unlucky, murdered friend was more than I could bear in my weakened state; I burst into tears. \u201cYes, Father,\u201d I answered, \u201csome terrible fate hangs over me, and I must live to see it through, or I would have died on Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d We weren\u2019t allowed to speak for long, since my fragile health meant every precaution was needed to keep me calm. Mr. Kirwin came in and insisted that my strength mustn\u2019t be drained by too much emotion. Yet my father\u2019s presence was like that of a guardian angel, and gradually my health returned. As my illness faded, I was consumed by a bleak, suffocating melancholy that nothing could disperse. Clerval\u2019s image hovered before me, pale and murdered. More than once the turmoil these thoughts stirred made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Why, in heaven\u2019s name, did they keep such a wretched, hated life alive? Surely it was so I could fulfill my fate, which now seemed near its end. Soon, very soon, death would still these throbbings and free me from the crushing weight of suffering that drove me into the dust; and while carrying out justice\u2019s sentence, I too would find rest. Then death still seemed distant, though I longed for it constantly, and I often sat motionless and speechless for hours, wishing for some great upheaval that would bury both me and my destroyer in its ruins. The assize season was approaching. I had already spent three months in prison, and though still weak and in constant danger of relapsing, I was forced to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court convened. Mr. Kirwin took it upon himself to gather witnesses and organize my defense.\"}"
