          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9df9b85add5fa7ac6a249ca8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse\u2014handsome, clever, and rich, with a comfortable home and cheerful nature\u2014seemed to possess some of life\u2019s best gifts, and had spent nearly twenty-one years with very little to trouble or upset her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters of a loving, indulgent father, and because her sister married early, Emma had been mistress of the house from a young age. Her mother had died so long ago that Emma could only faintly remember her affection; in her place stood an excellent governess who had given her nearly a mother\u2019s devotion.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had lived with Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family for sixteen years, serving more as a friend than a governess. She loved both daughters, though she had a special fondness for Emma. Between them, the bond was more like that of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor stopped holding the title of governess, her gentle nature had kept her from imposing any restraint; and now that any hint of authority was long gone, they lived together as dear friends, mutually attached, with Emma doing just what she pleased. She respected Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment, yet still followed her own will.\\n\\nThe real drawbacks of Emma\u2019s situation were the ability to have rather too much her own way and a tendency to think too highly of herself. These were the only flaws threatening to tarnish her many pleasures. The danger went so unnoticed that she never counted them as misfortunes.\\n\\nSorrow did come\u2014gentle sorrow\u2014but not through any unpleasant realization. Miss Taylor married. That loss brought Emma her first real grief. On the day of her beloved friend\u2019s wedding, Emma sat longer than ever before in somber thought. Once the ceremony ended and the newlyweds departed, her father and she were left to dine alone, with no third friend to brighten the long evening. After dinner, her father settled into his usual nap, and Emma had only her thoughts and the sense of what she had lost.\\n\\nEverything about the event promised happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of impeccable character, comfortable fortune, suitable age, and agreeable manners. Emma found some comfort in recalling how selflessly and generously she had encouraged the match. Still, it felt like a dark day for her. She knew she would feel Miss Taylor\u2019s absence every hour of every day. She remembered the kindness of sixteen years\u2014the lessons and games since Emma was five, the unflagging dedication to keeping her attached and entertained when well, and the tender care through every childhood illness.\"}"
