          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d58056f15867ef016cc18c5b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOn that point, then, I will remain entirely silent; and you may be sure that once we are married, I will never utter an ungenerous reproach.\u201d\\n\\nShe had to interrupt him immediately.\\n\\n\u201cYou are too quick, sir,\u201d she exclaimed. \u201cYou forget that I have not answered. Let me do so without further delay. Thank you for the compliment you pay me. I understand the honor of your proposal, but I cannot do anything except decline it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am already aware,\u201d Mr. Collins replied with a formal wave of his hand, \u201cthat it is customary for young women to reject the offers of the man they secretly mean to accept when he first asks for their favor, and that sometimes they refuse a second or even a third time. Therefore, I am not at all discouraged by what you have just said, and I hope to lead you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHonestly, sir,\u201d Elizabeth cried, \u201cyour hope is extraordinary after what I have stated. I assure you I am not one of those young women\u2014if such women really exist\u2014who are bold enough to gamble their happiness on being asked again. I am completely serious in my refusal. You could not make me happy, and I am sure I am the last woman in the world who could make you so. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am convinced she would find me entirely unfit for the role.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins very gravely, \u201cbut I cannot imagine her Ladyship would disapprove of you. You may be certain that when I have the honor of seeing her again, I will speak most highly of your modesty, thrift, and other admirable qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally, Mr. Collins, any praise of me will be unnecessary. Allow me to judge for myself, and pay me the compliment of believing what I say. I wish you very much happiness and prosperity, and by refusing your hand, I do everything I can to keep you from being otherwise. In making the offer, you have satisfied your conscience regarding my family, and may take possession of the Longbourn estate when it passes to you without any self-reproach. Therefore, this matter may be considered completely settled.\u201d Rising as she spoke, she would have left the room if Mr. Collins had not addressed her again\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honor of speaking to you next on the subject, I shall hope for a more favorable reply than the one you have just given me; though I am far from accusing you of cruelty, because I know it is the established custom among women to turn down a man at his first application, and perhaps you have already said as much to encourage my suit as would be consistent with the true delicacy of your sex.\u201d\"}"
