          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"986a0fc165f0482f8a8dbc56\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Country folk, butchers, drovers, hawkers, urchins, thieves, loungers, and vagabonds of every shabby order were jumbled in one heap; the drovers whistled, dogs barked, oxen bellowed and flung about, sheep bleated, pigs grunted and squealed, hawkers cried their wares, and shouts, oaths, and squabbles rose on every side; bells clanged and voices roared from every public-house; the jostling, shoving, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling; the hideous, jarring din reverberating from every corner of the market; and the grimy, unshaven, ragged wretches constantly running here and there, bursting in and out of the press, made it a deafening, bewildering scene that utterly confounded the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, towing Oliver after him, forced a passage through the densest portion of the crowd and paid scant heed to the endless sights and sounds that so amazed the child. He nodded once or twice to a passing acquaintance, and, fending off as many invitations for a morning dram, pushed straight onward until they escaped the tumult, and, threading Hosier Lane, came out into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d cried Sikes, glancing up at St. Andrew\u2019s clock, \u201cclose upon seven! step lively. Come, don\u2019t be lagging already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nWith these words he gave his small companion\u2019s wrist a jerk; Oliver, breaking into a half-run somewhere between a brisk walk and a trot, contrived to keep pace with the burglar\u2019s rapid stride as best he might.\\n\\nThey kept on at this gait until Hyde Park Corner lay behind them and they were bound for Kensington; then Sikes slackened until an empty cart, some way in the rear, drew level. Spying \u201cHounslow\u201d painted upon it, he asked the driver, with such politeness as he could muster, to carry them as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cClimb up,\u201d said the fellow. \u201cThat your lad?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy; he\u2019s mine,\u201d answered Sikes, fixing Oliver with a hard stare and slipping his hand, as if unconsciously, into the pocket where the pistol lay.\\n\\n\u201cYour father steps rather smartly for you, eh, youngster?\u201d the driver remarked, noting Oliver\u2019s breathless state.\\n\\n\u201cNot a whit,\u201d said Sikes, cutting in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, grip my hand, Ned. Up you go!\u201d\\n\\nSo saying, he boosted Oliver into the cart; and the driver, pointing to a pile of sacks, bade him lie there and rest himself.\\n\\nAs milestone after milestone slid by, Oliver\u2019s wonder grew at the destination his companion had in mind. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, Brentford were all left behind; yet they rolled on as steadily as though the journey had barely begun.\"}"
