          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92611e7f5ee84211ed76114b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He bowed, withdrew, and noticed no one in the street at that moment. It was an out-of-the-way lane, very desolate.\\n\\n\u201c_William Bird_, a tailor, testifies that he was among those who entered the house. Says he is English and has lived in Paris two years. Was among the first up the staircase. Heard the arguing voices. The deep voice was French. Could catch several words, though he cannot now recall them all distinctly. Clearly heard \u2018_sacr\u00e9_\u2019 and \u2018_mon Dieu_.\u2019 At the same instant there were sounds as of multiple people struggling\u2014a scraping and scuffling. The shrill voice rang out very loudly\u2014even louder than the gruff one. Is convinced it was not English. Seemed more like a German\u2019s voice. Might have been that of a woman. Does not speak German.\\n\\n\u201cFour of the previously named witnesses, recalled, stated that the chamber door where Mademoiselle L.\u2019s body lay was locked from the inside when they reached it. Everything was utterly silent\u2014no groan or other sound. When they forced the door, the room appeared deserted. Front and back room windows were closed and securely fastened within. A connecting door between the rooms stood shut but unlocked. The door leading from the front room into the hall was locked, the key inside. At the head of the passage, on the fourth floor, a small room at the front of the house stood with its door ajar. It was jammed with old mattresses, boxes, and similar items, which were carefully removed and inspected. Not a single inch of the premises went unsearched. Chimneys were swept up and down. The building had four stories with garrets (_mansardes._) A trap in the roof was nailed solidly and looked untouched for years. Witnesses varied in saying how much time passed between hearing the dispute and the breaking of the chamber door\u2014some said three minutes, others up to five. The door yielded only with difficulty.\\n\\n\u201c_Alfonzo Garcio_, undertaker, affirms he lives on the Rue Morgue. Born in Spain. Was among those entering but did not venture upstairs. Being nervous, he feared the effects of any excitement. Heard the clashing voices. The deep voice was French. Could not distinguish the words. The shrill voice, he insists, was English.\u201d\"}"
