          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92dbe83d0752048131f9f912\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d I said as I handed the slip back to him, \u201cI am just as confused as before. Even if the jewels of Golconda waited for me as a reward, I\u2019m sure I couldn\u2019t earn them by solving this riddle.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet,\u201d Legrand replied, \u201cthe answer isn\u2019t nearly as hard as a quick look at the characters might make you think. These characters, as anyone could guess, form a cipher\u2014that is, they carry a meaning. But from what we know of Kidd, I couldn\u2019t imagine him capable of creating any advanced cryptographs. I decided at once that this was a simple type\u2014one that, to the rough mind of a sailor, would seem impossible without the key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you really solved it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEasily. I\u2019ve solved others ten thousand times more complex. Circumstances and a certain turn of mind have made me interested in such puzzles, and one may well doubt whether human ingenuity can make a cipher that human ingenuity, properly applied, can\u2019t solve. Once I had connected, readable characters, I hardly gave a thought to the difficulty of finding their meaning.\\n\\n\u201cIn this case\u2014indeed, in every case of secret writing\u2014the first question concerns the language of the cipher, because the method of solving it, especially when dealing with simple ciphers, depends on and shifts according to the nature of the language. Usually, there is no choice but to experiment, guided by probabilities, with every language known to the solver until the right one is found. But with the cipher before us, the signature removed all difficulty. The pun on the word \u2018Kidd\u2019 only works in English. Without that clue I would have begun with Spanish and French, the languages in which a pirate of the Spanish Main would most likely write a secret. As it was, I assumed the cryptograph was in English.\\n\\n\u201cYou notice there are no spaces between words. If there had been, the task would have been much easier. In that case, I would have started by comparing and analyzing the short words, and if a single-letter word had appeared, as would be most likely\u2014\u2018a\u2019 or \u2018I,\u2019 for example\u2014I would have considered the solution all but certain. But, since there were no spaces, my first step was to determine which letters appeared most often and which appeared least.\u201d\"}"
