          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d3b98d6b3e84d33144ffa6d6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he kept one secret from these dear friends\u2014a secret he had nurtured since childhood, and although he cherished his fellow students, he would not entrust it to the delicacy or sympathy of any of them. He was in love. He feared the intensity of his longing might become their amusement; he could not endure having them profane it by treating as trivial and fleeting what he felt to be the core of his life.\\n\\nNear his family estate lived a gentleman of modest means with three lovely daughters. The eldest was by far the most beautiful, but her beauty merely crowned her other virtues\u2014her mind was clear and strong, her nature angelically gentle. She and my father had been playmates from infancy: Diana, even as a child, had been a favorite with his mother. That partiality deepened as the bright, lively girl grew, and during his school and college holidays they were inseparable. Novels and all the devices by which civilized youth learn of passions before they truly feel them had left a deep impression on him, who was so unusually sensitive to every emotion. At eleven, Diana was his favorite playmate, but he already spoke the language of love. Though she was nearly two years older, her sheltered upbringing left her more childish, at least in matters of feeling; she accepted his ardent declarations with innocence and returned them without understanding what they meant. She had read no novels and spent her time only with her younger sisters\u2014how could she know the difference between love and friendship? When the unfolding of her understanding finally revealed the real nature of their bond, her heart was already pledged to her friend, and her only fear was that other attractions and fickleness might make him break his childhood vows.\\n\\nBut each day their feelings grew more intense and tender. This passion had grown as he grew; it had woven itself into every power and every 