          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1a015599c272753243584bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was set for an explanation, so our honour would not suffer from delay. Drummle showed up with a polite note in Estella\u2019s hand saying she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. That left me no choice but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and to refuse, as untenable, any idea that I could be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove contradicted everyone indiscriminately, and finally it was declared that good feeling had advanced at a remarkable pace.\\n\\nI tell it lightly, but it was not light to me. I cannot convey how much it hurt to think that Estella would show any favour to a contemptible, clumsy, sulky boor so far below the usual standard. To this day I believe it sprang from the pure generosity and selflessness of my love for her that I could not bear the thought of her stooping to that hound. I would have been miserable no matter whom she favoured, but a worthier recipient would have caused me a different kind and degree of pain.\\n\\nIt was easy to learn, and I soon did learn, that Drummle had begun to trail her, and that she let him. Before long he was always chasing after her, and he and I crossed paths every day. He held on in a dull, stubborn way, and Estella held him on\u2014now encouraging him, now discouraging him, now almost flattering him, now openly despising him, now knowing him well, now barely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers called him, was used to lying in wait and had his tribe\u2019s patience. He also had a blockheaded confidence in his money and family importance, which sometimes served him well, almost acting like focus and determination. So the Spider, dogging Estella, outwatched many brighter insects, and would often uncoil and drop at just the right moment.\\n\\nAt a certain Assembly Ball at Richmond\u2014there used to be Assembly Balls almost everywhere then\u2014Estella outshone every other beauty. This blundering Drummle hung about her with so much tolerance from her that I resolved to speak to her about him. I took the next opportunity, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to go.\"}"
