          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb111fb3f1a762653e322652\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He bowed and left. Saw no one in the street at that time. It is a side street\u2014very deserted.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam Bird, tailor, states that he was one of the group who entered the house. He is English. Has lived in Paris for two years. Was among the first to go up the stairs. Heard the arguing voices. The deep voice belonged to a Frenchman. Could make out several words but cannot now recall them all. Heard clearly \u2018sacr\u00e9\u2019 and \u2018mon Dieu.\u2019 At the same moment he heard sounds as if several people were struggling\u2014a scraping and scuffling. The high voice was extremely loud\u2014louder than the deep one. He is sure it was not an English voice. It seemed German. It might have been a woman\u2019s voice. He does not speak German.\\n\\n\u201cFour of the witnesses named above, when called back, stated that the door of the room where Mademoiselle L.\u2019s body was found was locked from the inside when they reached it. Everything inside was completely quiet\u2014no groans or any noises. When they forced the door, they found no one. The windows in both the front and back rooms were closed and firmly fastened from within. A door between the two rooms was closed but not locked. The door from the front room into the passage was locked, with the key on the inside. A small room at the front of the house on the fourth floor, at the head of the passage, was open, the door slightly ajar. This room was packed with old beds, boxes, and similar things. These were carefully moved and checked. Every inch of the house was thoroughly searched. Chimney sweeps were sent up and down the flues. The house had four stories, with garrets (mansardes). A trap door on the roof was nailed down very securely\u2014it looked as if it had not been opened for years. The time between hearing the arguing voices and breaking open the room door was described differently by the witnesses. Some said it was as short as three minutes; others said up to five. The door was difficult to open.\\n\\n\u201cAlfonzo Garcio, undertaker, states he lives on the Rue Morgue. He is Spanish. Was one of the party who entered the house. Did not go upstairs. Is nervous and feared the effects of the excitement. Heard the voices arguing. The deep voice was French. Could not tell what was said. The high voice was English\u2014he is sure of it.\u201d\"}"
