          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3da5d99a05334b0c6aaa13d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI followed this line of thought after the fact. The killers escaped through one of these windows. If that were true, they could not have refastened the sashes from inside, since they were found fastened\u2014a point so obvious that it kept the police from looking further at this option. Yet the sashes were secured. They had to be able to fasten themselves. There was no escaping that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed window, pulled out the nail with some effort, and tried to raise the sash. It resisted me completely, just as I expected. There had to be a hidden spring, and finding proof of my idea confirmed that my reasoning was sound, even if the situation with the nails still seemed strange. After a careful search I found the hidden spring. I pressed it and, satisfied, did not lift the sash.\\n\\n\u201cI put the nail back and studied it closely. Someone leaving through this window could have shut it again, and the spring would have caught, but the nail could not have been replaced. That conclusion was clear and narrowed my investigation further. The killers had to have escaped through the other window. Assuming the springs on both sashes were the same, as seemed likely, there had to be some difference in the nails or at least in how they were fixed. Standing on the bed\u2019s sacking, I looked over the headboard at the second window. Running my hand down behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring, which was just like the first one. Then I examined the nail. It was as sturdy as the other and seemed set in the same way, driven in almost to the head.\\n\\n\u201cYou might say I was confused; but if you think so, you misunderstand my reasoning. In hunting terms, I was never off the scent. Not once was there a break in the chain of logic. I had traced the secret to its final point\u2014and that point was the nail. It looked exactly like the nail in the other window, yet that similarity meant nothing compared with the fact that the trail ended right here. \u2018Something must be wrong with the nail,\u2019 I said. I touched it, and the head, with about a quarter inch of the shank, broke off in my fingers.\u201d\"}"
