          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9c91066c84d1e840067c880b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted a fiery horse, the same one that had carried him to victory in Greece; he exhausted himself with relentless exercise, drowning the torment of his troubled mind in physical strain.\\n\\nHe gradually came to his senses; at last, as if recovering from poison, he lifted his head above the fog of fever and passion into the clear air of calm reflection. He considered what ought to be done. He was struck by the amount of time that had slipped by while madness, rather than reason, guided his actions. A month had passed, and during that time he had not seen Evadne. Her influence, which had little connection with the lasting emotions of his heart, had greatly faded. He was no longer her slave\u2014no longer her lover: he would never see her again, and by fully returning to himself, he would earn Perdita\u2019s trust.\\n\\nYet, even as he resolved this, his imagination summoned the wretched home of the Greek girl, which she had refused to trade, out of principle, for a more luxurious one. He remembered her brilliance when he first met her; he remembered her life in Constantinople, surrounded by every mark of eastern magnificence; he thought of her current poverty, her daily toil, her lonely state, her cheek wasted by hunger. Compassion filled his chest; he would see her once more; he would devise a plan to restore her to society and the privileges of her rank; then their separation would naturally follow.\\n\\nAgain he reflected on how, throughout this month, he had avoided Perdita, fleeing from her as he fled from his own conscience. But now he was awake; all this would be set right, and future devotion would erase the memory of this sole stain on the serenity of their life. He grew cheerful as he thought of this and firmly mapped out the course he would follow. He remembered that he had promised Perdita to attend that very evening (the nineteenth of October, the anniversary of his election as Protector) a festival held in his honor. Let this festival be a good omen of happiness in the coming years. First, he would visit Evadne\u2014not linger, but he owed her some explanation, some compensation for his long, unexplained absence\u2014and then he would return to Perdita, to the neglected world, to duty, social splendor, and the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the scene described earlier, Perdita had anticipated a complete change in Raymond\u2019s behavior and habits. She expected open communication and a return to the affectionate routines that had been the joy of her life.\"}"
