          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c8971537a8b93aff023df88d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent to Adrian, chosen as arbiter. They had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and ravaged countryside, feeding their mutual hatred, and now they argued their causes with undimmed party spirit.\\n\\nBy questioning the deputies separately and after much inquiry, we learned the real situation in Paris. Since parliament appointed him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all surviving English people had submitted to Adrian. He was our leader to guide us from home to unknown lands, our lawgiver, our guardian. When we first arranged our emigration plan, we never expected to stay apart, and command of the entire group, rising by degrees, ended with the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events altered our plans and kept most of us separated from the supreme leader for nearly two months. They had crossed in two distinct groups, and once in Paris quarrels sprang up between them.\\n\\nThey had found Paris a desert. At first, when the plague appeared, returning travellers, merchants, and letters kept us informed of the disease\u2019s progress on the continent. As deaths increased, this contact dwindled and stopped. Even in England, communication from one part of the island to another became slow and rare. No ship crossed the channel between Calais and Dover; or if a sorrowful voyager left the French shore to learn the fate of his family among us, often the greedy sea swallowed his little boat, or after a day or two he caught the plague and died before he could tell the tale of France\u2019s desolation. We therefore remained largely ignorant of conditions on the continent and still clung to a faint hope of finding many companions in its wide stretch. But the same causes that devastated England had worked even more widely in the sister land. France was empty; along the long road from Calais to Paris not a single human being appeared. In Paris there were a few, perhaps a hundred, resigned to their fate, wandering the streets and gathering to talk of former days, still showing that brightness and even cheerfulness that seldom deserts people of that nation.\\n\\nThe English took Paris without resistance. Its tall houses and narrow streets were lifeless. A few pale figures lingered at the usual meeting-place in the Tuileries; they wondered why the islanders approached their doomed city\u2014for in extreme misery people always imagine their own share of suffering is the worst, just as in intense pain we would trade the torment we endure for any other that might strike another part of the body.\"}"
