          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4225379af32359bf70b51162\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, aghast at Sikes\u2019s deed, fell into a chain of thoughts as to whether an upright life were not, after all, the wiser course. Convinced at length that it surely was, he turned away from the former scenes, bent on reforming himself in some fresh field of endeavour. He toiled severely and endured no small misery for a season; yet, being blessed with a contented temper and steadfast aim, he prospered at last; and, from a farmer\u2019s drudge and a carrier\u2019s boy, he is now the blithest young grazier in Northamptonshire.\\n\\nAnd now the hand that writes these lines trembles as it draws near the end of its labour, and would gladly spin out, for yet a brief while, the story of these adventures.\\n\\nI could wish to tarry still with a few of those among whom I have so long dwelt, and partake of their happiness by trying to portray it. I would show Rose Maylie in all the bloom and grace of early maidenhood, casting on her sheltered path a soft and gentle radiance that lighted every companion beside her and warmed their hearts. I would paint her the spirit and joy of the fireside ring and the merry summer party; I would follow her through the hot fields at noon, and listen to the low music of her sweet voice in the moonlit stroll; I would watch her abroad in all her goodness and charity, and at home in the smiling, tireless performance of household offices; I would portray her and her departed sister\u2019s child rejoicing in their mutual love, spending long hours together conjuring images of the friends they had so sadly lost; I would call again before me those joyous little faces that gathered around her knee and drink in their lively chatter; I would remember the clear peal of laughter, and summon the sympathetic tear that glimmered in the gentle blue eye. These, and a thousand expressions, smiles, and inflections of thought and tongue\u2014I would willingly summon them all again.\\n\\nHow Mr. Brownlow proceeded, day by day, to enrich the mind of his adopted boy with stores of learning, and how he became more devoted to him as the lad\u2019s nature unfolded, revealing the thriving germs of all he hoped to see\u2014how he traced in him fresh traits of his early friend, awakening in his own heart old recollections, mournful yet sweet and comforting\u2014how the two orphans, schooled by hardship, remembered its lessons in pity for others, in mutual affection, and in fervent gratitude to Him who had shielded and sustained them\u2014these are matters that need no rehearsal.\"}"
