          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b43e25ad8f2178c044ebffba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks slipped by, and my absorption in you only deepened. Then something new arose. I had already portrayed you as Paris in delicate armour, and as Adonis in huntsman\u2019s cloak with a gleaming boar-spear. Wreathed with heavy lotus blossoms you had sat at the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, staring across the murky green Nile. You had bent over a still pool in some Greek grove and beheld in the water\u2019s quiet silver the wonder of your own reflection. All of it had been what art ought to be\u2014uninvolved, ideal, distant. One day\u2014sometimes I call it fatal\u2014I resolved to paint an extraordinary portrait of you as you truly are, not dressed in bygone finery, but in your own clothes and era. Whether it was the realistic approach, or simply the marvel of your own nature presented to me with no mist or veil, I cannot say. Yet as I worked, each speck and veil of colour seemed to betray my secret. I feared others would discover my worship. I felt, Dorian, that I had confessed too much, that I had poured too much of myself into it. So I resolved the picture must never be exhibited. You were slightly irritated, but you had no idea what it meant for me. Harry, when I told him, laughed. I did not care. When the portrait was done and I sat alone before it, I knew I was justified\u2026. After a few days it left my studio, and once free of the oppressive fascination of its presence, it struck me that I had been foolish to think I saw anything in it beyond your extraordinary beauty and my own skill. Even now I cannot shake the sense that believing the passion felt in creation truly appears in the work is a mistake. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and colour speak only of form and colour\u2014that is the sum. I often think art hides the artist far more than it reveals him. So when I received that offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the centrepiece of my exhibition. It never occurred to me you would refuse. I understand now that you were right. The picture must remain unseen. Do not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have confessed.\"}"
