          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d9d60b5fc89934edb6e14b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s last reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was even more crowded than usual. Six Cabinet Ministers had come straight from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e with their stars and ribbons, every pretty woman wore her smartest gown, and at the end of the picture gallery stood Princess Sophia of Carlsruhe, a heavy Tartar-looking woman with tiny black eyes and dazzling emeralds, speaking bad French at the top of her voice and laughing wildly at everything anyone said. It was truly a mixed crowd. Splendid peeresses chatted pleasantly with fierce Radicals, fashionable preachers rubbed shoulders with famous skeptics, a whole flock of bishops kept following a stout prima donna from room to room, several Royal Academicians stood on the staircase pretending to be ordinary artists, and they said the supper room was at one point packed with geniuses. It was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s finest evenings, and the Princess stayed until almost half-past eleven.\\n\\nAs soon as the Princess left, Lady Windermere returned to the picture gallery, where a famous political economist was solemnly lecturing an indignant Hungarian virtuoso on the scientific theory of music. She began talking to the Duchess of Paisley. Lady Windermere looked astonishingly beautiful, with her proud ivory neck, big blue forget-me-not eyes, and heavy coils of golden hair. They were truly golden\u2014not the pale straw color that now claims the name, but the gold woven into sunbeams or hidden in mysterious amber. They framed her face like a saint\u2019s halo, though with a good measure of a sinner\u2019s charm. She was a fascinating study of character. Early on she had learned the crucial fact that nothing resembles innocence as much as indiscretion, and through a string of reckless escapades, half of them quite harmless, she had earned all the privileges of a celebrity. She had changed husbands more than once\u2014Debrett credits her with three marriages\u2014but since she had never changed her lover, society had long ago stopped gossiping about her. She was now forty, childless, and possessed of that excessive love of pleasure that keeps one young.\\n\\nSuddenly she looked eagerly around the room and said, in her clear contralto voice, \u201cWhere is my cheiromantist?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour what, Gladys?\u201d exclaimed the Duchess, giving a sudden little jump.\\n\\n\u201cMy cheiromantist, Duchess; I can\u2019t live without him at the moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys! you are always so original,\u201d murmured the Duchess, trying to remember what a cheiromantist actually was and hoping it was not the same as a chiropodist.\\n\\n\u201cHe comes to look at my hand twice a week, every week,\u201d continued Lady Windermere, \u201cand he says the most interesting things about it.\u201d\"}"
