          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f4f60678a4316d17fc9c73d8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At least he believed so. Yet who could be sure? \u2026 No. Nothing further had happened. Out of vanity he had spared her. Out of hypocrisy he had worn the guise of virtue. From mere curiosity he had experimented with self-denial. He saw that now. But this murder\u2014was it destined to haunt him forever? Would his past forever weigh upon him? Was he truly going to confess? Never. Only one piece of evidence still stood against him: the portrait itself. He would destroy it. Why had he kept it so long? Once, seeing it change and wither had delighted him. Lately, no such delight remained. It had robbed him of sleep. Whenever he was away, dread seized him lest other eyes should behold it. It had cast sadness over his pleasures. Even its recollection had spoiled many a joyful hour. It had been like a conscience. Yes, it had been conscience. He would destroy it. He looked around and spotted the knife that had killed Basil Hallward. He had polished it many times until no stain remained. It shone, gleaming. As it had slain the painter, so it would slay the painter\u2019s creation and all it symbolized. It would kill the past, freeing him once it was dead. It would end this monstrous existence of the soul, and without its ghastly rebukes, he would know peace. He grasped the knife and plunged it into the painting. A scream rang out, followed by a crash. The scream was so hideous in its anguish that the terrified servants awoke and crept from their rooms. Two gentlemen crossing the square below halted and gazed up at the grand house. They continued until they encountered a policeman and led him back. He rang repeatedly, but no one answered. Apart from a light in one of the upper windows, the house lay in darkness. After a while, he withdrew to a nearby portico and kept watch. \u201cWhose house is that, Constable?\u201d the elder gentleman inquired. \u201cMr. Dorian Gray\u2019s, sir,\u201d replied the policeman. They exchanged glances, sneering as they walked off. One was Sir Henry Ashton\u2019s uncle. Meanwhile, in the servants\u2019 quarters, the half-dressed domestics whispered among themselves. Old Mrs. Leaf wept and wrung her hands. Francis was white as death. After about fifteen minutes, he roused the coachman and one of the footmen, and together they crept upstairs.\"}"
