{"request_id":"07069428c0e599b4b149fcc0","rewritten_text":"I hail from a sea-girdled corner, a land hooded in cloud, which, when the globe’s surface with its borderless oceans and unread continents rises before my mind, seems but a trivial speck in the vastness; yet, when weighed upon the scale of intellect, it far overbalanced realms of broader reach and denser populace. Thus it stands proven that the human mind alone fashioned whatever was excellent or noble for mankind, and Nature herself served merely as its first minister. England, set far to the north amid the turbid sea, now visits my dreams as a mighty, well-manned vessel, conquering the winds and striding proudly over the swells. In my childhood she comprised the entire universe. When I stood upon my native heights and watched plain and mountain stretch to the farthest bounds of sight, flecked by the homes of my compatriots and rendered fertile by their labour, the very centre of the earth fixed itself there for me, and the remaining portions of the sphere were but a fable whose neglect would have taxed neither my fancy nor my reason.

My fortunes have been, from the outset, an illustration of the sway that mutability may exercise over the shifting tenor of a man’s existence. In my own case this was almost hereditary. My father belonged to that race of men on whom nature had lavishly bestowed the coveted endowments of wit and imagination, and then abandoned the bark of his life to be driven by those winds without supplying reason as a rudder or judgment as a helmsman. His origin was obscure, yet circumstance early pushed him into public view, and his modest paternal estate was quickly squandered in the dazzling scene of fashion and luxury where he played a part. Through the brief years of heedless youth, he was adored by the highbred triflers of the time, not least by the young monarch, who would flee the intrigues of faction and the stern burdens of sovereign duty to find unfailing diversion and exhilaration in his company. My father’s impulses, never governed by himself, constantly plunged him into scrapes from which only his ingenuity could win escape; and the continually mounting heap of debts of honour and of trade, which would have crushed another man, he bore with a light spirit and irrepressible mirth; while his presence was so indispensable at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy that his lapses were deemed venial, and he was greeted with intoxicating adulation.

Yet this species of popularity, like every other, soon evaporates; and the perils of all kinds with which he wrestled increased in dreadful proportion to his scanty means of deliverance."}

