          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1af7ef482edca599622f635b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I call for them? Or for Miss Bates? She is closer still\u2014and I cannot be sure Miss Bates would not read the others\u2019 feelings as well as anyone. I think we do require a larger council. Suppose I go and ask Miss Bates to come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you like,\u201d replied Mrs. Weston with some hesitation, \u201cif you believe she can be of any service.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will glean nothing worth having from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, but she will give you no information. She will scarcely attend to your questions. I do not see any benefit in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining, so very entertaining! I am extremely fond of listening to Miss Bates. And I need not bring the entire family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Weston now joined them, and, when he heard what was intended, he sanctioned it decidedly.\\n\\n\u201cAy, do, Frank.\u2014Go fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle everything immediately. She will relish the plan, I am sure; and I know no fitter person to teach us how to clear away obstacles. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming rather too fastidious. She is a perpetual lesson in being cheerful. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! The old lady?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great simpleton, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not instantly remember. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And off he ran.\\n\\nLong before he returned, escorting the short, neat, quick-stepping aunt and her graceful niece, Mrs. Weston, like a good-tempered woman and a devoted wife, had re-examined the passage, and discovered the inconveniences much less formidable than she had imagined\u2014indeed barely worth mentioning; and so the difficulty of deciding ended. Everything else, at least in theory, was entirely smooth. The minor arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, arranged themselves; or were left as mere trifles to be settled whenever Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes chose.\u2014Every invited person was certainly to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to request remaining a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be denied. And it was to be a delightful ball.\\n\\nMost heartily, when Miss Bates arrived, did she agree that it must be so. As an adviser she was unnecessary; but as one who approved (a much safer role) she was thoroughly welcome. Her praise, at once broad and detailed, eager and unceasing, could not fail to gratify; and for another half-hour they all paced back and forth between the various rooms, some proposing, some attending, and all rejoicing in the anticipation of what was to come.\"}"
