          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f1986dc10a7b17672441ce10\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If you want one for home use, I can give you an excellent piece, and I promise you will be pleased with it. May I ask who it is for? If it is for the police, or anyone connected to Scotland Yard, I am sorry but I cannot help you. The English detectives are really our best allies, and by counting on their stupidity, we are free to do whatever we wish. I would not part with one of them.\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, \u201cit has nothing to do with the police. The clock is meant for the Dean of Chichester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear me! I had no idea you felt so strongly about religion, Lord Arthur. Few young men do nowadays.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you give me too much credit, Herr Winckelkopf,\u201d said Lord Arthur, blushing. \u201cIn truth, I know nothing about theology.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo it is a completely private affair?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEntirely private.\u201d\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf shrugged and left the room, returning in a few minutes with a round piece of dynamite about the size of a penny, and a charming little French clock, topped by an ormolu figure of Liberty trampling the hydra of Despotism.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s face lit up at the sight. \u201cThat is exactly what I want,\u201d he exclaimed. \u201cNow tell me how it goes off.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! That is my secret,\u201d replied Herr Winckelkopf, studying his creation with justified pride. \u201cLet me know when you want it to explode, and I will set it to that exact moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cToday is Tuesday, and if you could send it off right away\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat cannot be done; I have important projects to finish for friends of mine in Moscow. Still, I might send it tomorrow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, that will be quite good!\u201d said Lord Arthur politely. \u201cIf it is delivered tomorrow night or Thursday morning, it will do. For the explosion, let it be Friday at noon exactly. The Dean is always at home then.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFriday at noon,\u201d repeated Herr Winckelkopf, and he wrote it down in a large ledger on the bureau near the fireplace.\\n\\n\u201cAnd now,\u201d said Lord Arthur, rising, \u201cplease let me know how much I owe you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is such a small matter, Lord Arthur, that I do not care to make a charge. The dynamite costs seven and sixpence, the clock three pounds ten, and the delivery about five shillings. I am only too happy to serve any friend of Count Rouvaloff\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut your trouble, Herr Winckelkopf?\u201d\"}"
