          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7124f9a79577be5ec7d46489\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself a moment to think, and hardly aware of her own feelings, Elizabeth finished that letter, grabbed the other, and opened it with intense impatience. It had been written a day after the first one ended.  \u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you must have my hurried letter; I hope this one will make more sense, but even though I\u2019m not rushed, my head is so confused I can\u2019t promise to be coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what to say, but I have bad news that can\u2019t wait. As foolish as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now desperate to be certain it has already happened, because there\u2019s far too much reason to fear they never went to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday; he left Brighton the day before, just hours after the express. Even though Lydia\u2019s brief note to Mrs. F. claimed they were headed to Gretna Green, Denny mentioned he believed W. never meant to go there or to marry Lydia at all; that was repeated to Colonel F., who immediately panicked, left B., and went after them. He traced them easily to Clapham but no farther, because when they reached that place, they switched to a hackney coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All anyone knows after that is they were seen continuing on the London road. I don\u2019t know what to think. After exhausting every inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. came into Hertfordshire, asking anxiously at every tollgate, and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but with no success\u2014no such couple had been seen. With the kindest concern he came to Longbourn and shared his fears in a manner that did credit to his character. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F.; but no one can blame them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is terrible. My father and mother assume the worst, but I cannot think so ill of him. Many circumstances might make it more sensible for them to marry privately in town rather than follow their original plan; and even if he could devise such a scheme against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections, which seems unlikely, could I believe she would be so devoid of sense? Impossible! Yet I am upset to find that Colonel F. is not confident they will marry: he shook his head when I voiced my hopes and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\u201d\"}"
