          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"048baa14b1df15107ab9faef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that the sum of it? That is no great matter! Many fall under the Republic\u2019s displeasure and must look out through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am. Inevitably, since I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry has answered every preceding inquiry. He is the one who has stepped down and stands with his hand on the coach door, replying to a knot of officials. They stroll around the carriage and slowly climb onto the box to inspect the scant luggage on the roof; country-folk loiter about, crowd nearer the doors, and stare greedily inside; a small child, held by its mother, stretches out its tiny arm to touch the wife of an aristocrat sent to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWe may depart, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou may depart. Forward, my postilions! A prosperous journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.\u2014And so the first peril is over!\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry speaks again as he clasps his hands and lifts his eyes. Terror fills the coach, weeping breaks out, and the heavy breathing of the unconscious traveler is heard.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not travelling too slowly? Can they not be urged to greater speed?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would appear like flight, my darling. I must not press them too far; it would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see if we are followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road lies clear, my dearest. Thus far, no one pursues us.\u201d\\n\\nPairs and trios of houses slip past, lone farms, decaying structures, dye-works, tanneries, and such, stretches of open country, rows of leafless trees. The hard, uneven paving stones lie under us, deep soft mud to either side. At times we edge into the bordering mire to escape the clattering stones that shake us; at times we bog down in the ruts and sloughs there. Our impatient torment grows so intense that, in our frantic fear and haste, we feel for springing out and running\u2014hiding\u2014doing anything but halting.\\n\\nOut from the open country and once more among crumbling buildings, isolated farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in pairs and threes, avenues of leafless trees. Have they tricked us, carrying us back by another route? Is not this the same place twice? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look behind, look behind, and see if we are chased! Silence! the posting-house.\\n\\nLeisurely our four horses are unhitched; leisurely the coach rests in the little street, stripped of its team, showing no likelihood of moving again; leisurely the fresh horses come into sight, one after another; leisurely the new postilions appear, sucking and braiding the lashes of their whips; leisurely the old postilions count their coins, make false totals, and end in discontent.\"}"
