          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e1ad2fe81fd06b85fe45c5e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThen listen! You!\u201d Monks shot back. \u201cWhen his father fell ill in Rome, his wife\u2014my mother\u2014whom he\u2019d been separated from for ages, left Paris and brought me along. Maybe she wanted to guard his fortune; she certainly didn\u2019t love him, and he didn\u2019t love her. He never knew we were there, because his senses were gone, and he kept drifting in and out until he died the next day. In his desk we found two papers written the night he first became sick. Both were addressed to you,\u201d he said to Mr. Brownlow, \u201cand wrapped in a short note warning that nothing should be delivered until after his death. One was a letter to Agnes; the other was a will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat about the letter?\u201d asked Mr. Brownlow.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter? A sheet covered back and forth with a confession full of remorse, begging God for mercy. He had duped the girl with some secret tale\u2014promising he\u2019d explain someday why he couldn\u2019t marry her just yet\u2014and she kept believing him until she trusted him too much and lost what could never be restored. She was only a few months from giving birth. He told her what he had planned to do to hide her disgrace, if he had lived, and begged her, if he died, not to curse his memory or blame herself or their young child for their sin, because all the guilt was his. He reminded her of the day he gave her the locket and ring that had her first name engraved on it, with space left for the name he hoped to add when he could finally marry her. He begged her to keep wearing it close to her heart, as she always had\u2014and then he raved, repeating the same words wildly, like a man losing his mind. I believe he had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, while Oliver\u2019s tears fell in streams.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cmatched the letter. He wrote of the misery his wife had caused him; of your defiance, vice, malice, and corrupt passions, you his only son, raised to despise him; and he left you and your mother each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. The rest of his estate he split into two equal parts\u2014one for Agnes Fleming, the other for their child, if the child were born alive and reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
