          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5257a5df4758be482ad487d9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When the wedding took place about three weeks later, St. Peter\u2019s was packed with a whole crowd of fashionable people. The Dean of Chichester read the service with great solemnity, and everyone agreed they had never seen a handsomer couple than the bride and groom. Yet they were more than handsome\u2014they were happy. Not once did Lord Arthur regret everything he had endured for Sybil, while she, in turn, gave him the greatest gifts a woman can offer a man: devotion, tenderness, and love. Reality never crushed their romance. They always felt young.\\n\\nSome years later, after they had two beautiful children, Lady Windermere came to visit Alton Priory, the lovely old estate the Duke had given his son as a wedding present. One afternoon, while she sat with Lady Arthur beneath a lime tree in the garden, watching the little boy and girl race up and down the rose walk like flickering sunbeams, she suddenly took her hostess\u2019s hand and asked, \u201cAre you happy, Sybil?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere, of course I\u2019m happy. Aren\u2019t you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI never have time to be happy, Sybil. I always like the last new person I\u2019m introduced to, but generally, as soon as I get to know people, I grow tired of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t your lions satisfy you, Lady Windermere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, no! Lions are only fun for one season. Once their manes are trimmed, they\u2019re the dullest creatures alive. Besides, they behave terribly if you\u2019re genuinely kind to them. Do you remember that awful Mr. Podgers? He was a complete fraud. Of course I didn\u2019t mind that, and even when he tried to borrow money I forgave him, but I couldn\u2019t stand his flirting with me. He made me absolutely loathe palmistry. I\u2019m into telepathy now. It\u2019s far more entertaining.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou mustn\u2019t speak against palmistry here, Lady Windermere; it\u2019s the one subject Arthur doesn\u2019t like people teasing about. I assure you he takes it very seriously.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou can\u2019t mean he believes in it, Sybil?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAsk him yourself, Lady Windermere\u2014here he comes,\u201d said Sybil, as Lord Arthur strolled up the garden with a large bunch of yellow roses in his hand, his two children dancing around him.\\n\\n\u201cLord Arthur?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, Lady Windermere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re not telling me you believe in palmistry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I do,\u201d the young man said with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cBut why?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBecause I owe all the happiness of my life to it,\u201d he murmured, dropping into a wicker chair.\"}"
