          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1207c74e8d84bed555108a3f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her situation was a severe and unusual trial. She longed to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s past, yet she felt bound to respect the trust the miserable woman had placed in her, as an innocent young girl. The woman\u2019s words and manner had moved Rose Maylie deeply; alongside her love for her young charge burned an equally strong desire to lead the outcast back toward repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to stay in London only three days before traveling to a distant coastal town for several weeks. It was already midnight on the first of those days. What could she do within the remaining forty-eight hours? How could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was with them and would remain so for two more days, but Rose knew the good doctor\u2019s impulsive nature too well. She could foresee the fury with which he would, in his first burst of anger, treat the person responsible for Oliver\u2019s capture. She dared not tell him the secret when no experienced advocate could support her plea for the girl. All these were reasons to act with great caution before confiding in Mrs. Maylie, whose first instinct would surely be to consult the doctor. Going to a lawyer, even if she knew how, was equally impossible for the same reason. She briefly thought of appealing to Harry, but that reminded her of their last parting; it felt unworthy to call him back when\u2014tears filled her eyes at the thought\u2014he might already have learned to forget her and might be happier away.\\n\\nTormented by these conflicting thoughts, pulled toward one plan and then another and rejecting each as it arose, Rose spent a sleepless, anxious night. After further reflection the next day, she made the desperate decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it pains him to return here,\u201d she thought, \u201cit will pain me just as much. Perhaps he will refuse; perhaps he will write, or he might come in person but carefully avoid me\u2014he did that when he left. I hardly expected it, yet it was better for us both.\u201d Rose then laid down the pen and turned away, as though even the paper meant to carry her message should not see her tears.\"}"
