          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3609ab544f1e48ccb7b3ad8a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond exulted in the keen sensations that attended the return of health; he felt that commanding the Athenians set him in a position fit for his aspirations, and in dreaming of taking Constantinople he anticipated an event that would stand like a beacon through the desolate ages, an achievement unmatched in human chronicles; when that city, rich in historical associations, whose wondrous site astonished the world, and which for centuries had remained the stronghold of the Moslems, should be redeemed from bondage and savagery and handed back to a race illustrious for intellect, refinement, and love of liberty. Perdita reposed on his regained companionship, his affection, his ambition and renown, as a Sybarite might sink into a sumptuous couch; every thought was rapture, each feeling seemed immersed in a soothing, congenial atmosphere.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on the seventh of July. The weather throughout our pilgrimage had been tranquil. Daily, before dawn glimmered, we deserted the night\u2019s lodge and watched the shade flee from hill and vale, while the sun\u2019s golden pageantry drew nigh. The soldiers who accompanied us caught, with their native vivacity, an ardent delight from beautiful scenery. The uprising sun was celebrated with triumphant music, and between the strains the birds, heard in fragments, supplied the pauses. At noon we raised our tents in some shaded glen or leafy woodland among the mountains, while a rivulet babbling over pebbles invited grateful slumber. Our march at evening, more composed, yet surpassed the morning\u2019s eager excitement. If the musicians played, without design they selected melodies of restrained feeling; a parting of lovers or a plaint for absence would be followed and concluded by a solemn hymn, which accorded with evening\u2019s placid loveliness and lifted the spirit to lofty, devout meditation. Often every sound was hushed so that we might listen to the nightingale, the fire-flies fluttering in luminous rhythm, while the gentle coo of the aziolo promised fair skies to the wayfarers. Should we thread a valley? Soft shadows enclosed us, and crags wore lovely tints. Should we cross a mountain, Greece spread beneath us like a living map, her famed heights cleaving the clear air, her rivers running in silver lines through fertile fields. Hardly daring to breathe, we English travellers gazed with ecstasy on that magnificent landscape, so unlike the grave tones and pensive charms of our native scenes. Beyond Macedonia, the fruitful yet flat plains of Thrace offered fewer charms, but the road still held our interest. A vanguard announced our coming, and the villagers were soon astir to render homage to Lord Raymond.\"}"
