          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bf7ad3594963eabc45e97478\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No reference escaped his lips until their visitor wished them good night; but the moment he was gone, he turned to his daughter and remarked\u2014\\n\\n\u201cJane, I heartily congratulate you. You are going to be a most contented woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane at once approached him, kissed his cheek, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are an excellent girl,\u201d he resumed, \u201cand I take great satisfaction in believing you will be so well established. I have not the least doubt that you two will do admirably together. Your tempers are wonderfully alike. Each of you is so accommodating that no decision will ever be reached; so easygoing that every servant will impose upon you; and so liberal that you will be forever exceeding your income.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not. Imprudence or heedlessness regarding money would be inexcusable in me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExceed their income! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife exclaimed, \u201cwhat nonsense is that? Why, he has four or five thousand a year, perhaps more.\u201d Then to her daughter she cried, \u201cOh, my dearest Jane, I am beside myself with joy! I am sure I shall not close my eyes all night. I knew it would happen. I always said it must, in the end. I was convinced you could not be so beautiful for nothing! I remember, the instant I saw him when he first arrived in Hertfordshire last year, I thought how probable it was that you should be united. Oh, he is the most handsome young man that ever existed!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia vanished from her thoughts. Jane stood far above every other child in her affection. At that moment she had regard for no one else. Her younger sisters promptly began to cultivate her favour for future pleasures she might be able to grant.\\n\\nMary requested access to the Netherfield library; Kitty pleaded vehemently for several balls there every winter.\\n\\nFrom then on, Bingley was naturally a daily visitor at Longbourn; often arriving before breakfast and never leaving till after supper; except when some barbarous neighbor, not to be sufficiently detested, had invited him to dinner, and he felt duty-bound to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now found very little opportunity for discourse with her sister; for while he stayed, Jane could spare attention for no one else. Yet Elizabeth considered herself of considerable use to both, during those intervals of separation that occasionally took place. When Jane was absent, he invariably attached himself to Elizabeth for the gratification of talking about her; and when Bingley was gone, Jane always pursued the same relief.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d she said one evening, \u201cby assuring me he had not the slightest idea of my being in town last spring!\u201d\"}"
