{"request_id":"bd3258e0b6624d6b44e6b4a5","rewritten_text":"Even now I had not drained the bitter cup; I was not yet convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel in every heartbeat, in every nerve, in every thought, that I stood alone of my kind—that I was the LAST MAN.

The day had grown overcast, and a thin rain began at sunset. Even the supposedly eternal sky wept, I thought; how could mortal man be ashamed to do the same? I remembered the old tales where people dissolved through weeping into ever-flowing springs. If only that were true; then my fate would resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Grief is strange; it spins a web on which it traces the story of its sorrow from every shape and change around it; it blends with everything living; it feeds on every object; like light, it fills all things and gives everything its own colors.

In my wandering search I had strayed some distance from the place where I was cast ashore and reached one of those watchtowers set at intervals along the Italian coast. I was glad for shelter, glad to find something made by human hands after staring so long at nature’s bleak emptiness; so I went inside and climbed the rough spiral stairs to the guardroom. Fate was kind so far that no harrowing trace of former inhabitants remained; a few planks laid across two iron trestles and scattered with dry maize leaves formed the bed offered to me, and an open chest with some half-moldy biscuits stirred an appetite I perhaps already had but hadn’t noticed until now. Thirst too—burning and fierce, born of the seawater I had swallowed and my body’s exhaustion—tormented me. Kindly nature made satisfying these needs feel pleasurable, so that I—even I!—was refreshed and soothed as I ate the meager food and drank a little of the sour wine half filling a flask left in this deserted room. Then I stretched out on the bed, not to be scorned by a shipwrecked victim. The earthy scent of the dry leaves was a balm after the foul odor of seaweed. I forgot my loneliness. I looked neither back nor ahead; my senses slipped into rest; I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes, of haymakers, of a shepherd’s whistle to his dog as he called for help to drive the flock into the fold, of sights and sounds from my mountain-boyhood, long forgotten until now."}

