          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"971ede5c88fb2cb37bd3ae55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOn that point I shall remain entirely silent, and you may be sure that once we are married, I will never utter an unkind reproach.\u201d\\n\\nThis had to be stopped immediately. \u201cYou are too quick, sir,\u201d she said. \u201cYou forget that I have not answered. Allow me to do so without delay. Thank you for the compliment you pay me. I truly appreciate the honour of your proposal, but I must decline it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know well,\u201d Mr. Collins replied with a solemn wave of his hand, \u201cthat young ladies usually refuse the man they secretly mean to accept the first time he applies, and sometimes even the second or third time. So I am not at all discouraged by your words, and I hope to lead you to the altar soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHonestly, sir,\u201d Elizabeth exclaimed, \u201cyour hope is rather strange after my declaration. I am not one of those young women\u2014if such exist\u2014who dare to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked again. I am entirely serious in my refusal. You could not make me happy, and I am sure I would be the last woman to make you happy. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am convinced she would find me unfit in every way for the role.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins gravely, \u201cbut I cannot believe her Ladyship would disapprove of you. You may be sure that when I next have the honour of seeing her, I shall praise your modesty, thrift, and other delightful qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally, Mr. Collins, there is no need to praise me. Allow me to decide for myself and do me the honour of believing what I say. I wish you all happiness and wealth, and by refusing your offer, I am doing all I can to ensure you have both. By proposing, you have satisfied your feelings about my family, and you may take possession of Longbourn whenever it comes to you without any guilt. This business is therefore settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had Mr. Collins not stopped her.\\n\\n\u201cWhen I next have the honour of speaking to you on this subject, I hope for a more favourable answer than the one you have given now. Yet I do not accuse you of cruelty, for I know it is customary for your sex to reject a man on his first proposal, and perhaps you have already said as much to encourage my suit as is proper for a woman of true delicacy.\u201d\"}"
