          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe44cddffecb09ed3e9a97d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet there was one secret he guarded even from these beloved companions; one he had cherished since his earliest days, and though he cherished his fellow collegiates, he would not expose it to their fineness of feeling or sympathy. He was in love. He feared the vehemence of his passion would become their jest, and he could not endure that they profane it by deeming that trivial and fleeting which he knew to be the very essence of his life.\\n\\nA gentleman of modest means resided near his family estate, with three charming daughters. The eldest surpassed the rest in beauty, yet her loveliness merely accompanied other qualities\u2014her mind was lucid, her character angelically gentle. She and my father had been companions since infancy: Diana, even as a child, enjoyed the favour of his mother; that partiality grew with the years of this graceful, vivacious girl, so that during his school and college holidays they were constantly together. Novels and the various means by which youth in civilized society learn of passions before truly feeling them, had exerted great influence over one so susceptible to every impression. At eleven, Diana was his preferred playmate, but he already spoke the language of love. Though she was nearly two years older, her schooling left her more childlike, at least regarding the understanding and expression of emotion; she accepted his fervent vows innocently and responded without comprehending their meaning. She had read no novels and was confined to the company of her younger sisters\u2014how could she discern the distinction between love and friendship? And when her maturing mind revealed the true nature of their dealings, her heart was already pledged to her friend, and her only dread was that other allurements or inconstancy might prompt him to break those infant pledges.\\n\\nYet each day their attachment grew more fervent and tender. It was a passion maturing with his own growth; woven through every faculty and sentiment, to be lost only with life itself. None but their two hearts knew of this affection; and though in all else, and even here, he feared the criticism of his companions for loving one beneath him in fortune, nothing could for an instant shake his resolve to unite himself to her as soon as he could summon the courage to confront the obstacles he was fixed upon overcoming.\\n\\nDiana was in every way worthy of his deepest devotion.\"}"
