          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a7859c515f2d8ef7757f4e81\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that no task occupied him, he crossed the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of the left, then reversed them, then swept a hand along his bearded chin, repeating the motions in steady alternation without pause. Rousing him from the vagabond reverie into which he always drifted after speaking was like hauling some feeble creature back from a faint, or trying, in hope of a revelation, to detain the spirit of a man at the point of death.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that everything?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he bent over the work again until the quiet was once more broken.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d Mr. Lorry inquired, his gaze steady upon him.\\n\\nHis gaunt eyes turned toward Defarge, as if to hand the question over; but no support coming from that side, they sank to the floor before returning to the man addressing him.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe slipped away again, for whole minutes, always repeating those rhythmic movements with his hands. At last his eyes crept back to the face they had abandoned; when they fastened on it, he started, resuming like a dreamer abruptly wakened and reverting to a subject from the night before.\\n\\n\u201cI asked leave to teach myself, and after a long time I obtained it with great difficulty, and I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he reached for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry, still fixing his face, asked:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the floor, and he stared steadily at the interrogator.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry set his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Does no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time arise in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nWhile the long-imprisoned man stared fixedly, in turn, at Mr. Lorry and at Defarge, faint traces of an intensely active intelligence in the middle of his brow struggled through the black haze that enveloped him. They clouded over again, weakened, and vanished; yet they had been visible.\"}"
