{"request_id":"383e55985df9da7b8e687716","rewritten_text":"“Oh, that’s nothing! I enjoy it. I don’t work for money; I live entirely for my art.”

Lord Arthur set £4, 2 shillings, and 6 pence on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after successfully declining an invitation to meet some anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and headed for the Park.

For the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at noon he drove down to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon the stolid hall porter kept posting telegrams from all over the country announcing horse-race results, divorce verdicts, the weather, and so on, while the ticker churned out tedious details about an all-night session in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the Pall Mall, the St. James’s, the Globe, and the Echo, much to Colonel Goodchild’s intense annoyance, as he wanted to read the reports of a speech he had given that morning at the Mansion House about South African missions and whether there should be black bishops in every province, and for some reason or other had a deep prejudice against the Evening News. None of the papers, however, contained even the slightest mention of Chichester, and Lord Arthur felt sure the attempt had failed. It was a crushing disappointment, and for a time he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he went to see the next day, was full of elaborate apologies and offered to provide him with another clock for free, or a case of nitroglycerin bombs at cost price. But he had lost all faith in explosives, and even Herr Winckelkopf admitted that everything is so adulterated nowadays that it’s nearly impossible to find dynamite in a pure state. The little German, though conceding that something must have gone wrong with the machinery, still hoped the clock might yet go off, citing the case of a barometer he once sent to the military governor at Odessa which, though set to explode in ten days, didn’t do so for nearly three months. True, when it finally went off it only managed to blow a housemaid to bits—the governor having left town six weeks earlier—but at least it showed that dynamite, when controlled by machinery, is a strong if somewhat unpredictable force for destruction."}

