          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"87070ce4e241029968678e76\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After joining in the general lamentations over the terrible consequences of this event\u2014which Elizabeth saw as all but certain, and Miss Bennet could not say was entirely impossible\u2014Elizabeth continued, \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I haven\u2019t already heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they any idea before the elopement happened? They must have seen them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted he had often suspected a fondness, especially on Lydia\u2019s part, but nothing that alarmed him. I feel so sorry for him. He behaved with the greatest care and kindness. He was actually on his way to us to assure us of his concern before he had any notion they had not gone to Scotland; once that fear spread, it hurried his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny certain that Wickham would not marry? Did he know they planned to run off? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Colonel Forster questioned him, Denny denied knowing anything of their plan, and refused to give his real opinion. He no longer insisted they would not marry, and from that I hope he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came himself, none of you, I suppose, doubted they were actually married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea even cross our minds? I felt a little uneasy\u2014a bit fearful for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage, because I knew his behavior had not always been entirely proper. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only sensed how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then admitted, with very natural pride at knowing more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had been prepared for such a step. She had known, it seems, that they\u2019d been in love for many weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I don\u2019t believe so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think badly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit he did not speak as well of Wickham as he once did. He thought him reckless and extravagant, and since this dreadful business occurred, it\u2019s said he left Meryton deeply in debt; but I hope that isn\u2019t true.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret, if we had revealed what we knew about him, this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut exposing someone\u2019s past faults without knowing how they feel now seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
