          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9df865f99cd263e979ffb4a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was quite upset by the second postponement of the wedding, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her gown, did everything she could to persuade Sybil to end the engagement. Yet, devoted as Sybil was to her mother, she had given her entire life to Lord Arthur, and nothing Lady Julia said could shake her trust. As for Lord Arthur, it took him several days to recover from the crushing disappointment, and for a while his nerves were completely shattered. Still, his excellent common sense soon reasserted itself, and his practical mind quickly settled on a course of action. Since poison had been a total failure, it was obvious that dynamite, or some other explosive, was the next thing to attempt.\\n\\nHe therefore went back over the list of his friends and relatives and, after careful consideration, decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of notable culture and learning, was incredibly fond of clocks and possessed a remarkable collection of timepieces, from the fifteenth century to the present, and it seemed to Lord Arthur that this passion of the good Dean\u2019s offered an ideal opportunity for carrying out his plan. Where to find an explosive device was, of course, another issue altogether. The London Directory could give him no help, and he felt it would be useless to go to Scotland Yard, since they never seemed to know anything about dynamite plots until after a blast, and even then had only a vague notion of what had occurred.\\n\\nSuddenly he remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with strong revolutionary leanings whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s that winter. Count Rouvaloff was supposed to be writing a biography of Peter the Great and to have come to England to study the documents relating to the Tsar\u2019s time here as a shipwright; but it was generally suspected that he was a Nihilist agent, and there was no doubt the Russian Embassy viewed his presence in London with deep distrust. Lord Arthur was convinced that he was just the man for the job, and one morning he drove to Rouvaloff\u2019s lodgings in Bloomsbury to seek his advice and help.\\n\\n\u201cSo you\u2019re getting serious about politics?\u201d Count Rouvaloff asked when Lord Arthur had explained why he had come; but Lord Arthur, who despised any show of pretension, felt compelled to admit that he had not the slightest interest in social issues, and wanted the explosive device solely for a strictly family matter that concerned no one but himself.\"}"
