          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc4b89cefd606a551ab99793\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day was fixed for the disclosure (lest our honour should take chill from delay), and on that day Drummle turned up with a neat little acknowledgment in Estella\u2019s hand, stating that she had been honoured to dance with him several times. This left me no alternative but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and generally to reject, as indefensible, the supposition that I was to be discovered anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove indulged in indiscriminate contradiction, and at last the advancement of good feeling was proclaimed to have proceeded at a prodigious pace.\\n\\nI recount it lightly, but it was no light matter for me. For I cannot adequately relate what misery it brought me to imagine that Estella could display any favour toward a despicable, lumpish, sullen lout, so far beneath the average. To this instant, I believe it was owing to some pure blaze of generosity and selflessness in my love for her that I could not bear the idea of her bending to that cur. Certainly I should have been wretched whoever she had preferred; yet a worthier choice would have afflicted me with a different form and measure of suffering.\\n\\nIt was easy for me to ascertain, and I did soon ascertain, that Drummle had begun to dog her steps, and that she permitted it. Shortly, he was perpetually trailing after her, and he and I crossed paths daily. He clung on, in a dreary stubborn fashion, and Estella held him on; now encouraging, now discouraging, now almost flattering him, now openly scorning him, now knowing him intimately, now barely recalling who he might be.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had dubbed him, was accustomed to lurking, however, and possessed the patience of his species. Added to this, he carried a blockheaded assurance in his wealth and family grandeur, which at times served him well\u2014almost substituting for concentration and resolute aim. Thus, the Spider, doggedly surveilling Estella, outwatched many astuter creatures, and would often unwind himself and drop at the proper instant.\\n\\nAt a particular Assembly Ball at Richmond (Assembly Balls were held in most places then), where Estella had eclipsed every other beauty, that blundering Drummle hovered about her under such toleration from her that I determined to speak to her regarding him. I seized the next chance; it came when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to escort her home, and she sat apart among some flowers, ready to depart.\"}"
        }
      ],
