          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b50a60e11613de238863210c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had truly been unaware of the influence he held. He followed her into the shrubs with no thought of using it. He came only out of concern to see how she was enduring Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, with no selfish purpose\u2014no goal beyond trying, if she allowed a chance, to comfort or advise her. Everything else was spontaneous, the immediate effect of what he heard on his feelings. The joyful certainty that she felt nothing for Frank Churchill, that her heart was entirely free of him, sparked the hope that, in time, he might win her affection himself. But that hope was not for the present; in a brief moment when eagerness overruled judgment, he only dared to ask whether she would allow him to try to win her. And the higher hopes that unfolded afterward were even more enchanting. The love he had asked permission to create\u2014if he could\u2014was already his. Within half an hour, he went from utter distress to something so close to perfect happiness it deserved no other name.\\n\\nHer transformation matched his. That same half-hour gave them both the precious certainty that they were loved, removing from both the same amount of ignorance, jealousy, and distrust. His jealousy had been long-standing, dating back to Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival\u2014or even the expectation of it. He had loved Emma and envied Frank Churchill almost from the same moment, one feeling probably revealing the other. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that drove him from the country. The Box Hill gathering convinced him to leave. He would spare himself from seeing such welcomed, encouraged attentions again. He left to learn to be indifferent. But he went to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother\u2019s home; womanhood appeared too kind there; Isabella was so like Emma\u2014differing only in those notable inferiority that made Emma shine more brightly\u2014that even more time would have accomplished little. Yet he stayed on determinedly, day after day, until that very morning\u2019s post brought the news about Jane Fairfax. Then, with the happiness he could not deny\u2014having never believed Frank Churchill remotely worthy of Emma\u2014blended with such tender concern, such intense anxiety for her, he could stay no longer. He rode home through the rain, and walked up immediately after dinner to see how this sweetest and best of all women, flawless despite her faults, endured the revelation.\"}"
