          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bb2db30857fa87840244ec51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But when I saw you become cherished by another; when I imagined that you might be adored as something other than a sacred emblem of beauty and virtue, or that you could return an affection more fervent than the one you felt for me, the demon stirred in my heart. I drove away your suitor, and from that moment peace deserted me. I chased sleep in vain; my eyelids would not close, and my blood boiled ceaselessly. I awakened to a new existence as a hopeful soul might wake in Hell. I will not tarnish your thoughts by recounting my battles, my fury at myself, my despair. Let a veil fall over the unthinkable impressions of a guilty father; such torments should not be made common. Everything was tumult, sin, remorse, and hatred, yet the softest love still survived. What first roused me to resolve that I would subdue this passion and restore her father to my child was witnessing your bitter, sympathetic grief. That is what brought me here: I believed that if I could stir again the sorrow I had felt at your mother\u2019s death, and revive the memories associated with her that had slept for seventeen years, then all affection for her daughter would fade. In an effort at heroism I decided to come alone, to depart from you\u2014the very life of my being\u2014and not look upon you until I might do so without guilt. It failed; I misjudged my courage or underestimated my love. I should surely have died had you not hurried to me. Would that I truly had been snuffed out!\\\" \\n\\n\\\"And now, Mathilda, I must share my final confession. I was pitiably deluded to imagine I could overcome my love for you; I never shall. The sight of this dwelling, these fields and woods, once inhabited by my first love, has only increased it: in my frenzy I dared tell myself\u2014Diana died to give her birth; her mother\u2019s spirit passed into her, and she should be as Diana to me. However I strive to banish it, this