          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6824ac4be1b6a6bd24e8bb23\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how was a confused village boy like me supposed to avoid that marvelous inconsistency the best and wisest people fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be perfectly true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I\u2019m terribly taken with her.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, I rolled onto my face when I got to that point, grabbed handfuls of hair on either side of my head, and yanked hard. All the while I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so completely mad and misplaced that I was quite aware it would have served me right if I\u2019d lifted my head by my hair and knocked it against the pebbles to punish my face for belonging to such a fool.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she didn\u2019t try to argue with me anymore. She laid her hand, a comforting hand though roughened by work, on my hands one after the other and gently took them out of my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder in a soothing way while, with my face on my sleeve, I cried a little\u2014just as I had in the brewery yard\u2014and felt vaguely convinced I\u2019d been badly treated by someone, or by everyone; I can\u2019t say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d Biddy said, \u201cwhich is that you felt you could trust me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another thing, which is that of course you know you can count on me to keep it and always do my best to deserve it. If your first teacher (dear me! such a poor one, and so needing lessons herself!) were teaching you now, she thinks she knows what lesson she\u2019d set. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you\u2019ve outgrown her, and it\u2019s no use anymore.\u201d Then, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, changing her voice to a fresh, pleasant tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, standing up, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI\u2019ll always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never will be, so that means always. Not that I ever need to tell you anything, because you already know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d Biddy said, barely above a whisper, as she looked away at the ships. Then she repeated, with her earlier pleasant tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we\u2019d walk a little farther, and we did, and the summer afternoon softened into a summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
