          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b79315cd6024ab1e5aea524a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox! I rushed to the king and murmured in his ear, \u201cOut the door at once, sire! That woman is dying of the sickness that swept Camelot two years ago.\u201d He never moved. \u201cTruly I shall stay\u2014and help.\u201d I whispered again, \u201cKing, you mustn\u2019t. You have to go.\u201d \u201cYou mean well,\u201d he said, \u201cand your words have sense. Yet it would shame a king to show fear, and shame a knight to hold back his hand when help is needed. Be at peace, I will not go. It is you who must leave. The Church\u2019s ban is not on me, but it forbids you to stay here, and she will punish you harshly if word reaches her of this trespass.\u201d It was a deadly place for him and could cost his life, but arguing was useless. Once he set his honor on the matter, nothing could move him; I knew that, so I said no more. The woman spoke: \u201cKind sir, will you climb the ladder and tell me what you see? Do not fear to tell the truth, for a mother\u2019s heart can reach a point where it cannot break again\u2014being already broken.\u201d \u201cWait,\u201d said the king, \u201cfeed the woman. I will go,\u201d and he laid down the knapsack. I turned to go, yet the king was already on his way. He stopped and looked at a man lying in the dim light who had neither noticed nor spoken. \u201cIs it your husband?\u201d asked the king. \u201cYes.\u201d \u201cIs he asleep?\u201d \u201cThank God for that mercy\u2014yes, these three hours. Where can I turn to repay such gratitude? My heart is bursting with it for the sleep he sleeps now.\u201d I said, \u201cWe will be careful. We will not wake him.\u201d \u201cNo, you will not, for he is dead.\u201d \u201cDead?\u201d \u201cYes. What a victory to know it! No one can hurt or insult him anymore. He is in heaven now and happy; and if not there, then he stays in hell content, because in that place there is neither abbot nor bishop. We were boy and girl together; we were man and wife for five and twenty years, never apart until this day. Think how long it is to love and suffer together. This morning he lost his mind, and in his fancy we were children again wandering through bright fields; so in that innocent, joyful talk he strayed farther and farther, still smiling lightly, and went into those other fields we do not know and passed from mortal sight.\u201d\"}"
