          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1a3269830916a8be260190b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 7th. I rose at dawn and, to my profound delight, at last saw what I could only conclude was the northern Pole itself. There it lay, unquestionably, immediately below my feet; yet, alas, I had risen to such an immense altitude that nothing could be observed with precision. Indeed, judging from the sequence of numbers marking my altitudes at different times between six in the morning on April second and twenty minutes to nine the same day (when the barometer fell), one might fairly deduce that at four o\u2019clock on the morning of April seventh the balloon had attained no less than, certainly, 7,254 miles above sea level. Although this height may appear tremendous, the calculation likely gives a figure far beneath the reality. At any rate, I unquestionably surveyed the entire major diameter of the globe; the complete northern hemisphere spread below me like an orthographic chart, and the great equatorial circle itself traced the edge of my horizon. Your Excellencies may readily imagine that the tightly bounded territories never yet explored inside the Arctic circle, though lying directly under me and therefore presenting no foreshortening, were still, by their smallness and the immense remoteness of my vantage point, too minute to allow very exact scrutiny. Yet whatever was visible proved both singular and stirring. North of that colossal rim earlier mentioned, which, with slight qualification, may be termed the final boundary of human discovery in these latitudes, there stretched an unbroken\u2014or nearly unbroken\u2014expanse of ice. During its first degrees this surface appeared sensibly flattened, later depressed into a plane, and finally grew slightly concave, terminating at the Pole itself in a sharply defined circular nucleus whose apparent diameter formed at the balloon an angle of about sixty-five seconds. Its dusky color, shifting in intensity, was always darker than any other point on the visible hemisphere, and at times deepened into an absolute and impenetrable black. Beyond this, little more could be determined. By noon the circular centre had notably shrunk in circumference, and by seven in the evening I had lost it altogether; the balloon crossed the western edge of the ice and sped swiftly off toward the equator.\\n\\nApril 8th. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter exhibited a sensible decrease, along with a considerable change in its general hue and aspect.\"}"
