          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"34e61921ce25d2832a3a306a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In a certain town, which I will not name for various reasons, there stands one public building that most towns, large or small, share: a workhouse. In that workhouse, on a day I need not specify because it matters little right now, the subject of this chapter entered the world.\\n\\nFor a long while after the parish surgeon delivered this child into a world of sorrow and trouble, it was doubtful whether the infant would live long enough to receive a name. If it had not, these memoirs would never have existed; or if they had, they would have filled only a couple of pages and been praised as the most concise and accurate biography in any literature.\\n\\nThough I will not argue that being born in a workhouse is a blessing in itself, I will say that in Oliver Twist\u2019s case, it was the best thing that could have happened. There was great difficulty in getting Oliver to accept the task of breathing\u2014a tedious habit, yet one custom has made essential to our comfort\u2014and for some time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, balanced unevenly between this life and the next, with the odds in favor of the latter. Had there been attentive grandmothers, worried aunts, knowledgeable nurses, and wise doctors hovering over him during this fragile time, he would most certainly have died at once. Instead, there was only a pauper old woman, muddled by an unusual ration of beer, and a parish surgeon who delivered babies by contract. Thus, Oliver and Nature settled the matter themselves. After a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and informed the workhouse inmates that the parish had gained a new burden by raising as loud a cry as could be expected from a male infant who had possessed a voice for barely three minutes and a quarter.\"}"
