          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0baa5a04830afae55c73bf57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of that. I will begin my story: it is my last duty, and I hope I have the strength to finish it. I confess no crimes; my mistakes may be forgiven, for they came not from malice but from poor judgment, and I believe few would claim they could, with wiser choices, have avoided the misery that has claimed me. My fate has been ruled by necessity, a dreadful necessity. It would have taken hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger than any human hands, I think\u2014to break the thick, iron chain that has kept me, once full of joy and love of goodness, bound to a misery that ends only now, with death. But I wander; my story remains untold. I will pause, wipe my dim eyes, and try to trade this dull yet heavy sorrow for the sharper feelings of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank: he lost his father early and was raised by a weak mother who gave him all the indulgence she thought due a wealthy noble. He was sent to Eton and then to college, and from childhood he enjoyed the free use of large sums of money, gaining from the start the independence that a boy with such advantages always finds at a public school.\\n\\nUnder these conditions his passions found rich ground where they could take root and grow as flowers or weeds, depending on their nature. Always left to act for himself, his character became distinct very early, showing many facets where a sharp observer could see the seeds of both virtues and misfortunes. His careless extravagance, which made him waste immense sums to satisfy passing whims he glorified as passions, often showed itself in boundless generosity. Yet while he busied himself with the needs of others, his own desires were satisfied entirely. He gave his money, but no wish of his own was sacrificed for others; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was eager to have engaged in any way.\\n\\nI do not claim that, had his own desires been set against those of others, he would have shown undue selfishness, but he never faced that test. Raised in prosperity and surrounded by its blessings, he was loved by everyone, and everyone wanted to please him.\"}"
