          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"04ef759e2728d299b470a7e9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His manner was stubborn to the extreme, and \u201cdat deuced bug\u201d were the only words he spoke along the way. I carried two dark lanterns, while Legrand made do with the scarab, which he had tied to the end of a bit of whip-cord. He swung it back and forth like a magician. Seeing this latest sign of his unbalanced mind, I could barely keep from tears. I decided it was best to indulge his whim for now, at least until I could find a better plan that might work. Meanwhile I tried, without success, to get him to talk about the point of the expedition. After persuading me to come, he refused to talk about anything less than essential, answering every question with \u201cwe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the head of the island in a small skiff and climbed the high land on the mainland shore. Then we headed northwest through a stretch of country so wild and lonely that there was no sign any person had ever passed there. Legrand walked ahead with purpose, stopping only now and then to check what seemed to be landmarks he had set on some earlier trip.\\n\\nIn this way we traveled for about two hours, and the sun was just setting when we reached a region even more desolate than any we had seen. It was a kind of plateau near the top of an almost unreachable hill, thickly wooded from bottom to peak, broken by giant crags that seemed to rest loosely on the ground and, in many cases, were kept from sliding into the valleys only by the trees against which they leaned. Deep ravines ran in several directions, giving the scene an even grimmer solemnity.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed was choked with brambles. We soon learned we could not force our way through without a scythe, so Jupiter, on his master\u2019s orders, cut a path for us to the base of an enormously tall tulip-tree. It stood on the level with eight or ten oaks, towering over them all and over any tree I had ever seen in the grace of its leaves and shape, the spread of its branches, and its general majesty.\"}"
        }
