          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"adefe5397eee756bada6bbf2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJoseph! Joseph!\u201d He shook his head and tapped it, showing he thought Joseph was lacking. \u201cBut my dear young friend,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, \u201cyou must be hungry, you must be tired. Sit down. Here\u2019s a chicken from the Boar, here\u2019s a tongue from the Boar, here are one or two other little things from the Boar, which I hope you won\u2019t disdain. But do I,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, springing up again as soon as he sat, \u201csee before me the one I always played with in his happy childhood? And may I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d He meant to ask if he could shake hands. I agreed, and he shook my hand warmly before sitting again. \u201cHere is wine,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook. \u201cLet us drink, thanks to Fortune, and may she always choose her favourites wisely! And yet I cannot,\u201d he said, rising yet again, \u201csee before me someone\u2014and drink to someone\u2014without again asking\u2014May I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d I said he could, and he shook my hand again, emptied his glass, and turned it upside down. I did the same, and the wine went straight to my head as if I had stood on it first. Mr. Pumblechook served me the liver wing and the best slice of tongue (none of those odd back streets of pork now), and took little for himself. \u201cAh! poultry, poultry! Little did you think,\u201d he told the bird in the dish, \u201cwhen you were a young chick, what awaited you. You never thought you would be refreshment under this humble roof for one who\u2014Call it a weakness if you like,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again, \u201cbut may I? may I\u2014?\u201d It was soon pointless to keep saying he could, so he just did it. I wonder how he managed so many handshakes without cutting himself on my knife. \u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he went on after eating steadily, \u201cwho had the honour of raising you by hand! It\u2019s a sad picture to think she can no longer fully understand that honour. May\u2014\u201d I saw he was coming at me again and stopped him. \u201cWe\u2019ll drink her health,\u201d I said. \u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, sinking back in his chair, limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s how you know them, sir!\u201d (I don\u2019t know who sir was, but it wasn\u2019t me and there was no third person there.) \u201cThat\u2019s how you know the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
