          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35a8e066e25203e5ec18485c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is barely four o\u2019clock; yet winter\u2019s grip has already driven down the sun: not a cloud stains the clear, frosty vault overhead to catch its slanting rays, but the air itself holds a faint rosy tint which the snow-draped earth reflects. I occupy a solitary cottage on a wide, desolate heath: no voice of living thing reaches my ear. All around stretches a white and lifeless expanse, broken only by a few black spots where the noon sun laid bare the edges of sharp hillocks, the snow falling there having slid aside and lay thinner than on the flat. A couple of birds tap at the hard ice sealing the pools\u2014the frost has endured long. \\n\\nMy mind sits in a strange mood. I am alone\u2014utterly alone\u2014in this world; misfortune\u2019s blight has swept over me and left me withered. I know I am about to die, and the knowledge brings a wild happiness, a lightness. I touch my wrist; the pulse races. I raise my thin hand to my cheek; it burns. A slight, quick spirit within me crackles with its final sparks. Never again shall I see another winter\u2019s snow\u2014I truly believe I shall never feel the life-giving heat of a summer sun; and under that conviction I begin writing my tragic tale. Perhaps such a history should perish with me, yet some indefinable impulse drives me on, and I have no strength, either in body or mind, to oppose the smallest urge. While vigor filled me, I felt a sacred dread in my story that made it unfit for telling, and now, on the brink of death, I profane its mystic terrors. It is as the grove of the Eumenides, which only the dying may tread; and Oedipus stands at the grave. \\n\\nWhat am I setting down? I must gather my thoughts. I know not whether anyone will read these lines besides you, my friend, who shall have them at my death. I do not address you alone, for I would take pleasure in dwelling on our friendship in a manner that would be needless if you were the sole reader. I shall therefore narrate as though for strangers. Often you have asked why I live in solitude, why I weep, and most of all why I wrap myself in impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I could not speak; in death I lift the veil. Others may skim these pages carelessly: to you, Woodville, dear, affectionate friend, they shall be treasured\u2014the last memorials of a heart-broken girl who, even while dying, is warmed by gratitude towards you. Your tears will fall on these words recounting my misfortunes; I am certain of it\u2014and while breath remains, I thank you for your compassion.\"}"
