          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5ec64e71663ecee1b0438345\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, hang my hat! I want Virginia!\u201d shouted the little Duke with a laugh, and they galloped on to the train station. There Mr. Otis asked the station master if anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but no one had. The station master telegraphed up and down the line and promised a strict watch for her; after Mr. Otis bought a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper closing his shop, he rode off to Bexley, a village about four miles away, said to be a favorite gypsy hangout because of the big common nearby. They woke the local policeman, but he knew nothing, and after riding all over the common they turned their horses toward home, reaching the Chase around eleven, exhausted and nearly heartbroken. Washington and the twins were waiting for them at the gatehouse with lanterns, since the avenue was very dark. There was no sign of Virginia. The gypsies had been caught on Brockley meadows, but she wasn\u2019t with them; they explained their sudden departure by saying they had mistaken the date of Chorton Fair and rushed off for fear of being late. They were sincerely upset to hear Virginia was missing, grateful as they were to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of them had stayed behind to help search. The carp pond had been dredged, and the entire Chase thoroughly explored, but to no effect. Clearly, for that night at least, Virginia was lost to them, and in the deepest gloom Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house, the groom following with the two horses and the pony. In the hall they found a cluster of terrified servants, and in the library poor Mrs. Otis lay on a sofa, nearly out of her mind with fear and worry, while the old housekeeper bathed her forehead with cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she eat something and ordered supper for everyone. It was a mournful meal; hardly anyone spoke, and even the twins were hushed and subdued, being very fond of their sister. When they finished, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s pleading, sent everyone to bed, saying nothing more could be done that night and that he would telegraph Scotland Yard in the morning to send detectives at once.\"}"
