          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"698094160ac784eecbb48ccd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was the opening of February; and Anne, having already spent a month in Bath, grew restless for tidings from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted far more than Mary\u2019s scant report. Three weeks had gone by without any word. All she knew was that Henrietta had returned to the family home; that Louisa\u2014though pronounced to be mending quickly\u2014remained in Lyme; and she was thinking of them all very earnestly one evening when a thicker-than-usual letter from Mary was handed to her, enlivened further by Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s compliments. The Crofts must be in Bath! That was something to engage her. They were people toward whom her heart readily turned. \u201cWhat\u2019s this?\u201d exclaimed Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts are come to Bath? The very Crofts who lease Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d \u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d \u201cOh, such letters serve excellently as passports. They ensure one\u2019s presentation. I should have called on Admiral Croft regardless. I am aware of what is due to my tenant.\u201d Anne could not listen a moment longer; she would not even have known how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion fared; her letter had seized all her thoughts. It had been begun some days earlier. \u201cFebruary 1st. \u201cMY DEAR ANNE, \u201cI make no excuses for my silence, because I know how little people value letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too amused to care about Uppercross, which, as you well know, offers very little to set down. We have passed a wretchedly dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove did not give a single dinner party the whole season. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. However, the holidays are finally ended: I do not believe any children ever had such lengthy ones. I know I never did. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be astonished to learn they have never gone home. Mrs Harville must be a queer sort of mother to part with them so long. I do not understand it. They are by no means pleasant children, to my mind; yet Mrs Musgrove seems to like them at least as well as, if not better than, her own grandchildren. What dreadful weather we have endured! It may not be felt in Bath, with your fine pavements; but in the country it matters a good deal. Not a soul has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who has been coming much more often than agreeable. Between ourselves, I think it is a great pity that Henrietta did not stay at Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little out of his path.\u201d\"}"
        }
