          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"386ae0736533763607b38cae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He let her know that Frank admired her greatly\u2014thought her very beautiful and very charming; and with so much to recommend him overall, she felt she had no right to judge him harshly. As Mrs. Weston remarked, \u201call young people will have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nThere was one person among his new acquaintances in Surrey who was not so forgiving. Generally he was judged throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury with great fairness; people made generous allowances for the small excesses of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so often and bowed so well. But there was one mind among them that bows or smiles could not soften or distract from criticizing\u2014Mr. Knightley. The news was brought to him at Hartfield; for a moment he was silent, but Emma heard him almost at once mutter to himself, over a newspaper he held, \u201cHum! Just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.\u201d She half thought of taking offense, but a quick look convinced her he said it only to ease his own feelings, not to provoke anyone; so she let it go.\\n\\nThough in one instance they brought less pleasing news, Mr. and Mrs. Weston\u2019s visit that morning was in another way especially well timed. Something occurred while they were at Hartfield that made Emma seek their advice; and, even better, she wanted exactly the guidance they offered.\\n\\nThis was the situation: The Coles had lived in Highbury for several years and were very decent people\u2014friendly, generous, and unpretentious. Yet they were from a lower background, involved in trade, and only moderately genteel. When they first came to the country, they lived within their income, quietly, entertaining little, and that modestly; but the past year or two had brought them a large increase of wealth. Their town business had yielded higher profits, and fortune in general had favored them. With their wealth, their ambitions grew: they wanted a larger house, more company. They enlarged their home, hired more servants, and increased their expenses in every area; by now they were, in fortune and style of living, second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of society, along with their new dining room, prepared everyone to expect them to host dinner parties; a few gatherings, mostly of single men, had already happened. Emma could hardly imagine they would dare invite the regular and best families\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing would tempt her to go if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal carry less weight than she wished.\"}"
