          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d9d20123cf3472e2f7eb9fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once our friend was settled in his new office, we turned our attention toward Windsor. Its nearness to London eased the sting of leaving Raymond and Perdita. We said goodbye in the Protectoral Palace. It was amusing to watch my sister immerse herself in the part she had to play, trying to hold her new position with suitable dignity. Her pride and her modest demeanor clashed like never before. Her shyness was genuine, born from a fear of being misjudged and from her tendency to shrug off the world\u2019s neglect, traits she shared with Raymond. Yet Perdita thought of others more than he did; some of her bashfulness came from wanting to spare the people around her from feeling inferior\u2014an idea she never applied to herself. Idris, thanks to her birth and upbringing, would have fit more naturally into ceremonial rituals; but her habitual ease made such tasks dull, while Perdita enjoyed her new role despite its drawbacks. Filled with fresh ideas, she barely felt sorrow at our departure. She kissed us goodbye lovingly, promised to visit soon, and did not resent the necessity for our separation. Raymond\u2019s spirits were boundless; he had no idea what to do with his newly gained power. His mind overflowed with plans, though none were settled yet. He promised himself, his friends, and the world that his time as Protector would be marked by some act of extraordinary glory. So we spoke of them and reflected as our diminished party returned to Windsor Castle. Glad to have escaped political turbulence, we hurried toward solitude with doubled enthusiasm. We had plenty to occupy us; my restless nature now expressed itself through sheer intellectual effort, and rigorous study proved a perfect remedy for the fevered energy that would have plagued me in idleness. Perdita allowed us to bring Clara back to Windsor; she and my two beautiful infants became endless sources of interest and joy.\\n\\nOnly one thing disturbed our peace: Adrian\u2019s health. It clearly declined, yet no symptom revealed the disease, unless his shining eyes, animated features, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption. Still, he felt neither pain nor dread. He threw himself into books, and when he needed rest, he found it with those he loved best\u2014his sister and me.\"}"
