{"request_id":"a9b1498efa51d743770b5704","rewritten_text":"We endured a few grim hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial would begin. My father and the rest of the family had to appear as witnesses, so I went with them to the court. During this wretched mockery of justice I felt unending torment. It would be decided whether my curiosity and reckless inventions would bring about the deaths of two people: one a smiling baby full of innocence and joy, the other murdered in a way so foul that every detail intensified the horror. Justine was also a girl of worth, with traits that promised a happy life; now all would be erased in a shameful grave, and I was the cause. A thousand times I would have confessed to the crime laid on Justine, but I had been away when it happened, and such a claim would have been dismissed as lunacy and would not have cleared the one who suffered through me.

Justine appeared composed. She wore mourning clothes, and her face, always pleasant, was made exquisitely lovely by the solemnity of her feelings. She seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, even under the gaze and curses of thousands, for whatever kindness her beauty might have inspired was erased in the spectators’ minds by the idea of the crime she supposedly committed. She was calm, yet her calm was clearly forced; since her earlier confusion had been used as proof of guilt, she steeled herself to appear brave. When she entered the court she glanced around and quickly saw where we were sitting. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she noticed us, but she regained control, and a look of sorrowful affection stood as proof of her complete innocence.

The trial began, and after the prosecutor stated the charge, several witnesses were called. A series of strange facts mounted against her, enough to sway anyone who lacked the certainty of her innocence that I possessed. She had been out the entire night the murder was committed, and toward morning a market-woman saw her near the spot where the child’s body was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked odd and gave only a confused, senseless reply. She returned home around eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and begged to know if anything had been heard of him."}

