{"request_id":"6dee196f76c5cc8b977dcbf2","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became close with Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a series of misfortunes left him nearly penniless. To escape the shame that followed his losses, he left New Orleans, the city of his ancestors, and settled on Sullivan’s Island near Charleston, South Carolina.

This island is a very unusual place. It is composed almost entirely of sea sand and stretches about three miles in length. Nowhere is it more than a quarter mile wide. A barely noticeable creek separates it from the mainland, winding through a wilderness of reeds and sludge, favored by marsh hens. The plant life is sparse and stunted. There are no large trees. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands and where a few miserable frame houses shelter summer refugees from Charleston’s dust and fever, the bristly palmetto grows. But everywhere else, except this western point and a strip of hard white beach along the sea, the island is covered with thick sweet myrtle, highly prized by English gardeners. The shrub often grows fifteen or twenty feet high here, forming an almost impenetrable thicket that perfumes the air.

In the deepest part of this thicket, not far from the island’s eastern and more distant end, Legrand had built a small hut, which he occupied when I first met him by chance. Our acquaintance soon deepened into friendship, for there was much about the recluse to spark interest and respect. He was well educated and had remarkable mental powers, yet he was moody and misanthropic, veering between bursts of enthusiasm and fits of melancholy. He owned many books but rarely used them. His main pleasures were shooting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtle thickets looking for shells or insects—his insect collection could have made a Swammerdamm envious. During these outings he was usually accompanied by an elderly Black man named Jupiter, who had been set free before the family’s financial collapse but could be persuaded neither by threats nor promises to give up what he saw as his right to attend his young “Massa Will.” It is quite possible Legrand’s relatives, thinking his mind somewhat unsettled, had encouraged Jupiter’s stubborn loyalty so the old man could watch over the wanderer."}

