          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4406be45206009f468e4c1ac\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Three or four months went by, and winter settled in. I had been in school most of that time and could spell, read, and write a little, and I could recite the times table up to six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t think I could ever go further, even if I lived forever. I never cared for arithmetic, anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I could stand it. Whenever I got extra tired I skipped, and the whipping I got the next day did me good and lifted my spirits. The longer I stayed in school, the easier it became. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they didn\u2019t rub me so hard. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed still pressed on me, but before the weather turned cold I would slip out sometimes and sleep in the woods, and that gave me a rest. I liked the old ways best, but I was beginning to like the new ones a little, too. The widow said I was improving slow but steady and doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast I knocked over the salt cellar. I reached for some salt fast, wanting to toss it over my left shoulder to ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson got there first and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; you\u2019re always making a mess!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but that couldn\u2019t keep bad luck away, and I knew it. After breakfast I went out feeling worried and shaky, wondering where the trouble would land on me and what it would be. There are ways to keep off certain kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of them, so I didn\u2019t try anything. I just walked along, low and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile that led through the tall board fence. An inch of fresh snow lay on the ground, and I saw someone\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry, lingered around the stile for a while, then gone on around the garden fence. It seemed strange that after waiting there they hadn\u2019t come in. I couldn\u2019t figure it out. It felt mighty odd, somehow. I meant to follow the track around, but first I crouched to study it.\"}"
