          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"54b2fec947e46fcb117f7a63\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both loved Adrian, though she was still too young to fully appreciate his gifts or share my sympathy for his pursuits and opinions. I spent all my time with him. His sensitivity and sweetness gave our conversations a tender, otherworldly quality. Then he could be as cheerful as a lark singing from its perch in the sky, his thoughts soaring like an eagle, as innocent as a gentle dove. He could shake Perdita out of her seriousness and soothe the restless drive that tormented me. I looked back on my own frustrated desires and harsh struggles with others like a troubled dream, and I felt as transformed as if I had moved into another body, whose fresh senses and nervous system showed the world differently in the mirror of the mind. Yet it wasn\u2019t so; I was still the same in strength, in my hunger for sympathy, in my need for action. My manly virtues remained, for the sorceress Urania spared Samson\u2019s hair while he slept at her feet; only everything was softened and humanized. Nor did Adrian teach me merely the dry facts of history and philosophy. As he used them to help me rein in my reckless, untamed spirit, he also opened the living page of his own heart and let me feel and understand its strange nature.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had tried, even when he was a child, to plant daring and ambitious plans in her son\u2019s mind. She saw that he had genius and exceptional talent; she cultivated these so she could later use them to serve her own purposes. She encouraged his thirst for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even tolerated his untamed love of freedom, hoping it would, as so often happens, turn into a craving for power. She tried to raise him with a sense of grievance and a desire to avenge himself on those who had brought about his father\u2019s abdication. In this she failed. Accounts\u2014however distorted\u2014of a great and wise people asserting their right to govern themselves stirred his admiration: from early on he became a republican by principle. Yet his mother didn\u2019t lose hope. To her love of authority and proud sense of birth she added iron determination, patience, and self-control. She devoted herself to studying her son\u2019s temperament. By using praise, criticism, and exhortation, she tried to find and strike the right chords; and though the music she produced sounded like discord to her, she pinned her hopes on his abilities and remained convinced she would ultimately bend him to her will.\"}"
