          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e8444f9f594987611ee3996f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHeavens above!\u201d the Duchess murmured to herself. \u201cSo he really is some kind of cheiropodist, after all. How utterly dreadful. I do hope he turns out to be a foreigner\u2014at least that would soften it a little.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI simply must introduce him to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIntroduce him!\u201d exclaimed the Duchess. \u201cYou don\u2019t mean he is actually here?\u201d She began hunting for a little tortoise-shell fan and a very frayed lace shawl, so she could leave at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, naturally he is here; I should never think of hosting a party without him. He assures me my hand is psychically pure, and that if my thumb had been the tiniest fraction shorter, I would have become a hopeless pessimist and entered a convent.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, now I understand!\u201d said the Duchess, greatly comforted. \u201cHe tells fortunes, does he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd misfortunes, too,\u201d replied Lady Windermere. \u201cSome in abundance. Next year, for instance, I face terrible peril both on land and sea, so I plan to live in a balloon and hoist up my dinner in a basket every night. It is written on my little finger, or else on my palm\u2014I forget which.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Duchess, surely Providence has learned to resist temptation by now. I think everyone should have their palms read monthly, just to know what they ought not to do. Of course we go and do it anyway, but at least the warning is delightful. If someone doesn\u2019t fetch Mr. Podgers this instant, I shall be forced to fetch him myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAllow me, Lady Windermere,\u201d said a tall, handsome young man who had been listening nearby with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u201cThank you, Lord Arthur, but I fear you would not recognize him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he is as exceptional as you claim, Lady Windermere, I cannot fail to notice him. Tell me his appearance, and I\u2019ll bring him straight to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, he doesn\u2019t resemble a cheiromantist in the least. Not in the mysterious, esoteric, or romantic sense. He is a short, stout fellow, with a comical bald head and large gold spectacles; somewhere between a family physician and a village solicitor. I am truly sorry, but it isn\u2019t my doing. People are so tiresome. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets look just like pianists; and I recall last season inviting an appalling conspirator to dinner\u2014a man who had blown up ever so many people, wore a coat of mail, and kept a dagger up his sleeve\u2014and would you believe that when he arrived he resembled a pleasant old clergyman and spent the evening telling jokes?\u201d\"}"
