          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8b76f6d418759601c85af781\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Just as they were leaving the dining-room, midnight commenced booming from the clock tower, and with the last stroke came a crash and a piercing scream; a terrible peal of thunder shook the house, uncanny music drifted through the air, a panel at the staircase\u2019s summit flew open with a loud report, and onto the landing stepped Virginia, pale and white, holding a little casket. In an instant they all hurried toward her. Mrs. Otis clasped her fervently to her heart, the Duke covered her with frantic kisses, and the twins whirled around them in a wild war-dance.\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens! child, where have you been?\u201d Mr. Otis demanded rather sharply, convinced she had been playing some foolish prank. \u201cCecil and I have been riding everywhere looking for you, and your mother has nearly died of fright. You must never try such practical jokes again.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExcept on the Ghost! except on the Ghost!\u201d cried the twins, dancing about.\\n\\n\u201cMy darling, thank God you are found; you must never leave me again,\u201d murmured Mrs. Otis, kissing the trembling girl and smoothing the tangled gold of her hair.\\n\\n\u201cPapa,\u201d Virginia said quietly, \u201cI have been with the Ghost. He is dead, and you must come and see him. He had been very wicked, but he was truly sorry for all he had done, and before he died he gave me this box of lovely jewels.\u201d\\n\\nThe whole family stared at her in stunned silence, yet she remained solemn and composed; turning about, she led them through the gap in the wainscot along a narrow hidden passage, Washington following with a candle he had snatched from the table. At last they reached a heavy oak door studded with rusty nails. The moment Virginia touched it, it swung back on massive hinges, and they stepped into a small low chamber with a vaulted ceiling and a single tiny barred window. Set into the wall was a great iron ring, and fastened to it lay a gaunt skeleton, stretched full length on the stone floor, its long fleshless fingers seeming to reach for an old-fashioned trencher and ewer placed just beyond its grasp. The jug had clearly once held water, for green mould lined its interior. On the trencher there remained only a mound of dust. Virginia knelt beside the skeleton, folded her little hands, and began to pray silently, while the rest of the company watched in awe the grim tragedy whose secret had now been revealed to them.\"}"
