          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64ec9dfc9bd36b4b20e6f2f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known her sister wanted to pull her away from such hopes and circumstances, what would she have felt? Only her mother, who likely would have felt almost the same, could have understood. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was the only thing that eased her sorrowful certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nYet none of them knew what had happened, and their excitement went on, with hardly a pause, until the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. She had often been in his company since her return, so her nerves were mostly calm; the feelings of earlier fondness were gone completely. She had even learned to see in the very gentleness that first charmed her a showiness and monotony that now annoyed and tired her. His current conduct toward her gave her fresh annoyance, because his quick desire to revive the attentions of their early acquaintance could now only irritate her. She lost all concern for him when she found herself picked out for such empty, trivial flirtation; and while she firmly discouraged him, she could not help feeling the rebuke in his believing that, after however long and for whatever reason his attentions had ceased, her vanity would still be pleased and her preference won at any time by their renewal.\\n\\nOn the regiment\u2019s final day in Meryton, he dined with other officers at Longbourn; and Elizabeth was so unwilling to part on friendly terms that when he asked about her time at Hunsford, she told him Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings, and asked if he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a brief pause and a returning smile, he answered that he used to see him often, and after noting that he was a very gentlemanlike man, asked how she had liked him. Her reply praised him warmly. With an air of indifference he then added, \u201cHow long did you say he stayed at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves once you know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, giving her a look she noticed. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d but stopping himself, he went on in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in his manner that he improves?\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
