          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7361aa1d335136a8eac849f4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut do not think I did not feel it was an evil in itself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDepend on it, they were all perfectly comfortable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might not like them any better for that. So many women and children have no right to be comfortable on board.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Frederick, you are talking nonsense. What would become of us sailors\u2019 wives, who often need to be carried from one port to another after our husbands, if everyone felt as you do?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy feelings, you see, did not stop me from taking Mrs Harville and her whole family to Plymouth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I cannot bear to hear you talking like a fine gentleman, as if women were all fine ladies instead of sensible creatures. None of us expect smooth water every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, my dear,\u201d said the Admiral, \u201cwhen he has a wife, he will sing another tune. Once he is married, if we live to see another war, we will see him doing what you and I, and many others, have done. He will be very grateful to anyone who brings him his wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, that we shall.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow I have done,\u201d cried Captain Wentworth. \u201cWhen married people start in on me with, \u2018You will feel differently once you are married,\u2019 I can only say, \u2018No, I shall not.\u2019 Then they say again, \u2018Yes, you will,\u2019 and that ends it.\u201d\\n\\nHe rose and walked away.\\n\\n\u201cWhat a great traveler you must have been, ma\u2019am!\u201d said Mrs Musgrove to Mrs Croft.\\n\\n\u201cPretty well, ma\u2019am, in the fifteen years of my marriage, though many women have done more. I have crossed the Atlantic four times, and once went to the East Indies and back, besides visiting places nearer home\u2014Cork, Lisbon, Gibraltar. But I never went beyond the Straits, and never was in the West Indies. We do not call Bermuda or Bahama the West Indies, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMrs Musgrove had nothing to say in disagreement; she could not remember ever calling them anything at all.\\n\\n\u201cAnd I assure you, ma\u2019am,\u201d continued Mrs Croft, \u201cnothing can beat the accommodations of a man-of-war. I speak of the higher rates. In a frigate, of course, you are more confined, though any sensible woman can be perfectly happy there; and I can honestly say the happiest part of my life was spent on board ship. While we were together, you know, there was nothing to fear.\u201d\"}"
