{"request_id":"8fd832408f39d8580908b768","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals—poorly followed by mourners, yet watched by the crowd as tokens of the blackest fate. Some fixed their eyes on the processions with feverish intensity, others shrank away, and a few broke into loud sobs.

After first relieving the sick, Adrian had laboured to conceal the plague’s signs and progress from the citizens of London. He well knew that fear and gloomy expectation were potent allies of pestilence, that dejection made the human frame peculiarly open to contagion. Thus no distressing spectacle was permitted: the shops mostly kept open, and a modest current of passengers still moved through the streets. Even so, to me, who had not seen the city since the visitation began, London looked sufficiently altered. Carriages had disappeared, grass flourished high along the paving, the houses wore a forsaken air, shutters stood closed, and every face bore the pallid look of one who dreaded some unseen foe; nothing remained of the old brisk, business-like mien of Londoners. My solitary coach drew eyes as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets on the way were even more dismal and empty. Adrian’s ante-room was full; he was in his hour of audience. Unwilling to interrupt him, I lingered, observing people come and go. They were chiefly of the middling and poorer ranks, whose livelihood perished with the stoppage of trade and that restless enterprise for which our land was known. The newly arrived wore anxious, sometimes terrified looks, but those emerging from his presence seemed resigned, even cheered. I saw my friend’s hand in their quickened step and brightened faces. When the clock struck two, further admissions ceased; those denied entry departed sullenly or in tears, and I passed into the audience chamber.

The improvement in Adrian’s health astonished me. He was no longer bowed like a tender spring stalk overburdened by its own crown of bloom. His eyes shone, his features were calm, his whole frame breathed concentrated energy in place of former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries who sorted petitions and recorded the notes of that day’s interviews. Two or three petitioners still awaited their turn. I marveled at his fairness and patience. Whoever had the means to live away from London he immediately counseled to depart, providing the resources to make their escape."}

