          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1e030f432742e41c27f08aad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking at him, one might have sworn that Nemesis had filched the shield of Pallas and displayed the Gorgon\u2019s visage to him. He appeared turned to stone, his face marble-like in its sorrow. He had led the delicate, opulent existence of a young man of lineage and wealth, a life exquisite for its freedom from sordid anxieties, its charming boyish indifference; and now, for the first time, he became aware of Destiny\u2019s dreadful mystery, of Doom\u2019s alarming significance.\\n\\nHow insane and grotesque it all appeared! Could it be that inscribed upon his hand, in characters unreadable to himself yet legible to another, there lay some horrible secret of guilt, some scarlet sign of crime? Was there no possible escape? Were we nothing better than chess pieces, shifted by an invisible force, vessels shaped by the potter\u2019s whim, for honour or for disgrace? His reason rebelled against the thought, yet he felt some catastrophe hovered above him, and that he had suddenly been summoned to bear an intolerable load. Actors are fortunate indeed. They can decide whether they will show themselves in tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or make merry, to laugh or to weep. In real life it is not so. Most men and women must act roles for which they possess no fitness. Our Guildensterns perform Hamlet for us, and our Hamlets must jest like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, yet the casting is wretched.\\n\\nAbruptly Mr. Podgers came into the room. At the sight of Lord Arthur he started, and his coarse, flabby face turned a sort of greenish yellow. The two men locked eyes, and for an instant there was silence.\\n\\n\u201cThe Duchess has left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and has asked me to fetch it,\u201d said Mr. Podgers at last. \u201cAh, there it is on the sofa! Good evening.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Podgers, I insist that you give me a direct answer to a question I am about to ask.\