          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d3ffd68d9d73f493bda70eb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t help it. It drove him crazy to think Becky Thatcher didn\u2019t even suspect he was alive. But she saw, and she knew she was winning, and she was glad to watch him suffer as she had.  Amy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted that he had things to do, that time was short. It didn\u2019t work\u2014the girl kept chirping. Tom thought, \u201cOh, hang her, will I never get rid of her?\u201d Finally he had to be off to those errands\u2014and she innocently said she would be \u201caround\u201d when school let out. He hurried away, hating her for it.  \u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in town but that Saint Louis fop who thinks he dresses so fine and is high society! All right, I beat you the first day you ever saw this town, mister, and I\u2019ll beat you again! Just wait till I catch you! I\u2019ll just\u2014\u201d  He acted out a fight with an imaginary boy\u2014pounding the air, kicking and gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You yell enough, do you? There, let that teach you!\u201d He finished the imaginary whipping to his satisfaction.  Tom bolted home at noon. His conscience couldn\u2019t stand any more of Amy\u2019s thankful cheer, and his jealousy couldn\u2019t take any more of the other torment. Becky went back to looking at pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes crawled and no Tom showed up to suffer, her victory started to fade and she lost interest; she grew solemn and distracted, then sad. Two or three times she perked up at a footstep, but it was wishful thinking. No Tom came. At last she felt completely miserable and wished she hadn\u2019t pushed things so far. When poor Alfred, seeing he was losing her and not knowing why, kept saying, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a jolly one! Look at this!\u201d she finally lost patience and cried, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care for them!\u201d She burst into tears, stood up, and walked away.  Alfred caught up beside her and was about to try comforting her, but she cried, \u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d  So he stopped, not knowing what he had done\u2014she had said she would look at pictures all through the noon break\u2014and she walked on, crying. Then Alfred went pondering into the empty schoolhouse. He felt humiliated and angry. He easily guessed the truth\u2014the girl had used him just to work out her spite on Tom Sawyer.\"}"
