          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"431c32647584dc6ab6074f4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he had wronged no one, jailed no one; far from collecting what he was owed with harsh insistence, he had willingly given it up, casting himself into a world that offered him no favor, earning his own place there, and working for his own bread. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the bankrupt and tangled estate under written orders to spare the people, to give them what little could be given\u2014whatever firewood the heavy creditors allowed in winter, and whatever produce could be rescued from their clutches in summer\u2014and no doubt he had stated and proved this for his own protection, so it would inevitably come to light now.\\n\\nThis supported the desperate decision Charles Darnay had begun to form: he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor from the old tale, winds and currents had carried him within the pull of the Loadstone Rock, which now drew him irresistibly, and he had to go. Everything that rose in his mind pushed him onward, faster and faster, more steadily toward that terrible attraction. His buried unease had been that wicked purposes were being pursued in his unhappy homeland by wicked hands, and that he, who could not help but know he was better than they, was absent when he should have been trying to stop the bloodshed and uphold mercy and humanity. With that unease half suppressed and half accusing him, he had found himself contrasted with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so strong; that comparison (damaging to himself) was immediately followed by the scorn of Monseigneur, which hurt him deeply, and by Stryver\u2019s sneers, which were especially coarse and wounding for long-standing reasons. After those came Gabelle\u2019s letter: the plea of an innocent prisoner, facing death, to his justice, honor, and good name.\\n\\nHis decision was made. He must go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was pulling him, and he had to keep sailing until he struck it. He knew of no rock; he barely saw any danger. The motive with which he had done what he had done, even if left unfinished, presented itself to him as something that would be gratefully recognized in France when he appeared to claim it. Then that shining vision of doing good, so often the hopeful mirage of generous minds, rose before him, and in that illusion he even saw himself wielding some influence to guide this ferocious Revolution that was spinning disastrously out of control.\"}"
