          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7da602a5bb6cb33a4fd3d7eb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you talking about Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I am. I never imagined anyone else\u2014and I thought you understood. When we discussed him, it seemed perfectly clear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot entirely,\u201d Emma replied with forced calm. \u201cEverything you said then seemed to refer to another man. I could almost swear you mentioned Mr. Frank Churchill. I remember you praising the service Mr. Frank Churchill did you when he protected you from the gipsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Miss Woodhouse, how you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I clearly remember what I said. I told you I did not wonder at your attachment\u2014that after what he had done for you, it was perfectly natural. You agreed, speaking warmly about that service, even describing how you felt when he stepped in to save you. That impression is vivid in my mind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I remember what you mean; but I was thinking of something entirely different at the time. I did not mean the gipsies or Mr. Frank Churchill. No!\u201d\u2014her voice rose\u2014\u201cI was thinking of a much more precious moment\u2014when Mr. Knightley came and asked me to dance after Mr. Elton refused, when no one else would stand up with me. That was the kind act; that was the noble generosity; that was the service that made me realize how far above every other person he was.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d exclaimed Emma. \u201cThis is the most unfortunate, most dreadful mistake! What can be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have encouraged me if you had understood me?\u201d asked Harriet. \u201cStill, I cannot be worse off than if the other gentleman had been the one\u2014and now, it might be possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe paused. Emma could not speak.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot blame you, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d Harriet resumed, \u201cfor seeing a huge difference between the two, for me or anyone. You must think one far more beyond my reach than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing\u2014if\u2014strange as it seems\u2014yet they were your own words, that stranger things have happened and matches of greater inequality than between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. So it appears such a thing as this may have happened before\u2014and if I should be so extremely fortunate as to\u2014if Mr. Knightley truly\u2014if he is not troubled by the difference, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not oppose it or try to create obstacles.\u201d\"}"
