          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1800113ecf8bb79e766e3df8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I said, \u201cyou might tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have it. All the nobles do.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just how he was. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something, he wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week and it wouldn\u2019t matter.\\n\\nHe\u2019d already settled the coat of arms business, so now he started to finish the rest of that part of the work, which was planning a mournful inscription\u2014said Jim had to have one, like they all did. He made up several, wrote them on a paper, and read them out:\\n\\n1. Here a captive heart burst.\\n\\n2. Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and friends, wore out his sorrowful life.\\n\\n3. Here a lonely heart broke, and a worn spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity.\\n\\n4. Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV.\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook as he read them, and he almost broke down. When he finished he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall\u2014they were all so good. Finally he said Jim could scratch them all. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch that much onto the logs with a nail, and he didn\u2019t even know how to make letters; but Tom said he would outline them for him, and then Jim would only have to follow the lines. Then pretty soon he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, the logs won\u2019t do; they don\u2019t have log walls in a dungeon. We have to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll bring a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than logs. He said it would take him such an awfully long time to carve them into a rock he\u2019d never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he checked how me and Jim were getting along with the pens. It was tediously hard, slow work, and it didn\u2019t give my hands any chance to heal from the sores, and we hardly made any progress; so Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
