          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2c5666c4a4a7ad2a67c44710\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cListen carefully; I have learned the language of despair, know it by heart, because I am Despair\u2014joyous, triumphant Despair. Yet those words are untrue, for the wave may be dark but it is not bitter. We lie down, close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake we are free. Come now, no more delay, slow one! See the pleasant potion! Look, I am a spirit of good, not a mortal maid calling you, and with persuasive tones (oh, that they might persuade you!) I say, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I fixed my eyes on his face, and his exquisite beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, his gentle yet earnest expression of pleading and wonder even before he spoke, changed my taut emotions, taking away the hardness of despair and filling me with the softest grief. I saw his eyes grow wet as he took both my hands in his; and sitting down beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sorrowful act into which you would lead me, dearest friend, and your torment must be deep indeed to fill you with such wretched thoughts. You long for death yet fear it, and you wish me to be your companion. But I have less courage than you, and even with you I dare not die. Listen to me, and then decide if you should convince me of your plan, even if with the overpowering eloquence of despair you could make black death so inviting that fair heaven would seem darkness. Listen, I beg you, to the words of one who has himself nurtured desperate thoughts, who longed impatiently for death, but at last crushed that phantom underfoot and blunted its sting. Come; as you have played Despair with me, I will play Una with you and lead you unharmed from his dark cavern. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words untouched by selfish passion.\\n\\n\u201cWe cannot tell what this vast world means, with its strange mix of good and evil. Yet we have been placed here and ordered to live and hope. I do not know what we are meant to hope for, but there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that search is our earthly duty. If misfortune strikes us, we must fight her; we must push her aside and still press on to find whatever it is in our nature to desire. Whether this glimpse of future good prepares us for another existence I do not know, or whether it simply means that we, as laborers in God\u2019s vineyard, must lend a hand to smooth the way for those who come after us.\u201d\"}"
