          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"255269a4d4b5dedd1ee0db44\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would be settled among people who loved her and were wiser than she was; secluded enough to be safe, yet busy enough to stay cheerful. She would never be led into temptation, nor left exposed for it to find her. She would be respectable and happy; and Emma conceded she was the luckiest creature alive to have inspired such steady and persistent devotion in a man like that\u2014or, if not quite the luckiest, second only to Emma herself.\\n\\nHarriet, necessarily drawn away by her obligations to the Martins, spent less and less time at Hartfield, which was no cause for regret. The closeness between her and Emma had to diminish; their friendship needed to settle into a calmer goodwill, and, fortunately, what ought to happen and must happen already seemed to be unfolding in the most gradual, natural way.\\n\\nBefore September ended, Emma accompanied Harriet to church and watched her hand placed in Robert Martin\u2019s with complete satisfaction, untouched even by memories connected with Mr. Elton standing before them. Perhaps, indeed, at that moment she could barely see Mr. Elton except as the clergyman whose blessing at the altar might next be for herself. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, the last of the three couples to become engaged, were the first to marry.\\n\\nJane Fairfax had already left Highbury and returned to the comforts of her beloved home with the Campbells. The Mr. Churchills were also in London, merely waiting for November.\\n\\nEmma and Mr. Knightley chose the intervening month as boldly as they dared. They had decided their marriage should be completed while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, so they could take the planned fortnight\u2019s trip to the seaside. John and Isabella, and every other friend, agreed with the decision. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how could they win his consent? He had never yet mentioned their marriage except as something far off.\\n\\nWhen first approached on the subject, he was so miserable they nearly lost hope. A second reference, however, caused less pain. He began to think it would happen and that he could not prevent it\u2014a very encouraging step in the mind\u2019s journey toward acceptance. Still, he was not content. In fact, he seemed so unhappy that his daughter lost her nerve. She could not bear to see him suffering, to know he imagined himself neglected; and though her reason nearly accepted both Mr. Knightleys\u2019 assurance that once the event was over his anguish would soon pass, she hesitated\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
