          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a6f35142f4c80b66fd9428ad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After they joined in the general grief over the dreadful outcome Elizabeth saw as nearly certain, though Miss Bennet could not claim it was totally impossible, Elizabeth pressed on. \u201cTell me everything I have not yet heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no warning before the elopement? They must have seen them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted he had often suspected a preference, especially from Lydia, but nothing that alarmed him. I feel so sorry for him. His manners were as attentive and kind as possible. He was on his way to us to express his concern before he imagined they had not gone to Scotland; when that fear began to spread, it sped his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny sure Wickham would not marry? Did he know they meant to run off? Had Colonel Forster spoken to Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Colonel Forster questioned him, Denny denied knowing anything of their plan and would not share his real opinion. He did not repeat that he believed they would not marry, and from that I hope he might have been misunderstood earlier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster appeared, none of you doubted that they were actually married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea enter our heads? I felt a little uneasy\u2014somewhat afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage, because I knew his conduct had not always been quite right. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only saw how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then claimed, with very natural pride at knowing more than the rest of us, that Lydia\u2019s last letter had prepared her for such a step. It seems she had known of their being in love for many weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I do not think so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think badly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit he no longer spoke as well of Wickham as he once did. He thought him imprudent and extravagant; and since this sad event, it is said he left Meryton heavily in debt, though I hope that is false.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secret, if we had told what we knew about him, this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut to reveal someone\u2019s past faults without knowing their present feelings seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
