          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c35895785c1680cab8132e48\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters in the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island seldom turn truly harsh, and in autumn it is an uncommon occurrence to think a fire necessary. Nevertheless, around the middle of October, 18\u2014, there came a day of exceptional chill. Just before sunset I fought my way through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut, a place I had neglected for several weeks\u2014my home being then in Charleston, nine miles distant from the island, with the means of going back and forth far inferior to those of the present day. Reaching the hut, I knocked, as I usually did, and receiving no answer, hunted for the key in its usual hiding place, unlocked the door, and stepped in. A cheerful fire was roaring on the hearth, a rarity, yet a welcome one. I shed my overcoat, sank into an arm-chair beside the crackling logs, and waited serenely for my hosts to appear.\\n\\nAfter nightfall they arrived, greeting me with the warmest hospitality. Jupiter, his smile stretching from ear to ear, bustled about preparing marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his moods\u2014what else shall I call them?\u2014of fervor. He had discovered an unfamiliar bivalve forming a new genus and, more than that, had tracked down and captured, with Jupiter\u2019s help, a scarab\u00e6us he was convinced entirely novel, though he wished to hear my opinion on the morrow.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I said, rubbing my hands over the blaze and consigning the whole tribe of scarab\u00e6i to perdition.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I had known you were here!\u201d replied Legrand. \u201cBut it\u2019s been so long since last we met, and how could I guess you would visit me tonight of all nights? On my way home I encountered Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and, quite foolishly, I lent him the insect; so you cannot see it until morning. Stay overnight, and I\u2019ll send Jup down for it at sunrise. It is the most beautiful thing on earth!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat? Sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNonsense! No\u2014the bug. It glows a brilliant gold, about the size of a large hickory-nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back and another, somewhat longer, near the other. The antenn\u00e6 are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey aint no tin in him, Massa Will, I keep tellin you,\u201d Jupiter broke in; \u201cde bug a goole bug, solid, ebery part of him, inside and out, ceptin his wing\u2014nebber felt so hebby a bug in all my born days.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
