          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"05d5e0910d1d1d6f42d5c21d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not everyone would have been so determined and kind as she was, refusing to accept Jane\u2019s response; but she insisted she would not write that sort of refusal yesterday, as Jane wanted. She would wait\u2014and sure enough, last evening it was settled that Jane would go. Quite a surprise to me! I had absolutely no idea. Jane pulled Mrs. Elton aside and told her outright that, after considering the benefits of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s position, she had decided to accept it. I did not know a word until it was all arranged.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us; Mrs. Elton insisted we come. It was arranged like that on the hill while we were strolling with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You simply must all spend the evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I absolutely must have you all come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he declined from the start. Even though I thought he would attend, since Mrs. Elton said she wouldn\u2019t let him off, he didn\u2019t; but my mother and Jane and I were all there, and we had a very pleasant evening. With such kind friends, Miss Woodhouse, one must always have a good time, though everyone seemed rather worn out after the morning\u2019s outing. Even fun, you know, can be tiring\u2014and I cannot say any of them appeared to enjoy it very much. Still, I will always remember it as a very pleasant gathering and feel deeply grateful to the kind friends who included me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you didn\u2019t realize it, had been making up her mind all day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the moment comes, it must be unwelcome to her and to all her friends\u2014but I hope her engagement will have every possible consolation\u2014I mean regarding the family\u2019s character and manners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, everything that could make her happy is there. Besides the Sucklings and Bragges, there isn\u2019t another nursery establishment so generous and elegant in all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge is the most delightful woman\u2014a lifestyle almost equal to Maple Grove\u2014and as for the children, except for the little Sucklings and little Bragges, nowhere are there such sweet, well-bred children. Jane will be treated with such respect and kindness! It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure. And her salary! I truly cannot bring myself to tell you her salary, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
