          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"385960babfc428358c4e25cc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You don\u2019t know anything about me unless you\u2019ve read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but that doesn\u2019t matter. That book was written by Mr. Mark Twain, and he mostly told the truth. He stretched a few things, but mostly it was true. That\u2019s nothing special, though. I never met anybody who didn\u2019t lie sooner or later, except Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly\u2014and Mary and the Widow Douglas are all written about in that book, which is mostly true, with a few exaggerations like I said before.\\n\\nHere\u2019s how that book ends: Tom and I found the money the robbers hid in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars each\u2014all gold. It was a fearful lot of money when it was piled up. Judge Thatcher took it and invested it, and it brought us a dollar a day each all year long\u2014more than a person could figure what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me in as her son and said she would civilize me; but it was hard living in that house all the time, with the widow so solemn and proper in everything she did. When I couldn\u2019t take it anymore, I ran off. I got back into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead again, and I was free and content. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he was starting a band of robbers, and I could join if I went back to the widow and behaved. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, and she called me a lot of other things too, but she didn\u2019t mean any harm. She put me in those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and sweat and feel squeezed in. Then the old routine started over. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to show up on time. When you got to the table you couldn\u2019t start eating right away; you had to wait for the widow to bow her head and mumble over the food, even though there wasn\u2019t anything wrong with it\u2014except everything was cooked separately. In a barrel of odds and ends it\u2019s different; things get mixed, the juices trade places, and the food tastes better.\\n\\nAfter supper she took out her book and taught me about Moses and the bulrushes, and I was eager to learn all about him; but pretty soon she let slip that Moses had been dead a long time. After that I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t put much stock in dead people.\"}"
