          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5aa0a8780f7c427b1bc61acc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 19th. This morning, around nine o\u2019clock, to my great relief, the moon\u2019s surface lying terribly close and my fear at its peak, the pump of my condenser finally showed clear signs that the atmosphere was changing. By ten o\u2019clock I had reason to believe the air had grown considerably denser. By eleven, the apparatus required almost no effort, and at noon, after some hesitation, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. When nothing bad happened, I threw open the rubber chamber and detached it from the car. As expected, spasms and agonizing headaches struck immediately after such a rash, dangerous experiment. Yet since these and other breathing difficulties were not severe enough to threaten my life, I resolved to endure them as best I could, knowing I left them behind with every moment I descended toward the moon\u2019s denser layers. Still, my descent remained violently fast, and it soon became terrifyingly clear that, although I was likely right about an atmosphere proportional in density to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had been wrong in assuming this density, even at the surface, would support the heavy load inside my balloon car. It should have done so, in equal measure with Earth, given that actual weight on either world should match the degree of atmospheric compression. That it did not was evident from my precipitous plummet; why it failed must relate to those possible geological disturbances I mentioned earlier. Regardless, I was now extremely close to the planet and descending with dreadful velocity. I lost no time in throwing overboard my ballast, then my water kegs, then the condensing apparatus and rubber chamber, and finally every item in the car. It made no difference. I continued to fall with horrible speed and was now less than half a mile above the surface. As a last resort, after discarding my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car loose from the balloon\u2014it weighed no small amount\u2014and, clutching the net with both hands, I scarcely had time to note that the whole countryside was crowded with tiny dwellings before I plunged headlong into the very center of a fantastical-looking city and the midst of a vast crowd of ugly little people. None of them said a word or made the slightest effort to help me; they merely stood there like fools, grinning absurdly, staring sideways at me and my balloon with their arms akimbo.\"}"
