          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"766a94a16dffe5ec80bba469\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you doing?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. It was after sunrise, and I had been sleeping hard. Pap stood over me looking sour and sick. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t remember anything about what he\u2019d been up to, so I said:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right. Don\u2019t stand there jabbering all day; go see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll come in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I headed up the riverbank. I noticed pieces of branches and other trash floating by, and some loose bark; that told me the river was rising. I figured I\u2019d be having a grand time if I were over in town. Every June rise brought luck for me, because as soon as the water starts rising, cordwood and logs come floating along\u2014sometimes a dozen together. All you have to do is grab them and sell them to the wood-yards and sawmill.\\n\\nI walked up the bank, one eye open for Pap and the other eye out for what the river might bring. Suddenly a canoe showed up\u2014a real beauty, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I dived headfirst off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam hard for the canoe. I half expected someone to be lying in it, because people liked to trick others that way: they\u2019d play possum, and when someone pulled a skiff up close, they\u2019d sit up and laugh at him. But not this time. It was really a drift canoe, and I climbed in and paddled her to shore. I figured the old man would be pleased\u2014she was worth ten dollars. But when I reached land Pap still wasn\u2019t in sight, and as I was pushing her into a little creek like a gully, covered with vines and willows, another idea came to me: I\u2019d hide her well, and instead of running into the woods when I made my getaway, I\u2019d go fifty miles downriver and settle in one spot, so I wouldn\u2019t have such a hard time traveling on foot.\\n\\nThe creek was close to the shanty, and I kept thinking I could hear the old man coming, but I got the canoe hidden. Then I stepped out and looked past a clump of willows, and there he was, down the path a bit, taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
