          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3841c8505624befe1b414866\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that the whole of it? That is not much! Many stand under the Republic\u2019s anger and must watch through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. I must be, as I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry has answered all the questions. He has stepped down and stands with his hand on the coach door, talking to a group of officials. They slowly stroll around the coach and slowly climb onto the box to inspect the few bags on the roof; peasants lingering nearby press closer to the doors and stare greedily inside. A small child in its mother\u2019s arms has its short arm stretched out so it can touch the wife of an aristocrat who has already gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay we depart, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou may depart. Forward, my postilions! Safe travels!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.\u2014And the first danger is past!\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry says this again, clasping his hands and looking up. Terror fills the carriage; someone is sobbing; the unconscious traveler breathes heavily.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not going too slowly? Can they not be made to go faster?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would look like fleeing, my darling. I must not push them too hard; it would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see if we are pursued!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, no one follows us.\u201d\\n\\nHouses pass in twos and threes, lonely farms, ruined structures, dye-works, tanneries, open fields, rows of bare trees. Hard uneven pavement lies below us, deep soft mud on each side. Sometimes we veer into the bordering mud to avoid the stones that rattle and jolt us; sometimes we get stuck in ruts and sloughs there. Our impatience torments us so much that in our frantic fear we think of jumping out and running\u2014hiding\u2014doing anything but remaining still.\\n\\nOut of the open land, back again among ruined buildings, lonely farms, dye-works, tanneries, cottages in twos and threes, avenues of bare trees. Have these men tricked us, leading us back by another route? Is this not the same place twice? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see if we are pursued! Quiet! The posting-house.\\n\\nOur four horses are removed slowly; slowly, the coach sits in the narrow street, without horses and seemingly doomed to never move again; slowly, the new horses appear, one after another; slowly, the fresh postilions arrive, sucking and braiding their whip lashes; slowly, the old postilions count their money, make mistakes, and end up dissatisfied.\"}"
