          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14d706ac7953da97431e7815\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Early in the morning, slipping once more past my creditors, I hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall and spent the little cash I had on volumes about Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Back home, I devoured them whenever I could, and soon felt confident I understood enough to carry out my plan. During this time I worked hard to appease the three creditors who had vexed me so much. I did it partly by selling some household furniture to cover half their claim, and partly by promising to pay the rest once I finished a small project I told them I was planning, for which I asked their help. Since they were ignorant men, they agreed easily.\\n\\nWith matters settled, I quietly arranged with my wife to sell what property we had left and to borrow small sums under various pretenses, not worrying about future repayment, until we had a good amount of ready money. Little by little I bought fine cambric muslin in twelve-yard pieces, twine, some caoutchouc varnish, and a large deep wicker basket made to order, along with everything else needed to build a balloon of unusual size. I told my wife to sew it as quickly as possible and instructed her exactly how to proceed. Meanwhile, I turned the twine into netting of the right size, fitted it with a hoop and the necessary cords, and bought a quadrant, a compass, a spyglass, a barometer with important modifications, and two more obscure astronomical instruments. By night I moved to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam five iron-bound casks holding about fifty gallons each and one larger cask, six ten-foot tinned tubes three inches in diameter, a quantity of a certain metallic substance or semi-metal I will not name, and a dozen demijohns of a common acid. The gas produced from these materials has never been made by anyone else, or at least never used for such a purpose. I would gladly share the secret if it were mine, but it belongs to a citizen of Nantz in France, who revealed it to me on certain conditions.\"}"
