          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d6f542816adfb4fc16cf43d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without stopping to think, and barely aware of her own emotions, Elizabeth finished this letter, grabbed the other, and with the greatest impatience opened it and read the following, written a day after the first had ended.\\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received my hurried letter. I hope this one is clearer, but although I am not pressed for time, my head is so muddled that I cannot promise to be coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what I mean to say, but I have bad news that cannot wait. Reckless as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now desperate to know it has happened, for we have far too much reason to fear they did not go to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, only a few hours after the express. Though Lydia\u2019s short letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe they were going to Gretna Green, Denny let something slip suggesting he believed W. never meant to go there or to marry Lydia at all. This was reported to Colonel F., who, immediately alarmed, set off from B. to follow their route. He followed them easily to Clapham, but no farther; for when they entered that town, they switched to a hackney coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known after this is that they were seen continuing on the London road. I do not know what to think. After making every inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. went on to Hertfordshire, anxiously repeating his questions at every turnpike, and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but with no success; no such people had been seen. With the warmest concern, he came to Longbourn and shared his fears with us in the kindest way. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F., yet no one can blame them. Our distress, dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother expect the worst, but I cannot think so poorly of him. Many circumstances might make it preferable for them to marry privately in town rather than pursue their first plan; and even if he could form such a scheme against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections, which seems unlikely, can I think her so lost to all sense? Impossible! Yet I am distressed to find that Colonel F. will not count on their being married: he shook his head when I expressed hope and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\u201d\"}"
