          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a7ebff3a0693648c416646ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I hasten now to the portion of my history most touching, for I am about to recount incidents that impressed me with emotions that transformed me from what I had been into what I have become.\\n\\nSpring advanced swiftly; the days grew fine and the skies clear. It astonished me to see the region, formerly dreary and desolate, suddenly adorned with exquisite flowers and rich verdure. My senses were indulged and revived by a thousand delightful fragrances and an equal number of lovely sights.\\n\\nOne day when the cottagers paused from their labor\u2014the old father playing his guitar while the children listened\u2014I noticed that Felix\u2019s face was sorrowful beyond expression. He sighed repeatedly, and once the old man stopped his music; by his gestures I assumed he was questioning the reason for his son\u2019s melancholy. Felix answered in a cheerful tone, and the old man was about to resume his playing when a knock sounded at the door.\\n\\nA lady appeared, mounted on horseback and attended by a countryman acting as her guide. She wore dark attire and a thick black veil. Agatha put a question to her, but the stranger simply responded by uttering, in a gentle voice, Felix\u2019s name. Her accent was musical yet unlike any of my companions. Hearing his name, Felix hastened toward her; she threw back her veil as soon as she beheld him, revealing a face of celestial beauty and expression. Her hair, a lustrous raven hue, was intricately braided; her eyes were dark yet mild, though lively; her features were symmetrical, and her complexion marvelously fair, each cheek touched with a charming blush.\\n\\nFelix seemed enraptured at the sight of her. Every trace of grief vanished, replaced by a rapture I could scarcely have imagined. His eyes shone, his cheeks flushed with joy, and in that moment I thought him almost as beautiful as the stranger. She seemed moved differently; brushing tears from her lovely eyes, she extended her hand to Felix, who pressed it ardently and called her, so far as I could discern, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand the words, yet smiled. He helped her dismount, dismissed the guide, and led her into the cottage. After some talk between Felix and his father, the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet and was about to kiss his hand, but he raised her and embraced her warmly.\\n\\nIt soon became clear that although the stranger spoke in articulate tones, possessing a language of her own, neither she nor the cottagers could understand the other.\"}"
