          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"99fff8feeb8aa6d9744a1653\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s last reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was more crowded than ever. Six Cabinet Ministers had arrived straight from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e in their decorations, every beautiful woman wore her most fashionable gown, and at the end of the picture gallery stood Princess Sophia of Carlsruhe, a heavy, Tartar-looking lady with tiny black eyes and magnificent emeralds, speaking dreadful French at the top of her voice and laughing wildly at everything said to her. It was certainly a remarkable mix of people. Splendid peeresses chatted amiably with fiery Radicals, famous preachers brushed shoulders with noted skeptics, and a whole cluster of bishops kept trailing a portly prima donna from room to room; on the staircase several Royal Academicians, disguised as artists, were posted, and it was said that at one point the supper room was absolutely packed with geniuses. In truth, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s finest nights, and the Princess remained until nearly half past eleven.\\n\\nAs soon as she left, Lady Windermere returned to the picture gallery, where a celebrated political economist was solemnly explaining the scientific theory of music to an indignant Hungarian virtuoso, and began talking to the Duchess of Paisley. She looked wonderfully beautiful with her elegant ivory throat, her large blue forget-me-not eyes, and her heavy coils of golden hair. They were real golden hair\u2014not the pale straw shade that now steals the gracious name of gold, but the gold woven into sunbeams or hidden in strange amber; it gave her face the framing of a saint, yet with no small measure of a sinner\u2019s allure. She was a fascinating study in psychology. Early in life she had discovered the important truth that nothing looks as much like innocence as indiscretion; and through a series of reckless escapades, half of them quite harmless, she had obtained all the privileges of a distinctive personality. She had more than once changed her husband\u2014Debrett lists three marriages\u2014but since she had never changed her lover, the world long ago stopped gossiping about her. She was now forty, childless, and possessed of that excessive passion for pleasure which is the secret to staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she looked around the room eagerly and said in her clear contralto voice, \u201cWhere is my palm reader?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour what, Gladys?\u201d exclaimed the Duchess, starting despite herself.\\n\\n\u201cMy palm reader, Duchess; I can\u2019t do without him just now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys! You are always so original,\u201d murmured the Duchess, trying to remember what exactly a palm reader was, and hoping it wasn\u2019t the same thing as a chiropodist.\\n\\n\u201cHe comes to examine my hand twice a week without fail,\u201d Lady Windermere continued, \u201cand he is endlessly fascinating about it.\u201d\"}"
