          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ccea7c0940e0817e187eba8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was in all this a peculiar charm that drew and entranced me. He seemed roused from a long, dreamlike slumber, feeling somewhat like one of the seven sleepers, or like Nourjahad in that delicate imitation of an eastern story: Diana was gone; his friends were altered or dead, and, on his awakening, I was the only being left for him to love on earth. How precious the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond became now that I had so beloved a companion for my wanderings. With my father I sought every delightful nook, whether on the islands or beside the tree-sheltered cascades; each shaded lane or dell tangled with underwood and fern. His conversation expanded my ideas. I felt as though I had been remade and bore all the vigor and brightness of a new creature: his arrival seemed to transport me from a cramped spot of earth into a universe without bounds for imagination and intellect. My life had once resembled a pleasant country brook, never meant to leave its native meadows, but, when its course was done, to be quietly swallowed and leave no trace. Now it appeared a varied river winding through a fruitful, exquisite landscape, ever shifting, ever fair. Alas! I did not foresee the desert it was soon to meet; the rocks that would rend its waters, and the hideous prospect that would be mirrored yet more grotesquely in its waves. Life glittered then; I began to learn to hope, and what brings sharper despair than hope destroyed? Is it not marvelous that sorrow should so swiftly track such celestial joy? I tasted from an enchanted cup, yet gall lay at the bottom of its slow-drawn sweetness. My heart brimmed with deep affection, yet its very profundity and fullness kept it serene. I never imagined that misery could spring from love; the lesson that all must eventually take was taught me in a fashion few are forced to receive. I mourn still\u2014must eternally mourn\u2014those brief months of Edenic bliss; I broke no command, I ate no forbidden fruit, yet I was mercilessly expelled. Alas! my companion did, and I was hurled down in his ruin. But I stray from my tale\u2014let sorrow arrive at its appointed moment; at this point in my narrative I may still speak of happiness. Three months slipped by in this enchanting communion when my aunt fell ill. I spent a whole month in her room tending her, yet her malady was mortal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for a time. Death is so dreadful to the living; the chains of habit are so strong, even when affection does not forge them, that the heart must writhe when they snap.\"}"
