          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"06bc50034eae0427a07edcc2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her colour altered, and she remained silent.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner had stationed herself a little to the rear; and when Elizabeth paused, he asked Mrs. Gardiner if she would favour him with an introduction to her companions. This was a politeness she had not expected; and she almost had to smother a smile at his seeking the acquaintance of some of the very persons his pride had spurned when he addressed herself. \u201cHow astonished he will be,\u201d she thought, \u201conce he learns who they are! At present he assumes they belong to the fashionable world.\u201d\\n\\nYet the introduction was made at once; and as Mrs. Gardiner explained their relationship to her, she allowed herself a covert glance at him, curious how he might take it, and half expecting him to flee at top speed from such degrading associates. That the connection startled him was unmistakable: nevertheless, he bore it with resolution; instead of withdrawing, he turned back with them and entered into conversation with Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth could not help feeling satisfaction, could not help exulting. It soothed her that he should perceive she had relations who need inspire no shame. She listened with the greatest attention to every exchange between them, delighting in each remark, each phrase of her uncle that displayed his sense, his taste, or his good breeding.\\n\\nBefore long, the talk drifted to fishing; and she heard Mr. Darcy, with perfect courtesy, invite Mr. Gardiner to fish there whenever he pleased during his stay in the neighbourhood, offering also to provide him with tackle, and indicating the stretches of the stream where sport was generally best. Mrs. Gardiner, walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, cast her a look that spoke her astonishment. Elizabeth said nothing, but could not be more gratified; the compliment must be meant wholly for herself. Still, her amazement was extreme; over and over she asked herself, \u201cWhy is he so changed? What can be the cause? It cannot be on _my_ account, it cannot be for _my_ sake that his manners are thus softened. My reproaches at Hunsford could not produce an alteration like this. It is impossible that he should still cherish love for me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter they had proceeded some way thus, the two ladies leading, the two gentlemen following, upon returning to their former arrangement after descending to the river\u2019s edge to examine more closely a curious aquatic plant, a small change occurred. It sprang from Mrs. Gardiner, who, wearied by the morning\u2019s exertion, found Elizabeth\u2019s support insufficient, and therefore chose to lean upon her husband instead.\"}"
        }
