          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4c499154e8aa99ef97104d08\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I told myself right off I didn\u2019t aim to get tangled up in any business like that.\\n\\nCome morning, when I went downstairs, the parlor was closed tight and the watchers had cleared out. Nobody hung around but the family, the widow Bartley, and our lot. I studied their faces, trying to read if anything had taken place, but couldn\u2019t make out a thing.\\n\\nAround midday the undertaker arrived with his helper. They set the coffin in the center of the room on two chairs, arranged our chairs in rows, and borrowed extras from neighbors until the hall, the parlor, and the dining room were crammed. I noticed the coffin lid was fixed as before, but I didn\u2019t dare peek underneath it with folks nearby.\\n\\nThen people started pouring in. The beaters and the girls settled in the front row at the coffin\u2019s head, and for half an hour the crowd filed by slow, single file, pausing to gaze on the dead man\u2019s face, some shedding a tear. It stayed mighty still and solemn, except for the girls and the beaters holding their handkerchiefs to their eyes, heads bowed, sobbing a little. No other noise but shoes scraping the floor and noses blowing\u2014the sort of thing that gets noisier at funerals, unless it\u2019s church.\\n\\nOnce the place was packed, the undertaker slipped around in his black gloves with his smooth, soothing style, giving final touches, arranging folks and furniture so everything sat tidy and easy, and making no more racket than a cat. He never spoke a word; he shifted folks, squeezed in those arriving late, opened passageways, and did it all with nods and hand signs, then stationed himself against the wall. He was the softest, glidiest, stealthiest fellow I ever laid eyes on; there wasn\u2019t any more grin to him than to a ham.\\n\\nThey\u2019d borrowed a melodeon\u2014a squeamish one\u2014and once all was set a young woman sat down to play it. It screeched and whimpered, and everybody joined in the singing, and Peter was the only one who caught a break, if you ask me. Then Reverend Hobson started up, slow and solemn, and as soon as he did the wildest uproar bust loose down in the cellar you ever heard. It was just one dog, but he kicked up a powerful hullabaloo and kept at it steady; the parson stood there over the coffin waiting\u2014you couldn\u2019t even hear yourself think.\"}"
