{"request_id":"b1c8bf3da8c745310970d1fc","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to learn that no misfortune has attended the launch of the enterprise you viewed with such gloomy expectations. I reached this place yesterday, and my foremost duty is to inform my beloved sister of my safety and mounting confidence in the triumph of my project.

I am now well north of London, and as I move through the streets of Petersburgh, a keen northern breeze brushes my cheeks, steadying my nerves and filling me with delight. Do you grasp this sensation? This wind, having journeyed from the realms toward which I press, offers me a taste of those icy regions. Roused by this promise-laden gust, my reveries grow more intense and luminous. I strive in vain to accept that the pole is merely a domain of frost and barrenness; it ever arises before my imagination as a land of loveliness and joy. There, Margaret, the sun remains eternally in view, its wide orb skimming the horizon and shedding unending splendour. There—forgive me, sister, if I place some reliance on those navigators who went before me—there snow and ice are banished; and, gliding over tranquil waters, we may be carried to a land surpassing every known region on the inhabited earth in marvels and beauty. Its productions and features may be without parallel, as are certainly the motions of the heavenly bodies in those unexplored solitudes. What wonders may not await us in a country of perpetual light? I may uncover the marvelous force that draws the compass needle and may arrange a thousand celestial observations, which need only this voyage to harmonise their apparent irregularities forever. I shall sate my fervent curiosity with the sight of a portion of the globe never before visited, and perhaps set foot upon soil never yet pressed by human tread. These allurements are enough to subdue all dread of danger or death and to urge me to begin this arduous journey with the same joy a child feels when he launches a tiny boat with his holiday companions to explore his native river. But even if these guesses prove groundless, you cannot dispute the priceless service I shall render to all humanity, even to the latest age, by discovering a passage near the pole to those lands that currently require so many months to reach; or by determining the mystery of the magnet, which, if it can be solved at all, can be achieved only by such a venture as mine.

Such contemplations have calmed the uneasiness with which I opened this letter, and now my heart burns with an ardour that seems to lift me to the heavens, for nothing soothes the mind so much as a determined aim—a fixed point upon which the soul may steady its intellectual eye."}

