          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b105510339d66105233bd401\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It\u2019s strange, but everyone who disappears is supposedly seen in San Francisco. It must be a glorious city, full of all the charms of the next world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you think happened to Basil?\u201d asked Dorian, holding his Burgundy up to the light and marveling at how calmly he could talk about it.\\n\\n\u201cI have no idea. If Basil wants to hide, that\u2019s his business. If he\u2019s dead, I don\u2019t want to think about him. Death is the only thing that ever frightens me. I despise it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d the younger man asked wearily.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, raising the gilded trellis of an open vinaigrette box to his nose, \u201cnowadays we can survive everything except death. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts in the nineteenth century that can\u2019t be explained away. Let\u2019s have our coffee in the music room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran away with played Chopin beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was very fond of her. The house is rather lonely without her. Of course, marriage is merely a habit, a bad habit. But one feels the loss even of one\u2019s worst habits. Perhaps we miss those the most. They become such an essential part of one\u2019s character.\u201d\\n\\nDorian said nothing, but rose from the table, went into the next room, sat at the piano, and let his fingers wander over the black and white ivory keys. After the coffee was brought in, he stopped and looked over at Lord Henry. \u201cHarry, has it ever occurred to you that Basil was murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was very popular and always carried a Waterbury watch. Why would anyone murder him? He wasn\u2019t clever enough to have enemies. Of course, he had a brilliant talent for painting. A man can paint like Vel\u00e1zquez and still be terribly dull. Basil was rather dull. He only interested me once, when he told me years ago that he adored you and that you were the driving force of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was very fond of Basil,\u201d said Dorian, sadness in his voice. \u201cBut don\u2019t people say he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, some newspapers do. It doesn\u2019t seem at all likely to me. I know Paris has dreadful neighborhoods, but Basil wasn\u2019t the kind of man who would go to them. He had no curiosity. That was his biggest flaw.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry, if I told you I murdered Basil?\u201d asked the younger man.\"}"
