          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"503fbafa2d28095e1987d5bb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was handed over to you. You see I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s the truth, certainly,\u201d said Defarge. A nudge from his wife\u2019s elbow as she knitted and hummed had warned him to answer, but keep it short.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and it was from your care that his daughter took him, accompanied by a tidy brown gentleman\u2014what was his name?\u2014with a small wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company\u2019s bank\u2014over to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s the truth,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting memories!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t hear much about them now?\u201d asked the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIn fact,\u201d Madame broke in, looking up from her work and her soft song, \u201cwe never hear about them. We got the news of their safe arrival, and maybe another letter, or maybe two; but since then they have gone their way in life\u2014we ours\u2014and we have had no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so, madame,\u201d replied the spy. \u201cShe is going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing?\u201d echoed Madame. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have been married long ago. You English are cold, it seems to me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I\u2019m English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can tell from your tongue,\u201d said Madame. \u201cAnd what the tongue is, I suppose the person is.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not take the comparison as flattering, but he made the best of it and laughed it off. After finishing his cognac, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is going to be married. But not to an Englishman; to someone who, like herself, was born French. And speaking of Gaspard (ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!), it\u2019s curious that she\u2019s going to marry the nephew of the Marquis for whom Gaspard was lifted to that height of so many feet; in other words, the current Marquis. But he lives anonymously in England; he isn\u2019t a Marquis there; he\u2019s Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge kept knitting, but the news had a clear effect on her husband. No matter how he busied himself behind the little counter, striking a light and lighting his pipe, he was unsettled, and his hand wasn\u2019t steady. The spy wouldn\u2019t have been much of a spy if he hadn\u2019t noticed it, or stored it away.\\n\\nHaving scored at least this one point, whatever it might end up being worth, and with no customers coming in to help him to any other, Mr. Barsad paid for what he had drunk and took his leave, politely saying before he left that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
