          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9fe222a241b7e595a7c55feb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently resting his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm again after a brief silence, \u201cwhat do you think caused this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d Doctor Manette replied, \u201cthat there was a powerful and extraordinary resurgence of the chain of thoughts and memories that first caused the illness. Some intense, deeply distressing associations were vividly brought back, I think. It\u2019s likely he had long feared that those associations would resurface\u2014perhaps under certain circumstances, perhaps at a particular moment. He tried in vain to prepare himself; maybe the attempt to prepare made him less able to endure it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d asked Mr. Lorry, naturally hesitant.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked bleakly around the room, shook his head, and answered in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, about the future,\u201d Mr. Lorry hinted.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d the Doctor said, regaining his composure, \u201cI would have great hope. Since Heaven in its mercy restored him so quickly, I would have great hope. He gave way under the pressure of something complicated, long feared and dimly anticipated and fought against, and after the storm had broken and passed, he recovered. I would hope the worst is over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That\u2019s comforting. I\u2019m thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am thankful,\u201d the Doctor echoed, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other points,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201con which I\u2019m eager to be informed. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou can\u2019t do your friend a greater favor.\u201d The Doctor offered him his hand.\\n\\n\u201cTo the first, then. He\u2019s studious and unusually energetic; he throws himself with great zeal into acquiring professional knowledge, running experiments, and many other pursuits. Now, does he overdo it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think not. It may be the nature of his mind to need constant occupation. That may be partly inherent, partly the result of suffering. The less it was engaged with healthy things, the more danger there was of it turning elsewhere. He may have noticed this in himself and realized it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re sure he isn\u2019t strained too far?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m sure of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt that could happen easily. There\u2019s been a violent strain in one direction, and it needs a counterbalance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForgive me, as a persistent man of business. Suppose, for a moment, he *was* overworked; would the disorder flare up again?\u201d\"}"
