          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8917e044762640e700d51f54\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often mused on the old notion of the Bi-Part Soul and entertained myself with imagining a double Dupin\u2014one creative, the other analytic.\\n\\nDo not infer from this that I am unveiling some mystery or writing a romance. What I witnessed in the Frenchman was only the product of an excited, perhaps disordered intellect. Yet his comments at such times are best illustrated by an example.\\n\\nOne night we were wandering along a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. We both seemed absorbed in thought and had not exchanged a word for at least fifteen minutes when Dupin suddenly burst out:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very little fellow, no doubt; he would suit the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly,\u201d I answered without thinking, not at first realizing\u2014so wrapped up was I in my reflections\u2014how uncannily he had echoed my musings. The next instant I recovered myself, and my amazement was immense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said solemnly, \u201cthis passes my understanding. I confess I am astonished and can scarcely trust my senses. How could you know I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d Here I halted, to make sure whether he really knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy stop? You were observing inwardly that his tiny figure disqualified him for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been reflecting on. Chantilly was formerly a cobbler in the Rue St. Denis who, seized with stage-mania, had attempted the role of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy and been famously lampooned for his trouble.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is one\u2014by which you have plumbed my mind in this matter.\u201d In truth I was even more startled than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of soles lacked sufficient stature for Xerxes et id genus omne.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer! You amaze me\u2014I know no fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who jostled you as we entered this street\u2014perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nThen I remembered that a fruiterer, balancing a large basket of apples on his head, had nearly knocked me over by accident as we left the Rue C\u2014\u2014 for the lane where we now stood; yet how that encounter connected with Chantilly remained utterly beyond me.\"}"
        }
