          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dc629619f34f400eb91b04a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had nothing to hold, he pressed the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left into the hollow of his right, then ran a hand over his bearded chin, cycling through the motions without a pause. Bringing him back from the drift he always fell into after speaking was like calling a fragile person out of a faint, or trying, in hopes of some revelation, to hold the spirit of a dying man. \u201cDid you ask me my name?\u201d \u201cOf course I did.\u201d \u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d \u201cIs that all?\u201d \u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d He made a tired sound that was neither sigh nor groan and bent to work again, until the quiet was broken once more. \u201cYou\u2019re not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked, staring at him. His hollow eyes turned to Defarge, as if to pass the question to him; but when no help came, they dropped to the floor before returning to the questioner. \u201cI\u2019m not a shoemaker by trade? No, I wasn\u2019t a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d He slipped away again, for minutes, all the while repeating those measured movements with his hands. His eyes finally drifted back to the face they had left; when they focused on it, he jolted, as though waking from sleep and picking up last night\u2019s conversation. \u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and I gained it with great difficulty after a long time, and I\u2019ve been making shoes ever since.\u201d As he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still watching his face: \u201cMonsieur Manette, don\u2019t you remember me at all?\u201d The shoe fell to the floor, and he stared fixedly at the questioner. \u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry put a hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdon\u2019t you remember this man? Look at him. Look at me. Isn\u2019t there some old banker, old business, old servant, old time rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d The prisoner of many years stared in turn at Mr. Lorry and Defarge, and faint signs of sharp intelligence emerged at the center of his brow, forcing their way through the dark fog that had settled over him. They clouded again, they faded, they vanished\u2014but they had been there.\"}"
