          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f7e3739d2dcd80709c8358e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"About ten minutes later the tea bell rang, and when Virginia didn\u2019t come down, Mrs. Otis sent a footman upstairs to fetch her. After a short while he returned and reported that he couldn\u2019t find Miss Virginia anywhere. As she usually went out to the garden every evening to pick flowers for the dinner table, Mrs. Otis wasn\u2019t alarmed at first, but when six o\u2019clock struck and Virginia still didn\u2019t appear, she became genuinely agitated. She sent the boys out to look for her, while she and Mr. Otis searched every room in the house. At half past six the boys came back and said they couldn\u2019t find any sign of their sister. All of them were now in a state of intense anxiety and didn\u2019t know what to do, when Mr. Otis suddenly remembered that a few days earlier he had given a band of gypsies permission to camp in the park. He immediately set off for Blackfell Hollow, where he knew they were staying, taking his eldest son and two farmhands with him. The little Duke of Cheshire, utterly frantic with worry, pleaded to be allowed to go along, but Mr. Otis refused because he was afraid there might be trouble. When they arrived, however, they found that the gypsies had already left, and it was clear their departure had been rather hurried, as the fire was still burning and plates lay scattered on the grass. After sending Washington and the two men to scour the area, he raced home and sent telegrams to every police inspector in the county, instructing them to look for a little girl who had been kidnapped by tramps or gypsies. He then ordered his horse to be brought around, and, after insisting that his wife and the three boys sit down to dinner, rode off with a groom along the Ascot Road. He had only gone a couple of miles when he heard someone galloping behind him, and, looking back, saw the little Duke riding up on his pony, face flushed and bareheaded. \u201cI\u2019m terribly sorry, Mr. Otis,\u201d the boy gasped, \u201cbut I can\u2019t eat dinner while Virginia is missing. Please don\u2019t be angry with me; if you\u2019d let us get engaged last year, none of this would have happened. You won\u2019t send me back, will you? I can\u2019t go! I won\u2019t go!\u201d\\n\\nThe Minister couldn\u2019t help smiling at the handsome young rascal and was genuinely moved by his devotion to Virginia. Leaning down from his horse, he patted the boy kindly on the shoulders and said, \u201cWell, Cecil, if you won\u2019t go back, I suppose you\u2019ll have to come with me, but I\u2019ll need to get you a hat at Ascot.\u201d\"}"
