          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8629bde30f61a374ed3a8cba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo, no, that is to-morrow; and I especially wanted to see Knightley today for that very reason. Such a horribly broiling morning! I even walked across the fields\u201d\u2014she spoke as if deeply wronged\u2014\u201cwhich made everything worse. And then he was not at home! I assure you I am not pleased at all. No apology left, no message for me. The housekeeper insisted she knew nothing about my being expected. So strange! And no one could say where he had gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, perhaps to the Abbey Mill, perhaps into his woods. Miss Woodhouse, this is not like our friend Knightley! Can you explain it?\u201d\\n\\nEmma entertained herself by agreeing that it was indeed very strange and that she had not a word to defend him.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot imagine,\u201d said Mrs. Elton, feeling the insult as a wife ought to, \u201cI cannot imagine how he could behave so to you, of all people! The very last person one would expect him to forget! My dear Mr. E., he must have left a message for you, I am sure he did. Even Knightley could not be so wildly careless; his servants must have forgotten. Depend on it, that was what happened. It is very likely with the Donwell servants; I have often noticed they are terribly awkward and negligent. I would not have such a creature as his Harry waiting at our sideboard for anything. And as for Mrs. Hodges, Wright thinks very poorly of her. She promised Wright a recipe and never sent it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI met William Larkins,\u201d continued Mr. Elton, \u201cas I reached the house, and he told me I would not find his master at home, but I did not believe him. William seemed rather out of sorts. He said he could not imagine what was happening to his master lately, but he could hardly ever get a word with him. I have nothing to do with William\u2019s troubles, yet it is very important that I see Knightley today; and it is therefore a serious annoyance that I took this hot walk for nothing.\u201d\\n\\nEmma felt she could do nothing better than go home at once. She was probably wanted there even now; and Mr. Knightley might be kept from falling further into offense with Mr. Elton, if not with William Larkins.\\n\\nShe was pleased, as she took her leave, to find Miss Fairfax determined to walk out of the room with her and even downstairs; it gave her the chance she instantly used, to say,\"}"
