          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"596e3b74d2b96202561b113d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I hurried along, and we soon reached my college. Then I remembered, with a shiver, that the creature I had left in my rooms might still be there, alive and roaming about. I dreaded seeing the monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. So I begged Henry to stay a few minutes at the bottom of the stairs while I ran up to my rooms. My hand was already on the lock before I came to my senses. I stopped, shaking with cold dread. I flung the door open, the way children do when they expect a ghost waiting on the other side, but nothing appeared. I stepped in with fear. The room was empty, and my bedroom was clear of its hideous visitor. I could hardly believe my luck, but once I was sure my enemy was gone, I clapped my hands for joy and ran back down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up to my room together, and the servant soon brought breakfast, but I could not control myself. It was not only joy that surged through me; my skin tingled with sharp sensitivity, and my pulse raced. I could not stay still for a moment; I leaped over chairs, clapped my hands, and burst into wild laughter. At first Clerval thought my excitement came from delight at seeing him, but when he watched me more closely, he saw a strange madness in my eyes he could not explain, and my loud, unrestrained, almost cruel laughter alarmed and astonished him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cfor God\u2019s sake, what is wrong? Do not laugh like that. You are very ill! What is causing all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not ask me,\u201d I shouted, covering my eyes, for I imagined the feared specter slipping into the room. \u201cHe can tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I imagined the monster grabbing me; I struggled wildly and collapsed in a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A meeting he had expected with such joy turned so strangely bitter. But I saw none of his grief, for I lay senseless and did not return to consciousness for a very long time.\\n\\nThis was the start of a nervous fever that kept me confined for many months. Through all of it Henry alone nursed me. Later I learned that, knowing my father was too old and frail for such a long journey and how unhappy my sickness would make Elizabeth, he spared them this sorrow by hiding how serious my illness was.\"}"
