          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68bf848467991fa4b1a9360b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must go, unsure what awaits me, but I will come back here or join your group as soon as I can. A single word or look will decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house tonight or never.\u201d\\n\\nSuch a letter was not something she could recover from quickly. Half an hour alone to think might have calmed her, but the ten minutes she had before being interrupted, along with the constraints of her situation, did nothing to soothe her. Each moment only brought fresh excitement. It was overwhelming joy. Before she even moved past the first rush of feelings, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all came in.\\n\\nThe urgent need to seem her usual self led to an immediate effort, but after a while she could do no more. She stopped understanding a word they were saying and had to claim she felt unwell and excuse herself. They could then see she looked very ill, were frightened and worried, and refused to leave her. This was awful. If they had only gone away and left her to enjoy the quiet of that room, she would have been cured; but having them all standing or hovering around her was maddening, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cOf course, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once and take care of yourself, so you can be well enough for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair was impossible. Worse than anything! To give up even the chance of saying two words to Captain Wentworth during her calm, solitary walk through town (and she was almost sure she would meet him) was unbearable. She protested against the chair, and Mrs Musgrove\u2014who thought only of one kind of illness\u2014after making sure, with some anxiety, that there had been no fall, that Anne had not slipped lately and hit her head, that she was certain she had had no fall, could let her go cheerfully and expect to find her better in the evening.\\n\\nDetermined to skip no possible precaution, Anne struggled and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid, ma\u2019am, that it is not perfectly clear. Please mention to the other gentlemen that we hope to see your whole party tonight. I fear there was some mistake, and I want you especially to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear, it is quite clear, I promise you.\u201d\"}"
        }
