          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d0fd8e701beee5b33d0de1ef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked why I wanted it, and what right I thought I had to it, I would tell him, even though he cared little for such poor dreams, that I had loved Estella deeply and for a long time, and that although I had lost her and must live a mourning life, anything that affected her was still nearer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. Seeing Mr. Jaggers stand there completely still and silent, apparently unmoved by my appeal, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you\u2019re a man with a gentle heart. I\u2019ve seen your pleasant home, and your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful ways with which you brighten up your business life. I beg you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and explain to him that, given all the circumstances, he should be more open with me!\u201d\\n\\nI have never seen two men look more strangely at each other than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this speech. At first I worried that Wemmick would be instantly fired, but that fear faded when I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and Wemmick grow bolder.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s all this?\u201d asked Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou with an old father, and you with pleasant, playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what does it matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, laying his hand on my arm and smiling broadly, \u201cthis man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d said Wemmick, growing bolder still. \u201cI think you\u2019re the impostor.\u201d\\n\\nAgain they traded their earlier strange looks, each apparently still suspecting the other of trying to fool him.\\n\\n\u201cYou with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cSo long as it doesn\u2019t interfere with business,\u201d Wemmick replied, \u201clet it be so. Now that I look at you, sir, I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if you\u2019re planning and contriving to have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you\u2019re tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded thoughtfully two or three times and actually sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201clet\u2019s not talk about \u2018poor dreams;\u2019 you know more about such things than I do, having much fresher experience of that kind. But now about this other matter. I\u2019ll put a scenario to you. Mind! I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe paused, waiting for me to say that I fully understood he had expressly stated that he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201cconsider this scenario. Suppose a woman, under circumstances like those you mentioned, kept her child hidden, and was forced to tell her lawyer, because he insisted that, in order to understand the full scope of his defense, he needed to know the truth about that child.\u201d\"}"
