          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c4208723c0ed9a43bce37595\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never addressed Bath herself; the drudgery of a slow, frustrating correspondence with Elizabeth devolved entirely upon Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had scarcely ended when Captain and Mrs Harville, along with Captain Benwick, joined them for their final stroll through Lyme. They needed to leave for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and until then were determined to remain together, outdoors as long as possible.\\n\\nAnne soon found Captain Benwick drawing near her as they entered the street. Their talk the previous evening had not discouraged him from seeking her company again, and they walked awhile conversing once more of Mr Scott and Lord Byron, still unable, as any two readers might be, to align their opinions on either\u2019s merits. Some incident, however, brought about an almost general reshuffling among their friends, and Anne found Captain Harville at her side in Benwick\u2019s place.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said, lowering his voice, \u201cyou have performed a kindness in coaxing that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he had such company more often. It does him harm, I believe, to be so shut up; but what can we do? We cannot part.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d Anne replied, \u201cI can readily believe that to be impossible; yet in time perhaps\u2014we know what time accomplishes in every sorrow, and you must remember, Captain Harville, your friend is still a young mourner\u2014only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, quite true,\u201d he sighed deeply, \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps not made known to him immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, newly appointed to the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading to hear of him; he sent letters inland, but the Grappler was ordered to Portsmouth. The news had to follow him there, yet who was to deliver it? not I. I would almost sooner have been run up to the yard-arm. No one could do it but that fine fellow\u201d (gesturing to Captain Wentworth). \u201cThe Laconia had entered Plymouth the week before; no fear of her being sent out again. He chanced the rest; wrote for leave of absence, but without waiting for an answer, travelled night and day till he reached Portsmouth, hurried straight to the Grappler, and stayed with the poor man a whole week. That was what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. Judge, Miss Elliot, how dear he must be to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete conviction, and said as much as her own feeling, and Captain Harville\u2019s composure, allowed; for he was too moved to pursue the topic further, and when he spoke again, it was on something entirely different.\"}"
        }
      ],
