          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"157a452ef4ee71cfb31bd281\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. The perfect thing would be for all of us to ride on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014with my dear husband walking alongside. I really must talk to him about buying one. In the country it seems almost essential; no matter how many ways a woman has to occupy herself, she can\u2019t stay shut indoors forever\u2014and those long walks, you know\u2014dust in summer, mud in winter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou won\u2019t find either between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and right now it\u2019s completely dry. Still, come on a donkey if that\u2019s what you prefer. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I want everything to suit you in every possible way.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I\u2019m sure you do. I really must give you full credit, my good friend. Beneath that peculiar dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you\u2019re the truest humorist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I completely appreciate your thoughtfulness in this whole plan. You\u2019ve come up with exactly what will delight me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table set in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the outing; and he knew that if any of them sat outdoors to eat, Mr. Woodhouse would inevitably fall ill. Under the harmless pretense of a morning drive and a couple of hours at Donwell, Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured into misery.\\n\\nHe was invited in good faith. No hidden horrors were to reproach him for his easy trust. He did agree to go. He had not visited Donwell in two years. \u201cOn some very fine morning, he and Emma and Harriet could go perfectly well; he could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls strolled around the gardens. He didn\u2019t think they could be damp in the middle of the day. He would be very glad to see the old house again and very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton and any other neighbor. He could see no objection at all to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there on some very fine morning. He considered it extremely well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014much cleverer than dining out. He did not care for dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was lucky in everyone\u2019s ready agreement. The invitation was so well received everywhere that it seemed as if, like Mrs. Elton, each person took the plan as a personal compliment. Emma and Harriet declared themselves very eager for the pleasure it promised; and Mr. Weston, unprompted, promised to bring Frank over to join them if he could\u2014a display of approval and gratitude that might have been done without. Mr. Knightley was then obliged to say he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston promised to write at once and spare no argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
