          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1defa93a44570be6211f6b0b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered and went back, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it without even glancing at the murdered man. He felt that the key to the whole affair was not to let himself grasp what had happened. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait that had caused all his misery was gone from his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he thought of the lamp. It was an unusual Moorish piece, dull silver worked with burnished steel arabesques and studded with rough turquoise. His servant might notice it was missing, and questions would follow. He hesitated, then turned back and took it from the table. He couldn\u2019t avoid seeing the dead body. How still it was! How horribly white the long hands looked! It was like a dreadful wax figure.\\n\\nAfter locking the door behind him, he crept quietly downstairs. The wood groaned and seemed to cry out in pain. He stopped several times and listened. No\u2014everything was silent. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nIn the library he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They would have to be hidden. He unlocked a concealed cabinet in the wainscoting, where he kept his own strange disguises, and put them inside. He could burn them later. Then he checked his watch. Twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and began to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were hanged in England for exactly what he had done. A fever of murder seemed to be in the air. Some blood-red star must have drifted too close to the earth... And yet, what evidence was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him return. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed... Paris! Yes, Basil had gone to Paris, and by the midnight train, just as he had intended. With his secretive ways, it would be months before anyone became suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before that.\\n\\nSuddenly he had an idea. He put on his fur coat and hat and went out into the hall. There he paused, hearing the slow heavy tread of a policeman on the pavement outside and seeing the flash of the bull\u2019s-eye lantern reflected in the window. He waited, holding his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments he lifted the latch and slipped outside, closing the door very gently behind him. Then he started ringing the bell.\"}"
