          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"524f2fe42eb9e39981d29224\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to study them were sure to come. Before long, Anne had seen the four together often enough to form an opinion, though she was too prudent to share it at home, where neither husband nor wife would be pleased. While she believed Louisa was the slight favorite, she also felt certain\u2014so far as memory and experience allowed\u2014that Captain Wentworth loved neither of them. They were more taken with him; yet even that feeling was not real love, only a brief fever of admiration that might, and likely must, slip into love for some of them. Charles Hayter seemed to know he was being overlooked, and still Henrietta sometimes looked torn between the two men. Anne wished she could point out to them all what they were doing, and warn them of the trouble they risked. She did not suspect anyone of deceit. Her greatest comfort was believing that Captain Wentworth had no idea of the pain he caused. There was no gloating, no petty triumph in him. He had probably never heard, and never considered, Charles Hayter\u2019s claims. His only fault was in accepting the attentions (for \u201caccepting\u201d was the word) of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to withdraw. Three days passed without a single visit to Uppercross\u2014a striking change. He even turned down a formal dinner invitation; and when Mr Musgrove found him surrounded by heavy books, both Mr and Mrs Musgrove were certain something was wrong, and gravely remarked that he was studying himself to death. Mary hoped and felt sure Henrietta had given him an outright dismissal, while her husband kept expecting to see him again tomorrow. Anne could only think Charles Hayter was being sensible.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth had gone shooting together, and while the sisters at the Cottage sat quietly working, their visitors appeared at the window\u2014the sisters from the Mansion-house.\\n\\nIt was a lovely November day, and the Miss Musgroves walked through the small grounds and paused solely to announce that they planned a long walk; therefore, they assumed Mary would not want to join them. When Mary instantly answered, a little jealous at being thought a poor walker, \u201cOh, yes, I should like that very much; I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne was sure, from the expression on the girls\u2019 faces, that her company was exactly what they did not want. Again she marveled at the family habit that seemed to require everything to be shared and every plan to be communicated and carried out together, no matter how inconvenient or unwanted.\"}"
