{"request_id":"27bf7ff4eced6fd602d32cbd","rewritten_text":"My father had lived, and his memory was the core of my own life. I might feel gratitude to someone else, but I could never again love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering. Even my pleasures were endured, not enjoyed. I felt like a lonely spot among mountains, hemmed in on every side by steep black cliffs, where no warmth could reach and from which there was no escape to sunnier fields. So although the spirit of friendship soothed me for a time, it could not restore me. It arrived like a gentle visitor; it left, and I barely noticed the loss. The spirit within me was dead; do not be surprised that when it came I did not welcome, or when it departed I did not mourn, the best gift of heaven—a friend.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story so you may see how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquence and tender sympathy, and how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we should have comforted each other if I had not been hardened to stone by the Medusa’s head of misery. Woodville’s sorrows were not at the heart’s core like mine; his grief was natural, meant not to destroy but to purify, so that once its shadow passed he might shine brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had a classical education. He was one of those rare people whom fortune favors from birth, on whom she heaps every gift of mind and body without limit, and whom she shelters so well that no imperfection, however slight, and no disappointment, however brief, can touch them. She seemed to have made his mind of some metal that no stain could dim, and his understanding was such that no mistake could skew it. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a bright star in the east all eyes turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet, though that title has been cheapened so often it cannot express all he was. He resembled a poet of old, crowned by the muses in the cradle and fed by bees on his lips. As he walked among other men, he seemed wrapped in a heavenly halo that set him apart and raised him above them. His surpassing beauty, the blazing fire of his eyes, and his words whose rich tones left listeners mute in ecstatic wonder made him tower above all others, so that beside him they seemed born only to serve his superior excellence."}

