{"request_id":"1f9572be18fca62784ac0c5c","rewritten_text":"I cannot in the least understand why she desired to be on intimate terms with me; yet, if the same situation arose again, I am sure I should be taken in once more. Caroline only returned my call yesterday; during the interval I had not a note or even a line from her. When she did arrive, it was plain that paying the visit gave her no pleasure; she offered a slight, formal apology for not coming sooner, uttered nothing about wishing to see me again, and in every particular seemed so changed that, once she left, I made up my mind to let the acquaintance go. I pity her, though I cannot help censuring her. She was decidedly wrong in singling me out as she did; I can truly say every approach toward closeness began on her side. Still, I pity her, because she must be conscious she has acted badly, and because I am convinced anxiety for her brother is behind it all. I need not explain further; and though we know that anxiety to be entirely needless, yet if she feels it, it explains her conduct toward me; and as he is so justly dear to his sister, any concern she has for him is natural and amiable. Yet I marvel at her having such fears now, for if he had cared for me at all, he and I must long since have met. She herself let slip that he knows I am in town; yet from her way of speaking, it was as if she wished to convince herself he truly prefers Miss Darcy. I cannot fathom it. Were I not afraid of judging too severely, I should be tempted to say there is a strong semblance of duplicity in her behaviour. I will strive to dismiss every distressing thought, and think only of what brings me joy—your affection, and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write me again very soon. Miss Bingley hinted at his never returning to Netherfield, at giving up the house, though she was far from certain. We had better keep that to ourselves. I am very glad you hear such pleasant news from our friends at Hunsford. Pray go visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will find yourself very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

The letter pained Elizabeth somewhat; yet her spirits rose in the reflection that Jane would no longer be imposed upon, at least by the sister. Any hope from the brother was now wholly extinguished."}

