          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"19f880fd04a3788c9e95ff48\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If she had known that her sister meant to pull her away from such prospects and realities, what would she have felt? Only her mother could have understood, for she might have felt almost the same. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was her sole consolation for the bleak conviction that her husband never intended to go there himself.\\n\\nYet they were completely unaware of what had happened, and their giddy excitement lasted, with barely a pause, right up to the day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since she had often been in his company since coming back, her agitation was mostly gone; the turmoil of her earlier attraction had entirely vanished. She had even learned to perceive, in the very gentleness that had first charmed her, an affected sameness that disgusted and exhausted her. In his present behaviour toward her she found a new source of annoyance, because the inclination he soon showed to renew the attentions that had marked the beginning of their acquaintance could only, after all that had since occurred, provoke her. She lost all concern for him when she realized that he had singled her out as the target of such idle, frivolous flirtation; and while she firmly rebuffed him, she could not help but feel rebuked that he believed her vanity would be gratified and her preference secured at any moment by the return of his attentions, however long absent and for whatever reason.\\n\\nOn the very last day the regiment remained in Meryton, he dined with other officers at Longbourn; and Elizabeth was so little inclined to part from him on good terms that when he casually asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings, and asked if he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, even alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s recovery and a returning smile, said he had once seen him often; then, noting that he was a very gentlemanlike man, he asked how she had liked him. Her reply praised him warmly. With an air of nonchalance, he soon added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves once you know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look she noticed. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d but stopping himself, he added more lightly, \u201cIs it in his manners that he improves?\u201d\"}"
