          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7b690377e207a03d5e6ea8a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYour father might let you stay, if your mother agrees. Fathers never think daughters as important. If you stay another full month, I can take one of you as far as London, for I am going there in early June for a week. Since Dawson has no objection to the barouche-box, there will be plenty of room for one of you\u2014and if the weather is cool, I might even take both, as neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are all kindness, madam; but we must follow our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine looked resigned. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always speak plainly, and I cannot bear the idea of two young women traveling post alone. It is highly improper. You must find someone to send. I have the strongest dislike of such things. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended according to their station. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted she have two man-servants with her. Miss Darcy, daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not properly travel in any other way. I am very careful about these matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad I thought to mention it, for it would reflect badly on you to let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle will send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant, does he? I am glad someone thinks of these things. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, you will be well attended to.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions about their journey, and, since she did not answer all of them herself, Elizabeth had to pay attention\u2014which she counted fortunate, for with her mind so full she might have forgotten where she was. Reflection had to be saved for solitary hours; whenever she was alone, she indulged in it as her greatest relief, and every day she took a solitary walk so she could enjoy the strange pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nMr. Darcy\u2019s letter was becoming something she would soon know by heart. She studied every sentence, and her feelings toward its writer varied greatly. When she remembered his manner of address, she still burned with indignation; but when she thought how wrongly she had judged and reproached him, her anger turned against herself, and his disappointed feelings became the object of her compassion.\"}"
