          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d938ab8f18913996ece57854\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The studio was saturated with the lush scent of roses, and whenever the light summer breeze rustled the garden trees, the heavy fragrance of lilacs or the subtler perfume of pink-blossoming hawthorn drifted through the open door.\\n\\nFrom the corner of the Persian saddlebag divan where he lounged, doing what he always did\u2014smoking countless cigarettes\u2014Lord Henry Wotton could just glimpse the honey-sweet, honey-colored laburnum blooms, their trembling branches barely able to carry such fiery beauty. Every so often, the fantastical silhouettes of birds crossing the long tussore silk curtains in front of the enormous window created a fleeting Japanese effect, reminding him of those pale, jade-faced Tokyo painters who try to suggest speed and motion through an art that must remain still. The low drone of bees forcing their way through the tall, unmown grass or circling over and over around the dusty gilt trumpets of the sprawling woodbine only made the stillness feel heavier. The faint roar of London sounded like the deep bourdon note of a distant organ.\\n\\nIn the center of the room, clamped to a tall easel, stood the full-length portrait of a young man of extraordinary beauty. Seated a little way in front of it was the artist himself, Basil Hallward, whose sudden disappearance years ago stirred so much public excitement and inspired so many strange theories.\\n\\nAs the painter studied the elegant, handsome figure he had so skillfully captured, a pleased smile crossed his face and seemed ready to stay. But he abruptly jumped up, shut his eyes, and pressed his fingers over his lids, as though he were trying to trap within his mind some elusive dream he feared waking from.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s your finest work, Basil, the best thing you\u2019ve ever done,\u201d Lord Henry said lazily. \u201cYou must send it to the Grosvenor next year. The Academy is too large and too vulgar. Every time I\u2019ve gone, either there were so many people I couldn\u2019t see the paintings\u2014which was dreadful\u2014or so many paintings I couldn\u2019t see the people\u2014which was worse. The Grosvenor is really the only option.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I\u2019ll send it anywhere,\u201d Basil replied, tossing back his head in that odd way that used to make his Oxford friends laugh.\"}"
