          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5c9cc3ece964336d5405a024\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I returned, I found this letter from my father:\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting impatiently for a letter setting the date of your return to us, and I was first tempted to write only a few lines telling you when to come home. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I dare not do it. How shocked you would be, my son, expecting a joyful welcome, only to find tears and misery. And how, Victor, am I to tell you of our disaster? Your time away cannot have dulled your feeling for our joys and sorrows, yet how can I cause my long-absent son such pain? I want to prepare you for the horrible news, yet it\u2019s impossible. Even now I imagine your eyes running over this page, searching for the words that carry the dread news.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead\u2014that sweet child whose smile warmed my heart, who was so gentle but so lively! Victor, he was murdered.\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t try to console you, only to tell you what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th), my niece, your two brothers, and I walked at Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, and we stayed out longer than usual. It was already twilight when we thought of going back, and then we realized that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were missing. We sat on a bench to wait for their return. Soon Ernest arrived and asked if we had seen William. He said they had been playing, that William had run off to hide, and that he had searched in vain, then waited a long time, but William never came back.\\n\\n\u201cThis report alarmed us, and we continued searching until night fell. Elizabeth suggested he might have gone home. He wasn\u2019t there. We went back out with torches; I couldn\u2019t rest, thinking my sweet boy was lost and exposed to the damp night air, and Elizabeth was in torment. Around five in the morning I found my lovely boy, whom I had last seen full of health the night before, lying on the grass, pale and still, the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cWe carried him home, and the anguish on my face gave the secret away to Elizabeth. She begged to see the body. I tried to stop her, but she insisted, and once in the room where he lay, she quickly examined the poor child\u2019s neck and, clasping her hands, cried, \u2018Oh God!\u2019\u201d\"}"
