          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"915392e0f93084ebb69b2b87\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Tom managed to scratch up the cupboard with it and was about to start on the bureau when he was called in to get ready for Sunday school.\\n\\nMary handed him a tin basin of water and a bar of soap, and he went outside, set the basin on the little bench, dipped the soap in the water and set it aside, rolled up his sleeves, poured the water out on the ground very carefully, then went back into the kitchen and started rubbing his face with the towel behind the door. But Mary took the towel away and said:\\n\\n\u201cNow aren\u2019t you ashamed, Tom? You mustn\u2019t be so bad. Water won\u2019t hurt you.\u201d\\n\\nTom was a bit thrown off. The basin was filled again, and this time he stood over it a moment, working up his nerve, took a deep breath, and began. When he came into the kitchen soon after, both eyes shut and hands searching for the towel, a respectable amount of suds and water was running from his face. But when he was done with the towel, he still wasn\u2019t passable, because the clean patch stopped at his chin and jaw like a mask; below and beyond that line stretched a dark stretch of untouched dirt down his chest and around his neck. Mary took over, and when she finished he was a man and a brother without color lines, his soaked hair neatly brushed and the short curls arranged into a tidy, even look. [He privately flattened the curls with effort and plastered his hair close to his head; he thought curls were girlish, and his own made his life miserable.] Then Mary brought out the suit he had worn only on Sundays for two years\u2014they were simply called his \u201cother clothes,\u201d which tells us how many he had. After he dressed, she straightened him up; she buttoned his neat jacket to his chin, turned his wide shirt collar down over his shoulders, brushed him off, and topped him with his speckled straw hat. He now looked greatly improved\u2014and just as uncomfortable. He felt as uncomfortable as he appeared, because whole clothes and cleanliness always cramped him. He hoped Mary would overlook his shoes, but that hope died; she smeared them well with tallow, as people did, and brought them out. He lost his temper and said he was always made to do everything he didn\u2019t want to do.\"}"
