          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"143a00ea596a9f053adbe515\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something new happened. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armour, and as Adonis with a huntsman\u2019s cloak and polished boar-spear. You had sat crowned with heavy lotus blossoms on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking out over the green, muddy Nile. You had bent over the still pool of a Greek woodland and seen in its silent silver the marvel of your own face. All of it had been what art should be\u2014unconscious, ideal, distant. One day, a day I sometimes call fatal, I decided to paint a wonderful portrait of you as you really are, not in the clothes of past ages but in your own dress and in your own time. Whether it was the realism of that approach, or the simple wonder of your personality shown to me so directly, without mist or veil, I do not know. But as I worked, every small stroke of colour seemed to expose my secret. I grew afraid others would see my worship. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much, that I had poured too much of myself into it. So I resolved never to let the picture be exhibited. You were a little annoyed, but you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, who heard about it, laughed at me. I did not mind. When the picture was finished and I sat alone with it, I felt certain I was right. After a few days the piece left my studio, and once that unbearable fascination was gone, it seemed silly that I had ever believed it showed anything beyond your remarkable looks and my own skill. Even now I cannot help thinking it is wrong to believe the passion one feels while creating is ever truly shown in the work. Art is always more abstract than we like to think. Form and colour only tell us about form and colour\u2014that is all. Often it seems art hides the artist far more than it reveals him. So when I got the offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the main piece in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I see now you were right. The picture cannot be shown. Please do not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have confessed.\"}"
        }
