          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ae2e841639c0aed60f289bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma felt no remorse for stooping to visit the Coles. The outing supplied her with many agreeable remembrances the following day; and whatever she might have sacrificed in the way of stately exclusiveness was more than compensated by the glimmer of popular approval. Doubtless she had enchanted the Coles\u2014worthy souls deserving of delight\u2014and she had left behind a reputation that would not quickly fade.\\n\\nComplete happiness, even in recollection, is rare; and there were two matters on which she was not entirely at ease. She questioned whether she had violated the bond of woman to woman by betraying her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill. It was scarcely proper; yet the idea had been so powerful that it broke out in spite of her, and his ready acquiescence in all she said flattered her discernment so much that she could not be perfectly convinced she ought to have remained silent.\\n\\nHer other regret likewise concerned Jane Fairfax, and here she entertained no uncertainty. She sincerely and plainly lamented the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She deeply lamented the idle hours of her childhood\u2014and seated herself to practise with vigor for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet\u2019s arrival; and if Harriet\u2019s admiration could have soothed her, she would soon have been appeased.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if only I could play like you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not couple us, Harriet. My playing resembles hers no more than a lamp resembles sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear\u2014I think you are the better performer of the two. I rate your playing quite as high as hers. I would much rather listen to you. Everyone last evening praised your performance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThose who knew anything about it must have felt the difference. The fact is, Harriet, my playing is just good enough to earn praise, while Jane Fairfax\u2019s far surpasses it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always maintain you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is any difference, nobody could ever detect it. Mr. Cole spoke of the elegance of your taste; and Mr. Frank Churchill went on and on about your taste, saying he valued taste far more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax possesses both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCan you be sure? I saw she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I detest Italian singing.\u2014One cannot make out a word of it. Besides, even if she does play so extremely well, it is merely what she is bound to do, since she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
