          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0507e8212fd6f6e46d2afdf3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo you won\u2019t, won\u2019t you? Oh, you sweet little flat!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s wicked, ain\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d Charley Bates asked. \u201cHe\u2019ll end up scragged, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve no idea what you mean,\u201d Oliver replied.\\n\\n\u201cSomething like this, old fellow,\u201d said Charley. With that, Master Bates snatched up the tail of his neckerchief; holding it upright, he let his head drop sideways and forced a queer sound through his teeth, presenting by this lively mime that scragging and hanging were one and the same.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s the ticket,\u201d Charley said. \u201cSee how he gapes, Jack! I never met such capital company as that boy there; he\u2019ll finish me off, I\u2019m certain.\u201d Having laughed himself nearly silly again, Master Charley Bates resumed his pipe, tears standing in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve been badly raised,\u201d remarked the Dodger, inspecting his boots with immense satisfaction after Oliver had buffed them. \u201cStill, Fagin will make something of you, or you\u2019ll be the first he ever had that didn\u2019t pay. You\u2019d best start straightaway; you\u2019ll be in the line long before you expect it, and you\u2019re only wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates supported this counsel with sundry moral observations of his own; when these were spent, he and his companion Mr. Dawkins launched into a glowing account of the myriad delights belonging to their way of life, lacing the description with countless suggestions that the best course for Oliver was to win Fagin\u2019s favour without delay, by the same methods they themselves had used.\\n\\n\u201cAnd always keep this in mind, Nolly,\u201d said the Dodger, as the Jew\u2019s key was heard rattling in the door overhead, \u201cif you won\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the point of jawing like that?\u201d Master Bates broke in; \u201che doesn\u2019t understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you won\u2019t take pocket handkerchers and watches,\u201d the Dodger continued, translating his speech to Oliver\u2019s comprehension, \u201csome other bloke will; then the coves who lose them will be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off, too, and no soul a halfpenny the better except the chaps who nab them\u2014and you\u2019ve as good a claim to them as they have.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, certainly!\u201d said the Jew, who had slipped in without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt\u2019s all contained in a nutshell, my dear; in a nutshell, take the Dodger\u2019s word. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the catechism of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old fellow rubbed his hands gleefully while he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s logic in this fashion, chuckling with delight at his pupil\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe talk went no further just then, for the Jew had come home accompanied by Miss Betsy and a gentleman unfamiliar to Oliver, though the Dodger greeted him as Tom Chitling; after lingering on the stairs to swap a few compliments with the lady, he now entered the room.\"}"
