          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a307eb7d6ce145789f2c92b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said that as he left home and passed through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke coming from the inn\u2019s chimney. Thinking travelers might be gathered there, he knocked and was let in. Inside he found only Lucy and her mother. Her mother had lost the use of her limbs from rheumatism, so little by little all the other residents of the area had departed, leaving them alone. Lucy begged him to stay; in a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could set out, but if they were left abandoned like this they would surely die. The man said his wife and children were already among the emigrants, so he believed it impossible to remain. As a last resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to be delivered wherever he met us. At least he carried out this task, and Idris, deeply moved, received the following letter:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHonored lady,\\n\\n\u201cI know you will remember and pity me, and I dare to hope you will help me; what other hope do I have? Forgive my way of writing; I am so confused. A month ago my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already better, and in another month she would surely be able to travel, in the way you kindly offered to arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As each person left, they said perhaps my mother would be better before we were completely deserted. Three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who had stayed to the end because of his newborn child; since there were many of them, I thought I might convince them to wait a bit longer for us. But I found their house deserted. I hadn\u2019t seen another soul until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother doesn\u2019t know our situation; she is so ill that I\u2019ve kept it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWon\u2019t you send someone to us? I\u2019m sure we will die miserably if things stay as they are. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road, and even if she were better and I somehow knew the roads and could travel the many miles to the sea, you would all be in France by then, and the great ocean would lie between us, which is terrifying even to sailors.\u201d\"}"
        }
