{"request_id":"a4072965271a7f3e468e51a5","rewritten_text":"During his brief stay, Emma had hardly seen him, yet enough to know the first meeting was past and to notice the new blend of pique and pretension in his manner. She was already wondering how she had ever found him agreeable. His presence was tied to unpleasant feelings, and, except as a moral exercise—a penance, a lesson, a useful humiliation—she would gladly have been assured she need never see him again. She wished him well, yet he caused her pain, and his being twenty miles away would bring the most comfort.

Still, the discomfort of his remaining in Highbury must be eased by his marriage. Many vain worries would end, many awkward moments be avoided. A Mrs. Elton would justify any change in their acquaintance; former intimacy might quietly fade. It would be almost like starting their polite acquaintance anew.

Emma thought little about the lady herself. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, certainly; accomplished enough for Highbury—good-looking enough—to appear plain, likely, beside Harriet. As for family connections, Emma had no worries; after all his proud claims and his disdain of Harriet, he had achieved nothing special. On that point, the truth seemed within reach. What she was might be uncertain; but who she was could be learned, and setting aside the ten thousand pounds, she did not appear to surpass Harriet at all. She brought no name, no lineage, no alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant—so he must be called—but since the profits of his business life seemed modest, it was fair to guess the dignity of his trade had been modest too. Every winter she spent some time in Bath, yet Bristol was her home, the very core of it; for although her parents had died some years before, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more precise or honorable was claimed than that he was in the law line; and with him she lived. Emma imagined him a drudge for some attorney, too dull to advance. The grandeur of the connection rested entirely on the elder sister, who was very well married to a gentleman of significant means near Bristol, a man with two carriages. That was the conclusion of the tale; that was Miss Hawkins’s crowning glory."}

