          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f72d296e45acccd8f081fdbf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival had lit a kind of fire in the heart of Saint Antoine, spreading fast as they moved along, flashing like flames across the faces at nearly every door and window. Still, no one followed them, and no one spoke when they stepped into the wine-shop, though every eye there fixed on them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nThat seemed to loosen every tongue. A chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d answered him.\\n\\n\u201cIt is bad weather, gentlemen,\u201d Defarge said, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt that, each man glanced at his neighbor, then everyone lowered their eyes and sat silent. One man alone rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d Defarge said aloud to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have traveled several leagues with this good mender of roads, named Jacques. I met him by chance a day and a half outside Paris. He is a good child, this mender of roads named Jacques. Give him something to drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge set wine before the mender of roads called Jacques, who lifted his blue cap to the others and drank. He carried coarse dark bread inside his blouse; he ate it now and then, munching and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge took a draught of wine, though less than he had poured for the stranger, being himself used to it, and waited until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He looked at no one, and now no one looked at him; not even Madame Defarge, who had picked up her knitting again and was at work.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked when the time was right.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome, then! You shall see the room I told you you could use. It will suit you perfectly.\u201d\\n\\nThey went out of the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep staircase, from the staircase into a garret\u2014the same garret where once a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, stooping forward, busily making shoes.\\n\\nNo white-haired man waited there now; but the three men who had left the wine-shop one by one were there. And between them and that white-haired man far away, the only link was that they had once peered at him through cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge shut the door carefully and spoke in a low voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness I met as arranged, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
