{"request_id":"938fb023d229dc0c64f11b1d","rewritten_text":"They were her very existence; more beloved than any sanctuary of affection, more cherished than everything else the world could offer. The boys heard of our impending move with childish delight, while Clara asked whether our destination might be Athens. “Perhaps,” I answered, and her face shone with joy. There she would visit her parents’ grave and wander through the land saturated with memories of her father’s fame. Quietly yet unceasingly she had dwelt on these images. Such recollections had changed her early gaiety into gravity and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

Many beloved companions could not be abandoned, humble as they seemed. There was Lord Raymond’s spirited yet docile horse given to his daughter; Alfred’s dog; and a pet eagle whose eyes had dimmed with age. Yet compiling this list of favorites to bring along awoke sorrow for our heavy losses, and deep sighs for the countless things we must leave behind. Idris’s eyes brimmed with tears, while Alfred and Evelyn by turns brought a treasured rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting these were indispensable, lamenting that we could not transport the castle and forest, the deer and the birds, and every familiar cherished possession. “Foolish, loving children,” I said, “we have forever lost riches far more precious than these; we abandon them only to safeguard treasures beside which they are worthless. Let us hold fast to our goal and our hope; let them be a barrier strong enough to restrain our regret for trifles.”

The children were soon diverted again and returned to planning future delights. Idris had vanished. She had gone to conceal her frailty; slipping from the castle, she descended to the little park in search of solitude where she might weep. I found her clinging to an ancient oak, pressing its rough bark with her rose-colored lips while tears streamed freely, and sobs with broken words escaped her; with aching heart I watched my beloved overwhelmed in grief. I drew her to me; feeling my kisses on her eyelids, my arms enfolding her, she remembered what blessings remained. “You are so kind not to chide me,” she murmured. “I weep, and an intolerable pang rends my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives are bereft of husbands, while you and my children are still with me.”"}

