          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1527b87dcfca684fe13794fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All this, and even more\u2014vastly more\u2014would I most cheerfully relate. Yet, to be concise, I must insist on my recompense. I yearn to return to my family and my home; and as the condition of any further disclosures from me\u2014in view of the illumination I can offer upon many most weighty branches of both physical and metaphysical science\u2014I must entreat, through the good offices of your honorable body, forgiveness for the crime I committed in causing the deaths of the creditors at the time of my departure from Rotterdam. This, therefore, is the aim of the present communication. Its bearer, a native of the moon, whom I have persuaded and carefully coached to act as my envoy to earth, will await your Excellencies\u2019 decision, and bring back the sought pardon to me, if it be obtainable in any fashion.\\n\\n\u201cI have the honor to remain, etc., your Excellencies\u2019 very humble servant,\\n\\n\u201cHANS PFAALL.\u201d\\n\\nUpon finishing this remarkable manuscript, Professor Rub-a-dub, it is related, let his pipe tumble to the floor in sheer astonishment, and Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, having removed his spectacles, polished them, and returned them to his pocket, so far forgot propriety and his own dignity as to turn thrice upon his heel, in the pure essence of amazement and admiration. There could be no doubt\u2014the pardon must be procured. Thus swore, with a hearty oath, Professor Rub-a-dub, and thus the illustrious Von Underduk ultimately concluded, as he took his fellow savant by the arm and, without uttering a syllable, set forth at full speed for home to ponder the measures to be pursued. Upon reaching the burgomaster\u2019s threshold, however, the professor ventured to remark that since the messenger had seen fit to vanish\u2014surely terrified to death by the ferocious looks of the Rotterdam burghers\u2014the pardon would scarcely be of service, as no one save a lunar being would dare undertake so prodigious a voyage. The burgomaster concurred in this, and the matter was therefore dropped. Not so, however, the gossip and conjecture. Once published, the letter stirred a multitude of tales and views. Some ultra-wise souls even rendered themselves absurd by deriding the whole affair as nothing more than a hoax. Yet hoax, in the vocabulary of such people, seems a blanket term for everything beyond their understanding. For my own part, I cannot imagine on what grounds they have leveled such a charge. Let us examine their statements:\"}"
