          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"236b19bb79c9e05b1815214c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not tell if the voice was a man\u2019s or a woman\u2019s. Could not catch the words, but believed the language was Spanish. This witness described the condition of the room and the bodies just as we reported yesterday.\\n\\n\u201cHenri Duval, a neighbor and silver-smith, testifies that he was among the first to enter the house. He generally agrees with Mus\u00e8t\u2019s account. As soon as they forced their way in, they shut the door again to keep out the quickly growing crowd, despite the late hour. This witness thought the high voice belonged to an Italian. He knew it was not French. Could not be certain it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He did not know Italian. Could not make out the words, but the tone convinced him the speaker was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter and had spoken with both often. He was sure the high voice was neither of the two deceased women.\\n\\n\u201cOdenheimer, a restaurateur, offered his testimony. Because he does not speak French, he was questioned through an interpreter. He is from Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the screams began. They lasted several minutes\u2014perhaps ten. They were long, loud, and terribly distressing. He was among those who entered the building. He confirmed the earlier evidence in every respect except one. He was sure the sharp voice was a man\u2019s\u2014a Frenchman\u2019s. Could not make out the words. They were loud and quick, uneven, spoken as if the speaker were afraid and angry. The voice was rough\u2014not really shrill but rough. He would not call it a shrill voice. The gruff voice said \u201csacr\u00e9,\u201d \u201cdiable,\u201d and once \u201cmon Dieu.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJules Mignaud, banker of the firm Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the older Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She opened an account with his banking house in the spring\u2014eight years before. She often deposited small sums. She drew nothing until the third day before her death, when she personally withdrew 4000 francs. This amount was paid in gold, and a clerk escorted the money home.\\n\\n\u201cAdolphe Le Bon, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her home with the 4000 francs, packed in two bags. When the door opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from him, while the old lady took the other.\u201d\"}"
