          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f44fb19eb77057fbf785f34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Such were the events that weighed on Felix\u2019s heart and made him, when I first saw him, the unhappiest member of his family. He could have borne poverty, and while hardship was the reward of his virtue he took pride in it; but the Turk\u2019s ingratitude and the loss of his beloved Safie were miseries far more bitter and beyond repair. The arrival of the Arabian woman revived his spirit.\\n\\nWhen the news reached Leghorn that Felix had lost his wealth and rank, the merchant ordered his daughter to think no more of her lover and to prepare to return to her native land. Safie\u2019s generous nature was outraged by this command; she tried to reason with her father, but he left her in anger, repeating his tyrannical order.\\n\\nA few days later, the Turk rushed into his daughter\u2019s room and told her that he had reason to believe someone had revealed his stay in Leghorn, and that he would soon be handed over to the French government; he had therefore hired a ship to take him to Constantinople, where he would sail in a few hours. He meant to leave his daughter in the care of a trusted servant to follow at her convenience with most of his property, which had not yet arrived in Leghorn.\\n\\nOnce alone, Safie considered what she ought to do in this sudden crisis. A life in Turkey filled her with loathing; both her faith and her feelings opposed it. From some papers of her father\u2019s that came into her hands she learned of her lover\u2019s exile and the name of the place where he was living. She hesitated for a while, but finally made up her mind. Taking some of her own jewels and a sum of money, she left Italy with an attendant, a native of Leghorn who knew the common tongue of Turkey, and set out for Germany.\\n\\nShe reached a town about twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage without trouble, but there her attendant fell dangerously ill. Safie tended her with devoted care, yet the poor girl died, and the Arabian was left alone, unable to speak the language of the country and entirely ignorant of the world\u2019s customs. She was fortunate, though. The Italian had mentioned their destination, and after her death the mistress of the house where they had stayed ensured that Safie safely reached her lover\u2019s cottage.\"}"
