          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f1664524bdc1540cec4a6c90\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading his letter again as she answered it softened her so much that she had to bolster herself with a few firm phrases, and she was so distressed at the thought of making him unhappy, so troubled over what his mother and sisters would think and say, so anxious that they not judge her ungrateful, that Emma believed if the young man had crossed her path at that moment, he would have been accepted after all.\\n\\nStill, the letter was written and sealed and sent. The matter was settled and Harriet safe. She felt rather low all evening, but Emma could excuse her gentle regrets and sometimes eased them by speaking of her own affection, sometimes by mentioning Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she said in a slightly mournful tone.\\n\\n\u201cAnd if you were, I could never bear to let you go, my Harriet. You are far too essential at Hartfield to be spared for Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I\u2019m sure I should never wish to go there, for I\u2019m never happy anywhere but Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nLater she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what happened. I am sure Miss Nash would\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and he is only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne would not want greater pride or polish in a schoolteacher, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you this chance of marriage; even this success would seem valuable to her. As for anything better for you, she is probably quite unaware. The attentions of a certain person can hardly have made it into Highbury gossip yet. So far I suspect you and I are the only ones his looks and manners have clued in.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed, smiled, and remarked that it was odd anyone should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton certainly lifted her spirits; but before long she was tender-hearted again toward the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has my letter,\u201d she murmured. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he won\u2019t take it too hard.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think instead of our absent friends who are occupied more cheerfully,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cPerhaps at this very moment Mr. Elton is showing your portrait to his mother and sisters, telling them how much lovelier the original is, and after being asked five or six times, finally letting them hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
