          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2174d44397ef27f80de6275\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Week after week passed, and I became more and more obsessed with you. Then something new happened. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armor, and as Adonis with a hunter\u2019s cloak and polished boar spear. You sat crowned with heavy lotus blossoms on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, staring across the green, murky Nile. You leaned over a quiet pool in some Greek forest and saw the miracle of your own face in the silent silver of the water. All of it had been what art should be\u2014unconscious, ideal, distant. One day\u2014sometimes I think it was fatal\u2014I decided to paint an extraordinary portrait of you as you really are, not in the costumes of dead ages but in your own clothes and in our own time. Whether it was the realism of the approach or simply the wonder of your personality shown to me so directly, without haze or veil, I can\u2019t say. But as I worked on it, every layer of color seemed to reveal my secret. I grew afraid that others would discover my worship. I felt, Dorian, that I had confessed too much, that I had put too much of myself into it. That was when I resolved never to let the picture be exhibited. You were slightly irritated, but you didn\u2019t understand what it meant to me. Harry, when I told him about it, laughed at me, but I didn\u2019t care. When the picture was finished and I sat alone with it, I felt I was right... Well, after a few days it left my studio, and once I\u2019d rid myself of the unbearable fascination of its presence, it seemed foolish to think I had seen anything in it beyond your remarkable beauty and my ability to paint. Even now I can\u2019t help feeling it\u2019s a mistake to believe that the passion we feel during creation ever truly shows in the work we produce. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and color tell us only about form and color\u2014that\u2019s all. It often strikes me that art hides the artist far more completely than it ever reveals him. So when I got this offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the centerpiece of my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I understand now that you were right. The picture can\u2019t be shown. Don\u2019t be angry with me, Dorian, for what I\u2019ve confessed to you.\"}"
