          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4cfdb70d25de3425564ac116\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s feelings overpowered her ambition, and, for the young man\u2019s sake, she gave up any further plotting for Sir Walter. Yet she has skill as well as emotion, and it now seems uncertain whose schemes will win in the end, his or hers. After stopping her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he may yet be coaxed and flattered into making her Lady William instead.\\n\\nNo one can doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were shocked and humiliated by losing their companion and discovering her deceit. They could turn to their important cousins for comfort, certainly, but they must feel keenly how empty it is to flatter and follow others without being flattered and followed in return.\\n\\nAnne, reassured early that Lady Russell now meant to cherish Captain Wentworth as she should, found no shadow on her future happiness except the painful thought that she had no relations to give him whom a sensible man could value. In that, she felt her inferiority sharply. Their unequal fortunes did not disturb her for a moment; but having no family ready to welcome and appreciate him properly\u2014nothing respectable, harmonious, or kindly to offer in return for the worth and eager welcome she received from his brothers and sisters\u2014caused as much pain as her mind could feel amid such a joyful future. She could only add two friends to his list, Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. Yet he was quite willing to attach himself to them both. Lady Russell, despite all her former mistakes, he now truly esteemed. Though he could not pretend she had been right in separating them at first, he was ready to praise nearly everything else about her; and Mrs Smith had many reasons to win his quick and lasting regard.\\n\\nMrs Smith\u2019s recent good deeds toward Anne were reason enough, and their marriage, instead of costing her a friend, gave her two. She was their first visitor when they settled, and Captain Wentworth repaid her kindness by helping her recover her late husband\u2019s West Indian property. He wrote letters, handled business, and worked through every small difficulty with the energy and courage of a bold man and loyal friend, fully repaying all the services she had given or ever intended to give his wife.\"}"
