          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2f9c6ec78b9b85324c1ee64\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With no work in his hands, he pressed the knuckles of his right hand into the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left into the hollow of his right, then swept a hand across his bearded chin, over and over without a pause. Drawing him back from the wandering state he always fell into after he spoke was like trying to revive a frail person from a faint, or like trying, in hope of one last revelation, to hold back the spirit of a dying man.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI certainly did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was neither sigh nor groan, he bent to work again until the silence broke once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d asked Mr. Lorry, staring steadily at him.\\n\\nHis gaunt eyes turned to Defarge as if to pass the question on; when no help came, they dropped to the ground and then crept back to Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe drifted away again, even for minutes, still repeating those measured motions with his hands. At last his gaze slowly returned to the face it had left; when it settled there, he started and spoke like a man waking suddenly, picking up a thought from the night before.\\n\\n\u201cI asked to teach myself, and after a long time I received permission with great difficulty, and I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he held out his hand for the shoe taken from him, Mr. Lorry, still looking intently at his face, said:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the floor, and he stared fixedly at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry placed his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nThe prisoner of many years sat staring in turn at Mr. Lorry and Defarge. Faint traces of a once keen intelligence on his forehead forced their way through the dark fog that enveloped him. They were quickly clouded again; they faded and vanished. But they had been there.\"}"
