          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"283991bf10d28c7b712583b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want to see you securely well connected, and for that reason it\u2019s best to keep your circle as refined as possible; so, if you\u2019re still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your friendship with his sisters won\u2019t pull you into knowing his wife, who will probably be some farmer\u2019s daughter with no education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course. Yes. Not that I believe Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who hadn\u2019t been educated and properly brought up. Still, I don\u2019t want to set my opinion against yours\u2014and I\u2019m sure I won\u2019t want to know his wife. I\u2019ll always think highly of the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and I\u2019d be very sorry to give them up, because they\u2019re just as well educated as me. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then yes, I\u2019d better keep away from her if possible.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her through the twists and turns of this speech and saw no worrying signs of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted there was no stronger bond, and that Harriet wouldn\u2019t be a serious obstacle to any friendly plan of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day while walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot; after a respectful glance at Emma, he looked with obvious happiness at Harriet. Emma was glad for the chance to inspect him, and by walking a few yards ahead while they talked, her sharp eye quickly became familiar with Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was neat, and he looked like a sensible young man, but he had no other advantages; once compared with gentlemen, she thought he would lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s affections. Harriet did care about manners; she had commented on her father\u2019s gentleness with admiration and surprise. Mr. Martin looked as if he didn\u2019t know what manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, because Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; then Harriet came running to her with a smiling face and a flutter of excitement, which Miss Woodhouse hoped to calm soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust imagine our meeting him! How very odd! Pure chance, he said, that he hadn\u2019t gone around by Randalls. He didn\u2019t think we ever walked this road. He thought we walked toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
