          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2498c69f5e9e5f19b3b706c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The omission fell back on the offender; for when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no condolence arrived at Kellynch, and there was every reason to fear that the Dalrymples now considered the connection ended. How to settle this anxious matter and be received as cousins again was the question: and it was one that, in a sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot thought trivial. Family connections were always worth keeping, good company always worth seeking; Lady Dalrymple had taken a house for three months in Laura Place and would be living in style. She had been in Bath the year before, and Lady Russell had heard her praised as a charming woman. It was very desirable to renew the connection, if it could be done without compromising the Elliots\u2019 dignity.\\n\\nSir Walter, however, insisted on his own approach, and finally wrote a very polished letter full of explanation, regret, and entreaty to his right honorable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot admired the letter, but it accomplished its aim by producing three scrawled lines from the Dowager Viscountess. She was very much honored and would be happy to know them. The hard part was done, the rewards began. They called in Laura Place, and they received the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honorable Miss Carteret to be displayed wherever they would show best; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place,\u201d \u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret,\u201d were repeated to everyone.\\n\\nAnne felt ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been delightful, she would still have been embarrassed by the fuss they caused, but they were nothing special. They had no superior manners, accomplishments, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple was called a charming woman simply because she had a smile and a polite answer for all. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she would never have been endured in Camden Place if not for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted she had expected something better; yet it was still \u201can acquaintance worth having.\u201d When Anne expressed her opinion of them to Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, but insisted that, as family connections, as good company, as people who would draw other good company, they had their worth. Anne smiled and said, \u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of clever, well-informed people, who have a great deal of conversation; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
