          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"911fabb4c543f0198c33c557\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To glimpse a star by looking slightly away\u2014using the outer parts of the retina, which are more sensitive to weak light\u2014lets us see the star clearly. It gives the best sense of its glow, a glow that fades as soon as we stare straight at it. More rays strike the eye when we look directly, yet in the sidelong glance there is a finer ability to understand. Too much depth of scrutiny confuses and weakens thought; we can even make Venus disappear from the sky by staring too long, too hard, or too directly.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let us make some inquiry for ourselves before forming an opinion. The investigation will amuse us,\u201d (I found the word odd in that context but said nothing) \u201cand besides, Le Bon once did me a service, and I am not ungrateful. We will go see the place with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and can easily get permission.\u201d\\n\\nPermission secured, we set off immediately for the Rue Morgue. It is one of those poor streets between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. Afternoon was far advanced when we arrived, as that district lies far from where we lived. The house was easy to spot, since many people still stood across the way, staring up at the closed shutters with idle curiosity. It was an ordinary Paris house, with a gateway and, on one side, a glassed-in watch-box with a sliding panel, showing it to be a concierge\u2019s lodge. Before going inside we walked up the street, turned down an alley, and after another turn entered the rear of the building\u2014Dupin examining the whole neighborhood and the house itself with a careful attention whose purpose I could not guess.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps, returned to the front door, rang, and, after showing our credentials, were admitted by the officers in charge. We went upstairs into the room where the body of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye had been found, and where both victims still lay. The disorder in the room had, as usual, been left untouched. I saw nothing beyond what the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux\u201d had reported. Dupin inspected everything, including the bodies themselves. We then went through the other rooms and out into the yard, a gendarme accompanying us the whole time.\"}"
