          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ce1d65c49577286a816316f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe would be such a delightful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed, but added, \u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith is also just such a charming young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear it\u2014but Jane Fairfax, everyone knows, is so accomplished and so superior\u2014and exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis topic was discussed pleasantly, and other subjects of similar importance followed and ended just as harmoniously. Yet the evening did not finish without a small return of agitation. The gruel arrived and prompted a great deal of conversation\u2014praise and comments\u2014firm opinions about how wholesome it was for everyone, and rather sharp criticisms of many houses that could never produce it properly; but unfortunately, among the failures her daughter could cite, the most recent, and so the most notable, was her own cook at South End, a temporary hire who never could understand what she meant by a basin of nice smooth gruel, thin but not too thin. She had asked for it many times, but never received anything acceptable. This opened a dangerous topic.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. The sound murmured in Emma\u2019s ear, \u201cAh! there is no end to the sad consequences of your visiting South End. It is too upsetting to discuss.\u201d For a moment she hoped he would stay silent and think it over, enough to enjoy his own smooth gruel again. After a few minutes, however, he began, \u201cI shall always regret that you went to the sea this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you regret it, sir? I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you had to go to the sea, it should not have been South End. South End is unhealthy. Perry was surprised you chose South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think so, but it is really a mistake, sir. We were all perfectly healthy there and never had the slightest inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to consider the place unhealthy. I am sure he is reliable, for he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry spent a week at Cromer once, and he considers it the best of all the sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
