          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66897e4cbb90d15e266d0556\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning set for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to see Kellynch Hall, Anne naturally took her usual walk to Lady Russell\u2019s to stay out of the way until it was over; once it was, she immediately regretted missing the chance to see them. The meeting went wonderfully well and settled everything at once. Each lady had already been inclined to reach an agreement, so each saw only good manners in the other; as for the men, the Admiral\u2019s hearty good humor and open, trusting generosity had to affect Sir Walter, who had also been coaxed into his most polished behavior by Mr. Shepherd telling him that, according to rumor, the Admiral knew him as the perfect example of a gentleman. The house, grounds, furniture, the Crofts themselves, the terms, the timing, everything and everyone met approval; and Mr. Shepherd\u2019s clerks were set to work without a single preliminary clause needing alteration from \u201cThis indenture showeth.\u201d Sir Walter, without hesitation, declared the Admiral the best-looking sailor he had ever seen, and even said that if his own valet could do the Admiral\u2019s hair he would not be ashamed to be seen with him anywhere; while the Admiral, with matching warmth, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI thought we\u2019d make a deal quickly, my dear, despite what they told us at Taunton. The baronet will never set the Thames on fire, but there seems no harm in him.\u201d Each set of compliments would have been considered about equal. The Crofts would take possession at Michaelmas; and since Sir Walter planned to leave for Bath during the previous month, they had no time to lose in making the necessary arrangements. Lady Russell, sure that Anne would not be allowed any influence or importance in choosing their new house, disliked seeing her rushed away so soon and wanted to find a way for her to stay until she could take her to Bath herself after Christmas. But because she had her own commitments that would bring her away from Kellynch for several weeks, she could not give the full invitation she wished. And though Anne dreaded Bath\u2019s possible September heat in all its white glare and hated giving up the sweet yet mournful influence of the country in autumn, she decided that, all things considered, she did not wish to stay.\"}"
