{"request_id":"433596f9b84f0ce98e6e32e9","rewritten_text":"After this, a few more loops were detached from the rim, another section of the fabric was fed through, and the freed loops were fastened to their proper buttons. This allowed me to slide the entire upper part of the bag between the netting and the hoop. It was obvious the hoop would now drop down inside the car, leaving the entire weight of the car and its contents supported only by the buttons. At first glance this seemed unreliable, but it wasn’t: the buttons were not only very strong on their own, they were set so close together that each one bore only a small fraction of the total load. Even if the car and its cargo had been three times heavier, I wouldn’t have worried. I then lifted the hoop again inside the gum-elastic covering and braced it nearly back to its original height with three light poles I had prepared for the job. This kept the bag stretched at the top and held the lower part of the netting in its proper position. The last step was to seal the mouth of the enclosure, which I did by gathering the folds of material and twisting them very tightly inside with a kind of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering fitted around the car, I had inserted three round panes of thick but clear glass, through which I could easily look in any horizontal direction. In the section of fabric forming the bottom there was a fourth window of the same type, matching a small opening in the car’s floor, so I could look straight down. Because I couldn’t find a way to install a similar window overhead—owing to the way the top opening had to be closed and the resulting wrinkles in the cloth—I had no view directly overhead. That was of little importance, because even if I had managed to set a window at the top, the balloon itself would have blocked the view.

“About a foot beneath one of the side windows there was a circular opening eight inches wide, edged with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded like a screw. Into this rim I screwed the large tube of the condenser, the rest of the machine remaining inside the gum-elastic chamber.”"}

