          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dcb59df8ecf1ec4278784bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had elapsed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons were almost complete in their cycle\u2014it was now late May; the woods were clad in their freshest green, and the sweet scent of new-cut grass floated over the fields. I believed the balmy air and fair visage of Nature might steady him with mild sensations, gifting him gentle feelings of peace and love so that I might gain the confidence I longed to draw from him.\\n\\nI therefore chose the evening of one such day for my effort. Inviting him to walk, I guided him to a nearby beech wood whose light shade protected us from the slanting, piercing rays of the setting sun. After some time of silent walking, I seated myself with him upon a mossy knoll. It is strange, yet even now the spot seems present before me\u2014the slender, smooth trunks were many of them entwined with ivy, whose shining darkest-green leaves contrasted with the pale bark and the light foliage of the young beech shoots springing from their parent stems. The short grass mingled with moss and was partly strewn with the dead leaves of the previous autumn which the wind had sometimes gathered into little heaps\u2014there were a few moss-covered stumps near. The breeze stirred the leaves gently, and through their green canopy the bright blue sky was visible. As evening advanced, the distant trunks glowed red from the sun, the wind wholly ceased, and a few birds passed over us on their way to rest.\\n\\nHere we sat together, and when you learn all that took place\u2014all that tore our spirits in this tranquil spot, which save for wild passions might have been a paradise to us\u2014you will not wonder that I recall it as I gazed upon it so its calm might lend me quiet and embolden me with persuasive words. I saw all this and idly noted each detail in my mind while striving to arrange my thoughts in proper order for my purpose. My heart beat quickly as I urged myself toward speech; I was resolved not to be refused, yet I trembled to imagine how my words might strike him. At last, with great hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the overwhelming affection\u2014you bore me when you first returned will, I hope, excuse my daring to speak to you, though I do so with a daughter\u2019s tenderness, yet also with the liberty of a friend and equal.\u201d\"}"
