          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e7220b911b0e5fee788271c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would be settled among those who cherished her and possessed more judgment than she did; secluded enough for security, yet busy enough for spirits. She would neither be drawn into temptation nor left exposed to it. She would be proper and contented; and Emma acknowledged her to be the most fortunate creature alive, to have inspired so steady and enduring an attachment in such a man; or, if not quite the most fortunate, surpassed only by herself.\\n\\nHarriet, inevitably occupied with the Martins, was less and less at Hartfield; which was unregretted. The closeness between her and Emma had to decline; their friendship must soften into a calmer benevolence; and, happily, what should happen, and must happen, appeared already to be beginning, in the most gentle and natural way.\\n\\nBefore September ended, Emma accompanied Harriet to church, and witnessed her hand placed in Robert Martin\u2019s with a perfect contentment, which no recollections\u2014even of Mr. Elton standing there\u2014could diminish. Perhaps, indeed, at that moment she hardly regarded Mr. Elton except as the clergyman whose benediction at the altar might shortly fall upon herself. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, though the last couple of the three to be engaged, were the first to marry.\\n\\nJane Fairfax had already left Highbury and returned to the comforts of her beloved Campbells. The Mr. Churchills likewise were in town; and they only awaited November.\\n\\nThe intervening month was the period Emma and Mr. Knightley had fixed, as far as they dared. They resolved their marriage should take place while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, allowing them the planned fortnight away on a seaside tour. John and Isabella, and every other friend, agreed in approving it. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how was Mr. Woodhouse to be persuaded? He had never yet mentioned their marriage except as a remote concern.\\n\\nWhen first approached on the subject, he was so wretched that they nearly despaired. A second mention, in truth, caused less agony. He started to believe it would happen, and that he could not prevent it\u2014a very encouraging step for a mind moving toward resignation. Still he was not content. Indeed he appeared so far from it that his daughter\u2019s resolve failed. She could not endure seeing him in torment, aware he imagined himself forsaken; and though her reason almost yielded to the assurances of both Mr. Knightleys that once the event was past his agitation would soon subside, she faltered\u2014she could not go on.\"}"
