          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"002778e174c377d6aa1e9b3f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo, what conclusion have you reached? What impression have I made on your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nI felt my skin crawl when Dupin asked this. \u201cA madman,\u201d I answered, \u201cmust have done it\u2014some raving maniac who escaped from a nearby Maison de Sant\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some ways,\u201d he said, \u201cyour idea isn\u2019t off the mark. But the voices of madmen, even in their wildest fits, never match the strange voice heard on those stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and their language, however incoherent their words, still keeps the structure of syllables. Besides, a madman\u2019s hair is not like what I\u2019m holding. I pulled this little tuft out of Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s tightly clenched fingers. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, shaken to the core. \u201cThis hair is highly unusual\u2014this is not human hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI never said it was,\u201d he replied. \u201cBut before we settle that point, take a look at this sketch I\u2019ve drawn on this paper. It\u2019s an exact copy of what one witness described as \u2018dark bruises and deep indentations of fingernails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what Messrs. Dumas and Etienne called \u2018a series of livid spots, clearly the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou can see,\u201d he went on, laying the paper on the table, \u201cthat the drawing shows a firm, steady grip. There\u2019s no slipping. Each finger held on\u2014likely until the victim died\u2014with the same fierce grasp it first sank in with. Now try to place all your fingers at once into the impressions as you see them.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but I couldn\u2019t manage it.\\n\\n\u201cMaybe we aren\u2019t testing this properly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper is flat, but a human throat is round. Here\u2019s a piece of wood about the same circumference as a throat. Wrap the drawing around it and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI followed his instructions, but the mismatch was even clearer. \u201cThese marks,\u201d I said, \u201cwere not made by a human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read this passage from Cuvier,\u201d Dupin replied.\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical description of the large tawny orangutan of the East Indian islands. Its immense size, tremendous strength and agility, savage ferocity, and talent for mimicry are common knowledge. I grasped the full terror of this murder immediately.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the fingers,\u201d I said when I finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
