          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37ceca9e97f635bf0595f219\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019s generous hospitality was a tempting bait, and together with Tom\u2019s urging it soon won. They decided not to tell anyone about the night\u2019s plan. Then Tom realized Huck might arrive that very night and give the signal. The thought drained much of his excitement. Still, he couldn\u2019t bear to give up the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019s house. Why should he, he reasoned\u2014the signal hadn\u2019t come the night before, so why expect it tonight? The certain fun of the evening outweighed the uncertain treasure, and, like a typical boy, he chose to follow the stronger temptation and promised himself not to think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town the ferry stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the forest depths and rocky heights echoed far and near with shouts and laughter. They tried every possible way to get hot and tired, and after a while the wanderers straggled back to camp with serious appetites, and then the feast\u2019s destruction began. Afterward came a cooling spell of rest and conversation beneath spreading oaks. Before long someone shouted:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. Bundles of candles were gathered, and immediately there was a mad scramble up the hill. The cave\u2019s mouth was partway up the hillside, shaped like a giant letter A. The massive oak door stood unbarred. Inside lay a small chamber, as cold as an icehouse, walled by solid limestone that sweated with damp chill. It felt romantic and mysterious to stand there in the deep gloom and look out at the sunlit green valley. But the solemn mood soon faded and the roughhousing resumed. As soon as someone lit a candle, everyone rushed at its owner; a struggle and brave defense followed, but the candle was quickly knocked down or blown out, and then came a burst of laughter and another chase. But all things end. Soon the line of explorers filed down the steep slope of the main passage, their flickering row of lights just enough to reveal the lofty rock walls nearly meeting sixty feet overhead. This main passage was only eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps other tall and even narrower cracks branched off to either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s Cave was a great maze of twisting aisles that ran into one another, split apart, and led nowhere.\"}"
