          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c420969b852112d02cdc0ee3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now arrive at the account of myself. Of the opening stages of my life there is little worth noting, so I will be concise; yet I must linger somewhat over my childhood, so it may be understood how, when one hope collapsed, existence became a void, and how, when the single affection allowed me was withered, my life perished along with it.\\n\\nI have already mentioned that my aunt bore hardly any resemblance to my father. I am persuaded that, though guiltless of malice, she possessed the chilliest heart that ever beat in a human breast\u2014entirely incapable of tenderness. She received me under her guardianship merely from a sense of obligation; but she had long been accustomed to solitude, undisturbed by childish noise and chatter, and would not permit me to ruffle her calm. She had never married, and for the past five years had lived completely alone upon an estate descending to her from her mother, on the banks of Loch Lomond in Scotland. My father had expressed, through his letters, a desire that she should live with me at his ancestral mansion situated in lovely country outside Richmond, Yorkshire. She refused this plan, but as soon as she had settled the business our father\u2019s departure forced into her hands, she left England and carried me with her to her Scottish property.\\n\\nThe charge of me in infancy, and up to my eighth year, fell to a servant of my mother\u2019s, who had joined us in our retreat for that duty. I occupied a secluded wing of the house and saw my aunt only at fixed intervals. Twice each day she appeared: near midday she visited my nursery, and after her dinner I was led to her. She never embraced me, and throughout my stay in the room she seemed afraid I would vex her with some childish whim. My devoted nurse coached me carefully before daring to bring me into the parlour\u2014and the dread stirred by my aunt\u2019s wintry glance and clipped words was so intense that I seldom brought shame upon her lessons or broke the exemplary stillness she bid me adopt during those brief audiences.\\n\\nIn my nurse\u2019s kind supervision I roamed unchecked through our park and the neighbouring fields. Born of the sincerest love, I showed from earliest days a most sensitive nature. I cannot convey with what fervour I cherished everything\u2014yes, even the lifeless objects that surrounded me.\"}"
