          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c084ee90d0225eeda8b0bd5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Count Rouvaloff stared at him in amazement for a few moments, then, realizing he was completely serious, he wrote an address on a slip of paper, initialed it, and slid it across the table. \u201cScotland Yard would pay dearly for this address, my dear fellow.\u201d \u201cThey\u2019re not getting it,\u201d Lord Arthur said with a laugh; after shaking the young Russian\u2019s hand warmly, he ran downstairs, checked the paper, and told the driver to head for Soho Square. There he dismissed the cab, wandered down Greek Street until he reached a place called Bayle\u2019s Court, went through the archway, and found himself in a curious dead-end apparently taken over by a French laundry, as clotheslines crisscrossed between the houses and white linen fluttered in the morning air. He walked all the way to the end and knocked on a small green house. After a long wait\u2014long enough for every window in the court to blur with curious faces\u2014the door was opened by a rather rough-looking foreigner who, in very poor English, asked what business he had there. Lord Arthur handed him the paper Count Rouvaloff had given him; as soon as the man saw it, he bowed and showed him into a shabby front parlor on the ground floor. A moment later Herr Winckelkopf, as he was known in England, bustled in with a wine-stained napkin around his neck and a fork in his left hand. \u201cCount Rouvaloff has given me an introduction to you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, bowing. \u201cI would like a brief meeting with you on a business matter. My name is Smith\u2014Robert Smith\u2014and I want you to supply me with an explosive clock.\u201d \u201cDelighted to meet you, Lord Arthur,\u201d the cheerful little German said with a laugh. \u201cDon\u2019t look so frightened; it\u2019s my job to know everyone, and I remember spotting you one evening at Lady Windermere\u2019s. Is her ladyship well? Would you mind sitting with me while I finish my breakfast? The p\u00e2t\u00e9 is excellent, and my friends tell me my Rhine wine is better than anything they get at the German Embassy.\u201d Before Lord Arthur had recovered from being recognized, he found himself in the back room, drinking exquisite Marcobr\u00fcnner from a pale yellow hock glass marked with the Imperial monogram and chatting like an old friend with the notorious conspirator. \u201cExplosive clocks,\u201d Herr Winckelkopf said, \u201cdon\u2019t travel well for foreign delivery. Even if they slip through Customs, the train schedules are so erratic that they usually explode before they reach their intended destination.\u201d\"}"
