{"request_id":"2dd83bcc885e9f3a0c84f9bd","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the staircase he steadied himself and decided to unleash his famous burst of demonic laughter, a trick he had found useful on more than one occasion. People said it had turned Lord Raker’s wig grey overnight and had definitely driven off three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses before their month was finished. He let loose his most dreadful laugh, making the old vaulted roof echo over and over, but just as the last terrible echo faded, a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing gown. “I’m afraid you’re quite unwell,” she said, “so I’ve brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it’s indigestion, you’ll find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost glared at her in rage and immediately began preparing to transform himself into a huge black dog, a feat for which he was rightly famous and which the family doctor always blamed for Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton, going permanently mad. The sound of footsteps approaching, however, made him falter in his deadly plan, so he merely turned faintly phosphorescent and vanished with a deep churchyard groan just as the twins ran up to him.

Once he reached his room he collapsed completely and fell into violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s crude materialism were naturally extremely aggravating, but what really upset him was that he had been unable to wear the suit of armor. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be awed by the sight of a Specter in Armor, if not from good sense, then at least out of respect for their national poet Longfellow, whose graceful and charming poems had helped him pass many weary hours when the Cantervilles were staying in London. Besides, it was his own armor. He had worn it to great acclaim at the Kenilworth tournament and had been graciously complimented on it by none other than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet when he put it on, he had been utterly crushed by the weight of the massive breastplate and steel helmet, and had toppled heavily onto the flagstones, banging both knees badly and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was very ill and barely left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. Still, by taking meticulous care of himself, he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

