          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"15aa927f0897de1da3389327\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You hinted at it yourself two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember\u2014the first time the Church tested its strength against us and then decided to wait for a better season. Well, how did you set up the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI run twelve very strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014from a big dynamo in the cave, with only a positive and a negative brush.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires go out from the cave and enclose a flat circle a hundred yards across. They form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014so twelve circles inside each other\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight; continue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires are fixed to heavy oak posts set only three feet apart, sunk five feet into the ground.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s good and solid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. The wires have no ground connection outside the cave. They go out from the positive brush of the dynamo; there\u2019s a ground connection through the negative brush; the other ends of the wires come back to the cave, and each one is grounded separately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s too costly\u2014wastes power. You need only one ground connection, through the negative brush. The other end of every wire must come back into the cave and be fastened separately without any ground connection. Now look at the savings. A cavalry charge slams into the fence; you\u2019re using no power, spending nothing, because there\u2019s only one ground connection until those horses hit the wire. The instant they touch it they complete the circuit with the negative brush through the ground and drop dead. Don\u2019t you see? You use no energy until it\u2019s needed; your lightning waits like a loaded gun, but it costs you nothing until you fire it. Yes, stick to the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I don\u2019t know how I missed that. It\u2019s not only cheaper but more effective, because broken or tangled wires cause no trouble.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, especially if we have an indicator in the cave and disconnect any broken wire. Go on. The Gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014that\u2019s ready. In the center of the innermost circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I set up a battery of thirteen Gatling guns with plenty of ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it. They cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights arrive, there\u2019ll be music. The edge of the cliff above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve put a wire fence there too, and a Gatling.\u201d\"}"
