          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"91df72af76c743beee198c5c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had secured an excellent residence in Camden Place, a lofty, dignified spot befitting a man of importance; he and Elizabeth were installed there entirely to their liking. \\n\\nAnne crossed the threshold with a sinking spirit, bracing for a confinement of many months, and whispering to herself, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Yet an unexpected warmth in their greeting heartened her. Her father and sister were eager to display the new house and furnishings, and received her with real civility. Her presence making a fourth at dinner was even remarked upon as a benefit. \\n\\nMrs Clay was all pleasantness and smiles, but, as always, her courtesies seemed automatic. Anne had anticipated that she would perform all that was proper at first meeting; what surprised her was the genuine complacency of the others. They were obviously in high spirits, and she was soon to hear the reasons. They had no desire to listen to her. Having positioned themselves for compliments about being lamented in their former neighbourhood\u2014praise Anne could not offer\u2014they put only a few mild questions before insisting the conversation be entirely theirs. Uppercross roused no interest, and Kellynch scarcely any; the subject was all Bath. \\n\\nThey delighted in assuring her that Bath exceeded their expectations in every particular. Their house, beyond doubt, was the best in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms possessed advantages over every other they had seen or heard about, and the superiority extended equally to the arrangement and taste of the furnishings. Their acquaintance was highly coveted. Everyone wished to visit them. They had declined numerous introductions, yet still received cards from people wholly unknown to them. \\n\\nHere were abundant sources of enjoyment. Could Anne be surprised that her father and sister were content? She might refrain from surprise, yet she could only sigh that her father felt no disgrace in the alteration of his circumstances, saw nothing to lament in relinquishing the duties and dignity of a resident landowner, and found so much to be proud of in the trivialities of town life. She must sigh, and smile, and marvel too, when Elizabeth flung open the folding-doors and strode triumphantly from one drawing-room into the next, exulting in their size; marvel at the notion of that woman, who had once presided over Kellynch Hall, feeling pride in expanses bounded by walls scarce thirty feet apart. \\n\\nBut their happiness had another ingredient. They possessed Mr Elliot as well.\"}"
