          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bef1f6835d58b5ba6c5bfd99\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I met the peculiar stranger I want to describe while touring Warwick Castle. Three traits drew me to him: his straightforward innocence, his astonishing knowledge of ancient armor, and how calming his presence was\u2014probably because he did all the talking. As modest visitors do, we drifted toward the rear of the group being guided through, and he immediately began saying things that captivated me. As he spoke\u2014softly, pleasantly, smoothly\u2014he seemed to slip, almost without my noticing, out of the present day and into some distant era, some long-forgotten land; gradually he cast such a spell over me that I felt I was moving among the ghosts, shadows, dust, and mold of a gray antiquity, conversing with one of its relics. He talked about Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Lancelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table just as I would talk about my closest friends, enemies, or neighbors\u2014and the longer he went on, the older he seemed, impossibly old, faded, dry, musty, ancient. Finally he turned to me and said, as casually as someone might talk about the weather, \u201cYou know about transmigration of souls; do you know about the transposition of eras\u2014and bodies?\u201d I said I had never heard of it. He seemed so uninterested\u2014exactly like someone making small talk about the weather\u2014that he didn\u2019t notice whether I answered or not. A brief silence followed, broken by the droning voice of the paid guide: \u201cAncient hauberk, sixth century, time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramore the Desirous; note the round hole in the chain mail on the left breast; no known explanation; believed to have been made by a bullet after the invention of firearms\u2014maybe vandalism by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d My new acquaintance smiled\u2014not a modern smile, but one that must have vanished from general use centuries ago\u2014and muttered, apparently to himself, \u201cKnow this, I saw it happen.\u201d Then, after a pause, he added, \u201cI did it myself.\u201d By the time I had recovered from the jolt of that remark, he was gone. All evening I sat by my fire at the Warwick Arms, wrapped in a dream of bygone days while the rain battered the windows and the wind howled around the eaves and corners. Now and then I opened old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s enchanting book and feasted on its wonders and adventures, drank in the fragrance of its forgotten names, and drifted back into the dream.\"}"
