          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"45c23b34676f762fd5f1384a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I repeated to myself that beautiful passage describing Dante entering the earthly Paradise, and I imagined how sweet it would be, when I walked along those lovely banks, to see the car of light descend bringing back my long-lost parent. While I waited for that moment, I pictured winding a garland from the flowers there and crowning myself in joy. I would sing sul margine d\u2019un rio, my father\u2019s favorite song, and my voice, gliding through the still air, would tell him in whatever bower he waited for our reunion that his daughter had come. Then the mark of misery would fade from my brow, and I would lift my eyes without fear to meet his, which always shone with the soft glow of innocent love. When I thought of the magic in those deep eyes, I wept softly, afraid that my sobs might disturb the fairy scene. \\n\\nI was so completely lost in this daydream that I walked on without noticing where I went, until I stooped to pick a flower for my wreath on that bleak plain where no flower grew. Then I woke from my dream and realized I had no idea where I was. \\n\\nThe sun had set, and the rosy light that the clouds kept from his descent was nearly gone. A wind swept the plain. I looked around and saw nothing to tell me my location. I had lost my way and could not find the path. I wandered, but the growing darkness blurred every possible guide. At last the blackest night covered everything. I grew tired, and since my servant was to sleep in the nearby village and would not be alarmed by my absence, and since I was safe in this wild place from any intruder, I decided to spend the night there. I was too weary to walk farther. The air was cold, but I did not care about discomfort; I thought I was hardened to the weather after two years of solitude when no change of season stopped my constant wandering. \\n\\nI lay on the grass, wrapped in darkness without the faintest ray of light. Nothing made a sound, for the deep night had lulled the insects to sleep, and they were the only creatures living on that lonely spot where no tree or shrub offered shelter to anything else. A wondrous silence filled the air, calming my senses yet stirring my soul. My mind rushed from image to image and seemed to grasp eternity.\"}"
