          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"39b5381a89f54d38e538722e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There arrives a season in every properly fashioned boy\u2019s life when he burns to run off somewhere and dig up buried treasure. That fever struck Tom one day. He set out to track down Joe Harper but came up empty. Then he went hunting for Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Before long he bumped into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom drew him aside to a secluded spot and laid the scheme before him. Huck was agreeable. Huck was always ready to join any undertaking that promised amusement and demanded no cash, for he possessed a vexing surplus of that kind of time that isn\u2019t money. \u201cWhere we going to dig?\u201d asked Huck.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it planted all over?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, sir, it ain\u2019t. It\u2019s hidden in mighty particular spots, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests buried under the tip of a limb of an old dead tree, right where the shadow falls at midnight; but mostly under the floors of ha\u2019nted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho puts it there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, of course\u2014who else would you reckon? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it belonged to me I wouldn\u2019t hide it; I\u2019d spend it and have fun.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t. They always tuck it away and leave it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they aim to, but they generally forget the signs, or else they die. Anyway, it stays there for ages and gets rusty; and eventually somebody comes on an old yellow paper that tells how to track the marks\u2014a paper you\u2019ve got to puzzle over for about a week because it\u2019s mainly signs and hy\u2019roglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro\u2014what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHy\u2019roglyphics\u2014pictures and such, you know, that look like they don\u2019t mean a thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGot one of them papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how you going to find the marks?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t want any marks. They always stash it under a ha\u2019nted house or on an island, or beneath a dead tree with one branch sticking out. Well, we\u2019ve worked on Jackson\u2019s Island a bit and we can try it again sometime; and there\u2019s the old ha\u2019nted house up the Still-House branch, and there are scads of dead-limb trees\u2014cartloads of \u2019em.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHear that! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how will you know which one to pick?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGo at all of \u2019em!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that\u2019ll eat up the whole summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if it does? Imagine you dig up a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest crammed with di\u2019monds. How about that?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes blazed.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s bully. Plenty bully for me. Just give me the hundred dollars and I don\u2019t care a rap for di\u2019monds.\u201d\"}"
