          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dd97c94b7602f7c322fdf79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, never opened any book for pleasure except the Baronetage. In its pages he found something to fill idle hours and to soothe him when he was upset. There his mind stirred with admiration and respect as he studied the few remaining families with ancient patents; there any unwelcome feelings about his home life slipped into pity and contempt while he scanned the countless new creations of the last century. And if nothing else in the volume could hold him, he only had to read his own entry to feel constant interest. The book always fell open to this page:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq., of South Park in the county of Gloucester, by whom (she died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThis was exactly how the printer had first set the paragraph; but Sir Walter improved it, as he thought, by adding for the benefit of himself and his family these words after Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq., of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and by carefully inserting the exact day of the month on which he lost his wife.\\n\\nThen came the family history and rise, told in the usual terms: how they first settled in Cheshire; how they were noted in Dugdale; how they served as high sheriff, represented a borough in three parliaments, showed loyalty, and gained the dignity of a baronetcy in the first year of Charles II, along with all the Marys and Elizabeths they married. Altogether it formed two neat duodecimo pages and ended with the arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed by Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting once more:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character, vanity about his looks and his status. He had been remarkably handsome in youth, and at fifty-four he was still a very fine-looking man. Few women thought more about their appearance than he did, and no valet to any newly made lord could take greater pleasure in the place he held in society. He ranked the blessing of beauty as second only to the blessing of a baronetcy; and the Sir Walter Elliot who possessed both was always the object of his sincerest admiration and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and his rank did earn one genuine attachment, for through them he had secured a wife whose character was far better than anything he himself deserved.\"}"
        }
