          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6359248aa70a2d6fd90103cb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Spirits of storm, take me in! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever\u2014unless some kinder power has planned another end, so that after long endurance I may be rewarded and feel my heart beat beside another heart like mine.\\n\\nThe Tiber, nature\u2019s own roadway threading her continent, lay at my feet, its banks lined with boats. I would take a few books, supplies, and my dog, climb into one of them, and drift down the current to the sea. Staying near the land, I would follow the glowing shores and sunny capes of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and Calabria, brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis, then with fearless aim\u2014what had I to lose?\u2014skim toward Malta and the outer Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople; the sight of its familiar towers and harbors belongs to another life than mine now. I would follow the coasts of Asia Minor and Syria, pass the seven mouths of the Nile, then steer north again until, after losing sight of forgotten Carthage and empty Libya, I reached the Pillars of Hercules. And then\u2014no matter where\u2014the slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean may be my home before I finish this long voyage, or the arrow of sickness may pierce my heart as I drift alone on the heaving Mediterranean. Or perhaps, somewhere I stop, I may find what I seek\u2014a companion; and if not, then for endless years, decrepit and gray-headed\u2014my youth already buried with those I loved\u2014the lone wanderer will still set his sail and grip the tiller, still obey the heavenly winds, forever rounding promontory after promontory, dropping anchor in bay after bay. He will still plow the barren ocean, leaving behind the green lands of Europe, down along Africa\u2019s tawny shore, and after battling the fierce seas of the Cape, may at last moor his worn skiff in some creek shaded by the spicy groves of the fragrant islands in the far Indian Ocean.\\n\\nThese are wild dreams. Yet since they came to me a week ago, as I stood on the summit of St. Peter\u2019s, they have ruled my imagination. I have picked out my boat and loaded my scant stores. I have chosen a few books\u2014chiefly Homer and Shakespeare\u2014though the libraries of the world lie open to me, and I can restock anywhere. I expect no better change; but the dull present is unbearable.\"}"
