{"request_id":"88c80bb1710b67b43d9e3557","rewritten_text":"We endured some mournful hours until eleven, when the trial was set to begin. My father and the rest of the household were obliged to attend as witnesses, and I went with them to the courtroom. Throughout this miserable parody of justice I suffered continual torment. The question was whether the fruits of my curiosity and reckless experiments would bring about the deaths of two fellow creatures: one an innocent, joyous infant; the other slaughtered with a savagery so aggravated that the murder would remain notorious in horror. Justine too was a deserving girl endowed with qualities that might have made her life contented; now everything was to be effaced in a shameful grave, and I was the cause! I would a thousand times rather have proclaimed myself guilty of the crime laid to her charge, yet I was absent when it occurred, and such an avowal would have been taken for a madman’s delirium and could not have cleared the one who suffered through me.

Justine’s bearing was composed. She wore mourning, and her face, ever attractive, was made, by the solemnity of her emotions, surpassingly lovely. Still she seemed assured of her innocence and refrained from trembling, though a multitude stared at and cursed her, for whatever sympathy her beauty might once have inspired was erased from the spectators’ minds by their imaginings of the enormity imputed to her. She remained serene, though that serenity was plainly forced; and as her previous agitation had been cited as proof of her guilt, she braced herself to appear courageous. On entering the court she cast her eyes around and soon discovered our place. A tear seemed to film her gaze when she saw us, but she quickly mastered it, and a look of mournful tenderness appeared to proclaim her complete innocence.

The trial commenced, and after the prosecutor declared the accusation, a number of witnesses were summoned. Various odd circumstances aligned against her, enough to shake any who lacked the certainty of her innocence that I possessed. She had been abroad the entire night when the murder occurred, and toward morning a market-woman had observed her near the spot where the child’s corpse was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked in a strange way and returned only a confused, incoherent response. She reached the house around eight, and when someone asked where she had passed the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently inquired whether anything had been heard of him."}

