          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4dfdd3f1aef16e053b8fc7a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, midnight boomed from the tall tower at Westminster, and with each strike of the deep bell the night seemed to shiver. Then the railway lights blinked out, leaving a single lamp glowing like a huge ruby on a towering mast, and the city\u2019s roar faded.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and wandered toward Blackfriars. How unreal everything appeared, like some strange dream! The houses across the river seemed built from darkness itself. It was as if silver and shadow had remade the world. The enormous dome of St. Paul\u2019s loomed like a bubble in the dim air.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle he spotted a man leaning over the parapet, and when he came closer the man looked up, the gaslight shining directly on his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the palm-reader! That fat, flabby face, the gold-rimmed glasses, the wan, weak smile, and the sensual mouth could belong to no one else.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A brilliant idea flashed through him, and he crept silently up from behind. In an instant he grabbed Mr. Podgers by the legs and hurled him into the Thames. There was a crude curse, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur peered anxiously over the edge but could see nothing of the palm-reader except a tall hat spinning in a whirl of moonlit water. Eventually it sank too, and no sign of Mr. Podgers remained. Once he thought he glimpsed the bulky misshapen figure swimming toward the staircase by the bridge, and a terrible feeling of failure swept over him, but it turned out to be just a reflection, and when the moon slid out from behind a cloud it vanished. At last he seemed to have fulfilled destiny\u2019s decree. He let out a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u201cHave you dropped something, sir?\u201d a voice asked suddenly behind him.\\n\\nHe turned to see a policeman with a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u201cNothing important, sergeant,\u201d he replied with a smile, and hailing a passing hansom, he climbed in and told the driver to head to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swung between hope and fear. Sometimes he half expected Mr. Podgers to stroll into the room, yet at other moments he felt Fate couldn\u2019t be so unjust. Twice he went to the palm-reader\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but could not bring himself to ring the bell. He craved certainty and dreaded it.\\n\\nAt last it arrived. He was sitting in the club smoking-room, sipping tea, and listening with mild boredom to Surbiton\u2019s account of the latest comic song at the Gaiety when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
