          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"421e615b0f977f3a9c4e8f06\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known her sister was intent on wrenching her from such prospects and realities, what feelings would have possessed her? Only her mother, who might have experienced nearly the same, could have understood. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was the sole comfort offsetting her sorrowful conviction that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nYet they remained wholly unaware of what had occurred; their delight went on, with scarcely a pause, until the very day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to meet Mr. Wickham for the last time. As she had often been in his company since returning, her agitation had subsided; the emotions of past preference entirely so. She had even learned to discern, in the very gentleness that had once charmed her, an affectation and monotony that disgusted and fatigued. In his present conduct toward her, moreover, she found a new source of displeasure; for the inclination he soon displayed to revive the attentions that had marked the early stage of their acquaintance could now serve only to provoke. Discovering herself singled out by such idle, frivolous gallantry, she ceased to feel any concern for him; and while she resolutely checked it, she could not miss the implied reprimand in his supposing that, however long and for whatever reason his attentions had been suspended, her vanity would be pleased and her preference secured at any moment by their renewal.\\n\\nOn the very last day the regiment remained in Meryton, he dined, with other officers, at Longbourn; and Elizabeth was so little disposed to part from him affably that when he casually asked how her time had been spent at Hunsford, she mentioned Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy having both stayed three weeks at Rosings, and inquired whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked astonished, displeased, alarmed; but with a moment\u2019s recollection and a returning smile, said he had formerly seen him often; and after remarking that he was a very gentlemanlike man, asked how she had liked him. Her reply warmly favored him. With an air of indifference, he soon after added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him frequently?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners differ greatly from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves on closer acquaintance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with a look she did not miss. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d but checking himself, he continued, in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in address that he improves?\u201d\"}"
