          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"76e746752ce0fd3710167126\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Elliot looked up with such deference.\u201d \u201cPerfectly charming!\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Clay, though she dared not turn her gaze toward Anne. \u201cJust like father and son! Dear Miss Elliot, am I not allowed to say father and son?\u201d \u201cOh! I do not forbid anyone their expressions. If you must think so! But, upon my honour, I scarcely perceive his attentions surpassing those of other gentlemen.\u201d \u201cMy dear Miss Elliot!\u201d cried Mrs. Clay, raising hands and eyes, then suppressing the rest of her astonishment into a convenient silence. \u201cWell, Penelope, you need not feel such alarm about him. Remember, I invited him. I dismissed him with smiles. When I discovered he truly meant to spend the entire day tomorrow with friends at Thornberry Park, I took pity on him.\u201d Anne admired her friend\u2019s skill in appearing so delighted by both expectation and actual arrival of the very person whose presence must interfere with her principal aim. Surely Mrs. Clay detested the sight of Mr. Elliot; yet she could adopt an obliging, tranquil expression, and appear perfectly content with having her licence to devote herself to Sir Walter reduced to only half the usual degree. To Anne, Mr. Elliot\u2019s entrance proved exceedingly distressing, and his approaching to speak to her was painful. Previously she had suspected him of lacking complete sincerity; now she detected duplicity everywhere. His respectful deference to her father, set against his earlier commentary, was detestable; and thinking of his cruel behaviour toward Mrs. Smith, she could barely endure his smiles and gentle manner or the sound of his contrived good sentiments. She intended to avoid any change in demeanor that might provoke remonstrance from him. She sought to escape all questions or display; nonetheless she meant to be as unmistakably cool toward him as their kinship allowed, and to withdraw, quietly as possible, from those few steps of needless intimacy she had imperceptibly taken. Thus she was more guarded and colder than the evening before. He was eager to rekindle her curiosity about how and where he had heard her formerly praised; anxious to be gratified by renewed inquiry; but the spell had been broken: he discovered the bustle and excitement of a public room were necessary to arouse his modest cousin\u2019s vanity; at least he found it could not now be achieved by any efforts he could attempt amid the authoritative claims of the others.\"}"
        }
