          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd878a2927ff223aae29653f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t rush him suddenly and risk botching the job; no, I move off to one side and work my way toward him slowly, so he never guesses I\u2019m going to strike. Then, all at once, he\u2019s flat on his back, and he can\u2019t figure out how it happened. That\u2019s how I handled Brother Dowley. I began talking in a lazy, easy way, as if I only wanted to pass the time; and the oldest man alive couldn\u2019t have tracked where I started and guessed where I meant to end up.\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are a lot of curious things about law, and custom, and use, and all that, once you look at them; yes, and about the flow and advance of human opinion and movement, too. There are written laws\u2014they die; but there are unwritten laws\u2014they are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says wages have to rise, little by little, down through the centuries. And see how it works. We know what wages are now, here and there and yonder; we take an average and call that today\u2019s wage. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and two hundred years ago; that\u2019s as far back as we can go, but it\u2019s enough to give us the law of progress, the rate of regular increase; and so, without a document to help, we can come close to figuring what wages were three, four, five hundred years ago. Good so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking back; we turn around and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what wages will be at any time you want, for hundreds and hundreds of years ahead.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will be six times what they are now in your region, and farmhands will get 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019d I might die now and live then!\u201d broke in Smug the wheelwright, greed shining in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that isn\u2019t all; they\u2019ll get their board besides\u2014such as it is: it won\u2019t swell them up. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014listen close now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wage will be\u2014remember, this is law, not guesswork; a mechanic\u2019s wage will be twenty cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a general gasp of awed surprise, and Dickon the mason murmured, eyes and hands raised:\"}"
