          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ecf474ca933b45a9bcb26931\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Going back over his letter before answering it had such a melting effect that she needed to steel herself with some firm phrases; she was so distressed at making him miserable, so taken up with thoughts of what his mother and sisters would think or say, and so eager they should not suspect her of ingratitude, that Emma believed, had the young man crossed her path just then, he would have been accepted after all. Still, the letter was written, sealed, and dispatched. The matter was concluded, and Harriet safe. She felt rather dejected for the rest of the evening, but Emma made allowance for her gentle regrets, and now soothed them by speaking of her own affection, now by mentioning Mr. Elton. \u201cI shall never be asked to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she murmured with a touch of sorrow. \u201cNor, if you were, could I endure to part with you, my Harriet. You are far too needful at Hartfield to be lent to Abbey-Mill.\u201d \u201cAnd I am sure I should never wish to go; I am content only at Hartfield.\u201d Later she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be vastly surprised if she knew what had passed. I am certain Miss Nash would be\u2014for she considers her sister very well married, and it was only to a linen-draper.\u201d \u201cOne would be sorry to see greater pride or delicacy in the mistress of a school, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would covet such a chance of marriage herself. Even this triumph would seem precious to her. As for anything higher for you, I imagine she is utterly ignorant. The attentions of a certain person can scarcely have entered the small talk of Highbury yet. Thus far, I suspect you and I alone have interpreted his looks and manners.\u201d Harriet blushed, smiled, and murmured something about her amazement that people should be so partial to her. The idea of Mr. Elton was unquestionably heartening; yet before long she was tender once more towards the dismissed Mr. Martin. \u201cNow he has received my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI cannot help wondering what they are all about\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they must be unhappy as well. I hope he will not feel it too deeply.\u201d \u201cLet us think of our absent friends who are more pleasantly occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cPerhaps at this very moment Mr. Elton is displaying your portrait to his mother and sisters, declaring how much lovelier the original is, and after being begged for it five or six times, is at length consenting to let them hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
        }
