          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5d25ac14ec3fd64a34c18c62\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 12. The balloon suddenly changed direction in a way I had expected but which still thrilled me. It had been traveling along the twentieth parallel south when it sharply turned east and kept going that way through the day, staying almost perfectly within the plane of the moon\u2019s ellipse. The car, however, began to sway noticeably because of this new course and continued to do so for many hours. \\n\\nApril 13. I was again frightened by the loud crackling sound that had alarmed me on the tenth. I thought about it for a long time but could not find an explanation. The apparent size of the earth shrank dramatically and now filled an angle of only a little more than twenty-five degrees from the balloon. The moon was nearly at my zenith and could not be seen at all. I was still moving in the plane of the ellipse but gained very little ground toward the east. \\n\\nApril 14. The apparent size of the earth dropped even more quickly. I became convinced that the balloon was traveling along the line of apsides toward perigee\u2014in other words, following the exact path that would take me to the moon at the point where it comes closest to the earth. The moon was directly overhead and therefore out of sight. Condensing the air required long, exhausting labor. \\n\\nApril 15. By now I could no longer make out even the faint outlines of continents or seas on the earth. Around midday I heard the terrifying noise for the third time. It lasted longer and grew louder as it went on. I stood stunned, expecting some horrible destruction, when the car shook violently and a huge burning mass, of a material I could not identify, roared past the balloon with the voice of a thousand thunders. After my fear eased, I decided it must have been a massive volcanic fragment thrown off by the world I was rapidly approaching, likely one of those strange objects that sometimes fall to earth and are called meteoric stones for lack of a better name. \\n\\nApril 16. Today, by straining to look upward through each side window in turn, I was delighted to see a small part of the moon\u2019s disk protruding on all sides beyond the balloon\u2019s huge circumference.\"}"
