          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b9587421074225753d5c7db8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was then that both his gaze and mine settled upon a scrap of parchment, which I initially took for paper. It lay half hidden in the sand, one corner protruding. Close to that spot I noticed the remains of what seemed once to be the hull of a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck had clearly been there ages; scarcely anything in its frame still resembled boat timbers.\\n\\n\u201cJupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Soon after we turned homeward and met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 on the way. I showed him the insect, and he entreated me to let him take it to the fort. When I consented, he at once slipped it into his waistcoat pocket, leaving behind the parchment in which it had been enclosed, which I had continued to hold while he examined the beetle. Perhaps he feared I might reconsider and thought best to secure the treasure immediately\u2014you know how fervent his interest is in everything touching Natural History. Meanwhile, without realizing it, I must have placed the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou recollect that when I approached the table intending to sketch the beetle, I found no paper at its usual place. I opened the drawer and discovered none there either. I searched my pockets, hoping to find some old letter, when my hand encountered the parchment. I recount exactly how it came into my keeping, for the details struck me with unusual force.\\n\\n\u201cDoubtless you will think me fanciful, but I had already formed a sort of linkage. I had connected two links of a long chain. There was a boat along a sea-coast, and near that boat lay a parchment\u2014no mere paper\u2014bearing the image of a skull. You will naturally ask, \u2018what is the relationship?\u2019 I answer that the skull, the death\u2019s-head, is the familiar emblem of the pirate. The banner of the death\u2019s-head flies in every engagement.\\n\\n\u201cI mentioned that the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment endures\u2014almost indestructible. Matters of little significance are seldom committed to parchment; for the routine needs of drawing or writing it serves far less well than paper. This thought hinted at some meaning\u2014some relevance\u2014behind the death\u2019s-head. I also took note of the parchment\u2019s shape. Though one corner had been destroyed by some mishap, the original form was plainly oblong.\u201d\"}"
