{"request_id":"17f62ca17dc2ee17d0e1e7cc","rewritten_text":"Few could claim a heart as pure as hers, or match her genuine humility combined with unwavering trust in her own integrity and faith in the honor of others. She had lived a quiet life from birth. Her mother had died when she was very young, but her father devoted himself to her education. He held many eccentric ideas that shaped the approach he adopted for her. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England who had lived centuries earlier, yet she was almost ignorant of current affairs. She had read very few authors from the past fifty years, but apart from that omission her reading was extensive. So even though she seemed less initiated into the secrets of modern life and society than he, her knowledge was deeper and built on firmer foundations. If her beauty and kindness had somehow failed to captivate him, her mind would still have held his in thrall. He looked up to her as his guide, and his devotion was so intense that he actually enjoyed heightening, in his own thoughts, the sense of inferiority she sometimes made him feel.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college and, shaking off his old friends for a time, withdrew to the neighborhood of his Diana, drawing all his comfort from her sweet voice and dearer embraces. That brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to claim his independence. He sensed that, although they might mock his plan to marry, they would not dare do so once the marriage was accomplished. He therefore secured his guardian’s consent—difficult though it was—and won the approval of his beloved’s father, which came more easily. Without informing anyone else of his plans, he became Diana’s husband by the time he turned twenty.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness held such allure that he could think of nothing but her. He invited several college friends to visit, but their frivolity repelled him. Diana had lifted the veil that had kept him in boyhood: he had become a man, and he marveled that he had ever shared the empty words and ideas of his fellow students, or had worried about their disapproval. He cast off his old friendships, not out of fickleness, but because they truly were unworthy of him."}

