          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"351807a5d5d3d91c65871f8c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But when I saw someone else fall in love with you\u2014when I imagined you being loved as anything less than a sacred emblem of beauty and virtue\u2014or when I imagined you loving someone else more passionately than you loved me, a demon woke inside me. I drove your lover away, and from that moment I have known no peace. I have searched in vain for sleep and rest; my eyelids refused to close, and my blood was forever raging. I woke into a new life the way someone dying in hope might wake in Hell. I will not tarnish your imagination by describing my battles, my rage at myself, and my despair. Let a veil fall over the unimaginable feelings of a guilty father; the secrets of such a tormented heart must not be made common. Everything was upheaval, crime, remorse, and hate, and yet still the softest love; and what first inspired me to resolve to conquer my passion and to restore my child to her father was the sight of your bitter and sympathetic grief. That is what brought me here: I thought if I could awaken again the sorrow I felt at your mother\u2019s death, if I could revive the memories of her that had lain dormant for seventeen years, then any love for her child would die out. In a fit of heroism I decided to go alone, to leave you\u2014the life of my life\u2014and not see you again until I could do so without guilt. But it failed: I overestimated my strength, or underestimated my love. I would certainly have died if you hadn\u2019t hurried to me. I almost wish I had been extinguished.\\n\\nAnd now, Mathilda, I must confess one last thing. I was tragically wrong to think I could defeat my love for you; I never can. Seeing this house, these fields, and woods where my first love lived has made it stronger. In my madness I dared tell myself\u2014Diana died to give her child life; her spirit was passed into her daughter, and so the daughter should be as Diana to me. The more I try to tear it out, the tighter it grips: this guilty love more unnatural than hate, which ruins your hopes and destroys me forever.\\n\\nBetter to have loved despair itself, and safer to have kissed her.\\n\\nNo time or distance can rip from my soul what has become part of it. Since I arrived here, not for a moment have I been free of the hellish passion planted in me to burn until everything is cold, rigid, lifeless.\"}"
