          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3e96d32f0a446cecdb095317\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew glanced back at him, without success, as he headed for the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d the uncle said. \u201cI look forward to the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Show my nephew to his room there!\u2014And burn my nephew in his bed, if you like,\u201d he muttered to himself before ringing his little bell again and summoning his valet to his own chamber.\\n\\nOnce the valet had come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis paced in his loose robe to ease himself gently toward sleep on that hot, still night. Rustling around the room, his soft slippers silent on the floor, he moved like a cultivated tiger\u2014looked like some unrepentantly wicked marquis from a tale, at the moment when his periodic change into a tiger was either just ending or just beginning.\\n\\nHe walked from end to end of his luxurious bedroom, reviewing the fragments of the day\u2019s journey that rose unbidden in his mind: the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the setting sun itself, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the road-mender in his blue cap pointing out the chain beneath the carriage. That fountain reminded him of the Paris fountain, the small bundle lying on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms raised, shouting, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m calm now,\u201d Monsieur the Marquis said, \u201cand can go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nLeaving only one light burning on the wide hearth, he let his thin gauze curtains fall around him and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he settled himself to sleep.\\n\\nThe stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly into the black night for three heavy hours; for three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled their harness, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound with very little resemblance to the noise poets say the owl makes. But creatures like that are stubborn about saying what is written for them.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly into the night. Dead darkness covered the landscape, and dead darkness laid its own hush over the hushed dust on every road. The burial ground had reached the point where its small mounds of poor grass were indistinguishable from one another; the figure on the Cross might have stepped down, for all anyone could see. In the village, taxer and taxed slept soundly. Dreaming, perhaps, of feasts\u2014as the starving often do\u2014and of comfort and rest, as a driven slave or yoked ox might, its gaunt inhabitants slept deeply, fed and freed in their dreams.\"}"
