          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0afa2d049a88728e5adf85af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I told myself I wasn\u2019t about to get tangled up in anything like that. When I went downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed and the watchers were gone. Only the family, Widow Bartley, and our crew were around. I studied their faces to see if anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t tell. Around midday the undertaker arrived with his assistant. They set the coffin on two chairs in the middle of the room, arranged our chairs in rows, and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were packed. The coffin lid looked as it had before, but I didn\u2019t dare peek underneath with everyone around. Soon people started pouring in. The frauds and the girls sat in the front row at the head of the coffin, and for half an hour the guests filed past slowly in a single line, gazing at the dead man\u2019s face for a moment. Some shed a tear, and everything was hushed and solemn, except for the girls and the con men holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, heads bowed, sniffling a little. There was no other sound beyond shuffling feet and people blowing their noses\u2014folks always do that more at a funeral than anywhere except church. When the place was full, the undertaker glided around in his black gloves with quiet, comforting efficiency, putting on the final touches and getting people and things tidy and orderly without making more noise than a cat. He never spoke; he just shifted people around, squeezed in latecomers, opened passageways, all with nods and hand signals. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the softest, smoothest, stealthiest man I\u2019d ever seen, and his face wasn\u2019t any more cheerful than a slab of ham. They\u2019d borrowed a wheezy melodeon, and once everything was ready a young woman sat down to play it. It screeched and wheezed, but everybody joined in singing, and in my opinion Peter was the only one with a good thing going on. Then Reverend Hobson began, slow and solemn, and right away the most outrageous commotion erupted in the cellar that anyone had ever heard. It was just one dog, but he made an awful racket and kept at it nonstop. The preacher had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t even hear yourself think.\"}"
