          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cbbbb1010584678f3eaafb7e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My heart thudded wildly and with an unaccountable dread I dismissed the servant, locked the door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were its contents.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child,\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed the trust you placed in me; I have tried to stain your mind and acquainted your innocent heart with the gaze and speech of unlawful, monstrous desire. I must atone for these crimes, and should attempt to match my punishment to my guilt. You must already anticipate what I am about to declare: we must separate, and forever part.\\n\\n\u201cI rob you of your parent and only friend. I cast you, unprotected, upon the world: your hopes are shattered; the peace and security of your untainted mind ruined; memory will bring before you ghastly images of guilt and the torment of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who draw all this misery down upon you; I, who cast you out and without mercy have stamped distrust and anguish on the heart and brow of my own child, who with fiendish frivolity tried to steal her loveliness to replace it with the foul deformity of sin; I, in the overflowing agony of my soul, implore you to pardon me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must and do abhor me: yet pardon me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts pursue me in my exile with unforgiving anger. I must never see you again; never hear your voice; yet the soft murmur of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning in my distraught brain and heart; I am sure I would feel it even within my grave. And I dare urge this plea by telling how miserably I was ensnared in this fiery torment and of my every struggle to free myself: truly, had your soul been less pure and radiant I would not attempt to justify myself to you; I would fear that by making you view me with less abhorrence you might likewise loathe vice less. But in speaking to you I feel I appeal to an angelic judge. I cannot depart without your pardon and must strive to wrest it from you, or else plunge into despair. I implore you therefore to listen to me, and if in the virtuous, guilt may in some measure be lessened by keen suffering and remorse that shatters the brain like madness, perhaps you may believe\u2014though I dare not\u2014that I possess some claim to your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI beseech you to recall our first happy days beside the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned after a weary wandering of sixteen years, during which, though I had endured many perils and sorrows, my affections had been utterly blank.\u201d\"}"
