{"request_id":"05a63e474cae8813deb46490","rewritten_text":"I next bored a small aperture in the keg’s end above the pitcher and inserted a plug of soft wood, trimmed to a taper. By shoving the plug inward or withdrawing it slightly, and after a few trials, I obtained that precise snugness which allowed the water seeping from the hole to drip into the pitcher so steadily that it brimmed exactly in sixty minutes. This result was quickly verified by observing how much of the pitcher filled within any set interval. Once this was settled, the remainder of the contrivance becomes clear. I had arranged my bed along the car’s floor so that my head, when I lay down, rested immediately beneath the pitcher’s mouth. Plainly, as soon as an hour elapsed, the pitcher, being full, must overflow—doing so from the mouth, which sat a bit lower than the rim. Equally evident, the water tumbling from a height exceeding four feet could only fall upon my face, and such a shock could not fail to rouse me instantly, however deep my sleep. 

“It was fully eleven when I finished these preparations, and I at once turned in, thoroughly relying on my new device. Nor was my faith misplaced. Every sixty minutes exactly my faithful chronometer awakened me; then, after emptying the pitcher back into the keg through the bung-hole and attending to the condenser, I returned straightaway to bed. These periodic intrusions upon my rest proved even less troublesome than I had foreseen; and when at last I rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, with the sun already elevated many degrees above my horizon. 

“April 3rd. I found the balloon soaring at an enormous altitude, with the earth’s seeming convexity markedly increased. Beneath me lay the ocean, where a sprinkling of black dots were doubtless islands. Far off to the north I discerned a narrow, white, and intensely radiant streak along the horizon’s verge, and I felt assured it was the southern boundary of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity grew intense, for I hoped to penetrate much farther north, and perhaps, at some point, to hover directly over the Pole itself. I now regretted that my lofty station would, in such a case, keep me from making quite as exact an observation as I desired.”"}

