          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7687cf6b4adc327af0d3085f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you call Miss Havisham a he. And even you wouldn\u2019t go that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, uptown?\u201d Joe asked.\\n\\n\u201cIs there another Miss Havisham downtown?\u201d my sister shot back.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go and play there. Of course he\u2019s going. And he had better play there,\u201d my sister said, shaking her head at me in warning to be as lively as possible, \u201cor I\u2019ll make sure he does.\u201d\\n\\nI had heard of Miss Havisham uptown\u2014everyone for miles had heard of her\u2014as an extremely rich and stern lady who lived in a huge, gloomy house barricaded against thieves, and who kept to herself.\\n\\n\u201cWell, I\u2019ll be!\u201d Joe exclaimed, stunned. \u201cHow did she ever hear of Pip?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFool!\u201d my sister cried. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Because someone,\u201d Joe hinted politely, \u201csaid she wanted him to go and play there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCouldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew a boy who could go and play there? Isn\u2019t it possible that Uncle Pumblechook might be her tenant, and that he sometimes\u2014we won\u2019t say every quarter or half-year, because that would be expecting too much of you\u2014but sometimes goes there to pay his rent? Couldn\u2019t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, always so considerate and thoughtful for us\u2014though you may not see it, Joseph,\u201d she added with the deepest reproach, as if he were the most unfeeling of nephews, \u201cthen mention this boy, standing prancing here\u201d\u2014which I swear I was not doing\u2014\u201cthat I have always been a willing slave to?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d cried Uncle Pumblechook. \u201cWell said! Nicely pointed! Excellent! Now, Joseph, you understand the situation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d my sister said, still reproachful, while Joe sheepishly rubbed the back of his hand across his nose again and again, \u201cyou do not\u2014though you may think you do\u2014understand the situation. You may believe you do, but you do not, Joseph. Because you don\u2019t know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing that for all we know this boy\u2019s fortune might be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, to keep him there tonight, and to take him himself to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lord have mercy!\u201d my sister cried, ripping off her bonnet in sudden desperation, \u201chere I am talking to a pair of mooncalves, while Uncle Pumblechook waits, the mare catches cold at the door, and the boy is smeared with grime from the hair on his head to the soles of his feet!\u201d\"}"
