          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d1e2fb09187ba4976cec59a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma had no later cause to recant her poor estimation of Mrs. Elton. Her observations had been tolerably accurate. Just as Mrs. Elton struck her at their second encounter, so she remained whenever they met thereafter\u2014self-satisfied, overbearing, free and easy, uninformed, and ill-mannered. She possessed a touch of beauty and a trace of accomplishment, yet so little judgment that she fancied herself entering with superior worldly knowledge to enliven and refine a rural neighbourhood; and imagined Miss Hawkins to have held a position in society that only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own consequence could surpass.\\n\\nThere was no sign that Mr. Elton held any opinion different from his wife\u2019s. He appeared not only content with her, but proud. He seemed to congratulate himself on bringing to Highbury a woman whom even Miss Woodhouse could not rival; and most of her new acquaintances, inclined to praise, or unaccustomed to judge, guided by Miss Bates\u2019s kind intentions, or assuming that the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she asserted, were perfectly satisfied; so Mrs. Elton\u2019s commendation passed from mouth to mouth as it should, unhampered by Miss Woodhouse, who readily maintained her initial offering and spoke with good grace of her being \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one regard Mrs. Elton became even worse than at first. Her feelings shifted toward Emma. Probably affronted by the scant encouragement her overtures of intimacy received, she retreated in her turn and gradually grew much colder and more distant; and although the result was welcome, the ill will that prompted it necessarily heightened Emma\u2019s dislike. Her manners, and Mr. Elton\u2019s as well, were disagreeable toward Harriet. They were sneering and careless. Emma trusted it must quickly effect Harriet\u2019s cure; but the sentiments that could inspire such conduct lowered them both considerably. It could not be doubted that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been laid before conjugal confidence, and Emma\u2019s own part in the affair, under colouring least favourable to her and most soothing to him, had likely been supplied also. Harriet was, of course, the object of their mutual dislike. When conversation flagged, they could readily begin abusing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not show by open disrespect toward her took a wider outlet in scornful treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton conceived a great notion of Jane Fairfax, and from the start. Not merely when one might suppose a feud with one young woman would recommend another, but from the earliest moment; and she was not content with a natural and reasonable admiration\u2014rather, without invitation, claim, or excuse, she must needs be anxious to aid and advocate her. Before Emma had forfeited her trust, and about their third meeting, she heard the whole of Mrs. Elton\u2019s knight-errantry on the subject.\"}"
