          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f61ac7b841143b252135af0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You wouldn\u2019t know about me unless you\u2019d read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer; but that doesn\u2019t make any difference. That book was written by Mr. Mark Twain, and he mostly told the truth. There were some things he stretched, but for the most part it was the truth. That\u2019s nothing. I never met anybody who hadn\u2019t lied one time or another, unless it was Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, she is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all set down in that book, which is mostly true, with a few stretchers, like I mentioned. \\n\\nNow the way that book finishes is like this: Tom and me came across the money the robbers hid in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars apiece\u2014in gold. It looked like a powerful heap when you stacked it. Judge Thatcher took it and put it out at interest, and it brought in a dollar a day each, right through the year\u2014more than anyone could think what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me for her son and said she\u2019d civilize me; but it was tough staying in the house steady, considering how mournful proper and tidy the widow was in every way; and after I couldn\u2019t bear it no more I took off. I got into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead again, and felt free and content. But Tom Sawyer found me and said he was fixing to start a band of robbers, and I could join if I\u2019d go back to the widow and be respectable. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me, called me a poor lost lamb, and a batch of other names, too, though she never meant harm. She dressed me in those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and sweat, feeling squeezed and stiff. Well, then it all started over. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to be on time. When you got to the table you couldn\u2019t begin eating right away; you had to wait for the widow to bow her head and mumble something over the victuals, though nothing was wrong with them\u2014only everything was cooked separate. In a barrel of odds and ends it\u2019s different; things mix, the juices trade around, and the food goes down better.\\n\\nAfter supper she fetched out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was in a hurry to hear the whole of it; but after a while she let slip that Moses had been dead a good long while; so then I didn\u2019t care any further, because I don\u2019t take stock in dead folks.\"}"
        }
