          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ede2758194e86535b48fb252\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly about his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am very pleased to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cPlease tell her from me that she cannot expect to excel unless she practices a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he answered, \u201cthat she does not need that advice. She practices very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One can never practice too much. When I next write to her, I will insist she never neglect it. I often tell young ladies that no one can become excellent at music without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play truly well unless she practices more. And though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is perfectly welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. In that part of the house she would be in no one\u2019s way.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked slightly ashamed of his aunt\u2019s lack of manners and stayed silent.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth she had promised to play for him, and she sat down at once at the instrument. He moved a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then began talking again to her other nephew, until he walked away from her and, with his usual calm, positioned himself by the pianoforte so he could watch the performer\u2019s face clearly. Elizabeth saw what he was about and, at the first pause, turned to him with a playful smile.\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to scare me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in all this state to hear me. But I will not be frightened, even if your sister does play so well. I have a stubborn streak that will never let others frighten me. My courage grows every time someone tries to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not say you are wrong,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly believe I would try to frighten you. And I have known you long enough to see that you take great pleasure in occasionally claiming opinions that are not really yours.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this description of herself and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin is giving you a very fine idea of me and teaching you not to believe a single word I say. I am especially unlucky to meet someone so capable of revealing my true character in a place where I had hoped to pass myself off with some credit.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
