{"request_id":"ec5f72457cfa2248b10b1f9d","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each wearing a large tuft of nodding ostrich feathers, and the heavy coffin was covered by a rich purple cloth embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants walked beside the hearse and the coaches carrying lit torches, and the whole procession was striking. Lord Canterville, who had come up from Wales just for the funeral, was the chief mourner and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. Next came the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys, and in the last carriage rode Mrs. Umney. Everyone felt that, as she had endured the ghost’s fright for over fifty years, she deserved to see his end. A deep grave had been dug in the corner of the churchyard under the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great solemnity. When the ceremony ended, the servants followed an old Canterville custom by putting out their torches, and as the coffin was lowered, Virginia stepped forward and placed on it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. As she did, the moon came out from behind a cloud and bathed the quiet churchyard in silver light, and a nightingale started to sing from a distant thicket. She remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death, tears filled her eyes, and she barely spoke on the drive home.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville returned to town, Mr. Otis met with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were truly magnificent, especially a ruby necklace with an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century work, and their value was so great that Mr. Otis hesitated to let his daughter keep them.

“My lord,” he said, “I know that in this country mortmain applies to trinkets as well as land, and it seems clear to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms in your family. I must ask you to take them to London and consider them part of your property that has been returned to you under unusual conditions. My daughter is only a child and, I am glad to say, still has little interest in such showy luxuries.”"}

