          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"206309966162fcfa6b601fae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm raged violently all night, but nothing particularly noteworthy happened. The next morning, however, when they came down to breakfast, they found the terrible bloodstain on the floor once more. \u201cI can\u2019t blame Paragon Detergent,\u201d said Washington, \u201cbecause I\u2019ve tried it with every cleaner imaginable. It must be the ghost.\u201d He scrubbed the stain away again, yet on the following morning it reappeared. It was there again on the third morning, even though Mr. Otis himself had locked the library the night before and taken the key upstairs. The whole family was now thoroughly intrigued; Mr. Otis began to suspect he had been too dismissive in denying ghosts existed, Mrs. Otis announced she intended to join the Psychical Society, and Washington prepared a long letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore about the persistence of bloodstains associated with crime. That night all doubts about the tangible reality of phantoms vanished forever.\\n\\nThe day had been warm and sunny, and in the cool of the evening the entire family went out for a drive. They did not return until nine o\u2019clock, when they ate a light supper. The conversation never once touched on ghosts, so there were not even the basic conditions of receptive expectation that so often precede psychic phenomena. The topics discussed, as I have since heard from Mr. Otis, were simply what cultured, upper-class Americans usually talk about: the overwhelming superiority of Miss Fanny Davenport over Sarah Bernhardt as an actress; the difficulty of obtaining green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy even in the finest English homes; Boston\u2019s crucial role in the development of the world-soul; the benefits of the baggage check system in railroad travel; and the sweetness of the New York accent compared to the London drawl. Nothing supernatural was mentioned, nor was Sir Simon de Canterville referenced at 