          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"51dcba4e37f41d3cb8a8e38c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhy, we could build quite a thrilling superstition on that idea. I suppose you plan to call the bug scarabaeus caput hominis, or something similar\u2014Natural Histories have plenty of those names. But where are the antennae you mentioned?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe antennae!\u201d Legrand exclaimed, growing strangely heated. \u201cSurely you see them. I drew them as clearly as they appear on the real insect, and that should be enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d I replied, \u201cmaybe you did\u2014but I don\u2019t see them.\u201d I handed the paper back without further comment, hoping not to upset him. Still, the situation surprised me; his irritability made no sense, and the drawing absolutely showed no antennae. It really did resemble the usual illustrations of a death\u2019s-head.\\n\\nHe snatched the paper peevishly and was about to crumple it and toss it into the fire when a quick glance at the sketch suddenly fixed his attention. His face turned bright red, and in the next moment deathly pale. For several minutes he sat there poring over the drawing. Then he got up, took a candle from the table, and sat on a sea chest in the far corner. He studied the paper again, rotating it in every direction. He stayed silent, and his behavior astonished me; yet I thought it wiser not to irritate his growing moodiness with a remark. Soon he pulled a wallet from his coat, slipped the paper carefully inside, and placed both in a writing desk, which he locked. His manner became calmer, though the earlier enthusiasm was gone. He seemed not exactly sullen, but rather lost in thought. As the evening passed he sank deeper into his reverie, and nothing I said could rouse him. I had planned to spend the night at the hut, as I often had, but seeing him in this state, I decided to leave. He did not urge me to stay, yet as I departed he shook my hand with more than his usual warmth.\\n\\nAbout a month later\u2014during which I saw nothing of Legrand\u2014his servant Jupiter came to visit me in Charleston. I had never seen the good old man look so downcast, and I feared some serious trouble had befallen my friend.\"}"
