          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a8c5d802f9491d9af0143ea3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later, with his face pale from terror and his eyes wild with grief, Lord Arthur Savile burst out of Bentinck House, forcing his way through the ring of fur-coated footmen around the big striped awning, seeming blind and deaf to everything. The night was bitter cold, and the gas-lamps around the square flared and flickered in the sharp wind, yet his hands burned with fever and his forehead felt ablaze. He stumbled on, almost like a drunk. A policeman gave him a curious glance as he passed, and a beggar who shuffled out of an archway to ask for alms shrank back, frightened by a misery greater than his own. Once he stopped under a lamp and stared at his hands. He thought he already saw blood staining them, and a faint cry slipped from his shaking lips. Murder! That was what the palm-reader had foreseen. Murder! The night itself seemed aware of it, and the desolate wind howled it in his ears. The dark corners of the streets were filled with it. It leered at him from the rooftops. First he reached the Park, whose somber woods seemed to draw him in. He leaned wearily against the railings, cooling his forehead on the wet metal and listening to the trembling silence of the trees. \u201cMurder! Murder!\u201d he kept repeating, as if repetition could dull the horror of the word. The sound of his own voice made him shudder, yet he almost hoped Echo might hear him and rouse the sleeping city from its dreams. He felt a mad urge to stop any passer-by and confess everything. Then he wandered across Oxford Street into narrow, sordid alleys. Two women with painted faces mocked him as he went by. From a dark courtyard came curses and blows, followed by shrill screams, and on a damp doorstep he saw the hunched shapes of the poor and old. A strange pity swept over him. Were these children of sin and misery doomed to their end, just as he was? Were they, like him, mere puppets in a monstrous show? Yet it was not the mystery but the grotesque comedy of suffering that struck him: its uselessness, its absurd lack of meaning. How jumbled everything seemed! How empty of harmony! He marveled at the clash between the shallow optimism of the day and the real facts of life. He was still very young. After a while he found himself standing before Marylebone Church. The silent road looked like a long ribbon of polished silver, streaked here and there with the dark, waving arabesques of shadows.\"}"
