          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5074b7d5a566c317c7dd0463\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But my father stayed beside me, comforting me and replacing bitter memories with bright hopes: I even thought it sweet to cry so he could dry my tears. He led my thoughts away from my grief by comparing it to the despair he had felt when my mother died. Even then I recoiled from the picture he painted of his emotions: he had a poet\u2019s imagination, and when he described the whirlwind that had torn his feelings, his words were so alive that I believed him even as I trembled. I wondered how he could have re-entered everyday life after such wild visions seemed to link him to the unearthly. While he spoke, his ideas were so overwhelming it seemed the human heart was too confined to contain them. His feelings seemed suited for a spirit whose home is earthquakes and volcanoes, not for someone limited to a mortal body and human features. Yet those were only memories; he had changed since then. He was all love and gentleness now, and when I lifted my eyes to him in wonder as he spoke, the smile on his lips showed me that his heart was filled with the kindest passions.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death we moved to London, where my father encouraged me to pursue studies deeper than any I had attempted before. My progress delighted him; he stayed with me through every lesson, helping or joining me in each one. We mingled frequently in society, and not a day passed without his trying to adorn it with some new enjoyment. The tender attachment he felt for me, and the love and reverence I felt for him, cast a charm over every moment. Time moved slowly because every minute was fully used; we lived more in a week than many do in months, and the variety and novelty of our pleasures gave a fresh edge to each.\\n\\nWe were constantly going on outings together. Whether we sought beautiful landscapes, admired fine paintings, or set out with no purpose but to find whatever amusements appeared, I was happiest whenever I was near my father. I regretted it whenever a third person joined us, yet if I looked at him with even a momentary shadow in my expression, the gentle warmth in his eyes instantly restored my joy. Oh, hours of intense delight! Brief as you were, you stretch across my memory like an entire lifetime now that grief has risen like a mist to shut you from my sight.\"}"
        }
