{"request_id":"2cd8046f1b539693d3fa049a","rewritten_text":"“I went on to reason in this way—_a posteriori_. The killers escaped by one of these windows. If that is true, they could not have fastened the sashes again from inside, as they were discovered secured; this obvious point had halted the police inquiry on that side. Nevertheless, the sashes _were_ secured. They _must_, therefore, possess a mechanism for fastening themselves. There was no eluding this deduction. I moved to the unobstructed casement, tugged the nail loose with some trouble, and tried to raise the sash. It withstood every attempt, just as I had expected. I now knew a hidden spring must exist; this confirmation of my notion satisfied me that my initial premises were sound, however puzzling the circumstances of the nails remained. A meticulous search soon revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it and, contented with the find, refrained from lifting the sash.

“I then replaced the nail and studied it closely. Someone passing out by this window might have shut it again, and the spring would latch; but the nail could not have been reset. The conclusion was clear, and again winnowed the range of my inquiry. The murderers _must_ have left via the other window. Assuming, as was likely, a similar spring on each sash, there _must_ be some difference in the nails or in the manner in which they were fixed. Standing upon the bedstead’s sacking, I scrutinized the second casement over the headboard. Sliding my hand down behind the board, I soon located and depressed the spring, identical, as conjectured, with its twin. I next examined the nail. It was as sturdy as the first and apparently secured in the same fashion—driven in nearly to the head.

“You may say I was baffled; but if you think so, you have misread the nature of the deductions. To borrow a hunting term, I had never once been ‘at fault.’ The scent had not been lost for an instant. No link of the chain showed a weakness. I had tracked the secret to its ultimate issue—and that issue was _the nail._ It presented, I repeat, every feature of its counterpart in the other window; but this fact was utterly meaningless (however decisive it might appear) compared to the larger point that here the clue came to an end. ‘There _must_ be some discrepancy,’ I concluded, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it; the head, along with about a quarter inch of the shank, detached in my fingers.”"}

