          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"128432ca652d79d46d1b707d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly about his sister\u2019s skill. \\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m very glad to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine. \u201cAnd please tell her from me that she cannot expect to excel unless she practices a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he replied, \u201cshe does not need that advice. She practices very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practice too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist she does not neglect it under any circumstances. I often tell young ladies that no real excellence in music can be attained without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play truly well unless she works harder; and though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is very welcome\u2014as I have often told her\u2014to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She wouldn\u2019t impede anyone there, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked somewhat embarrassed by his aunt\u2019s lack of tact and said nothing.\\n\\nWhen the coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth of her promise to play for him, so she sat down at the instrument at once. He pulled a chair close beside her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then began talking again to her other nephew, until he walked away from her and, moving with his usual deliberation toward the piano, positioned himself to have a full view of the lovely performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing and, at the first convenient pause, turned to him with a mischievous smile and said,\\n\\n\u201cYou intend to intimidate me, Mr. Darcy, by coming with such ceremony to listen. But I will not be frightened, even though your sister plays so well. There is a stubbornness in me that cannot bear to be frightened by others\u2019 wills. My courage always rises when someone tries to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI won\u2019t declare that you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you cannot really suppose I meant to frighten you; and I have known you long enough to recognize that you greatly enjoy occasionally claiming opinions that are not actually yours.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this portrait of herself and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very charming impression of me and teach you not to believe a word I say. I am particularly unlucky to meet someone so capable of exposing my true character in a part of the world where I hoped to pass myself off with some degree of credit.\u201d\"}"
