          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"671f3b25c0c180fbb476cabc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet now she wished the whole thing had never happened. Convinced he had indulged too freely in Mr. Weston\u2019s superior wine, she felt certain he would insist on spouting foolishness. Determined to rein him in as much as her own behaviour could manage, she was already arranging to speak with composed serenity and solemnity about the weather and the night; but she had barely begun, barely had they cleared the sweep-gate and rejoined the other carriage, before her chosen topic was dashed to pieces\u2014her hand seized\u2014her attention claimed\u2014and Mr. Elton, seizing the opportune moment, was outright declaring fervent love to her. He professed feelings he assumed must already be known, hopeful, fearful, worshipful, ready to perish if she spurned him; yet confident his burning devotion, unmatched affection, and unprecedented passion must make some impression, in short firmly determined to be accepted in earnest without delay. So it truly was. Without hesitation\u2014without excuse\u2014without even a show of timidity, Mr. Elton, Harriet\u2019s suitor, proclaimed himself _her_ suitor. She attempted to silence him; but in vain; he persisted and poured out the entire speech. Furious as she felt, she determined in the moment to master herself before replying. She sensed that half this foolishness must be wine-inspired, and thus hoped it might be limited to the present hour. Therefore, blending seriousness with playfulness, which she trusted would fit his half-and-half condition, she answered, \u201cI am quite amazed, Mr. Elton. This\u2014addressed to _me_! You are forgetting yourself\u2014you take me for my friend\u2014any message to Miss Smith I will gladly deliver; but no more of this directed at _me_, if you please.\u201d \u201cMiss Smith!\u2014message to Miss Smith!\u2014What could she possibly intend!\u201d He echoed her words with such confident emphasis, such swaggering pretence of astonishment, that she could not help replying sharply, \u201cMr. Elton, this is the most extraordinary behaviour! I can explain it only one way; you are not yourself, or you could not speak to me, or about Harriet, in this manner. Control yourself enough to say no more, and I shall try to forget it.\u201d Yet Mr. Elton had taken only sufficient wine to lift his spirits, not to muddle his faculties. He entirely understood his own intentions; and, after vehemently insisting her suspicion was grossly unjust, and briefly touching upon his regard for Miss Smith purely as her friend\u2014while admitting his surprise that Miss Smith should be mentioned at all\u2014he returned to expounding his own passion and pressed earnestly for a favourable reply.\"}"
