          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f710c619081431a5221cc934\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cFarewell, Uncle George. If I linger, I\u2019ll never reach luncheon on time. Thank you for giving me what I wanted. Nothing delights me more than learning everything about new acquaintances and nothing about old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you dining, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve invited myself and Mr. Gray along. He\u2019s her latest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, to stop pestering me with her charitable schemes. I\u2019m weary of them. The good soul acts as though I have no occupation but signing cheques for her whims.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs you wish, Uncle George, though it will do no good. Philanthropists lose every trace of humanity. That\u2019s their defining trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe elder man grumbled assent and summoned his servant with the bell. Lord Henry walked up the low arcade into Burlington Street and headed toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo this was how Dorian Gray\u2019s lineage ran. Baldly told though it was, the tale evoked a strange, almost modern romance. A lovely woman risking everything for a mad devotion, a handful of wild weeks of bliss, abruptly broken by some hideous, treacherous act; months of voiceless anguish, then a child born in torment; the mother taken by death, the boy consigned to loneliness and the rule of an aged, loveless guardian. Yes, it made an intriguing background. It gave the youth a setting, rendered him closer to perfection. Behind every exquisite thing lay something tragic. Worlds must labour that even the meanest blossom might unfold.... And how entrancing he had been at dinner the previous evening, when he sat across from him at the club, eyes startled, lips parted in tremulous delight, the crimson shades deepening the awakening wonder of his expression. Speaking to him felt like playing a superb violin. He responded to every touch and vibration of the bow.... The act of influence held something horribly fascinating. Nothing else resembled it. To project one\u2019s soul into a gracious shape and let it rest there briefly; to hear one\u2019s intellectual notions returned, now with the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another as though it were some subtle essence or peculiar fragrance\u2014there was genuine joy in that\u2014perhaps the richest joy that remained to us in an age so cramped and vulgar, grossly sensuous in its pleasures and grossly common in its ambitions.... He was a remarkable specimen, this youth he had met so curiously in Basil\u2019s studio, or could, at least, be moulded into one.\"}"
