          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b0da1f7be21d707737a5a5b0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMark my words; I\u2019ve mastered the language of despair. I know it by heart, because I am Despair\u2014a strange creature, jubilant, triumphant Despair. Yet those words ring false, for the wave may be dark, but it isn\u2019t bitter. We lie down, close our eyes with a soft good night, and when we wake, we are free. Come then, no more delays, you slow one! See the pleasant draught! Look, I am a spirit of goodness, not a mortal girl who invites you, and with pleading tones (oh, that they might win you!) says, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I fastened my gaze on his face, and his unearthly beauty, the divine compassion shining in his eyes, his gentle yet earnest look of disapproval and amazement\u2014even before he spoke\u2014changed my strained mood, stripped away my harsh despair, and left me with the gentlest sorrow. I saw tears in his eyes too as he took both my hands in his; sitting down beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a grievous act you would lead me to, dearest friend, and your sorrow must be deep indeed to fill you with these miserable thoughts. You long for death and yet you fear it, and wish me to share it with you. But I have less courage than you, and even with you beside me, I dare not die. Listen to me, then decide if you should win me to your plan, even if the overwhelming eloquence of despair could make death so tempting that heaven itself seemed dark. Listen, I beg you, to one who has nurtured his own desperate thoughts and longed impatiently for death, but who at last has trampled the phantom underfoot and crushed its sting. Come, since you have played Despair with me, I will play Una with you and lead you unhurt from his dark cavern. Listen, and let yourself be softened by words free of selfish passion.\\n\\n\u201cWe don\u2019t know what this vast world means; its strange blend of good and evil. Yet we have been placed here, told to live and to hope. I know not what we are to hope for, but there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that is our task on earth. If misfortune comes against us, we must fight her off, throw her aside, and press forward to find what our nature requires. Whether this vision of future good prepares us for another existence, I cannot say; or whether we, as laborers in God\u2019s vineyard, must lend a hand to clear the way for our descendants.\u201d\"}"
