          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0024158a9fa9062334bc4e2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The examination detained us until nightfall, when we withdrew. As we returned, my companion paused briefly to drop into the office of one of the daily journals.\\n\\nI have remarked that my friend\u2019s caprices were numerous, and that I humored them\u2014_Je les m\u00e9nageais_; there is no English phrase for it. Just now he chose to refuse any discussion of the murder until about midday on the morrow. At that hour he suddenly asked whether I had noticed anything _peculiar_ at the site of the deed.\\n\\nSomething in the way he stressed the word \u201cpeculiar\u201d made me shiver without knowing the cause.\\n\\n\u201cNo, nothing _peculiar_,\u201d I replied; \u201cnothing, at least, beyond what was reported in the paper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe \u2018Gazette,\u2019\u201d he said, \u201chas failed, I fear, to convey the extraordinary horror of the affair. But put aside the idle comments of that print. It seems to me that this enigma is judged insoluble precisely because of that which ought to render it easy to solve\u2014I mean the _outr\u00e9_ nature of its features. The police are bewildered by the apparent lack of motive\u2014not for the murder as such\u2014but for the hideousness of the murder. They are also perplexed by the supposed impossibility of reconciling the voices overheard in dispute with the facts that no one was found above stairs except the slain Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye, and that there was no avenue of escape without attracting the attention of those on the stair. The ferocious confusion of the chamber; the corpse wedged head downward in the chimney; the dreadful mangling of the old lady\u2019s body; these facts, with those already stated and others I need not detail, have sufficed to benumb the faculties and wholly baffle the vaunted _acumen_ of the government officers. They have fallen into the crude yet common mistake of confusing the uncommon with the recondite. Yet it is through such departures from the ordinary plane that reason gropes its way, if at all, toward the truth. In inquiries such as the one now before us, the proper question is not so much \u2018what has occurred,\u2019 as \u2018what has occurred that never happened before.\u2019 Indeed, the readiness with which I shall reach, or have reached, the key to this mystery is directly proportional to the appearance of its insolubility in the eyes of the police.\u201d\\n\\nI gazed at the speaker in silent wonder.\\n\\n\u201cI am now waiting,\u201d he went on, glancing toward our chamber door\u2014\u201cI am now expecting a person who, though possibly not the actual author of these butcheries, must have been in some degree involved in their execution.\u201d\"}"
        }
