          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3248d2a49194af55ab40ac26\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI know she would!\u201d\\n\\nWidow Douglas\u2019s generous hospitality was a tempting lure. Between that and Tom\u2019s arguments the plan soon won out. They agreed to keep the evening\u2019s scheme to themselves. Then it struck Tom that Huck might show up that very night and give the signal. The idea drained much of the excitement from his plans. Still, he couldn\u2019t stand the thought of missing the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019s place. Why should he give it up, he asked himself\u2014there had been no signal the night before, so why would it be more likely tonight? The guaranteed amusement outweighed the uncertain treasure, and like the boy he was, he chose the stronger urge and refused to think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles downstream the ferry stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the forest depths and rocky heights rang with shouts and laughter. They tried every possible way to get hot and tired, and after a while the wanderers straggled back to camp with hearty appetites; then they set about demolishing the good food. After the feast came a pleasant spell of rest and conversation in the shade of broad oaks. After a while someone called out:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. They gathered bundles of candles, and immediately there was a mad dash up the hill. The cave entrance was partway up the slope, shaped like a capital A. Its heavy oak door stood unbarred. Inside lay a small chamber, as cold as an icehouse, lined by nature with solid limestone slick with chill dampness. Standing there in the gloom and looking out at the sunlit green valley felt both romantic and mysterious. But that solemn feeling faded fast, and the horseplay resumed. As soon as someone lit a candle, everyone piled onto the owner; after a brief scuffle and spirited defense, the candle was knocked down or blown out, prompting cheers of laughter and another chase. But everything ends. Eventually the group filed down the steep slope of the main passage, a wavering row of lights barely revealing the high stone walls rising almost to their meeting point sixty feet above. This main corridor was only eight or ten feet across. Every few steps, other lofty but narrower openings branched away on either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s Cave was nothing but a sprawling maze of twisting passages that looped into each other, then out again, and led nowhere.\"}"
