          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"264ed9bb6b3ec14de4aee131\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut that hardly matters.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, clearly hoping to insult me, you told me you liked me against your will, against your reason, and even against your character. Wasn\u2019t that some excuse for my rudeness, if I was rude? But I have other reasons. You know I do. Even if my feelings hadn\u2019t already been against you, even if they had been neutral or even favorable, do you think anything could make me accept the man who has perhaps ruined forever the happiness of a dearly loved sister?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, Mr. Darcy changed colour, but the feeling passed quickly, and he listened without interrupting her while she went on\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason to think the worst of you. Nothing can excuse the unfair and ungenerous part you played there. You dare not, you cannot deny that you were the main, if not the only, cause of separating them, of exposing one to the world\u2019s censure for fickleness, the other to its scorn for disappointed hopes, and dragging them both into the cruelest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and with sharp indignation saw that he listened with an expression that showed he felt no regret. He even looked at her with a smile that feigned disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny it?\u201d she demanded again.\\n\\nWith forced calm he replied, \u201cI do not wish to deny that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. Toward him I have shown more kindness than toward myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth refused to acknowledge this polite remark, though she understood its meaning, and it did nothing to win her over.\\n\\n\u201cBut my dislike does not rest on this alone,\u201d she continued. \u201cLong before that happened, my opinion of you was fixed. Your character was revealed to me in the account I heard many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On that subject, what can you say? With what imagined act of friendship can you defend yourself? Under what false story can you deceive anyone now?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a keen interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, less calmly, his colour rising.\\n\\n\u201cWho, knowing what his misfortunes have been, could help feeling for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated scornfully. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have been very great indeed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd caused by you,\u201d cried Elizabeth, fervently. \u201cYou have reduced him to his current state of poverty\u2014relative poverty.\u201d\"}"
