          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"25aa3cc453634762e1fe9f21\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, knights of the castle, met Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay, and the four crashed together and shattered their spears down to their hands. Then Sir Pertolope rode out from the castle, and Sir Lionel met him; Pertolope, the Green Knight, struck down Sir Lionel, brother to Sir Launcelot. Noble heralds watched everything, noting who fought best and their names. Sir Bleobaris broke his spear on Sir Gareth, yet the blow threw Sir Bleobaris to the ground. When Sir Galihodin saw that, he told Sir Gareth to defend himself, and Gareth knocked him down. Sir Galihud grabbed a spear to avenge his brother, but Gareth served him the same way. Sir Dinadan, his brother La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore le Desirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage all charged, and Gareth unhorsed them with the same spear. King Agwisance of Ireland marveled at Sir Gareth, seeing him appear green at one moment and blue the next, changing colors each time he rode back and forth so that no king or knight could recognize him. King Agwisance rode at Sir Gareth, and Gareth struck him from the horse, saddle and all. King Carados of Scotland came next, and Gareth toppled him horse and rider. He served King Uriens of Gore the same way. Sir Bagdemagus then entered the lists, and Gareth threw him down, horse and man, to the ground. Bagdemagus\u2019s son Meliganus broke a spear hard and knightly against Gareth. Then the noble prince Sir Galahault shouted, \u201cKnight with the many colors, well have you jousted; now prepare yourself so I may joust with you.\u201d Sir Gareth heard him, took up a great spear, and they met. The prince broke his spear, but Gareth struck him on the left side of the helm, sending him reeling, and he would have fallen if his men had not caught him. \u201cTruly,\u201d said King Arthur, \u201cthat knight with the many colors is a good knight.\u201d The king called Sir Launcelot and asked him to meet that knight. \u201cSire,\u201d said Launcelot, \u201cI would rather spare him now, for he has endured enough today. When a good knight does this well, it is not right to hinder his honor, especially when one sees he has labored so hard. Perhaps he fights for his own cause today, and perhaps he is the lady\u2019s favorite above all others here, for I see he strives to do great deeds. Therefore,\u201d said Launcelot, \u201ctoday he shall have the honor. Even if I could take it from him, I will not.\u201d\"}"
