          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e0e92c293e55c376276af72\",\"rewritten_text\":\"How could I, a bewildered village boy, escape the strange contradictions that even the best and wisest fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be true,\u201d I told Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nAfter that, I rolled onto my face, grabbed the hair on each side of my head, and pulled hard. I knew how foolish my heart was\u2014all wild and out of place\u2014and I felt it would serve me right if I yanked my face up by my hair and knocked it against the pebbles to punish it for belonging to such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy, ever the wisest girl, stopped trying to argue. She put her work-roughened but comforting hand over mine, one after another, and gently drew them out of my hair. Then she lightly patted my shoulder in a soothing way, while I cried into my sleeve, just as I had in the brewery yard, and felt a vague conviction that someone\u2014or everyone\u2014had treated me unfairly.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d Biddy said, \u201cand that is that you felt you could trust me, Pip. I\u2019m glad of another thing too: you know you can rely on me to keep your secret and try to deserve your confidence. If your first teacher\u2014poor thing, so in need of learning herself\u2014were teaching you now, she thinks she knows the lesson she would set. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you\u2019ve moved beyond her, so it\u2019s useless now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and added in a fresh, pleasant tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, jumping up, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI will always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d Biddy said.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never will be, so that means always. Not that I need to tell you anything, because you know all I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d Biddy breathed, almost whispering, as she looked out at the ships. Then she repeated, in her earlier cheerful tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told her we would walk a little farther, and we did, as the summer afternoon softened into summer evening\u2014so beautiful to see.\"}"
