{"request_id":"ef864c0227d9affefb87defa","rewritten_text":"We finally lightened the box by removing two thirds of what was inside, and only then, with considerable effort, could we lift it out of the pit. We stashed the removed items among the brambles and left the dog to guard them, with strict instructions from Jupiter not to budge or make a sound until we came back. We rushed home with the chest, reaching the hut safely but exhausted at one in the morning. Completely spent, we could do no more right away. We rested until two, had supper, then set out for the hills again with three sturdy sacks that we were fortunate to have on hand. Just before four we reached the pit, divided the remaining loot as evenly as possible, and, leaving the holes unfilled, returned once more to the hut, where we dropped off our golden loads just as the first pale streaks of dawn appeared above the eastern treetops.

We were now utterly worn out, but the intensity of the moment kept us from sleeping. After a fitful three or four hours of rest, we rose as if we had a prior agreement to examine our treasure.

The chest had been packed to the brim, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night inspecting its contents. There had been no attempt at order; everything was thrown in at random. After sorting it all carefully, we realized we were far richer than we first believed. In coins alone there were somewhat more than four hundred fifty thousand dollars—calculated as best we could from contemporary tables. There wasn’t a trace of silver. The hoard was entirely gold from earlier eras, a wide range of French, Spanish, and German pieces, with a few English guineas, and several counters we had never seen before. Some very large, heavy coins were so worn we couldn’t make out their inscriptions. There was no American money. The jewels were harder to appraise. We counted one hundred ten diamonds—many of them large and superb—a set of eighteen dazzling rubies, three hundred ten beautiful emeralds, and twenty-one sapphires plus a single opal. All of the stones had been pried from their settings and tossed loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we collected from the rest of the gold, seemed to have been smashed with hammers, as if someone wanted to avoid identification."}

