          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da1518119e3b9fd696507a26\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I spent two years. Day after day hundreds passed; nothing outward changed, yet some days quietly worked on my mind as I slid toward death. I studied more; I connected more with other people\u2019s thoughts in books; I read history and felt my own identity fade among the crowd who had lived before me. Perhaps as the sting of immediate suffering eased, I became more human. Solitude also lost some of its charm: I began to want sympathy again; not that I wished to seek a crowd, but I longed for one friend to love me. You may say I was slowly becoming ready to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I wanted had to be so pure, so free of outer influence, that in the world I would surely be thwarted by the coarse elements that mix even with its best feelings. Believe me, I was then less fit for any communion with other people than before. When I left them, they tormented me, but only as pain and sickness torment: something outside the mind that hurts it, and that I wanted to cast away. But now I would have wished for sympathy; I would have tried to bind my soul to someone else\u2019s, and so I would have prepared for myself deep drafts of disappointment and suffering; for I was as tender as the sensitive plant, nothing but nerve. I did not seek sympathy and help in ambition or wisdom, but sweet and mutual affection; smiles to cheer me and gentle words of comfort. I wanted one heart into which I could pour my complaints without restraint, and from whose heavenly nature blessed fruit might grow from such poor seed. Yet how could I find this? The love that is the soul of friendship is a gentle spirit seldom found except when two kind beings are joined from early youth, or when bound by shared suffering and pursuits; it comes to a few chosen ones unsought and unaware; it descends as gentle dew on selected spots which, however barren before, grow under its kind influence into gardens of sweet plants; but when desired it flies; it mocks the prayers of those who worship it; it will bestow itself, but will not be sought.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go out to seek sympathy; yet there on my solitary heath, under my low roof with desert all around, it came to me like a sunbeam in winter that adorns while it melts the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone on ruined fruit; I did not revive under its light, for I was too utterly undone to feel its kindly power.\"}"
