          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e0db27bc3c801c2f752023b0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is peculiar, but everyone who vanishes is reported to have been seen in San Francisco. It must be a charming city and contain all the attractions of the next world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you suppose has become of Basil?\u201d asked Dorian, raising his Burgundy toward the light and marveling at his own composure in discussing it.\\n\\n\u201cI have not the faintest notion. If Basil chooses to hide, it is no concern of mine. If he is dead, I prefer not to think about him. Death is the sole thing that ever frightens me. I loathe it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d murmured the younger man, tiredly.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d replied Lord Henry, drawing beneath his nose the gilt lattice of an open vinaigrette, \u201cone can endure everything nowadays save that. Death and vulgarity are the only two realities in the nineteenth century that cannot be explained away. Let us take our coffee in the music-room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran off with played Chopin divinely. Poor Victoria! I was very fond of her. The house feels rather desolate without her. Naturally, marriage is only a habit, a bad one. Still, one regrets the loss even of one\u2019s most wretched habits. Perhaps one regrets them most of all. They are so integral to one\u2019s personality.\u201d\\n\\nDorian remained silent, but left the table, moved into the adjoining room, sat at the piano, and let his fingers wander over the black and white ivory of the keys. When the coffee had been brought, he paused, and glancing at Lord Henry, said, \u201cHarry, did it ever cross your mind that Basil was murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was popular and always wore a Waterbury watch. Why should anyone murder him? He was not brilliant enough to make enemies. Of course, he had extraordinary talent for painting. But a man may paint like Velasquez and still be perfectly dull. Basil was decidedly dull. He interested me only once, and that was when he confessed, years ago, that he idolized you and that you were the governing motive of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was deeply attached to Basil,\u201d said Dorian with a sad note in his voice. \u201cBut don\u2019t people claim he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, certain papers do. It strikes me as highly improbable. I know there are terrible quarters in Paris, but Basil was not the type to frequent them. He lacked curiosity. It was his principal failing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry, if I told you that I had murdered Basil?\u201d asked the younger man.\"}"
