          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6b70f9fb4848841b4d5adb0f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was dressed for the role of \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d one of his most famous disguises and the very cause of the Cantervilles\u2019 quarrel with their neighbor, Lord Rufford. It was about a quarter past two in the morning, and as far as he could tell, everyone was asleep. He was heading toward the library to see whether any trace of the bloodstain remained when two figures suddenly sprang out of a dark corner, waved their arms wildly over their heads, and shouted \u201cBOO!\u201d in his ear.\\n\\nPanic seized him, understandable enough under the circumstances, and he ran for the staircase, only to find Washington Otis waiting there with the big garden syringe. Trapped on all sides by his enemies and driven almost to desperation, he fled into the great iron stove, which luckily was unlit, and had to crawl home through the flues and chimneys, reaching his room filthy, disordered, and in despair.\\n\\nAfter that night he made no more attempts at nocturnal outings. The twins set ambushes for him several times and scattered nutshells all over the hallways every night, to the great irritation of their parents and the servants, but it was useless. It was clear his feelings were so hurt that he would not show himself. Mr. Otis went back to his massive history of the Democratic Party, a project he had been working on for years; Mrs. Otis organized a spectacular clam-bake that astonished the whole county; the boys devoted themselves to lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American games; and Virginia rode through the lanes on her pony with the young Duke of Cheshire, who was spending the last week of his holiday at Canterville Chase. Everyone assumed the ghost had left, and indeed Mr. Otis wrote to Lord Canterville saying so. Lord Canterville replied with great pleasure at the news and sent his congratulations to the Minister\u2019s excellent wife.\\n\\nBut the Otises were mistaken. The ghost was still in the house, though now nearly a wreck, and he had no intention of giving up, especially after learning that the young Duke of Cheshire was among the guests. The Duke\u2019s great-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered Colonel Carbury a hundred guineas that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost, and he was found the next morning on the card-room floor, paralyzed so completely that, though he lived to an old age, he could never say anything again but \u201cDouble Sixes.\u201d\"}"
        }
