          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"74bf6ef5b601b9d4da6b7d04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His story went thus: traveling from home and passing through Datchet, he was startled to see smoke curling from the inn\u2019s chimney. Supposing he might find fellow travelers gathered there, he knocked and was let in. He discovered no one inside but Lucy and her mother; the latter, disabled by rheumatism, had lost the use of her limbs, and so, one after another, every remaining inhabitant had departed, leaving the two alone. Lucy begged him to stay; within a week or two her mother would improve, and they could then set forth, but they would surely die if abandoned helpless and desolate. The man replied that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, so in his view it was impossible to remain. As a desperate measure, Lucy entrusted him with a letter for Idris, to be delivered wherever he should meet us. This duty at least he performed, and Idris, moved, received the following letter:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI know you will remember me and take pity, and I dare hope you will help me; what other hope exists? Forgive my manner of writing; I am utterly bewildered. A month ago my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and in another month I am sure she could travel, in the way you kindly promised to arrange. But now everyone is gone\u2014everyone. As they departed, each said perhaps my mother would recover before we were entirely deserted. Three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his newborn child, remained behind till the end; the family being large, I thought I might persuade them to wait a little longer for us. Instead I found their house empty. Since then I have not seen a single soul until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother does not know our condition; she is so sick that I have concealed it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? We must surely perish miserably as we are. Were I to attempt to move my mother now, she would die on the road; and even if, once she improves, I could somehow learn the roads and travel so many miles to the sea, you would all be in France, and the vast ocean would separate us\u2014a terror even to sailors.\u201d\"}"
        }
