          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c041b89e11f6aa0ba77b6d77\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her situation was truly one of uncommon trial and difficulty. Though she was consumed with eager determination to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s past, she knew she had to honor the trust placed in her by the wretched woman she had just spoken with, as the young and innocent girl that she was. The woman\u2019s words and manner had touched Rose Maylie deeply; alongside her love for her young charge, and nearly as intense in its devotion, burned her tender hope of guiding the outcast back toward repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey planned to remain in London only three days before leaving for several weeks on a distant stretch of coast. Midnight of the first day had come. What plan could she devise that could be carried out within forty-eight hours? And how could she delay the journey without rousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was with them and would remain for the next two days; but Rose knew the excellent gentleman\u2019s impetuosity too well and could too clearly envision the fury with which he would, in the first burst of anger, condemn the young woman responsible for Oliver\u2019s capture. She could not confide in him when there was no experienced voice to back her plea for the girl. All this demanded extreme caution and careful behavior when telling Mrs. Maylie, whose first instinct would surely be to consult the good doctor. As for turning to any legal adviser, even if she had known where to begin, it was scarcely conceivable for the same reason. She briefly thought of seeking help from Harry, but this immediately brought back the memory of their last parting, and she felt it unworthy to summon him when\u2014tears rising as she followed the thought\u2014he might by now have learned to forget her and found greater happiness away.\\n\\nTorn by these conflicting reflections, leaning now toward one course and then another, and shrinking back from all of them as each new consideration rose before her, Rose passed a sleepless, anxious night. After further wrestling with herself the next day, she reached the desperate decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it pains him to come back here,\u201d she thought, \u201chow much it will pain me! Yet perhaps he won\u2019t come; he may write, or he may come and carefully avoid meeting me\u2014he did when he left. I hardly believed he would, but it was better for us both.\u201d With that, Rose set down the pen and turned away, unwilling even to let the paper that would carry her message witness her tears.\"}"
