          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e5392910f497bc93bd8c16db\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not everyone would have insisted so kindly, refusing to take Jane\u2019s answer, but she flatly said she would not write any denial yesterday, as Jane wanted; she would wait\u2014and sure enough, by last evening it was all settled that Jane would go. Quite a surprise to me! I had no idea! Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and told her immediately that, after thinking over the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s position, she had decided to accept it. I did not know a thing until it was finished. \\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d \\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us; Mrs. Elton insisted we come. The plan was made on the hill while we were walking with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You must all spend your evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I absolutely must have you all come.\u2019\u201d \\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, was he?\u201d \\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he refused from the start; and though I thought he would come, since Mrs. Elton said she would not let him off, he did not. But my mother, Jane, and I were all there, and we had a very pleasant evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, are always pleasant, though everyone seemed rather tired after the morning party. Even pleasure is tiring\u2014and I cannot say any of them seemed to have enjoyed it much. However, I will always think it a very pleasant gathering, and feel very grateful to the kind friends who included me.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you did not notice, had been making up her mind all day?\u201d \\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cWhenever it happens, it must be unwelcome to her and to every friend\u2014but I hope her position will have every comfort possible\u2014I mean, in the character and manners of the family.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, everything that could make her happy is there. Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there is not another nursery in Mrs. Elton\u2019s circle so generous and elegant. Mrs. Smallridge is the most delightful woman! Her style of living almost equals Maple Grove\u2014and as for the children, except for the little Sucklings and Bragges, there are no sweeter, more elegant children anywhere. Jane will be treated with such respect and kindness! It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure. And her salary! I really cannot even bring myself to tell you the amount, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
        }
