{"request_id":"2f81d8f8e49b9e83a42839a7","rewritten_text":"When this was done, a few more loops were released from the rim, another section of cloth was pulled through, and the freed loops were fastened to their proper buttons. In this manner I could slide the entire upper part of the bag between the netting and the hoop. The hoop would then drop down inside the car, leaving the car and all it carried supported only by the buttons. At first this might have seemed risky, but it was not. The buttons were not only very sturdy, but also so close together that each one held only a small share of the weight. Even if the car and its load had been three times heavier, I would have remained calm. I lifted the hoop again inside the gum-elastic cover and braced it almost at its original height with three light poles made for the purpose. This kept the bag spread out at the top and held the lower part of the netting in place. All that remained was to close the mouth of the enclosure, which I did by pulling the material’s folds together and twisting them tightly inside with a kind of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the cover arranged around the car were three circular panes of thick yet clear glass, which allowed me to see easily around the horizon. The cloth forming the bottom held a fourth window of the same sort, aligned with a small opening in the car’s floor. This let me look straight down. I could not install a similar window overhead because the way the opening at the top was sealed left the cloth wrinkled, so I would not see anything directly above me. This hardly mattered, since even with a top window, the balloon itself would have blocked my view.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a circular hole eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge matched the threads of a screw. Into this rim I screwed the large tube of the condenser, with the main body of the machine, of course, inside the gum-elastic chamber.”"}

