          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8770f155e85b531d687e7d2b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I passed several funeral processions; few mourners joined them, and the onlookers treated them as omens of disaster. Some watched with fierce eagerness, others ran off in fear, and a few cried aloud. \\n\\nAfter helping those who were already ill, Adrian had focused on hiding the plague\u2019s symptoms and spread from London\u2019s people. He knew that fear and dark expectations fed disease, that hopelessness and constant worry made bodies more open to infection. So no disturbing sights were allowed: shops mostly stayed open, and there were still some people in the streets. Even so, since I had not seen the city since the plague began, London looked changed enough to me. No carriages rolled by, grass grew tall between the paving stones, the houses seemed abandoned, and most shutters were closed. Everyone I passed wore a ghastly, frightened look, nothing like the usual brisk manner of Londoners. My lone carriage drew stares as it clattered toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets around it were even bleaker and emptier. \\n\\nAdrian\u2019s antechamber was crowded when I arrived; it was his scheduled hour for audiences. Not wishing to interrupt him, I waited and watched people come and go. They were merchants and tradespeople, their income gone with the halt of commerce and the pause in Britain\u2019s usual money-making bustle. A new arrival often showed anxiety or outright terror, yet those who left after being heard looked calm, even content. I could see my friend\u2019s influence in their quicker steps and brighter faces. When the clock struck two, no one else was admitted; the disappointed went away sullen or sorrowful while I entered the audience chamber. \\n\\nAdrian\u2019s improved health struck me at once. He was no longer bent like an over-tended spring flower that grows too fast for its strength and droops under its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were clear, his face composed, and a concentrated energy showed in every movement\u2014so different from his earlier languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries who sorted petitions or recorded notes from that day\u2019s meetings, and two or three petitioners still waited nearby. I admired his fairness and patience. Anyone who could afford to live outside London he advised to leave at once, and he gave them the means to do so.\"}"
