          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c017e9cb9fc96c9c3e9d81b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Rising early, and once more contriving to elude the watchfulness of my creditors, I hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall, spending the small stock of cash I had on volumes devoted to Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Once I returned home safely with these acquisitions, I dedicated every spare instant to studying them, and soon judged myself adequately skilled for carrying out my design. During these same days I strove constantly to appease the three creditors who had plagued me so severely. In the end I prevailed\u2014partly through selling enough household furniture to pay off half of what I owed, and partly by promising to settle the remainder once I completed a small enterprise I hinted at, begging their cooperation. They were uneducated men, and by these means I brought them readily into my scheme.\\n\\n\u201cWith matters fixed in this fashion, I managed, with my wife\u2019s help and under the strictest secrecy, to dispose of the last of my possessions and to borrow, here and there, under different pretexts, a respectable store of ready money, without any regard to how I might repay it later. With these resources I obtained at intervals fine cambric muslin in twelve-yard pieces, twine, a supply of caoutchouc varnish, a large deep wicker basket custom-made, and several other items necessary for building and outfitting a balloon of remarkable size. I instructed my wife to sew it together as quickly as she could and provided her with all the detailed directions she would need. Meanwhile, I fashioned the twine into netting of proper extent, fitted it with a hoop and the required cords, and purchased a quadrant, a compass, a spyglass, a common barometer with certain important alterations, and two astronomical instruments seldom seen. By night I then transported, to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam, five iron-bound casks holding about fifty gallons each and another of larger capacity, along with six properly shaped tin tubes three inches wide and ten feet long, a quantity of a certain metallic or semi-metallic substance I shall leave unnamed, and twelve demijohns filled with a very ordinary acid. The gas produced from these materials has never been generated by anyone else\u2014at least, never for such a purpose. I would willingly disclose the secret, were it not that it properly belongs to a citizen of Nantz, in France, who granted me the information under conditions.\u201d}\"}"
