{"request_id":"ce7f16f49cafdf31c1d68c48","rewritten_text":"Each compares it—not to the speech of any nation whose language he commands—but the opposite. The Frenchman holds it was a Spaniard’s voice, and “might have caught some words had he understood Spanish.” The Dutchman asserts it was that of a Frenchman; yet we read that “not understanding French, this witness was questioned through an interpreter.” The Englishman takes it for a German voice, and “does not understand German.” The Spaniard “is sure” it belonged to an Englishman, but judges wholly “by intonation,” since “he knows no English.” The Italian credits it to a Russian voice, yet “has never spoken with a native of Russia.” A second Frenchman disagrees with the first, insisting it was Italian; still, “being unacquainted with that tongue,” he, like the Spaniard, is “convinced by the intonation.” Consider how singular that voice must truly have been, to evoke testimony such as this—to possess tones in which residents of the five chief European regions recognized nothing familiar! You may suggest the voice was Asiatic—or African. Few Asiatics or Africans inhabit Paris; yet, without contesting the idea, I now direct your notice to three particulars. One witness calls the voice “harsh rather than shrill.” Two others describe it as “rapid and unequal.” No witness heard words—or even sounds resembling words—distinctly enough to report them.

“I know not,” Dupin continued, “what effect I have thus far produced upon your own perception; but I do not hesitate to affirm that legitimate deductions even from this fragment of the testimony—the part concerning the rough and shrill voices—suffice, in themselves, to awaken a suspicion that ought to guide every subsequent step in probing the enigma. I said ‘legitimate deductions;’ yet that phrasing is imperfect. I meant to convey that these deductions are the only rightful ones, and that the suspicion springs inevitably from them, as their sole outcome. What suspicion it is, though, I will not disclose yet. I simply ask you to remember that, in my mind, it proved strong enough to give a definite shape—a specific inclination—to my inquiries within the chamber.

“Let us now carry ourselves, imaginatively, to this chamber. What shall we first investigate there? The means of egress used by the murderers. It is no overstatement to declare that neither of us credits supernatural occurrences.”"}

