          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"513c51c05a7607f8c71d06d5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While events unfolded at the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the same old den from which the girl had taken Oliver, brooding over a dull, smoky fire. He rested a pair of bellows on his knee, as if he had been trying to coax the flames into livelier action. But he had sunk into deep thought; his arms folded over the bellows, his chin on his thumbs, his gaze fixed absently on the rusty bars.\\n\\nBehind him, the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling sat at a table, intent on a game of whist. The Dodger played dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The sharp face of the Dodger, intelligent even in idleness, seemed even keener as he studied the game and carefully read Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand. From time to time he cast intent glances, adjusting his own play according to what he saw in his neighbor\u2019s cards. The night was cold, and the Dodger wore his hat indoors, as he often did. He clenched a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he needed a sip from the quart pot on the table, filled with gin-and-water for the group.\\n\\nMaster Bates watched the play closely, but being more excitable than his polished friend, he reached for the gin-and-water more often. He also tossed out jokes and stray remarks, all quite improper during a serious rubber. The Dodger, relying on their close friendship, took the liberty of scolding him more than once for these slip-ups. Master Bates took the lectures in good humor, merely telling his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d to stick his head in a sack, or responding with some equally tidy joke that won praise from Mr. Chitling. It was noteworthy that Mr. Chitling and his partner always lost, yet this never angered Master Bates. Instead it delighted him. At the close of every deal he roared with laughter, insisting he had never seen such a merry game in all his life.\\n\\n\u201cThat makes two doubles and the rub,\u201d Mr. Chitling said, face drawn, as he pulled a half-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never saw a fellow like you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
