          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dec973f7ed9baa973235529\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We declared with such volume and conviction that we were free men that the liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic crowd grew interested. They gathered around us, taking on a very firm posture. The orator said: \u201cIf you truly are freemen, you have nothing to fear\u2014Britain\u2019s God-given liberties shield and protect you! (Applause.) You shall soon see. Produce your proof.\u201d \u201cWhat proof?\u201d \u201cProof that you are freemen.\u201d Ah\u2014now I remembered! I came to my senses and said nothing. But the king burst out: \u201cYou\u2019re mad, man. It would be better and more reasonable for this thief and scoundrel here to prove that we are not freemen.\u201d You see, he knew his own laws the way many people do\u2014by words, not by consequences. They take on real meaning and become vivid when you apply them to yourself. Everyone shook their heads and looked disappointed; some walked off, no longer interested. The orator spoke again, this time in a businesslike tone instead of sentiment: \u201cIf you do not know your country\u2019s laws, it is time you learned them. You are strangers to us; you won\u2019t deny that. You may be freemen; we don\u2019t deny that; but you may also be slaves. The law is clear: it does not require the claimant to prove you are slaves\u2014it requires you to prove you are not.\u201d I said: \u201cDear sir, just give us time to send to Astolat; or give us time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d \u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and you cannot hope to have them granted. It would take much time and inconvenience your master unjustifiably\u2014\u201d \u201cMaster, you idiot!\u201d the king raged. \u201cI have no master, I myself am the k\u2014\u201d \u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d I got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in trouble enough; it would only make things worse to let these people think we were lunatics. There\u2019s no point in dragging out the details. The earl put us up for sale and auctioned us off. This same infernal law had existed in our own South in my era over thirteen hundred years later, and under it hundreds of freemen who couldn\u2019t prove their status had been sold into lifelong slavery without it making any particular impression on me\u2014but the moment the law and the auction block entered my personal experience, something that had merely seemed improper suddenly became hellish. That\u2019s just how we are built. Yes, we were sold at auction, like pigs. In a large town with an active market we might have fetched a good price, but this place was utterly stagnant, so we sold for a sum that makes me feel ashamed every time I think of it.\"}"
