          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2fec8a6ddcde466854baad0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The studio was filled with the rich scent of roses, and when the light summer wind moved through the garden trees, it carried in through the open door the heavy smell of lilac or the lighter perfume of pink-flowering thorn.\\n\\nFrom the corner of the divan made of Persian saddle-bags where he lay smoking endless cigarettes, as he always did, Lord Henry Wotton could just see the glow of the honey-sweet, golden blossoms of a laburnum, whose shaking branches seemed barely strong enough to support such fiery beauty. Every so often the fanciful shadows of birds in flight flickered across the long tussore-silk curtains stretched before the wide window, creating a brief Japanese effect and making him think of those pale, jade-faced painters of Tokyo who, through an art that must remain still, try to suggest speed and motion. The low buzzing of bees forcing their way through the tall unmown grass, or circling insistently around the dusty gilt horns of the wild woodbine, only seemed to make the silence heavier. The faint roar of London sounded like the deep drone of a distant organ.\\n\\nIn the center of the room, secured to an upright easel, stood a full-length portrait of a young man of extraordinary beauty, and in front of it, a little distance away, sat the artist himself, Basil Hallward, whose sudden disappearance some years before had caused so much public excitement and so many strange rumors.\\n\\nAs the painter studied the graceful, handsome figure he had captured so well, a pleased smile passed over his face and seemed about to remain. But he suddenly jumped up and, closing his eyes, pressed his fingers to his eyelids as if he were trying to lock inside his mind some strange dream from which he dreaded waking.\\n\\n\u201cIt is your best work, Basil, the best thing you have ever done,\u201d said Lord Henry lazily. \u201cYou must absolutely send it next year to the Grosvenor. The Academy is too large and too vulgar. Whenever I have gone there, there have been either so many people that I could not see the pictures, which was dreadful, or so many pictures that I could not see the people, which was worse. The Grosvenor is really the only place.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I will send it anywhere,\u201d Basil replied, throwing his head back in that odd way that used to make his friends laugh at him at Oxford.\"}"
