          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1e7e1d5850866406a9dd3d5a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very respectable-looking old gentleman and a good-looking younger one whose right arm was in a sling. And, my goodness, how the crowd hollered and laughed and wouldn\u2019t stop. I couldn\u2019t see the joke in it, and figured the duke and king couldn\u2019t either. I expected them to turn pale. But no, not even a hint of pale. The duke never let on he knew anything was wrong; he just wandered around smiling and contented, like a jug gently bubbling over with buttermilk. And the king stared down mournfully at the newcomers, as if it hurt his heart to think there were such frauds and scoundrels in this world. He played it perfectly. Many of the leading people gathered around the king to show they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked baffled out of his wits. Soon he began to speak, and I saw right away he sounded like an Englishman\u2014not exactly like the king, though the king\u2019s imitation was pretty good. I can\u2019t give his exact words or imitate his way of talking, but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I wasn\u2019t expecting. I\u2019ll admit, plain and honest, I\u2019m not in the best shape to handle it. My brother and I have had bad luck: he broke his arm, and our luggage was mistakenly taken off at a town upriver last night. I am Peter Wilks\u2019s brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can\u2019t hear or speak, and now can\u2019t even make useful signs since he only has one hand to do it with. We are who we say we are, and in a day or two, when I get our baggage, I can prove it. But until then I\u2019ll say nothing more and head to the hotel to wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new dummy left. The king laughed and blustered:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014very likely, isn\u2019t it?\u2014and so convenient for a fraud who has to make signs and doesn\u2019t know how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s really good\u2014and mighty clever under the circumstances!\u201d\\n\\nHe laughed again, and so did everyone else except maybe three or four people, or maybe half a dozen. One was that doctor. Another was a sharp-looking gentleman with an old-style carpetbag made of carpet material, who had just come off the steamboat and was whispering with the doctor while glancing over at the king and nodding. That was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville. Another was a big rough fellow who had come up, listened to all the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
