          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd92adb6030ea9854247a2e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His character and temperament, though different from hers, would have satisfied all her hopes. Their marriage would have benefited them both: her ease and liveliness could have softened his mind and refined his manners, while his judgment, experience, and knowledge of the world would have offered her far greater advantages.\\n\\nBut no such happy marriage would now show the admiring public what true marital happiness looked like. Another engagement, very different and preventing the first, was about to take place in their family.\\n\\nShe could not imagine how Wickham and Lydia would be supported in even modest independence. Yet she could easily imagine how little lasting happiness awaited a couple brought together only because their passion outweighed their virtue.\\n\\nMr. Gardiner soon wrote again to his brother. In reply to Mr. Bennet\u2019s thanks, he briefly assured him of his eagerness to promote the welfare of any member of the family, and ended by begging that the subject never be mentioned to him again. The main purpose of his letter was to tell them that Mr. Wickham had decided to leave the militia.\\n\\n\u201cIt has long been my wish that he should do so,\u201d he added, \u201cas soon as his marriage was settled. And I think you will agree that removing him from that corps is highly advisable for both himself and my niece. Mr. Wickham intends to enter the Regulars; among his old friends, there are still some willing and able to help him in the army. He has been promised an ensigncy in General ----\u2019s regiment, now stationed in the north. It is good that it lies so far from this part of the country. He makes fair promises; and I hope that among different company, where each will have a reputation to maintain, they will both behave more prudently. I have written to Colonel Forster to inform him of our plans and to ask him to reassure Mr. Wickham\u2019s various creditors in and near Brighton with promises of quick payment, for which I have pledged myself. Will you take the trouble to offer the same assurances to his creditors in Meryton? I will include a list, based on his information. He has listed all his debts; I hope at least he has not deceived us. Haggerston has our instructions, and everything should be settled in a week. They will then join his regiment unless they are first invited to Longbourn; and Mrs. Gardiner tells me my niece very much wishes to see you all before she leaves the south.\u201d\"}"
