          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a66cce410df3f0fb3b4abd2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJupiter,\u201d he said, when we stood at the tree\u2019s base, \u201ccome here! Was that skull tacked to the branch with the face outward, or turned inward against the wood?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDe face turned out, massa, so de crows could peck de eyes easy, widout no bother.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cVery well\u2014was it this eye or that one through which you dropped the beetle?\u201d Legrand tapped each of Jupiter\u2019s eyes in turn.\\n\\n\u201cDis one, massa\u2014the left eye\u2014jis same as you telled me,\u201d he answered, pointing, however, to his right eye.\\n\\n\u201cThat suffices\u2014we\u2019ll try once more.\u201d\\n\\nNow my friend, in whose madness I fancied I detected a certain method, shifted the peg that marked the beetle\u2019s fall to a position about three inches west of where it had been. As before, he measured from the trunk to this peg and then prolonged the line straight out to fifty feet, which brought us to a spot several yards away from the site of our previous digging.\\n\\nAround this new point he traced a circle, somewhat broader than the earlier one, and we set to with our spades again. I was desperately tired, yet, scarcely aware what had transformed my mood, I no longer felt such distaste for the toil he demanded. Indeed, I had grown inexplicably absorbed\u2014nay, even animated. Perhaps there was something in Legrand\u2019s wild behavior\u2014an air of planning, of cool calculation\u2014that impressed me. I dug zealously, and once or twice I caught myself actually peering, with something strikingly like anticipation, for the imaginary treasure whose image had crazed my poor companion. When these wandering thoughts held me most strongly, and after we had labored perhaps an hour and a half, the dog again broke out in fierce howls. His earlier agitation had been, clearly, mere play or whim; now he sounded fierce and earnest. When Jupiter tried once more to muzzle him, the animal fought savagely, sprang into the pit, and raked away the soil in a frenzy. Within moments he had laid bare a heap of human bones\u2014two complete skeletons tangled together\u2014mixed with several metal buttons, and what seemed the powdery remains of rotted woollen cloth. A couple of strokes of a spade brought up the blade of a large Spanish knife, and, as we dug deeper, three or four loose coins of gold and silver came into view.\"}"
