          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b190b05d8c61119f0170713b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I longed to feign my death; my satisfied heirs would lay claim to my fortune, and I should buy my liberty. Yet the stratagem must be artfully wrought; I could not be left penniless, some funds must be secured. Alas, to what vile expedients must I stoop? Still, an entire existence steeped in falsehood was otherwise my lot: and whenever shame at plotting any deceit made me recoil from my intention, some aunt or cousin would come, and their visit irresistibly drew me back and fixed me in my scheme. They would remind me that death is every person\u2019s destiny; then insist my father had surely lost his senses since my mother died, that he was deranged and that I was fortunate\u2014for in one frenzy he might have slain me instead of ending his own wild life. All this, of course, was gently expressed; not bluntly, lest my feelings suffer, but\\n\\nmurmured thus and thus, in cautious hints soft and low,\\n\\nwith lowered lashes and sympathetic smiles or whimpers; and I listened with a composed face though every nerve quivered; I who dared not murmur yea or nay to such profanity. Oh, what a delectable life, entirely without guile! I with my dove\u2019s visage and fox\u2019s heart: for in truth I felt only the abasement of lying, without any sacred sense of conscious innocence to redeem it. I, who had formerly been clothed in the radiant robe of sincerity, must now borrow a coat of many colours: it would seem ill-fitted at first, but habit would teach me to drape it in graceful folds, to practice deception with elegance. Aye, I might dye my spirit with falsehood until its native hue was wholly concealed. Oh, beloved father! Receive the pure heart of your wretched daughter; allow me to join you as stainless as I once was, else you will fail to know my altered form. As sorrow might transform Constance, so would deceit transform me until in heaven you would exclaim, \u201cThis is not my child\u201d\u2014My father, to be happy now and when we meet again I must flee from this entire existence, which is mockery to one such as I. Only in solitude shall I remain myself; in solitude I shall remain yours.\\n\\nAlas! Even now I look back with loathing on my devices and contrivances by which, after many painful struggles, I secured my escape. I might detail, at great length, the methods I employed\u2014first to obtain a modest support for the rest of my years, and next to assure the belief of my demise: I might, but I refuse.\"}"
