{"request_id":"a95cc6daa651276b73853342","rewritten_text":"Even now I had not drained the bitter cup; I was not yet convinced of my loss, did not feel in every heartbeat, every nerve, every thought, that I was the last of my race—that I was the LAST MAN.

The day grew overcast, and a light rain began at sunset. Even the endless sky weeps, I thought; so why should a mortal be ashamed to pour out tears? I remembered old tales of people who wept until they turned into ever-flowing fountains. If only it were so; then my fate would resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Grief is strange—it weaves a web so it can trace its sorrow through every change around it; it blends with all living things, feeds on every object; like light, it fills everything and casts its own colors over all.

In my search I had wandered far from the spot where I was cast ashore and reached one of the watchtowers that dot the Italian coast. I was glad for shelter, glad to find something made by human hands after staring so long at nature’s desolate emptiness. I entered and climbed the rough spiral stairs to the guardroom. Fate was kind enough that no painful trace of former occupants remained. A few planks laid across iron trestles and covered with dried corn leaves formed the bed. An open chest held some half-moldy biscuit, which awakened an appetite I must have had before but had not noticed. Thirst, harsh and burning—caused by the seawater I had swallowed and my exhausted body—tormented me. Kind nature made the relief of these needs pleasant, so even I felt refreshed and calmed when I ate that meager food and drank a little of the sour wine left in a flask in this deserted place. Then I stretched out on the bed, not unworthy of a shipwrecked man. The earthy smell of the dried leaves soothed my senses after the foul seaweed odor. I forgot my solitude. I neither looked back nor forward; my senses drifted to rest. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes—of haymakers, a shepherd whistling for his dog when he needed help to drive the flock to the fold, of sights and sounds from my mountain childhood that I had long forgotten."}

