          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9b5c3cff607de63f72e92d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThere wasn\u2019t any tender nurturing by hand back then. Not at all!\u201d\\n\\nJoe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to accept.\\n\\n\u201cHe was no end of trouble to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble in sympathy with my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d my sister repeated. \u201cTrouble?\u201d Then she launched into a terrifying list of every illness I had caused, every night I had kept her awake, every high place I had fallen from, every low place I had fallen into, every injury I had inflicted on myself, and all the times she had wished me dead and I had stubbornly refused to oblige her.\\n\\nI think the Romans must have driven each other crazy with their noses. Maybe that\u2019s why they were so restless. Whatever the reason, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose aggravated me so much during the recounting of my offenses that I wanted to yank it until he yelped. Still, everything I had suffered so far was nothing compared to the horror that seized me when the silence after my sister\u2019s tirade finally broke, during which everyone, as I painfully knew, had been looking at me with disgust and outrage.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently steering the conversation back to its original topic, \u201cpork\u2014boiled\u2014is rich, isn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave a little brandy, Uncle,\u201d said my sister.\\n\\nOh heavens, the moment had arrived! He would find it weak, call it weak, and I would be undone. I clutched the table leg under the cloth with both hands and waited for the verdict.\\n\\nMy sister fetched the stone bottle, came back with it, and poured his brandy: no one else had any. The wretched man toyed with his glass\u2014picked it up, held it to the light, set it down\u2014stretching out my agony. Meanwhile Mrs. Joe and Joe were busily clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI couldn\u2019t take my eyes off him. Still gripping the table leg with hands and feet, I watched the miserable creature play with his glass, pick it up, smile, throw his head back, and drain the brandy. Immediately afterward, everyone was struck with unspeakable panic as he leaped to his feet, spun around several times in a horrifying coughing fit like a mad dance, and bolted outside; then we saw him through the window, violently spitting and making the ugliest faces, clearly out of his senses.\\n\\nI kept holding on while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran after him.\"}"
