          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3ae877afe4026916ba8cb137\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival kindled a sort of blaze in Saint Antoine\u2019s bosom, spreading swiftly as they passed, flickering in fiery faces at most doorways and windows. Still, none trailed behind them, and no man spoke when they stepped into the wine-shop, though every eye there fixed on them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nIf it was a cue to loosen tongues, it worked, bringing forth an echoed \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe weather is poor, gentlemen,\u201d Defarge added, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt that, each man glanced at his neighbour, then every gaze dropped and they sat mute. Only one man rose and departed.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d Defarge called aloud to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have travelled certain leagues with this worthy mender of roads, named Jacques. I met him\u2014by chance\u2014one day and a half from Paris. He is a fine fellow, this mender of roads named Jacques. Give him beverage, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nAnother man stood and left. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender called Jacques, who lifted his blue cap to the room and drank. Tucked inside his blouse he carried coarse dark bread; between sips he ate, sitting there chewing and drinking near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter. A third man got up and went out.\\n\\nDefarge refreshed himself with a swallow of wine\u2014but he took less than was poured for the newcomer, being himself a man to whom wine was no rarity\u2014and waited until the countryman finished his meal. He looked at no one present, and now no one regarded him; not even Madame Defarge, who had picked up her knitting and was at it.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked when it was timely.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome then! You shall see the lodging I told you you might occupy. It will fit you wonderfully.\u201d\\n\\nOut of the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, out of the courtyard up a steep stair, off the stair into a garret\u2014once the garret where a white-haired man sat on a low bench, bent forward and intensely busy, making shoes.\\n\\nNo white-haired man sat there now; yet the three men were present who had individually left the wine-shop. And between them and the distant white-haired man remained the single small link that they had once peered in at him through wall chinks.\\n\\nDefarge shut the door with care and spoke in a subdued tone:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! Here stands the witness encountered by arrangement, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
