          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0fb3a889354a57688ee309f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often pondered on the old idea of the split soul and privately amused myself with imagining two Dupins\u2014the one who invented and the one who solved.\\n\\nLet no one suppose from this that I\u2019m revealing a mystery or writing a romance. What I\u2019ve described about the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited, perhaps even unhealthy, intelligence. But the best way to explain the nature of his remarks at such times is to offer an example.\\n\\nOne night we were walking down a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Both of us seemed deep in thought, and we hadn\u2019t spoken for at least fifteen minutes. All at once Dupin broke out with these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s a very small fellow, that\u2019s true, and he\u2019d be better suited for the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere\u2019s no doubt about that,\u201d I answered automatically, not at first realizing\u2014so engrossed was I in my own reflections\u2014how perfectly he\u2019d matched my thoughts. A second later I came to my senses, and my amazement was immense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said seriously, \u201cthis is beyond me. I admit I\u2019m stunned and can hardly trust my senses. How could you possibly know I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I stopped to make absolutely sure he knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy pause? You were telling yourself his tiny frame made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly the subject of my thoughts. Chantilly was once a cobbler in the Rue St. Denis who, crazed for the stage, had taken on the role of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of the same name and had been publicly ridiculed for it.\\n\\n\u201cFor heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201ctell me how\u2014if there\u2019s any method at all\u2014you managed to read my mind like this.\u201d In truth I was even more shaken than I cared to show.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruit seller,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of soles lacked the height for Xerxes and the rest of that sort.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruit seller? You astound me\u2014I don\u2019t know any fruit seller at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who ran into you when we entered the street\u2014maybe fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now remembered that a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples on his head had nearly knocked me over by accident as we left the Rue C\u2014\u2014 and stepped into the street where we now stood; but how that connected to Chantilly was beyond me.\"}"
