{"request_id":"2cddb0d492d11d011b6002d9","rewritten_text":"It took some time before I pulled myself together enough to tend to the usual needs of the balloon. When I finally did, I inspected it carefully and, to my immense relief, found it unharmed. All my tools were intact, and I had, luckily, lost neither ballast nor provisions. I had secured everything so firmly that such a mishap was simply impossible. A glance at my watch showed six o’clock. I was still climbing fast, and my barometer indicated an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly beneath me on the ocean lay a small black object, slightly oblong, roughly the size and appearance of one of those little children’s toys called dominoes. Training my telescope on it, I clearly saw it was a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled and plunging heavily in the sea with her bow toward the west-southwest. Besides this vessel, I saw nothing but ocean, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is high time I explain to your Excellencies the purpose of this perilous journey. You will recall that the hardship I endured in Rotterdam finally drove me to decide on suicide. It was not that I had a direct loathing for life itself, but that I was tormented beyond endurance by the incidental miseries of my condition. In this frame of mind, wanting to live yet exhausted by life, the treatise at the bookseller’s stall offered my imagination an escape. I made my decision then. I would depart yet remain alive—to leave the world yet continue to exist—in short, to dispense with riddles: whatever the outcome, I resolved to force a passage to the moon, if it could be done. And so you do not think me a greater madman than I am, I will explain, as well as I can, the ideas that led me to believe that such an achievement, although undeniably difficult and fraught with danger, was not entirely beyond the reach of a daring spirit.

“The moon’s true distance from Earth was the first point to address. The average space between the centers of the two bodies is 59.9643 Earth equatorial radii, or only about 237,000 miles. I say average, but remember that the moon’s orbit, being an ellipse of eccentricity equal to 0.05484 of its major semi-axis, with Earth’s center at its focus, would let me greatly reduce the distance if I could contrive to meet the moon, so to speak, at its perigee.”"}

