          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"756665a26514768094c558e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The neglect had fallen on the offender\u2019s own head; when poor Lady Elliot died, no letter of condolence reached Kellynch, and there was every reason to fear that the Dalrymples now viewed the relationship as ended. How to settle this anxious business properly and be accepted as cousins again was the question, and it was a question that neither Lady Russell nor Mr. Elliot, in their sensible way, considered unimportant. \u201cFamily connections are always worth preserving; good company is always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple has taken a house in Laura Place for three months and will be living in style. She was in Bath last year, and Lady Russell heard her praised as a delightful woman. It would be very desirable to renew the connection, if it could be done without the Elliots compromising their propriety.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, insisted on his own approach, and finally wrote a very elegant letter full of explanation, regret, and entreaty to his right honorable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr. Elliot admired the letter, but it accomplished its purpose by eliciting three scrawled lines from the Dowager Viscountess. \u201cShe was very much honored, and would be happy to make their acquaintance.\u201d The toil of the business was over, and the rewards began. They visited at Laura Place, they possessed the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honorable Miss Carteret, displayed wherever they would be most conspicuous; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place,\u201d \u201cOur cousin Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret,\u201d were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been exceptionally agreeable, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they inspired, but they were nothing. There was no superiority of manners, accomplishments, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the name of \u201ca charming woman\u201d merely because she had a smile and a polite answer for everyone. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she would never have been tolerated in Camden Place but for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted she had expected something better; still, \u201cit was an acquaintance worth having.\u201d And when Anne ventured to share her opinion of them with Mr. Elliot, he agreed they were insignificant in themselves, but maintained that, as family connections, as good company, as people who would gather good company around them, they had value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr. Elliot, is the company of clever, well-informed people who have a lot to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
        }
