          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c6c00631ccbb5f970299638\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don't rush him out of the blue and risk bungling halfway; instead, I slip off to one side and edge in on him bit by bit, so he never dreams I'm about to clip him; then, suddenly, he's flat on his back without the faintest notion how it came about. That\u2019s just how I went after brother Dowley. I began talking slow and easy, as though I were merely idling away the time; and the oldest soul alive couldn\u2019t have taken the compass of where I set out and guessed where I meant to land: \u201cBoys, there\u2019s plenty that\u2019s curious in law, and custom, and usage, and all that, once you come to studying it; yes, and in the course and growth of human thought and movement, too. Written laws wear out; but unwritten laws\u2014they endure forever. Take the unwritten law of wages: it decrees they must creep upward, bit by bit, through the centuries. Mark the workings of it. We know what wages are now, here and there and elsewhere; we strike an average and call that the pay of to-day. We know what wages stood at a hundred years since, and two hundred; that\u2019s as far back as we can get, yet it\u2019s enough to show the law of progress, the measure and rate of the regular increase; and so, though we have no documents, we can come close to deciding what wages were three and four and five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we halt there? No. We quit peering backward; we swing around and apply the law to what lies ahead. Friends, I can name what people will earn at any date in the future you please, for centuries on. \u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d \u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will have risen to six times what they are now in your parts, with farm laborers getting 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d \u201cI would I might die now and live then!\u201d broke in Smug the wheelwright, his eyes shining greedily. \u201cAnd that\u2019s not the whole of it; they\u2019ll get their board besides\u2014such as it is: it won\u2019t puff them up. Two hundred and fifty years after that\u2014now keep your ears open\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wages will be\u2014remember, we\u2019re talking law here, not guesswork; a mechanic\u2019s wages will be twenty cents a day!\u201d A hushed gasp of wonder ran through them, and Dickon the mason whispered, eyes and hands uplifted:\"}"
