          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66c4f5c5dd1ec99a62406f6f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma never had any later reason to revise her low opinion of Mrs. Elton; her first impression was all too accurate. However Mrs. Elton struck her on their second meeting was exactly how she appeared at every meeting afterward\u2014full of self-importance, presumptuous, overly familiar, ignorant, and ill-mannered. She had a little beauty and a few accomplishments, yet so little judgment that she believed she arrived armed with superior worldly knowledge to enliven and improve a country neighborhood, convinced that Miss Hawkins had held a social standing only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance could surpass.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton saw her any differently. He seemed not only happy with her but proud, wearing the look of a man congratulating himself on having brought to Highbury a woman even Miss Woodhouse could not rival. Most of Mrs. Elton\u2019s new acquaintances, inclined to praise or unused to judging, followed Miss Bates\u2019s good humor or simply assumed the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she claimed. Thus compliments on Mrs. Elton passed from person to person as they naturally would, unhindered by Miss Woodhouse, who cheerfully continued her initial contribution by speaking pleasantly of Mrs. Elton\u2019s being \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one respect Mrs. Elton became even worse than she seemed at first: her feelings toward Emma shifted. Probably offended by how little Emma encouraged her offers of friendship, she pulled back and gradually grew much colder and more distant. Although Emma welcomed the cooler behavior, the malice causing it only increased her dislike. Mrs. Elton\u2019s manners toward Harriet\u2014and Mr. Elton\u2019s as well\u2014were equally disagreeable: sneering and dismissive. Emma hoped such treatment would quickly cure Harriet\u2019s attachment, yet the feelings that fueled it lowered the Eltons considerably in her eyes. Clearly, Harriet\u2019s affection had been offered up to marital confidences, and Emma\u2019s own part in the story, painted in the harshest colors for her and the most flattering for him, had likely been shared as well. Naturally, Harriet became the object of their shared resentment. Whenever conversation lagged, it must have been effortless to fall into criticizing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not show Emma through open disrespect found freer expression in the contemptuous way they treated Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton instantly developed a strong interest in Jane Fairfax\u2014right from the start. It was not merely that a quarrel with one young woman made the other more appealing; from the very beginning she indulged in excessive admiration, and without invitation or justification insisted she was there to help and protect Jane. Before Emma had lost her confidence and on about their third meeting, she heard the full extent of Mrs. Elton\u2019s self-appointed knight-errantry on the subject.\"}"
