{"request_id":"888f5f5f5af07cd242825349","rewritten_text":"The line of wavering gas-lamps stretched far into the distance, and outside a small walled house a lone hansom stood, its driver asleep within. He hurried toward Portland Place, glancing back now and then, as if afraid of being followed. Two men were standing at the Rich Street corner, reading a small bill pasted on the hoarding. A sudden curiosity seized him, and he crossed over. As he approached, the word “Murder,” bold in black letters, caught his eye. He started, a dark flush rising in his cheeks. It was a notice offering a reward for any clue to the capture of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, in a billy-cock hat, black coat, and check trousers, with a scar on the right cheek. He read it repeatedly, wondering whether the miserable man would be taken, and how he had come by his scar. Perhaps, one day, his own name would be plastered on London walls. Perhaps some day, a reward would be offered for him too.

The notion filled him with a sickened horror. He wheeled round and hurried farther into the night.

He scarcely knew where his steps led. He had a blurred memory of threading a maze of shabby houses, of losing himself in a vast web of dusky streets, and it was full morning when he finally reached Piccadilly Circus. As he sauntered homeward toward Belgrave Square, he met the heavy wagons bound for Covent Garden. The white-smocked carters, their cheerful sunburnt faces framed by coarse curls, swung along stoutly, cracking whips and calling to one another; on the back of a massive grey horse, leading a jangling team, perched a plump boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat, gripping the mane with small hands and laughing aloud; the heaps of vegetables resembled piles of jade against the morning sky, like green jade against the pink petals of some wondrous rose. Lord Arthur was oddly moved, though he knew not why. There was a delicacy in the dawn’s loveliness that struck him as indescribably plaintive, and he thought of the days that break in beauty only to end in storm. These countryfolk, with their rough good-natured voices and careless manner—what a strange London they beheld! A London without the sin of night or the smoke of day, a wan, spectral city, a barren town of tombs! He wondered what they made of it, whether they knew anything of its splendour and its disgrace, of its vivid, flame-coloured pleasures and its ghastly hunger, of all it creates and destroys from morning until nightfall."}

