          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a74c7349590361f9beca7c4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After supper the whole gang trooped out to the bar to go turtle-egg hunting. They prodded the sand with sticks, and whenever the ground felt loose they dropped to their knees and dug with their hands. Often a single pit yielded fifty or sixty eggs. They were flawless white globes, just a little smaller than an English walnut. That night they devoured a celebrated fried-egg supper, and repeated the feast on Friday morning.\\n\\nOnce breakfast was over, they charged out whooping across the bar, chasing one another round and round and shedding their clothes as they ran until they were bare. Then they carried on their romp far up the shallow bar against the strong current, which every so often swept their legs out from under them and made the game all the more amusing. Now and again they huddled together and slapped water into each other\u2019s faces with their palms, edging closer with turned faces to dodge the choking spray, until they grappled and wrestled, the victor ducking his mate, and the whole knot went under in a whirl of pale arms and legs, rising again all at once, puffing, spluttering, laughing, and gasping for air.\\n\\nWhen fatigue set in, they dashed ashore, sprawled on the dry blazing sand, and buried themselves in it, only to bolt back into the river and repeat the first act over again. At last it dawned on them that their bare hides imitated flesh-colored \u201ctights\u201d well enough, so they traced a ring in the sand and staged a circus\u2014with three clowns in the ring, since none would resign that finest rank to another.\\n\\nAfterward they produced their marbles and played \u201cknucks,\u201d \u201cringtaw,\u201d and \u201ckeeps\u201d until the sport went dull. Joe and Huck plunged in for another dip, but Tom kept out, having discovered that when he kicked off his trousers he had flung his string of rattlesnake rattles from his ankle, and he marveled that he had eluded cramp so long without that mystic amulet. He held back until he found it, and by then the other boys were weary and wanted a rest. Bit by bit they drifted apart, slipped into the \u201cdumps,\u201d and stared wistfully across the broad river toward the village napping in the sun. Tom caught himself tracing \u201cBECKY\u201d in the sand with his big toe; he scratched it away and scolded himself for his frailty. Yet he wrote it anew despite himself; he could not resist. He rubbed it out once more and saved himself by herding the other boys together and joining their company.\"}"
