          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14ebdab48afb43c9cce1134f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, though, the lieutenant inquired why he had ventured so far over the ice in such an unusual conveyance. His face immediately darkened with profound melancholy, and he answered, \u201cTo find one who escaped from me.\u201d \u201cAnd did the man you pursued travel in the same way?\u201d \u201cHe did.\u201d \u201cThen likely we have seen him, for the day before we took you aboard we witnessed a span of dogs pulling a sledge, with a man inside, crossing the ice.\u201d This stirred the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked numerous questions about the path the d\u00e6mon, as he named him, had taken. Shortly afterward, when he and I were alone, he said, \u201cI have no doubt roused your curiosity, along with that of these good people; but you are too considerate to press me with questions.\u201d \u201cCertainly; it would indeed be most impertinent and unkind for me to trouble you with any curiosity of mine.\u201d \u201cAnd yet you saved me from a situation both strange and perilous; you compassionately restored me to life.\u201d Soon after, he asked whether I believed the breaking of the ice had destroyed the other sledge. I responded that I could not say with any certainty, for the ice did not give way until near midnight, and the traveller might have reached a safe place before then; but I had no means to judge. From that moment, a fresh vigor animated the stranger\u2019s waning frame. He displayed fierce eagerness to stand upon the deck to look out for the sledge that had appeared before; but I have convinced him to stay in the cabin, for he is far too frail to brave the raw air. I have promised that someone will keep watch on his behalf and notify him at once should any new sight come into view. Such is my journal regarding this singular episode up to the present day. The stranger has slowly recovered his strength yet remains very quiet and seems uncomfortable when anyone besides myself enters his cabin. Nevertheless, his manners are so winning and gentle that the sailors all take an interest in him, even though they have had minimal dealings together. For my own part, I begin to love him like a brother, and his persistent, profound sorrow moves me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble being in happier times, since even in ruin he remains so engaging and amiable. I told you once in a letter, dear Margaret, that I expected no friend upon the open sea; yet I have found a man whom, before grief broke his spirit, I would have been proud to call the brother of my heart.\"}"
