          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"db931afb5fd79041cad62757\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCome on, Tom,\u201d I said. \u201cYou could tell a guy. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I don\u2019t know. But he\u2019s got to have it. All nobles do.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just how he operated. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something, he simply wouldn\u2019t. You could keep at him for a week and it wouldn\u2019t matter.\\n\\nHe had all that coat-of-arms stuff settled, so now he moved on to the rest of it, which was to come up with a sorrowful inscription\u2014said Jim had to have one, same as the rest. He came up with a bunch, wrote them on a sheet of paper, and read them out loud:\\n\\n1. Here a captive heart burst.\\n\\n2. Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and friends, wore out his sorrowful life.\\n\\n3. Here a lonely heart broke, and a tired spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity.\\n\\n4. Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, died a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV.\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook as he read, and he nearly lost it. When he was finished he couldn\u2019t decide which of them Jim should scratch on the wall, they were all so good; finally he figured he\u2019d let him scratch every one of them. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch that much stuff onto the logs with a nail, and besides he didn\u2019t know how to make letters; but Tom said he would draw them out for him, and all Jim had to do was follow the lines. Then pretty soon he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, the logs won\u2019t work; dungeons don\u2019t have log walls. We\u2019ve got to carve the inscriptions in a rock. We\u2019ll bring in a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said a rock was worse than the logs; it would take him such a terribly long time to carve them into stone he would never get out. But Tom said I could help him. Then he checked how Jim and I were doing with the pens. It was awful hard work, real slow, and it wouldn\u2019t let my hands heal up from the blisters, and we were hardly making any progress at all; so Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the sad inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
