          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8290e26e9fb143ec5ab61389\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, known as Darnay,\u201d finally stood arraigned. The judges wore feathered hats on the Bench, yet the coarse red cap with its tricolour cockade ruled upon other heads. Surveying Jury and raucous spectators, he could imagine the established order overturned, as though criminals were judging the innocent. The vilest rabble of the city\u2014ever equipped with its share of base, cruel souls\u2014governed the spectacle, voicing noisy comments, cheers, hisses, predictions, and rushes toward the verdict, unchecked. Most of the men carried arms of one kind or another; among the women some wielded knives or daggers, others ate and drank as they watched, many knitted. Among these last sat one woman with spare knitting tucked beneath her arm, working steadily. She occupied the front row beside a man Darnay had not glimpsed since reaching the Barrier, yet instantly identified as Defarge. He noticed her lean once or twice to whisper in his ear, and judged her to be his wife; still, what most struck him was that though the pair had stationed themselves as close to him as possible, they never once turned his way. They appeared to await something with stubborn resolve, fixing their attention on the Jury and nothing else. Beneath the President took his seat Doctor Manette in his customary neat attire. So far as the prisoner could discern, only he and Mr. Lorry among those unconnected with the Tribunal kept their ordinary garments, refusing the coarse Carmagnole dress.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, styled Darnay, faced the public prosecutor\u2019s charge as an emigrant whose life was forfeit to the Republic under the decree outlawing all emigrants upon pain of death. The decree\u2019s date after his return to France mattered not. There he stood, and there stood the decree: seized within France, his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d roared the crowd. \u201cAn enemy of the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang for silence and asked whether the prisoner had not lived many years in England. Certainly he had.\\n\\nWas he then an emigrant? How did he classify himself?\\n\\nNot an emigrant, he hoped, in the spirit and meaning of the law.\\n\\nWhy not, the President inquired.\\n\\nBecause he had voluntarily given up a title and station that repelled him, and had left his country\u2014he submitted, before the present Tribunal\u2019s sense of the word emigrant came into use\u2014to live by his own labour in England rather than feed upon the labour of France\u2019s overburdened people.\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
