          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa6858adc9203722dc227704\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for the information. I like to know everything about my new friends and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray. He\u2019s her latest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, to stop bothering me with her charity requests. I\u2019m sick of them. The woman thinks I have nothing to do but write checks for her ridiculous projects.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, though it won\u2019t make any difference. Philanthropic people completely lose their sense of humanity. That\u2019s their defining trait.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man grunted in approval and rang the bell for his servant. Lord Henry walked through the low arcade into Burlington Street and turned toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s birth. Even though it had been told crudely, it still struck him with the suggestion of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a wild passion. A few mad weeks of happiness cut short by a vicious, treacherous crime. Months of silent agony, then a child born in pain. The mother carried off by death, the boy left to loneliness and the rule of an old, loveless man. Yes, it was an intriguing background. It framed the boy, made him more perfect, in a way. Behind every exquisite thing that exists, there\u2019s something tragic. Worlds have to suffer so that even the humblest flower may bloom. And how charming he had been at dinner the night before, sitting across from him at the club, eyes wide and lips parted in anxious delight, the red candle shades deepening the dawning wonder of his face to a richer rose. Talking to him was like playing a fine violin. He responded to every touch and shiver of the bow. There was something terrifyingly captivating in wielding influence. Nothing else was like it. To project one\u2019s soul into a graceful form and let it rest there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own ideas echoed back with all the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another as if it were a subtle liquid or strange perfume\u2014there was real pleasure in that, perhaps the most satisfying pleasure left to us in an era so limited and vulgar, so crudely sensual in its pleasures and coarse in its aims. He was a remarkable type, this boy he had met by such a curious chance in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or at least he could be shaped into a remarkable type.\"}"
        }
