          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d96e2b8ba33f644d419582a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you talking about Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I am. I never imagined you\u2019d think of anyone else\u2014and I assumed you understood. When we discussed it, I thought it was as clear as it could be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d Emma replied, struggling to stay composed. \u201cEverything you said seemed to point to someone else. I could almost swear you mentioned Mr. Frank Churchill by name. I know you talked about how he protected you from the gypsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Miss Woodhouse, how forgetful you are!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember exactly what I said. I told you I wasn\u2019t surprised by your attachment; that given what he\u2019d done for you, it was completely natural. You agreed, spoke passionately about how grateful you were, and even described what you felt when he stepped forward to save you. That\u2019s burned into my memory.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d Harriet cried, \u201cnow I remember what you\u2019re referring to; but at the time I was thinking of something else entirely. It wasn\u2019t the gypsies or Mr. Frank Churchill. No!\u201d\u2014with a lift of her head\u2014\u201cI was thinking of a far more precious moment\u2014when Mr. Knightley came and asked me to dance after Mr. Elton refused to and no one else was available. That was the kind act, the noble generosity, the moment that made me realize how far above everyone else he was.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d exclaimed Emma. \u201cThis has been the most disastrous, most heartbreaking misunderstanding! What can be done now?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t have encouraged me if you\u2019d understood? At least I can\u2019t be worse off than I would have been if the other man had truly been the one\u2014and now there is a chance\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe stopped for a few seconds. Emma couldn\u2019t speak.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t blame you, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she went on, \u201cfor seeing a big difference between the two, whether for me or anyone. You must think one of them is five hundred million times too good for me compared to the other. Still, I hope that if\u2014however unlikely it may seem\u2014but they were your own words, that stranger matches than between Mr. Frank Churchill and me had happened; so perhaps even something like this has happened before. And if I should be so extraordinarily lucky\u2014if Mr. Knightley actually\u2014if he doesn\u2019t care about the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, that you won\u2019t oppose it or try to create obstacles.\u201d\"}"
