          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6c646ea673f261591b8288a7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I bored a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted in a plug of soft wood carved into a tapering cone. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out as needed until, after a few trials, I hit the exact tightness where the water seeping through the hole and dripping into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in exactly sixty minutes. That was easily determined by watching how much of the pitcher filled in a given time. With that settled, the rest of the plan was obvious. I set up my bed on the car floor so that when I lay down my head was directly beneath the pitcher\u2019s mouth. It was clear that after an hour the pitcher, once full, would overflow at the mouth, which sat a bit lower than the rim. It was equally clear that the water falling from more than four feet up couldn\u2019t help but land on my face, and that would instantly wake me up, even from the deepest sleep imaginable.\\n\\n\u201cIt was already eleven by the time I finished these preparations, and I went straight to bed, confident my invention would work. I wasn\u2019t disappointed. Every sixty minutes on the dot my trusty timekeeper roused me; then, after emptying the pitcher back into the keg\u2019s bung-hole and tending to the condenser, I lay down again. These regular interruptions to my sleep bothered me even less than I had expected, and when I finally got up for the day it was seven o\u2019clock, with the sun already many degrees above my horizon.\\n\\n\u201cApril 3rd. I found the balloon at an enormous altitude, and the earth\u2019s apparent curvature had grown noticeably. Below me, out in the ocean, lay a cluster of black specks that were undoubtedly islands. Far to the north I saw a thin, brilliant white line along the edge of the horizon, which I had no doubt was the southern face of the Polar Sea ice. My curiosity soared, because I hoped to continue much farther north and might eventually find