          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82ecf0040293883244afbfaa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But it is truly remarkable that she always hears Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so clearly! Still, she will find her grandmother no deafer than she was two years ago\u2014which is saying a lot at my mother\u2019s age\u2014and it really has been a full two years, you know, since she was here. We have never gone so long without seeing her before, and as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we will hardly know how to make enough fuss over her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you expecting Miss Fairfax soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally! That must be a great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you; you are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised, and everyone says the same kind things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends in Highbury as they will be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell will want the carriage himself one of those days. So good of them to send her the whole way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is why she wrote outside the usual schedule, as we call it; because ordinarily we would not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, so I thought. I feared there was little chance of hearing anything about Miss Fairfax today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo obliging of you! No, we would not have heard, except for this particular circumstance of her coming so soon. My mother is thrilled\u2014she is to stay with us for at least three months. Three months, she says positively, as I am about to have the pleasure of reading to you. The situation is, you see, that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon persuaded her parents to come and see her immediately. They had not planned to go until summer, but she is so eager to see them again\u2014for until she married last October, she had never been away from them for more than a week, which must make it very strange to be in different kingdoms\u2014I was going to say, but anyway different countries\u2014and so she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I truly do not know which, but we shall see soon in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014wrote in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own to urge them to come immediately. They were going to meet them in Dublin and bring them back to their estate, Bally-craig, a beautiful place, I imagine.\"}"
