          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ab4d8b212c67c8e17df8faa5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, though, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far over the ice in such a strange vehicle. The man\u2019s expression at once sank into deepest gloom, and he answered, \u201cTo chase one who ran from me.\u201d \u201cAnd did the man you chased travel the same way?\u201d \u201cYes.\u201d \u201cThen I think we saw him, for the day before we found you we saw dogs pulling a sledge with a man in it across the ice.\u201d This roused the stranger, and he asked countless questions about the path the demon, as he called him, had taken. Soon after, when we were alone, he said, \u201cI have surely stirred your curiosity, as well as that of these kind people; but you are too considerate to ask.\u201d \u201cOf course; it would be very rude and cruel of me to press you with questions.\u201d \u201cAnd yet you saved me from a strange and dangerous situation; you have kindly brought me back to life.\u201d Soon after, he asked whether I thought the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I replied I could not say with any certainty, since the ice did not break up until near midnight, and the traveler might have reached safety before then; but I could not judge. From then on a new energy seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed great eagerness to be on deck to watch for the sledge that had passed before; but I persuaded him to stay in the cabin, for he was far too weak to endure the raw air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and alert him at once if any new object appeared. Such is my journal of this strange event up to now. The stranger has slowly regained health but remains very quiet and grows uneasy when anyone but me enters his cabin. Yet his manners are so agreeable and gentle that all the sailors take an interest in him, though they have spoken with him very little. For my part, I begin to love him like a brother, and his constant, deep sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble being in his better days, since even now, in ruin, he is still so appealing and kind. I once wrote to you, dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the vast ocean; yet I have found a man who, before misery broke his spirit, I would have been proud to call the brother of my heart.\"}"
