          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c23c012c8c7f3245f3336243\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMen marry because they are weary; women marry out of curiosity. Both end up disillusioned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI doubt I shall ever marry, Harry. I am far too in love. That is one of your own little sayings, and I am putting it into practice, as I do everything you advise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd who is it you adore?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a moment.\\n\\n\u201cAn actress,\u201d Dorian Gray answered, colouring.\\n\\nLord Henry lifted his shoulders. \u201cThat is an exceedingly ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would never call it ordinary if you saw her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is her name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever heard the name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has. Yet the day will come when everyone will. She is a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women belong to the decorative sex. They never have anything to say, though they manage to say it delightfully. Women stand for matter triumphant over mind, just as men stand for mind prevailing over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you talk like that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it is simply true. I am dissecting women just now, so I should know. The subject is far less complex than I supposed. In the end, women divide into two groups, the plain and the painted. Plain women are extremely serviceable: if you want to acquire a reputation for propriety, just take them down to supper. The other women are exceptionally delightful. They fall into one error, though, painting themselves to seem youthful. Our grandmothers painted so that they might speak brilliantly. Rouge and esprit once went hand in hand. That has finished. So long as a woman can appear ten years younger than her own daughter, she feels entirely content. As for talk, there are not more than five women in London worth listening to, and two of those can\u2019t be received in respectable circles. But tell me about this prodigy of yours. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! Harry, your opinions make me shudder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you encounter her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall tell you, Harry, only you must not be unkind over it. After all, none of it would have occurred if I had not met you. You infected me with a fierce craving to learn every secret of life. For days after our first meeting, something seemed to pulse through my veins. As I lounged in the park or sauntered along Piccadilly, I would look at everyone who passed me and wonder, with a crazed curiosity, what manner of existence they led. Some enthralled me. Others filled me with dread. The very air seemed laden with some exquisite poison. I longed for sensations\u2026\u201d\"}"
