          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c6bf33ed5ff816086b1a21d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months slid by, and it was deep into winter now. I\u2019d been at school almost steady and could spell and read and write a little, and knew the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five, and I don\u2019t reckon I could ever go any further even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t set much store by arithmetic, anyhow.\\n\\nAt the start I despised the school, but after a spell I found I could put up with it. When I got mighty tired I\u2019d play hookey, and the whipping I\u2019d catch the next day seemed to do me good and hearten me. So the longer I attended school the easier it grew. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they weren\u2019t so sharp on me. Staying in a house and sleeping in a bed mostly weighed on me, but before the cold weather I used to slip out and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that rested me. I liked the old ways best, yet I was getting so I kind of liked the new ones a little bit. The widow said I was coming along slow but sure, and doing very satisfactory. She claimed she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning I happened to upset the saltcellar at breakfast. I reached for some as quick as I could to toss over my left shoulder and head off bad luck, but Miss Watson was ahead of me and brushed me aside. She says, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you\u2019re always making!\u201d The widow spoke a good word for me, but that wasn\u2019t going to fend off the bad luck, I knew that well enough. I went out after breakfast feeling anxious and shaky, wondering where it would hit me and what it would be. There are ways to dodge some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of them, so I didn\u2019t try anything, just prowled along down in the mouth and on the lookout.\\n\\nI headed down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you pass through the tall board fence. A new inch of snow lay on the ground, and I spotted somebody\u2019s tracks. They\u2019d come up from the quarry, lingered around the stile a while, then went on circling the garden fence. It was strange they hadn\u2019t come in after standing around so. I couldn\u2019t figure it. It seemed mighty peculiar. I was fixing to follow the trail, but I bent down to study the tracks first.\"}"
        }
      ],
