{"request_id":"a7636cab3c066d8c2bf0915d","rewritten_text":"I began reasoning from the effect back to the cause. The killers must have escaped through one of these windows. If that was true, they couldn’t have refastened the sashes from inside as the police found them, which is why the officers stopped looking in this direction. Yet the sashes were fastened. They therefore had to be able to lock themselves. There was no way around that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed window, pulled out the nail with some effort, and tried to raise the sash. It resisted exactly as I expected. So there had to be a hidden spring, and the fact that its existence matched my theory reassured me that my premises were sound, even if the details of the nails still seemed mysterious. After a careful search I found the spring, pressed it, and, satisfied with the discovery, decided not to lift the sash.

I replaced the nail and examined it closely. Someone leaving by this window could have shut it behind them so the spring caught, but they could not have put the nail back. The conclusion was obvious, and again narrowed my investigation. The killers had to escape through the other window. Assuming the mechanisms on both sashes were identical, which was likely, there had to be a difference between the nails, or at least in how they were fixed. I climbed onto the bed’s sacking and peered closely at the second window. Sliding my hand behind the headboard, I quickly found and pressed the spring, which matched the first exactly. Then I inspected the nail. It was as sturdy as the other and seemed to be set the same way, driven nearly to the head.

You might say I was baffled, but if you think that, you’ve misunderstood my deductions. To borrow a hunting metaphor, I had never been “off the scent.” The trail had not disappeared for a moment. There wasn’t a weak link in the chain. I had followed the mystery to its final point, and that point was the nail. It looked exactly like its twin in the other window, but that fact meant nothing when compared to the realization that my entire clue ended here. “There must be something wrong,” I said to myself, “with the nail.” I touched it, and the head, along with about a quarter inch of the shank, broke off in my fingers."}

