          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1685a58580d4db1536133d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She was right about the rats; every now and then you\u2019d see one stick his nose out of a hole in the corner. She said she had to keep things close at hand to throw at them when she was alone, or they\u2019d never let her rest. She showed me a bar of lead twisted into a knot, saying she was usually a good shot with it, but she\u2019d wrenched her arm a day or two back and wasn\u2019t sure she could throw straight now. Still, she watched for a chance, and right away she threw at a rat, missed him by a mile, and cried \u201cOuch!\u201d because it hurt her arm so badly. Then she told me to try for the next one. I wanted to leave before the old man got back, but of course I didn\u2019t show it. I took the thing, and the first rat that showed his nose I let fly, and if he\u2019d stayed where he was he\u2019d have been a very sick rat. She said that was first-rate and figured I\u2019d get the next one. She fetched the lump of lead and brought back a hank of yarn she wanted help with. I held up both hands while she slipped the hank over them and went on talking about her own and her husband\u2019s affairs. But she broke off long enough to say:\\n\\n\u201cKeep your eye on the rats. You\u2019d better have the lead in your lap, ready.\u201d\\n\\nSo she dropped the lump into my lap right then, and I clamped my legs over it while she kept talking. But only for a minute. Then she took off the hank, looked me straight in the face, very pleasantly, and said:\\n\\n\u201cCome on now, what\u2019s your real name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWh\u2014what, ma\u2019am?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s your real name? Is it Bill, or Tom, or Bob?\u2014or what is it?\u201d\\n\\nI guess I shook like a leaf and hardly knew what to do. But I said:\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t make fun of a poor girl like me, ma\u2019am. If I\u2019m in the way here, I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, you won\u2019t. Sit down and stay right where you are. I\u2019m not going to hurt you, and I\u2019m not going to tell on you either. Just tell me your secret and trust me. I\u2019ll keep it, and what\u2019s more, I\u2019ll help you. My old man will too if you want him to. You see, you\u2019re a runaway apprentice, that\u2019s all. That\u2019s nothing. There\u2019s no harm in it. You\u2019ve been treated badly and decided to run. Bless you, child, I wouldn\u2019t tell on you.\u201d\"}"
