          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"394572b453e9146edaa2aeab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire was a man who, for his own amusement, never opened any book except the Baronetage. There he found occupation for an idle hour and comfort when troubled, there his mind was stirred to admiration and respect by studying the dwindling traces of the earliest patents, there any unpleasant feelings caused by domestic matters turned naturally into pity and contempt as he flipped through the almost endless creations of the last century, and there, if every other page failed him, he could read his own history with an interest that never faded. This was the page his favorite volume always fell open to:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq., of South Park in the county of Gloucester; by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a stillborn son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThis was exactly how the paragraph had first left the printer\u2019s hands, but Sir Walter had improved it for his own and his family\u2019s information by adding, after Mary\u2019s birth date, the words, \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq., of Uppercross in the county of Somerset,\u201d and by inserting, with great precision, the day of the month on which he lost his wife.\\n\\nAfterward came the history and rise of the ancient, respectable family, in the usual terms: how it first settled in Cheshire, how it was mentioned in Dugdale, how it served as high sheriff, represented a borough in three consecutive parliaments, displayed loyal zeal, and obtained the dignity of baronet in the first year of Charles II, along with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. Altogether it filled two handsome duodecimo pages and ended with the arms and motto\u2014\u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset\u201d\u2014with Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting again in the finale:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great-grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity about his looks and his position. He had been remarkably handsome in his youth, and at fifty-four he was still a very fine man. Few women thought more about their own appearance than he did, and the valet of any newly made lord could not be more pleased with his social standing. He considered the blessing of beauty second only to the blessing of a baronetcy, and the Sir Walter Elliot who possessed both gifts was constantly the object of his warmest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and his rank did have one honest claim on his affection, since thanks to them he had secured a wife of far better character than he himself could ever have deserved.\"}"
        }
