          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"de83375722ed1199233dd969\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped quietly out of the wainscoting, an evil smile on his cruel, wrinkled mouth, and the moon withdrew behind a cloud as he crept past the great oriel window where his own arms and those of his murdered wife gleamed in azure and gold. On he slid like a dark shadow, the very night seeming to hate him as he went. Once he thought he heard someone call and stopped, but it was only a dog baying from the Red Farm, so he continued, muttering strange sixteenth-century curses and now and then waving his rusty dagger in the midnight air. At last he reached the corner of the passage leading to luckless Washington\u2019s room. For a moment he stood there, the wind blowing his long gray hair around his head and twisting the unnamed horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud into grotesque folds. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he knew the moment had come. He chuckled and turned the corner, but immediately gave a pitiful wail, stumbled backward, and covered his pale face with his long bony hands. Right before him stood a horrible specter, still as a carved statue and monstrous as a madman\u2019s dream. Its head was bald and polished, its face round, fat, and white, and hideous laughter had twisted its features into an eternal grin. Scarlet rays shot from its eyes, the mouth was a wide pit of fire, and a hideous garment like his own wrapped its monumental body in silent snow. On its chest hung a placard with strange writing in antique letters, some scroll of shame, some record of wild sins, some dreadful calendar of crime, and in its right hand it held high a gleaming steel falchion.\\n\\nHaving never seen another ghost before, he was naturally terrified, and after a quick second glance at the awful phantom, he fled back to his room, tripping over his long winding-sheet as he raced down the corridor and dropping the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jack-boots, where the butler found it in the morning. Once in his own apartment he threw himself on a small pallet-bed and buried his face under the covers. After a time, though, the brave old Canterville spirit reasserted itself, and he resolved to speak to the other ghost as soon as daylight came. So, just as dawn touched the hills with silver, he returned to the spot where he had first seen the grisly phantom, thinking that two ghosts were better than one and that, with the help of his new ally, he might safely deal with the twins.\"}"
