          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e617f0fa2ae248da16614d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYeah, it is\u2014and I could\u2019ve had it if I\u2019d been big enough; I saw him first. Who caught him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt was some old guy\u2014a stranger\u2014and he sold his claim on him for forty dollars because he has to head up the river and can\u2019t stick around. Imagine that! You better believe I\u2019d wait, even if it took seven years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s me, every time,\u201d I said. \u201cBut maybe his claim isn\u2019t worth more than that if he\u2019s willing to sell it so cheap. Maybe something\u2019s not right about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it is\u2014straight as a string. I saw the handbill myself. It tells everything about him, down to the last detail\u2014describes him like a picture, and even names the plantation he\u2019s from, down below New Orleans. No sir, there\u2019s no trouble with that deal, you can bet on it. Hey, give me a chew of tobacco, will you?\u201d\\n\\nI didn\u2019t have any, so he left. I went to the raft and sat in the wigwam to think. But I couldn\u2019t figure anything out. I thought until my head was sore, but I couldn\u2019t see any way out of the mess. After this whole long trip, after everything we\u2019d done for those scoundrels, it had all come to nothing, everything smashed and ruined, because they had the heart to play a trick like that on Jim and make him a slave again, for life, among strangers, for forty filthy dollars.\\n\\nOnce I told myself it would be a thousand times better for Jim to be a slave at home where his family was, if he had to be a slave, so I might as well write a letter to Tom Sawyer and ask him to tell Miss Watson where Jim was. But I dropped that idea for two reasons: she\u2019d be furious and disgusted at his running off and being ungrateful, and she\u2019d sell him straight down the river again; and if she didn\u2019t, everybody naturally despises an ungrateful black man, and they\u2019d make Jim feel it all the time, so he\u2019d feel low and disgraced. And then think of me! Word would get around that Huck Finn helped a black man get free, and if I ever met anyone from that town again I\u2019d be ready to drop down and lick his boots from shame. That\u2019s how it is: a person does a lowdown thing and then doesn\u2019t want to face the consequences. Thinks that as long as he can hide it, it\u2019s no disgrace. That was exactly my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience gnawed at me, and the more wicked, low, and worthless I felt.\"}"
        }
