          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7afde4905d180757be092d6e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired escort went riding in Brockley Meadows, where she ripped her riding skirt badly squeezing through a hedge. When she returned home, she decided to slip up the back stairs so no one would see her. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door happened to be open, she thought she saw someone inside, and assuming it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her sewing there, she looked in to ask for help mending her skirt. To her huge surprise, it was the Canterville Ghost himself! He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees flying through the air, and the red leaves spinning wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, his whole posture radiating deep despair. He looked so wretched, so worn out, that Virginia\u2014who at first thought of running away and locking herself in her room\u2014felt overwhelmed with compassion and decided to try to comfort him. Her tread was so light, and his sorrow so deep, that he didn\u2019t even notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m so sorry for you,\u201d she said, \u201cbut my brothers are going back to Eton tomorrow, and then, if you behave yourself, no one will bother you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s ridiculous to ask me to behave,\u201d he answered, turning in astonishment to the pretty little girl who had dared to address him. \u201cUtterly ridiculous. I have to rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and roam around at night, if that\u2019s what you mean. It\u2019s my only reason for existing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s no reason at all for existing, and you know you\u2019ve been terribly wicked. Mrs. Umney told us the first day we got here that you killed your wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I freely admit it,\u201d the Ghost replied peevishly, \u201cbut it was purely a family affair and nobody else\u2019s concern.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s terribly wrong to kill anyone,\u201d said Virginia, whose voice sometimes held a gentle Puritan solemnity inherited from some old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u201cOh, I can\u2019t stand the cheap severity of abstract morals! My wife was very plain, never starched my ruffs properly, and had no idea how to cook. Why, there was a stag I shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket\u2014and do you know how she sent it up to the table? Still, it doesn\u2019t matter now, because it\u2019s all over, and I don\u2019t think it was very nice of her brothers to starve me to death, even if I did kill her.\u201d\"}"
