          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6be3e977aed83eb32b65ddb8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now examined the skull drawing carefully. Its outer edges\u2014the parts closest to the edge of the parchment\u2014were much sharper than the rest. It was clear the heat had worked unevenly. I immediately lit a fire and held every portion of the parchment to the intense heat. At first, the only result was that the faint lines of the skull grew darker; but when I kept at it, a figure appeared at the corner of the slip, diagonally opposite the skull. At first I thought it was a goat. After looking more closely, though, I realized it was meant to be a kid.\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d I said. \u201cGranted, I have no business laughing\u2014a million and a half is too serious for jokes\u2014but you\u2019re not going to build a third link in your chain. You won\u2019t find any special connection between pirates and goats. Pirates have nothing to do with goats; they belong to the farming crowd.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I just told you the figure wasn\u2019t a goat.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, a kid\u2014almost the same thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAlmost, but not entirely,\u201d Legrand replied. \u201cYou\u2019ve probably heard of Captain Kidd. I immediately saw the animal figure as a kind of punning or hieroglyphic signature. I call it a signature because its position on the parchment gave me that idea. The skull at the opposite corner had the look of a stamp or seal. But I was badly bothered by what was missing\u2014the body of the instrument I imagined, the text that would match my context.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assume you expected a letter between the stamp and the signature.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSomething like that. The truth is, I couldn\u2019t shake the feeling that some great fortune was coming. I can hardly explain why. Maybe it was more desire than belief. But you know, Jupiter\u2019s foolish claim that the bug was solid gold really fired my imagination. And then the string of accidents and coincidences\u2014those were so extraordinary. Do you see how mere chance made these things happen on the one day of the year cool enough for a fire? And without that fire, or without the dog showing up at just that moment, I would never have discovered the skull, and never become the owner of the treasure.\u201d\"}"
