          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a7ebcf66e4b5d8420f3259bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They rode past deserted brickfields. The fog thinned there, and he could make out the odd bottle-shaped kilns with orange tongues of fire fanning out. A dog barked as they passed, and somewhere far off a stray sea-gull screamed in the dark. The horse stumbled in a rut, then swerved and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled again over rough, paved streets. Most windows were dark, but here and there wild shadows showed against a blind lit from behind. He watched them with interest. They moved like giant puppets and gestured as if alive, and he hated them. A dull rage filled his heart. When they turned a corner, a woman shouted something at them from an open door, and two men ran after the hansom for nearly a hundred yards. The driver lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nPeople say passion makes the mind move in circles. Certainly, Dorian Gray\u2019s bitten lips silently repeated those subtle words about soul and sense until he felt they gave complete voice to his mood, granting intellectual approval to passions that would have ruled him even without it. The same thought crept from one corner of his mind to another, and the fierce desire to live, the most terrible of man\u2019s hungers, sent energy racing through every shaking nerve and fiber. Ugliness that once repelled him for its harsh reality became precious to him now for that very truth. Ugliness was the lone reality. Coarse fights, filthy dens, the brutal chaos of disordered life, the sheer vileness of thieves and exiles, struck him as more vivid, in their raw immediacy, than all the graceful forms of art or the dreamy echoes of music. They were what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the man halted with a jerk at the top of a dark lane. Beyond the low roofs and jagged chimney-stacks, the black masts of ships rose up. White mist curled like ghostly sails around 