          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4ea3768709c1ed97e07a5b14\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm roared furiously throughout the night, yet nothing noteworthy transpired. At breakfast next morning they discovered the dreadful bloodstain once more on the floor. \u201cIt cannot be the fault of Paragon Detergent,\u201d Washington declared. \u201cI have used it with every adjunct imaginable. It must be the ghost.\u201d He erased the stain again, only for it to reappear on the second morning. On the third morning it was present yet again, although Mr. Otis had himself locked the library overnight and carried the key upstairs. Now the whole household grew genuinely absorbed; Mr. Otis suspected he had been overly dogmatic in denying ghosts, Mrs. Otis announced she would join the Psychical Society, and Washington drafted a lengthy letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore about the Persistence of Sanguineous Stains in connection with Crime. That night all hesitation about the objective reality of phantasmata was finally dispelled.\\n\\nThe day had been warm and bright, and in the cool evening the entire family went driving. They did not return until nine, when they ate a light supper. Their talk never once touched on spirits, so even those preliminary conditions of receptive anticipation that often prelude psychical phenomena were absent. According to Mr. Otis, their subjects were only those common in the conversation of cultivated Americans of the higher type: the incomparable superiority of Miss Fanny Davenport over Sarah Bernhardt on the stage; the difficulty of procuring green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy even in the finest English homes; Boston\u2019s role in shaping the world-soul; the merits of the baggage-check system in railway travel; and the sweetness of the New York accent compared with the London drawl. No reference at all was made to anything supernatural, nor was Sir Simon de Canterville mentioned. At eleven the family retired, and by half past the house lay dark. Some time later Mr. Otis was roused by an odd sound in the corridor outside his room, a clanking as of metal that drew closer every moment. He was immediately on his feet, struck a match, and checked the time: exactly one o\u2019clock. He remained perfectly composed and took his pulse, which was entirely normal. The peculiar noise continued, and together with it he clearly heard footsteps. He slipped into his slippers, removed a small oblong vial from his dressing-case, and opened the door.\"}"
