          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c40ec1c4d47e5b5614addffe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJupiter,\u201d he said as we reached the base, \u201ccome here! Was the skull nailed to the branch with the face outward or pressed against the wood?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe face was outward, massa, so the crows could get at the eyes easily, without trouble.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen was it this eye or that one you dropped the beetle through?\u201d Legrand tapped each of Jupiter\u2019s eyes as he spoke.\\n\\n\u201cIt was this eye, massa\u2014the left eye\u2014just like you told me,\u201d he answered, indicating his right eye.\\n\\n\u201cThat settles it\u2014we have to try again.\u201d\\n\\nNow my friend, whose madness I was beginning to see, or thought I saw, showed signs of having a method. He moved the peg marking where the beetle had fallen about three inches west of its original spot. Then, measuring from the trunk to the new peg and extending the line fifty feet straight ahead as before, he pointed out a place several yards from where we had been digging.\\n\\nWe drew a circle around this new position, larger than the previous one, and started digging again with the spades. I was exhausted, yet without quite knowing why, my feelings changed; I no longer felt much aversion to the work. I had become inexplicably fascinated\u2014no, even excited. Perhaps something in Legrand\u2019s wild behavior\u2014a hint of planning or deliberation\u2014impressed me. I dug with enthusiasm, and every now and then I caught myself actually looking with something like expectation for the imagined treasure that had driven my unfortunate friend mad. At the height of these wandering thoughts, after we had worked for about ninety minutes, we were interrupted again by the dog\u2019s fierce howling. At first his restlessness had been clearly playful or impulsive, but now it took on a harsh, serious tone. When Jupiter tried to muzzle him again, the dog fought furiously, leaped into the hole, and clawed wildly at the dirt. In moments he uncovered a pile of human bones\u2014two complete skeletons\u2014mixed with several metal buttons and what looked like decayed wool dust. A few blows of a spade unearthed the blade of a large Spanish knife, and as we dug deeper, three or four loose gold and silver coins appeared.\"}"
