          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0979034977e8bd4eecce25b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe and the children might remain quite comfortably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! papa\u2014that is precisely what you have never managed, nor do I imagine you ever will. Isabella is never happy apart from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThe fact was too clear for debate. Distasteful as it proved, Mr. Woodhouse could only let out a resigned sigh; and seeing his spirits damped by the notion of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband, Emma quickly shifted to a topic certain to raise them.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must allow us as much of her company as possible while my brother and sister are here. I know she will like the children. We are exceedingly proud of the children, are we not, papa? I wonder which she will find the handsomest, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAye, I wonder which indeed. Poor little souls, how delighted they will be to come. They love being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they do, sir. Truly, I know not who does not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John is the very picture of his mama. Henry is the eldest, named after me, not his father. John, the second, takes his father\u2019s name. Some folks wonder, I believe, that the eldest was not called after his father; yet Isabella insisted on Henry, and I thought it exceedingly lovely of her. And he is remarkably clever. They are all very clever, and have so many charming ways. They will come and plant themselves by my chair, and say, \u2018Grandpapa, might we have a length of string?\u2019 and once Henry asked for a knife, but I told him knives were made only for grandpapas. I think their father is often too rough with them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are yourself so extremely gentle; but could you compare him with other fathers, you would not call him rough. He desires his boys to be lively and hardy; and if they misbehave, he can give a sharp word now and then; yet he is a tender father\u2014Mr. John Knightley is certainly an affectionate father. The children all adore him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and flings them up to the ceiling in a most alarming manner!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they enjoy it, papa; nothing pleases them more. It is such delight that, if their uncle did not enforce turns, whichever child began would never yield to the other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, I cannot comprehend it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is how it is with all of us, papa. Half the world cannot comprehend the pleasures of the other half.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
