          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"399e19277932158cff1dc20c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThen hear me! You there!\u201d Monks answered. \u201cHis father, stricken with illness in Rome, was joined by his wife\u2014my mother\u2014whom he had long been apart from. She traveled from Paris, taking me along, to oversee his affairs, I suppose, for neither she nor he in truth cared much for each other. He lay unaware of us, his senses gone, and slept until the following day, when he died. In his desk were two papers, written the evening his sickness began, addressed to you,\u201d he said to Mr. Brownlow, \u201cwrapped in a few short lines, with a note on the cover stating they were not to be sent till after his death. One was a letter to that girl Agnes; the other, a will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat of the letter?\u201d Mr. Brownlow asked.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter? A sheet covered, crossed and re-crossed, full of repentant confession and pleads to God for aid. He had fooled the girl with a tale of some secret\u2014one day to be revealed\u2014that hindered their marriage for the time; and she waited, patient and trusting, until her trust carried her too far and she lost what couldn\u2019t be restored. She was then only months from her confinement. He detailed what measures he had intended, had he lived, to shield her disgrace, and begged her, should he die, not to curse his memory or imagine the weight of their sin would fall on her or their child; the blame, he said, was solely his. He recalled the day he gave her the small locket and the ring bearing her Christian name, leaving the other side blank for the name he hoped to share with her one day\u2014beseeched her to keep it still, wearing it next her heart as before\u2014and wandered on, wildly repeating the same words again and again, as if driven mad. I believe he was.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow prompted while Oliver\u2019s tears dropped fast.\\n\\nMonks made no reply.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cmatched the tone of the letter. He spoke of misery his wife had laid upon him; of the rebellious disposition, vice, malice, and untimely evil passions of you, his only son, schooled to despise him; and he left to you and your mother annuities of eight hundred pounds each. The greater share of his estate he divided into two halves\u2014one portion for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, should it be born alive and reach maturity.\u201d\"}"
        }
