          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6108cf13b9edcdb8a2a47a1f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs Clay\u2019s affections got the better of her prudence, and for the young gentleman\u2019s sake she relinquished any further plotting for Sir Walter. Yet she possesses ability as well as feeling; and it remains uncertain whether his craft or hers will ultimately prevail, whether, after he has hindered her from becoming Sir Walter\u2019s wife, he might not, after all the coaxing and fond attention, be brought to make her Lady William instead.\\n\\nNo one could doubt that Sir Walter and Elizabeth were shocked and mortified at losing their companion and having her duplicity exposed. They could, of course, retreat to their grand cousins for solace; but for a long time they must taste how incomplete the pleasure is when one flatters and trails after others without being flattered and followed in return.\\n\\nAnne, having soon perceived Lady Russell\u2019s intention of loving Captain Wentworth as she ought, had only one alloy upon the happiness of her hopes: the awareness that she had no relations to present to him whom a sensible man could prize. In that respect she felt her inferiority acutely. The disparity in fortune mattered nothing; it never cost her a regret. But possessing no family to welcome and value him properly, nothing respectable, harmonious, or kindly to offer in exchange for all the merit and hearty reception awaiting her in his brothers and sisters, produced as piercing a pain as her mind could feel amid otherwise high felicity. Only two friends remained for her to add to his circle, Lady Russell and Mrs Smith. Towards both, however, he was very willing to attach himself. Lady Russell, despite all her past offences, he could now sincerely admire. Though he need not declare that she was justified in separating them originally, he was prepared to say nearly every other favourable thing of her. As for Mrs Smith, her claims were manifold and secured a quick, lasting esteem.\\n\\nMrs Smith\u2019s recent kind offices for Anne were merit enough, and their marriage, rather than costing her one friend, secured her two. She became their earliest visitor in their established home; and Captain Wentworth, by guiding her towards the recovery of her husband\u2019s property in the West Indies\u2014writing on her behalf, acting for her, and with all the spirited diligence of a bold man and faithful friend seeing her through every petty complication of the affair\u2014fully repaid the benefits she had bestowed, or intended to bestow, upon his wife.\"}"
