{"request_id":"71dab2011c28e79240dff1eb","rewritten_text":"You know I had stuffed my handkerchief into my helmet—along with some other odds and ends—and it turned out to be one of those helmets you can’t remove alone. That detail hadn’t crossed my mind when I tucked it in, and in truth I didn’t even realize it. I had supposed it would be especially handy there. The mere thought of it being so near and yet unreachable only made the craving worse. Yes, what you are denied is what you are most desperate for; everybody has observed that. So my thoughts left everything else, fixed themselves on that helmet, and mile after mile stayed there, conjuring up my handkerchief, picturing it; the salt sweat kept trickling into my eyes and I couldn’t reach it, which was maddening. On paper it may look trivial, but it certainly was not; it was sheer torment. I wouldn’t say it if it were otherwise. I resolved that next time I would carry a reticule, appearances be hanged and people gossiping as they pleased. Naturally these iron gentlemen of the Round Table would consider it improper, maybe make a racket over it, yet for me comfort comes first and style afterwards. We jogged along, and every so often we hit a dusty stretch that billowed up in clouds, got in my nose, and made me sneeze and cry; and of course I let slip things I shouldn’t have, I admit that. I’m no better than the next man.

We couldn’t seem to encounter a living soul in this desolate Britain, not even an ogre—and given my mood it would have been well for the ogre, provided he carried a handkerchief. Most knights would think only of stripping his armor; but if I got his bandanna he could keep his hardware for all I cared.

Meanwhile it grew hotter and hotter inside. The sun kept beating down, heating the iron more every moment. When you’re that overheated, each small annoyance grates on you. When I trotted I clattered like a crate of crockery, which vexed me; besides, I couldn’t bear that shield slatting and banging, now against my breast, now around my back; yet if I dropped to a walk my joints creaked and shrieked in that tedious wheelbarrow fashion, and since we generated no breeze at that speed, I was near roasting in that oven; and moreover, the slower you went the more the iron settled on you, and the heavier you felt with each passing minute."}

