{"request_id":"775e58f2e3734ec1115d83c5","rewritten_text":"The stars still gleamed brilliantly when I woke, and Taurus poised high in the southern sky proclaimed midnight. I roused from troubled dreams. It seemed I had been bidden to Timon’s final banquet; I arrived ravenous, the dishes were uncovered, the hot water sent up its disappointing vapors, and I fled the wrath of the host, who took on Raymond’s likeness; to my sickened imagination, the vessels he flung after me brimmed with foul exhalations, and my friend’s figure, warped by countless distortions, swelled into a colossal specter, bearing the seal of pestilence upon its brow. The mounting shadow climbed higher and higher, filling, then striving to burst past the adamant dome that arched above, supporting and enclosing the world. The incubus became torment; with a desperate effort I cast off sleep and restored reason to her customary post. My first thought was of Perdita; to her I must return, her I must uphold, drawing sustenance from despair itself to strengthen her wounded spirit; guiding her back from grief’s wild extremes by the austere code of duty and the gentle solace of remorse.

The stars’ alignment served as my sole compass. I turned from the dreadful wreck of the Golden City and, after great labor, contrived to free myself from its confines. Outside the walls I encountered a troop of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one and sped toward my sister. The look of the plain had altered in this brief span; the camp was dismantled; remnants of the disbanded army gathered in scattered knots; every visage was overcast; each motion proclaimed bewilderment and dread.

With a heavy spirit I entered the palace, fearing to advance, to speak, even to gaze. In the hall’s center sat Perdita; she rested upon the marble floor, her head bowed upon her breast, hair disordered, fingers intertwining ceaselessly; she was pale as the stone beneath, each feature drawn tight with anguish. She noticed me and lifted her eyes in inquiry; that flicker of hope was torment; words failed before I could form them; I felt a ghastly grin crease my mouth. She read my sign; again her head drooped, again her fingers writhed. At last speech returned, yet my tone frightened her; the poor girl had comprehended my look, and for all the world she would not have her dire sorrow shaped and sealed by stark, irrevocable words. Indeed, she seemed bent on turning my thoughts elsewhere: she rose from the ground. “Hush!” she whispered; “after much weeping, Clara sleeps; we must not disturb her.”"}

