          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b47fe9b6f3822b3fe07b75a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t be sure whether the voice belonged to a man or a woman. He couldn\u2019t make out the words, but believed the language was Spanish. He described the condition of the room and the bodies just as we did yesterday.\\n\\n\u201cHenri Duval, a neighbor and a silversmith by trade, testifies that he was among the first to enter the house. He confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s account in general. As soon as they broke in, they shut the door again to keep out the crowd, which grew quickly despite the late hour. He thinks the sharp voice belonged to an Italian. He was sure it wasn\u2019t French. He couldn\u2019t tell if it was a man\u2019s voice. It might have been a woman\u2019s. He didn\u2019t know Italian. He couldn\u2019t make out the words, but was convinced from the tone that the speaker was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter and had often spoken with both. He was certain the sharp voice wasn\u2019t either of the deceased.\\n\\n\u201cOdenheimer, a restaurateur, gave his testimony voluntarily. Since he didn\u2019t speak French, he was examined through an interpreter. He\u2019s a native of Amsterdam. He was passing the house at the time of the screams. They lasted several minutes\u2014probably ten. They were long, loud, and terribly distressing. He was one of those who entered the building. He confirmed the earlier evidence in every respect but one. He was sure the sharp voice belonged to a man\u2014a Frenchman. He couldn\u2019t make out the words. They were loud and rapid, uneven, spoken in fear as well as anger. The voice was harsh, rather than shrill. He wouldn\u2019t call it shrill. The gruff voice kept repeating \u201csacr\u00e9,\u201d \u201cdiable,\u201d and once \u201cmon Dieu.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJules Mignaud, banker of the firm Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She opened an account with his bank in the spring of the year\u2014eight years earlier. She made frequent deposits in small amounts. She hadn\u2019t drawn anything until three days before her death, when she personally withdrew 4000 francs. This sum was paid in gold, and a clerk escorted her home with the money.\\n\\n\u201cAdolphe Le Bon, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, testifies that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence with the 4000 francs, divided into two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from his hands, while the elderly lady relieved him of the other.\u201d\"}"
