          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"812d4b5f4247b240af312d03\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo, no, that is for to-morrow; and I particularly wished to see Knightley today for that very reason.\u2014What an oppressively broiling morning!\u2014I crossed the fields as well\u2014\u201d (in a tone of profound ill-usage), \u201cwhich only made matters worse. And then he was not at home! I assure you I am thoroughly displeased. No apology left, no message for me. The housekeeper declared she had no notion I was expected.\u2014Most extraordinary!\u2014No one had any idea where he had gone. Possibly to Hartfield, or to the Abbey Mill, or perhaps into his woods.\u2014Miss Woodhouse, this is not what one expects from our friend Knightley!\u2014Can you account for it?\u201d\\n\\nEmma diverted herself by insisting that it was indeed exceedingly extraordinary, and that she had not a word to offer in his defence.\\n\\n\u201cI cannot conceive,\u201d said Mrs. Elton (suffering the affront as a wife ought), \u201cI cannot conceive how he could behave so to you, of all people! The very last person one could imagine being forgotten!\u2014My dear Mr. E., he must have left a message for you; he surely must.\u2014Even Knightley could not be so capricious;\u2014the servants must have overlooked it. Depend upon that: and it is very likely with the Donwell servants, who, I have often observed, are astonishingly clumsy and negligent.\u2014I would not, for any inducement, allow that fellow Harry to stand at our sideboard. And with respect to Mrs. Hodges, Wright holds her in the poorest estimation.\u2014She promised Wright a recipe and never forwarded it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI met William Larkins,\u201d continued Mr. Elton, \u201cas I approached the house, and he told me I would not find his master within, but I paid him no heed.\u2014William seemed somewhat out of sorts. He did not know what had come over his master lately, he said, but he could scarcely ever get a word with him. I care nothing for William\u2019s complaints, yet it truly is of the highest consequence that *I* see Knightley today; and therefore it is a matter of serious inconvenience that I should have taken this hot walk for nothing.\u201d\\n\\nEmma felt she could do no better than make straight for home. In all likelihood she was expected there at that very moment; and Mr. Knightley might be spared from sinking deeper into offence with Mr. Elton, if not with William Larkins.\\n\\nShe was gratified, in taking leave, to find Miss Fairfax resolved to accompany her out of the room, even down the stairs; it offered her a chance, which she seized without delay, to say,\"}"
