          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"13e12aeed61ab0aa8265f3f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years Dorian Gray could not shake the hold this book had on him\u2014or rather, he never even tried. He ordered no fewer than nine large-paper first editions from Paris and had each one bound in a different color to match his moods and the shifting whims of a nature he sometimes felt he had almost lost control of. The hero, a marvelous young Parisian whose romantic and scientific sides were oddly united, became a kind of preview of himself. Indeed, the whole book seemed to contain the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic hero. He never knew\u2014never even had reason to know\u2014the strange fear of mirrors, polished metal, and still water that seized the young Parisian early on, brought about by the sudden decay of beauty that had once been extraordinary. With almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps cruelty hides in nearly every joy, certainly in every pleasure\u2014he would read the book\u2019s final section, where the sorrow and despair of someone who had lost what he valued most in others and in the world was told in tragic, if slightly exaggerated, detail.\\n\\nFor the magnificent beauty that had captivated Basil Hallward and many others never seemed to leave Dorian. Even those who heard the worst rumors about his way of life\u2014and now and then strange whispers spread through London and became club gossip\u2014could not believe anything dishonorable about him when they saw him. He always looked like someone unstained by the world. Men who spoke crudely fell silent when Dorian Gray entered. The purity of his face seemed to rebuke them. His presence alone made them remember the innocence they had ruined. They wondered how someone so charming and graceful could have avoided the corruption of an age both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, when he returned from those mysterious, lengthy absences that stirred so much speculation among those who were his friends\u2014or thought they were\u2014he would creep upstairs to the locked room, open the door with the key he always kept, and stand with a mirror before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him, looking from the wicked, aging face on the canvas to the fair young face smiling back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
