          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5fd68852bc0b076ffd45b57f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Great God! If I had guessed even for a moment what my fiendish enemy truly intended, I would have exiled myself forever and wandered the world alone rather than consent to this wretched marriage. Yet the monster, as if using magic, hid his real purpose from me; and while I thought I was preparing only for my own death, I hurried the fate of someone far dearer.\\n\\nAs the wedding day drew closer, whether from cowardice or some prophetic feeling, my heart sank. I hid my unease behind a show of cheerfulness that brought smiles to my father but hardly fooled the watchful eyes of Elizabeth. She looked toward our union with calm contentment, tinged with fear born from past misfortunes, that what seemed solid happiness might dissolve into nothing and leave only lasting regret.\\n\\nPlans were made, guests offered congratulations, and everything appeared joyful. I tried to lock the torment inside my heart and joined in my father\u2019s designs, though they might only serve as decorations for my tragedy. Through his efforts, part of Elizabeth\u2019s inheritance had been restored by the Austrian government, including a small estate on the shores of Como. We agreed that immediately after our wedding we would go to Villa Lavenza and spend our first days of happiness beside the beautiful lake.\\n\\nMeanwhile I took every precaution to defend myself if the fiend attacked. I carried pistols and a dagger everywhere and stayed alert against trickery, which gave me a measure of calm. Indeed, as the time approached, the threat seemed like a delusion, unworthy of disturbing my peace, while the happiness I expected in my marriage became more certain as the date was spoken of constantly as something no accident could prevent.\\n\\nElizabeth appeared content; my calm behavior helped steady her. Yet on the day that was to fulfill my wishes and determine my fate, she grew sorrowful, filled with a strong se