          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c866d73ddf060d4b4eb44a0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Arnold and Sir Gauter, the castle\u2019s knights, rode hard against Sir Brandiles and Sir Kay, and those four clashed fiercely, shattering their lances down to the gauntlets. Then Sir Pertolope issued forth, and Sir Lionel met him, but Pertolope, the Green Knight, struck down Sir Lionel, who was Sir Launcelot\u2019s brother. Noble heralds noted all, recording who bore himself best and their names. Sir Bleobaris broke his spear upon Sir Gareth, yet from that blow Bleobaris toppled. Witnessing this, Sir Galihodin bade Sir Gareth defend himself, and Sir Gareth cast him to the ground. Sir Galihud seized a spear for his brother\u2019s revenge, and Gareth served him likewise. Sir Dinadan, his brother La Cote Male Taile, Sir Sagramore le Desirous, and Sir Dodinas le Savage\u2014all those he unhorsed with a single lance. When King Agwisance of Ireland saw Gareth\u2019s feats, he wondered who he might be, for at one moment he seemed green, and upon his return he appeared blue. Thus with each course the knight altered his hue, so that no king or knight could readily recognize him. Then King Agwisance of Ireland ran upon Sir Gareth and Gareth cast him from horse and saddle together. King Carados of Scotland rode next, and Gareth struck him down, horse and rider. He dealt the same to King Uriens of the land of Gore. After that came Sir Bagdemagus, and Gareth sent him crashing to the ground, horse and man. Bagdemagus\u2019s son Meliganus splintered a spear on Sir Gareth mightily and knightly. Then the noble Prince Galahault cried aloud, \u201cKnight of the many colors, thou hast jousted well; now arm thyself to tilt with me.\u201d Gareth heard and seized a great lance, and they met; the prince\u2019s spear shattered, while Gareth smote him on the left of the helm so that he reeled to and fro and would have fallen had his men not caught him. \u201cTruly,\u201d said King Arthur, \u201cthat knight of many colors is a valiant knight.\u201d So the king called Sir Launcelot to ride against him. \u201cSir,\u201d answered Launcelot, \u201cI can as readily find it in my heart to spare him for now, for he hath labored enough today. When a worthy knight excels on some day, it is no good knight\u2019s part to hinder him of his worship, especially when he sees such toil accomplished; for perchance,\u201d said Sir Launcelot, \u201chis cause lies here this day, and perchance this lady loves him best of all that be present, for plainly he strives and exerts himself to perform great deeds. Therefore,\u201d said Sir Launcelot, \u201cthis day he shall keep the honor; though I might deprive him of it, I will not.\u201d\"}"
