          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"185e5cfc834a0f04254004ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so satisfied with their conversation that he never again troubled himself or bothered his dear sister Elizabeth by mentioning it; she was glad to know she had said enough to keep him silent.\\n\\nThe day came for him and Lydia to leave, and Mrs. Bennet had to accept the separation, which, since her husband had no intention of joining her scheme of taking them all to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen will we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Maybe not for two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time for writing. My sisters can write to me. They will have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s farewells were much warmer than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many charming things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, as soon as they were out of the house, \u201cas ever I saw. He simpers, smirks, and makes love to us all. I am extremely proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas to show a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter made Mrs. Bennet very low for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthere is nothing worse than parting with friends. One feels so forlorn without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is what comes, you see, madam, of marrying a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt should make you more content that your other four are single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no such thing. Lydia is not leaving me because she is married, but only because her husband\u2019s regiment is so far away. If it had been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nBut the dull mood this event threw her into was soon relieved, and her spirits stirred again with hopeful excitement, by a piece of news just being spread. The housekeeper at Netherfield had orders to prepare for her master\u2019s arrival; he was coming in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet could hardly sit still. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head by turns.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for Mrs. Philips first brought her the news). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, though. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never want to see him again. But still, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield if he likes.\u201d\"}"
