{"request_id":"f9f1646ef756c3a0b88a8965","rewritten_text":"The next morning at breakfast the Otis family discussed the ghost at length. The United States Minister was understandably annoyed that his gift had been rejected. “I have no wish to harm the ghost personally, and considering how long he has lived here, it’s hardly polite to throw pillows at him,” he said—a perfectly fair remark, though the twins burst into loud laughter. “On the other hand,” he went on, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we’ll have to take his chains away. No one can sleep with that racket outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week they were left in peace, the only notable event being the constant reappearance of the bloodstain in the library. This was certainly peculiar, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and the windows were barred tight. The stain’s changing color caused even more comment. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it would turn vermilion, then a deep purple, and once, when they went down for family prayers according to the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, it gleamed bright emerald green. These shifting colors amused the whole party, and they made bets about it every evening. The only one who didn’t join in was little Virginia, who for reasons no one understood was always distressed by the stain and nearly cried the morning it turned emerald green.

The ghost’s second appearance came on Sunday night. Not long after they’d gone to bed, a tremendous crash in the hall jolted everyone awake. They hurried downstairs to find a large suit of old armor had toppled from its stand onto the stone floor, while the Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense pain. The twins, who had brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him with the precision that only long practice on a writing tutor could perfect, while the United States Minister covered him with his revolver and ordered him, in the Californian fashion, to put his hands up. The ghost sprang to his feet with a furious shriek and swept through them like a mist, snuffing out Washington Otis’s candle as he passed and leaving them in total darkness."}

