          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2dc7e84391a46f9b6ff80795\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well past noon when he finally woke up. His valet had tiptoed into the room several times to check if he was stirring, puzzled by how long his young master was sleeping. At last the bell rang, and Victor came in quietly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters on a small tray of old S\u00e8vres porcelain, then drew back the olive satin curtains with their shimmering blue lining that hung before the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur slept well this morning,\u201d he said with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cWhat time is it, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked groggily.\\n\\n\u201cOne fifteen, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late it was! He sat up, sipped some tea, and began sorting through his letters. One was from Lord Henry, delivered by hand that morning. He hesitated before setting it aside. He opened the others without much interest. They held the usual assortment of calling cards, dinner invitations, tickets to private previews, programs for charity concerts, and similar things that pour in on fashionable young men every morning during the season. There was a rather steep bill for a chased silver Louis XV dressing set that he still hadn\u2019t had the nerve to send to his guardians, who were terribly old-fashioned and couldn\u2019t grasp that we live in an age when unnecessary things are our only necessities; there were also several very polite letters from Jermyn Street moneylenders offering to advance any amount of cash instantly at the most reasonable interest rates.\\n\\nAfter about ten minutes he got up, pulled on an ornate silk-embroidered cashmere dressing gown, and went into the onyx-floored bathroom. The cool water revived him after his long sleep. He seemed to have forgotten everything he had gone through. Now and then a faint sense of having taken part in some strange tragedy came over him, but it felt as unreal as a dream.\\n\\nOnce he was dressed, he went into the library and sat down to the light French breakfast set out on a small round table beside the open window. The day was exquisite. The warm air seemed scented with spices. A bee flew in and buzzed around the blue dragon bowl filled with sulfur-yellow roses that stood in front of him. He felt completely happy.\\n\\nSuddenly his gaze fell on the screen he had placed in front of the portrait, and he started.\\n\\n\u201cToo chilly for Monsieur?\u201d asked his valet, setting an omelet on the table. \u201cShall I close the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
