          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"febed9f1075903e894f20b85\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a gentleman, through and through, and so was his whole family. He was well born, as they say, and the Widow Douglas claimed that was just as valuable in a man as in a racehorse, and nobody ever denied she was top aristocracy in our town. Even Pap said so, though he was no more fancy than a mudcat. Colonel Grangerford was very tall and very slim, with a dark, pale complexion and not a trace of redness anywhere. He shaved his thin face clean every morning. His lips were the thinnest kind, so were his nostrils, and he had a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and the darkest eyes, set so deep they seemed to stare at you from caverns. His forehead was high, his hair black and straight, hanging to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin, and every day he wore a fresh shirt and a full linen suit so white it almost hurt to look at. On Sundays he put on a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver head. There wasn\u2019t anything frivolous about him, and he never raised his voice. He was as kind as could be\u2014you could feel it, and it made you trust him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was something to see. But when he straightened up like a flagpole and the lightning started flashing from under his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree first and ask questions later. He never needed to tell anyone to mind their manners; everybody behaved around him. People loved having him around; he felt like sunshine most of the time, like he made the weather good. If he turned into a storm cloud, it got dark for half a minute, but that was enough\u2014nothing went wrong for a week after that.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family stood up and wished them good day, and didn\u2019t sit again until they did. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard where the decanter sat, mixed a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He held it until Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s were ready, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d The colonel and the old lady bowed just the slightest bit and said thank you, and then all three drank. Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and that dash of whiskey or apple brandy in the bottom of their glasses and gave it to me and Buck, and we drank to the old folks, too.\"}"
