          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d19fc7f7301ad0a8f592261b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI trust I shall shortly enjoy the pleasure of presenting my son to you,\u201d observed Mr. Weston.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, perfectly ready to imagine a special compliment meant for herself in this hope, returned the most gracious of smiles.\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I suppose,\u201d he went on, \u201cand know that he is my son, though he does not carry my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! certainly, and I shall be delighted to make his acquaintance. I am sure Mr. Elton will hasten to call on him, and we shall both find much pleasure in receiving him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are exceedingly kind.\u2014Frank will be quite happy, I know.\u2014He is expected in town next week, if not earlier. We had notice of it in a letter today. I came upon the letters in my walk this morning, and seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, ventured to open it\u2014although it was not addressed to me\u2014it was meant for Mrs. Weston. She is his chief correspondent, I assure you. I scarcely ever get a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so you actually broke the seal of what was directed to her! Oh! Mr. Weston\u2014(laughing with affected reproach) I must protest.\u2014A truly dangerous precedent!\u2014Pray do not allow your neighbours to adopt such conduct.\u2014Upon my word, if this is what is before us, we married women must put forth our energies!\u2014Oh! Mr. Weston, I could never have credited it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, we men are sad creatures. You must beware, Mrs. Elton.\u2014This letter informs us\u2014it is a brief note\u2014penned in haste merely to supply the tidings\u2014it informs us they are all coming up to town immediately, on Mrs. Churchill\u2019s account\u2014she has been unwell all winter, and finds Enscombe too cold\u2014so they are to shift southward without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014from Yorkshire, if I remember. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, about one hundred and ninety miles from London\u2014a long journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, truly, very long. Sixty-five miles further than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of ample fortune?\u2014You would be astonished to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, dashes about. You will scarcely credit me\u2014but twice within one week he and Mr. Bragge traveled to London and back with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe difficulty about the distance from Enscombe,\u201d remarked Mr. Weston, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand, has not been able to leave the sofa for a full week at a time. In Frank\u2019s latest letter she declared, he said, that she was too feeble to reach her conservatory without the support of both his arm and his uncle\u2019s!\u201d\"}"
