          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"913dfa6a2f19b1552803186b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the force of gravitation would steadily wane in proportion to the squares of the increasing distances, and thus, with velocity swelling to a prodigious acceleration, I should ultimately reach those remote tracts where Earth\u2019s attraction yields to the moon\u2019s. In keeping with this view, I deemed it unnecessary to burden myself with more provisions than would suffice for forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was, nevertheless, another obstacle that gave me a measure of uneasiness. It has been noted that in balloon ascents of considerable elevation, apart from the discomfort of breathing, intense distress arises in head and body, often with bleeding at the nose and other alarming symptoms, all of which become more oppressive as altitude increases. This reflection was somewhat disquieting. Was it not likely these symptoms would augment without limit, or at least until life itself ended? I ultimately decided otherwise. Their source was to be found in the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure upon the body\u2019s surface and the resulting distention of the superficial blood-vessels\u2014not in any actual disorganization of the animal system, as happens with respiratory difficulty, where insufficient atmospheric density fails to chemically renovate the blood within a heart ventricle. Absent this failure of renovation, I could discern no reason life might not continue even in a vacuum; for the chest\u2019s expansion and contraction, commonly termed breathing, is a purely muscular effort and cause, not consequence, of respiration. In short, I concluded that as the frame grew accustomed to lacking atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would steadily lessen\u2014and while they lasted, I trusted in the iron hardness of my constitution to bear them.\\n\\n\u201cThus, if it please your Excellencies, I have set forth some, though not all, of the reflections that induced me to devise the project of a lunar voyage. I shall now proceed to bring before you the outcome of a venture seemingly so bold in theory, and certainly so utterly without precedent in human annals.\\n\\n\u201cHaving reached the altitude already specified\u2014three miles and three-quarters\u2014I flung from the car a quantity of feathers and perceived that I still rose with ample speed; consequently, there was no occasion to release ballast. This pleased me, for I wished to retain as much weight as possible, for reasons I shall elucidate later.\u201d\"}"
