          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9a223fc4101cf4863041467c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She suddenly longed to reach the outer door; she wished to know whether it rained. Why should she accuse herself of any hidden motive? Captain Wentworth must already be gone from sight. She rose and resolved to go; she would not permit one half of herself to be forever reputed wiser than the other, or to suspect the other of being worse than it was. She would see about the rain. Yet she was immediately driven back when Captain Wentworth himself entered, accompanied by a set of ladies and gentlemen clearly of his acquaintance, whom he must have met a short way below Milsom Street. He appeared more visibly struck and flustered at seeing her than she had ever before witnessed; he flushed deeply. For the first time since they had renewed their acquaintance, she felt she betrayed the lesser composure of the two. In the few moments preceding his appearance she had gained the advantage of preparation. The overwhelming, dazzling, confusing first shock was past for her. Still, there was much to endure! Agitation, distress, joy\u2014something between rapture and despair\u2014filled her.\\n\\nHe spoke, then turned aside. His whole manner bespoke confusion. She could not have named it either cold or cordial\u2014nothing so precise as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a brief pause, he again approached and addressed her. Mutual inquiries on ordinary topics followed: neither likely became wiser from their conversation, and she remained fully aware of his being less at ease than he once had been. Simply by the necessity of constant meetings they had learned to speak to one another with apparent calmness and indifference; yet he could not manage it now. Time had altered him, or Louisa had altered him. Some sort of consciousness hung upon him. He looked exceedingly well, not as one suffering either in health or spirits, and he talked of Uppercross, the Musgroves, indeed even of Louisa, catching for an instant his old arch expression as he pronounced her name; nevertheless, it was Captain Wentworth ill at ease, uncomfortable, unable to pretend the contrary.\\n\\nAnne felt no surprise, though she was pained, to observe Elizabeth refuse to recognize him. She saw that he noticed Elizabeth, that Elizabeth noticed him, that there was perfect internal recognition on both sides; she felt certain he stood ready to be acknowledged, expecting it, and she suffered to watch her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot was becoming quite impatient, now stopped at the door; the servant entered to announce it.\"}"
