          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a8c60b802993b286e2f73464\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou must adopt a portion of my philosophy: dwell on the past only when its recollection yields delight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot credit you with such a philosophy. _Your_ reflections must be so free from reproach that the calm they bring is ignorance, not philosophy. It is otherwise with _me_. Painful memories force themselves forward, and they cannot, should not, be dismissed. I have been selfish all my life in conduct, though not in creed. Childhood instructed me in what was _right_, yet no one taught me to master my temper. I received sound principles, but was left to follow them with pride and conceit. Sadly, as an only son (and for years an only _child_), I was indulged by my parents, who, though virtuous themselves\u2014my father especially, so kindly and amiable\u2014allowed, encouraged, nearly instructed me to be self-centered and domineering, to care for no one beyond my own household, to think poorly of everyone else, to _wish_ to think poorly of their sense and worth compared with mine. Thus I was from eight to eight-and-twenty; and thus I might have remained, but for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! What do I not owe you? You taught me a lesson, severe at the outset, yet most profitable. You humbled me as I deserved. I sought you with no doubt of a welcome. You proved how insufficient were all my claims to charm a woman deserving to be charmed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you persuaded yourself that I should?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What say you to my presumption? I fancied you wished, anticipated my attentions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen my manners were at fault, though unintentionally, I assure you. I never meant to mislead, though my spirits might often betray me. How you must have despised me after _that_ evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDespise you! I was angry at first, perhaps; but very soon my anger turned where it ought.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hardly dare ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed; surprise was all I felt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not exceed _mine_ at being treated with attention. My conscience told me I deserved no unusual politeness, and I admit I did not expect _more_ than was due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy intention _then_,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, by every civility I possessed, that I was not so base as to resent the past; and I hoped to win your pardon, to soften your bad opinion, by convincing you your rebukes had been observed.\u201d\"}"
