          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7ea0e83328ed658f241199e6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have already said he was a poet. At twenty-three he brought out his first poem, and the whole nation greeted it with enthusiasm. Fortune shone on him without pause; never had fame grown so quickly, and it was complete. The crowd praised the very verses that scholars admired in their studies; not a single voice disagreed.\\n\\nDuring this peak of glory he met Elinor. She was a young heiress of exquisite beauty, living with her guardian; from the moment people saw them together, they seemed made for each other. Elinor lacked Woodville\u2019s genius, yet she was generous and noble, lifted by her youth and the love she inspired above any knowledge but that of virtue and goodness. She was lovely, her manners frank and simple; her deep blue eyes shone with a light born of sensibility joined with wisdom.\\n\\nThey were made for each other, and soon they loved. Woodville felt love\u2019s delight for the first time, and Elinor was thrilled to win the heart of one so beautiful and glorious among his fellows. Could anything but pure joy come from such a union?\\n\\nWoodville was a poet, sought by every society, every eye fixed on him when he appeared; yet he was the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor was rich. Her guardian did not oppose their affection: Woodville\u2019s merit was too great for anyone to object to his lack of wealth. But her father\u2019s dying will forbade her to marry before reaching the age of twenty-one, and her fortune depended on her obeying this command. She had just turned twenty, so she and her lover had to accept the delay. Still, they were always together, their happiness like Paradise. They studied side by side, planned their future work, and, drinking love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and words, they barely resented the wait for their complete union. Woodville\u2019s glory kept growing, and Elinor became even lovelier and wiser under the guidance of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be twenty-one; everything was ready for their marriage. How can I tell the catastrophe that struck such joy? But the earth would no longer be the earth, covered with blight and sorrow, if one such pair of angelic beings had been allowed to live only for one another. Search the world and you will not find the perfect happiness their marriage would have given them. For such supreme joy to exist, the whole order of things among us miserable earth-dwellers would have had to change.\"}"
