{"request_id":"3685ab56e2738851412032c5","rewritten_text":"The next morning at breakfast the Otis family discussed the ghost at length. The United States Minister was understandably annoyed that his gift had been refused. “I don’t want to hurt the ghost personally,” he said, “but considering how long he has lived in this house, it’s not at all polite to throw pillows at him.” This very reasonable remark made the twins burst into loud laughter. “On the other hand,” he went on, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we will have to take his chains away. It would be impossible to sleep with that racket outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week they were not disturbed. The only odd event was the constant reappearance of the bloodstain on the library floor. This was certainly strange, since Mr. Otis locked the door every night and the windows were barred. The stain’s shifting color caused great excitement. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it would change to vermilion; then a rich purple; and once, when they came down for family prayers, according to the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, it was a bright emerald green. These kaleidoscopic changes amused them greatly, and every evening they made bets about what color it would be next. The only person who didn’t enjoy the joke was little Virginia, who, for reasons no one understood, was always upset by the stain and almost cried the morning it turned emerald green.

The ghost appeared again on Sunday night. Shortly after everyone had gone to bed they were shocked by a loud crash in the hall. They ran downstairs to find a large suit of old armor had fallen from its stand onto the stone floor. Sitting in a high-backed chair nearby was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with a look of intense pain. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him with the skill that comes from long, careful practice on a writing master, while the United States Minister pointed his revolver and, in proper Californian style, ordered him to raise his hands. The ghost leapt up with a wild shriek of rage and swept through them like a mist, putting out Washington Otis’s candle as he passed and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

