          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b97f794a9a5816db24cc5f5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man\u2014a very nice, kindly looking old man when I woke him. I told him my brother and I were over on Spanish Island hunting yesterday afternoon, and we camped on part of a raft we found. Around midnight he must have kicked his gun while dreaming, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted the doctor to go there, fix him up, and keep quiet about it so we could go home this evening and surprise the folks.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he says.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. After a minute he says:\\n\\n\u201cHow\u2019d you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I say, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStrange dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nSo he lit his lantern, grabbed his saddle-bags, and we headed out. But when he saw the canoe, he didn\u2019t like the looks of it\u2014said it was big enough for one but didn\u2019t seem safe for two. I say:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you don\u2019t have to be afraid, sir; she carried the three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns; that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nBut he stepped on the gunwale, rocked her, shook his head, and said he reckoned he\u2019d look around for a bigger one. But they were all locked and chained, so he took my canoe and told me to wait till he came back, or I could keep looking, or maybe go home to get them ready for the surprise if I wanted. I said I didn\u2019t. So I told him exactly how to find the raft, and he left.\\n\\nPretty soon an idea struck me. I says to myself, suppose he can\u2019t fix that leg in no time flat, like the saying goes? Suppose it takes him three or four days? What are we going to do\u2014hang around until he spills the secret? No, sir; I know what I\u2019ll do. I\u2019ll wait, and if he comes back saying he has to go again, I\u2019ll get down there too, even if I have to swim. We\u2019ll tie him up, keep him, and shove off down the river; and when Tom\u2019s done with him we\u2019ll pay him what it\u2019s worth, or everything we got, then let him back on shore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a lumber pile to sleep, and next time I woke, the sun was high overhead! I jumped up and ran to the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he\u2019d left sometime in the night and hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
