          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1d692ab5565a98e87102abc2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I rose early the next morning, slipped past my creditors once more, and hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall, where I spent the little cash I had on books about Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. After getting home safely with them, I poured all my spare time into studying and quickly made the progress I believed necessary to carry out my plan. During this period I did everything I could to win over the three creditors who had been tormenting me. I eventually succeeded\u2014partly by selling enough household furniture to cover half of what I owed, and partly by promising to pay the remainder after completing a small project I claimed to be planning, for which I asked their help. They were uneducated men, so it wasn\u2019t hard to bring them around to my purpose.\\n\\n\u201cOnce everything was arranged, my wife and I, with great secrecy and care, sold what little property we still owned, and borrowed various small sums under different pretenses, never worrying about how we would repay them later. With the funds I gathered this way, I gradually purchased fine cambric muslin in twelve-yard lengths; twine; a supply of caoutchouc varnish; a large, deep, custom-made wicker basket; and several other items necessary to build and outfit an unusually large balloon. I instructed my wife to sew it together as quickly as possible, giving her all the directions she needed for the exact method to follow. Meanwhile, I wove the twine into a net of sufficient size, fitted it with a hoop and the required lines, and acquired a quadrant, a compass, a spyglass, a modified barometer, and two less common astronomical instruments. I then seized chances to move by night, to a secluded spot east of Rotterdam, five iron-bound casks holding about fifty gallons each and one larger cask; six tinned tubes, each three inches across, properly shaped, ten feet long; some of a particular metallic or semi-metallic substance whose name I won\u2019t reveal; and a dozen demijohns of a very common acid. The gas to be produced from these materials had never been generated by anyone else\u2014or at least never used for such a purpose. I would gladly disclose the secret if it were mine to share, but it belongs to a citizen of Nantes, France, who told it to me under certain conditions.\u201d\"}"
