          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"84925cb84376a7fb5c779fb2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been the very time in Mr Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cthat has always stirred my special curiosity. It must have been when he first met my father and sister. I never met him myself; I only heard about him; but there was something in how he behaved toward them then, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that I have never been able to match with what he is now. It suggested a different sort of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d cried Mrs Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I met him, but he talked of them endlessly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I can satisfy you, perhaps, on points you would hardly expect; and as for his marriage, I knew every detail at the time. I knew every reason for and against it; I was the friend he trusted with his hopes and plans; and though I had not known his wife before, her lower place in society made that impossible, I knew her all the rest of her life, or at least until the last two years, and can answer any question you like.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular question about her. I always understood they were not a happy couple. But I want to know why, at that time, he slighted my father\u2019s acquaintance the way he did. My father was certainly ready to show him all the kindness and attention that was proper. Why did Mr Elliot hold back?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs Smith, \u201cat that point in his life, had one goal: to make his fortune, and by a quicker route than the law. He was determined to make it through marriage. He was determined, at least, not to ruin it with an imprudent marriage; and I know he believed\u2014rightly or wrongly, I cannot say\u2014that your father and sister, in their politeness and invitations, were planning a match between the heir and the young lady. Such a match could never have met his ideas of wealth and independence. That was why he drew back, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He hid nothing from me. It was curious that, after leaving you behind me in Bath, the first and closest acquaintance I made on marrying should be your cousin; and that, through him, I should keep hearing of your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
