          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b276a7e05f54c7b3809e42b0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIf you wish for one for domestic purposes, I can furnish an excellent piece and assure you that you will be satisfied with the effect. Might I enquire for whom it is meant? Should it be for the police, or for anyone associated with Scotland Yard, I fear I cannot accommodate you. The English detectives are truly our greatest allies, and by trusting in their folly, we may accomplish exactly what we desire. I could not spare even one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI promise you,\u201d declared Lord Arthur, \u201cit bears no reference to the police whatsoever. In truth, the clock is designed for the Dean of Chichester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear me! I was unaware that you felt so passionately about religion, Lord Arthur. Few young men do at present.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI fear you credit me with too much, Herr Winckelkopf,\u201d said Lord Arthur, colouring. \u201cIn fact, I am quite ignorant of theology.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is then a strictly private business?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEntirely private.\u201d\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf lifted his shoulders and withdrew, returning shortly with a round cake of dynamite about the size of a penny, and a charming little French clock, topped with an ormolu Liberty trampling the hydra of Despotism.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s face lit up at the sight. \u201cThat is precisely what I require,\u201d he exclaimed, \u201cand now explain how it is set off.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! that is my mystery,\u201d replied Herr Winckelkopf, surveying his creation with justified pride; \u201clet me know the exact moment you wish it to explode, and I will adjust the mechanism accordingly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, today is Tuesday, and if you could dispatch it at once\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat cannot be; I have a great deal of pressing work to manage for some acquaintances in Moscow. Yet I might send it away tomorrow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, that will be perfectly timely!\u201d said Lord Arthur courteously, \u201cif it arrives tomorrow night or Thursday morning. As for the explosion, let us say Friday at noon on the dot. The Dean is invariably at home then.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFriday, at noon,\u201d Herr Winckelkopf repeated, jotting the detail into a large ledger that rested on a bureau near the hearth.\\n\\n\u201cAnd now,\u201d said Lord Arthur, rising, \u201cpray inform me how much I owe you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is such a trifling affair, Lord Arthur, that I hesitate to exact payment. The dynamite costs seven and sixpence, the clock three pounds ten, and the carriage roughly five shillings. I am delighted to oblige any companion of Count Rouvaloff\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut your trouble, Herr Winckelkopf?\u201d\"}"
