          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3c9f07f81a53d5cf97916979\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a strange, enchanting quality in all of this that drew me in. It was as if he had finally woken from a long, dreamlike sleep, feeling like one of the seven sleepers or like Nourjahad from that charming imitation of an eastern tale. Diana was gone; his friends were altered or dead, and on his awakening I was all he had left to love on earth.\\n\\nHow dear the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond became now that I had such a beloved companion for my wanderings. With my father I visited every delightful spot, whether on the islands or near the tree-sheltered waterfalls, every shady path or hollow tangled with underwood and fern. His conversation widened my mind. I felt renewed, with the freshness and vigor of a new being. His arrival seemed to lift me from a narrow patch of earth into a universe boundless to imagination and understanding. My life before had resembled a pleasant country rill, destined never to leave its native fields but to fade quietly when its course was done. Now it seemed a varied river flowing through a rich and lovely landscape, ever changing and ever beautiful. Alas! I did not foresee the desert it was about to reach, the rocks that would tear its waters, and the hideous scene that would return its image in a more twisted form. Life was bright then; I was learning to hope, and what brings more bitter despair than hope destroyed?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should so quickly follow such heavenly happiness? I drank from an enchanted cup, yet gall lay at the bottom of its long sweetness. My heart overflowed with affection, yet it was calm because of its great depth and richness. I never imagined that misery could arise from love, and the lesson we all must learn eventually was forced on me in a way few ever endure. I mourn now, I must always mourn, those few short months of paradise; I broke no command, I tasted no apple, yet I was mercilessly driven out. Alas! my companion did, and I was cast down in his fall. But I stray from my tale\u2014let sorrow come when its time arrives; here I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this joyful companionship when my aunt fell ill. I spent an entire month in her chamber nursing her, but her disease was mortal and she died, leaving me inconsolable for a time. Death is so terrible to the living; the chains of habit are so strong even when love does not bind them, and the heart must agonize when they break.\"}"
