{"request_id":"35fa726c4c8bceb15a7e1d49","rewritten_text":"“Oh, that is nothing at all! It gives me pleasure. I do not work for money; I live wholly for my art.”

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. upon the table, expressed his thanks to the little German for his kindness, and, after successfully refusing an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, quitted the house and made his way to the Park.

For the following two days he was in the highest state of agitation, and on Friday at noon he drove to the Buckingham to await tidings. All through the afternoon the impassive hall-porter continued to post up telegrams from different parts of the kingdom reporting horse-race results, divorce verdicts, weather conditions, and the like, while the tape rattled out dreary particulars about an all-night session in the House of Commons and a slight panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to the fierce annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who was eager to read the reports of a speech he had delivered that morning at the Mansion House on South African Missions and the expediency of appointing black Bishops in every province, and who for some reason bore a violent prejudice against the _Evening News_. None of the journals, however, made even the most indirect reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that the effort must have failed. It was a dreadful disappointment, and for a while he was completely shaken. The next day he called on Herr Winckelkopf, who poured forth elaborate apologies and offered to furnish another clock free of charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. But Lord Arthur had lost all confidence in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything is so adulterated now that even dynamite is scarcely obtainable in a pure state. The little German, while conceding that some flaw must have occurred in the machinery, still hoped the clock might yet go off, citing the example of a barometer he had once dispatched to the military Governor at Odessa, which, although timed to explode in ten days, delayed nearly three months. Admittedly, when it finally detonated it only managed to blow a housemaid to fragments, the Governor having left town six weeks earlier, but it at least demonstrated that dynamite, though somewhat unpunctual under mechanical regulation, is as a destructive power extremely effective."}

