          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71be402630c49fcbe4fc62dc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During the meal, Mr. Bennet hardly spoke; but once the servants had departed, he deemed it fitting to converse with his visitor, and opened a topic where he anticipated Mr. Collins would excel. He remarked upon Mr. Collins\u2019s fortunate patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and her concern for his comfort seemed quite extraordinary. Mr. Bennet could not have selected a better subject. Mr. Collins waxed eloquent in her commendation. The theme raised him to an even more solemn demeanour, and with the gravest expression he declared he had never witnessed such conduct in a person of rank\u2014such affability and condescension as those he himself had received from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both the sermons he had had the honour to deliver before her. She had invited him twice to dine at Rosings and had summoned him only the previous Saturday to complete her quadrille table that evening. He knew many regarded Lady Catherine as proud, yet he himself had seen nothing but affability in her. She had always addressed him as she would any other gentleman; she raised no objection to his mingling with the neighbourhood\u2019s society, nor to his leaving his parish now and then for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as he could, provided he chose wisely; and had once called upon him in his modest parsonage, where she fully approved the alterations he had made and even stooped to propose some additional ones\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is all exceedingly proper and polite, I am sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a very agreeable woman. It is a pity great ladies in general are not more like her. Does she reside near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden of my humble dwelling is parted only by a lane from Rosings Park, her ladyship\u2019s residence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you mentioned she was a widow, sir? Has she a family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has but one daughter, the heiress of Rosings and of very large property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better off than many girls. And what sort of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is an exceedingly delightful young lady. Lady Catherine herself asserts that, in genuine beauty, Miss de Bourgh surpasses the most handsome of her sex, for there is in her features the mark of a young woman of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
