          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6785f587b6b739f86492080e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I wanted to feign death; then my contented heirs would grab my wealth, and I could buy freedom. But I had to plan carefully. I would not be left penniless, so I had to secure some money. Alas, to what loathsome shifts must I resort? Yet a whole life of falsehood was otherwise my fate, and any time remorse over being the author of a deception made me shrink, I was irresistibly pushed back into it by another visit from some aunt or cousin. They would tell me death is the end of all people, and then declare that my father had surely lost his senses since my mother\u2019s death; that he was mad and I was fortunate, because in one of his fits he might have killed me instead of destroying his own crazed life. And all this, to be sure, was delicately worded; not in coarse language that might hurt my feelings but\\n\\nwhispered so and so\\nin dark hint soft and low\\n\\nwith their eyes downcast, and sympathizing smiles or whimpers; and I listened with a calm face while every nerve trembled. I dared not say yes or no to any of this blasphemy. Oh, what a delightful life without guile! I with my dove\u2019s look and fox\u2019s heart; for I felt only the degradation of lying, and none of the sacred sense of conscious innocence that might atone for it. I, who once clothed myself in the bright garment of sincerity, now had to borrow many-colored garments. They might fit awkwardly at first, but practice would let me arrange them in elegant folds, to lie with grace. Yes, I might dye my soul with lies until I had completely hidden its native color. Oh, beloved father! Accept the pure heart of your unhappy daughter; let me join you unspotted as before, or you will not recognize my altered appearance. As grief might change Constance, so would deceit change me until in heaven you would say, \u201cThis is not my child.\u201d My father, to be happy now and when we meet again, I must flee this life that is mere mockery for someone like me. Only in solitude shall I be myself; in solitude I shall be yours.\\n\\nAlas! Even now I look back with disgust at the tricks and schemes by which, after many painful struggles, I carried out my retreat. I could describe at length the steps I took, first to secure a slight maintenance for the rest of my life, and then to make everyone believe I was dead. I could, but I will not.\"}"
