{"request_id":"64048b85149f83e141692499","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. The killers were human and escaped in a physical way. How? Thankfully, there is only one way to reason this out, and it must give us a clear answer. Let us inspect each possible exit. The killers were in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the next room, when the group went up the stairs. So we only need to look for exits from those two rooms. The police have torn up the floors, ceilings, and masonry of the walls in every direction. No hidden passage could have escaped them. Still, not trusting only their eyes, I examined everything myself. There were no secret passages. Both doors opening from the rooms into the hall were securely locked, with the keys inside. Let us consider the chimneys. These, although of normal width for about eight or ten feet above the hearths, are too narrow elsewhere for even a large cat. Since escape through these means is impossible, we are left with the windows. Through the front-room windows no one could have fled without the street crowd noticing. The killers must have gone through the back-room windows. Reaching that conclusion so clearly, we cannot, as logical thinkers, dismiss it because it seems impossible. We only have to prove that these apparent impossibilities are not impossible at all.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One is unobstructed by furniture and fully visible. The lower part of the other is hidden behind the headboard of the clumsy bed pushed close against it. The first window was found firmly fastened inside. It resisted all force from those who tried to raise it. A large gimlet hole had been bored in its frame to the left, and a very stout nail fitted tightly into it, almost to the head. When we examined the other window, we saw a similar nail fitted in the same way, and a strong attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were completely convinced that no one escaped through these openings. Therefore, they considered it unnecessary to pull out the nails and open the windows.

“My own inspection was more careful, for the reason I have just explained—because here, I knew, we had to prove that all seeming impossibilities were not truly impossible.”"}

