          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b77accd9b1e378298e75672\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is quite striking that she always hears Jane better than she hears me. Jane speaks so clearly! Still, she will not find her grandmother any deafer than she was two years ago, which says a lot for someone my mother\u2019s age\u2014and it really has been a full two years since she was here. We have never gone so long without seeing her before, and as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we will hardly know how to pay her enough attention now.\\n\\n\u201cAre you expecting Miss Fairfax here soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! That must be a great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Everyone is so surprised, and everyone says such nice things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury as they will be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell wants the carriage for one of those days. So kind of them to send her the whole way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is why she wrote sooner than usual, as we call it; normally we would not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, so I imagined. I was afraid there was little chance of hearing anything of Miss Fairfax today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo kind of you! No, we would not have heard, if it had not been for this particular reason\u2014her coming here so soon. My mother is delighted! She is to stay with us for at least three months. Three months, she says so positively, as I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you. The situation is that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her immediately. They had not meant to go until the summer, but she is so impatient to see them again\u2014for until she married last October, she was never away from them for more than a week, which must make it very odd to be in different countries, I was going to say in different kingdoms. So she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother\u2014or her father, I honestly do not know which, but we shall see in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014wrote in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, urging them to come right away, and they would meet them in Dublin and take them back to their country estate, Balycraig, which I imagine is a beautiful place.\"}"
