{"request_id":"a195fe44e0fb974c734d5ff5","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, but it is winter and the sun has already set. There are no clouds in the clear, frosty sky to catch its slanting rays, yet the air itself holds a faint rosy hue that glows again on the snow covering the ground. I live in a lonely cottage on an empty, wide heath where no voice reaches me. I look out over the desolate plain, white everywhere except for a few dark patches where the midday sun melted the snow on those sharp hilltops, leaving it thinner than on the flat. A few birds peck at the hard ice covering the pools—for the frost has lasted long.

I am in a strange state of mind. I am alone—completely alone—in the world. Misfortune has swept over me and left me withered. I know I am about to die, and I feel happy—joyous. I feel my pulse; it races. I lay my thin hand on my cheek; it burns. There is a slight, quick spirit inside me now throwing off its last sparks. I will never see another winter’s snow—I truly believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun; and with that belief I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a story like mine should die with me, but some feeling I cannot name pushes me forward, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist the faintest impulse. When life was stronger in me I thought the sacred horror of my tale made it unfit to tell, and now, as I approach death, I break that mystic silence. It is like the forest of the Furies—only the dying may enter—and Oedipus is ready to die.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know if anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. I do not address them to you alone because I take comfort in dwelling on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you were the only one to read what I write. I will relate my story, then, as if writing for strangers. You have often asked why I live so secluded, why I weep, and above all why I keep such impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I did not dare; in death I reveal the mystery. Others may skim these pages without care, but to you, Woodville—kind, loving friend—they will be precious, the cherished relics of a heartbroken girl who, even as she dies, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall on the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will—and while breath remains I thank you for your sympathy."}

