content.text:
{"request_id":"0401b7e9a104c77d887e0475","rewritten_text":"Few could claim as pure a heart as hers, or such genuine humility joined to confidence in her own integrity and faith in others. She had lived a quiet, secluded life since birth. Her mother died when she was very young, and her father devoted himself to her education. He held many unusual ideas that shaped the system he used to teach her. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England from centuries past, yet she was almost ignorant of current events. She had read few authors from the last fifty years, though otherwise her reading was extensive. So while she seemed less versed in the ways of society than he was, her knowledge ran deeper and rested on firmer foundations. Even if her beauty and sweetness had not charmed him, her mind alone would have held him captive. He looked up to her as his guide, and he adored her so much that he enjoyed increasing the sense of inferiority she sometimes made him feel.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college and, putting his old friends aside for a time, withdrew to the neighborhood where his Diana lived, finding all his comfort in her gentle voice and dearer embraces. This short break from his companions gave him the courage to claim his independence. He felt that, though they might mock his plan to marry, they would not dare once the marriage was done. He therefore sought his guardian’s consent, which he obtained with difficulty, and her father’s, which was easier to secure. Without telling anyone else of his intentions, he married Diana by the time he turned twenty.

He loved her with passion, and her tenderness enchanted him so much that he could think of nothing but her. He invited some college friends to visit, but their frivolity repelled him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him in boyhood. He was now a man and wondered how he had ever joined in the empty talk and ideas of his fellow students, or how he had feared their judgment even for a moment. He abandoned his old friendships not from fickleness, but because they were unworthy of him."}

