          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90bf9dc41f2e7530baf6036e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, then,\u201d he pressed on, \u201cwhat conclusion have we drawn? What manner of impression have I fixed upon your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nDupin\u2019s question sent a chill crawling over me. \u201cA madman,\u201d I answered, \u201cmust have committed this act\u2014some crazed maniac who broke loose from a nearby Maison de Sant\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn certain points,\u201d he returned, \u201cyour notion bears relevance. Yet the voices of lunatics, even in their wildest fits, never correspond to that singular voice heard upon the stairs. However erratic a madman may be, he belongs to some nation, and his tongue, despite disjointed words, keeps regular syllables. Moreover, the hair of a madman is nothing like what I now hold. I unwound this little tuft from the stiffly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Consider what you can make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, thoroughly shaken; \u201cthis hair is extraordinary\u2014it is not human.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not claimed it is,\u201d he replied. \u201cBut before we pronounce on that, look at this slight sketch I have drawn. It reproduces exactly what part of the testimony described as \u2018dark bruises and deep indentations of fingernails\u2019 upon Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what Messrs. Dumas and Etienne called a \u2018row of livid spots, plainly finger marks.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will observe,\u201d he continued, laying the paper on the table, \u201cthat the sketch conveys the idea of a firm, unmoving grip. There is no trace of slipping. Each finger seems to have maintained\u2014perhaps up to the instant of death\u2014the dreadful hold with which it first sank in. Try now to place all your fingers simultaneously within the respective impressions as shown here.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, and could not manage it.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing properly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe sheet lies on a flat plane; a human throat is cylindrical. Here is a billet of wood, roughly equal in circumference. Wrap the drawing around it and repeat the experiment.\u201d\\n\\nI complied, but the impossibility stood out even clearer. \u201cThis,\u201d I declared, \u201ccannot be the mark of a human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen read,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was an exact anatomical and general description of the grand fulvous Orang-Outang of the East Indian islands. Everyone knows well the enormous size, immense strength and agility, savage ferocity, and mimicking instincts of those animals. At once I grasped the full horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the digits,\u201d I said, finishing the reading, \u201ccorresponds perfectly with this drawing.\u201d\"}"
