          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"29b0d7747a5580ee03fe929e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"How long I lingered in that condition no human arithmetic could determine. It must, nevertheless, have been a substantial interval, for when I partially regained some consciousness of being, dawn was breaking, the balloon towering at an immense altitude over a desert of sea, and not the faintest hint of land could be detected anywhere within the sweeping circle of the horizon. Yet the sensations that greeted my recovery were far less agonizing than might be expected. In truth, there was a certain budding insanity in the tranquil inspection I began to make of my predicament. I raised each hand, one at a time, to my eyes, marveling at what incident could have produced the bulging veins and the ghastly blackness staining my fingernails. Next I scrutinized my head, shaking it again and again, and feeling it with meticulous care until I was satisfied it was not, as I had been more than half suspecting, larger than my balloon. Then, with an air of sagacity, I thrust my fingers into both breeches pockets, and, missing a notebook and a toothpick case, tried to account for their loss; as no explanation offered itself, I experienced a sting of vexation. Only then did it occur to me that the joint of my left ankle was suffering great uneasiness, and a dim comprehension of my situation began to flicker through my brain. But, strangely enough, I felt neither amazement nor terror. If any emotion stirred at all, it was a kind of inward chuckle at the ingenuity I was ready to display in escaping this scrape; never did I for a moment treat my final safety as a matter open to doubt. For several minutes I lay plunged in the deepest contemplation. I clearly remember frequently compressing my lips, placing my forefinger beside my nose, and employing other gestures and grimaces familiar to men who, seated comfortably in their chairs, ponder affairs of intricacy or consequence. When I believed my thoughts sufficiently marshaled, I carefully and deliberately slipped my hands behind me and unfastened the large iron buckle attached to the waistband of my inexpressibles. The buckle had three teeth which, being somewhat rusty, rotated on their pivots only with great difficulty. After some labor I managed to turn them to a right angle from the buckle\u2019s body and was pleased to see they held firm in that pose. Clamping the makeshift instrument between my teeth, I then set about untying the knot of my cravat. Several times I was obliged to pause before the maneuver could be accomplished, yet accomplished it was at last.\"}"
