          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a4e32c3a46ea809790fe6a34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water to conclude the feast. After a while Becky proposed they move on. Tom fell silent a moment before saying, \u201cBecky, can you stand it if I tell you something?\u201d Becky\u2019s face went white, but she believed she could. \u201cWell, Becky, we have to stay here where there\u2019s water to drink. That little piece is our last candle!\u201d Becky broke into tears and laments. Tom tried to soothe her, but with little success. Presently Becky said, \u201cTom!\u201d \u201cWhat is it, Becky?\u201d \u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and search for us!\u201d \u201cYes, they will! Of course they will!\u201d \u201cMaybe they\u2019re searching already, Tom.\u201d \u201cI suppose maybe they are. I hope so.\u201d \u201cWhen would they miss us, Tom?\u201d \u201cWhen they return to the boat, I guess.\u201d \u201cTom, it might be dark then\u2014would they see we weren\u2019t there?\u201d \u201cI don\u2019t know. But your mother would miss you as soon as they got home.\u201d Becky\u2019s frightened expression brought Tom to his senses and he realized his blunder. Becky hadn\u2019t been expected home that night! The children fell silent and reflective. Soon Becky\u2019s renewed sobs told Tom that what he feared had struck her too\u2014Sabbath morning might be half gone before Mrs. Thatcher found out Becky was not at Mrs. Harper\u2019s. They fixed their gaze on their little candle, watching it waste away without mercy; saw the half-inch wick stand alone at last; watched the weak flame flutter, climb a thin trail of smoke, pause a moment at the top, and then\u2014the terror of total darkness! Becky later slowly realized she was crying in Tom\u2019s arms; neither knew how long it had taken. All they remembered was that after what felt an endless stretch of time, both awoke from a heavy stupor of sleep and took up their misery once more. Tom said it might already be Sunday\u2014perhaps even Monday. He tried to get Becky talking, but her anguish was overwhelming\u2014every hope crushed. Tom insisted they must have been missed long ago and someone surely sought them. He shouted in case someone might hear, but the echo in the darkness sounded so ghastly he dared not try again. Hours crept by, and hunger came to plague them. Tom still had part of his half of the cake; they split it and ate. Yet they only felt more ravenous than before.\"}"
