          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"83ba598c55e05de38ad17650\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while, midnight boomed from the tall tower at Westminster, and with each deep stroke the night seemed to quiver. Then the railway lights went out, leaving one lamp glowing like a large ruby on a giant mast, and the city\u2019s roar faded. At two o\u2019clock he got up and wandered toward Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal, as if in a strange dream. The houses across the river seemed built from darkness. It was as if silver and shadow had remade the world. The huge dome of St. Paul\u2019s loomed like a bubble in the dim air. As he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle he saw a man leaning over the parapet, and when he drew closer the man looked up, the gaslight shining on his face. It was Mr. Podgers, the palm reader. No one could mistake the fat, loose face, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the weak smile, the sensual mouth. Lord Arthur stopped. A brilliant idea flashed through him, and he crept quietly forward. In a moment he grabbed Mr. Podgers by the legs and threw him into the Thames. There was a coarse oath, a heavy splash, and silence. Lord Arthur peered over but saw nothing of the palm reader except a tall hat spinning in a swirl of moonlit water. After a while it too sank, and Mr. Podgers vanished. At one point he thought he saw the bulky misshapen figure swimming toward the bridge stairs, and dread of failure swept over him, but it was only a reflection, and when the moon came out from the clouds it disappeared. At last he seemed to have fulfilled the decree of fate. He let out a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips. \u201cHave you dropped anything, sir?\u201d asked a voice behind him suddenly. He turned and saw a policeman with a bull\u2019s-eye lantern. \u201cNothing important, sergeant,\u201d he replied with a smile, hailed a passing hansom, jumped in, and told the driver to go to Belgrave Square. For the next few days he wavered between hope and fear. At times he almost expected Mr. Podgers to walk in, yet at other moments he felt Fate could not be so unfair to him. Twice he went to the palm reader\u2019s address on West Moon Street, but he could not bring himself to ring. He longed for certainty yet feared it. At last it came. He was in the club smoking-room having tea, listening wearily to Surbiton describe the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
