          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36fc510063cce81b8c9c0918\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ten minutes later Lord Arthur Savile burst out of Bentinck House, face ashen with terror and eyes blazing with grief, pushing through the ring of fur-clad footmen about the striped awning, apparently blind and deaf to everything. The night was piercingly cold; the gas-lamps around the square flared and sputtered in the sharp wind, yet his hands burned with fever and his brow felt aflame. He forged on, with the unsteady pace of a drunken man. A policeman eyed him curiously as he passed, and a beggar who slunk from an archway to plead for coins shrank back, frightened by a wretchedness deeper than his own. Once he halted beneath a lamp and stared at his hands. He fancied he already saw the blot of blood there, and a faint cry slipped from his shaking lips.\\n\\nMurder! That was what the cheiromantist had discerned in his palm. Murder! The very night seemed aware of it, and the desolate wind shrieked it in his ear. The shadowed corners of the streets teemed with it. It grinned down from the roofs.\\n\\nHis first stop was the Park, its sombre grove exerting a strange spell. He leaned, exhausted, against the railings, pressing his brow to the damp metal, listening to the quivering hush among the trees. \u201cMurder! murder!\u201d he kept muttering, as if sheer repetition might dull the horror of the word. The sound of his own voice made him quake, yet he almost wished Echo would catch it and rouse the sleeping city. He felt an insane impulse to halt any passer-by and pour out everything.\\n\\nNext he drifted over Oxford Street into cramped, sordid lanes. Two painted women jeered at him as he went past. From a dark courtyard came oaths and blows, followed by piercing screams, and on a wet doorstep he glimpsed the hunched shapes of poverty and age. A strange compassion seized him. Were these children of vice and want destined for their fate, just as he was for his? Were they, like him, no more than puppets in some monstrous spectacle?\\n\\nYet it was not the enigma of suffering but its comedy that struck him, its utter futility, its grotesque lack of meaning. How incoherent everything appeared! How bereft of harmony! He marvelled at the clash between the day\u2019s shallow optimism and the true facts of life. He was still very young.\\n\\nEventually he found himself before Marylebone Church. The mute roadway stretched like a long ribbon of polished silver, marked here and there by the dark arabesques of wavering shadows.\"}"
