          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6383945c9cfa65697765182e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He carefully drove a peg into the exact spot where the beetle had fallen. Then he pulled a tape measure from his pocket. He fixed one end to the nearest part of the tree trunk, ran the tape down to the peg, and extended it farther along the line already set by the tree and peg for fifty feet, while Jupiter cleared the brambles with a scythe. At that point he drove another peg. Using that second peg as the center, he marked a rough circle about four feet wide. He took up a spade, gave one to Jupiter and another to me, and urged us to start digging at once.\\n\\nTo be honest, digging held no charm for me, and at that time I would gladly have refused. Evening was approaching, and I was worn out from the day\u2019s exertions. Yet I saw no way to avoid it and dreaded upsetting my poor friend by refusing. If I could have counted on Jupiter\u2019s help, I would have tried to drag the madman home by force, but I knew too well the old servant\u2019s loyalty to hope he would ever oppose his master. I felt sure my friend had fallen for one of those Southern tales about buried treasure, and that finding the beetle\u2014or Jupiter\u2019s claim that it was \u201ca real gold bug\u201d\u2014had convinced him. A mind already leaning toward madness could easily latch onto such ideas, especially when they fit its favorite notions. I remembered, too, how he had called the beetle \u201cthe key to his fortune.\u201d Altogether, I was frustrated and bewildered, but finally decided to make the best of it, dig willingly, and end the fantasy sooner by showing him the truth with my own eyes.\\n\\nOnce we lit the lanterns, we attacked the ground with a zeal that would have suited a saner purpose. As the light shone on our bodies and tools, I could not help thinking what a striking group we made, and how odd and suspicious our work would have seemed to any wanderer who might have come upon us.\\n\\nWe dug steadily for two hours.\"}"
