          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52ea3f955f4c6e90b1d97f67\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning fixed for Admiral and Mrs Croft to inspect Kellynch Hall, Anne felt it most natural to make her nearly daily visit to Lady Russell\u2019s, keeping out of the way until the whole matter was settled, and then felt it most natural to regret missing the chance of seeing them. The meeting of the parties turned out extremely satisfactory, and settled the affair immediately. Each lady had been predisposed toward agreement, and therefore perceived nothing but courtesy in the other; and regarding the gentlemen, there was such thorough good humour, such frank, trusting generosity in the Admiral, that Sir Walter could not help responding\u2014especially as Mr Shepherd had flattered him with reports that the Admiral already knew him as a paragon of manners, leading him to his very finest, most polished behaviour. House, grounds, furniture, all met approval, the Crofts were approved, terms, timing, every aspect was right, and Mr Shepherd\u2019s clerks were set to work without a single preliminary point to adjust of all that \u201cThis indenture sheweth.\u201d Sir Walter promptly declared the Admiral the handsomest sailor he had ever encountered, and even said that if his own valet might regulate the Admiral\u2019s hair, he would never feel embarrassed to appear anywhere in his company; and the Admiral, with equal warmth, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI imagined we should come quickly to terms, my dear, despite what they told us at Taunton. The Baronet will never set the Thames on fire, yet there seems no harm in him\u201d\u2014a pair of mutual compliments that would have been reckoned nearly equivalent. The Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas; and as Sir Walter intended to move to Bath during the preceding month, no time could be lost in arranging every dependent detail. Lady Russell, convinced Anne would not be permitted any usefulness or importance in selecting the house they were to secure, was most unwilling to have her rushed off so soon, and hoped to contrive that she might remain behind until she herself could escort her to Bath after Christmas; but having commitments that would take her away from Kellynch for several weeks, she could not extend the invitation she wished, and Anne, though apprehensive of possible September heat in the white glare of Bath and deeply sorry to forfeit the influence, so sweet and so melancholy, of autumn in the countryside, still did not feel, all things considered, that she desired to stay.\"}"
