{"request_id":"7b36e7509ba6754c7ea4c65f","rewritten_text":"It was after two when, by an abrupt turn, I suddenly stood beside the lake near a cove where a skiff lay moored. This spot was not far from our home, and I could see my father and aunt strolling on the lawn. I sprang into the boat, and being no novice in such exploits, I shoved off and pulled with all my might for the opposite side. Clad in white, with only my tartan rachan thrown about, my hair loose over my shoulders, and seeming to drive the skiff faster than anyone could credit, my father has often said I resembled a phantom rather than a mortal girl. I drew near the bank, he caught the boat, I bounded ashore, and in an instant I was clasped in his arms.

Then life began afresh for me. Everything around was transformed from dreary sameness to a scene of sparkling delight. The bliss I tasted with my father’s companionship surpassed even my most hopeful dreams. We were constantly together, and our conversations never ran dry. He had spent the sixteen years of absence among nations scarcely known in Europe, journeying through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, pressing into the native dwellings with a freedom granted to few Europeans. His tales of their customs, his anecdotes and descriptions of the landscapes, filled charming hours when we paused from discussing our own plans for the future.

The sound of tender feeling was so strange to me that I listened rapturously as he recounted what he had endured for me during those long years that had seemed neglect. “At first,” he said, “I could not bring myself to think of my poor little girl; later, as sorrow subsided and hope returned, my thoughts could rest only on her, and in city or desert, her tiny fairy figure, as I pictured it, fluttered ever before me. The northern breeze that cooled me was sweeter and softer, for it seemed to carry a part of your spirit. I often imagined I would come back at once, take you with me to some fertile island where we might dwell in peace forever. While I journeyed homeward, my ardent hopes were knocked against so many fears; my impatience grew almost unbearable. I dared not imagine the sun and moon shining not on you alive but upon your grave. Yet no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my comfort, my hope.”"}

