          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"908d0e4fd905792b35699b4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With careful precision he drove a peg into the earth exactly where the beetle had dropped, then drew from his pocket a measuring tape. Fixing one end at that portion of the trunk nearest the peg, he unwound the tape down to the peg itself, and continued stretching it along the line already determined by those two points for another fifty feet\u2014Jupiter meanwhile clearing the brambles with the scythe. At the extremity thus reached, a second peg was planted, and a rough circle four feet across was traced with this as its center. Legrand seized a spade, handed another to Jupiter and a third to me, and urged us to begin digging without delay.\\n\\nTo be candid, I had little appetite for such sport at any time, and just then would most gladly have declined; evening was closing in, and I was already worn out from our earlier exertions. Yet there seemed no escape, and I feared that refusing would unsettle my poor friend\u2019s fragile composure. Had there been any chance of relying on Jupiter, I would have tried to haul the madman home by main force; but I knew too well the old servant\u2019s loyalty to expect his help in any struggle against his master. I felt sure that Legrand had absorbed some of those countless Southern fables about buried treasure, his fancy perhaps strengthened by the discovery of the scarab\u00e6us or by Jupiter\u2019s insistence that it was \u201ca bug of solid gold.\u201d A mind inclined toward mania could easily be swept away by notions that echo cherished prejudices, and I recalled his remark that the beetle was \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d Altogether I was sorely annoyed and perplexed, yet finally resolved to make a virtue of necessity\u2014to dig heartily and thus the sooner show the dreamer, by plain sight, the emptiness of his beliefs.\\n\\nOnce the lanterns were lit, all three of us set to work with an energy worthy of a saner undertaking; as their glare shone on our figures and tools, I could not help reflecting how picturesque we looked, and how odd and suspect our efforts would appear to any passer-by who might chance upon us.\\n\\nWe labored steadily with the spades for two solid hours.\"}"
