          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d482cf6d4a6a08a5f5e00812\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d \u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d \u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d \u201cYes, sir. You told me to wake you.\u201d \u201cOh! I remember. All right, all right.\u201d After a few sluggish tries to fall back asleep, which the servant cleverly prevented by poking the fire for five minutes, he got up, threw on his hat, and went out. He entered the Temple and revived himself by pacing King\u2019s Bench Walk and Paper Buildings twice before heading to Stryver\u2019s chambers. Stryver\u2019s clerk, who never joined these meetings, had gone home, so Stryver opened the door himself. He wore slippers, a loose dressing gown, and left his throat bare for comfort. His eyes showed the wild, strained lines common to heavy drinkers of his kind, marks you can trace through portraits of every drinking age, from Jeffries onward. \u201cYou\u2019re a little late, Memory,\u201d Stryver said. \u201cAbout the usual time; maybe fifteen minutes later.\u201d They went into a shabby room lined with books and strewn with papers, warmed by a roaring fire. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the chaos of papers a table gleamed with plenty of wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons. \u201cYou\u2019ve had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d \u201cTwo tonight, I think. I\u2019ve been dining with the client\u2014or watching him dine\u2014it\u2019s all the same!\u201d \u201cThat was a sharp point you used in the identification, Sydney. How did you hit on it? When did it occur to you?\u201d \u201cI thought he was a handsome fellow, and that I might have been much like him, if I\u2019d had any luck.\u201d Mr. Stryver laughed until his early paunch shook. \u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d With a sullen air, the jackal loosened his clothes, went into the next room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. Soaking the towels and partially wringing them out, he folded them onto his head in a way dreadful to see, sat down at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d \u201cNot much boiling down tonight, Memory,\u201d Stryver said cheerfully as he sorted through his papers. \u201cHow much?\u201d \u201cOnly two sets.\u201d \u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d \u201cThere they are, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d The lion stretched out on his back on a sofa on one side of the drinking table, while the jackal sat at his own littered table on the other side, bottles and glasses within easy reach.\"}"
