          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a675db365997ed14bc6b40d6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I would gladly tell all this and much more, but to be brief, I must have my reward. I long to return to my family and home, and as the price for any further communication from me\u2014considering the illumination I can cast on many important branches of physical and metaphysical science\u2014I must request, through the influence of your honorable body, a pardon for the crime I committed in the deaths of the creditors when I left Rotterdam. That is the purpose of this paper. Its bearer, an inhabitant of the moon whom I have persuaded and carefully instructed to serve as my messenger to the earth, will await your Excellencies\u2019 decision and bring back the requested pardon to me, if it can be obtained at all.\\n\\n\u201cI have the honor to be, etc., your Excellencies\u2019 most humble servant,\\n\\n\u201cHANS PFAALL.\u201d\\n\\nAfter finishing this remarkable document, Professor Rub-a-dub reportedly dropped his pipe in sheer surprise, and Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, having removed his spectacles, wiped them, and slipped them into his pocket, forgot his usual gravity and spun three times on his heel in pure astonishment and admiration. There was no doubt about it\u2014the pardon had to be secured. So swore Professor Rub-a-dub with a hearty oath, and so thought the illustrious Von Underduk as he took his colleague\u2019s arm and, without a word, set off home to decide on their course of action. Yet when they reached the burgomaster\u2019s door, the professor pointed out that, since the messenger had vanished\u2014likely terrified by the fierce looks of the Rotterdam burghers\u2014the pardon would be useless, as only a moon-man would undertake such a long voyage. The burgomaster agreed, and the matter seemed settled. Not so, however, the rumors and speculations. Once the letter was published, it sparked endless gossip and opinion. Some self-important people even made fools of themselves by claiming the whole affair was nothing but a hoax. But \u201choax\u201d for such people seems to be a catch-all term for anything beyond their understanding. For my part, I cannot imagine what evidence they used to make such a charge. Let us see what they say:\"}"
