          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cbfb9ed1197cc21b93945883\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We finally agreed to live together while I stayed in the city; and since my finances were less strained than his, I paid to rent and furnish a place whose style matched the rather theatrical gloom we both shared\u2014a timeworn, grotesque mansion, long abandoned thanks to superstitions we didn\u2019t bother to investigate, and now tottering toward collapse in a secluded, desolate corner of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad anyone known how we spent our days in that house, we would have been thought mad\u2014though likely harmlessly so. We were completely secluded. We let no visitors in. I had kept our address secret from all my former acquaintances, and it had been many years since Dupin had either known or been known in Paris. We existed entirely within ourselves.\\n\\nIt was one of Dupin\u2019s whims (what else can I call it?) to adore the night for its own sake; and, as with all his eccentricities, I quietly indulged him, surrendering myself to his wild fancies without reserve. The dark goddess could not stay with us always, but we could imitate her presence. At the first hint of dawn we shut all the grimy shutters of our old house, lighting a couple of tapers that were heavily perfumed yet gave off only the palest, ghastliest light. By their glimmer we occupied our minds in dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the arrival of real Darkness. Then we went out arm in arm, continuing the day\u2019s discussions or wandering far and wide until late, seeking in the wild lights and shadows of the crowded city that endless mental stimulation which quiet observation can offer.\\n\\nDuring these nights I couldn\u2019t help noticing, and admiring (though I should have expected it from his vivid imagination), Dupin\u2019s peculiar gift for analysis. He seemed eager to practice it\u2014if not exactly to show it off\u2014and freely admitted how much pleasure it gave him. He told me, with a low, amused laugh, that most men were transparent to him, and he liked to follow such claims with direct, startling displays of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. In those moments his manner was cold and distant; his eyes looked blank; and his voice, usually a warm tenor, climbed to a treble that might have sounded petulant were it not for the deliberateness and clarity of his speech.\"}"
