          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"79a59c39732d0c16ea327705\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou\u2019ve encouraged an artificial sense of soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am, unfit for someone of his station\u2014just as the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, who are practical philosophers, would tell you. What business have paupers with souls or spirits? It\u2019s enough that we let them have living bodies. If you had kept the boy on gruel, ma\u2019am, this would never have happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d cried Mrs. Sowerberry, lifting her eyes devoutly to the kitchen ceiling. \u201cThis is what comes of being generous!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s generosity to Oliver had amounted to bestowing upon him all the filthy scraps no one else would touch; so there was considerable meekness and self-sacrifice in her taking Mr. Bumble\u2019s heavy accusation to heart, although, to do her justice, she was entirely innocent in thought, word, or deed.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, when the lady lowered her gaze, \u201cthe only thing left to do now, that I can think of, is to keep him in the cellar for a day or two, until he\u2019s starved down a bit; then bring him out and keep him on gruel throughout the apprenticeship. He comes from bad blood. Highly excitable temperaments, Mrs. Sowerberry! Both the nurse and the doctor said that his mother forced her way here despite agony and obstacles that would have killed any well-disposed woman weeks sooner.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point in Mr. Bumble\u2019s speech, Oliver, catching just enough to know his mother was being mentioned, resumed kicking with such ferocity that every other sound was drowned out. Mr. Sowerberry returned at that moment. Oliver\u2019s offense was explained to him with the embellishments the ladies thought best to inflame his anger, and he unlocked the cellar door in an instant, dragging his rebellious apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes had been torn during the beating he\u2019d taken; his face was bruised and scratched, and his hair hung in wild strands over his forehead. The fiery flush still burned, however; and when he was hauled from his prison, he scowled boldly at Noah, looking quite undaunted.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a fine young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, shaking Oliver and boxing his ear.\\n\\n\u201cHe called my mother names,\u201d Oliver said.\\n\\n\u201cWell, what if he did, you ungrateful brat?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe deserved what he said, and worse.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe didn\u2019t,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry burst into a torrent of tears.\\n\\nThis torrent gave Mr. Sowerberry no choice. Had he hesitated for even a moment to punish Oliver savagely, it would be obvious to every seasoned reader that he would have been, by every precedent in marital disputes, a brute, an unnatural husband, an insulting wretch, a base imitation of a man, and a score of other agreeable titles too many to list within the bounds of this chapter.\"}"
