          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ddb01af606556b2c8289cb52\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut, in fact, when we ascend to any given altitude, the mass of air we leave beneath us in any further ascent is by no means proportional to the extra height climbed (as is clear from what I explained earlier), but steadily decreases. It is therefore clear that, no matter how high we go, we cannot literally reach a boundary beyond which there is no atmosphere. I argued it must exist, even if it exists in a state of infinite thinness. \u201cOn the other hand, I knew that many arguments attempt to prove a real and definable limit to the atmosphere, beyond which there is absolutely no air. Yet a consideration overlooked by those who defend such a limit seemed to me, though not a definitive refutation of their belief, a point worth very serious investigation. When we compare the intervals between Encke\u2019s comet\u2019s successive arrivals at perihelion, after accounting with utmost precision for all disturbances caused by planetary attraction, we find the periods gradually shortening; that is, the major axis of the comet\u2019s ellipse shrinks, slowly but with perfect regularity. This is exactly what should occur if the comet experiences resistance from an extremely rare ethereal medium pervading the region of its orbit. For it is clear that such a medium, by slowing the comet\u2019s speed, would increase its centripetal force by weakening its centrifugal force. In other words, the sun\u2019s attraction would constantly gain strength, drawing the comet closer with each revolution. Indeed, there is no other way to explain this variation. Yet again: the actual diameter of the comet\u2019s nebulosity contracts rapidly as it approaches the sun and expands just as rapidly as it heads toward aphelion. Was I not justified in agreeing with M. Valz that this apparent decrease in volume originates from compression by the same ethereal medium I just mentioned, which is only denser the nearer it lies to the sun? The lens-shaped phenomenon called the zodiacal light also deserved careful thought. This glow, so striking in the tropics and impossible to mistake for any meteoric brightness, rises obliquely from the horizon and generally follows the sun\u2019s equator. It seemed to me clearly a kind of rare atmosphere extending outward from the sun, beyond at least the orbit of Venus, and, I believed, indefinitely farther. Indeed, I could not suppose this medium was limited to the comet\u2019s orbital path or to the sun\u2019s immediate surroundings.\u201d\"}"
