          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8fd088e55006be2ac8a2644\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The party separated into two adjoining benches, Anne seated among those in front, while Mr Elliot, aided by his ally Colonel Wallis, managed to secure the spot beside her. Miss Elliot, encircled by her cousins and the central recipient of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attentions, felt perfectly satisfied.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s spirits were well suited for an evening\u2019s amusement; it occupied her mind just enough. She could indulge the tender, laugh at the lively, heed the learned, and endure the tedious, and she had never enjoyed a concert more, at least through the first act. Near its end, during the pause after an Italian song, she explained its words to Mr Elliot. They shared a concert bill between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201ccaptures nearly the sense\u2014or really the meaning\u2014of the lines, for surely the sense of an Italian love-song is not to be discussed; yet it is as close as I can render it, since I make no claim to knowing the language. My Italian is very poor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I see it,\u201d he replied. \u201cYou clearly know nothing about it. You possess only enough Italian to turn these inverted, rearranged, clipped lines into plain, intelligible, polished English at first sight. There is no need to protest your ignorance further. The evidence is before us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot argue against such gracious praise; but I should dread undergoing a real scholar\u2019s scrutiny.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not enjoyed the privilege of visiting Camden Place so long,\u201d he rejoined, \u201cwithout learning something of Miss Anne Elliot; and I consider her as one whose modesty prevents the world from recognizing half her talents, and whose talents are more than modesty would allow any other woman naturally to possess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame, for shame! Such flattery is excessive. I forget what comes next,\u201d she said, turning back to the bill.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d Mr Elliot murmured, \u201cI have known your character longer than you suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! In what way? You could only know of me since my arrival in Bath, unless you heard of me earlier through my own relations.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was acquainted with you by report long before you reached Bath. I had listened to those who knew you well describe you. I have been familiar with your character for many years. Your person, your mind, accomplishments, demeanor\u2014they were all known to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr Elliot achieved the effect he sought. No one can resist the allure of such mystery.\"}"
        }
      ],
