{"request_id":"b0e1af397c2deda7139bff35","rewritten_text":"Words can hardly capture the ecstatic joy I felt when I turned away from the political fights at home and the physical sufferings of far-off lands, back to my own beloved house—a chosen haven of goodness and love, of peace and every sacred bond of sympathy. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings wouldn’t have burned so brightly; but in Greece I had been consumed by fear and wrenching change. There, after a stretch of anxiety and sorrow, I witnessed the departure of two souls whose very names meant greatness and virtue. Such griefs could never invade the family circle that remained, while we lived secluded in our cherished forest, passing our days in tranquility. Time, of course, left its subtle marks; as the years went by, it etched mortality into our pleasures and expectations. Idris, the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend, was a devoted and loving mother. This role wasn’t a pastime for her as it is for many; it was a passion. We had three children; the middle one died while I was in Greece. That loss dimmed the triumphant, ecstatic joys of motherhood with grief and dread. Before that, the little lives born of her own seemed to hold an unquestioned right to their future; now she lived in fear that the relentless destroyer might snatch her remaining treasures as it had their brother. The slightest illness sent waves of terror through her; she couldn’t stand to be away from them even for a short time. She had stored her entire fortune of happiness in their fragile lives, keeping constant watch lest some sly thief once again steal these precious gems. Fortunately, she had little reason to fear. Alfred, now nine, was a straight, sturdy little boy with a radiant forehead, gentle eyes, and a kind yet independent spirit. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheek glowed with health, and his restless energy filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had outgrown that silent, ignorant stage that had most stirred Idris’s fears. Clara was beloved by her and by everyone. She blended intelligence with innocence, sensitivity with patience, seriousness with pure good humor, and her extraordinary beauty was joined to such endearing simplicity that she gleamed like a pearl in the shrine of our family treasures, a gift of wonder and perfection.

At the start of winter, Alfred—now nine—went to school at Eton for the first time. To him, this seemed the very first step toward manhood, and he was excited in proportion. Sharing studies and play with others brought out the best in him: his steady determination, his generosity, and his well-directed strength of will."}

