          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"644e470dac574005f00efc13\",\"rewritten_text\":\"About ten minutes later the tea-bell sounded, and, as Virginia failed to come down, Mrs. Otis sent one of the footmen to fetch her. After a short interval he returned, saying he could not find Miss Virginia anywhere. Since she usually went out into the garden each evening to gather flowers for the dinner table, Mrs. Otis felt no concern at first; but when six o\u2019clock struck and there was still no sign of Virginia, she grew genuinely distressed. She dispatched the boys to search the grounds while she and Mr. Otis went through every room in the house. By half-past six the boys were back, reporting they could discover no trace of their sister. Everyone was now in a state of extreme agitation, uncertain how to proceed, when Mr. Otis suddenly recollected that a few days earlier he had granted a band of gypsies permission to camp in the park. He immediately set off for Blackfell Hollow, where he knew they had been, taking his eldest son and two farm-servants. The little Duke of Cheshire, nearly beside himself with worry, entreated to accompany them, but Mr. Otis refused, fearing there might be some disturbance. On reaching the clearing, he found the gypsies gone, and their departure plainly abrupt, for the fire still burned and plates lay scattered on the grass. After sending Washington and the two men to comb the neighbourhood, he hurried home and wired every police inspector in the county, instructing them to watch for a little girl said to have been abducted by tramps or gypsies. He then ordered his horse and, insisting that his wife and the three boys sit down to dinner, rode off along the Ascot road with a groom. He had scarcely covered a couple of miles when he heard a horse pounding behind him. Turning, he saw the little Duke racing up on his pony, flushed and bareheaded. \u201cI\u2019m dreadfully sorry, Mr. Otis,\u201d the boy panted, \u201cbut I can\u2019t touch dinner while Virginia is lost. Pray don\u2019t be cross with me. If you had let us become engaged last year, all this trouble would never have happened. You won\u2019t send me back, will you? I can\u2019t go! I won\u2019t go!\u201d The Minister could not help smiling at the handsome young rogue, and was deeply moved by his devotion to Virginia, so he leaned from his saddle, clapped him affectionately on the shoulders, and said, \u201cVery well, Cecil, if you refuse to return, I suppose you must ride with me. But I shall have to buy you a hat at Ascot.\u201d\"}"
