          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bc70aca7e53160b6587acc29\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft and he started counting, he saw he had collected eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also walked off with a three-gallon jug of whiskey he found under a wagon on his way home through the woods. The king said that, all things considered, it beat any day he\u2019d ever had in the missionary business. He said there was no point pretending otherwise\u2014heathens were nothing compared to pirates when you wanted to work a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke had thought he was doing pretty well until the king showed up, but after that he wasn\u2019t nearly as impressed with himself. He had set up and printed two small jobs for farmers at that printing office\u2014horse bills\u2014and pocketed four dollars. He had also taken in ten dollars\u2019 worth of advertisements for the paper, which he promised to run for four dollars if they paid upfront\u2014so they did. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he sold three subscriptions at half a dollar each on the condition they pay in advance; they were planning to pay with cordwood and onions like usual, but he claimed he\u2019d just bought the business, cut the price as low as he could, and was going to run it for cash. He set up a little poem he made himself\u2014three verses\u2014kind of sweet and sad, called \u201cYes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart\u201d\u2014and he left it all set up ready to print without charging a cent. All told, he took in nine dollars and a half and said he\u2019d earned it with a decent day\u2019s work.\\n\\nThen he showed us another little job he\u2019d printed and hadn\u2019t charged for because it was for us. It showed a picture of a runaway Black man with a bundle on a stick over his shoulder, with \u201c$200 reward\u201d underneath. The text was all about Jim and described him exactly. It said he ran away from St. Jacques\u2019 plantation forty miles below New Orleans last winter, and probably headed north, and that anyone who caught him and sent him back would get the reward plus expenses.\\n\\n\u201cNow,\u201d says the duke, \u201cafter tonight we can travel in daylight if we want. Whenever we see anyone coming we can tie Jim hand and foot with a rope, lay him in the wigwam, show this handbill, and say we caught him up the river. We can say we were too poor to ride a steamboat, so we got this little raft on credit from friends and are heading down to collect the reward.\u201d\"}"
