          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe77ae486ec250448ec8132f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Lady Windermere was giving her final reception before Easter, and Bentinck House seemed more thronged than ever. Six Cabinet Ministers had drifted over from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e wearing their stars and ribbons, every pretty woman displayed her most stylish gown, and at the far end of the picture-gallery stood the Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a stout Tartar-like figure with minute black eyes and superb emeralds, shouting dreadful French and laughing uproariously at every remark addressed to her. The assembly was certainly a remarkable mixture. Resplendent peeresses chatted amiably with fierce Radicals, popular preachers brushed shoulders with celebrated sceptics, a whole cluster of bishops endlessly pursued a plump prima-donna from room to room, several Royal Academicians lingered on the staircase disguised as artists, and rumor insisted that for a time the supper-room was completely overflowing with geniuses. Indeed, it ranked among Lady Windermere\u2019s finest evenings, and the Princess did not depart till close upon half-past eleven.\\n\\nWhen the Princess left, Lady Windermere went back to the picture-gallery, where a renowned political economist was earnestly expounding the scientific basis of music to an outraged Hungarian virtuoso, and began chatting with the Duchess of Paisley. She looked marvelously lovely, with her stately ivory throat, her large forget-me-not blue eyes, and the weighty masses of her golden hair. True _or pur_ it was\u2014not that pale straw shade which now presumes to claim the graceful name of gold, but the very gold woven into sunbeams or concealed within rare amber; it bestowed upon her face something of the halo of a saint, mingled with no small measure of a sinner\u2019s allure. She presented a curious psychological puzzle. Early on she had grasped the significant truth that nothing so closely resembles innocence as a misstep; through a succession of daring escapades, half of them entirely harmless, she had won all the privileges of having a personality. More than once she had changed her husband\u2014Debrett actually credits her with three marriages\u2014but as she never changed her lover, society long ago stopped whispering scandal. She was now forty, without children, and possessed of that insatiable hunger for pleasure which is the secret to perpetual youth.\\n\\nSuddenly she scanned the room with bright eagerness and exclaimed, in her clear contralto, \u201cWhere is my cheiromantist?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour what, Gladys?\u201d cried the Duchess, giving a startled jump.\\n\\n\u201cMy cheiromantist, Duchess; I cannot exist without him at the moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys! you are perpetually so inventive,\u201d murmured the Duchess, striving to remember what exactly a cheiromantist might be, and praying it was not identical with a chiropodist.\\n\\n\u201cHe comes to inspect my hand twice every week without fail,\u201d Lady Windermere went on, \u201cand he is endlessly fascinating about it.\u201d\"}"
