          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1520025e7128cfd9f52bdfed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was handed over to you. You see I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is the fact, assuredly,\u201d Defarge replied. His wife, still knitting and trilling, had brushed his elbow to remind him to respond, but always briefly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d the spy went on, \u201cthat his daughter came; and it was from your keeping that she took him, attended by a trim brown gentleman\u2014what is his name?\u2014with the little wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company\u2019s bank\u2014to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is the fact,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cMost interesting recollections!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou hear little of them now?\u201d he asked.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d Defarge answered.\\n\\n\u201cIn truth,\u201d Madame interposed, glancing up from her handiwork and her soft tune, \u201cwe hear nothing of them. We learned they arrived safely, and perhaps received one letter, perhaps two; but since then they have pursued their path in life\u2014we, ours\u2014and there has been no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so, madame,\u201d the spy returned. \u201cShe is about to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout to be?\u201d Madame echoed. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have married long before. You English are cold, I think.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see your tongue is,\u201d she answered; \u201cand what the tongue is, I presume the man is.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not appreciate the comparison; still, he let it pass with a laugh. Finishing his cognac, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is to be married. But not to an Englishman; to one who, like herself, is French by birth. And speaking of Gaspard\u2014ah, poor Gaspard! It was savage, savage!\u2014it is curious that she is going to wed the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was lifted to that lofty height of so many feet; in short, the current Marquis. But he lives concealed in England; there he is no Marquis, but Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge knitted unruffled, yet the news visibly unnerved her husband. Behind the little counter, try as he might with striking a light and kindling his pipe, he was unsettled, and his hand failed him. The spy would not have earned his name if he had not noticed and fixed the fact in his mind.\\n\\nHaving at least scored this single point, whatever value it might hold, and no new patrons arriving to offer fresh opportunity, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and withdrew; taking care, before departing, to remark with courtesy that he anticipated the pleasure of meeting Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
