          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ebfdaa804bebdebb38467e79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My father had lived, and his memory sustained my very existence. I might feel gratitude to others, but I could never again love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering; even my pleasures were merely endured, never enjoyed. I was like a solitary hollow among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep, black cliffs where no warmth could reach and from which there was no escape to brighter fields. So it happened that although friendship\u2019s spirit soothed me for a moment, it could not restore me. It came like some gentle visitation; it departed and I hardly felt the loss. The spirit of life was dead within me; do not be surprised, then, that when it arrived I did not welcome the best gift of heaven\u2014a friend\u2014more gladly, or grieve more bitterly when it left. My friend\u2019s name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story so you may judge how cold my heart must have been not to be revived by his eloquence and tender sympathy; and how, since he too was profoundly unhappy, we might have been a comfort to each other had I not been hardened to stone by the Medusa head of Misery. Woodville\u2019s misfortunes did not pierce the heart as mine did; his grief was natural, meant not to destroy but to purify the heart, and once its shadow moved past him, he might shine brighter and happier than before. Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of those rare souls whom fortune favors from birth, on whom she lavishes every gift of intellect and appearance without limit, and whom she guards so closely that no flaw, however slight, nor disappointment, however brief, can touch them. She seemed to fashion his mind with an excellence no dross could tarnish, and his understanding was such that no error could corrupt it. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a brilliant star in the east, all eyes turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet\u2014a word so often degraded that it fails to convey his greatness. He resembled one of the ancient poets crowned by the muses in their cradle and fed with honey by bees. As he moved among other men he seemed wrapped in a heavenly halo that set him apart and raised him above them. His surpassing beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and his words, whose rich tones enveloped the listener in silent and ecstatic wonder, set him above everyone else, so that in his presence they seemed born only to serve his superior brilliance.\"}"
