          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f998559129ca9398697f35f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He let her know that Frank admired her greatly\u2014thought her very beautiful and very charming; and with so much to recommend him overall, she felt she ought not to judge him too harshly. As Mrs. Weston remarked, \u201call young people will have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nThere was one person among his new acquaintances in Surrey who was not so forgiving. Throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury he was generally judged with considerable generosity; people made allowances for the small excesses of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so frequently and bowed so gracefully; but there was one spirit among them that could not be softened from its critical stance by bows or smiles\u2014Mr. Knightley. The incident was reported to him at Hartfield; for a moment he said nothing, but Emma almost immediately heard him mutter to himself over the newspaper in his hand, \u201cHum! exactly the trivial, foolish fellow I always thought him.\u201d She was half inclined to take offense; but a moment\u2019s observation convinced her he spoke only to relieve his own feelings and not to provoke anyone, so she let it pass.\\n\\nEven though they were, in one respect, bearers of unwelcome news, Mr. and Mrs. Weston\u2019s visit that morning was particularly timely in another. Something happened while they were at Hartfield that made Emma seek their advice; and, even luckier, she wanted exactly the advice they offered.\\n\\nThis was the situation: The Coles had been settled in Highbury for some years and were very decent people\u2014friendly, generous, and unpretentious; yet they were of low origins, engaged in trade, and only moderately genteel. When they first moved to the country they lived within their means, quietly, entertaining little company and that very modestly; but over the past year or two their finances had improved considerably\u2014their town business had produced greater profits, and fortune in general had favored them. With their wealth, their ambitions grew: they wanted a larger house and more society. They enlarged their house, increased their household staff, and expanded their expenses in every direction; and by now, in wealth and lifestyle, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their passion for company and their new dining room prepared everyone for their entertaining dinner guests; several parties, mostly involving single men, had already been held. Emma could scarcely imagine they would presume to invite the most established families\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing could induce her to go if they did; and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal less meaningful than she wished.\"}"
