          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b29f7a816b310f1a2a3f94d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Upon my return, I discovered this letter from my father:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have likely been impatient for a letter fixing the date of your homecoming, and at first I intended to send only a few lines naming the day I expected you. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I cannot do it. How great would be your shock, my son, if, anticipating a joyful welcome, you instead met tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I recount our calamity? Distance cannot have dulled your sense of our happiness and sorrow; how shall I inflict such pain on my long-absent child? I long to prepare you for the dreadful news, yet I know it is impossible; even now your eyes fly over the page in search of the words that bear the horrific message.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that dear little boy whose smiles enlivened and warmed my heart, so gentle yet so merry! Victor, he has been murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not attempt consolation, but merely describe the circumstances.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th), my niece, your two brothers, and I walked in Plainpalais. The evening was mild and tranquil, and we extended our stroll farther than usual. It had already grown dusk before we considered returning, and then we realized William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were missing. We sat down to wait for them. Presently Ernest came and asked if we had seen his brother; he said they had been playing together, that William had darted off to hide, that he searched in vain and afterward waited a long time, but William did not return.\\n\\n\u201cThis tale alarmed us, and we continued searching until darkness, when Elizabeth guessed he might have gone home. He was not there. We set out again with torches; I could not rest while imagining my darling boy lost and subject to the night\u2019s damps and dews; Elizabeth suffered torment as well. Around five in the morning I found my lovely child, whom I had seen the evening before full of life and health, lying on the grass pale and motionless; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was carried home, and the anguish plain on my face revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She was desperate to view the body. At first I tried to hold her back, but she insisted, and entering the room where he lay, she quickly examined the victim\u2019s throat, then wrung her hands and cried, \u2018O God!\u2019\u201d}\"}"
