{"request_id":"55eb0f5fbc5b3bf7f7246b50","rewritten_text":"Years ago, I became close friends with Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a series of misfortunes left him destitute. To escape the humiliation that followed, he left New Orleans, his ancestral city, and settled on Sullivan’s Island near Charleston, South Carolina.

The island is remarkable. It is little more than sea sand, roughly three miles long. Nowhere is it wider than a quarter mile. A barely noticeable creek separates it from the mainland, seeping through a tangle of reeds and muck that marsh hens favor. As you might expect, vegetation is sparse or stunted. No large trees grow there. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands and a few shabby frame houses shelter Charleston’s summer refugees from dust and fever, you can find the bristly palmetto. But aside from that point and a strip of hard white beach along the sea, the island is covered in dense sweet myrtle, the shrub English gardeners prize. Here, it often grows fifteen or twenty feet high and forms an almost impenetrable thicket, filling the air with its scent.

Deep within this thicket, not far from the island’s more remote eastern end, Legrand built a small hut, and he was living there when I first met him by chance. Our acquaintance quickly deepened into friendship—there was much about this recluse to inspire interest and respect. I found him educated, with unusual intellect, but afflicted with misanthropy and prone to alternating fits of enthusiasm and melancholy. He owned many books, yet rarely used them. His main pastimes were hunting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells or insect specimens—an entomological collection that might have made Swammerdamm envious. On these outings he was usually accompanied by an old Black servant named Jupiter, who had been freed before the family’s downfall but could not be persuaded, by threats or rewards, to give up what he saw as his right to attend his young “Massa Will.” It is likely Legrand’s relatives, thinking him somewhat unbalanced, had encouraged Jupiter’s stubborn loyalty so the old man could watch over and guard the wanderer."}

