          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e19f6468d2928c1dd375161\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAh, I mistook your meaning. I imagined you said \u2018anybody,\u2019 therefore I supposed that meant\u2014well, anybody; in other words, everybody.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt doth\u2014so long as the party be of exalted lineage; all the better if he be royal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn sooth, that seemeth reasonable,\u201d replied the abbot, spying his chance to smooth matters and stave off disaster, \u201cfor it were scarce likely that so marvelous a boon should be granted for the unveiling of the affairs of folk of mean degree, rather than for those born hard by the summits of greatness. Our Arthur the king\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWouldst thou hear of him?\u201d the enchanter cut in.\\n\\n\u201cMost joyfully, yea, and with thanks.\u201d\\n\\nInstantly everyone was steeped in awe and curiosity once more, the hopeless simpletons. They gloated over the charms and turned toward me with a \u201cWell then, what have you to answer to that?\u201d expression, once the pronouncement came:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, worn out with the chase, lies these two hours in his palace, tasting dreamless sleep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHeaven\u2019s blessing rest upon him!\u201d murmured the abbot, crossing himself; \u201cmay that slumber renew both flesh and spirit.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so it might, were he indeed sleeping,\u201d said I, \u201cbut the king is not asleep; he is in the saddle.\u201d\\n\\nFresh trouble\u2014clashing authorities once more. None knew which of us deserved credence; my name yet held some weight. The wizard\u2019s contempt awoke, and he declared:\\n\\n\u201cLo, I have met many marvelous seers and prophets and magicians in my days, yet never one who could sit idle and descry the heart of things without an incantation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have dwelt in forests and have missed much. I myself employ charms, as these brethren can attest\u2014but only when the matter is worth it.\u201d\\n\\nWhen it comes to dealing out satire, I fancy I can hold my side up. That thrust made the fellow writhe. The abbot inquired concerning the queen and the court, and received this answer:\\n\\n\u201cThey, too, lie asleep, their bodies spent even as the king\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\nI retorted:\\n\\n\u201cThat is just another falsehood. Half of them are busy with their diversions, and the queen with the rest are not asleep\u2014they ride. Very well, perhaps now you can spread your talents somewhat and inform us whither the king and queen, and all riding with them, are bound at this moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey sleep now, as I have declared; but on the morrow they shall ride, for they start a journey toward the sea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd by vespers of the day after tomorrow, where shall they be?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFar to the north of Camelot, with the half of their way accomplished.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is yet another lie, erring by the measure of a hundred and fifty miles.\u201d\"}"
