          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9c66b8149664585c8c6b5a4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood heavens!\u201d the Duchess muttered to herself. \u201cHe really is some sort of chiropodist after all. How dreadful. I do hope he\u2019s a foreigner, at least. It wouldn\u2019t seem quite so awful then.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI simply must introduce him to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIntroduce him!\u201d cried the Duchess. \u201cYou can\u2019t mean he\u2019s here?\u201d She began hunting for a small tortoiseshell fan and a very worn lace shawl so she could slip away at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u201cOf course he\u2019s here. I would never dream of having a party without him. He says I have a perfect psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been the tiniest bit shorter, I would have been a hopeless pessimist and retired to a convent.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I see!\u201d said the Duchess in obvious relief. \u201cHe\u2019s a fortune-teller, I take it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd a misfortune-teller too,\u201d Lady Windermere replied. \u201cA whole heap of them. Next year, for example, I am in deadly danger both on land and at sea, so I\u2019m going to live in a balloon and haul up my dinner in a basket every evening. It\u2019s all written there on my little finger\u2014or on the palm of my hand\u2014I forget which.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut surely that\u2019s tempting Providence, Gladys.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Duchess, surely Providence can resist temptation by now. I think everyone ought to have their hands read once a month, just to know what not to do. Naturally one goes ahead and does it anyway, but it\u2019s delightful to be warned. Now, if someone doesn\u2019t go fetch Mr. Podgers at once, I\u2019ll have to go myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAllow me, Lady Windermere,\u201d said a tall, handsome young man who had been standing nearby, listening with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u201cThank you so much, Lord Arthur, but I\u2019m afraid you would never recognize him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he\u2019s as remarkable as you claim, Lady Windermere, I can hardly miss him. Describe him to me, and I\u2019ll bring him to you at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, he\u2019s not in the least like a chiromantist. I mean, he isn\u2019t mysterious, or esoteric, or romantic-looking. He\u2019s a short, stout little man with a comical bald head and large gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014something between a family doctor and a country solicitor. I\u2019m terribly sorry, but it isn\u2019t my fault. People are so exasperating. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets look exactly like pianists. Last season I even invited a notorious conspirator to dinner\u2014a man who had blown up any number of people, always wore a coat of mail, and kept a dagger up his sleeve\u2014and do you know, when he arrived he looked just like a kindly old clergyman and cracked jokes all evening?\u201d\"}"
