          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b465aba66c944613d42ac7fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short but held himself very straight, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his lecture by summing up the history of chemistry and the improvements made by learned men, speaking with passion as he named the most famous discoverers. Then he briefly described the current state of the science and explained many basic terms. After a few introductory experiments, he ended with a tribute to modern chemistry, words I will never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised impossibilities and achieved nothing. The modern masters promise little; they know metals cannot be transformed and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem made only to handle dirt, and whose eyes peer endlessly into microscopes and crucibles, have performed miracles. They reach into nature\u2019s hidden places and show how she works in secret. They climb to the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new, almost unlimited powers; they can command the thunder, imitate the earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nSuch were the professor\u2019s words\u2014rather, the words of fate\u2014spoken to destroy me. As he continued I felt as if my soul were wrestling a solid enemy; each key that formed the mechanism of my being was struck; chord after chord sounded, until my mind filled with one thought, one idea, one purpose. So much has been done, cried the soul of Frankenstein\u2014more, far more, will I achieve; following the steps already marked, I will pioneer a new path, reveal unknown powers, and uncover for the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\\n\\nI did not close my eyes that night. My inner self was in rebellion and turmoil; I felt that order would eventually rise from it, but I lacked the power to bring it about. Gradually, after dawn, sleep came. When I awoke, my thoughts from the previous night seemed like a dream. What remained was a resolve to return to my former studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. That same day I visited M. Waldman. In private his manners were even gentler and more appealing than in public, for the dignity he showed in the lecture hall was at home replaced by warmth and kindness.\"}"
