          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a2a999154277b44dfa52d1d1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and fully armored horses couldn\u2019t move very fast. They would do the best they could, and now that the ground was firm, with no snow or mud, they would probably keep up a seven-mile pace. They would have to switch horses twice, and they\u2019d arrive around six, or a little after; there\u2019d still be plenty of daylight. They would spot the white cloth I planned to tie around my right arm, and I would take charge. We\u2019d surround the prison and have the king out in no time. It would look dramatic and picturesque enough, all things considered, though I would have preferred noon for its more theatrical flair.\\n\\nNext, to add to my options, I figured I\u2019d track down some of the people I once recognized and reveal myself to them. That could get us out of this mess without involving the knights. But I had to be cautious; it was risky. I needed luxurious clothing, and I couldn\u2019t just leap into it. I had to work up to it gradually, buying suit after suit from shops far apart, choosing something a little finer each time, until I finally reached silk and velvet and was ready for my plan. So I set out.\\n\\nBut the plan collapsed instantly. The first corner I turned, I ran smack into one of our slaves, poking around with a watchman. I coughed right then, and he shot me a look that cut to my core. I\u2019m pretty sure he thought he\u2019d heard that cough before. I ducked into a shop immediately and moved along the counter, asking prices while keeping an eye on them. They had stopped, talking together and peering in through the doorway. I decided to slip out the back way, if there was one, and asked the shopwoman if I could go back there to search for the escaped slave rumored to be hiding somewhere behind the shop. I told her I was an officer in disguise, and my partner was out front with one of the murderers in custody; would she be kind enough to tell him he didn\u2019t need to wait, but should hurry down to the far end of the back alley so he could block the fugitive when I flushed him out.\\n\\nShe was burning with excitement to see one of those already famous murderers, and she set off on the errand at once.\"}"
