          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c626d3461415ca9e8f70381c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and offered my apology. He replied, \u201cNot in the least,\u201d and continued. \\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, and you can imagine she was pursued as a splendid match. Her half-brother once more had plenty of resources, yet through debt and a fresh madness squandered them miserably all over again. The quarrel between him and her was more intense than the quarrel between him and his father, and it is suspected he nurtured a deadly grudge against her for having provoked their father\u2019s anger. Now I reach the cruel part of this tale\u2014only pausing, dear Handel, to point out that a dinner-napkin is not meant to be inserted into a tumbler.\u201d \\n\\nWhy I was trying to stuff mine into my tumbler I cannot conceive. I only know that with a determination worthy of a nobler cause, I was straining every effort to fold it within those boundaries. Once more I thanked him and expressed regret, and once more he said in the brightest tone, \u201cNot at all, I assure you!\u201d before going on. \\n\\n\u201cThere appeared in the picture\u2014at the races, or the assemblies, or any place you choose\u2014a certain man who wooed Miss Havisham. I never saw him (for this occurred five-and-twenty years ago, before our day, Handel), yet I heard my father say he was a flashy figure, well suited for the part. But that he wasn\u2019t to be confused, even by ignorance or prejudice, with a gentleman, my father stoutly insists; because his principle is that no man who isn\u2019t a gentleman at heart has ever been, since the world began, a gentleman in manner. He says no polish can conceal the wood\u2019s grain, and the more polish you add, the more the grain insists on showing. Well then! This man chased Miss Havisham diligently and professed utter devotion to her. I believe she had not displayed much warmth before that time; but whatever warmth she possessed blazed forth then, and she adored him. She unquestionably idolized him. He worked upon her love so methodically that he obtained great sums of money from her, and persuaded her to buy her brother out of his share in the brewery\u2014which weak kindness had left him at their father\u2019s death\u2014at an enormous price, using the argument that once he became her husband he must possess and administer the whole concern. Your guardian had no part in Miss Havisham\u2019s counsels at that time, and she was too proud and too much enamoured to take advice from anyone.\u201d\"}"
