          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3760b5c5e0175745280146a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Otis was quite upset when Lord Canterville refused, and asked him to change his mind, but the kindly peer was firm. At last he convinced the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift the ghost had given her, and when the young Duchess of Cheshire was introduced at the Queen\u2019s first drawing-room after her marriage in the spring of 1890, her jewels drew universal admiration. Virginia received the coronet that rewards all good little American girls, and married her childhood sweetheart as soon as he came of age. They were both so charming and so in love that everyone was pleased with the match, except the old Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had tried to secure the Duke for one of her seven unmarried daughters and had hosted no fewer than three costly dinner parties to that end, and, strangely, Mr. Otis himself. Mr. Otis was very fond of the young Duke personally, but in theory he disliked titles, and, as he said, \u201cwas not without fear that, under the weakening influence of a pleasure-loving aristocracy, the true principles of republican simplicity might be forgotten.\u201d His objections were completely overruled, however, and I believe that when he walked up the aisle of St. George\u2019s, Hanover Square, with his daughter on his arm, there was not a prouder man in all England.\\n\\nAfter the honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess went down to Canterville Chase, and the day after they arrived they walked in the afternoon to the lonely churchyard by the pine woods. There had been much trouble at first about the inscription on Sir Simon\u2019s tombstone, but at last they decided to engrave only the old gentleman\u2019s initials and the verse from the library window. The Duchess had brought some lovely roses, which she scattered on the grave, and after standing there awhile they wandered into the ruined chancel of the old abbey. The Duchess sat on a fallen pillar, while her husband lay at her feet, smoking a cigarette and gazing up at her beautiful eyes. Suddenly he tossed away the cigarette, took her hand, and said, \u201cVirginia, a wife should have no secrets from her husband.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Cecil! I have no secrets from you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, you do,\u201d he replied with a smile. \u201cYou have never told me what happened when you were shut up with the ghost.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have never told anyone, Cecil,\u201d Virginia said gravely.\"}"
