{"request_id":"ecdea526a24604cadca8c86b","rewritten_text":"The following day he remained indoors and, in fact, lingered chiefly in his own chamber, sick with a frantic fear of death and yet apathetic toward life. The sense of being pursued, ensnared, driven to bay began to master him. If the tapestry merely stirred in the breeze, he started. The dead leaves that the wind dashed against the leaded glass struck him as emblems of his own spent resolves and furious remorse. When he closed his eyes, the sailor’s face glimmered once more through the mist-blurred pane, and dread seemed yet again to press its hand upon his heart.

Yet perhaps it had only been his imagination that summoned vengeance out of the darkness and marshaled the horrid shapes of chastisement before him. Real life was chaos, but the imagination followed a grim logic. Imagination set remorse hounding the heels of guilt. It was imagination that forced every crime to foster its distorted brood. In the ordinary world of fact, the wicked were not scourged, nor the virtuous rewarded. Success belonged to the strong, failure was foisted on the weak. That was the whole of it. Besides, had a stranger been lurking near the house, the servants or the keepers must have noticed. Had there been footprints on the flower-beds, the gardeners would have told him. Certainly, it had been nothing but fancy. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to slay him. He had sailed away to drown in some winter tempest. From that man, at any rate, he was secure. Why, the fellow did not know who he was, could not know. The mask of youth had preserved him.

And still, if it were only delusion, how dreadful that conscience could conjure such grisly specters, grant them bodily shape, and make them move before one’s eyes! What sort of existence would his be if, day after day, shades of his guilt prowled around him, mocked from hidden corners, murmured in his ear as he feasted, chilled him awake with frosty fingers as he slept! As the notion crept through his mind, he turned pale with fright, and the air seemed suddenly colder. Oh, in what insane hour had he killed his friend! How monstrous even the recollection of the scene! He saw it all once more. Each abhorrent detail returned with redoubled horror. Out of the black cavern of time, dreadful and wrapped in scarlet, the image of his sin arose."}

