          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d27c3d6835e259081fb29253\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, gravity would keep decreasing with the squares of the distances, and with my speed increasing greatly, I would eventually reach regions where the moon\u2019s pull would replace the earth\u2019s. With this in mind, I decided it was not worth carrying more supplies than would last forty days. During another moment of worry, I recalled accounts from balloon ascents at great heights. Aside from painful breathing, travelers felt severe discomfort in their heads and bodies, often with nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms that worsened the higher they went. Would these symptoms keep increasing, perhaps until death? I finally decided they would not. They came from the gradual loss of normal atmospheric pressure on the body, which swelled the surface blood vessels, rather than from any true disorder of the organs, as with breathing trouble when the air grows too thin to refresh the blood in the heart. Unless the blood failed to renew, I saw no reason life could not continue even in a vacuum, since the expansion and contraction of the chest, commonly called breathing, is a purely muscular action and the cause, not the result, of respiration. In short, I believed the body would adjust to the lack of air pressure and the pain would slowly fade; while it lasted, I trusted in my strong constitution to endure it. Thus, your Excellencies, I have shared some, though not all, of the reasons that led me to plan a journey to the moon. I will now tell you the outcome of an effort so bold in idea and so entirely unmatched in human history. When I reached the altitude already mentioned\u2014three miles and three quarters\u2014I tossed a handful of feathers from the car and saw that I still rose quickly enough, so I did not need to throw off ballast. I was glad, because I wanted to keep as much weight as possible with me, for reasons I will explain later.\"}"
