          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"88fa4ab2b8de96b206f3fc18\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 19th. This morning, to my great relief, around nine o\u2019clock, the moon\u2019s surface lay terrifyingly close, and my fears were at their peak, when the pump of my condenser finally showed clear signs that the atmosphere was changing. By ten I believed the air had grown much denser. By eleven, the apparatus needed very little attention, and at noon, though nervous, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding I suffered no harm, I opened the gum-elastic chamber and detached it from the car. As expected, the rash danger of this experiment brought on spasms and fierce headache. Yet these breathing difficulties were not severe enough to threaten my life, and I chose to bear them, knowing I was leaving them behind as I moved toward the denser layers near the moon. Still, my descent stayed violently swift, and I soon realized with alarm that, although I had likely been right about finding an atmosphere dense enough for the moon\u2019s mass, I was wrong to think even the surface air could support the heavy load in my balloon\u2019s car. It should have matched Earth\u2019s surface, with gravity on each body balancing the compressed atmosphere. My headlong fall proved it did not; the cause could only be those possible geological disturbances I mentioned earlier. Whatever the reason, I was now almost on the planet and dropping with dreadful force. I wasted no time: over went the ballast, then the water-kegs, then the condensing gear and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every object inside the car. It made no difference. I still plunged down at an awful speed and was no more than half a mile above the ground. As a last hope, after tossing away my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car loose, since it weighed a good deal, and clung with both hands to the netting. I barely had time to see that the landscape for as far as I could look was dotted thickly with tiny houses before I crashed headlong into the center of a fantastical city and into a huge crowd of ugly little people. Not one of them said a single word or tried in the least to help me. They simply stood like a bunch of fools, grinning ridiculously, staring sideways at me and my balloon, arms planted on their hips.\"}"
