          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"641dd706bc3396ca81fb0068\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was a little surprised by the match, but she spoke more about how sincerely she wished them happiness than about her astonishment, and Elizabeth could not convince her to see it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia felt no envy of Miss Lucas, because Mr. Collins was only a clergyman; the news mattered to them only as gossip to share in Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not help feeling triumphant at having a daughter well married, something she could now throw back at Mrs. Bennet, and she visited Longbourn more often than usual to say how happy she was, even though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour expressions and spiteful remarks might have been enough to chase happiness away.\\n\\nThere was a reserve between Elizabeth and Charlotte that kept them both silent on the subject, and Elizabeth was convinced that real trust could never exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her turn with greater affection to her sister, whose integrity and grace she was sure she could never doubt, and for whose happiness she grew more worried by the day, because Bingley had been gone a week with no word of returning.\\n\\nJane had replied to Caroline\u2019s letter right away and was counting the days until she might reasonably expect another. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks arrived on Tuesday, addressed to their father and written with all the solemn formality that a year\u2019s stay in the family might have inspired. Having eased his conscience on that matter, he went on to declare with abundant enthusiasm how happy he was to have secured the affection of their charming neighbor, Miss Lucas, and then explained that he had been so ready to accept their invitation to Longbourn solely in hopes of enjoying her company; he hoped to return on Monday fortnight. Lady Catherine, he added, so wholeheartedly approved of his marriage that she wanted it to happen as soon as possible