          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4675014d2f6e274e327292a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then I murmured over to myself that enchanting passage that recounts Dante\u2019s entrance into the earthly Paradise, imagining how blissful it would be, when I wandered along those lovely banks, to watch the chariot of light descend bearing my long-lost parent back to me. While I waited with that expectation, I imagined twisting a garland from the flowers growing there and crowning myself in delight: I would sing sul margine d\u2019un rio, my father\u2019s favourite song, and believed my voice drifting through the still air would carry to whatever bower he occupied, awaiting our reunion, the news that his daughter had come. The traces of sorrow would vanish from my brow, and I should lift my eyes without fear to meet his, always radiant with the gentle light of innocent affection. When I recalled the spell of those deep eyes I began to weep, though softly, lest my sobs should mar the fairy vision.\\n\\nSo completely was I absorbed in that dream that I walked on heedless until I bent to pluck a bloom for my wreath on that desolate plain where no flowers grew, and then I woke from the reverie to find myself I knew not where.\\n\\nThe sun had already gone down, and the rosy light the clouds had borrowed from his setting had all but faded. A wind swept the plain; I looked around and saw nothing by which to gauge my location; I was lost, and my efforts to recover the path were fruitless. I continued wandering, while the advancing dusk blurred any trace that might have guided me. Soon everything was draped in the profound shadow of the darkest night; I grew tired, and since my servant would sleep that night at the neighboring village, so no one would be alarmed at my absence, and I knew I was safe from intruders in that solitary region, I resolved to remain where I was. Truly I was too exhausted to go further: the air was cold, but bodily discomfort was nothing to me, and I fancied I had