          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa61f2e2776ed04cbc05e7bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water to end the feast with. After a while Becky suggested that they move on again. Tom was quiet for a moment. Then he said:\\n\\n\u201cBecky, can you bear it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face grew pale, but she thought she could.\\n\\n\u201cWell then, Becky, we have to stay here, where there\u2019s water to drink. That little piece is our last candle!\u201d\\n\\nBecky broke into tears and wailing. Tom tried to comfort her, but it hardly helped. At last Becky said:\\n\\n\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and hunt for us!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will! Of course they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe they\u2019re hunting for us now, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, I reckon maybe they are. I hope so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen would they miss us, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen they get back to the boat, I guess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it might be dark then\u2014would they notice we hadn\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. But anyway, your mother would miss you as soon as they got home.\u201d\\n\\nA frightened look on Becky\u2019s face brought Tom to his senses, and he knew he had made a mistake. Becky wasn\u2019t supposed to go home that night. The children fell silent and thoughtful. A moment later Becky burst into tears again, and Tom knew she was thinking the same thing he was\u2014that Sunday morning might be half over before Mrs. Thatcher realized that Becky wasn\u2019t at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children fixed their eyes on their small candle and watched it melt slowly and mercilessly away, saw the half inch of wick stand alone at last, watched the weak flame rise and fall, climb the thin column of smoke, linger at its top a moment, and then\u2014utter darkness took over.\\n\\nNeither knew how long it was before Becky slowly realized she was crying in Tom\u2019s arms. All they knew was that, after what felt like a huge stretch of time, both awoke from a dead stupor of sleep and took up their misery again. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014maybe Monday. He tried to get Becky to talk, but her grief was too heavy, and all her hopes were gone. Tom said they must have been missed long ago, and that the search was surely underway. He would shout and maybe someone would come. He tried it, but in the darkness the distant echoes sounded so horrible that he didn\u2019t try again.\\n\\nThe hours slipped away, and hunger returned to torment the captives. Some of Tom\u2019s half of the cake was left; they shared it and ate it. But they felt hungrier than before.\"}"
