          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3947bcad42f5ac3934741dd0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I remembered how things were during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. Up North a carpenter made three dollars a day at gold value; down South he earned fifty\u2014paid in Confederate shinplasters that were worth about a dollar a bushel. In the North a pair of overalls cost three dollars, a single day\u2019s pay; in the South they cost seventy-five, equal to two days\u2019 wages. Everything else scaled the same way. So wages in the North were effectively twice as high as in the South, because Northern paychecks bought twice as much.\\n\\nI got to know various people in the village, and I was pleased to see our new coins circulating everywhere\u2014plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, plenty of cents, lots of nickels, some silver; all among the artisans and common folk; and even some gold, though that was in the bank, meaning the goldsmith\u2019s shop. I stopped in while Marco, son of Marco, was arguing with a shopkeeper over a quarter-pound of salt, and I asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They obliged\u2014after they\u2019d bitten the coin, rung it on the counter, tested it with acid, and interrogated me about where I got it, who I was, where I\u2019d come from, where I was headed, when I expected to arrive, and maybe a couple hundred more questions. When they ran out, I kept right on volunteering information: told them I owned a dog named Watch, that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, her grandfather was a Prohibitionist, I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip who died trusting in a glorious resurrection, and on and on until even that ravenous village busybody started looking satisfied and maybe a little annoyed. Still, he had to respect a man with my financial clout, so he didn\u2019t mouth off to me, though I noticed he took it out on his underlings, which was only natural. Yes, they broke my twenty, but I could tell it strained the bank, as you\u2019d expect\u2014it was like walking into a tiny nineteenth-century general store and suddenly asking the owner to change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might manage it, but he\u2019d be wondering why a small farmer was carrying that much cash, and I suspect the goldsmith thought the same. He followed me to the door and stood there watching me leave with reverent admiration.\"}"
        }
