          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bddc81bd7738829b2f4ffc10\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies rejoiced at seeing their dear friend once more, declared it an age since their last meeting, and again and again inquired how she had been occupying herself during their absence. The rest of the family received little notice; they shunned Mrs. Bennet as much as possible, spoke scarcely to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. They soon departed, springing up with an alacrity that astonished their brother, and hastening off as though to flee Mrs. Bennet\u2019s civilities.\\n\\nThe prospect of the Netherfield ball was exceedingly pleasing to every female in the house. Mrs. Bennet chose to regard it as a tribute to her eldest daughter and felt especially gratified that Mr. Bingley, rather than a formal card, delivered the invitation himself. Jane imagined a cheerful evening among her two friends and the attentions of their brother; Elizabeth anticipated dancing much with Mr. Wickham, and observing in Mr. Darcy\u2019s looks and conduct the confirmation of all she believed. The happiness Catherine and Lydia envisioned was less tied to any single circumstance or person; for though each, like Elizabeth, intended to dance half the night with Mr. Wickham, he by no means was the only partner capable of pleasing them, and, after all, a ball was simply a ball. Even Mary could tell her family that she felt no reluctance toward it.\\n\\n\u201cSo long as I may have my mornings to myself,\u201d she said, \u201cthat suffices. I do not think it any sacrifice to join occasionally in evening engagements. Society lays claims upon us all; and I count myself among those who deem intervals of diversion and amusement desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so lively on the occasion, that although she seldom addressed Mr. Collins without necessity, she could not resist asking whether he meant to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation, and, if so, whether he considered it proper to share in the evening\u2019s entertainments. She was somewhat astonished to discover he harbored no hesitation about it, and was far from fearing any reprimand, whether from the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh, for daring to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI by no means imagine, I assure you,\u201d said he, \u201cthat a ball of this nature, offered by a young man of character to respectable company, can carry the least ill effect; and so far from objecting to dance myself, I shall hope to be honoured with the hands of all my fair cousins during the evening; and I seize this moment to solicit yours, Miss Elizabeth, for the first two dances in particular; a preference which I trust my cousin Jane will ascribe to the proper motive, and not to any want of regard for her.\u201d\"}"
