          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e585ab91486b00b84d0e2cf3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sydney Carton paused in the street, uncertain where to head next. \u201cAt Tellson\u2019s bank at nine,\u201d he murmured thoughtfully. \u201cShould I show myself in the meantime? I think so. These people ought to know that I\u2019m here; it\u2019s sensible, and might be necessary preparation. But caution\u2014caution\u2014caution! Let me think this through!\u201d\\n\\nReining in the steps that had started toward one destination, he paced the darkening street and followed the thought to its possible outcomes. His first instinct held firm. \u201cIt\u2019s best,\u201d he said, firmly decided, \u201cfor these people to know there\u2019s such a man as me here.\u201d He turned his face toward Saint Antoine.\\n\\nDefarge had described himself that day as the proprietor of a wine-shop in the Saint Antoine suburb. Someone who knew the city, as Carton did, could find the place without asking. Once he confirmed its location, Carton left those cramped streets, ate at a small restaurant, and fell fast asleep after dinner. For the first time in years, he had no strong liquor. Since the previous night he had taken nothing stronger than a little thin wine, and the night before he had poured the brandy onto Mr. Lorry\u2019s hearth like a man who was done with it.\\n\\nIt was as late as seven when he woke refreshed and went back into the streets. On his way to Saint Antoine he paused at a shop window with a mirror and made slight adjustments to his loose cravat, his coat collar, and his unruly hair. That done, he went straight to Defarge\u2019s and stepped inside.\\n\\nOnly one customer was in the shop: Jacques Three, the man with nervous fingers and a rasping voice. Carton had seen him on the Jury. He stood drinking at the small counter, talking with the Defarges, husband and wife, while The Vengeance joined the conversation like an equal partner in the business.\\n\\nCarton walked in, took a seat, and requested\u2014in halting French\u2014a small measure of wine. Madame Defarge gave him a casual glance, then a sharper one, and another sharper still. She approached him herself and asked what he had ordered.\\n\\nHe repeated his request.\\n\\n\u201cEnglish?\u201d Madame Defarge asked, lifting her dark brows inquisitively.\\n\\nHe looked at her as if even a single French word took effort to form, then replied in the same strongly foreign accent as before.\"}"
