{"request_id":"e6d6abb25406b89580c03f73","rewritten_text":"Words cannot express the ecstatic joy with which I turned away from domestic political strife and the physical miseries of distant lands, to return to my own cherished home, to that chosen dwelling of goodness and love, to peace and the exchange of every holy sympathy. Had I never left Windsor, such feelings might never have grown so fervent; yet in Greece I had been haunted by dread and wretched change. There, after a season of anxiety and grief, I had witnessed the departure of two whose names alone signified greatness and virtue. Such woes could never infiltrate the domestic circle remaining to me, while, sheltered in our beloved woodlands, we spent our lives in serenity. True, the passing years brought slight changes even here, and time, as is its habit, marked the impress of mortality on our joys and hopes. Idris, the most affectionate of wives, sisters, and friends, was a tender and devoted mother. Motherhood was no diversion with her, as it is with many; it was a passion. We had three children; the second in age died while I was in Greece. This event mingled the triumphant delights of motherhood with sorrow and apprehension. Before, the little creatures sprung from her, the youthful heirs of her fleeting life, seemed to possess a secure tenure of existence; now she feared that the relentless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings as it had taken their brother. The slightest illness filled her with terror; she was wretched whenever absent from them. She had stored her treasure of happiness within their frail being, and remained ever watchful lest the stealthy thief should once more steal these precious gems. Luckily, she had little reason for alarm. Alfred, now nine, was an upright, manly little fellow, with a shining brow, gentle eyes, and a mild yet independent temperament. Our youngest was still a babe; his downy cheek bloomed with the roses of health, and his tireless merriment filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed beyond the age when mute ignorance inspired Idris’s anxieties. Clara was beloved by her, by all. Intelligence united with innocence, sensibility with endurance, seriousness with perfect good-humour, and a beauty so sublime paired with such captivating simplicity, that she seemed like a pearl enshrined among our possessions, a marvel of worth and excellence.

At the onset of winter our Alfred, nine years old, first entered Eton. He considered this the first stride toward manhood and delighted in it accordingly. Sharing study and play drew forth the noblest parts of his character—steady perseverance, generosity, and disciplined resolve."}

