          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"79498714a2ade3ad22932efa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\nNo answer.\\n\\n\u201cTOM!\u201d\\n\\nNo answer.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s become of that boy, I wonder? You, TOM!\u201d\\n\\nNo answer.\\n\\nThe old lady slid her spectacles down and peered over them around the room; then she pushed them up and peered beneath them. She hardly ever looked through them for something as small as a boy; they were her formal pair, her pride, built for \u201cstyle,\u201d not service\u2014she could have seen just as well through a couple of stove lids. She looked puzzled for a moment, then said, not angrily but still loud enough for the furniture to hear:\\n\\n\u201cWell now, if I get my hands on you I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe didn\u2019t finish, because by then she was bending down and jabbing the broom under the bed, and she needed her breath for the jabs. All she turned up was the cat.\\n\\n\u201cI never saw the like of that boy!\u201d\\n\\nShe went to the open door, stood there, and looked out over the tomato vines and jimson weeds that passed for a garden. No Tom. So she pitched her voice at a distance-setting and shouted:\\n\\n\u201cY-o-u-u, TOM!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a rustle behind her, and she turned just in time to grab a small boy by the back of his jacket and stop his escape.\\n\\n\u201cThere! I could\u2019ve guessed that closet. What were you doing in there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing! Look at your hands. And look at your mouth. What is that mess?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know, Aunt.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I do. It\u2019s jam\u2014that\u2019s what it is. Forty times I\u2019ve told you if you didn\u2019t leave that jam alone I\u2019d tan your hide. Hand me that switch.\u201d\\n\\nThe switch hovered in the air\u2014the danger was critical\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHey! Look behind you, Aunt!\u201d\\n\\nThe old lady spun around and snatched her skirts out of harm\u2019s way. The boy bolted instantly, scrambled up the high board fence, and vanished over it.\\n\\nHis Aunt Polly stood surprised a moment, then broke into a gentle laugh.\\n\\n\u201cConfound that boy, can\u2019t I ever learn? Hasn\u2019t he fooled me like that enough times that I ought to be ready for him by now? But old fools are the biggest fools of all. Can\u2019t teach an old dog new tricks, they say. But heavens, he never plays them the same way twice, and how\u2019s a body to know what\u2019s coming? He seems to know just how long he can tease me before I blow, and he knows if he can stall me for a minute or make me laugh, it\u2019s all over again and I can\u2019t land a lick on him.\u201d\"}"
