          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4d0e7f595a1331df9d436625\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhy, surely you don\u2019t mean\u2014\u201d my sister began.\\n\\n\u201cYes, I do, Mum,\u201d replied Pumblechook; \u201cbut hold on a moment. Go on, Joseph. Good man! Continue!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would folks present say,\u201d Joe went on, \u201cto twenty pound?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHandsome is the word,\u201d my sister answered.\\n\\n\u201cWell then,\u201d said Joe, \u201cit\u2019s more than twenty pound.\u201d\\n\\nThat abject hypocrite Pumblechook bobbed again, and remarked with a patronizing chuckle, \u201cIt\u2019s beyond that, Mum. Fine! Keep it up, Joseph!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen, to wind it up,\u201d Joe said, cheerfully handing my sister the bag, \u201cit\u2019s five-and-twenty pound.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s five-and-twenty pound, Mum,\u201d echoed that meanest of cheats, Pumblechook, standing to shake her hand; \u201cand it\u2019s only what you deserve (as I declared when they asked my opinion), and I wish you joy of it!\u201d\\n\\nHad the scoundrel stopped there, his offense would have been dreadful enough, but he darkened his guilt by proceeding to arrest me, assuming a patronage that cast all his earlier wrongdoing in the shade.\\n\\n\u201cNow you see, Joseph and wife,\u201d said Pumblechook, gripping my arm above the elbow, \u201cI\u2019m one of those who always carry through what they start. This lad must be bound, without delay. That\u2019s my way. Bound without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,\u201d my sister said (clutching the money), \u201cwe\u2019re greatly obliged.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t mind me, Mum,\u201d returned the infernal cornchandler. \u201cA pleasure\u2019s a pleasure everywhere. But this boy, you understand; we must have him bound. I said I\u2019d attend to it\u2014in honest truth.\u201d\\n\\nThe Justices sat in the nearby Town Hall, and we immediately crossed over to have me bound apprentice to Joe before their Magisterial honors. I say we crossed over, but Pumblechook shoved me across as though I had just picked a pocket or set a rick ablaze; indeed, the general notion in Court was that I\u2019d been caught in the act; for as Pumblechook hustled me along through the crowd, I heard some ask, \u201cWhat has he done?\u201d and others, \u201cHe\u2019s a youngster, but looks bad, doesn\u2019t he?\u201d A gentleman of gentle and kindly face even pressed on me a tract with a woodcut featuring a scowling youth tricked out with a whole sausage-shop of irons, titled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\\n\\nThe Hall struck me as a curious place, with higher pews than a church and people leaning over them to stare, and with mighty Justices (one with a powdered head) leaning back in their chairs, arms folded, or taking snuff, or nodding off, or writing, or reading the papers, and with a few glossy black portraits along the walls, which my untutored eye took for a mixture of hardbake and sticking plaster.\"}"
