{"request_id":"4abc55fa1b9e2d33124a977f","rewritten_text":"I remembered how things were during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North a carpenter earned three dollars a day, in gold value; in the South he earned fifty dollars—paid in Confederate paper worth a dollar a bushel. In the North a suit of overalls cost three dollars, one day’s wages; in the South it cost seventy-five, which was two days’ wages. Everything else matched those numbers. So wages were twice as high in the North because those dollars bought twice as much.

I made several acquaintances in the village, and it pleased me to see our new coins in use—plenty of milrays, mills, cents, many nickels, and some silver, all among the workers and common people. There was even some gold, though that stayed at the bank, or rather, the goldsmith’s shop. I dropped in while Marco, son of Marco, was arguing with a shopkeeper over a quarter pound of salt, and I asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They provided it—after chewing the coin, ringing it on the counter, testing it with acid, and asking where I got it, who I was, where I came from, where I was headed, when I expected to arrive, and maybe two hundred more questions. When they ran out, I kept going and offered all sorts of information on my own: that I owned a dog named Watch, that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, that her grandfather was a Prohibitionist, that I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip, who died in the hope of a glorious resurrection, and on and on until even that nosy villager began to look satisfied and a bit annoyed. Still, he had to respect someone with my finances, so he didn’t talk back, though I noticed he took it out on his assistants, which was only natural.

Yes, they changed my twenty, but I figured it strained the bank a little. That was expected; it was like walking into a shabby village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly demanding the owner change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might manage, but he would wonder why a small farmer carried so much cash, which was probably what the goldsmith thought of me. He followed me to the door and stood there staring after me with reverent admiration."}

