          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e444a1e2b2fc98215e4328c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day slipped by much as the previous one had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent several morning hours with the invalid, who kept improving, though gradually; and when evening came, Elizabeth joined them in the drawing-room. Yet the loo table was absent. Mr. Darcy sat writing, while Miss Bingley, placed close beside him, watched the evolution of his letter, constantly distracting him with messages intended for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were engaged at piquet, with Mrs. Hurst observing their play.\\n\\nElizabeth took up some needlework and found sufficient diversion in listening to the exchange between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant compliments\u2014on his penmanship, the straightness of his lines, or the length of his letter\u2014paired with the utter indifference with which he received them, made a curious conversational duet, fully matching Elizabeth\u2019s view of both participants.\\n\\n\u201cHow delighted Miss Darcy will be to get such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe offered no reply.\\n\\n\u201cYou write amazingly fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must need to write in a year! Business letters, too! I should think them dreadful!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is lucky, then, that they fall to me rather than to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease tell your sister I long to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already conveyed that, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI fear you dislike your pen. Let me mend it. I mend pens astonishingly well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow can you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe stayed silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am delighted to hear of her progress on the harp, and pray let her know I am entirely enraptured with her lovely little design for a table; I find it infinitely superior to Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you allow me to postpone your raptures until my next letter? At the moment I have no space to treat them properly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it does not signify. I shall see her in January. But do you always send her such charming long letters, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are generally long; whether always charming is not mine to judge.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is my rule that anyone who can write a long letter easily cannot write ill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat will never do as a compliment for Darcy, Caroline,\u201d her brother exclaimed, \u201cbecause he does not write with ease. He searches too hard for words of four syllables. Do you not, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy style differs very much from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d cried Miss Bingley, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless fashion imaginable.\u201d\"}"
