          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"30f45e7d790a378e8cba9d83\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Back home in my own era, that money was raised through tariffs, and people liked to imagine some foreign importer paid them; it soothed their minds to believe it. In reality Americans paid the duty themselves, and it was spread so evenly that a man worth a hundred million and the newborn child of a day laborer each shouldered exactly the same yearly burden\u2014six dollars apiece. I doubt anything could be fairer than that. Now, Scotland and Ireland both paid tribute to Arthur, and all the British Isles together held a little under a million souls. A mechanic earned about three cents a day when he covered his own food and lodging. Under that standard the national budget ran to ninety thousand dollars a year, or two hundred fifty a day. Therefore, by swapping gold for nickels during a king\u2019s-evil ceremony, I harmed no one, displeased nobody, and satisfied everyone, while slicing four-fifths off that day\u2019s public expense\u2014a savings equal to eight hundred thousand dollars in my America. I learned that move at a very distant source\u2014the wisdom of my boyhood\u2014because a true statesman never scorns wisdom however humble its beginnings. As a kid I saved my pennies and sent buttons to foreign missions. The button served the uninformed native just as well as coin, while the coin served me better than the button; everyone came out happy and no one got hurt.\\n\\nMarinel handled the patients as they showed up. He examined each applicant; if the fellow couldn\u2019t meet the requirement he was warned away, and if he could, he was ushered on to the king. A priest intoned, \u201cThey shall lay their hands on the sick, and they shall recover.\u201d Then the king stroked the infected sores while the scripture continued, and at the end the patient graduated with his nickel\u2014the king hung it around his neck personally\u2014and off he went. Does that sound like it would heal anyone? Yet it did. Any ritual will work if the patient\u2019s faith in it is strong enough. Near Astolat stood a chapel where the Virgin was said to have appeared to a goose-herding girl\u2014the girl herself swore it\u2014and they built the chapel on the spot and hung up a painting of the vision, a picture you\u2019d imagine would scare an invalid away. Instead, thousands of the infirm and the halt visited it every year, prayed before it, and departed cured; even the healthy could gaze on it without harm.\"}"
