          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a3bf955b67ccf35ebf5b734d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You\u2019d rouse up after a while and peer around to see what stirred you, and maybe catch a steamboat hacking her way upstream, so far over toward the opposite bank you couldn\u2019t make out a thing about her except whether she carried a stern-wheel or a side-wheel; then for near an hour there\u2019d be nothing to spy and nothing to hear\u2014just pure lonesomeness. Next a raft would glide past, way off in the distance, and likely there\u2019d be some fellow on it chopping, because that\u2019s near about all they ever do on a raft; you\u2019d see the axe glitter and drop\u2014you didn\u2019t hear a sound; you\u2019d watch it lift again, and by the time it was above his head you\u2019d catch the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014the sound had taken that whole stretch of time to cross the water. That\u2019s how we\u2019d spend a day, loafing around, listening to the hush. One time a heavy fog settled in, and every raft or scow drifting by was bashing on tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t plow into them. A scow or raft crept near enough we could hear their talk and swearing and laughing\u2014plain as anything; yet there was not a trace to see; it gave you the crawls, like ghosts fooling around in the air. Jim reckoned it truly was spirits; but I told him:  \u201cNo; spirits ain\u2019t going to say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d As soon as it turned night we pushed off; once we steered her to midstream we let her be, letting the current choose where she drifted; then we lit up our pipes, let our legs trail in the water, and jawed about all sorts of matters\u2014we stayed naked night and day whenever the skeeters allowed it\u2014the new clothes Buck\u2019s family fixed for me were too fine for comfort, and besides I never had much use for clothes anyhow. Sometimes we\u2019d have the whole river to ourselves for an age. Over yonder lay the banks and islands, across the water; and every so often a spark glowed\u2014that\u2019d be a candle in some cabin window; and at times you\u2019d see a spark or two on the water\u2014belonging to a raft or scow, you know; maybe a fiddle tune or a song would float across from one of those crafts. Living on a raft is a beautiful thing. We had the heavens above, all sprinkled with stars, and we\u2019d lie flat on our backs and stare up at them, arguing whether they\u2019d been made or merely happened. Jim insisted they were made, but I claimed they just happened; it seemed to me it would have taken too long to _make_ such a multitude.\"}"
