          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e200cfc1a3eea04525ef01a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the sight of it, the Ourang-Outang immediately leaped through the chamber door, ran down the stairs, and then, through a window that happened to be open, jumped into the street.\\n\\nThe Frenchman followed in despair. The ape, still clutching the razor, sometimes stopped to look back and make frantic gestures at him, waiting until he nearly caught up before bolting away again. The chase went on like this for a long time. The streets were silent, nearly three o\u2019clock in the morning. Passing down an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the fugitive caught sight of a light shining from the open window of Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s room on the fourth floor. Rushing to the building, it saw the lightning rod, climbed with unbelievable speed, grabbed the shutter flung flat against the wall, and swung itself straight onto the headboard of the bed. The whole maneuver took less than a minute. As it entered, the Ourang-Outang kicked the shutter open again.\\n\\nMeanwhile the sailor was both relieved and alarmed. He hoped he could finally recapture the animal, since it could barely get out of the trap it had entered except by climbing down the rod, where he might catch it. Yet he worried about what it might do inside the house. This fear pushed him to keep following. A lightning rod is easy to climb, especially for a sailor, but when he reached the level of the window, far to his left, he could do no more than lean over for a glimpse inside. That glance nearly made him lose his grip in horror. The night split with the terrible cries that had woken everyone on the Rue Morgue. Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, wearing their nightclothes, seemed to have been sorting papers in the iron chest already mentioned, which had been wheeled into the middle of the room. It stood open, its contents scattered on the floor. The victims must have been sitting with their backs to the window, and from the delay between the beast\u2019s entrance and the screams, it likely went unnoticed at first. The slam of the shutter would have been blamed on the wind.\\n\\nAs the sailor looked in, the huge creature had grabbed Madame L\u2019Espanaye by her loose hair and was waving the razor near her face, mimicking a barber\u2019s motions.\"}"
