{"request_id":"fffa07219d2e0c66aab1370f","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when, with a sudden turn, I found myself near the lake at a small cove where a skiff was tied. It wasn’t far from our house, and I could see my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat, and, used to such quick escapes, pushed off from the shore and rowed with all my strength to cross quickly. As I came across, dressed in white, covered only by my tartan rachan, my hair flying over my shoulders, and the boat gliding faster than anyone thought I could propel it, my father later said I looked more like a spirit than a mortal girl. I reached the shore, my father caught the boat, I sprang out lightly, and within a heartbeat I was in his arms.

Only then did life truly begin. Everything around me changed from dull monotony to the brightest scene of joy and wonder. The happiness I found in my father’s company far exceeded my hopeful expectations. We were together constantly, and the topics of our conversations never ran out. He had spent the sixteen years of his absence among nations almost unknown to Europe; he had roamed through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, and had moved freely among the native inhabitants with a liberty granted to few Europeans. His stories about their customs, his anecdotes and descriptions of landscapes filled blissful hours when we grew tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I listened in rapture whenever he spoke of what he had felt for me during those long years that had seemed like neglect. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl; but later, as grief softened and hope came back, I could turn to her again, and in the midst of cities and deserts your little fairy figure, as I pictured it, was always before me. The northern breeze that cooled me was sweeter and more fragrant because it seemed to carry a part of your spirit with it. I often thought of rushing home to take you with me to some fertile island where we would live in peace forever. But as I returned, my eager hopes were tormented by endless fears; my impatience grew painfully intense. I feared the sun might shine and the moon rise not on your living form but on your grave. Yet it wasn’t so; I have my Mathilda, my comfort, and my hope.”"}

