content.text:
{"request_id":"bbb2ca3b35dc1f2b8c02bb8f","rewritten_text":"Summer pressed on, and, crowned by the sun’s fierce beams, the plague launched its unfailing arrows across the earth. Nations bowed under their influence and died. The corn that had sprung up plentifully lay rotting in autumn fields, while the sorrowful man who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay rigid, struck down by pestilence in the furrow. The green woods waved their boughs in majesty while the dying stretched beneath their shade, answering the solemn music with discordant cries. Painted birds darted through the shadows; carefree deer rested unharmed on the bracken—the oxen and horses wandered from deserted stables and grazed among the wheat, for death claimed only humankind.

With summer and mounting mortality, our fears grew. My poor love and I looked at each other, and at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we will tell them of our fears, then gone with their cause. Even if they are the only ones left on earth, they will live; their cheeks will stay rosy, their sweet voices never fade.” Our eldest understood a little of the events around him, and sometimes, with grave looks, asked me why such vast ruin had fallen. Yet he was only ten, and youthful cheerfulness soon drove the unreasonable worry from his face. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful baby without sense of pain or sorrow, would toss back his light curls and make the halls echo with his laughter, drawing our attention to his games in a thousand innocent ways. Clara, gentle, lovely Clara, was our support, our comfort, our joy. She made it her mission to visit the sick, console the grieving, help the old, and share in the games and spark the happiness of the young. She moved through the rooms like a benevolent spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with a glow from elsewhere. Wherever she stepped, gratitude and praise followed. Yet when she stood before us in modest simplicity, playing with our children or performing small kind tasks for Idris with girlish care, one marveled where in her pure features or in the soft tones of her thrilling voice so much courage, wisdom, and active goodness could reside.

Summer dragged by, for we pinned our hopes on winter to at least stall the disease. To imagine it vanishing entirely was a hope too dear, too deeply felt, to voice. When someone carelessly breathed such a thought, those listening broke into tears and choking sobs, proof of the depth of their fears and the slightness of their hope."}

