content.text:
{"request_id":"2d8c51e75fb085ff52fb2e5b","rewritten_text":"No one spoke Perdita’s name, yet I felt sure that his wavering came from his love for her. Who could deserve love more than my noble sister? Who merited the hand of this self-styled king more than she, whose very glance seemed that of a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, even though disappointment had cooled her passion and his ambition fought hard against his heart.

We went together to the House that evening. Raymond, knowing his plans and prospects were to be debated and decided, remained cheerful and carefree. A hum like ten thousand swarming bee hives stunned us as we entered the coffee-room. Groups of politicians clustered together with worried brows and loud or hushed voices. The aristocratic party—England’s richest and most powerful—looked calmer than the rest, because the question would be settled without their involvement. Near the fire stood Ryland with his supporters. Ryland, of humble birth and vast wealth inherited from his manufacturer father, had as a young man witnessed the king’s abdication and the union of the Lords and Commons. He had supported these popular changes and spent his life strengthening them. Since then, the landowners’ influence had grown, and at first Ryland was glad to watch Lord Raymond’s plots draw away many of their allies. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty as something that would restore their lost power and rights. The fading spirit of royalty woke again in people’s minds, and they, willing slaves and self-made subjects, were ready to bow to the yoke. A few upright, steadfast spirits still stood as pillars of the state, but the word “republic” had become dull to common ears, and many—whether a majority would soon be shown—longed for the glitter and display of monarchy. Ryland stirred himself to oppose them; he claimed that only his tolerance had allowed this party to grow, but the time for leniency was over, and with one sweep of his arm he would brush away the cobwebs blinding his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him with something close to a shout. They surrounded him, counted their numbers, and explained why they expected to gain this or that member who had not yet declared a side."}

