          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1192b43f8bec9e0620b415ae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must take leave to observe, Sir Walter,\u201d Mr Shepherd remarked one morning at Kellynch Hall as he set the newspaper aside, \u201cthat the present moment is much in our favour. Peace will be sending all our wealthy naval officers ashore. They will all be searching for homes. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, for selecting tenants\u2014 tenants of the most responsible kind. Many a handsome fortune has been amassed during the war. Should a rich admiral fall in our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d \u201cHe would be an exceedingly lucky man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter returned; \u201cI have nothing more to say. Kellynch Hall would be a positive treasure to him\u2014 the greatest prize of all, though he had captured ever so many already\u2014 hey, Shepherd?\u201d Mr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he ought, at this sally, and then continued\u2014 \u201cIn a business light, Sir Walter, naval officers are easy to transact with. I have some acquaintance with their mode of conducting affairs; and I freely confess they entertain very generous views, and are as likely to prove acceptable tenants as any class one could meet. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would take leave to propose is, that if, in consequence of any rumours spreading regarding your purpose\u2014 which must be considered possible, for we know how hard it is to keep the affairs and intentions of one part of the world from the notice and curiosity of another; rank has its price; I, John Shepherd, might conceal any family matters I pleased, since no one would consider me worth observation; but Sir Walter Elliot has watchers he may find hard to evade\u2014 and thus I merely warn you that it will not greatly surprise me if, with all our discretion, some rumour of the truth should escape; in which case, as I was going to say, since applications will undoubtedly follow, I should judge any from wealthy naval commanders especially worth attention; and I beg to add that two hours will bring me over at any time to spare you the trouble of answering.\u201d Sir Walter only nodded. But soon after, rising and walking across the room, he observed with sarcasm\u2014 \u201cThere are few among the gentlemen of the navy, I fancy, who would not be astonished to find themselves in a house of this class.\u201d \u201cThey would survey their surroundings, no doubt, and bless their luck,\u201d said Mrs Clay\u2014 who was present; her father had brought her over, nothing being so beneficial to Mrs Clay\u2019s health as a drive to Kellynch\u2014 \u201cbut I perfectly agree with my father that a sailor might prove an exceedingly desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
        }
