          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2d7c48fd7f92ff7029963b92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of that. I will commence my tale: it is my final duty, and I trust I have strength enough to accomplish it. I chronicle no crimes; my errors may readily be excused, for they sprang not from wicked intent but from lack of judgement; and I believe few would assert that, through different conduct and loftier wisdom, they could have escaped the misfortunes that have made me their victim. My destiny has been ruled by necessity, a monstrous necessity. It demanded hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger, I am convinced, than any human power\u2014to shatter the thick, adamantine chain that held me, once breathing nothing but joy and wholly possessed by a fervent love of goodness, yet driven to misery that could only end, and is now about to end, in death. But I am losing the thread; my story is still untold. I will rest a moment, wipe my dimmed eyes, and strive to forget the present obscure yet heavy sorrow in the sharper feelings of the past.\\n\\nI drew my first breath in England. My father belonged to the nobility: he had been bereft of his own father early and was reared by a weak mother with all the indulgence she deemed fitting for a wealthy lord. He was sent to Eton and later to college, and from childhood was allowed free command of large sums of money, thus enjoying from his earliest youth the independence that a boy with such advantages inevitably acquires at a public school.\\n\\nUnder such influences his passions found a rich soil wherein they might strike roots and grow either as flowers or weeds according to their nature. Being always permitted to act for himself, his character became quickly and strongly defined, presenting a varied surface on which a keen observer might discern the seeds of both virtue and misfortune. His heedless extravagance, which led him to squander vast amounts to satisfy passing whims that he, because of their apparent energy, dignified with 