          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a079e04d1a094ee93527da57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning set for Admiral and Mrs Croft to view Kellynch Hall, Anne found it best to take her nearly daily walk to Lady Russell\u2019s and stay away until the visit was finished; afterward she regretted missing the chance to see them. The meeting between the parties pleased everyone and settled the matter immediately. Each lady already inclined toward an agreement and therefore saw only good manners in the other; and among the gentlemen, the Admiral\u2019s cheerful humor and open, trusting generosity impressed Sir Walter, who had also been coaxed into his most polished behavior by Mr Shepherd mentioning that the Admiral had heard of him as a model of good breeding. They approved the house, grounds, furniture, terms, timing\u2014everything and everyone\u2014and Mr Shepherd\u2019s clerks were set to work without any preliminary dispute about what \u201cThis indenture sheweth.\u201d Sir Walter quickly declared the Admiral the best-looking sailor he had ever met and even said that if his own servant had arranged the Admiral\u2019s hair, he would not be ashamed to be seen with him anywhere; and the Admiral, equally cordial, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI thought we would soon reach a deal, my dear, despite what they told us at Taunton. The Baronet will never set the Thames on fire, but he seems harmless\u201d\u2014reciprocal compliments considered much the same. The Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas, and since Sir Walter planned to move to Bath during the previous month, there was no time to lose in arranging everything. Lady Russell, convinced that Anne would not be allowed any role or importance in choosing their new house in Bath, was reluctant to have her hurried away so soon and wished to find a way for her to stay behind so she could take her to Bath herself after Christmas; but with her own commitments taking her away from Kellynch for several weeks, she could not extend the full invitation she wanted, and Anne, though dreading possible September heat in Bath\u2019s glaring whiteness and grieving to miss the bittersweet influence of autumn in the country, did not think she truly wanted to remain under the circumstances.\"}"
