          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a0af57e62eaa9693bf6e5925\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now turn to my own story. There is little to tell about the early part of my life, and I will be brief; still, I must linger over my childhood so it may be clear how, when one hope deserted me, all life became empty, and how, when the only affection I was allowed to nurture was destroyed, my existence went out with it.\\n\\nI have already said my aunt was very unlike my father. Without the slightest trace of malice, she possessed the coldest heart that ever beat in a human chest, utterly incapable of affection. She took me in because she saw it as a duty, but she had lived alone too long, undisturbed by children\u2019s noise and chatter, to let me disrupt her peace. She had never married, and for the last five years she had lived entirely alone on an estate inherited from her mother on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. My father had written asking her to live with me at his ancestral home, which stood in lovely country near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused, but as soon as she settled the matters left in her care by her brother\u2019s departure, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe charge of me as an infant, and later until I turned eight, fell to a servant of my mother\u2019s who came with us for that purpose. I lived in a secluded part of the house and saw my aunt only at set times. Twice a day she appeared: once around noon she would come to my nursery, and once after dinner I was brought to her. She never embraced me and seemed afraid the whole time I was in the room that I might irritate her with some childish mischief. My good nurse carefully drilled me before she dared lead me into the parlor, and the awe inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold gaze and stiff words was so intense that I rarely forgot her lessons or failed to maintain the exemplary stillness expected of me during those brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my nurse\u2019s care I roamed free through our park and the nearby fields. Born of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years a most sensitive nature. I cannot describe with what passion I loved everything, even the lifeless objects around me.\"}"
