{"request_id":"729a63d2729a1efd3133c7dc","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, once they leave home, adopt a look of constant ill-health, thinking it a mark of European refinement, but Mrs. Otis never made that mistake. She was wonderfully sturdy and full of high spirits. In many ways she seemed quite English, a proof that nowadays we share almost everything with America—except, of course, language. Her eldest son, named Washington in a burst of patriotism that he always regretted, was a fair-haired, rather handsome young man who had prepared for American diplomacy by leading the German dance at the Newport Casino three years running, and he was known in London as a superb dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weaknesses; otherwise he was very sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, slender and graceful as a fawn, with bold blue eyes. She was a remarkable horsewoman, once racing old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park and winning by a length and a half near the Achilles statue, to the huge delight of the young Duke of Cheshire, who proposed marriage on the spot and was sent back to Eton in tears that same night by his guardians. After Virginia came the twins, usually called “The Stars and Stripes” because they were always getting whipped. They were charming boys and, aside from the worthy Minister, the only true republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase lies seven miles from Ascot, the nearest train station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them, and they set off for the drive in high spirits. It was a lovely July evening, and the air carried the delicate scent of pine woods. Now and then they heard a wood pigeon crooning to itself, or glimpsed, deep in the rustling ferns, the shining breast of a pheasant. Little squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, and rabbits darted through the brushwood and over the mossy knolls with their white tails in the air. But as they entered the avenue to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly clouded over, an odd stillness fell, a great flock of rooks swept silently above them, and before they reached the house, heavy drops of rain began to fall.

An old woman stood on the steps to receive them, neatly dressed in black silk with a white cap and apron."}

