          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"caf652fafd9c996da9243e7b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not determine if the voice belonged to a man or a woman. Could not catch the words spoken, though believed the language to be Spanish. The condition of the room and of the bodies was described by this witness exactly as we detailed yesterday.\\n\\n\u201cHenri Duval, neighbor and silversmith, states that he was among the first group to enter the home. He confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s account overall. After forcing entry, they shut the door again to keep out the rapidly growing crowd, despite the late hour. The high-pitched voice, he believes, was that of an Italian. Was certain it was not French. Could not state with confidence that it was a man\u2019s voice; it may have been a woman\u2019s. Did not understand Italian. Could not make out the words, yet felt sure from the tone that the speaker was Italian. Knew Madame L. and her daughter well, having conversed with both often. Was sure the piercing voice did not belong to either of the victims.\\n\\n\u201cOdenheimer, restaurateur. This witness came forward of his own accord. He does not speak French and was questioned through an interpreter. A native of Amsterdam. Was passing the house when the screams were heard. They continued several minutes\u2014perhaps ten. They were prolonged, loud, terribly appalling. He was among those who entered the building and confirmed all earlier testimony except on one point. He insists the shrill voice was male\u2014French. Could not decipher the words. They came fast and loud, uneven, apparently spoken in fear as well as wrath. The voice was harsh\u2014more rough than shrill. Could hardly call it a shrill voice. The gruff voice repeated \u201csacr\u00e9,\u201d \u201cdiable,\u201d and once \u201cmon Dieu.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJules Mignaud, banker of the firm Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. The elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye possessed some property and opened an account at his bank in the spring of the year\u2014eight years prior. She frequently deposited small sums. She drew nothing until three days before her death, when she withdrew in person 4000 francs. The sum was paid in gold, and a clerk escorted her home with it.\\n\\n\u201cAdolphe Le Bon, clerk at Mignaud et Fils, attests that on that day, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her house carrying the 4000 francs divided between two bags. When the door opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from him, while the elderly lady took the other.\u201d\"}"
