          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5dfd0adf22b003d0b85dc9f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Countrymen, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and vagabonds of every low sort were mixed together in one mass. Drovers whistled, dogs barked, oxen bellowed and plunged, sheep bleated, pigs grunted and squealed, hawkers shouted, people swore and fought. Bells rang and loud voices poured from every public-house. The crowd pressed, shoved, drove, beat, whooped, and yelled; horrible clamor rose from every corner of the market; unwashed, unshaven, filthy figures ran back and forth, thrusting themselves in and out of the crush. It overwhelmed the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, dragging Oliver, forced his way through the thickest part of the crowd and paid little attention to the sights and sounds that astonished the boy. He nodded once or twice to passing friends and turned down invitations to take a morning dram, pushing forward until they left the chaos and reached Holborn through Hosier Lane.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young\u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, looking up at St. Andrew\u2019s clock. \u201cAlmost seven! You have to keep up. Come on, don\u2019t lag already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nHe jerked Oliver\u2019s wrist as he spoke, and Oliver broke into a half-trot to match the housebreaker\u2019s long strides as best he could.\\n\\nThey kept up this pace until they passed Hyde Park Corner and headed toward Kensington. Sikes slowed then, waiting for an empty cart approaching from behind. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d painted on it, he asked the driver as politely as he could to give them a ride to Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump in,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; he\u2019s my boy,\u201d Sikes answered, staring hard at Oliver and sliding his hand into the pocket where he kept his pistol.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a bit too fast for you, eh?\u201d the driver asked, noticing Oliver\u2019s short breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d said Sikes quickly. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, hold my hand, Ned. Get in!\u201d\\n\\nHe lifted Oliver into the cart, and the driver pointed to a pile of sacks, telling him to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed each milestone, Oliver wondered more and more where Sikes meant to take him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, Brentford\u2014each slipped behind them\u2014and still they moved on as steadily as if the journey had only just begun.\"}"
