          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"847718667cbca6c2bcdcbf1a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet the little town knew no mirth that tranquil Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s household were being dressed in mourning, weighed down with sorrow and tears. An unaccustomed hush settled over the village, though it was commonly quiet enough. The villagers went about their business distractedly and spoke rarely, though long sighs escaped them. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children; they lacked spirit for their games and soon abandoned them.\\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher roamed the empty schoolyard, sunk in gloom. Though she lingered there, she found no comfort. She murmured to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if only I had a brass andiron knob again! But I\u2019ve nothing now to remember him by.\u201d A little sob caught in her throat.\\n\\nAfter a moment she halted and said inwardly:\\n\\n\u201cIt happened right here. Oh, if only it could be done over, I would never say that\u2014not for the entire world. But he\u2019s gone; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThat thought broke her down, and she drifted away, tears slipping over her cheeks. Soon a knot of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s companions\u2014came along, leaned on the picket fence, and spoke in reverent whispers of how Tom did this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe mentioned some small detail (brimful of dreadful forewarning, as they now clearly perceived). Each pointed out the exact place where the missing lads had stood, adding, \u201cand I was standing just so\u2014just as I am now, and pretend you\u2019re him\u2014I was this close\u2014and he smiled exactly like this\u2014and then something ran all over me, like\u2014awful, you know\u2014and I never guessed what it signified then, but I can see it now!\u201d\\n\\nNext they quarreled over who had last seen the dead boys alive. Many asserted that melancholy honor and produced proofs, somewhat embellished by each witness. When they finally agreed who actually exchanged the last words with the departed, the fortunate ones assumed a sort of solemn significance, and the rest stared at them, full of envy. One poor youngster, having nothing else to boast of, reminded them with a rather obvious pride:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer licked me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at glory sank; nearly every boy could claim the same, which made the distinction meaningless. The cluster drifted away, still recounting memories of their lost heroes in hushed tones.\"}"
