          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1a081040ca922d78640a2866\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke in the morning, he puzzled over his whereabouts. He pushed himself upright, rubbed his eyes, and gazed around. Then awareness dawned. The dawn lay cool and gray, and the woods lay steeped in a delectable sense of rest, hushed and serene with the deep pervasive calm. Not a leaf moved; not a whisper intruded upon Nature\u2019s vast reverie. Dewdrops beaded every leaf and blade. A white sheet of ashes blanketed the fire, from which a thin blue thread of smoke climbed straight up. Joe and Huck still slept. \\n\\nFar off, a bird called; another responded; soon the tapping of a woodpecker took hold. The cool dim gray slowly paled, and sound by sound life quickened. The miracle of Nature rousing herself and setting to work unfolded before the meditative boy. A little green worm crawled over a dewy leaf, lifting two-thirds of its form into the air now and then to \u201csniff around,\u201d then moved on again\u2014for it was measuring, Tom declared. As the worm neared him of its own accord, he kept as motionless as stone, his hopes rising and falling in turn as the creature approached or seemed bent elsewhere; and when it paused with its curved body raised, pondered a painful moment, and then decisively dropped onto Tom\u2019s leg to begin a journey across him, his heart rejoiced\u2014for that meant, without a doubt, he was to get a new suit of clothes, a gaudy pirate uniform. Then a parade of ants emerged from no particular spot and busied themselves; one wrestled heroically with a dead spider five times its size and hauled it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug scaled the dizzy summit of a blade of grass, and Tom bent near and murmured, \u201cLady-bug, lady-bug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone,\u201d and she took wing and flew off to look after it\u2014no shock to the boy, for he already knew this insect was gullible about conflagrations, and he had exploited its innocence before. Next came a tumblebug, sturdily shoving its ball, and Tom touched it just to watch it clamp its legs tight and feign death. By now the birds were in full riot. A catbird, the northern mimic, settled in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and poured out her neighbors\u2019 songs in transported delight; then a sharp-voiced jay swooped down, a streak of blue flame, and perched on a twig almost within the boy\u2019s reach, cocked his head, and scrutinized the strangers with devouring curiosity; a gray squirrel and a big \u201cfox\u201d squirrel scurried along, sitting up every so often to inspect and chatter at the boys, for the wild things likely had never seen human beings before and scarcely knew whether fear was warranted.\"}"
