          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46545cc6dcd889216079f9f2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour told me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I remember. Very good, very good.\u201d\\n\\nAfter a few sluggish attempts to fall asleep again, which the man cleverly thwarted by poking the fire steadily for five minutes, he rose, flung on his hat, and went out. He turned into the Temple and, having revived himself by twice pacing the pavements of King\u2019s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, went into Stryver\u2019s chambers.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who never attended these conferences, had gone home, and Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing-gown, with his throat left bare for comfort. He bore that somewhat wild, strained, scarred look about the eyes that may be seen in all free livers of his sort, from the portrait of Jeffries downward, and which can be traced, in varying artistic guises, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re a little late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual hour; perhaps a quarter of an hour behind.\u201d\\n\\nThey moved into a dingy room lined with books and strewn with papers, with a roaring fire. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the litter a table gleamed with plenty of wine, and brandy, and rum, and sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I have been dining with the day\u2019s client; or watching him dine\u2014it comes to the same!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was a rare point, Sydney, that you brought to bear upon the identification. How did you hit upon it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was rather a handsome fellow, and I fancied I should have been much the same sort of fellow, if I had ever had any fortune.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his precocious paunch quivered.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Set to work, set to work.\u201d\\n\\nSullenly, the jackal loosened his dress, went into an adjoining room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. Soaking the towels and partially wringing them, he folded them over his head in a fashion hideous to see, sat down at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much boiling down to do tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerily, looking through his documents.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere you are, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled himself on his back on a sofa on one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own paperscattered table opposite, with bottles and glasses within easy reach.\"}"
