{"request_id":"c8425bdf5961accdb09e7aaf","rewritten_text":"Yet the same idea that once sustained me at the start of my career now only grinds me deeper into the dust. All my theories and hopes amount to nothing, and like the archangel who reached for omnipotence, I am chained in an endless hell. My imagination blazed, but my analytical and practical abilities were equally strong; by fusing these qualities I conceived the idea and carried out the creation of a man. Even now I cannot remember without emotion the daydreams that possessed me while the work was unfinished. I walked through heaven in my thoughts, sometimes exulting in my power, sometimes blazing with anticipation of what it could do. From childhood I had been filled with high hopes and soaring ambition; yet see how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this state of degradation. Despair rarely touched me then; a glorious destiny seemed to carry me forward, until I plummeted, never to rise again.

Must I lose this remarkable man? I have longed for a friend, sought someone who would understand and love me. Here, on these desolate seas, I have found such a person, yet I fear I have found him only to grasp his worth and then lose him. I try to reconcile him to life, but he rejects the idea.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind intentions toward such a miserable wretch. But when you speak of new ties and fresh affections, do you really think anyone could replace those I have lost? Could any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even when our affections are not stirred by exceptional qualities, the companions of childhood hold a certain power over our minds that almost no later friend can gain. They know our childish dispositions which, however they may be modified later, are never erased; they can judge our actions with clearer insight into the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never suspect the other of fraud or deceit unless signs of it appeared early, while any other friend, however devoted, may despite himself be regarded with suspicion. But my friends were precious not only through habit and shared memories, but through their own virtues; wherever I go, the soothing voice of Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval will always sound in my ears. They are dead, and only one feeling in such solitude can persuade me to keep living. If I were engaged in some great mission or design offering wide benefit to my fellow creatures, then I could endure to complete it.”"}

