          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"184bb896093c44b3594df5ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Tom joined the new chapter of the Cadets of Temperance because he liked their flashy regalia. He promised to avoid smoking, chewing, and swearing as long as he belonged. Soon he discovered something new\u2014that promising not to do something is the quickest way to make a person want to do exactly that. Tom was soon tormented by the urge to drink and curse; it grew so strong that only the hope of showing off his red sash kept him from quitting the order. Fourth of July was approaching; but he gave up that plan\u2014abandoned it before he had worn his shackles for forty-eight hours\u2014and set his hopes on old Judge Frazer, the justice of the peace, who seemed to be on his deathbed and would certainly have a grand public funeral, being such an important figure. For three days Tom was deeply worried about the Judge\u2019s health and starved for updates. Sometimes his hopes soared\u2014so high that he dared to take out his regalia and practice in front of the mirror. But the Judge had an infuriating habit of wavering. At last he was declared on the mend\u2014and then recovering. Tom was disgusted and felt cheated. He turned in his resignation at once\u2014and that night the Judge relapsed and died. Tom decided he would never rely on a man like that again.\\n\\nThe funeral was splendid. The Cadets marched in a manner designed to torment the ex-member with jealousy. Yet Tom was a free boy again, and there was some comfort in that. He could drink and swear now\u2014but to his surprise he did not want to. Simply knowing he could do it removed the desire and the thrill.\\n\\nSoon Tom was surprised to find that his longed-for vacation was starting to feel a bit dull.\\n\\nHe tried keeping a diary\u2014but nothing happened for three days, so he gave it up.\\n\\nThe first traveling troupe of blackface minstrels came to town and caused a stir. Tom and Joe Harper organised a troupe of their own