          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5654082dc5c4a9d8cd6aa734\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut that matters little.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might as well ask,\u201d she retorted, \u201cwhy, with such an obvious intention to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your inclination, against your judgment, and even against your character? Was that not justification enough for any incivility on my part, if I _was_ uncivil? Yet I have other grievances, as you well know. Even had my own feelings not already condemned you\u2014had they been neutral, or even favourable\u2014do you imagine any persuasion could induce me to accept the man who has been the cause of wrecking, perhaps forever, the happiness of a dearly loved sister?\u201d\\n\\nAt these words, Mr. Darcy\u2019s colour changed; but the feeling was brief, and he listened without attempting to interrupt while she went on,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason imaginable to think poorly of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous conduct you displayed _there_. You dare not, you cannot deny that you were the chief, if not the sole instrument of dividing them; that you exposed one to the world\u2019s censure for fickleness and whim, the other to its mockery for hopes defeated, and plunged the pair of them into the cruellest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused, and with rising indignation observed that he listened with an expression implying he was entirely untouched by remorse. He even regarded her with a smile of feigned disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny you have done this?\u201d she reiterated.\\n\\nAssuming composure, he replied, \u201cI have no desire to deny that I used every effort to part my friend from your sister, or that I take satisfaction in having succeeded. Towards _him_ I have acted with more kindness than towards myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth disdained to acknowledge this courteous comment, though its import did not escape her, nor was it likely to appease her.\\n\\n\u201cBut it is not solely this business,\u201d she continued, \u201con which my aversion rests. Long before it occurred, my opinion of you was fixed. Your character was unfolded in the account I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On that subject, what defence can you offer? Under what imaginary act of friendship could you justify yourself? Or by what misrepresentation could you impose upon others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a fervent interest in that gentleman\u2019s concerns,\u201d said Darcy, in a less composed tone and with deepened colour.\\n\\n\u201cWho that knows his misfortunes could help being interested in him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy echoed contemptuously,\u2014\u201cyes, his misfortunes have indeed been heavy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd inflicted by you,\u201d Elizabeth cried passionately. \u201cYou reduced him to his present condition\u2014comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
