          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc523f696061267aa81511bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Otis, who I may add is a sound judge of Art since she spent several winters in Boston when she was young, has told me that these jewels are very valuable and would sell for a high price. In these circumstances, Lord Canterville, I trust you see why I cannot let them stay with anyone in my family; all such showy ornaments, however fitting they may be for the British aristocracy, would be completely out of place for people raised on the stern\u2014indeed, I believe eternal\u2014principles of republican simplicity. I should mention that Virginia is very eager for you to let her keep the box as a keepsake of your unfortunate but misguided ancestor. As it is extremely old and quite damaged, you might consider agreeing to her request. For my part, I am rather surprised to hear one of my children express sympathy with anything medieval, and I can only explain it by noting that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs shortly after Mrs. Otis returned from a visit to Athens.\u2019\\n\\nLord Canterville listened very soberly to the worthy Minister\u2019s words, occasionally tugging at his grey moustache to hide a reluctant smile, and when Mr. Otis finished, he shook him warmly by the hand and said, \u2018My dear sir, your delightful little daughter did my unlucky ancestor, Sir Simon, a very great service, and my family and I owe her much for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels are clearly hers, and by heavens, if I were cruel enough to take them from her, the old rogue would be out of his grave in a fortnight, making my life a perfect torment. As for their being heirlooms, nothing is an heirloom unless it is named as such in a will or legal paper, and no one even knew these jewels existed. I assure you I have no more claim on them than your butler does, and when Miss Virginia grows up I imagine she will enjoy having pretty things to wear. Besides, you forget, Mr. Otis, that you bought the furniture and the ghost as a single lot, and anything that belonged to the ghost became yours at once, since whatever trouble Sir Simon caused in the corridor at night, in law he was quite dead, and you acquired his property by purchase.\u2019\"}"
