          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2d2aed55b79f168715ab535\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! There was something animal in the soul, and the body had its own moments of spirituality. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could become debased. Who could say where the physical impulse stopped or the psychic impulse began? How shallow were the arbitrary definitions of everyday psychologists! And yet how hard it was to decide between the claims of the different schools! Was the soul a shadow sitting in the house of sin? Or was the body actually inside the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? Separating spirit from matter was a mystery, and joining spirit to matter was a mystery as well.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether psychology could ever become such a precise science that every small spring of life would be revealed to us. As it stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and rarely understood others. Experience had no moral value. It was only the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists usually treated it as a kind of warning, credited it with a certain ethical power in shaping character, praised it as something that taught us what to follow and showed us what to avoid. But there was no driving force in experience. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly showed was that our future would mirror our past, and that the sin we once committed with disgust we would commit many times with pleasure.\\n\\nIt was obvious to him that the experimental method was the only way to reach any scientific analysis of the passions; and Dorian Gray was certainly a subject ready-made for him and seemed to promise rich, fruitful results. His sudden wild love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon of considerable interest. There was no denying that curiosity played a large part in it\u2014curiosity and the craving for new experiences\u2014yet it was not straightforward but deeply complex. Whatever there was in it of the purely sensual instinct of youth had been reshaped by the imagination, turned into something that seemed to the boy himself to be removed from the senses, and was therefore all the more dangerous. The passions whose origins we lied to ourselves about were the ones that ruled us most tyrannically. Our weakest motives were the ones we recognized clearly. It often happened that while we thought we were experimenting on others we were really experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nWhile Lord Henry sat brooding on these matters, there was a knock at the door, and his valet entered to remind him it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
        }
