          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0c10a3f45bf305e32da1a17\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had just broken when I put the Manuscript away. The rain had nearly stopped, the world lay gray and sorrowful, and the worn-out storm was sighing and sobbing as it drifted into rest. I went to the stranger\u2019s chamber and listened at his slightly open door. I caught the sound of his voice, so I knocked. He did not answer, yet he kept speaking. I looked inside. The man lay on his back in the bed, muttering in fragments yet with spirit, his arms lashing about in that uneasy way fevered people have. I slipped in quietly and leaned over him. His murmurs and exclamations continued. I spoke a single word to draw his notice. Instantly his glazed eyes and ashen features shone with delight, gratitude, joy, welcome.\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, at last you are here\u2014you cannot know how I yearned for you! Sit close\u2014don\u2019t abandon me\u2014never go away again, Sandy, never. Where is your hand?\u2014give it, dear, let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now everything is right, all is peace, and I am happy once more\u2014we are both happy, aren\u2019t we, Sandy? You are so dim, so shapeless, merely a mist, a cloud, but you are here, and that is blessing enough; and I have your hand; do not take it back\u2014it will be only for a brief space, I shall not ask it long.... Was that the child?... Hello-Central!... she makes no response. Sleeping, perhaps? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and bid her farewell.... Sandy! Yes, you are still there. I wandered for an instant, thought you had gone.... Have I been ill long? I suppose so; it feels like months. And such dreams! such strange and dreadful dreams, Sandy! Dreams as real as reality itself\u2014delirium, surely, yet so real! Why, I believed the king was dead, believed you were in Gaul unable to return, believed there was a revolution; in that wild chaos of dreams I thought Clarence and I and a mere handful of my cadets fought and annihilated all the chivalry of England! Yet even that was not the strangest. I seemed to become some being from a far unborn age, centuries away, and even that felt as real as the rest! Yes, I thought I had flown back out of that era into ours, and then forward to it once more, and was left, a stranger and desolate, in that uncanny England, with a gulf of thirteen centuries yawning between me and you! between me and my home and my comrades! between me and everything beloved, everything that could make life worth the living!\u201d\"}"
