          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ec32a36102ffee205df5eced\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost completed their circle. It was late May; the woods were wrapped in fresh green, and the fields carried the sweet scent of newly cut grass. I believed the warm air and the gentle beauty of nature might help me inspire him with softer feelings and prepare him for the trust I was determined to win.\\n\\nI chose an evening on one of those days for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me and guided him to a nearby beech wood whose light shade shielded us from the slanting, dazzling beams of the setting sun. After we walked silently for some time, I sat with him on a mound of moss. It is strange, but I still seem to see that place now: the slim, smooth trunks wrapped in ivy whose glossy dark leaves contrasted with the white bark and the light leaves of the young beech shoots sprouting from the parent trunks. Short grass mingled with moss, partly covered by dead leaves from the last autumn that the winds had piled into small mounds here and there. A few moss-covered stumps stood around. The leaves stirred gently in the breeze, and through their green canopy the bright blue sky showed through. As evening arrived, the distant trunks glowed red in the sunlight, the wind died away, and a few birds flew past us on their way to rest.\\n\\nThere we sat together, and when you hear all that passed\u2014how terrible events tore at our souls in this calm spot, which could have been a paradise if not for strange passions\u2014you will understand why I remember it so clearly, hoping its peace might give me courage and persuasive words. I saw all these details and noted them blankly in my mind while I tried to arrange my thoughts for what I meant to do. My heart pounded as I prepared to speak to him, for I was determined not to be turned away, yet I trembled at what my words might do to him. At last, with great hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the overwhelming affection\u2014you showed when you first returned will, I hope, excuse me in your eyes as I dare to speak to you, not only with the tender love of a daughter but also with the freedom of a friend and equal.\u201d\"}"
        }
