          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1c44b124a29dfde4773d7372\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself, so the tiresome task of keeping up a slow, disappointing correspondence with Elizabeth fell entirely on Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had barely finished when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick arrived; they had arranged one last walk around Lyme together. They were supposed to start for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and meant to stay together and outdoors as long as possible until then.\\n\\nAs soon as they were all in the street, Anne noticed Captain Benwick drawing near her. Their talk the night before clearly had not discouraged him, and they walked together for some time, speaking again of Mr. Scott and Lord Byron, still as unable as any other two readers to agree about either author\u2019s merits, until something caused a general rearrangement in the group and she suddenly found Captain Harville at her side instead of Captain Benwick.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said quietly, \u201cyou have done something good in getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he could have such company more often. It\u2019s bad for him to be shut up the way he is, but what can we do? We cannot separate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can easily believe that to be impossible; but in time, perhaps\u2014we know what time does for every sorrow, and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend can still be called a young mourner\u2014only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, true enough,\u201d he replied with a deep sigh, \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not know of it so soon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, just made first lieutenant of the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading to hear of him; he sent letters ahead, but the Grappler was ordered to Portsmouth. The news had to meet him there, but who was to tell him? Not I. I would as soon have been hoisted to the yardarm. No one could do it but that good man,\u201d he said, pointing to Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had come into Plymouth the week before, with no danger of being ordered back to sea. He took his chance with the rest, sent up a request for leave, but without waiting for the reply, traveled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed straight out to the Grappler, and never left the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. Judge for yourself, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne mentally answered the question without hesitation and said all that her feelings allowed or that Captain Harville\u2019s emotions could endure, for he was too moved to continue the topic, and when he spoke again it was of something entirely different.\"}"
