          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"27d406520c8b8d69fc6c5350\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWe went to your home,\u201d said the first man, \u201cbut you weren\u2019t there, and when we learned you were likely walking this way, we followed in hopes of catching up with you. Please get into the carriage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTheir manner was commanding, and as they spoke they positioned themselves so that I stood between them and the carriage door. They were armed. I was not.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018Gentlemen,\u2019 I said, \u2018forgive me, but I usually ask who seeks my help, and what sort of case I am being called to.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cThe second man answered. \u2018Doctor, your clients are people of rank. As for the nature of the case, we trust your skill to understand it better than we could explain it. That\u2019s enough. Please get into the carriage.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cI had no choice but to comply, and I stepped in without a word. Both of them followed\u2014the last jumped in after raising the steps. The carriage turned around and sped off at its previous pace.\\n\\n\u201cI repeat this conversation exactly as it happened. I\u2019m sure this is word for word. I\u2019m recording everything precisely as it occurred, forcing my mind to stay fixed on the task. Where I insert the dashed lines that follow, I pause for the moment and hide my notes.\\n\\n*****\\n\\n\u201cThe carriage left the city streets, passed through the North Barrier, and rolled onto the country road. Two-thirds of a league from the Barrier\u2014though I only calculated that later\u2014we turned off the main avenue and soon stopped at an isolated house. All three of us got out and walked along a damp, soft path through a garden, where an unattended fountain had overflowed, until we reached the door. It didn\u2019t open immediately when we rang, and one of my escorts hit the servant who opened it with his heavy riding glove across the face.\\n\\n\u201cThere was nothing unusual to me in that gesture; I had seen ordinary people struck more often than dogs. But the other man, also angry, struck him the same way with his arm, and their expressions and posture were so identical at that moment that I realized they were twin brothers.\\n\\n\u201cFrom the time we\u2019d arrived at the outer gate\u2014which was locked until one brother opened it for us and then locked it again\u2014I had heard cries coming from an upstairs room.\u201d\"}"
