          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d5c630a22a2eea34fe2c013\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is astonishing! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from the seat and paced along the garden path. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cto me, Dorian Gray is purely an artistic impulse. You might find nothing remarkable in him. I see everything there is. He is most present in my work when no likeness of him appears. He is, as I have told you, the hint of a new style. I discover him in the sweep of certain lines, in the delicacy and grace of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why refuse to exhibit his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I have breathed into it a hint of this strange artistic worship, of which I have never cared to speak to him. He knows nothing of it. He never shall. But the world might suspect, and I refuse to lay my soul before their barren, inquisitive eyes. I will not have my heart placed beneath their microscope. There is far too much of myself in that painting, Harry\u2014far too much!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are less particular than you. They know how convenient passion can be for print. Nowadays a broken heart yields countless editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI detest them for it,\u201d Hallward exclaimed. \u201cAn artist ought to make lovely things, yet leave nothing of his own life within them. We live in a time when people treat art as though it were meant to be memoir. We have lost the pure idea of beauty. Someday I shall show the world what it truly is; and for that reason, the world shall never look upon my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you are mistaken, Basil, but I will not dispute it. Only the intellectually adrift ever argue. Tell me, does Dorian Gray care greatly for you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter pondered a moment. \u201cHe is fond of me,\u201d he replied after a pause; \u201cI know he is. Naturally I flatter him outrageously. I take a strange delight in saying things to him that I know I shall regret. Usually he is delightful to me, and we sit in the studio and speak of countless matters. Yet now and again he grows horribly inconsiderate and seems to take a definite pleasure in hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, as though I have handed over my entire soul to someone who treats it like a blossom for his lapel, a little adornment to gratify his vanity, a ornament suited to a summer\u2019s day.\u201d\"}"
        }
