          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37d760d6a3a5129023ecd5be\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd only think, ma\u2019am,\u201d pleaded Oliver, tears forcing their way despite his struggle to hold them back, \u201cpray think how young she is, and how much kindness she gives, and the joy and comfort she brings to everyone near her. I\u2019m sure\u2014really sure\u2014quite persuaded\u2014that, for your sake, as good as you are; and for her own; and for the sake of all those she makes so happy; she will not die. Heaven will never suffer her to die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d replied Mrs. Maylie, resting her hand upon Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou speak like a child, poor boy. Yet you remind me of my duty, all the same. I let it slip for a moment, Oliver, but I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old, and have witnessed enough of sickness and death to understand the torment of parting from those we love. I have seen sufficient, too, to know it isn\u2019t always the youngest and best who are kept to those that cherish them; but that should console us in our grief; for Heaven is just; and such scenes teach us vividly that there exists a brighter world than this, and that the way to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was amazed to find that, as Mrs. Maylie uttered these words, she checked her lamenting as if by sheer resolve; and, drawing herself erect while she spoke, grew calm and steadfast. He was even more astonished to observe that this steadiness endured; and that, amid all the subsequent care and vigil, Mrs. Maylie remained ever ready and self-possessed: fulfilling every duty that had fallen on her, steadily, and, outwardly at least, even with cheerfulness. But he was young, and was ignorant of what strong minds can do in moments of trial. How should he know, when even those who possess such minds are seldom aware themselves?\\n\\nA fearful night followed. As morning broke, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s forebodings were only too sadly realized. Rose had entered the earliest stage of a high, dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not yield to idle grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her finger on her lips while she looked steadily into his face; \u201cthis letter must go, with the utmost speed, to Mr. Losberne. It has to be carried to the market-town, no more than four miles away, by the footpath across the meadows: and from there dispatched by special messenger on horseback, direct to Chertsey. The people at the inn will see to it: and I am certain I can trust you to have it done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could say nothing, but let his eager expression show how ready he was to be off at once.\"}"
        }
