          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"29dfca7c9af439a6fd929a66\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet this thought, which once sustained me at the start of my work, now only drives me deeper into despair. All my plans and hopes mean nothing, and like the archangel who reached for godlike power, I am bound in endless torment. My imagination was fierce, and my ability to analyze and carry out a plan was just as strong; with these two forces together I dared to imagine and accomplished the creation of a man. Even now I cannot remember without intense feeling the dreams I cherished while the task remained unfinished. In my mind I walked through heaven, sometimes rejoicing in my abilities, sometimes burning with visions of what they might achieve. From childhood I was filled with great hopes and towering ambition, and look how I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognize me in this wretched state. Despair rarely touched my heart; a grand fate seemed to carry me forward, until I fell, never again to rise.\\n\\nMust I lose this remarkable soul? I have yearned for a friend; I have searched for someone who would understand me and love me. Here, upon these desolate seas, I have at last found such a person, yet I fear I have met him only to grasp his worth and then lose him. I would bring him back to life, but he rejects the idea.\\n\\n\u201cI thank you, Walton,\u201d he said, \u201cfor your kind wishes toward such a miserable creature; but when you speak of new bonds and fresh affections, do you think anyone can replace those who are gone? Could any man ever be to me what Clerval was, or any woman what Elizabeth was? Even when our emotions are not roused by exceptional virtues, the companions of our childhood hold a sway over our minds that later friends can hardly achieve. They know our early dispositions, and though those traits may be altered later, they are never erased; such friends can judge our actions with a surer sense of the purity of our motives. A brother or sister can hardly suspect the other of deceit, unless signs appeared in youth, while another friend, however devoted, may still be viewed with doubt. But I cherished friends made dear not only through habit and shared memories, but through their own qualities; wherever I go, the soothing voice of my Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval will always echo in my mind. They are dead, and in this lonely place only one feeling could persuade me to preserve my life. If I were engaged in a great mission or design, one that promised wide benefit to my fellow beings, then I could live long enough to complete it.\u201d\"}"
