{"request_id":"f2c9999a10c10b6ba5148aa8","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was whom to dispose of, for he knew that murder, like the old pagan religions, needs both a priest and a victim. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies, and he felt this was not the moment to indulge any personal grudge: the mission before him was too solemn and serious. So he drew up a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper and, after thoughtful consideration, settled on Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old woman in Curzon Street who was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and since he was already extremely wealthy—having inherited all of Lord Rugby’s estate when he came of age—there was no chance of gaining any vulgar financial benefit from her death. In fact, the more he thought about it, the more she seemed the perfect choice, and, feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his arrangements immediately.

The first step, naturally, was to settle with the palm-reader. He sat down at a small Sheraton writing desk near the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, sealed it in an envelope, and told his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. He then telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he was leaving the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her learn what he was doing for her sake, but would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice locked forever in his heart.

On the way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, their white petals framing those bold pheasant-like eyes. Once at the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and ordered a lemon-and-soda along with a book on toxicology. He had already decided that poison was the best method for this troublesome task. Anything involving physical force disgusted him, and he was determined not to kill Lady Clementina in any manner that might draw public attention; the thought of being lionized at Lady Windermere’s, or seeing his name splashed across trashy society papers, horrified him."}

