{"request_id":"cbbe7a0c39bee94f2e113208","rewritten_text":"Even now I had not drained the bitter potion to its last drop; I was not convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel, with each pulse, through every nerve and every thought, that I stood solitary of my kind—that I was the LAST MAN.

The day grew overcast, and a thin rain began at sunset. Even the eternal heavens weep, I reflected; why should mortal man blush to exhaust himself in tears? I recalled ancient tales wherein humans are said to melt through weeping into ever-flowing springs. Ah! that it might be so; then my fate would resemble, in part, the watery death of Adrian and Clara. Grief is fantastic; it weaves a loom on which to trace its sorrow from each form and alteration about it; it mingles itself with all living nature; it feeds on every object; as light, it penetrates all things, and, like light, imparts its own hues to everything.

I had strayed in my search some distance from the place where I was cast ashore, and reached one of those watch-towers that stand at intervals along the Italian coast. I rejoiced in shelter, glad to see a work of human hands after long gazing on nature’s dreary barrenness; so I entered and climbed the rough winding stair into the guard-room. So far fate was merciful that no appalling traces of former occupants remained; a few planks stretched over two iron tressels, strewn with dried Indian-corn leaves, formed the bed offered me; and an open chest holding some half-mouldered biscuit roused an appetite which, perhaps, existed before, though I had not noticed it until now. Thirst too—keen, burning thirst, born of the sea-water I had swallowed and of my exhausted frame—tormented me. Kind nature made supplying these wants a pleasure, so that even I was refreshed and soothed as I ate this wretched fare and drank a little of the sour wine that half filled a flask left in the deserted dwelling. Then I lay upon the bed, not to be slighted by one who had suffered shipwreck. The earthy scent of the dried leaves was balm after the loathsome smell of seaweed. I forgot my loneliness. I neither looked backward nor forward; my senses were lulled to rest; I fell asleep and dreamed of dear inland scenes—hay-makers, the shepherd’s whistle to his dog when he called for help to drive the flock to fold—of sights and sounds peculiar to the mountain life of my boyhood, long forgotten."}

