{"request_id":"777c93ee18e83a39d888bae3","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the staircase he regained his composure and decided to unleash his famous howl of demonic laughter, a trick he had often found very useful. People said it had turned Lord Raker’s wig grey overnight and had driven three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to quit before their first month ended. He gave his most dreadful laugh, so awful that the old vaulted roof rang again and again. Yet before the last echo faded, a door opened and Mrs. Otis stepped out in a pale blue dressing gown. “I am afraid you are not well,” she said, “and have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If you suffer from indigestion, you will find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost glared at her in rage and immediately set about turning himself into a large black dog, an illusion for which he was rightly famous, and which the family doctor always said had caused the permanent madness of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton. But the sound of approaching footsteps made him pause in his wicked plan, so he only made himself glow faintly and disappeared with a deep graveyard groan just as the twins ran up to him.

When he reached his chamber he collapsed completely and was consumed by wild agitation. The twins’ rudeness and Mrs. Otis’s gross materialism were naturally infuriating, but what truly upset him was his failure to wear the suit of armor. He had hoped that even modern Americans would feel awe at the sight of a Specter in Armor, if not from common sense then at least out of respect for their national poet Longfellow, whose graceful verses had helped him pass many long nights when the Cantervilles were in London. Besides, it was his own set of armor. He had worn it triumphantly at the Kenilworth tournament and had been praised for it by no less a person than the Virgin Queen. Yet the moment he put it on he was crushed by the weight of the breastplate and steel helmet, fell heavily on the stone floor, scraped both knees badly, and bruised the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was very ill and barely left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. Still, by nursing himself carefully he recovered, and resolved to make a third attempt to terrify the United States Minister and his family."}

