          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d3898819c6002234ac255cb8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I come from a little corner of land surrounded by sea and veiled in clouds. When I picture the globe with its endless ocean and unexplored continents, my homeland seems only a tiny speck; yet when weighed by mental power, it outweighed nations of greater size and population. It is true that the human mind creates all that is good or great for humanity, and Nature is only its first helper. England, set far to the north in the rough sea, now appears in my dreams as a vast, well-crewed ship that mastered the winds and sailed proudly across the waves. In my boyhood it made up my whole world. Standing on my native hills, watching plains and mountains stretch as far as I could see, dotted with the homes of my countrymen and cultivated by their toil, I felt that the very center of the earth lay there. The rest of the globe seemed a tale, and forgetting it would have taken no effort of imagination or understanding.\\n\\nFrom the start, my life has shown how change can rule the course of a person\u2019s fate. For me this almost came by inheritance. My father was one of those men to whom nature lavishly gives wit and imagination, then lets their life\u2019s boat be driven by those winds without reason as a rudder or judgment as a pilot. His origins were obscure, but circumstance soon brought him into public notice, and his small inheritance was quickly lost amid the dazzling scene of fashion and luxury where he played a part. During his short years of careless youth he was adored by the fashionable idlers of the day, especially by the young sovereign, who escaped politics and royal duties to find constant amusement and cheer in his company. My father\u2019s impulses, never under his control, constantly led him into trouble, yet his ingenuity alone got him out. The mounting pile of debts of honor and trade that would have crushed another man he bore with a light spirit and unbroken gaiety. His presence was so desired at the tables and gatherings of the rich that his lapses were viewed as venial and he was welcomed with intoxicating flattery.\\n\\nThis kind of popularity, like all others, fades. His difficulties of every kind grew at a terrifying pace, while his means to escape them were very small.\"}"
