          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d5504d93455982b4e82aa782\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOn that topic I shall remain consistently silent; you may rely upon my never uttering an ungenerous reproach when once we are married.\u201d\\n\\nShe was compelled to cut him short at this point.\\n\\n\u201cYou are much too precipitate, sir,\u201d she exclaimed. \u201cYou seem to forget I have returned no answer. Let me do so without further delay. I am obliged by the compliment you intend me. I fully acknowledge the honour of your proposals, yet I am compelled to decline them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am perfectly aware,\u201d Mr. Collins rejoined, giving a ceremonious wave of his hand, \u201cthat young ladies commonly reject the man whom in secret they resolve to accept when he first sues for their favour; and that at times the refusal is repeated twice, even thrice. I am therefore in no way discouraged by what you have said, and still hope soon to conduct you to the altar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUpon my word, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cthat hope is astonishing after my declaration. Believe me, I am not one of those ladies (if such ladies exist) so bold as to stake their happiness on the chance of a second proposal. My refusal is in the utmost earnest. You could never make me happy, and I am convinced I should be the last woman on earth to render you so. Indeed, were your friend Lady Catherine acquainted with me, she would perceive I am unfit in every respect for that station.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins very solemnly, \u201cbut I cannot suppose her ladyship would in the least disapprove of you. Be assured, moreover, that when next I have the honour of seeing her, I shall extol your modesty, prudence, and other amiable qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBelieve me, Mr. Collins, any commendation of me is needless. Allow me to judge for myself, and favour me so far as to credit my words. I sincerely wish you both happy and prosperous, and by refusing your hand do all within my power to preserve you so. In offering it, you have, I think, fully satisfied your sense of delicacy toward my family, and may claim Longbourn estate when it descends, without the slightest self-reproach. The matter, therefore, may be considered definitively settled.\u201d Rising as she spoke, she would have left the room but for Mr. Collins, who resumed,\\n\\n\u201cWhen next I have the honour to address you on this subject, I trust to obtain a more agreeable reply than you have now afforded me; though I acquit you of cruelty for the present, as I know it is the established practice of your sex to reject a gentleman on his first application, and perhaps you have already said as much to encourage my suit as is compatible with the strict delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
