          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"328b6737602cad6a28c40c04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental traits commonly discussed under the label of analytical scarcely admit of dissection themselves. We recognize them solely through their manifestations. Among other things we know that, when they exist in excess, they are to their owner a source of keenest pleasure. As the muscular man rejoices in his bodily prowess, savoring any exertion that sets his sinews to work, so does the analyst revel in that moral activity which disentangles. He finds delight even in the slightest occupation that brings his gift into play. Riddles, puzzles, hieroglyphs are his favorites; in resolving each he displays a shrewdness that to ordinary minds seems almost supernatural. Though his conclusions result from the pure essence of method, they wear, in fact, the entire aspect of intuition.\\n\\nThe power of solving may perhaps be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, particularly by that highest branch which, unjustly and simply because its procedures retreat, has been styled, as if preeminent, analysis. Yet computation, in itself, is not analysis. The chess player, for instance, may perform the former without exerting the latter. Hence the influence of chess upon intellectual character is seriously misconceived. I am not composing a treatise, merely introducing a rather singular tale with scattered remarks; I shall therefore assert that the more refined and more practical exercise of the reflective intellect is demanded by the modest game of draughts than by all the laborious trifling of chess. In chess, where each piece has eccentric movements with varying and changeable values, mere complexity is mistaken\u2014an error not uncommon\u2014for depth. Here the attention is summoned with force. Let it droop for a moment, and an oversight follows, bringing harm or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but intricate, the hazards of such mistakes multiply; in nine instances out of ten it is the player of steadier concentration, rather than sharper penetration, who wins. In draughts, conversely, where the moves are uniform and scarcely vary, chances of inadvertence are reduced, and, the attention being comparatively unoccupied, any advantage gained by a player springs from superior acumen. To be less abstract, envision a draughts match with the pieces reduced to four kings, where naturally no oversight is to be anticipated. Plainly, victory can then be secured (the players being nearly equal) only through some recherch\u00e9 maneuver, born of a vigorous exertion of intellect.\"}"
