          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f22b6c4eafd059c5a3197f3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he had oppressed no man, imprisoned no man; so far was he from harshly exacting what was owing that he had surrendered his rights willingly, hurled himself upon a world offering him no favour, carved out a private station within it, and earned his own sustenance. Monsieur Gabelle had kept the bankrupt and tangled estate under written orders to spare the villagers, to give them the scraps available\u2014fuel the rapacious creditors grudged them in winter, produce that could be rescued from the same clutch in summer\u2014and doubtless he had urged this in his defence, so it could not fail to be evident now.\\n\\nThis inclined Charles Darnay to the desperate resolve he had begun to form: that he must go to Paris.\\n\\nIndeed. Like the sailor in the old tale, winds and currents had borne him into the sphere of the Loadstone Rock, it drew him, and he must yield. Everything that rose before his thoughts impelled him faster, more steadily, toward that formidable magnet. His suppressed uneasiness had been that wicked purposes were being enacted in his suffering country by evil agents, and that he, who knew himself better than they, was absent, not striving to stem the slaughter or uphold mercy and humanity. With this disquiet half smothered and half reproachful, he had been driven to compare himself with the gallant old gentleman whose sense of duty was so unshakable; at once there followed the sneers of Monseigneur, keenly wounding him, and those of Stryver, which most of all were coarse and exasperating, for reasons of long standing. Upon these came Gabelle\u2019s letter: the plea of a guiltless captive, in peril of death, appealing to his justice, honour, and reputation.\\n\\nHis mind was fixed. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nIndeed. The Loadstone Rock was hauling him, and he must sail onward, even to the crash. He knew of no reef; he scarcely perceived danger. The purpose with which he had acted, though the work was unfinished, presented itself to him in a shape that must win grateful recognition in France when he came to affirm it. Then arose that glorious dream of doing good, so often the hopeful mirage of many worthy spirits, and he even beheld himself within that illusion exerting some sway to guide this raging Revolution that was rushing so dreadfully wild.\"}"
