{"request_id":"b8a95e4c10bf578fa4bd2ac2","rewritten_text":"“Oh, that is nothing! It is a pleasure to me. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art.”

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his help, and, after politely refusing an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and headed for the Park.

For the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at noon he drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon the stolid hall-porter kept pinning up telegrams from all over the country with horse-race results, divorce verdicts, weather reports, and the like, while the tape machine droned on about an all-night session in the House of Commons and a small panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur shut himself in the library with the Pall Mall, the St. James’s, the Globe, and the Echo, to the enormous annoyance of Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read the reports of a speech he had delivered that morning at the Mansion House on South African Missions and the need for black bishops in every province, and who for some reason had a firm dislike of the Evening News. None of the papers, however, contained even the slightest mention of Chichester, and Lord Arthur felt the attempt must have failed. The blow was dreadful, and for a while he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, offered elaborate apologies and to give him another clock free of charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. But Lord Arthur had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything is so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite is hard to find in a pure form. The little German, however, though conceding that there must have been some trouble with the mechanism, still hoped the clock might yet go off, and recalled a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa which, though set to explode in ten days, did nothing for nearly three months. It was quite true that when it finally did go off, it only managed to blow a housemaid to atoms, since the Governor had left six weeks earlier, yet it at least proved that dynamite, while a powerful destructive force when controlled by machinery, could also be rather unpunctual."}

