          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ea850289a554cb2632ed5372\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was deeply upset by the second delay of the wedding, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her gown, did everything she could to make Sybil call off the match. Yet Sybil loved her mother dearly and had given her whole life to Lord Arthur, so nothing Lady Julia said could shake her trust. As for Lord Arthur, it took him days to recover from his awful disappointment, and for a while his nerves were completely shattered. Still, his good common sense soon returned, and his sound, practical mind did not leave him uncertain for long. Since poison had been a total failure, explosives\u2014dynamite or something similar\u2014seemed the obvious next attempt. \\n\\nHe went back over the list of his friends and relatives and, after careful thought, decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of great culture and learning, loved clocks and kept a remarkable collection of timepieces from the fifteenth century to the present. Lord Arthur decided this passion offered a perfect chance to carry out his plan. Finding an explosive device, however, was quite another issue. The London Directory gave him no help, and he felt there was no point in going to Scotland Yard, as they never seemed to know anything about dynamiters until after the blast, and not much even then. \\n\\nSuddenly he remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with very revolutionary leanings whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s that winter. Count Rouvaloff claimed to be writing a life of Peter the Great and said he had come to England to study documents about the Tsar\u2019s time here working as a ship carpenter, but it was widely suspected he was a Nihilist agent, and the Russian Embassy clearly disapproved of his presence in London. Lord Arthur felt he was exactly the man he needed and drove one morning to Rouvaloff\u2019s lodgings in Bloomsbury to ask for his advice and help. \\n\\n\u201cSo you are taking up politics seriously?\u201d said Count Rouvaloff after Lord Arthur explained why he had come. But Lord Arthur, who disliked any show of pretension, admitted that he had no interest in social issues at all and wanted the explosive device for a purely family matter that concerned no one but himself.\"}"
