          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"028527eb473e2171d606c929\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day unfolded much the same as the one before. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent several hours of the morning with the patient, who was still recovering, though slowly; in the evening, Elizabeth joined them in the drawing room. The loo table, however, was nowhere in sight. Mr. Darcy was writing, while Miss Bingley sat nearby, watching him compose his letter and repeatedly distracting him with messages for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were playing piquet, and Mrs. Hurst was studying their game.\\n\\nElizabeth picked up some needlework and was more than entertained by the exchange between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant praise\u2014whether of his handwriting, the straightness of his lines, or the length of his letter\u2014and his complete indifference to her compliments made for an amusing dialogue that matched Elizabeth\u2019s opinion of them both.\\n\\n\u201cHow delighted Miss Darcy will be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe gave no answer.\\n\\n\u201cYou write unusually fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou must have so many letters to write in a year! Business letters, too! How dreadful I would find them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it is fortunate that they fall to me instead of to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease tell your sister that I am eager to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already told her so once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid you don\u2019t like your pen. Let me fix it for you. I am exceptionally good at sharpening pens.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always take care of my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe remained silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I\u2019m delighted to hear about her progress on the harp, and please inform her that I\u2019m absolutely thrilled with her beautiful little table design; I consider it far superior to Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay I postpone your raptures until I write again? At the moment I lack the space to do them justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it doesn\u2019t matter. I\u2019ll see her in January. But do you always write such charmingly long letters to her, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are generally long; but whether they are always charming is not for me to say.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is my rule that anyone who can write a long letter with ease cannot write poorly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat won\u2019t do as a compliment for Darcy, Caroline,\u201d her brother interjected, \u201cbecause he does not write with ease. He searches too carefully for words of four syllables. Isn\u2019t that so, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy style of writing is quite different from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d Miss Bingley exclaimed, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless way imaginable.\u201d\"}"
