          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"547b05ebedbe98c8c7ce2f4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, monsieur le mauvais sujet,\u201d exclaimed the old lady as he came in, \u201cwhy have you stayed away from me so long?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lady Clem, I have not a moment to myself,\u201d Lord Arthur replied with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cI suppose that means you traipse about all day with Miss Sybil Merton, buying chiffons and talking nonsense? I cannot conceive why people make such a to-do about getting married. In my day we never dreamt of billing and cooing\u2014neither in public nor in private.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you I have not seen Sybil for twenty-four hours, Lady Clem. As far as I can tell, she belongs entirely to her milliners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; that is the only reason you visit an ugly old creature like me. I wonder you men don\u2019t take warning. On a fait des folies pour moi, and now look\u2014nothing but a rheumatic wretch with a false front and a sour temper. If it weren\u2019t for dear Lady Jansen, who sends me all the worst French novels she can ferret out, I do not believe I could get through the day. Doctors are worthless\u2014good only for collecting fees. They cannot even mend my heartburn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have brought you a remedy for that, Lady Clem,\u201d said Lord Arthur seriously. \u201cIt is a marvelous thing, invented by an American.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t fancy American inventions, Arthur. I am positive I don\u2019t. I read several American novels lately, and they were utter rubbish.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, but there is no rubbish about this at all, Lady Clem! I guarantee it is an infallible cure. You must promise to try it.\u201d Lord Arthur drew a small box from his pocket and handed it to her.\\n\\n\u201cWell, the box is delightful, Arthur. Is it truly a gift? How very kind. And is this the famous medicine? It resembles a bonbon. I shall take it immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, Lady Clem!\u201d cried Lord Arthur, seizing her hand. \u201cYou must not do anything of the sort. It is a homoeopathic remedy, and if you swallow it without an attack of heartburn, it may do you no end of damage. Wait until the pain begins, and then take it. You will be amazed at the result.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI should like to swallow it now,\u201d said Lady Clementina, holding the tiny transparent capsule up to the light, its bubble of liquid aconitine floating inside. \u201cI am sure it is delicious. Truth be told, though I hate doctors, I adore medicines. Still, I shall save it for my next attack.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd when is that to be?\u201d asked Lord Arthur eagerly.\"}"
