          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66d5cb1b58c4c5eb64265235\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I repeated to myself that beautiful passage about Dante entering the earthly Paradise, and imagined how wonderful it would be to walk along those lovely banks and see the chariot of light descend with my long-lost parent to return him to me. While I waited for that imagined moment, I pictured myself weaving a wreath from those beautiful flowers and crowning myself out of happiness: I would sing _sul margine d\u2019un rio_, my father\u2019s favorite song, so that my voice floating through the still air would tell him, wherever he sat anticipating our reunion, that his daughter had arrived. Then the mark of misery would fade from my forehead, and I would lift my gaze without fear to meet his eyes, which always shone with the gentle light of innocent love. Thinking of the enchantment in those deep eyes brought me to tears, though I wept softly so my sobs would not disturb the enchanted scene.\\n\\nI was so completely lost in this daydream that I kept walking without noticing where I went until I bent to pick a flower for my wreath on that barren plain where no flower grew, and then I woke from my reverie and realized I had no idea where I was.\\n\\nThe sun had already set and the rosy glow that the clouds had taken from it on its way down had almost faded. A wind swept over the plain; I looked around but nothing showed me where I might be. I was lost, and my effort to find the path was useless. I wandered on, and the approaching darkness erased every trace that might guide me. Soon everything was shrouded in the pitch blackness of night; I grew tired, and knowing that my servant was spending the night in the nearby village, so my absence would alarm no one, and feeling sure I was safe from intruders in this desolate place, I decided to spend the night there. Truly I was too weary to walk farther: the air was cold, but I cared little for physical discomfort, and I thought of how well I had learned to endure the weather during my two years of solitude, when no change of season had ever stopped my constant wandering.\\n\\nI lay down on the grass, surrounded by darkness untouched by even the faintest glimmer of light. No sound broke the stillness, for the deep night had lulled to sleep the insects\u2014the only living creatures in that lonely spot where not a tree or shrub could shelter anything else. There was a marvelous silence in the air that soothed my senses yet quickened my spirit; my mind leapt from one image to another and seemed to embrace eternity.\"}"
