          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a8b4d33e0fd38d99385f5e6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters at the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are rarely very harsh, and during autumn a fire is almost never needed. Around mid-October, 18\u2014, however, there was an unusually cold day. Just before sunset I pushed through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut, which I hadn\u2019t visited for several weeks\u2014at the time I lived in Charleston, nine miles from the island, and the travel back and forth was nothing like it is today. When I reached the hut I knocked, as usual, and when no one answered, I looked for the key where I knew it was hidden, unlocked the door, and went inside. A fine fire was blazing on the hearth. It was something different, and most welcome. I shed my overcoat, settled into an armchair by the crackling logs, and waited patiently for my hosts to return.\\n\\nSoon after dark they arrived and greeted me warmly. Jupiter, grinning from ear to ear, hurried around preparing marsh hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his\u2014what else should I call them?\u2014fits of enthusiasm. He had found a previously unknown bivalve, representing a new genus, and, beyond that, with Jupiter\u2019s help he had tracked down and captured a scarab he believed to be completely new, though he wanted my opinion on it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, rubbing my hands over the fire, wishing the whole tribe of scarabs to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I\u2019d known you were here!\u201d said Legrand. \u201cBut it\u2019s been so long since I\u2019ve seen you, and how could I predict you\u2019d visit me tonight of all nights? On my way home I ran into Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and, very foolishly, I lent him the bug; so you won\u2019t be able to see it until morning. Stay tonight, and I\u2019ll send Jup down for it at sunrise. It\u2019s the loveliest thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t be absurd! No\u2014the bug. It\u2019s a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back and another, somewhat longer, at the other end. The antennae are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAin\u2019t no tin in him, Master Will, I keep tellin\u2019 you,\u201d Jupiter cut in. \u201cThe bug is a gold bug, solid, all the way through, \u2019cept for his wings\u2014I never felt a bug so heavy in my life.\u201d\"}"
        }
