          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"322b811db30e09814aac8616\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wrapped him in de paper and shoved a scrap of it in his mouf\u2014that\u2019s how it went.\u201d \u201cAnd you are persuaded your master truly was bitten by the beetle, that the bite put him out of sorts?\u201d \u201cI ain\u2019t tinkin\u2019 nuttin\u2019\u2014I\u2019s knowin\u2019. What else make him dream \u2019bout de goole constant, if it ain\u2019t \u2019cause de goole-bug bit him? I done heerd tell o\u2019 dem goole-bugs afore now.\u201d \u201cBut how can you be sure he dreams of gold?\u201d \u201cHow I know? \u2019Cause he mutter \u2019bout it in his sleep\u2014that\u2019s how I knows.\u201d \u201cWell, Jup, perhaps you may be right; but to what lucky circumstance do I owe the pleasure of seeing you today?\u201d \u201cWhat\u2019s de trouble, massa?\u201d \u201cDid you bring any word from Mr. Legrand?\u201d \u201cNo, massa, I tote dis here pissel;\u201d with that, Jupiter passed me a note which read: \u201cMY DEAR \u2014\u2014Why have you been absent so long? I trust you have not been silly enough to take offence at any little brusquerie of mine; yet no\u2014that seems unlikely. Since last we met I have had grave cause for disquiet. I have something to impart, yet scarcely know how to frame it, or if I ought to tell it at all. I have not been altogether well for some days, and poor old Jup torments me, almost past endurance, with his well-meant fussing. Could you credit it?\u2014he got ready a tremendous cudgel, the other day, meaning to punish me for slipping away from him and passing the day, solus, among the mainland hills. I truly believe my haggard look alone kept me from a whipping. I have added nothing to my cabinet since you last saw it. If there is any way you can possibly manage it, come over with Jupiter. Do come. I desire to see you to-night on business of weight. I assure you it is of the highest weight. Ever yours, WILLIAM LEGRAND.\u201d A certain tone in this message troubled me greatly. The whole manner of it was markedly unlike Legrand\u2019s usual style. What could he be brooding over? What new whim had seized that ardent brain? What \u201cbusiness of the highest importance\u201d could he have? Jupiter\u2019s report promised nothing favorable. I feared the steady strain of hardship might at last have truly disturbed my friend\u2019s mind. So without the least delay I got ready to go with the negro. When we reached the wharf, I remarked a scythe and three spades, all seemingly fresh from the shop, lying at the bottom of the boat in which we were to set out. \u201cWhat does all this mean, Jup?\u201d\"}"
