          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"73a7bc63e7cdf4bcb28d968f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner, Mr. Bennet barely spoke; but once the servants left, he decided it was time to converse with his guest. He chose a topic on which he expected Mr. Collins to excel, remarking that Mr. Collins seemed very fortunate in his benefactor. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and concern for his comfort were truly remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have selected a better subject. Mr. Collins grew eloquent in her praise. The topic lifted him to an even more solemn manner, and with the utmost seriousness he protested that never in his life had he witnessed such behavior in a person of rank\u2014such graciousness and condescension\u2014as he had experienced from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both sermons he had already had the honor of preaching before her. She had invited him twice to dine at Rosings and had even sent for him just the previous Saturday to complete her quadrille table in the evening. Many considered Lady Catherine proud, he knew, but he himself had never seen anything but friendliness in her. She always spoke to him as she would to any other gentleman; she made no objection to his joining in the neighborhood\u2019s society, nor to his leaving his parish for a week or two to visit relatives. She had even advised him to marry as soon as he could, provided he chose carefully, and had once honored him with a visit at his modest parsonage, where she fully approved the alterations he had been making and even condescended to suggest some herself\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is all very proper and polite, I\u2019m sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a very agreeable woman. It\u2019s a pity more great ladies are not like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden in which my humble home stands is separated from Rosings Park, her Ladyship\u2019s residence, by only a lane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you said she was a widow, sir? Does she have any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has only one daughter, the heiress of Rosings and of very considerable property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better off than many girls. And what sort of young lady is she? Is she pretty?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a truly charming young lady. Lady Catherine herself says that, for genuine beauty, Miss de Bourgh far surpasses the most attractive of her sex, because there is something in her features that signifies a young woman of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
        }
