{"request_id":"4acf4f4e3c3d4a09720621d6","rewritten_text":"When Tom woke in the morning, he was confused for a moment. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and looked around, and it all came back to him. The dawn was cool and gray, and the woods were filled with a deep calm, quiet, and peace. Not a leaf moved; no sound disturbed nature’s thoughts. Dewdrops beaded the leaves and grass. The fire was covered in white ashes, and a thin blue thread of smoke rose straight up. Joe and Huck still slept.

Far off in the woods a bird called, and another answered. Soon the tapping of a woodpecker echoed nearby. Slowly the dim gray light grew brighter, and the sounds of life increased. The marvel of nature waking up and going to work unfolded before the thoughtful boy. A little green worm crawled across a dewy leaf, lifting most of its body into the air now and then, “sniffing around,” and then moving on. Tom said it was measuring. When it came toward him on its own, he sat as still as a stone, his hopes rising and falling as the creature approached or seemed ready to turn away. At last, after a pause with its curved body in the air, it came down on Tom’s leg and started crawling over him. His heart swelled with joy—that meant he was going to get a new suit of clothes, a bright pirate uniform without a doubt. Now a line of ants appeared from nowhere and got to work. One ant struggled bravely with a dead spider five times its size and lugged it straight up a tree trunk. A brown spotted ladybug climbed the dizzy height of a grass blade. Tom bent close and said, “Ladybug, ladybug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children’s alone,” and she flew off to check on it. That did not surprise Tom, since he knew this insect was gullible about fires, and he had taken advantage of its trust more than once. Then a tumblebug came pushing its ball. Tom touched it and watched it pull its legs in and play dead.

By now the birds were in full celebration. A catbird, the northern mimic, landed in a tree above Tom’s head and joyfully copied its neighbors’ songs. Then a sharp-voiced jay swept down like a flash of blue flame, perched on a twig almost within reach, cocked its head, and stared at the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a larger “fox” squirrel came racing along, sitting up now and then to inspect and chatter at the boys, for the wild creatures had probably never seen humans before and barely knew whether to be afraid or not."}

