          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5382f1a1e4cbfa5d2dd8558a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay,\u201d was finally brought to trial.\\n\\nHis judges sat on the Bench in feathered hats, but everywhere else rough red caps with tricolor cockades were the common headwear. Looking at the Jury and the restless crowd, he might have thought the world had turned upside down and that criminals were judging honest men. The poorest and most vicious people in the city, always present in some number, ruled the scene: loudly commenting, cheering, jeering, guessing, and trying to hurry the verdict with no restraint. Most of the men were armed in one way or another; among the women, some had knives, some had daggers, some ate and drank while watching, and many knitted. Among the knitters was one who carried an extra piece of knitting under her arm. She sat in a front row, next to a man Darnay had not seen since arriving at the Barrier, yet recognized immediately as Defarge. He noticed she whispered in his ear once or twice and seemed to be his wife; but what struck him most about them was that, although they had placed themselves as close to him as possible, they never looked his way. They seemed to wait for something with stubborn resolve, staring at the Jury and nowhere else. Doctor Manette sat below the President, wearing his usual quiet clothes. As far as the prisoner could tell, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men not connected with the Tribunal who still wore their ordinary dress and had not put on the coarse Carmagnole costume.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was charged by the prosecutor as an emigrant whose life was forfeit to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants on pain of death. It did not matter that the decree was dated after his return to France. He was there, and the decree was there; he had been taken in France, and his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d shouted the crowd. \u201cAn enemy to the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence them and asked the prisoner if it was true he had lived many years in England.\\n\\nHe admitted it was.\\n\\nWas he not then an emigrant? How did he describe himself?\\n\\nHe hoped he was not an emigrant in the meaning and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President asked.\\n\\nBecause he had willingly given up a title he detested and a position he detested. He had left his country\u2014he said this was before the Tribunal used the word emigrant in its current sense\u2014to support himself by his own work in England, rather than live in France on the labor of an already overburdened people.\"}"
        }
