          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8e051a5fb0f01aa81f6bd2d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut, in sober truth, when an ascension has been made to any altitude, the mass of atmosphere overcome by any further rise is far from proportional to the additional height (as should be clear from earlier remarks), and instead decreases in a continually diminishing ratio. Hence it is plain that, climb as far as we will, we cannot, strictly speaking, reach a boundary beyond which no air exists. The atmosphere must be there, I reasoned, though perhaps in a condition of immeasurable tenuity.\\n\\n\u201cConversely, I knew that many arguments had been advanced to establish a fixed and absolute limit to the atmosphere, beyond which air is wholly wanting. Yet a fact disregarded by those who advocate such a boundary seemed to me, if not a direct overthrow of their doctrine, at least a matter for weighty inquiry. When the intervals between Encke\u2019s comet\u2019s successive returns to perihelion are compared, after allowing with utmost precision for every disturbance from planetary attraction, we find the periods gradually lessening; that is, the major axis of its ellipse steadily shortens in a slow but perfectly regular contraction. This is exactly what must happen if the comet encounters resistance from an exceedingly tenuous ethereal substance filling the regions of its orbit. For such a medium, by slackening the comet\u2019s speed, would strengthen its centripetal while weakening its centrifugal force. In simpler terms, the sun\u2019s pull would be constantly gaining the advantage, drawing the comet closer at each revolution. In fact, no other explanation presents itself for the change observed. Furthermore\u2014the actual diameter of the comet\u2019s nebulous envelope shrinks swiftly as it nears the sun, and expands as rapidly as it heads toward aphelion. Was it not reasonable for me, with M. Valz, to suppose that this apparent contraction of bulk springs from the pressure of the same ethereal medium mentioned above, which becomes denser the nearer it lies to the sun? The lenticular phenomenon dubbed the zodiacal light likewise deserved study. This glow, so obvious in tropical latitudes and unmistakable for meteoric brilliance, climbs obliquely from the horizon and loosely follows the sun\u2019s equatorial line. It struck me as nothing less than a sparse atmosphere stretched outward from the sun, reaching beyond Venus\u2019s track at least, and, so I believed, indefinitely farther. Indeed, I could not imagine this medium limited to the comet\u2019s orbit or restricted solely to the immediate precincts of the sun.\u201d\"}"
