{"request_id":"f7834ef0de61b2eab0d146d1","rewritten_text":"Mrs. Musgrove took the first chance to be alone with Anne and said, “Miss Anne, I so wish Mrs. Charles had a bit of your way with those children. They are entirely different when you’re with them! Usually they’re terribly spoiled. It’s a pity you can’t show your sister how to manage them. They’re such strong, healthy children—poor little things, and I say that without favoritism—but Mrs. Charles really doesn’t know how they ought to be handled. Good heavens, they can be so troublesome. Truly, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them here as often as I otherwise would. I think Mrs. Charles isn’t quite happy that I don’t invite them more, but you know how dreadful it is to have children around when you’re forced to correct them every minute—‘don’t do this,’ ‘don’t do that’—or to keep them manageable only by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

Mary also had this to say. “Mrs. Musgrove thinks her servants are so reliable that questioning them would be treason, but I’m certain, without exaggeration, that her head housemaid and her laundry maid, instead of doing their work, wander all over the village all day. I run into them everywhere; honestly, I can’t step into my nursery twice without seeing something of them. If Jemima weren’t the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would ruin her, because she says they’re always tempting her to go on walks with them.” On Mrs. Musgrove’s side, it was, “I make it a rule never to interfere in any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs—I know it would not work—but I’ll tell you, Miss Anne, because you might be able to set things straight, that I don’t think highly of Mrs. Charles’s nursery maid. I hear strange stories about her; she’s always out and about, and from what I’ve seen myself, she’s such a fashionably dressed lady that she could ruin any servant she gets close to. Mrs. Charles practically swears by her, I know; but I’m giving you this hint so you can keep an eye out. If you notice anything wrong, don’t be afraid to mention it.”

Mary complained again that Mrs. Musgrove often failed to give her the precedence she was entitled to when they dined at the Great House with other families; she couldn’t see why being treated as part of the household should cost her her proper place."}

