          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed21831ef877f064755edcc3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you would have continued on your own. When would you have spoken if I had not asked? My plan to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had a strong effect. Too strong, I fear; what becomes of the moral if our happiness comes from my breaking my promise, since I ought not to have mentioned it? This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou need not worry. The moral stays intact. Lady Catherine\u2019s unjust attempts to separate us removed all my doubts. I do not owe my current happiness to your urgent wish to thank me. I was not in the mood to wait for you to begin. My aunt\u2019s news gave me hope, and I was determined to learn everything at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been of immense use, which should make her happy, since she loves being useful. But tell me, why did you come down to Netherfield? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and feel embarrassed, or did you plan something more serious?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true purpose was to see you and judge, if I could, whether I might ever hope you would love me. My stated purpose, or what I told myself, was to see if your sister still cared for Bingley, and if she did, to confess to him what I have since confessed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the nerve to tell Lady Catherine what is coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more likely to lack time than bravery, Elizabeth. But it must be done; and if you give me a sheet of paper, I will do it at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I did not have a letter to write, I might sit beside you and admire how steady your handwriting is, as another young lady once did. But I have an aunt too, and she must not be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth had not yet answered Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter, because she was reluctant to admit how she had overestimated her closeness with Mr. Darcy. Now that she had news she knew would delight her aunt, she felt almost guilty that her uncle and aunt had already missed three days of happiness, and she wrote at once:\\n\\n\u201cI would have thanked you sooner, dear aunt, as I ought to have done, for your long, kind, satisfying account; but to tell the truth, I was too out of spirits to write. You assumed more than truly existed. But now assume as much as you like; let your imagination run free in every direction the subject allows, and unless you believe me already married, you cannot be far wrong.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
