          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2c95cee67e8175cf2a577fac\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was delivered to you. You see I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is the fact,\u201d Defarge replied. His wife had brushed his elbow as she knitted and hummed, signaling that he should answer, but keep it brief.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d the spy continued, \u201cthat his daughter came; and from your care she took him, with that neat brown gentleman\u2014what is his name?\u2014the one with the small wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company\u2019s bank\u2014and carried him over to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is the fact,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting memories!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t hear much about them now?\u201d the spy asked.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIn truth,\u201d Madame broke in, looking up from her work and her little song, \u201cwe never hear about them. We received word of their safe arrival, and perhaps one more letter, or perhaps two; but since then they have gone their way in life, we ours, and we have kept no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPrecisely so, madame,\u201d the spy replied. \u201cShe is going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing?\u201d Madame echoed. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have been married long ago. You English must be cold.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI notice your tongue is,\u201d Madame returned; \u201cand as the tongue is, I suppose the man is.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not take that as a compliment, but he handled it as well as he could and laughed it off. After finishing his cognac, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is going to be married. But not to an Englishman; to someone who, like her, was born French. Speaking of Gaspard (ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!), it is curious that she is going to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was lifted to that height of so many feet\u2014meaning, the present Marquis. Yet he lives unknown in England; he has no title there. He is Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge kept knitting steadily, but the news had a clear effect on her husband. No matter how he tried behind the little counter, striking a light and lighting his pipe, he was unsettled, and his hand was unsteady. The spy would not have been a spy if he had failed to notice or to remember it.\\n\\nHaving made at least this one score, whatever it might be worth, and with no customers coming in to help him with any other, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and left, taking care to say politely before departing that he hoped to enjoy the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
