          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a9a6a933903deeb37a0b3684\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams meant to fade like this? We had written our name just a little lower than the angels, and now we were no better than mayflies. We called ourselves the paragon of animals, and yet we proved to be the quintessence of dust. We complained that the pyramids had outlasted the embalmed bodies of their builders. Alas, the simple shepherd\u2019s straw hut we passed on the road held within it the principle of greater longevity than the entire human race. How could we reconcile this grim change with our former ambitions and our seeming powers?\\n\\nSuddenly an inner voice, clear and articulate, seemed to say: From eternity it was decreed this way. The steeds that carry Time forward have had this hour and this fulfillment chained to them since the void first bore its burden. Would you seek to unwrite the unchangeable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world, servant of the Omnipotent, eternal and changeless Necessity, who sits with busy fingers ever weaving the unbreakable chain of events\u2014I will not complain of your acts. If my human mind cannot accept that everything that exists is right, still, since what is must be, I will sit among the ruins and smile. Truly we were not born to enjoy, but to submit and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader tire if I describe in detail our long journey from Paris to Geneva? If day after day I recorded in journal form the pressing miseries of our fate, could my hand write, or language provide words to express, the variety of our woe, the jostling of one deplorable event upon another? Have patience, reader\u2014whoever you are, wherever you dwell. Whether spirit or descendant of some surviving pair, your nature will be human, your home the earth. You will read here of the acts of the extinct race and will wonder if those who endured what you find recorded were made of frail flesh and soft substance like your own. Indeed they were\u2014weep then; for surely, solitary being, you must be of gentle disposition. Shed compassionate tears, yet lend your attention to the tale and learn the deeds and sufferings of your forerunners.\\n\\nStill, the final events that marked our passage through France were so filled with unsettling horror and gloomy misery that I should not linger too long over them. If I dissected each incident, every fragment of a second would hold a harrowing story, whose smallest word would curdle the blood in your young veins. It is right that I raise this monument to instruct you about the race that has passed, but not that I drag you through hospital wards or the hidden chambers of a charnel house.\"}"
