          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8b8ae259d3eb5bd3ccbc4868\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, your father might let you go, if your mother can spare you. Daughters never matter as much to a father. And if you stay one more full month, I\u2019ll be able to take one of you all the way to London, because I\u2019m going there in early June for a week. Since Dawson has no objection to the barouche-box, there will be plenty of room for one of you\u2014and if the weather happens to be cool, I wouldn\u2019t mind taking both, as neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are all kindness, madam, but I think we must stick to our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine seemed reconciled. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always say exactly what I think, and I cannot stand the idea of two young women traveling by post alone. It is highly improper. You must find a way to send someone. I detest that kind of thing. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended, in keeping with their station in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted she take two footmen with her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not have shown herself with propriety any other way. I am extremely attentive to such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I\u2019m glad it occurred to me to mention it, because it would truly reflect poorly on you to let them travel alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is sending a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a valet, does he? I\u2019m glad there is someone who thinks of such things. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you give them my name at the Bell, you will be well taken care of.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions about their trip, and since she did not answer all of them herself, attention was required\u2014something Elizabeth considered fortunate; with her mind so preoccupied, she might have forgotten where she was. Reflection would have to be reserved for solitary hours: whenever she was alone, she surrendered to it as her greatest relief, and not a day passed without a solitary walk in which she could relish all the bleak pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nShe was well on her way to knowing Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She pored over every sentence, and her feelings toward its writer shifted wildly. When she recalled the tone of his address, indignation still burned. But when she considered how unjustly she had condemned and rebuked him, her anger turned on herself, and his wounded feelings stirred her compassion.\"}"
        }
