          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7efa333c0fa358f1ff15f242\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNo hand-raising in those days. Not the least!\u201d Joe offered me another spoonful of gravy, which I dared not accept. \u201cHe\u2019s been a world of bother to you, ma\u2019am,\u201d remarked Mrs. Hubble, sympathizing with my sister. \u201cBother?\u201d echoed my sister; \u201cbother?\u201d and she plunged into a dreadful inventory of every illness I\u2019d indulged in, every night I\u2019d kept her awake, each height I\u2019d toppled off, every depth I\u2019d tumbled into, each injury I\u2019d inflicted on myself, and all the times she\u2019d wished me in my grave, while I obstinately declined the invitation. I suspect the Romans must have provoked each other terribly with their noses; perhaps that accounts for their restlessness. At any rate, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose so exasperated me during the account of my misdeeds, I felt I could have pulled it till he roared. Yet all I had borne so far was nothing compared to the dreadful sensations that seized me when the silence following my sister\u2019s recital was broken\u2014during which, as I was painfully aware, everyone had turned their eyes on me in indignation and disgust. \u201cStill,\u201d observed Mr. Pumblechook, gently steering the company back to the matter from which they had strayed, \u201cPork\u2014when viewed as biled\u2014is rich, eh?\u201d \u201cTake a little brandy, uncle,\u201d said my sister. O heavens, the moment had arrived! He would discover its weakness; he would pronounce it weak, and it would be all over with me! I clung fast to the table leg beneath the cloth, grasping it with both hands, and awaited doom. My sister fetched the stone bottle, returned with it, and poured him his brandy. None of the others touched any. The unhappy man dallied with his glass\u2014lifted it, examined it against the light, replaced it\u2014prolonging my torment. Meanwhile Mrs. Joe and Joe were lively in clearing the table for pie and pudding. I couldn\u2019t remove my gaze from him. Still gripping the table leg with hands and feet, I watched the poor soul toy with his glass, lift it, smile, toss back his head, and swallow the brandy. Immediately afterward, the company was struck with unspeakable consternation when he sprang to his feet, whirled round several times in a horrifying, spasmodic, whooping-cough dance, and dashed out of the door; he then appeared through the window, violently plunging and spitting, contorting his face hideously, and seemingly deranged. I held tightly while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran after him.\"}"
