          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dad82b9bc7304a9409ec150f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I rushed forward, gathered her into my arms, showered kisses on her, and pleaded, \u201cSpeak, dear heart\u2014tell me what it is!\u201d Her head drooped weakly against my chest, and she breathed, barely audible, \u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d \u201cMake haste!\u201d I cried to Clarence. \u201cTelephone the king\u2019s homeopath to come at once!\u201d Two minutes later I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, while Sandy was sending servants scurrying through every corridor of the palace. One glance told me the truth\u2014membranous croup. I bent close and murmured, \u201cWake up, darling! Hello-Central.\u201d Her soft eyes fluttered open slowly, and she managed a faint, \u201cPapa.\u201d That was some relief; she was still a long way from death. I ordered sulphur remedies, fetched the croup-kettle myself; I never wait for physicians when either Sandy or the child falls ill. I understood how to care for both of them from experience. This little lad had spent much of its brief life cradled in my arms, and often I could smooth away its troubles and coax a laugh glistening through tears when even its mother failed. Sir Launcelot, clad in his finest armor, came striding through the grand hall on his way to the stock-board. He presided over that board and had taken the Siege Perilous off Sir Galahad\u2019s hands, since the stock-board was now made up of the Knights of the Round Table using the Round Table for commercial matters. Seats there were worth figures you would never credit, so I won\u2019t attempt to quote them. Sir Launcelot was a bear; he had run a corner in one of the new enterprises and was ready today to squeeze the shorts. But no matter\u2014he remained the same old Launcelot, and one glance through the doorway, one glimpse of his little favorite lying ill, was enough. Bulls could gore bears and bears maul bulls to their hearts\u2019 content; he would be in here standing guard over Hello-Central with all the loyalty he possessed. And that\u2019s precisely what happened. He pitched his helmet into a corner, and within half a minute he was adjusting a fresh wick in the alcohol lamp and had the croup-kettle heating. Sandy by then had rigged a blanket canopy over the crib, so the arrangements were perfect. Sir Launcelot stoked the fire until the steam was up; together we packed the kettle with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a dash of lactic acid besides, then filled it with water and slipped the steam nozzle underneath the canopy. Everything was in order at last, and we took our positions on opposite sides of the crib to keep watch.\"}"
