          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"03bde4b8c7ed19b9068e7b68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet how could I, a bewildered village boy, escape that delicious inconsistency that even the greatest and wisest fall into daily?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be perfectly true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nSo I rolled onto my face at that thought, clutched the hair on both sides of my head, and gave it a hearty wrench. All the while I knew my heart\u2019s folly to be so outrageously foolish and misdirected that I felt it would have served my face right if I had hoisted it up by the hair and banged it against the pebbles for belonging to such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy, being the wisest of girls, tried to argue no further with me. She laid her hand, a comforting hand though roughened by work, on my hands one after the other, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder in a quieting way, while with my face buried on my sleeve I cried a little\u2014just as I had done in the brewery yard\u2014and felt dimly persuaded that I was much wronged by somebody, or by everybody; I can\u2019t say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cand that is, that you have felt you could trust me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another, and that is, that you know you may rely on my keeping your trust and always, so far, deserving it. If your first teacher (dear me! such a poor one, and so much in need of instruction herself!) were your teacher now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you have passed beyond her, and so it is no use now.\u201d Then, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and asked, with a fresh and cheerful change of tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, getting up, slipping my arm round her neck, and giving her a kiss, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be, so that\u2019s forever. Not that I need tell you anything, for you know everything I know\u2014as I said at home the other evening.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy, in a mere whisper, while she looked away toward the ships. Then, repeating her pleasant question, \u201cshall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did, and the summer afternoon mellowed into the summer evening, and it was very lovely.\"}"
