          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"85fbc91ef5c34427c3871ded\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The little man\u2019s body was disproportionately broad, giving him a very round and ridiculous shape. His feet were invisible, though a suspicious horn-like substance would sometimes poke out through a tear in the bottom of the car\u2014or more accurately, the top of the hat. His hands were enormous. His hair was very gray and tied in a queue at the back. His nose was long, crooked, and inflamed; his eyes were full, bright, and sharp; his chin and cheeks, though wrinkled with age, were broad, puffy, and doubled. There was not the slightest sign of ears anywhere on his head. This odd little gentleman wore a loose sky-blue satin surtout with tight breeches to match, secured by silver buckles at the knees. His vest was a bright yellow fabric; a white taffeta cap was cocked jauntily to one side; and, to finish his outfit, a blood-red silk handkerchief wrapped his neck and fell onto his chest in a dainty bow of immense size.\\n\\nWhen he had descended, as I noted before, to about a hundred feet above the ground, the little old gentleman suddenly took fright and seemed unwilling to come any closer to the earth. He dumped out some sand from a canvas bag he could barely lift and immediately stopped in place. Then, in a hurried and nervous way, he pulled a large morocco pocketbook from a side pocket of his surtout. He balanced it suspiciously in his hand, stared at it in astonishment, and was clearly surprised by how heavy it was. He finally opened it, took out a huge letter sealed with red wax and tied carefully with red tape, and let it fall right at the feet of the burgomaster, Superbus Von Underduk. His Excellency bent down to pick it up. But the aeronaut, still extremely agitated and apparently having no more business in Rotterdam, began at once to prepare for his departure. Because he needed to release ballast to rise again, he tossed out six bags in succession without bothering to empty them. Every one of them landed squarely on the burgomaster\u2019s back, rolling him over twenty-one times in front of all the citizens of Rotterdam. Of course the great Underduk did not allow such insolence from the little old man to go unpunished.\"}"
