          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"65b5b4b0d6ef07909dec0328\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is incredible! I have to meet Dorian Gray.\u201d Hallward rose from the bench and paced the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is nothing more than a source of inspiration to me. You might see nothing in him. I see everything. He is most powerful in my work when he isn\u2019t depicted at all. He is the hint of a new style. I find him in the sweep of certain lines, in the beauty and nuance of certain colors. That\u2019s it.\u201d \u201cThen why won\u2019t you show his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked. \u201cBecause, without meaning to, I poured all this strange artistic devotion into it, and of course I never wanted to tell him that. He doesn\u2019t know. He will never know. But the world might suspect, and I refuse to expose my soul to their shallow scrutiny. They won\u2019t put my heart under a microscope. There\u2019s too much of me in that painting, Harry\u2014too much!\u201d \u201cPoets aren\u2019t so careful. They know how marketable passion is. These days a broken heart gets a dozen editions.\u201d \u201cI despise them for that,\u201d Hallward exclaimed. \u201cAn artist should create beautiful things without putting his life into them. Today people treat art as if it were autobiography. We\u2019ve lost any sense of abstract beauty. One day I\u2019ll show the world what it is; and that is exactly why they will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d \u201cI think you\u2019re wrong, Basil, but I won\u2019t argue. Only the intellectually lost ever argue. Tell me, does Dorian Gray care for you?\u201d The painter thought for a moment. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he answered after a pause. \u201cI know he does. Of course I flatter him terribly. I take a strange pleasure in saying things to him that I know I\u2019ll regret. Generally he\u2019s wonderful to me, and we sit in the studio talking about a thousand topics. Every now and then, though, he\u2019s terribly inconsiderate, and seems to enjoy hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I\u2019ve handed over my entire soul to someone who treats it like a flower for his lapel, a decoration to feed his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
