          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ae08ce52dc1f0afdde10289c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were happening in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the same place from which the girl had spirited Oliver away\u2014brooding over a dull, smoky fire. He had a pair of bellows on his knees, with which he had evidently been trying to coax the fire into better spirits; but he had fallen into deep thought, and with his arms folded over them and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared absently at the rusty bars.\\n\\nAt a table behind him sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist, the Artful playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The face of the first-named gentleman, intelligent at all times, gained even greater interest from his close attention to the game and his careful reading of Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand; upon which, from time to time, as opportunity allowed, he cast various intent glances, prudently regulating his own play according to what he observed in his neighbor\u2019s cards. As it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat, as he often did indoors. He also held a clay pipe between his teeth, which he only removed briefly when he felt it necessary to reach for the quart mug on the table, filled with gin-and-water for everyone\u2019s use.\\n\\nMaster Bates was attentive to the play as well; but being more excitable than his skilled friend, he resorted more often to the gin-and-water, and also indulged in many jokes and irrelevant comments, all quite improper for a serious rubber. Indeed, the Artful, relying on their close friendship, more than once took it upon himself to lecture his companion seriously on these improprieties; all of which warnings Master Bates accepted in excellent spirits, merely asking his friend to \u201cbe blowed,\u201d or to stick his head in a sack, or replying with some other neatly turned quip of the same sort, whose clever timing impressed Mr. Chitling greatly. It was notable that the latter gentleman and his partner always lost, and that this fact, far from angering Master Bates, seemed to amuse him greatly, since he laughed uproariously at the end of every deal and swore he had never seen such a fun game in all his born days.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s two doubles and the rub,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, wearing a very long face as he pulled half a crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never saw such a fellow as you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
        }
