          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"62b814e47fe199155a7f6946\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading his letter again while answering it softened her so much that she felt she had to steady herself with a few firm phrases; and she was so upset at the thought of making him unhappy, so concerned about what his mother and sisters would think and say, so worried they might see her as ungrateful, that Emma believed if the young man had appeared at that moment, he would have won her after all.\\n\\nStill, the letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet was safe. She felt rather low all evening, but Emma understood her kindhearted regrets, easing them at times by speaking of her own affection, at times by bringing up Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she said in a mournful tone.\\n\\n\u201cNor could I ever bear to let you go, my Harriet. You are far too essential at Hartfield to be lent to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure I should never want to go anywhere else; I am happy only at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nLater she added, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what had happened. I am sure Miss Nash would\u2014Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and he is only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne would not wish for more pride or refinement in the teacher of a school, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you the chance of such a marriage. Even this triumph would seem valuable to her. As for anything better for you, I suppose she has no idea. The attentions of a certain person can hardly be gossip yet in Highbury. So far, I imagine you and I are the only ones whose eyes and ears have caught the meaning of his looks and manners.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, murmuring that she did not understand why people should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton certainly brightened her; but before long, her heart softened again toward the dismissed Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014if his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not feel it too deeply.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think of friends away from us who are more cheerfully occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cPerhaps at this moment Mr. Elton is showing your portrait to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful the original is, and after being asked five or six times, finally letting them hear your name, your very own dear name.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
