          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f80db871310e847c886b045\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She cast everything aside, save for the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nSince leaving Lady Russell\u2019s home in September, Anne had never set foot in Kellynch. There had been no need, and on the few occasions when visiting the Hall might have been possible, she contrived to avoid it. Her first return was to take up her position again in the refined modern rooms of the Lodge, and to brighten its mistress\u2019s sight.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s happiness at their reunion held a shade of unease. She knew who had been staying at Uppercross. Yet, fortunately, either Anne had truly filled out and looked better, or Lady Russell imagined it; and while Anne received the compliments, she could not help linking them to her cousin\u2019s quiet admiration, and relish the hope of enjoying another springtime of youth and allure.\\n\\nWhen conversation began, she quickly felt a shift in herself. The topics that had so filled her heart when leaving Kellynch, which she believed neglected and had had to suppress among the Musgroves, were now only of lesser importance. Of late she had scarcely thought of her father, sister, and Bath. Their concerns had been overshadowed by Uppercross affairs; and when Lady Russell returned to former hopes and anxieties, expressing satisfaction in the house secured in Camden Place and regret that Mrs Clay remained with them, Anne would have blushed to confess that Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her acquaintances there occupied her thoughts far more; that the household and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick interested her beyond her own father\u2019s residence in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs Clay. She had literally to labour at matching Lady Russell\u2019s apparent concern for matters that ought by nature to have had the foremost claim on her attention.\\n\\nTheir talk on another subject involved a touch of discomfort at first. They had to discuss the Lyme accident. Lady Russell had not been in the house five minutes the previous day before the entire story poured over her; still, it had to be spoken of. She must inquire, deplore the imprudence, lament the outcome, and Captain Wentworth\u2019s name had to pass both their lips. Anne felt she managed it less adeptly than Lady Russell. She could not utter the name while looking directly into Lady Russell\u2019s face until she resorted to the stratagem of briefly explaining what she believed of his attachment to Louisa.\"}"
