{"request_id":"4154b3c609f5101128a6b0da","rewritten_text":"I bored a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted a plug of soft wood shaped like a cone. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out until, after a few trials, I reached the exact tightness that let water seep through the hole and fill the pitcher to the brim in sixty minutes. This was easy to confirm by noting how much the pitcher filled in any set time. Once this was arranged, the rest of the plan was clear. I set up my bed on the floor of the car so that when I lay down my head was directly beneath the mouth of the pitcher. It was obvious that after an hour the pitcher would overflow at its mouth, which sat slightly lower than the rim. It was equally clear that water falling from more than four feet would land squarely on my face, and that the splash would wake me instantly, no matter how deeply I slept.

“It was eleven by the time I finished, and I went straight to bed, completely confident in my invention. Nor was I disappointed. Every sixty minutes my faithful chronometer woke me. I emptied the pitcher back into the keg’s bung-hole, tended to the condenser, and returned to bed. These regular disturbances bothered me less than I had expected; and when I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock and the sun was already well above my horizon.

“April 3rd. I found the balloon at a tremendous height, and the earth’s curve was plainly increased. Far below, on the ocean, lay a cluster of black specks that were surely islands. Far to the north I saw a thin, bright white streak along the horizon, and I was certain it was the southern edge of the Polar Sea ice. My curiosity grew, because I hoped to travel much farther north and perhaps one day hover directly above the Pole itself. I now regretted that, at such a height, I could not study the scene below as precisely as I wanted.”"}

