          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"138719ff3972e2c6fabc34a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those aged and fragile figures were always witnessed by the public bowing, and rumor had it that once they bowed a customer out, they continued bowing in the vacant office until they bowed the next customer in.\\n\\nThe barrister was shrewd enough to realize that the banker would never have gone so far in expressing his opinion without a foundation of moral certainty. Though he was unprepared for the large dose he had to swallow, he forced it down. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, brandishing his forensic forefinger toward the Temple at large when he had done so, \u201cmy way out of this is to put you all in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a specimen of Old Bailey tactics, which gave him considerable consolation. \u201cYou shall not put me in the wrong, young lady,\u201d Mr. Stryver declared; \u201cI will take care of that myself.\u201d\\n\\nThus, when Mr. Lorry arrived that night as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver, amid a clutter of books and papers spread out for effect, appeared to have nothing less on his mind than the morning\u2019s issue. He even feigned surprise at sight of Mr. Lorry and seemed wholly abstracted and preoccupied.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d exclaimed that good-humored emissary after a full half hour of fruitless efforts to lead him back to the topic. \u201cI have been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d echoed Mr. Stryver in a chill tone. \u201cOh, of course! What can I be thinking of!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am entirely satisfied,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat I was correct in the talk we had. My view stands confirmed, and I repeat my counsel.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI give you my word,\u201d replied Mr. Stryver with cordial politeness, \u201cI am sorry for it on your account, and sorry for it on the poor father\u2019s. I know this must always remain a tender point with the family; let us drop the subject.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI do not follow you,\u201d Mr. Lorry said.\\n\\n\u201cI dare say you do not,\u201d answered Stryver, nodding in a soothing yet conclusive manner; \u201cno matter, no matter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d urged Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cNo, it does not; believe me, it does not. Having imagined there was sense where there is none, and a worthy ambition where none exists, I am well rid of my blunder, and no harm ensues. Plenty of young women have played similar foolish tricks before now, and repented in poverty and obscurity often enough. Viewed unselfishly, I regret the termination, because it would have been unfortunate for me in the worldly sense; viewed selfishly, I am glad it is over, because it would have been unfortunate for me in the worldly sense\u2014it hardly needs stating that I could have gained nothing by it.\u201d\"}"
