          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"13b847c5f9eb862659be726b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, bought Canterville Chase, everyone said he was making a terrible mistake, since the place was undeniably haunted. Lord Canterville himself, an impeccably honorable man, felt it was his duty to bring up that point when they discussed terms.\\n\\n\u201cWe haven\u2019t cared to live there ourselves,\u201d Lord Canterville said, \u201cever since my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was terrified into a seizure she never truly recovered from, when two skeleton hands were laid on her shoulders as she dressed for dinner. I must tell you, Mr. Otis, that several living members of my family have seen the ghost, as has the parish rector, the Reverend Augustus Dampier, a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the poor Duchess\u2019s mishap, none of our younger servants would stay, and Lady Canterville barely slept because of the strange noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy Lord,\u201d replied the Minister, \u201cI\u2019ll pay for the furniture and the ghost. I come from a modern country where money can buy anything, and with our lively young men painting the Old World red and carrying off your best actresses and prima donnas, I figure that if there really were such a thing as a ghost in Europe, we\u2019d have it at home in one of our public museums, or touring as a show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m afraid the ghost does exist,\u201d Lord Canterville said with a smile, \u201ceven if it has ignored the offers of your ambitious impresarios. It\u2019s been notorious for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always appears before any member of our family dies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so does the family doctor, Lord Canterville. But there\u2019s no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I don\u2019t believe the laws of nature are going to be suspended for the British aristocracy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou certainly keep things natural in America,\u201d Lord Canterville answered, not entirely understanding Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark. \u201cIf you don\u2019t mind having a ghost in the house, that\u2019s fine. Just remember I warned you.\u201d\\n\\nA few weeks later, the sale was finalized, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family moved into Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, formerly Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street and once a celebrated New York beauty, was now a striking, middle-aged woman with fine eyes and a magnificent profile.\"}"
