{"request_id":"b1f1af5e3cb0dbd930662828","rewritten_text":"Do not mistake me; I was never truly mad. Even when my wild thoughts seemed to push me toward insanity, I knew exactly how I stood and shared my turmoil only with silence and solitude. Those around me saw none of it. They noticed only a poor girl, broken in spirit, who spoke softly, with downcast eyes that sometimes let slip tears she tried to hide. They knew a girl who kept to herself, shrinking from notice, who never smiled. Oh, no— I never smiled— and that was all they saw.

Still, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house and vanished; the letters I left behind and other hints convinced people that I had ended my life. Because of that, they did not search for me as carefully as they might have, and soon they lost all trace and memory of me. I left London on a small ship bound for a northern port in England. Having succeeded and now entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, the vessel moved gently onward, and I sat on deck beneath the open sky feeling myself changed. I was no longer wild, raving, and wretched Mathilda but a young hermit vowed to seclusion, trying to keep her heart free from turmoil and despair. The fanciful, nun-like clothes I wore, the knowledge that only I knew I still lived, the solitude that would be my fate forever— these nurtured gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze that played through my hair revived me, and I watched the sun glitter on the waves and the birds chase each other over the water, barely skimming it with their wings. I slept without disturbing dreams and woke refreshed, ready to enjoy my calm freedom again.

In four days we reached our harbor. I refused to stay on the coast and went inland at once. I had already chosen the kind of place where I would live: a solitary house on a wide plain, far from any other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander without being disturbed by the sight of other people. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the gentle flow of my feelings depended on my being alone. So I settled myself in a vast solitude— on a desolate heath strewn with stones, dotted with short grass, and here and there a few rushes beside a little pool."}

