          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3f38d6ca63900dcfd6313241\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have said he was a poet: at twenty-three he published his first poem, and the entire nation greeted it with excitement and delight. Fortune never stopped smiling on him; no reputation had ever risen so quickly, and soon everyone knew his name. The masses praised the very poems that amazed scholars in their studies, and not a single voice dissented.   It was at the height of this glory that he met Elinor. She was a young heiress of extraordinary beauty living under the care of her guardian, and the moment they were seen together it was clear they belonged to each other. Elinor lacked Woodville\u2019s genius, but she was generous and noble, lifted by her youth and the love she inspired everywhere into a state where she knew nothing but virtue and excellence. She was lovely, her manner open and simple, her deep blue eyes glowing with a light that only sensitivity combined with wisdom could produce.   They were made for each other, and soon they loved. Woodville felt the joy of love for the first time, and Elinor was ecstatic to hold the heart of someone so splendid and admired among men. How could anything but pure happiness come from such a union?   Woodville was a poet\u2014every society sought him, and whenever he appeared all eyes turned to him. Yet he was the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor was a rich heiress. Her guardian did not oppose their mutual affection; Woodville\u2019s worth was too great for anyone to object to his lack of wealth. But her father\u2019s dying will forbade her to marry before she came of age, and her fortune depended on obeying that command. She had just turned twenty, and she and her lover had to accept the delay. Still they were constantly together, living in what felt like Paradise: studying side by side, laying out future plans, and drinking in love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and words. They barely complained about postponing their final union. Woodville kept rising in fame, and Elinor grew lovelier and wiser under the guidance of her accomplished lover.   In two months, Elinor would turn twenty-one, and everything was ready for their marriage. How can I describe the catastrophe that struck such joy? Yet the earth would not be what it is\u2014covered with blight and sorrow\u2014if even one pair of angelic beings had been allowed to exist solely for each other. Search the world and you will not find the perfect happiness their marriage would have given them; everything in our miserable mortal order would have had to change to allow such complete joy.\"}"
