{"request_id":"4a3d520d7cfeab98c36029a7","rewritten_text":"I do not at all understand why she wishes to be close with me; yet, if the same circumstances happened again, I am sure I would be fooled again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday; in the meantime she sent no note, not even a line. When she finally came, it was obvious she took no pleasure in it; she offered a brief, formal apology for not calling sooner, kept silent about ever wanting to see me again, and was so transformed in every respect that when she left I firmly decided to keep up the acquaintance no longer. I pity her, although I cannot help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I can honestly say every step toward closeness began with her. But I pity her because she must know she has been acting wrongly, and because I am quite sure her anxiety about her brother is the cause of it. I need not explain myself further; and though we know this anxiety is entirely unnecessary, if she feels it, it easily explains how she behaves toward me; and since he is so deservedly precious to his sister, any worry she may feel for him is natural and admirable. Still, I cannot help wondering that she would have such fears now, because if he had cared for me at all, we would have met long ago. I’m sure he knows I am in town, from something she herself said; yet by the way she spoke, she seemed intent on convincing herself he is truly partial to Miss Darcy. I cannot make sense of it. If I were not afraid of being unfair, I should almost say there is a strong appearance of duplicity in all this. I will try to push away every painful thought and think only of what makes me happy—your affection, and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Please write very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned something about his never returning to Netherfield again, about giving up the house, but without any certainty. We had better not speak of it. I am very glad you hear such cheerful news from our friends at Hunsford. Please visit them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits revived as she considered that at least Jane would no longer be deceived by the sister. All expectations from the brother were now entirely over."}

