          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"789cd2522c552d16ddc3692d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper whom Mrs. Otis had agreed, at Lady Canterville\u2019s earnest plea, to retain in her old post. As they stepped down, she made each a low curtsey and, in a quaint, old-fashioned tone, said, \u2018I bid you welcome to Canterville Chase.\u2019 They followed her through the stately Tudor hall into the library, a long, low chamber paneled in black oak, with a large stained-glass window at its far end. Tea was set out for them there, and after shedding their wraps they took their seats and began to glance around, while Mrs. Umney attended to them.\\n\\nMrs. Otis suddenly noticed a dull red mark on the floor near the hearth and, unaware of its true meaning, remarked to Mrs. Umney, \u2018I fear something has been spilled there.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, madam,\u2019 the aged housekeeper replied softly, \u2018blood was spilled on that spot.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018How dreadful,\u2019 Mrs. Otis exclaimed; \u2018I do not like blood-stains in a sitting-room. It must be removed immediately.\u2019\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and, in the same low, mysterious tone, said, \u2018It is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, who was slain on that very spot by her husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon lived nine years longer, then vanished under very strange conditions. His body was never found, but his guilty spirit still roams the Chase. The stain has been admired by tourists and others and cannot be taken away.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ridiculous,\u2019 Washington Otis declared; \u2018Pinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will clean it in no time,\u2019 and before the frightened housekeeper could stop him he was on his knees, briskly scrubbing the floor with what looked like a black cosmetic stick. Within minutes no sign of the blood-stain remained.\\n\\n\u2018I knew Pinkerton would handle it,\u2019 he cried with triumph, looking over at his admiring family; yet scarcely had he spoken when a terrible flash of lightning lit the gloomy room, a thunderous crash made them leap to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u2018What an outrageous climate!\u2019 the American Minister observed calmly, lighting a long cheroot. \u2018I reckon the old country is so overpopulated there isn\u2019t enough decent weather to go around. I have always believed emigration is the only solution for England.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Hiram,\u2019 Mrs. Otis cried, \u2018what shall we do with a woman who faints?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Charge it to her like breakages,\u2019 the Minister replied; \u2018she won\u2019t faint again after that\u2019; and within a few moments Mrs. Umney indeed revived.\"}"
