          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d771017744909ca06acaadb7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He carefully drove a peg into the ground exactly where the beetle had fallen, then pulled a tape measure from his pocket. Fastening one end to the part of the tree trunk closest to the peg, he unrolled it to that peg, and, following the line established by the two points, continued another fifty feet\u2014Jupiter hacking away the brambles with the scythe. At the spot reached, he drove a second peg and scratched out a rough circle about four feet wide around it. Taking up a spade himself and handing one to Jupiter and one to me, Legrand asked us to start digging as quickly as possible.\\n\\nTo be honest, I had no taste for such entertainment at any time, and in that moment I would gladly have declined; night was approaching, and I was very tired from the day\u2019s exertions. Still, I saw no way out and feared upsetting my poor friend by refusing. If I could have counted on Jupiter\u2019s help, I would have had no qualms about trying to drag the madman home by force; but I knew the old Black man\u2019s temperament too well to expect him to assist me against his master. I was convinced Legrand had been infected by some Southern superstition about buried treasure and that the discovery of the scarab\u2014or perhaps Jupiter insisting it was \u201ca bug of real gold\u201d\u2014had bolstered his fantasy. A mind tilting toward madness could easily be carried away by such notions, especially when they encouraged cherished beliefs; I remembered his remark about the beetle being \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d Altogether, I was upset and baffled, but finally I decided to make a virtue of necessity\u2014to dig with good will so we could quickly prove to the dreamer, with plain evidence, that his ideas were false.\\n\\nOnce we lit the lanterns, we went to work with a zeal deserving a more sensible cause; and as the glare fell on our bodies and tools, I couldn\u2019t help thinking what a picturesque group we made, and how strange and suspicious our labors would have seemed to any passerby who might have stumbled upon us.\\n\\nWe dug steadily for two hours.\"}"
        }
