{"request_id":"2fa48edf8e9537ff60a69daf","rewritten_text":"They were her whole world—dearer than any place blessed by love, dearer than anything else on earth. The boys greeted the news of our relocation with childlike delight, while Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It’s possible,” I answered, and her face glowed with joy. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. She had pondered these scenes in silence, without rest; the memories had turned her childhood cheerfulness into solemnity and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

We had many beloved companions we couldn’t leave behind, humble though they might be. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; Alfred’s dog; and a pet eagle whose eyesight had dimmed with age. Yet listing the favorites who must accompany us brought grief over all we had already lost and a deep sigh for the many things we had to abandon. Tears filled Idris’s eyes as Alfred and Evelyn carried first a favorite rosebush, then a finely carved marble vase, insisting these must come and lamenting that we couldn’t take the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every cherished, familiar object with us. “Foolish, loving ones,” I said. “We’ve lost treasures far more precious than these forever, and we leave them behind to save treasures that outshine them all. Let’s not forget our purpose or our hope for even a moment; those alone can stop the flood of regret over small things.”

The children were easy to distract and soon returned to their excited plans for the future. Idris had disappeared. She had slipped away to hide her weakness, leaving the castle for the little park to seek solitude and give way to her tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing its rough trunk with her rosy lips while tears flowed freely and sobs and broken exclamations burst out despite her efforts. My heart ached to see the one I loved lost in such sorrow. I pulled her toward me; as my lips touched her eyelids and my arms held her close, she came back to what she still had. “You are so kind not to scold me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter pang of unbearable grief tears at my heart. Yet I am happy; there are mothers mourning their children, wives who have lost their husbands, but you and my children remain with me.”"}

