          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0bf73006d9dfdc6ca6371720\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The place looked like a maze of back lanes, ditches, and tiny gardens, and gave the impression of a rather dull retreat. Wemmick\u2019s house was a small wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, and its roof was cut and painted to resemble a battery with cannons.\\n\\n\u201cMy own work,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cLooks nice, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nI praised it highly. It was the smallest house I had ever seen, with the strangest gothic windows\u2014most of them fakes\u2014and a gothic door almost too small to get through.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s a real flagstaff,\u201d Wemmick continued, \u201cand on Sundays I raise a real flag. Now look. After I cross this bridge, I pull it up\u2014like this\u2014and cut off the connection.\u201d\\n\\nThe bridge was a plank spanning a gap about four feet wide and two feet deep. It was delightful to watch him lift it up and secure it, smiling as he did so with real enjoyment instead of just habit.\\n\\n\u201cAt nine every night, Greenwich time,\u201d Wemmick said, \u201cthe gun fires. There it is, you see! And when you hear it, you\u2019ll admit it\u2019s a Stinger.\u201d\\n\\nThe mentioned cannon stood in a separate little fortress made of lattice-work. It was shielded from the weather by a clever tarpaulin setup like an umbrella.\\n\\n\u201cThen, at the back,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cout of sight so it doesn\u2019t spoil the idea of fortifications\u2014because my rule is, if you have an idea, carry it through and keep it up\u2014I don\u2019t know if you agree\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI said I certainly did.\\n\\n\u201cAt the back there\u2019s a pig, and there are chickens and rabbits; then I build my own little frames, you see, and grow cucumbers; and at supper you\u2019ll see what kind of salad I can produce. So, sir,\u201d Wemmick added, smiling again but with seriousness as he shook his head, \u201cif you imagine the place under siege, it could hold out wonderfully for supplies.\u201d\\n\\nThen he led me to a bower about twelve yards away, reached by such clever twists in the path that it took a long while to get there, and in this hideaway our glasses were already set. Our punch was chilling in an ornamental lake, on the bank of which the bower stood. This body of water\u2014with an island in the middle that might have been the salad for supper\u2014was circular, and he had built a fountain in it which, when you started a small mill and pulled a cork from a pipe, played so vigorously it made the back of your hand quite wet.\"}"
