          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"745877c2d81d5163e8b2e9c6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To regard a star by mere sidelong glances\u2014to look at it obliquely, employing the outer zones of the retina (more receptive of faint impressions than the central)\u2014is to see the star clearly, to grasp its lustre most fully; that same lustre fades in exact ratio as we direct our gaze straight upon it. In the latter case a larger number of rays indeed enter the eye, yet, in the former, the faculty of apprehension is more delicate. Excessive profundity bewilders and weakens thought; even Venus herself can be banished from the heavens by an examination too prolonged, too intense, too direct.\\n\\n\u201cAs to these murders, let us conduct our own investigations before forming any conclusion concerning them. Such an inquiry will amuse us,\u201d [I deemed this an odd expression under the circumstances, but held my tongue] \u201cand, besides, Le Bon once did me a kindness, for which I remain grateful. We will go inspect the premises personally. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and shall readily secure the necessary authorization.\u201d\\n\\nThe authorization was secured, and we set out immediately for the Rue Morgue\u2014one of those wretched streets lying between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. It was late in the afternoon when we arrived, this district being at a considerable remove from our usual haunts. The house was easy to discover, many persons still standing across the way, staring up at the shuttered windows with idle curiosity. It was an ordinary Parisian dwelling, having a gateway, and, on one side, a glazed porter\u2019s lodge with a sliding panel in the window, denoting a loge de concierge. Before entering we walked up the street, turned down an alley, and then, doubling back, passed along the rear of the building\u2014Dupin all the while examining both the house and the surrounding neighborhood with a minuteness of scrutiny for which I could imagine no motive.\\n\\nRetracing our way, we returned to the front door, rang, and, after presenting our credentials, gained admission from the officials in charge. We ascended to the chamber where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye had been discovered, and where both corpses still lay. The disorder in the room, as usual, had been allowed to remain. I observed nothing beyond what had already been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin inspected everything\u2014even the bodies of the victims. Afterwards we visited the other rooms and the yard, a gendarme escorting us throughout.\"}"
        }
