          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"997c78f1dc074032067c541b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s wrong?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you talking about? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. You told me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Right. All right, all right.\u201d\\n\\nAfter a few sluggish attempts to fall asleep again\u2014efforts the servant expertly thwarted by poking the fire for several minutes\u2014he got up, threw on his hat, and went out. He turned into the Temple, and after restoring himself by pacing the pavement of King\u2019s Bench Walk and Paper Buildings twice, he headed to Stryver\u2019s chambers.\\n\\nStryver\u2019s clerk, who never attended these meetings, had gone home, so Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing gown, his throat bare for comfort. His eyes bore that wild, strained, scarred look common to every hard drinker of his type, from Jeffries\u2019s portrait onward, visible under every artistic disguise in the portraits of every drinking era.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re a bit late, Memory,\u201d Stryver said.\\n\\n\u201cJust about on time; perhaps fifteen minutes behind.\u201d\\n\\nThey entered a dingy room lined with books and littered with papers, where a fire roared. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the chaos of papers, a table gleamed with ample wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I\u2019ve been dining with the client\u2014or watching him dine, which is the same thing!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was a remarkable point you made about the identification, Sydney. How did you come up with it? When did it hit you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was rather handsome, and I figured I\u2019d have turned out about the same if I\u2019d had any luck.\u201d\\n\\nStryver laughed until his precocious paunch shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nGrudgingly, the jackal loosened his clothes, went to the next room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. Dipping the towels in the water and partially wringing them out, he folded them around his head in a hideous fashion, sat at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much boiling down tonight, Memory,\u201d Stryver said cheerfully as he sorted through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive me the worst first.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere you go, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion settled on his back on a sofa on one side of the drinking table, while the jackal took his seat at his own cluttered table on the other side, bottles and glasses within easy reach.\"}"
