          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7b2e82bac1c3e527d5023af2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General had to tell the jury that the prisoner before them, though young, had long been steeped in the treason that now threatened his life. This correspondence with the public enemy did not begin today, or yesterday, or even last year or the year before. It was certain the prisoner had for a longer time been coming and going between France and England on secret business he could not honestly explain. If treason ever prospered\u2014which fortunately it never did\u2014the true wickedness of his schemes might have stayed hidden. Providence, however, moved someone beyond fear and reproach to uncover the prisoner\u2019s plans and, horrified, report them to His Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and the most honourable Privy Council. This patriot would appear before them. His position and bearing were, altogether, sublime. He had been the prisoner\u2019s friend, but in an hour both fortunate and disastrous, the moment he detected his infamy he resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer cherish in his own heart on the sacred altar of his country. If statues were awarded in Britain, as in ancient Greece and Rome, to public benefactors, this shining citizen would surely have one. Since no such statues were decreed, he probably would not. Virtue, as the poets had observed (in countless passages which the jury, he felt certain, could recite word for word\u2014at which the jury\u2019s faces showed they knew nothing about those passages), is somewhat contagious, especially the radiant virtue called patriotism, or love of country. The high example of this flawless, unimpeachable witness for the Crown\u2014merely to mention whom, however unworthy the speaker, was an honour\u2014inspired the prisoner\u2019s servant and filled him with a holy resolve to inspect his master\u2019s desk drawers and pockets and to hide away his papers. Mr. Attorney-General expected attempts 