{"request_id":"f1db0dff5e6cba091fb57f41","rewritten_text":"Upon reaching the head of the staircase he regathered his composure and resolved to deliver his famous peal of fiendish laughter. More than once it had served him admirably. Rumor credited it with turning Lord Raker’s wig grey in a single night, and it had certainly driven three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to resign before their month expired. He therefore laughed his most ghastly laugh until the old vaulted ceiling echoed and re-echoed, but scarcely had the dreadful reverberation faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis stepped out in a pale blue dressing-gown. ‘I fear you are quite unwell,’ she remarked, ‘and have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. Should it be indigestion, you will find it an excellent cure.’ The ghost glowered at her in wrath and instantly began arranging to transform himself into a large black hound, a feat for which he was rightly celebrated, and to which the family physician always ascribed the lasting idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. The sound of oncoming footsteps, however, made him pause in his sinister design, so he contented himself with becoming faintly phosphorescent and disappeared with a deep churchyard groan just as the twins arrived beside him.

Once back in his chamber he completely collapsed and fell victim to the most violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s coarse materialism were naturally maddening, yet what really wounded him was his failure to wear the suit of armor. He had hoped even modern Americans might be thrilled by the spectacle of a Specter in Armour, if for no more sensible reason than in homage to their national bard Longfellow, whose graceful, charming verse had beguiled many a weary hour for him while the Cantervilles were in town. Besides, the suit was his own. He had displayed it with great éclat at the Kenilworth tournament and been warmly praised by none other than the Virgin Queen herself. Nevertheless, when he donned it he was utterly overwhelmed by the weight of the massive breastplate and steel helmet and had crashed down upon the stone flags, barking both knees badly and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days after this he was very ill and scarcely left his room at all, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. Yet by taking meticulous care of himself he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt to terrify the Minister of the United States and his household."}

