          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb2ae4477f81e7dad5de7d25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Furthermore, hardly anyone outside the sea-going profession can fashion this knot, and it is particularly Maltese. I retrieved the ribbon at the base of the lightning-rod, and it could not have belonged to either of the victims. Even if my deduction from the ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman served on a Maltese ship\u2014proves mistaken, nothing is lost by what I stated in the advertisement. If I err, he will imagine I was misdirected by some circumstance and will not bother to look into it. Yet if I am correct, we have gained a great advantage. Though aware of the crime, yet guiltless, the Frenchman will naturally hesitate to respond to the notice\u2014to claim the Ourang-Outang. He will reason: \u201cI am innocent, impoverished, and my Ourang-Outang is exceedingly valuable\u2014a fortune for a man in my position. Why should I forfeit it because of idle fears? It is within my reach. It was discovered in the Bois de Boulogne, far removed from that slaughter. How could anyone suspect a brute creature was responsible? The police are completely baffled\u2014they have not obtained the smallest clue. Even if they could track the animal, they could never prove I knew of the murder, or involve me in guilt through that knowledge. Above all, I am known. The advertiser points me out as the owner of the animal. I cannot be sure how much he actually knows. If I do not claim property so valuable, known to be mine, I will, at the very least, render the creature itself subject to suspicion. Drawing attention to myself or to it is not wise. I shall answer the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it hidden until the affair has blown over.\u201d\\n\\nJust then footsteps sounded on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d Dupin told me, \u201ckeep your pistols at hand, but do not display or use them until I give the signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe front door had been left open, so the visitor entered without ringing and had already taken several steps on the staircase. But now he seemed to falter. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin was heading swiftly to the door when the footsteps ascended once more. This time the visitor did not retreat but climbed firmly to the top and knocked on our chamber door.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d Dupin called, in a bright and cordial voice.\\n\\nA man walked in. He was plainly a sailor\u2014a tall, brawny figure with a certain reckless look about him, though not without a degree of rough attractiveness.\"}"
