          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dac49af5f7ed4104c6a81166\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShould I call them in? Or Miss Bates? She is even closer\u2014and I suspect Miss Bates is as able as anyone to guess what the others want. I believe we do need a larger council. What if I invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d Mrs. Weston answered, hesitating. \u201cIf you think she will be of any use.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will learn nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, yet she will tell you nothing. She will not even listen to your questions. I can see no benefit in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining, so very entertaining! I enjoy hearing Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the entire family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Weston joined them then, and when he heard the plan, he gave it his firm approval.\\n\\n\u201cAye, do, Frank\u2014go fetch Miss Bates and let us settle the matter at once. She will love the scheme, I am sure; and I do not know a fitter person to show us how to remove difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming a bit too particular. She is a constant lesson in how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady?\u2026\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great simpleton, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not recall it at once. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And he ran off.\\n\\nLong before he reappeared, escorting the short, neat, quick-moving aunt and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston\u2014like a kind-tempered woman and a good wife\u2014had inspected the passage again and found its flaws far less than she had believed before, truly trifling; and thus the hard part of the decision ended. Everything else, at least in planning, was perfectly smooth. All the minor arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, fell into place by themselves or were left as mere trifles to settle at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes. Everyone invited was certain to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to ask for a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be denied. And it was sure to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she agreed heartily it must be so. They did not need her as an adviser, but as an approver (a much safer role) she was sincerely welcome. Her approval\u2014both general and detailed, warm and unceasing\u2014could not help but please; and for another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the rooms, some suggesting, some listening, all happily enjoying the future.\"}"
