          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd0b2ffc368a813991ba32ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Count Rouvaloff stared at him in astonishment for several seconds, and then, perceiving he was perfectly in earnest, wrote an address on a slip of paper, initialed it, and slid it across the table.  \\n\\n\u201cScotland Yard would pay handsomely for that address, my dear friend.\u201d  \\n\\n\u201cThey shan\u2019t get it,\u201d laughed Lord Arthur; and after shaking the young Russian\u2019s hand warmly, he ran downstairs, inspected the paper, and instructed the coachman to drive to Soho Square.  \\n\\nThere he dismissed his driver and wandered down Greek Street until he reached a passage called Bayle\u2019s Court. Passing under the archway, he found himself in a curious cul-de-sac apparently devoted to a French laundry, for clothes-lines crisscrossed from house to house and white linen fluttered in the morning air. He walked to the far end and knocked at a tiny green house. After some delay\u2014during which every window in the court became a blurred patch of prying faces\u2014the door opened to reveal a rather rough-looking foreigner, who asked in dreadful English what his business might be. Lord Arthur produced the paper Count Rouvaloff had given him. Seeing it, the man bowed and invited him into a very shabby front parlour on the ground floor; a few moments later Herr Winckelkopf, as he was known in England, hurried into the room with a wine-stained napkin around his neck and a fork clutched in his left hand.  \\n\\n\u201cCount Rouvaloff has sent me to you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, bowing, \u201cand I wish to have a short interview on a matter of business. My name is Smith\u2014Robert Smith\u2014and I want you to provide me with an explosive clock.\u201d  \\n\\n\u201cDelighted to meet you, Lord Arthur,\u201d replied the jovial little German, laughing. \u201cPray don\u2019t look so shocked; it is my duty to know everyone, and I remember catching sight of you one evening at Lady Windermere\u2019s. I trust her ladyship is in perfect health. Would you mind sitting with me while I finish breakfast? There is an excellent p\u00e2t\u00e9, and my friends insist my Rhine wine surpasses anything at the German Embassy.\u201d And before Lord Arthur had recovered from the surprise of being recognized, he found himself in the back room, sipping exquisite Marcobr\u00fcnner from a pale yellow hock-glass bearing the Imperial monogram and chatting in the most amiable fashion imaginable with the notorious conspirator.  \\n\\n\u201cExplosive clocks,\u201d observed Herr Winckelkopf, \u201care seldom satisfactory for foreign shipment; even if they slip past the Custom House, the trains run so erratically that they usually explode long before arriving at their intended destination.\u201d\"}"
