          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a33102db2de47ab5b31a16e9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But now she wished it had never happened. She was sure he had been drinking too much of Mr. Weston\u2019s good wine and that he would want to talk nonsense.\\n\\nTo keep him in check as much as possible, she was preparing to speak about the weather and the night with perfect calm and seriousness. Yet hardly had she begun, hardly had they passed through the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, when her topic was cut short\u2014her hand was seized, her attention demanded, and Mr. Elton was suddenly pouring out intense declarations of love. He took advantage of the moment, insisting that his feelings must already be obvious, hoping and fearing and worshipping, ready to die if she rejected him, yet convinced that his passionate devotion and unmatched love could not fail to win her. He was determined to be accepted quickly. It was truly happening. Without hesitation, apology, or any sign of doubt, Mr. Elton, Harriet\u2019s admirer, was claiming to be her suitor. She tried to stop him, but without success; he insisted on saying it all. Angry as she was, she decided to keep her temper when she finally spoke. She believed that half this foolishness came from drunkenness and hoped it might last only for the night. So, using a mix of seriousness and playfulness that she thought might suit his half-sober state, she answered,\\n\\n\u201cI am very much astonished, Mr. Elton. This to me! You are forgetting yourself\u2014you mistake me for my friend. Any message for Miss Smith I will gladly deliver; but no more of this to me, if you please.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Smith! A message to Miss Smith! What could she mean!\u201d he cried, repeating her words with such confident and triumphant astonishment that she could not help replying quickly,\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elton, this is the most extraordinary behaviour! I can explain it only one way; you are not yourself, or you could not speak of either me or Harriet like this. Control yourself enough to say no more, and I will try to forget it.\u201d\\n\\nBut Mr. Elton had drunk only enough to heighten his spirits, not to confuse his mind. He knew exactly what he meant; and after insisting that her suspicion was very unfair, briefly touching on his respect for Miss Smith as her friend\u2014though expressing surprise that Miss Smith should be mentioned at all\u2014he returned to his declaration of love and pressed hard for a favourable reply.\"}"
