{"request_id":"a5d22eee60b6ef0e97d26118","rewritten_text":"The only point that bothered him was whom to kill, because he understood that murder, like the old pagan religions, needs both a priest and a victim. He had no enemies—he was no genius—and he knew this was not the moment to indulge any personal grudge, since his mission was serious and solemn. He therefore listed his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper and, after careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old woman who lived on Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and since he was already rich, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, he stood to gain nothing crude or monetary from her death. Indeed, the more he considered it, the more she seemed the right choice, and, knowing any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his arrangements at once.

The first step, of course, was to settle with the cheiromantist. He sat down at the small Sheraton writing table by the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, placed it in an envelope, and told his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. Then he telephoned the stables to send his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake but would carry the secret of his self-sacrifice forever in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham he stopped at a florist, sending Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus with pure white petals and staring pheasant eyes. When he reached the club, he went straight to the library, rang for the waiter, and ordered a lemon-and-soda and a book on toxicology. He had already decided that poison was the best method for this troublesome task. He found any form of personal violence deeply distasteful, and besides, he was anxious not to kill Lady Clementina in a way that would attract public attention; he hated the idea of being lionized at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name splashed across vulgar society newspapers’ columns."}

