          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"48e6820adbe805e6632f871d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sydney Carton paused in the street, unsure where to go. \u201cAt Tellson\u2019s bank at nine,\u201d he murmured thoughtfully. \u201cShould I show myself in the meantime? I think so. These people should know I am here; it\u2019s a sensible precaution and might be necessary. But careful, careful! Let me think this through.\u201d\\n\\nHe stopped moving toward his first impulse, paced the darkening street a few times, and followed the idea to its possible outcomes. His first instinct held. \u201cIt is best,\u201d he decided, \u201cthat these people should know there is such a man as I here.\u201d He turned toward Saint Antoine.\\n\\nDefarge had described himself that day as a wine-shop keeper in the Saint Antoine suburb. Someone who knew the city well could find his shop without asking directions. After locating it, Carton left the narrow streets, ate at a small restaurant, and fell sound asleep after the meal. For the first time in years, he had no strong liquor. Since the previous night he had taken only a little thin wine, and the night before he had poured the brandy onto Mr. Lorry\u2019s hearth as if he were finished with it.\\n\\nIt was around seven when he woke refreshed and went out again. On his way back toward Saint Antoine, he paused at a shop window with a mirror, straightened his loose cravat and coat collar, and smoothed his unkempt hair. Then he went straight to Defarge\u2019s and entered.\\n\\nThere were no customers except Jacques Three, with his restless fingers and croaking voice. This man, whom Carton had seen on the Jury, stood drinking at the counter, talking with Monsieur and Madame Defarge. The Vengeance joined in the conversation like one of the household.\\n\\nCarton walked in, sat down, and asked in indifferent French for a small measure of wine. Madame Defarge glanced at him carelessly, then more sharply, then walked over herself to ask what he had ordered.\\