          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"39d78ca2711c7c48f718d083\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If you choose otherwise, and look to my leniency and the mercy of those you have grievously wronged, take that chair in silence. It has been awaiting you for two whole days.\u201d\\n\\nMonks muttered some indistinct words, yet still faltered.\\n\\n\u201cYou shall act quickly,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow. \u201cShould I utter a word, the other course will be lost forever.\u201d\\n\\nThe man remained undecided.\\n\\n\u201cI have neither temper for argument,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, \u201cnor any right to indulge it while I champion the dearest concerns of others.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs there\u2014\u201d stammered Monks, his voice unsteady,\u2014\u201cis there\u2014no compromise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNone whatever.\u201d\\n\\nMonks eyed the old gentleman anxiously; but discovering in his face only stern resolve, he stepped inside, and with a shrug, took his seat.\\n\\n\u201cSecure the door from the outside,\u201d Mr. Brownlow instructed the attendants, \u201cand return when I ring.\u201d\\n\\nThey complied, leaving the pair by themselves.\\n\\n\u201cThis is gracious usage, sir,\u201d said Monks, casting down his hat and cloak, \u201cfrom the oldest friend my father had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is precisely because I was your father\u2019s oldest friend, young man,\u201d replied Mr. Brownlow; \u201cbecause the dreams and wishes of young and joyful days were entwined with him, and with that fair being of his blood and kin who met her God in early life, leaving me here a solitary, desolate man. It is because he knelt beside me at his only sister\u2019s death-bed when he was still a boy, on the morning which\u2014save for Heaven\u2019s decree\u2014would have made her my young bride; because my scarred heart clung to him from then on, through all his struggles and misdeeds, until his death; because ancient memories and ties surged within me, and even the sight of you calls up old impressions of him; for all these reasons I am driven to deal tenderly with you now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford, even now\u2014and blush for the disgrace of your bearing that name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat does the name matter?\u201d demanded the other, after regarding, half silently and half in stubborn astonishment, his companion\u2019s agitation. \u201cWhat is the name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, \u201cnothing to you. But it was _hers_, and even after so long it calls back to me\u2014the aged man I am now\u2014the warmth and thrill I once felt merely to hear it spoken by a stranger. I am truly glad you cast it off\u2014truly\u2014truly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very fine,\u201d said Monks (keeping his assumed title) after a considerable pause, during which he had flung himself back and forth in sullen defiance, while Mr. Brownlow sat with his face hidden by his hand.\"}"
