          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0253e486df3e375808c08163\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and horses in heavy armor couldn\u2019t travel very fast. They would go as quickly as they could, and now that the ground was dry with no snow or mud, they would probably move at seven miles an hour. They would have to change horses a couple of times and would arrive around six, or a little later. It would still be light, and they would see the white cloth I planned to tie around my right arm so they could recognize me. Then I would take command. We would surround the prison and get the king out quickly. The whole scene would be dramatic and impressive, although I would have preferred noon for the more theatrical effect it would give.\\n\\nTo add more options, I thought I would look up some people I had known before and reveal myself to them. That might help us out of our trouble without needing the knights. But I had to be careful, because it was risky. I needed to dress richly, and I couldn\u2019t just rush into it. I would build up to it slowly by buying several suits in shops far apart, each one a little finer than the last, until I reached silk and velvet and was ready for my plan. So I set out.\\n\\nBut the scheme collapsed immediately! At the first corner I ran into one of our slaves who was sneaking around with a watchman. I coughed at that moment, and he shot me a look that tore right through me. I think he believed he had heard that cough before. I stepped into a shop right away and moved along the counter, pricing things and watching them from the corner of my eye. They had stopped and were talking while staring in through the door. I decided to escape through the back, if there was a back way. I asked the shopwoman if I could step out there to search for the escaped slave, who was thought to be hiding somewhere in back, and said I was an officer in disguise. I told her my partner was at the door with one of the murderers in custody, and asked if she would kindly tell him not to wait but to go to the far end of the back alley and be ready to cut the slave off when I flushed him out.\\n\\nShe was burning with curiosity to see one of those already famous murderers and hurried off on the errand at once.\"}"
