          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"839df7a991e6521c2ef6f133\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Just then both his gaze and mine landed on the scrap of parchment, which I had first taken for paper. It lay half buried in the sand with one corner sticking up. Nearby I noticed the remains of what looked like the hull of a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck must have been there for ages, because it barely still resembled boat timbers.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Soon after, we started home and met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he begged me to let him take it to the fort. When I agreed, he promptly slipped it into his waistcoat pocket, without the parchment it had been wrapped in, which I\u2019d kept in my hand while he examined the beetle. Perhaps he feared I might change my mind and figured he should secure his prize at once\u2014you know how fervent he is about anything related to Natural History. At the same time, without realizing it, I must have tucked the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou remember when I sat down at the table to sketch the beetle, I couldn\u2019t find any paper where I usually kept it. I looked in the drawer and found none there. I searched my pockets, hoping for an old letter, when my hand touched the parchment. That\u2019s the exact way it came into my possession; the whole incident stuck with me vividly.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you\u2019ll think me imaginative\u2014but I\u2019d already made a sort of connection. I had linked two pieces of a larger chain. There was a boat on a shore, and not far from it, a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn on it. Naturally you\u2019ll ask, \u2018What\u2019s the connection?\u2019 I answer that a skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the recognized symbol of pirates. The death\u2019s-head flag flies in every engagement.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve said the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment is durable\u2014almost indestructible. Trivial matters are rarely recorded on parchment; for simple drawing or writing, it\u2019s far less handy than paper. That thought hinted at some purpose, some significance, in the death\u2019s-head. I also noticed the parchment\u2019s shape. Though one corner had been damaged by some accident, it was clear the original had been oblong.\u201d\"}"
