          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2555d8324b87efadf188fe0f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After seeing our friend settled in his new office, we turned back toward Windsor. The nearness of the place to London softened the thought of parting from Raymond and Perdita. We said goodbye in the Protectoral Palace. It was pleasant to watch my sister take on her role, trying to carry it with suitable dignity. Her inner pride and outward humility were still at odds. Her shyness was genuine, born from fear of being misunderstood and from the slight sting of neglect that also troubled Raymond. Yet Perdita was more mindful of others; part of her bashfulness came from wanting to spare those around her any feeling of inferiority, a feeling she never aimed to evoke. Given her birth and education, Idris would have handled every ceremonial form better; but her ease, built on habit, made such tasks tedious. Perdita, despite the difficulties, clearly enjoyed her place. Filled with new thoughts, she felt little sorrow when we left; she kissed us affectionately and promised to visit soon, yet she did not regret the cause of our separation. Raymond\u2019s spirits were boundless; unsure how to wield his fresh power, his head brimmed with plans without a single decision\u2014still, he promised himself, his friends, and the world that his Protectorship would shine through some act of supreme glory. Talking of them and drawing moral lessons, we returned with smaller numbers to Windsor Castle. We rejoiced in our escape from political upheaval and embraced solitude with renewed pleasure. Work was plentiful, yet my restless nature now aimed at intellectual effort alone, and serious study proved an excellent cure for the fever of spirit that would have overtaken me in idleness. Perdita let us bring Clara back to Windsor; she and my two beautiful infants were constant sources of delight and amusement.\\n\\nOnly one thing disturbed our peace: Adrian\u2019s health. It clearly declined, though no symptoms revealed the cause, unless his bright eyes, animated expression, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption; yet he felt neither pain nor dread. He threw himself eagerly into his books and rested from study in the company he cherished most, that of his sister and myself.\"}"
