{"request_id":"2d4f5b3c6140f53f05e23efd","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sunk in sorrow and despair; her usual pursuits no longer charmed her, and any hint of enjoyment felt like sacrilege to the dead. She believed perpetual grief and tears the only fitting homage to innocence so devastated. She was no longer the joyous being who once roamed the lakeshore with me in youthful days, speaking rapturously of what awaited us. The earliest of those sorrows meant to sever our ties to earth had descended on her, and its shadow dimmed her fondest smiles.

“When I consider, my dear cousin,” she said, “the dreadful end of Justine Moritz, the world and all within it no longer look as they once did. Formerly, when I heard of vice or injustice in books or conversation, I treated them as legends of bygone times or mere fantasies; they seemed distant and easier to grasp with reason than with imagination. But now misery has entered our home, and men seem to me like monsters eager for each other’s blood. Still, I know I am being unfair. Everyone believed that unfortunate girl guilty; and had she truly committed the act for which she died, she would have been one of the vilest of human beings. For a few jewels to murder her benefactor’s child—a boy she had cared for since infancy and appeared to love as her own! I could not sanction the death of any person, yet I would certainly have deemed such a wretch unfit for human society. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel it; and your agreement confirms me. Alas, Victor, when falsehood can masquerade as truth, how can anyone be sure of happiness? I feel as though I tread the brink of a precipice, with thousands pressing toward me, striving to hurl me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the assassin goes free; he moves through the world, perhaps even honored. Yet, even if I were sentenced to die on the scaffold for those very crimes, I would not exchange fates with such a villain.”

I heard her words with the most acute anguish. I—though not by deed, yet by consequence—was the real murderer. Elizabeth saw my torment reflected in my face and, gently taking my hand, said, “My dearest friend, you must compose yourself. Heaven knows how deeply I have been shaken, but I am not so miserable as you.”"}

