          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e6b0e2228d409c53c06c58f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We shouted and insisted so loudly that we were free men that the liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic crowd paid attention, gathered around us, and took on a very stern air. The orator said, \u201cIf you truly are freemen, you have nothing to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround you like a shield and shelter! (Applause.) You shall soon see. Bring forth your proof.\u201d \u201cWhat proof?\u201d \u201cProof that you are freemen.\u201d Then I remembered and kept quiet. But the king burst out, \u201cYou are mad, man. It is more reasonable that this thief and scoundrel prove that we are not freemen.\u201d He knew his own laws the way most people do\u2014by words, not by consequences. The words take on a vivid meaning when you must apply them to yourself. Everyone shook their heads, looking disappointed; some walked away, uninterested. The orator spoke again, now in a businesslike tone, not a sentimental one: \u201cIf you do not know your country\u2019s laws, it is time you learned them. You are strangers to us; you cannot deny it. You may be freemen; we do not deny that. But you may also be slaves. The law is clear: it does not require the claimant to prove you are slaves; it requires you to prove you are not.\u201d I said, \u201cDear sir, give us time to send to Astolat; or give us time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d \u201cPeace, good man. Those requests are extraordinary, and you cannot expect them to be granted. It would take much time and cause undue trouble to your master\u2014\u201d \u201cMaster, idiot!\u201d the king shouted. \u201cI have no master, I myself am the m\u2014\u201d \u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d I got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in enough trouble; nothing could be gained by convincing these people we were lunatics. There\u2019s no use drawing it out. The earl put us up and sold us at auction. This same infernal law existed in America\u2019s South in my own time, thirteen hundred years later. Under it, hundreds of freemen who couldn\u2019t prove their freedom were sold into lifelong slavery, and it never made much impression on me; but when the law and the auction block touched my own life, what had merely seemed improper before suddenly became hellish. That\u2019s how we are made. Yes, we were sold at auction like swine. In a big town with a busy market we would have brought a good price, but this place was utterly stagnant, so we sold for a sum that shames me every time I think of it.\"}"
