          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"af231c6ec8fe2e466e490620\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day he never left the house, and in fact stayed mostly in his own room, sick with a wild fear of dying yet indifferent to life itself. The awareness of being hunted, trapped, tracked down had begun to rule him. If the tapestry merely fluttered in the wind, he trembled. The dead leaves blown against the leaded windows seemed like his own squandered resolutions and reckless regrets. Whenever he shut his eyes, he again saw the sailor\u2019s face peering through the mist-streaked glass, and horror seemed to clamp its hand on his heart once more.\\n\\nBut perhaps it was only his imagination that had summoned vengeance out of the night and set the hideous shapes of punishment before him. Actual life was chaotic, yet the imagination had a terrible logic. It was the imagination that made remorse follow sin\u2019s footsteps. It was the imagination that made each crime bear its misshapen offspring. In the ordinary world of facts, the wicked were not punished, nor the good rewarded. Success was handed to the strong; failure forced on the weak. That was all. Besides, if any stranger had been prowling around the house, the servants or the guards would have seen him. If any footprints had been found in the flower beds, the gardeners would have reported it. Yes, it had been only his imagination. Sibyl Vane\u2019s brother had not returned to kill him. He had sailed off on his ship to sink in some winter sea. From that man, at least, he was safe. After all, the man did not know who he was; he could not know who he was. His mask of youth had saved him.\\n\\nAnd yet if it had been only an illusion, how terrible it was that conscience could raise such frightening phantoms, give them form, and make them move before you! What sort of life would he have if, day and night, the shadows of his crime were to watch him from silent corners, mock him from hidden places, whisper in his ear while he sat at a feast, wake him with icy fingers while he slept! As the thought crept through his mind, he grew pale with terror, and the air seemed suddenly colder. Oh, in what wild moment of madness he had killed his friend! How ghastly the mere memory of the scene! He saw it all again. Every hideous detail returned with fresh horror. From the black cave of time, wrapped in scarlet and terrible, rose the image of his sin.\"}"
        }
      ],
