content.text:
{"request_id":"4c91e6362f99d72d32e8ebf3","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name never came up, yet I couldn’t doubt that love for her caused the wavering he showed. And who was more worthy of love than my noble-minded sister? Who deserved the hand of this self-anointed king more than she, whose glance could belong to a queen of nations? She loved him as much as he loved her; even though disappointment had stifled her passion, ambition still battled fiercely with his.

We went together to the House that evening. Raymond, though he knew his plans and prospects would be examined and decided in the impending debate, seemed cheerful and carefree. A hum like that of ten thousand hives of swarming bees overwhelmed us as we stepped into the coffee-room. Clusters of politicians stood together with furrowed brows and loud or hushed voices. The aristocratic party—the wealthiest and most influential men in England—looked less rattled than the rest, because the question would be debated without their direct involvement. By the fire stood Ryland and his supporters. Ryland was of obscure birth but inherited immense wealth from his manufacturer father. As a young man he had seen the king’s abdication and the merging of the Houses of Lords and Commons; he supported those popular gains, and making them permanent had been his life’s work. Since then, the influence of landowners had grown; at first, Ryland wasn’t unhappy to see Lord Raymond’s schemes, which drew away many of his adversaries’ followers. But things had gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of monarchy as something that would restore their lost power and rights. The nearly extinct spirit of royalty revived in people’s minds; willing slaves, self-appointed subjects, they were ready to bow their necks to the yoke. A few upright, stalwart souls remained, pillars of the state, but the word republic had become stale to common ears, and many—whether a majority would soon become clear—yearned for the gilt and spectacle of kingship. Ryland rose to oppose them; he insisted that only his tolerance had allowed this faction to grow, but the time for indulgence was over, and with one sweep of his arm he would brush away the cobwebs that blinded his countrymen.

When Raymond walked into the coffee-room, his friends greeted him with near-cheers. They crowded around him, took stock of their numbers, and listed the reasons why they were about to gain certain additional members who had yet to declare themselves."}

