content.text:
{"request_id":"423b7cea393953f61ed13dc2","rewritten_text":"The only matter that seemed to disquiet him was whom he ought to dispose of; for he recognized perfectly well that murder, like every cult of the Pagan world, demands both a celebrant and a victim. Being no genius, he had no animosities, and indeed felt this was no season for indulging any personal grudge or aversion, since the undertaking before him was one of grave and solemn import. He therefore compiled a list of friends and relations on a sheet of notepaper and, after deliberate reflection, selected Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady residing in Curzon Street who was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been exceedingly fond of Lady Clem, as she was universally called, and, being very wealthy himself—having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property on attaining his majority—there was no possibility of reaping any vulgar pecuniary profit from her demise. Indeed, the more he pondered it, the more she appeared the fitting choice, and, feeling that any postponement would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to set his plans in motion immediately.

The first order of business was, naturally, to square matters with the cheiromantist; so he seated himself at the little Sheraton writing-table near the window, drew a cheque for £105 payable to the order of Mr. Septimus Podgers, and, sealing it in an envelope, instructed his valet to deliver it to West Moon Street. He then telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever befell, he would never let her learn what he was doing on her behalf, but would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice forever locked within his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham he paused at a florist’s and dispatched to Sybil a exquisite basket of narcissus, with pure white petals and bold pheasants’ eyes; and upon reaching the club he went straight to the library, rang for the waiter, and ordered a lemon-and-soda and a book on toxicology. He had settled quite firmly that poison was the most suitable method for this troublesome affair. Any form of personal violence was thoroughly repugnant to him, and he was particularly anxious not to kill Lady Clementina in any fashion that might draw public notice, for he detested the notion of being lionised at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name paraded in the columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

