          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"462eecc5f1aa4993e4df6738\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning she was found dead in her bed, apparently without having felt any pain. Sir Mathew Reid had been called at once, but naturally there was nothing he could do, and she was to be buried on the twenty-second at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death she had written her will, leaving Lord Arthur her little house in Curzon Street along with all her furniture, personal belongings, and paintings, except for her collection of miniatures, which was to go to her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which Sybil Merton was to have. The estate was not worth very much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was extremely eager for Lord Arthur to come back immediately, if possible, since there were many bills to settle, and Lady Clementina had never kept any proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s thoughtful remembrance of him, and he felt that Mr. Podgers had a great deal to answer for. Still, his love for Sybil overshadowed every other feeling, and the knowledge that he had done his duty brought him peace and comfort. When he reached Charing Cross, he felt perfectly content.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him warmly. Sybil made him promise he would never again let anything come between them, and the wedding was set for the seventh of June. Life seemed bright and beautiful to him once more, and all his former happiness returned.\\n\\nOne day, however, while he was going through the house on Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil, burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers full of odds and ends, the young woman suddenly gave a small cry of delight.\\n\\n\u201cWhat have you found, Sybil?\u201d asked Lord Arthur, looking up from his work with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cThis lovely little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it charming and Dutch? Please give it to me! I know amethysts won\u2019t suit me until I\u2019m well over eighty.\u201d\\n\\nIt was the box that had contained the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a faint blush touched his cheeks. He had almost completely forgotten what he had done, and it struck him as a strange coincidence that Sybil\u2014for whose sake he had endured all that terrible anxiety\u2014should be the first to remind him of it.\\n\\n\u201cOf course you can have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! thank you, Arthur; and may I have the bonbon too? I had no idea Lady Clementina liked sweets. I thought she was far too intellectual.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur turned deathly pale, and a horrifying thought flashed through his mind.\"}"
        }
