          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5223fda249d46bf4eb297408\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a moment in every well-made boy\u2019s life when he\u2019s hit with a fierce urge to go somewhere and dig for buried treasure. That feeling hit Tom one day. He headed out to find Joe Harper, but had no luck. Next he looked for Ben Rogers; he\u2019d gone fishing. Before long he ran into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a private spot and laid out the plan in confidence. Huck was up for it. Huck was always willing to jump into anything that promised fun and cost nothing, because he had a steady surplus of the kind of time that isn\u2019t money. \u201cWhere are we going to dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it hidden everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, of course not. It\u2019s hidden in very particular places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests under the tip of a branch of an old dead tree, right where the shadow falls at midnight; but mostly under the floor in haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho hides it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRobbers, naturally\u2014who else? Sunday school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it were mine I wouldn\u2019t hide it; I\u2019d spend it and have fun.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t do things that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they mean to, but they usually forget the landmarks, or else they die. Anyway, it sits there for ages and gets rusty; and eventually somebody finds an old yellow paper that tells how to find the signs\u2014a paper you have to puzzle over for about a week because it\u2019s mostly symbols and hieroglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro-what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics\u2014pictures and stuff, you know, that don\u2019t look like they mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you have one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the marks?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any marks. They always bury it under a haunted house or on an island, or under a dead tree with one branch sticking out. We tried Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can try it again sometime; and there\u2019s the old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and there are plenty of dead-limb trees\u2014loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a question! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how are you going to know which one to dig at?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDig at all of them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, that\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so what? Suppose you find a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. How would that be?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes lit up.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s great. More than great to me. Just give me the hundred dollars and I don\u2019t need any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
