          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1116389df85fa9fe7f380efc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox! I leaped to the king and murmured in his ear: \u201cSire, out with you at once! The woman is dying of that same plague that swept Camelot two years past.\u201d He did not stir. \u201cVerily, I will remain\u2014and render aid.\u201d I bent close again: \u201cYour Majesty, you must not. You must depart.\u201d \u201cYou mean kindly, and your words have weight. Yet it were shame for a king to show fear, and shame for a belted knight to hold back his hand when succor is due. Enough, I refuse. It is you who need to withdraw. The Church\u2019s ban spares me, but it forbids you this place, and should she learn of your trespass she will visit you harshly.\u201d It was peril for him, and could well cost his life, yet there was no moving him. If he deemed his knightly honor engaged, debate was wasted; he would remain, come what might. I recognized that and let the matter drop. The poor woman spoke up: \u201cKind sir, of your charity climb yonder ladder and bring me tidings of what you see? Fear not to tell the whole, for sometimes a mother\u2019s heart can break no further\u2014being already broken.\u201d \u201cStay,\u201d said the king, \u201cand give her food. I will go.\u201d He set down the knapsack. I turned to start, but the king was already on the way. He paused over a man lying in the dimness, unnoticed till now. \u201cIs that your husband?\u201d he asked. \u201cYes.\u201d \u201cIs he asleep?\u201d \u201cThanks be to God for that mercy, yes\u2014these three hours. Where can I find coin enough to pay my gratitude? My heart overflows for the rest he rests in now.\u201d I said: \u201cWe shall take care. We will not wake him.\u201d \u201cAh no, you cannot, for he is dead.\u201d \u201cDead?\u201d \u201cIndeed\u2014and what triumph it is to know it! No one can harm or insult him any more. He is in heaven now and joyful; or if not there, he waits in hell, content, for there he will meet no abbot and no bishop. We were boy and girl together; we have been man and wife five and twenty years, never parted till now. Think how long that is to love and suffer side by side. This morning his mind wandered, and in his fancy we were children again roaming the happy fields; and so, in that innocent, cheerful talk, he rambled farther and farther, still lightly chatting, until he stepped into those other meadows we know naught of, and vanished from mortal sight.\u201d\"}"
