          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4e54eb63db32fe478057091\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMen marry because they are tired; women because they are curious, and both end up disappointed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI doubt I will ever marry, Harry. I\u2019m too much in love. That\u2019s one of your sayings. I\u2019m simply following it, the way I follow everything you tell me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is it you\u2019re in love with?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a pause.\\n\\n\u201cWith an actress,\u201d Dorian Gray said, blushing.\\n\\nLord Henry shrugged. \u201cThat\u2019s a rather ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t say that if you saw her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is she?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHer name is Sibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever heard of her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has\u2014yet. People will someday. She\u2019s a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are the decorative sex. They never have anything to say, but they say that nothing charmingly. Women are the triumph of matter over mind, just as men are the triumph of mind over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you talk like that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it\u2019s the truth. I\u2019m analyzing women right now, so I ought to know. The subject is not as complex as I thought. In the end, there are only two kinds of women: the plain and the colorful. Plain women are very useful. If you want a reputation for respectability, just take them down to supper. The other women are delightful, but they make one mistake. They paint their faces to look young. Our grandmothers painted so they could talk brilliantly. Rouge and wit used to go together. That is finished now. As long as a woman can look ten years younger than her own daughter, she\u2019s perfectly content. As for conversation, there are only five women in London worth talking to, and two of them can\u2019t be invited into decent society. But tell me about your genius. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Harry, your views frighten me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you meet her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you, Harry, but don\u2019t be unsympathetic about it. None of it would have happened if I hadn\u2019t met you. You filled me with a wild urge to know everything about life. For days after I met you, something seemed to beat in my veins. As I lounged in the park or strolled down Piccadilly, I looked at everyone who passed and wondered, with a fierce curiosity, what kind of lives they led. Some of them fascinated me. Others terrified me. There was a sweet poison in the air. I craved every sensation...\"}"
