          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7b1aa08574ec777864075a30\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried to the skies on soft flowered beds of ease, while others fight for the prize and sail through bloody seas? He was considered a remarkable reader. At church socials he was always asked to read poetry, and when he finished, the ladies would lift their hands and let them fall limp in their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords can\u2019t express it; it\u2019s too beautiful\u2014too beautiful for this mortal world.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn was sung, Reverend Mr. Sprague turned himself into a bulletin board, reading off notices of meetings, societies, and other matters until it seemed the list would stretch to Judgment Day\u2014a peculiar custom that still persists in America, even in cities, in this age of plentiful newspapers. Often, the less reason there is to keep a tradition, the harder it is to discard it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a generous, detailed prayer: for the church and its children; for the other churches in town; for the village; the county; the state; the state officials; the United States; the churches of the United States; Congress; the President; the government officers; poor sailors tossed by stormy seas; the oppressed millions groaning under European monarchs and Eastern despots; those who have the light and good news yet lack eyes to see or ears to hear; the heathen in far-off islands; and finally a plea that the words he was about to speak would find grace and favor, like seed sown in fertile soil, yielding in time a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustle of skirts, and the standing congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if that. He was restless throughout; he unconsciously kept count of the prayer\u2019s details, for he wasn\u2019t really listening, but he knew the familiar territory and the clergyman\u2019s usual route. When a bit of new material slipped in, his ear caught it and his whole being rebelled; he considered additions unfair and downright wicked. In the middle of the prayer a fly landed on the back of the pew in front of him and tormented his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, wrapping its head with its arms, and polishing it so vigorously it seemed almost to detach from its body, exposing the thin thread of a neck; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them to its body as though they were coat tails; going through its entire grooming routine as serenely as if it knew it was perfectly safe.\"}"
