          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d78c40b776adb48492ff1207\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent in the petitions, I couldn\u2019t stay away from the places where they were lodged. I felt as if they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near. In that unreasonable restlessness and mental pain, I roamed the streets in the evenings, wandering past the offices and houses where I had left the requests. To this day, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night\u2014with their rows of stern, shuttered mansions and their long lines of lamps\u2014are melancholy to me because of those memories.\\n\\nThe daily visits I could make to him were shorter now, and he was more strictly guarded. Seeing\u2014or believing\u2014that I was suspected of trying to smuggle poison to him, I asked to be searched before I sat down at his bedside. I told the officer stationed there that I was willing to do anything to reassure him of my honest intentions. No one was hard on him or me. Duty had to be done, and it was done, but not cruelly. The officer always told me he was worse, and the other sick prisoners in the room, along with the prisoners who cared for them as nurses\u2014criminals, yet not incapable of kindness, thank God!\u2014always gave the same report.\\n\\nAs the days passed, I saw more and more how he would lie quietly staring at the white ceiling, his face empty of light until some word of mine brightened it for a moment before it faded again. Sometimes he was almost\u2014or completely\u2014unable to speak. Then he answered me with light pressures of his hand, and I came to understand him very well.\\n\\nThe days had reached ten when I noticed a greater change in him than ever before. His eyes were turned toward the door and lit up as I entered.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said as I sat down by his bed, \u201cI thought you were late. But I knew you couldn\u2019t be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s exactly the time,\u201d I said. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always wait at the gate, don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. So I don\u2019t lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear boy, thank you. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand silently, unable to forget that I had once meant to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd what\u2019s best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable with me since I was under a dark cloud than when the sun was shining.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
