          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"83ea6df1c8a41e7266670ef7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJoseph! Joseph!\u201d He shook his head and tapped it, showing how disappointed he was in Joseph.\\n\\n\u201cBut my dear young friend,\u201d Mr. Pumblechook said, \u201cyou must be hungry, you must be exhausted. Sit down. Here\u2019s a chicken sent over from the Boar, here\u2019s a tongue from the Boar, here are one or two other things from the Boar that I hope you won\u2019t turn up your nose at. But do I,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, springing up again the moment he had sat down, \u201csee before me him with whom I once sported in the happy days of his infancy? And may I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nThis \u2018May I?\u2019 meant could he shake hands. I agreed, and he did so fervently, then sat down again.\\n\\n\u201cHere is wine,\u201d Mr. Pumblechook said. \u201cLet us drink. Thanks to Fortune, and may she always choose her favorites with the same good judgment! And yet I cannot,\u201d he added, rising again, \u201clook upon One\u2014and also drink to One\u2014without again asking\u2014May I\u2014may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nI said he could, he shook hands with me again, emptied his glass, and turned it upside down. I did the same, and if I had stood on my head before drinking, the wine couldn\u2019t have gone to it faster.\\n\\nMr. Pumblechook served me the liver wing and the best slice of tongue (none of those obscure dead-ends of pork now) and, comparatively speaking, barely looked after himself at all. \u201cAh! poultry, poultry! You never guessed,\u201d Mr. Pumblechook said, addressing the bird on the platter, \u201cwhen you were a little fledgling, what lay ahead for you. You never thought you\u2019d be refreshment beneath this humble roof for someone who\u2014Call it a weakness if you like,\u201d he said, getting up yet again, \u201cbut may I? may I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nIt soon became useless to keep repeating that he could, so he just went ahead. How he managed to do it so often without stabbing himself on my knife, I can\u2019t imagine.\\n\\n\u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he continued after a steady bout of eating, \u201cwho had the honor of raising you by hand! It\u2019s a sad picture to think she\u2019s no longer capable of fully understanding the honor. May\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI saw he was about to lunge at me again, so I stopped him.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll drink to her health,\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s how you recognize them, sir!\u201d (I don\u2019t know who Sir was supposed to be, but it certainly wasn\u2019t me, and there was no third person present.) \u201cThat\u2019s how you know the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
