          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dbbc22d5d0e2eb012d32225\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With my heart racing and fear rising\u2014I could not say why\u2014I sent the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were its words.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child,\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your trust, tried to poison your mind, and forced your innocent heart to see and hear the expressions of forbidden, monstrous desire. I must atone for these crimes and try to match my punishment to my guilt. You are, I fear, prepared for what I must say: we must separate and remain apart forever.\\n\\n\u201cI rob you of your parent and only friend. You are cast out, without shelter, into the world; your hopes are ruined; the peace and safety of your clear mind destroyed. Memory will bring you terrifying images of guilt and the torment of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who bring all this misery upon you; I, who cast you out and have stamped distrust and agony on the heart and brow of my own child; I, who with devilish carelessness tried to steal her loveliness to replace it with the foul deformity of sin\u2014I, in the overflowing pain of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must hate me, and rightly so. But forgive me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts follow me into exile with relentless anger. I must never see you again; never hear your voice; yet the gentle whisper of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning in my disordered mind and heart; I am sure I would feel it even in my grave. I dare insist on this plea by telling you how I was horribly trapped in this web of fiery anguish and how I struggled to free myself. If your soul were less pure and bright I would never try to excuse myself to you; I would fear that if I made you despise me less you might also hate vice less. But in speaking to you I feel as if I appeal to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must try to win it or despair. I beg you to listen, and if guilt mixed with intense suffering and madness-like remorse can earn any mercy, perhaps you may think\u2014you, if not I\u2014that I have some claim on your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI urge you to remember our first happy days on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from sixteen weary years of wandering, during which, though I faced many dangers and misfortunes, my heart had remained entirely empty.\u201d\"}"
