{"request_id":"76b66141cbf97a37b67df595","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove caught the first moment alone with Anne to say, “Miss Anne, I wish Mrs Charles could borrow some of your way with those children. They behave like different creatures with you, but usually they are so spoiled! It’s a shame you cannot guide your sister in managing them. They are as fine and healthy as any children ever seen, poor little things! honestly; but Mrs Charles has no idea how they should be treated. Goodness, they are troublesome sometimes. I assure you, it keeps me from wanting them here as often as I otherwise would. I think Mrs Charles is not quite pleased that I do not invite them more, but you know how hard it is to have children in the house when you must be checking them every moment with ‘don’t do this’ and ‘don’t do that,’ or keeping them in order only by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

She heard this from Mary as well. “Mrs Musgrove thinks her servants all so steady that it would be treason to question it, but I know without exaggeration that her upper housemaid and laundrymaid, instead of working, roam the village all day. I meet them everywhere, and whenever I go into my nursery, I see or hear of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy creature in the world, it would ruin her, for she tells me they are always tempting her to go walking with them.” On Mrs Musgrove’s side it was, “I make it a rule never to interfere in my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because it would not do; but I will tell you, Miss Anne, since you might set things right, that I do not think very highly of Mrs Charles’s nurserymaid. I hear strange stories of her; she is always out and about, and from what I know, she dresses so finely that she could ruin any servant she comes near. Mrs Charles swears by her, I know; but I give you this hint so you can keep an eye out. If you see anything amiss, you need not hesitate to mention it.”

Mary also complained that Mrs Musgrove often failed to give her the precedence she was due when they dined at the Great House with other families. She saw no reason she should be considered so much at home that she lost her place."}

