          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6170fd62fc5117227653356a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Poor Jack was later killed in a duel by Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara died of a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year ended, so in every way it had been counted a great success. Still, it was an extremely difficult disguise, if I may use such a stage term for one of the greatest supernatural mysteries, or, to speak more scientifically, a marvel of the higher-natural world, and he needed a full three hours to prepare. At last everything was ready, and he was pleased with his appearance. The tall leather riding-boots that went with the outfit were a bit too large, and he could find only one of the two horse-pistols, but overall he was satisfied, and at quarter past one he slipped out of the wainscoting and crept down the corridor. When he reached the twins\u2019 room, called the Blue Bed Chamber because of the color of its hangings, he found the door slightly open. Wanting to make a dramatic entrance, he flung it wide, and a heavy jug of water dropped straight on him, soaking him to the skin and missing his left shoulder by only a couple of inches. At the same moment he heard muffled bursts of laughter coming from the four-poster bed. The shock to his nerves was such that he ran back to his room as fast as he could, and the next day he was laid up with a severe cold. The only thing that comforted him at all was that he had not brought his head with him, for if he had, the consequences could have been very serious.\\n\\nHe now abandoned all hope of frightening this insolent American family, and usually contented himself with creeping through the passages in soft slippers, with a thick red muffler around his throat to guard against drafts, and a small arquebuse in case the twins attacked him. The final insult he suffered came on the nineteenth of September. He had gone downstairs to the great entrance hall, certain that there, at least, he would be left in peace, and was amusing himself by making sarcastic comments on the large Saroni photographs of the United States Minister and his wife, which now hung where the Canterville family portraits once did. He was simply but neatly dressed in a long shroud spotted with graveyard mold, had his jaw tied up with a strip of yellow linen, and carried a small lantern and a sexton\u2019s spade.\"}"
