          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d99279f517b91706a428c3df\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was energized by the vivid sensations of restored health. As commander of the Athenians, he felt he now held a position worthy of his ambition, and in his dreams of taking Constantinople he anticipated an event that would stand as a landmark through the ages\u2014an accomplishment unmatched in human history. A city celebrated for its majestic associations, admired worldwide for the beauty of its setting, and for hundreds of years the stronghold of the Ottomans, was to be freed from bondage and barbarism and returned to a people renowned for genius, civilization, and a spirit of liberty. Perdita relied on his renewed companionship, his love, his hopes and fame, the way a sybarite rests on a luxurious couch; every thought brought rapture, each emotion seemed to bathe in a congenial, soothing element.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on the seventh of July. The weather during our journey had been clear. Each day, before dawn, we left our night\u2019s camp to watch the shadows draw back from hill and valley, and to see the golden blaze of the rising sun. The soldiers with us, full of national spirit, took delight in the sight of nature\u2019s beauty. Sunrise was greeted with triumphant music, while birdsong, catching our ears now and then, filled the pauses between the notes. At midday, we pitched our tents in some shady valley or leafy woodland among the mountains, while a stream, murmuring over pebbles, coaxed us into grateful sleep. Our evening marches, calmer, were even more pleasing than the restless energy of the morning. If the band played, they instinctively chose melodies of restrained feeling; the farewell of love or the melancholy of absence would give way to a solemn hymn, perfect for the peaceful loveliness of evening and raising the spirit toward grand, reverent thoughts. Often all sound fell silent so we could listen to the nightingales, while fireflies danced in bright patterns and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to travelers. When we crossed a valley, we were wrapped in gentle shadows and saw rocks tinted with beautiful colors. Crossing a mountain, we saw Greece laid out below like a living map, its famous peaks slicing the sky, its rivers threading the fertile land in silver lines. Barely daring to breathe, we English travelers gazed in awe at this splendid landscape, so unlike the muted tones and wistful charm of our own homeland. After we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer attractions, yet our journey remained engaging. An advance guard announced our approach, and the local people quickly stirred to show honor to Lord Raymond.\"}"
