          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1f673b961b847589e22c8f0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Everything about this carried a strangeness that pulled me in and fascinated me. It was as though he had awakened from a long, dream-filled sleep, feeling like one of the seven sleepers or Nourjahad in that lovely imitation of an Eastern tale. Diana was gone; his friends were altered or dead, and when he opened his eyes again, I was all that was left on earth for him to love.\\n\\nHow precious the waters, mountains, and forests of Loch Lomond became now that I had such a beloved companion for my wanderings. I visited with my father every delightful spot, whether on the islands or along the tree-sheltered waterfalls, every shaded path or hollow tangled with undergrowth and fern. His conversation broadened my thoughts. I felt remade, filled with the freshness and vitality of a new existence: since his arrival, I seemed transported from a narrow patch of ground to a universe limitless to imagination and understanding. My life had previously been like a pleasant country stream, never meant to leave its native fields, destined to disappear quietly when its task was done and leave no trace. Now it seemed to me a varied river running through a fertile, beautiful landscape, always changing and always lovely. Alas, I did not know the desert it was soon to reach, the rocks that would tear its waters, and the hideous scene that would mirror itself in their distorted reflections. Life was brilliant then; I was beginning to learn hope\u2014and what brings the heart more bitter despair than shattered hope?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should follow such divine happiness so quickly? I drank from an enchanted cup, but bitterness lay at the bottom of its long sweetness. My heart was full of deep affection, yet it was calm because of that depth and fullness. I had no notion that misery could spring from love, and that lesson, which everyone must learn eventually, was given to me in a way few are forced to endure. I mourn now, and will always mourn, those few short months of paradisiacal bliss. I broke no command, I tasted no forbidden fruit, yet I was mercilessly driven out. Alas, my companion did, and I was cast down in his fall. But I stray from my account\u2014let sorrow arrive when its time is set; at this point in my story I can still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship before my aunt fell ill. I spent a whole month in her room nursing her, but her sickness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for a time. Death is so terrible to the living; the chains of habit are so strong, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must writhe in agony when they break.\"}"
        }
