content.text:
{"request_id":"f4404aae84dc5cfb7c71702a","rewritten_text":"The instruments showed I had reached 132,000 feet, or about twenty-five miles, and I could see roughly one three-hundred-and-twentieth of Earth’s surface at once. By nine o’clock land to the east had vanished again, but not before I noted the balloon drifting fast toward the north-northwest. The ocean’s curve below was obvious, though shifting masses of cloud kept breaking up the view. I noticed that even the thinnest mists never rose higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers out through the valve. Instead of floating, they dropped straight down like a bullet, in one solid clump, disappearing in a few seconds. At first I could not grasp this strange sight; I knew I had not suddenly started ascending at a dramatically faster rate. Soon I realized the air was now too thin to support even feathers, so they truly were falling rapidly, and I had been startled by the combined speed of their drop and my own rise.

“By ten o’clock I had little that needed attention. Everything was going splendidly, and I felt sure the balloon was still climbing, its speed increasing by the moment, though I no longer had a way to measure how quickly. I felt no pain or discomfort of any sort, and my spirits were higher than they had been at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I kept busy checking my instruments and refreshing the air inside the chamber. I decided to do this every forty minutes, more for my health than because the air needed replacing so often. Meanwhile I could not help drifting into daydreams. My fancy roamed through the visionary landscapes of the moon. My imagination, freed at last, wandered through the shifting marvels of a shadowy, uncertain world. I saw ancient forests, steep cliffs, and waterfalls plunging with a roar into bottomless chasms. Then, suddenly, I entered still noonday plains where no breeze ever blew, with immense fields of poppies and tall, lily-like blossoms stretching out endlessly, silent and motionless forever.”"}

