content.text:
{"request_id":"f73b068a3a3f8cfc33111d12","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, once they leave their homeland, assume an habitual air of delicate health, imagining it an emblem of European refinement, but Mrs. Otis had never succumbed to that notion. She possessed a splendid constitution and a truly astonishing store of vitality. In fact, in numerous ways she was distinctly English, and illustrated admirably that, nowadays, we share virtually everything with America—language being the sole exception. Her eldest boy, named Washington by his parents in a patriotic impulse he forever lamented, was a fair-haired, rather handsome youth who had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino three seasons running, and was renowned in London as a brilliant dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only frailties; otherwise he was eminently practical. Miss Virginia E. Otis, a fifteen-year-old slip of a girl, was as lithe and lovely as a young deer, her large blue eyes shining with unfettered spirit. A splendid horsewoman, she had once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park and won by a length and a half just before the Achilles statue, to the immense delight of the young Duke of Cheshire, who promptly offered for her hand and was dispatched back to Eton that very night by his guardians, sobbing incessantly. After Virginia came the twins, generally nicknamed “The Stars and Stripes,” since they were always getting caned. They were charming boys and, save for the worthy Minister, the only genuine republicans in the household.

As Canterville Chase lies seven miles from Ascot, the closest railway station, Mr. Otis telegraphed for a waggonette to collect them, and they set off along the road in the best of spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was sweet with the scent of the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood-pigeon crooning over its own tender coo, or glimpsed, deep among the whispering ferns, the burnished chest of a pheasant. Little squirrels peered out at them from the beeches as they passed, and rabbits darted through the undergrowth and across the mossy mounds, their white tails held aloft. Yet as they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly filled with clouds, an uncanny stillness seemed to settle over the air, a large flock of rooks flew silently above their heads, and before they reached the house heavy drops of rain had begun to fall.

Waiting on the steps to greet them stood an elderly woman, neatly clad in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

