{"request_id":"a520759c202221b9a41e19c5","rewritten_text":"We endured several miserable hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. Because my father and the rest of the family had to testify, I went with them to the court. Throughout that entire grotesque parody of justice I suffered a torment as if alive and burning. It was about to be decided whether my curiosity and reckless experiments would lead to the deaths of two fellow beings: one a smiling infant full of innocence and happiness, the other murdered in a way so appalling that every disgraceful detail branded the crime with lasting horror. Justine was also a girl of worth, blessed with qualities that might have made her life joyful; now everything would be wiped away in a shameful grave, and I was the cause! A thousand times I would rather have confessed myself guilty of the crime laid upon her, but I had been absent when it occurred, and such a declaration would have been taken as the ravings of a madman and would not have cleared the innocent sufferer whose ruin I had wrought.

Justine’s appearance was composed. She wore mourning, and her face, always charming, was made exquisitely beautiful by the depth of her feelings. Yet she seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, even though thousands stared at her with loathing; whatever sympathy her beauty might have inspired was erased in the spectators’ minds by the monstrosity of the act they believed she had committed. She was calm, but that calm was clearly forced; and since her earlier confusion had been used as proof of guilt, she steeled herself to appear courageous. When she entered the courtroom she swept her eyes around it and quickly found where we were seated. A tear seemed to blur her gaze when she saw us, but she swiftly regained control, and a look of sorrowful affection seemed to declare her absolute innocence.

The trial opened, and after the prosecutor stated the accusation, several witnesses were called. A series of odd facts lined up against her, enough to shake anyone who lacked the certainty of her innocence that I possessed. She had been out the entire night of the murder and, near dawn, was seen by a market-woman not far from where the body was later discovered. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strangely and gave only a confused, unintelligible answer. She returned home around eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she replied that she had been searching for the child and demanded urgently whether any news of him had been heard."}

