          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5df40fd469223720d83d9ed8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed our freedom so loudly and earnestly that the liberty-spouting orator and his patriotic crowd took an eager interest, gathered around us, and assumed a very resolute stance. The orator spoke: \u201cIf indeed ye are freemen, ye need fear nothing\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround ye as shield and shelter! (Applause.) Ye shall quickly see. Produce your proofs.\u201d \u201cWhat proofs?\u201d \u201cProof that ye are freemen.\u201d Ah\u2014then I remembered! I recovered my wits and kept quiet. But the king burst out: \u201cThou\u2019rt mad, man. Reason would better say that this thief and knave prove we are not freemen.\u201d You see, he knew his own laws the way people commonly do\u2014by words, not by consequences. The meaning grows vivid enough when it applies to oneself. Everyone shook their heads and looked let down; some drifted away, uninterested. The orator remarked\u2014this time with businesslike tones rather than sentimental ones: \u201cIf ye know not your country\u2019s laws, \u2018tis time ye learned. Ye are strangers to us; that ye will not deny. Ye may be freemen\u2014we grant it; yet ye may also be slaves. The law is plain: it demandeth not that the claimant prove ye slaves, but that ye prove ye are not.\u201d I said: \u201cDear sir, grant us only time to send to Astolat; or grant only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d \u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and ye cannot hope they will be granted. \u2018Twould consume much time and unwarrantably inconvenience your master\u2014\u201d \u201cMaster, fool!\u201d the king stormed. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d \u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d I choked the words out in time to stop the king. We had troubles enough already; letting them think us lunatics could only harm us. No use spinning out the details. The earl took us in hand and sold us at auction. This same infernal law had prevailed in our own South in my day\u2014more than thirteen centuries later\u2014and under it hundreds of freemen unable to prove their status had been sold into lifelong bondage without stirring any special emotion in me; but the instant law and auction block crossed my personal path, what had been merely wrong became suddenly infernal. That\u2019s how we\u2019re built. Yes, we were sold at auction, like hogs. In a large city with brisk trade we\u2019d have fetched a handsome sum; but this place was dead stagnant, and so we went for a price that shames me every time I recall it.\"}"
