          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f20e633ef6def86a9e083f21\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note by post. Just seeing the outside sent me into a great flutter, for even though I had never seen the handwriting before, I guessed whose it was. It had no opening like Dear Mr. Pip, or Dear Pip, or Dear Sir, or Dear anything, but simply ran thus:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled you should meet me? At all events Miss Havisham thinks so, and I write to obey her. She sends you her regard.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nIf there had been time, I probably would have ordered several suits of clothes for the occasion; but since there was not, I had to be content with what I owned. My appetite disappeared at once, and I knew no peace or rest until the day arrived. Yet its arrival brought me no relief; instead, I grew worse and began haunting the coach-office in Wood Street, Cheapside, even before the coach had left the Blue Boar in our town. Though I knew perfectly well when it would start, I could not bear to let the coach-office out of my sight for more than five minutes. In this unreasonable state I had finished the first half-hour of what would be a four or five hour watch when Wemmick bumped into me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said; \u201chow do you do? I would hardly have thought this was your beat.\u201d\\n\\nI explained that I was waiting to meet someone arriving by coach, and I asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth flourishing, thank you,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cespecially the Aged. He\u2019s in wonderful spirits. He will be eighty-two next birthday. I\u2019m thinking of firing eighty-two times, if the neighborhood doesn\u2019t complain and that cannon of mine can stand the strain. But that\u2019s not London talk. Where do you think I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d said I, since he was heading that way.\\n\\n\u201cNext thing to it,\u201d Wemmick answered. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We\u2019re dealing with a banker\u2019s parcel case just now, and I\u2019ve been down the road taking a look at the scene of action. Now I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d answered Wemmick, very dryly. \u201cBut he is accused of it. So might you or I be. Either of us could be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
