          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b2f2bdb15923b37ad817d08\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier rode across Brockley meadows. She tore her riding habit badly squeezing through a hedge, so when she returned home she decided to take the back staircase to avoid being seen. As she ran past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door stood open, she thought she saw someone inside. Believing it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes worked there, she stepped in to ask her to mend the torn habit. To her great surprise, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees blown through the air and the red leaves whirling wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole posture showed deep depression. He looked so forlorn and shabby that little Virginia, who had first thought of running away and locking herself in her room, felt pity instead and decided to comfort him. Her step was so light, and his sorrow so deep, that he did not notice her until she spoke. \\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m so sorry for you,\u201d she said, \u201cbut my brothers go back to Eton tomorrow, and then, if you behave, no one will bother you.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cIt is ridiculous to ask me to behave,\u201d he answered, looking round in astonishment at the pretty little girl who had dared speak to him. \u201cQuite ridiculous. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night, if that\u2019s what you mean. It\u2019s my only reason for existing.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cThat is no reason to exist, and you know you have been very wicked. Mrs. Umney told us, the first day we arrived, that you killed your wife.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cWell, I admit it,\u201d said the Ghost crossly, \u201cbut it was purely a family matter and concerned no one else.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cIt is very wrong to kill anyone,\u201d said Virginia, who sometimes had a sweet Puritan seriousness from some old New England ancestor. \\n\\n\u201cOh, I hate the cheap severity of abstract morals! My wife was very plain, never starched my ruffs right, and knew nothing about cooking. Once I shot a buck in Hogley Woods, a magnificent pricket, and do you know how she had it sent up to the table? Anyway, it doesn\u2019t matter now. It is all over, and I don\u2019t think it was very nice of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
