          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"26ea72bf004d889c844e0d6a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was set for the proof (lest our honor catch cold from delay), and the next day Drummle arrived with a polite little note in Estella\u2019s hand, saying she had had the honor of dancing with him several times. This left me no choice but to regret that I had been \u201ccarried away into a warmth which,\u201d and overall to reject, as unsustainable, the idea that I could be found anywhere. Drummle and I sat there snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove took up indiscriminate contradiction, and finally it was announced that good feeling had advanced at a remarkable rate.\\n\\nI talk about it lightly, but it was no light matter to me. I can\u2019t describe the pain it caused me to think that Estella would show any favor to such a contemptible, clumsy, sullen fool, so far below the average. Even now, I believe it sprang from some pure fire of generosity and unselfishness in my love for her that I couldn\u2019t bear the thought of her lowering herself to that brute. No doubt I would have been miserable no matter whom she favored; but a worthier rival would have brought a different kind and degree of distress.\\n\\nIt was easy for me to find out, and I soon did, that Drummle had begun to follow her closely, and that she allowed it. Soon he was always pursuing her, and he and I crossed paths every day. He persisted in his dull, stubborn way, and Estella kept him going; sometimes encouraging, sometimes discouraging him, sometimes almost flattering him, sometimes openly despising him, sometimes knowing him very well, sometimes barely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers called him, was used to lying in wait and had the patience of his kind. On top of that, he had a blockheaded confidence in his money and family greatness, which sometimes served him well\u2014almost replacing focus and firm resolve. So the Spider, doggedly watching Estella, outlasted many brighter insects, and would often unwind himself and drop at just the right moment.\\n\\nAt an Assembly Ball in Richmond (most places had Assembly Balls then), where Estella outshone all other beauties, this blundering Drummle hung around her so persistently, with so much tolerance from her, that I decided to speak to her about him. I seized the next chance, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, sitting apart among some flowers, ready to leave.\"}"
        }
