{"request_id":"749c292fded3ada4701aad65","rewritten_text":"As I passed my hand through the valve with a cup of water for the old cat, my shirt sleeve caught on the loop holding the basket, and in an instant it came loose from the bottom. It could not have vanished more suddenly if it had actually dissolved into thin air. There could not have been a tenth of a second between the basket detaching and it disappearing completely, along with everything inside. My good wishes followed it down to the ground, but of course I had no hope that either the cat or her kittens would survive to tell their tale of misfortune.

“At six o’clock, I saw a large portion of the earth to the east covered in a heavy shadow, which moved forward quickly until, at five minutes to seven, everything in sight was swallowed by night. Even so, it was long after this before the setting sun stopped shining on the balloon; and although I expected it, the sight brought me immense pleasure. Clearly, in the morning, I would see the sun rise many hours earlier than the citizens of Rotterdam, despite their being much farther east, and so, day by day, as I climbed higher, I would enjoy sunlight for longer stretches. I decided to keep a journal of my journey, counting the days from one to twenty-four hours straight, without considering periods of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling sleepy, I decided to lie down for the night. But a problem arose—simple as it now seems, it hadn’t occurred to me until that moment. If I went to sleep, how would the air in the chamber be refreshed in the meantime? Breathing it for more than an hour at most would be impossible, and even extending it to an hour and fifteen minutes could lead to disaster. This dilemma worried me greatly; and it may be hard to believe that, after all the dangers I had already faced, I took it so seriously that I almost abandoned hope of completing my goal and made up my mind that I would have to descend. But that hesitation lasted only a moment."}

