          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc8a9bbc00c9f8010ecc6e42\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For that reason, I decided to hop out as soon as we reached the town and get away from his hearing. I pulled it off without trouble. My small portmanteau was in the boot under my feet; I only had to turn a hinge to take it out. I dropped it before me, climbed down after it, and was left standing at the first lamp on the first stones of the town pavement. The convicts went on with the coach, and I knew where they would be whisked away to the river. In my imagination, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting for them at the slime-slicked stairs\u2014heard again the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d like an order barked at dogs\u2014and saw once more the villainous Noah\u2019s Ark lying on the black water.\\n\\nI could not have said what I was afraid of, for my fear was completely undefined and vague, but it was strong within me. As I walked toward the hotel, I felt a dread far worse than simply being worried about an unpleasant recognition, and it made me shake. I\u2019m certain it never took any clear shape, and that it was a brief return of the childhood terror.\\n\\nThe coffee room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not only ordered dinner there, but begun eating it before the waiter recognized me. As soon as he apologized for forgetting me, he asked if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014the same one who had once brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was bound\u2014looked surprised, and soon took the first chance to place a dirty old copy of a local newspaper so directly in my path that I picked it up and read this paragraph:\\n\\nOur readers will be interested to learn, in connection with the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young ironworker from our area (what a subject, by the way, for the magical pen of our still not universally celebrated townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!) that the youth\u2019s earliest patron, companion, and friend was a highly respected figure with some connection to the corn and seed trade, whose very convenient and spacious business premises lie within a hundred miles of High Street. It is not entirely without pride that we note this gentleman as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is gratifying to know that our town produced the founder of the latter\u2019s fortunes.\"}"
