          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a17961ef4e693bfc538fb2c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had rented an excellent house in Camden Place, an elevated and dignified address suited to a man of importance, and both he and Elizabeth had settled there very much to their liking. Anne entered with a sinking heart, dreading a months-long imprisonment and silently asking herself, \u201cOh, when will I get away again?\u201d Yet the unexpectedly warm welcome helped her spirits. Her father and sister were genuinely pleased to show her the house and furnishings and greeted her kindly. Her being a fourth at dinner was mentioned as an advantage. Mrs. Clay was agreeable and smiling, but her courtesies and smiles felt routine. Anne had always expected her to play the part upon her arrival, but she had not expected real warmth from the others. They were clearly in excellent moods, and Anne soon heard their reasons. They had no wish to listen to hers. After setting up the chance to compliment themselves on how sorely they were missed in their old neighborhood\u2014which Anne could not do\u2014they asked only a few half-hearted questions before taking over the conversation entirely. Uppercross did not interest them, Kellynch hardly more; it was all about Bath. They assured her Bath exceeded their expectations in every way. Their house was undeniably the best in Camden Place, and their drawing rooms had many definite advantages over any others they had seen or heard about, with fitting-up and furniture just as superior. Their acquaintance was highly sought after. Everyone wanted to visit them. They had declined many introductions, yet cards were constantly being left by people they did not know. They had endless sources of enjoyment. Could Anne be surprised that her father and sister were pleased? She might not be surprised, but she had to sigh that her father felt no humiliation in his change, saw nothing to miss in the duties and dignity of the resident landowner, and could take 