          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"337e0c550bccab9faceac077\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always pretended Peter supplied him with the talk, but that was false\u2014he squeezed every last bit out of that young numbskull we ferried up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane brought the letter her father left, and the king read it aloud and blubbered over it. It left the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls; it left the tanyard (doing brisk trade) with some other houses and land (worth about seven thousand) and three thousand in gold to Harvey and William, and told how the six thousand cash was hidden down cellar. So the two frauds said they\u2019d fetch it up and keep everything square and above-board, and told me to bring a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the sack they emptied it on the floor, and it was a pretty sight, all them yaller-boys. Land, how the king\u2019s eyes sparkled! He claps the duke on the shoulder and says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, this ain\u2019t bully nor noth\u2019n! Oh, no, I reckon not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, don\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke said it did. They pawed the yaller-boys, sifting them through their fingers and letting them jingle to the floor; and the king says:\\n\\n\u201cThere ain\u2019t no use talkin\u2019; being brothers to a rich dead man and spokesmen of foreign heirs that\u2019s got left is the trade for you and me, Bilge. This comes of relyin\u2019 on Providence. It\u2019s the best course, in the long run. I\u2019ve tried \u2019em all, and there ain\u2019t no better.\u201d\\n\\nMost anyone would\u2019ve been pleased with the heap and taken it on faith; but no, they had to count it. So they counted, and it came up four hundred and fifteen dollars shy. Says the king:\\n\\n\u201cDern him, what did he do with that four hundred and fifteen?\u201d\\n\\nThey fretted over it awhile and hunted around for it. Then the duke says:\\n\\n\u201cWell, he was mighty sick, and likely he made a slip\u2014I reckon that\u2019s it. Best let it go and keep still. We can spare it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, shucks, sure we can spare it. I don\u2019t care noth\u2019n about that\u2014it\u2019s the count I\u2019m dwellin\u2019 on. We got to be powerful square and open and above-board here, mind. We got to lug this here money upstairs and count it before everybody\u2014then there ain\u2019t noth\u2019n fishy. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
