          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"29490f76761e4b9dc2f9fe25\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With my heart pounding and afraid though I didn\u2019t know why, I sent the servant away, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were its words.  \u201cMy dear child,  \u201cI have betrayed your trust; I tried to corrupt your mind and forced your innocent heart to witness the looks and language of unlawful, monstrous desire. I must atone for these crimes and try to make my punishment match my guilt. You are, I\u2019m sure, prepared for what I must announce: we must separate and be parted forever.  \u201cI am taking from you your parent and only friend. You are cast out into the world without shelter; your hopes are destroyed; the peace and safety of your pure mind ruined; memory will bring you terrifying images of guilt and the agony of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who bring all this misery down on you; I, who cast you out and have pressed the seal of distrust and agony onto the heart and brow of my own child; I, who with devilish carelessness tried to steal away your loveliness and replace it with the foul deformity of sin\u2014I beg you, in the overflowing anguish of my heart, to forgive me.  \u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must and do loathe me. But forgive me, Mathilda, and don\u2019t let your thoughts follow me into exile with unrelenting anger. I must never see you again, never hear your voice, but the soft murmurs of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my disordered brain and heart; I\u2019m sure I would feel it even in my grave. And I dare back this request by telling you how miserably I was trapped in this fiery torment and how I fought to free myself. Truly, if your soul were less pure and bright I wouldn\u2019t try to defend myself to you; I would fear that if I made you feel less disgust for me you might hate vice less. But in speaking to you, I feel as though I\u2019m appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness; I must try to earn it or I must despair. I implore you, then, to listen to my words, and if guilt can in any measure be lessened by sharp agony and remorse that tears at the mind like madness, perhaps you may believe, though I dare not, that I have some claim to your compassion.  \u201cI ask you to remember our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from sixteen weary years of wandering, during which, though I faced many dangers and misfortunes, my heart had remained completely empty.\"}"
        }
