          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5eddd1d71cfa566df9ce57f3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNothing but a tick.\u201d \u201cWhere\u2019d you get him?\u201d \u201cOut in the woods.\u201d \u201cWhat\u2019ll you take for him?\u201d \u201cI don\u2019t know. I don\u2019t want to sell him.\u201d \u201cAll right. It\u2019s a mighty small tick, anyway.\u201d \u201cOh, anybody can run down a tick that isn\u2019t theirs. I\u2019m satisfied with it. It\u2019s a good enough tick for me.\u201d \u201cShoot, there\u2019s plenty of ticks. I could have a thousand if I wanted.\u201d \u201cWell, why don\u2019t you? Because you know you can\u2019t. This is a pretty early tick, I guess. It\u2019s the first one I\u2019ve seen this year.\u201d \u201cSay, Huck\u2014I\u2019ll give you my tooth for him.\u201d \u201cLet\u2019s see it.\u201d Tom took out a bit of paper and carefully unrolled it. Huckleberry stared at it wistfully. The temptation hit hard. At last he said, \u201cIs it real?\u201d Tom lifted his lip and showed the gap. \u201cWell, all right,\u201d said Huckleberry. \u201cIt\u2019s a trade.\u201d Tom shut the tick in the percussion-cap box that had recently held the pinchbug, and the boys left, each feeling richer than before. When Tom reached the little isolated frame schoolhouse, he walked in briskly like someone who had come as fast as he could. He hung his hat on a peg and dropped into his seat with businesslike quickness. The master, perched high in his big splint-bottom armchair, was dozing, lulled by the sleepy buzz of study. The interruption woke him. \u201cThomas Sawyer!\u201d Tom knew that when his full name was called, it meant trouble. \u201cSir!\u201d \u201cCome up here. Now, sir, why are you late again, as usual?\u201d Tom was about to hide behind a lie when he saw two long tails of yellow hair hanging down a back he recognized by the spark of love; and by that figure was the only empty seat on the girls\u2019 side. He instantly said, \u201cI stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn!\u201d The master\u2019s pulse froze, and he stared helplessly. The buzz of study stopped. The pupils wondered if this reckless boy had lost his mind. The master said, \u201cYou\u2014you did what?\u201d \u201cStopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn.\u201d There was no mistaking it. \u201cThomas Sawyer, this is the most astounding confession I\u2019ve ever heard. No simple ferule will do for this offense. Take off your jacket.\u201d The master\u2019s arm worked until it was tired and the supply of switches was notably smaller. Then came the order: \u201cNow, sir, go sit with the girls! And let this be a warning to you.\u201d The giggle that rippled around the room seemed to embarrass the boy, but in truth the effect came more from his worshipful awe of his unknown idol and the fearful pleasure in his great good luck.\"}"
