          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"25d5d64ebea596668d4f4cee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My father stayed beside me to comfort me and to banish the sharp memories with bright hopes: I almost felt it sweet to weep because he could dry my tears.\\n\\nAgain he drew my thoughts away from sorrow by comparing it with his despair when he lost my mother. Even then I shuddered at the picture he painted of his own passions: he had a poet\u2019s imagination, and when he described the storm that tore his feelings he gave his words such vivid life that I believed them even while trembling. I wondered how he had ever returned to daily duties after those wild thoughts seemed to give him kinship with the supernatural; as he spoke, his ideas were so immense that it seemed the human heart was too narrow to hold them. His emotions sounded more suited to a spirit whose home is the earthquake and volcano than to someone bound to a mortal body and human features. Yet these visions belonged to the past; he had changed since then. Now he was all affection, all gentleness; when I lifted my eyes to him in wonder as he spoke, the smile on his lips showed me his heart was filled with the kindest feelings.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death we moved to London, where my father led me to study more deeply than ever before. My progress was his joy; he stayed with me through every lesson, helping or working alongside me. We mixed often with society, and not a day passed without my father trying to adorn it with some new pleasure. The tender devotion he felt for me, and the love and respect with which I answered it, cast a charm over every hour. The hours seemed slow only because each minute was put to use; we lived more in a single week than many do in several months, and the variety and novelty of our delights gave flavor to them all.\\n\\nWe constantly went on outings together. Whether we visited beautiful scenes, admired fine paintings, or sometimes set out with no plan but to let amusement arise as it might, I was always happy when near my father. I felt regret whenever a third person joined us, yet if I turned to my father with a troubled expression, his eyes fixed on me and filled with tenderness at once restored joy to my heart. Oh, hours of intense delight! Brief as they were, they stretch as long as a lifetime when I look back through the mist of grief that soon rose afterward, as if to hide them from my sight.\"}"
