          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e844e9ef884c8df9b100041\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father may release you, naturally, if only your mother can bear it. Daughters never weigh so heavily with their fathers. And should you remain another entire month, I could convey one of you as far as London, for I am to be there early in June for a week; and as Dawson makes no objection to the barouche-box, there would be excellent room for one of you\u2014and indeed, should the weather prove cool, I should not hesitate to take you both, since neither of you takes up much space.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are all generosity, madam; yet I think we must adhere to our original scheme.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared satisfied. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must dispatch a servant with them. You know I always speak plainly, and I cannot endure the notion of two young women travelling post alone. It is exceedingly improper. You must manage to send someone. Nothing displeases me more than that kind of thing. Young women ought invariably to be fitly protected and attended, according to their rank. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted on two men-servants attending her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley and Lady Anne, could not have appeared suitably otherwise. I am remarkably scrupulous about such matters. Send John along with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am thankful it entered my head to mention it; for it would truly reflect poorly on you to let them go unescorted.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle intends to send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant, then? I am delighted someone is attentive to those matters. Where will you change horses? Oh, at Bromley, certainly. If you give my name at the Bell, you will receive proper attention.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had numerous further inquiries concerning their trip; and as she did not respond to them all herself, attentiveness was necessary\u2014a circumstance Elizabeth considered fortunate; for with her mind so preoccupied she might otherwise have forgotten her surroundings. Reflection had to be saved for solitary hours: whenever she was alone, she indulged it as the greatest solace; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, in which she gave herself over to the wistful pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nMr. Darcy\u2019s letter she was on the verge of learning by heart. She scrutinized every line; and her sentiments toward its author were at times sharply opposed. When she recalled the tone of his address, she still burned with resentment; but when she reflected how unfairly she had condemned and reproached him, her anger turned inward; and his wounded feelings became objects of pity.\"}"
        }
