          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc690caafcca16f7655c7a36\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No one can imagine how awe-inspiring a similar sight would be if it happened in the full darkness of night. Hell itself might serve as a fitting picture. Even in daylight my hair stood on end as I looked down into the yawning pits, letting my mind roam through the strange vaulted halls, the glowing gulfs, and the red, ghastly chasms of that hideous and bottomless fire. I had escaped only by a hair\u2019s breadth. If the balloon had stayed in the cloud a little longer\u2014if getting drenched had not pushed me to drop the ballast\u2014the result would have been certain ruin. Dangers like these, though rarely thought about, may be the most serious faced in ballooning. By now, however, I had risen high enough that this threat no longer worried me.\\n\\n\u201cI was rising fast, and by seven o\u2019clock the barometer showed an altitude of nine and a half miles. It was growing very hard to breathe. My head throbbed violently; and, after feeling dampness on my cheeks for some time, I finally realized it was blood oozing quickly from my eardrums. My eyes also troubled me. When I passed my hand over them they seemed to bulge noticeably from their sockets, and everything in the car, even the balloon itself, looked warped to my sight. These symptoms were worse than I had expected, and they alarmed me. At that moment, foolishly and without thinking, I tossed overboard three five-pound bags of ballast. The increased rate of ascent carried me far too quickly into air that was extremely thin, and the result almost ended both the expedition and my life. I was suddenly gripped by a spasm that lasted more than five minutes, and even after it eased slightly, I could draw breath only at long intervals, gasping while bleeding heavily from nose and ears, and even a little from my eyes. The pigeons were in terrible distress and fought to escape, while the cat meowed miserably and staggered back and forth in the car, tongue hanging out, as if she had been poisoned. I realized too late how reckless it was to dump the ballast, and I was overwhelmed with panic. I expected nothing less than death, and death within minutes.\u201d\"}"
