          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52a76ae427cf2d7bc5af3f1d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t help himself. It infuriated him to think Becky Thatcher never even suspected he was still among the living. Yet she actually noticed, and knew she was winning her skirmish, delighted to watch him suffer as she had. \\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted at duties pressing on him, tasks needing attention, and the minutes slipping away. Useless\u2014the girl kept chirping. Tom fumed, \u201cOh, plague the girl, will I ever shake her?\u201d At last he decreed he must tend to those tasks\u2014and she said guilelessly she would be \u201caround\u201d when school let out. He hurried off, hating her for it. \\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, teeth grinding. \u201cAny other lad in the whole village except that Saint Louis loafer who thinks he dresses so fine and is aristocracy! All right, I drubbed you the first day you ever saw this place, mister, and I\u2019ll drub you again! Just wait till I catch you! I\u2019ll simply take and\u2014\u201d \\n\\nHe acted out the thrashing of an invisible opponent\u2014pummeling empty air, kicking, gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You holler `nough, do you? There, let that teach you!\u201d and the imaginary whipping ended to his liking. \\n\\nTom bolted home at noon. He could not stand any more of Amy\u2019s grateful bliss, and his jealousy could endure no more of the other torment. Becky resumed studying pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes crawled and no suffering Tom appeared, her triumph dimmed and she lost interest; solemnity and distraction came, then gloom. Twice or thrice she pricked up her ear at footsteps, but each time was vain hope; Tom never came. At last she was altogether wretched and wished she hadn\u2019t pushed it so far. When poor Alfred, puzzled because he felt her slipping and couldn\u2019t tell why, kept crying, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a jolly one! see this!\u201d she lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care for them!\u201d burst into tears, rose, and walked away. \\n\\nAlfred moved up beside her to offer comfort, but she snapped: \\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d \\n\\nSo he stopped, wondering what offense he\u2019d given\u2014for she had promised to look at pictures all through the noon hour\u2014and she walked on, weeping. Then Alfred wandered into the empty schoolhouse. Mortified and angry, he quickly hit upon the truth\u2014the girl had only used him as a handy tool to vent her malice against Tom Sawyer.\"}"
