{"request_id":"887f49eff4e77fa6e7b64416","rewritten_text":"Four days after those strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase around eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each with a grand spray of swaying ostrich plumes on its head, and the lead-lined coffin was draped in a rich purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants walked beside the hearse and the carriages carrying lit torches, and the entire procession was astonishingly solemn. Lord Canterville, who had come up from Wales specifically for the funeral, was the chief mourner and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. Behind them rode the United States Minister and his wife, then Washington and the three boys, and in the last carriage sat Mrs. Umney. Everyone agreed that, having been terrorized by the ghost for more than fifty years, she was entitled to witness his final farewell. A deep grave had been prepared in the corner of the churchyard beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier read the service with great dignity. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following a long-standing Canterville custom, extinguished their torches, and as the coffin was lowered, Virginia stepped forward and placed on it a large cross woven from white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud, bathing the small churchyard in quiet silver light, and a nightingale began singing from a distant thicket. She remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death, her eyes filled with tears, and she hardly spoke at all on the drive home.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville returned to the city, Mr. Otis met with him to discuss the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were breathtaking, especially a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb piece of sixteenth-century craftsmanship, and their value was so great that Mr. Otis hesitated to let his daughter keep them.

“My lord,” he began, “I understand that in this country mortmain is taken to apply to trinkets as well as to land, and it seems obvious that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms of your family. I must therefore ask you to take them back to London and treat them as part of your property, restored to you under rather extraordinary circumstances. As for my daughter, she is still a child, and I am glad to say she has very little interest so far in accessories of idle luxury.”"}

