          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2129d22cd236796b8ca14880\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The cramped streets and alleys finally opened upon a broad square; pens for livestock and other signs of a cattle-market stood scattered about. Sikes slackened his stride here, for the girl could no longer keep up the swift pace they had maintained. He swung toward Oliver and harshly ordered him to take Nancy\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cD\u2019ye hear?\u201d Sikes growled when Oliver hesitated, glancing around.\\n\\nThey had drawn into a shadowed corner, far from passers-by.\\n\\nOliver understood with painful clarity that resistance was useless. He extended his hand, which Nancy gripped tightly in hers.\\n\\n\u201cGive me t\u2019other,\u201d said Sikes, catching Oliver\u2019s free hand. \u201cHere, Bull\u2019s-Eye!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog lifted his head and gave a growl.\\n\\n\u201cLook here, lad!\u201d said Sikes, placing his remaining hand at Oliver\u2019s throat. \u201cIf he speaks the least soft word, hold him! D\u2019ye hear?\u201d\\n\\nThe dog responded with another growl and, licking his chops, stared at Oliver as though itching to fasten on his windpipe without delay.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s as ready as any Christian, strike me blind if he ain\u2019t!\u201d Sikes muttered, gazing at the beast with grim, savage approval. \u201cNow you know what\u2019s coming to you, my lad, so sing out as fast as you please; the dog\u2019ll soon stop that trick. March, youngster!\u201d\\n\\nBull\u2019s-eye wagged his tail, gratified by this rare affectionate remark; then, uttering another warning growl for Oliver\u2019s benefit, trotted ahead.\\n\\nThey were crossing Smithfield, though it might as well have been Grosvenor Square for all Oliver knew. The night lay dark and fog-laden. The shop lights could scarcely pierce the thickening mist, which every moment grew heavier, cloaking streets and houses in murk, making the strange place stranger still to Oliver, and deepening his misery and dread.\\n\\nThey had hurried but a few steps more when a deep church bell tolled the hour. At its first stroke both companions halted and turned toward the sound.\\n\\n\u201cEight o\u2019clock, Bill,\u201d Nancy murmured when the bell fell silent.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use o\u2019 telling me? I can hear it, can\u2019t I?\u201d Sikes snapped.\\n\\n\u201cI wonder if they hear it,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cCourse they do,\u201d Sikes replied. \u201cIt was Bartlemy time when I was shopped; there warn\u2019t a penny trumpet in the fair I couldn\u2019t catch the squeal of. After they locked me up for the night, the riot outside made the old thundering jail so quiet, I almost felt like banging my brains out against the iron plates of the door.\u201d\"}"
