          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f5003e53fc7e922306688c2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou must care, Dorian. Every gentleman cares about his good name. You can\u2019t want people to talk about you as if you were vile and ruined. Of course, you have status and wealth and all that. But status and wealth are not everything. Believe me, I don\u2019t credit these rumours at all. At least, I can\u2019t believe them when I look at you. Sin writes itself on a man\u2019s face. It cannot be hidden. People speak of secret vices, but there are none. If a miserable man has one, it shows in the lines of his mouth, the droop of his eyelids, even in the shape of his hands. Someone\u2014I won\u2019t give his name, though you know him\u2014came to me last year for a portrait. I had never seen him before and knew nothing about him then, though I have learned plenty since. He offered an enormous price. I refused. There was something in the shape of his fingers that repelled me. I now know I was right to suspect him. His life is dreadful. But you, Dorian, with your clear, bright, innocent face and your astonishing, untouched youth\u2014I cannot believe any accusation against you. Yet I hardly see you now. You never come to the studio, and when I am away from you and hear all the vile things people whisper, I don\u2019t know how to respond. Why is it that a man like the Duke of Berwick leaves a club room when you enter? Why do so many London gentlemen refuse to go to your house or invite you to theirs? You were once a friend of Lord Staveley. I met him at dinner last week. Your name arose over the miniatures you lent to the exhibition at the Dudley. Staveley curled his lip and said that you might have the most refined tastes, but you were someone no pure-minded girl should ever meet, and no chaste woman should share a room with. I reminded him I was your friend and asked what he meant. He told me. He said it plainly before everyone. It was awful! Why is your friendship so deadly to young men? There was that wretched boy in the Guards who killed himself. You were his closest friend. There was Sir Henry Ashton, forced to leave England with a stained name.\u201d\"}"
