          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"20764c4f40a0b931466efa45\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHang my hat, I want Virginia!\u201d cried the little Duke with a laugh, and they spurred on to the railway station. Mr. Otis questioned the station-master whether anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but no word of her came. The station-master telegraphed up and down the line and promised strict watchfulness, and after Mr. Otis purchased a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper who was closing his shutters, he rode on to Bexley, a village four miles off said to be a favorite gypsy resort because of the broad common nearby. There they woke the local policeman, yet he could give them no news, and after traversing the common they turned their horses homeward, reaching the Chase around eleven, exhausted and nearly broken-hearted. Washington and the twins met them at the gate-house with lanterns, for the avenue lay in deep darkness. Not the faintest sign of Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been intercepted on Brockley meadows, but she was not among them, and they explained their abrupt departure by saying they had misjudged the date of Chorton Fair and had hurried off lest they arrive late. In fact, they were quite troubled at Virginia\u2019s disappearance, feeling grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of them had remained to aid the search. The carp-pond had been dragged, and the entire Chase combed, yet nothing came of it. Clearly for that night Virginia was lost to them, and in profound gloom Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house, the groom trailing behind with both horses and the pony. In the hall they encountered a cluster of trembling servants, and poor Mrs. Otis lay on a library sofa, nearly distracted with fear and anxiety, the old housekeeper bathing her brow with eau-de-cologne. Mr. Otis immediately insisted she take some food and ordered supper for everyone. It proved a dismal meal, scarcely a word spoken, and even the twins, fond as they were of their sister, sat hushed and subdued. When they had finished, Mr. Otis, despite the little Duke\u2019s pleadings, sent them all to bed, declaring nothing more could be done that night and that he would telegraph Scotland Yard in the morning for detectives to be dispatched at once.\"}"
