          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3f978168c3dc5677a1d409fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If not for me, he would never have escaped at all. Morgan le Fay despised him with her entire being and would never have relented toward him. Still, his offense came from thoughtlessness more than from deliberate wickedness. He had remarked that she had red hair. Indeed she did; yet that was no way to describe it. When people of a certain station have red hair it is called auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: among these forty-seven prisoners were five whose names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known! One woman and four men\u2014each stooped, wrinkled, and mind-dulled relics. They had long since forgotten those particulars themselves; in any case they held only vague notions about them, never the same twice. The line of priests whose duty it was to pray daily with the captives, to remind them that God had shut them in for some inscrutable reason, and to teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were the virtues He liked to see in those of lesser rank, possessed traditions about these poor human ruins but nothing beyond that. Those traditions reached only so far as recording the length of confinement, not the offenses. And even with tradition\u2019s help, the sole fact provable was that none of the five had glimpsed daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer their deprivation stretched no one could conjecture. The king and queen knew nothing of these wretches except that they were heirlooms, assets passed down with the throne from the previous dynasty. Nothing of their story came with their persons, so the inheriting owners deemed them worthless and felt no curiosity about them. I asked the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why on earth didn\u2019t you set them at liberty?\u201d\\n\\nThe question was a stumper. She had no idea why it never occurred to her, the thought had simply never arisen. Thus she was forecasting the actual future of the prisoners of the Castle d\u2019If without realizing it. It stood plain to me now that, owing to her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were merely property to her\u2014no more, no less. And when we inherit property, we do not think to throw it away, even when we see no value in it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats out into the upper world and the blaze of the afternoon sun\u2014after blindfolding them, mercy for eyes so long spared light\u2014they made a spectacle. Skeletons, scarecrows, hobgoblins, pitiful terrors every one; the most legitimate offspring imaginable of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
