          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e781f0b30e58faf0f0de01f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft and he counted up, he saw he had gathered eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also grabbed a three-gallon jug of whiskey he\u2019d found under a wagon when he was starting home through the woods. The king said that, all things considered, it beat any day he\u2019d ever had in the missionary line. He claimed it was no use arguing\u2014heathens weren\u2019t worth a thing next to pirates when it came to working a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke had thought he was doing pretty well until the king showed his haul, and then he didn\u2019t feel so proud. He had set up and printed two small jobs for farmers in that printing office\u2014horse bills\u2014and got four dollars for them. He had also taken in ten dollars\u2019 worth of advertisements for the paper, which he offered to run for four dollars if they paid in advance\u2014and they did. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he took three subscriptions at half a dollar each on the condition they pay in advance. They wanted to pay in cordwood and onions as usual, but he said he had just bought the business, cut prices as much as he could, and was going to run it for cash. He set up a little poem of his own\u2014three verses, kind of sweet and sad\u2014titled \u201cYes, crush, cold world, this breaking heart,\u201d and left it ready to print without charging anything. He took in nine dollars and a half and said he\u2019d done a pretty fair day\u2019s work for it.\\n\\nThen he showed us another little job he\u2019d printed and hadn\u2019t charged for because it was for us. It had a picture of a runaway black man carrying a bundle on a stick over his shoulder, with \u201c$200 reward\u201d under it. The writing was all about Jim, describing him exactly. It said he\u2019d run away from St. Jacques\u2019 plantation, forty miles below New Orleans, last winter, and probably headed north; anyone who caught him and sent him back would get the reward and expenses.\\n\\n\u201cNow,\u201d said the duke, \u201cafter tonight we can travel in daylight if we want. Whenever we see anyone coming, we can tie Jim hand and foot with a rope, lay him in the wigwam, and show this handbill. We\u2019ll say we captured him up the river, and since we\u2019re too poor to ride a steamboat, we got this little raft on credit from friends and are going down to collect the reward.\u201d\"}"
