          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"468538d6a90f6f561446a667\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The party sat divided on two benches that touched each other. Anne was on the front one, and Mr. Elliot, helped by his friend Colonel Wallis, managed to sit beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and enjoying Colonel Wallis\u2019s gallant attention, was perfectly content.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s mind was well-tuned to the evening\u2019s entertainment; it occupied her just enough. She had feeling for the tender moments, liveliness for the cheerful, focus for the technical parts, and patience for the dull ones, and she had never enjoyed a concert more\u2014at least during the first act. Near its end, during the pause after an Italian song, she explained the words to Mr. Elliot. They shared a concert program between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis almost the sense\u2014the meaning of the words. I suppose the sense of an Italian love song is not to be discussed, but this is as close as I can manage. I do not claim to know the language. I am a very poor Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I see that. You clearly know nothing about it. You can only turn these jumbled, shortened Italian lines into clear, graceful English at sight. Please say no more of your ignorance. Here is proof to the contrary.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not argue with such kind politeness, but I would be sorry to face a true expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not visited in Camden Place so long,\u201d he said, \u201cwithout learning something of Miss Anne Elliot; and I consider her too modest for the world to recognize half her talents, and too accomplished for such modesty to seem natural in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! This is too much flattery. I forget what comes next,\u201d she said, turning back to the program.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d Mr. Elliot whispered, \u201cI have known your character longer than you think.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed? How? You only could have known it since I came to Bath, except for what you might have heard from my family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew of you long before you came to Bath. I heard you described by those who knew you well. I have been acquainted with you, in reputation, for years. Your person, your nature, your talents, your manner\u2014they were all familiar to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Elliot gained the interest he wanted. No one could resist the allure of such mystery.\"}"
