          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"86da0d2fbc7f345c32118f7d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mrs. Otis, who, I should note, knows a good deal about art\u2014having spent several winters in Boston as a girl\u2014has assured me that these jewels are extremely valuable, and would command a high price if sold. Under these conditions, Lord Canterville, I\u2019m sure you see why I can\u2019t let any member of my family keep them; in fact, all such showy ornaments, however fitting or even necessary to British aristocratic dignity, would be utterly out of place among people raised on the strict, and I believe lasting, principles of republican simplicity. Perhaps I should add that Virginia is very eager for you to let her keep the box as a keepsake of your unfortunate but misguided ancestor. Since it is extremely old and quite badly worn, you might consider granting her request. For my part, I admit I\u2019m rather surprised to hear one of my children express any sympathy for medievalism, and I can only explain it by the fact that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs shortly after Mrs. Otis returned from a trip to Athens.\u201d\\n\\nLord Canterville listened gravely to the worthy Minister, tugging at his gray moustache now and then to hide an involuntary smile, and when Mr. Otis finished he shook his hand warmly and said, \u201cMy dear sir, your delightful little daughter rendered my unlucky ancestor Sir Simon a most important service, and my family and I owe her a great deal for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels are clearly hers, and, honestly, I suspect that if I were heartless enough to take them back, the wicked old fellow would rise from his grave within a fortnight and make my life a misery. As for heirlooms, nothing counts as one unless a will or legal paper says so, and no one even knew these jewels existed. I assure you I have no more right to them than your butler does, and when Miss Virginia grows up I imagine she\u2019ll be glad to have pretty things to wear. Besides, you forget, Mr. Otis, that you bought the furniture and the ghost at an agreed price, and anything that belonged to the ghost immediately became yours, since whatever liveliness Sir Simon showed in the corridor at night, in the eyes of the law he was quite dead, and you acquired his property through the purchase.\u201d\"}"
