          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c7bdac9ea0d7840d115ea7d9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now come to my own story. The early part of my life holds little to tell, and I will be brief; but I must pause over my childhood so it is clear how, when a single hope failed, life became empty, and how, when the only affection I was allowed was destroyed, my existence ended with it.\\n\\nI have said my aunt was very different from my father. Without a trace of malice she possessed the coldest heart I have ever known: it could not feel any affection. She took me in because she felt it was her duty, yet she had spent too many years alone, undisturbed by children, to let me disrupt her peace. She had never married, and for the last five years had lived entirely alone on an estate she inherited through her mother on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. My father wrote, asking her to live with me at his family mansion in the lovely country near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused this plan, but once she settled the matters left to her care after her brother departed, she left England and took me to her estate in Scotland.\\n\\nA servant who had been my mother\u2019s took charge of me as a baby and stayed with us to care for me until I was eight. I lived in a remote part of the house and only saw my aunt at set times. Twice a day she appeared: around noon she came to my nursery, and after dinner I was taken to her. She never embraced me, and the whole time I sat in her room she seemed afraid I would annoy her with some childish whim. My good nurse drilled me carefully before we dared enter the parlor\u2014and the awe my aunt\u2019s cold looks and stiff words inspired kept me from disobeying her lessons or breaking the exemplary stillness required during these brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my nurse\u2019s watch I roamed freely through our park and the nearby fields. Born from the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the highest sensibility. I cannot tell how intensely I loved everything around me, even inanimate objects.\"}"
