          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b182c691c4b219683fcc264a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Directly before him, under the pallid glow of the moon, stood an aged figure of dreadful countenance. The eyes flamed like smoldering embers; tangled coils of long grey hair draped over the shoulders; the attire, fashioned in an old style, hung filthy and in tatters, and from both wrists and ankles dangled ponderous fetters and corroded shackles.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear sir,\u201d Mr. Otis remarked, \u201cI must really urge you to oil those chains, and I have brought, expressly for that purpose, a small bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator. One application is reputed completely effective, and the wrapper carries testimonials attesting to this from several of our most distinguished native clergymen. I shall leave it by the bedroom candles for you, and will gladly furnish more if you should need it.\u201d With that, the United States Minister set the bottle on a marble table and, after shutting his door, went back to bed.\\n\\nFor an instant the Canterville ghost remained stock-still in sheer outrage; then, hurling the bottle furiously upon the gleaming floor, he raced down the passageway, groaning hollowly and shedding a ghastly green glow. Yet as he came to the head of the broad oak staircase, a door swung open, two little white-clad figures appeared, and a substantial pillow shot past his skull! Clearly there was not a moment to waste, and choosing the Fourth Dimension of Space for his flight, he slipped through the wainscot, leaving the household in sudden calm.\\n\\nUpon arriving at a tiny secret room in the left wing, he leaned against a moonbeam to catch his breath and tried to grasp the situation. In an illustrious and unbroken stretch of three centuries he had never endured such an insult. He recalled the Dowager Duchess, whom he had terrified into a fit as she stood before her mirror in lace and diamonds; the four housemaids who fell into hysterics when he merely grinned at them from behind the curtains of one of the spare chambers; the parish rector, whose candle he had puffed out as the man returned late from the library, and who had needed Sir William Gull\u2019s care ever since, a complete martyr to nervous complaints; and old Madame de Tremouillac, who, waking early one morning to see a skeleton seated in an armchair by the hearth reading her diary, had been confined to bed six weeks with brain fever and, upon recovery, had reconciled herself to the Church and severed connections with that notorious sceptic Monsieur de Voltaire.\"}"
