          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c9eb9008c95dae253da747af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThen I may put two questions to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou may inquire,\u201d returned Monks, with a faint touch of surprise, \u201cbut whether I choose to respond is another question altogether.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhich brings the total to three,\u201d remarked Mr. Bumble, attempting a little wit.\\n\\n\u201cIs this what you expected from me?\u201d asked the matron.\\n\\n\u201cIt is,\u201d Monks answered. \u201cAnd the second?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you intend to do with it? Could it be used to injure me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d said Monks, \u201cnor to harm me. Now watch! But stir not an inch, or your life isn\u2019t worth a rush.\u201d\\n\\nWith that warning, he abruptly shoved the table aside, tugged an iron ring set in the planks, and flung back a broad trap that yawned at Mr. Bumble\u2019s toes, sending the beadle scrambling several paces in alarm.\\n\\n\u201cPeer below,\u201d said Monks, lowering the lantern into the void. \u201cHave no fear of me. If I had meant mischief, I\u2019d have let you slip quietly when you were sitting above it.\u201d\\n\\nThus reassured, the matron crept to the edge; Mr. Bumble, driven by curiosity, joined her. The muddy flood, swollen by the downpour, raced beneath, drowning every other sound with the splash and whirl where it struck the slimy, green-stained piles. A mill had once stood there; the tide, foaming and fretting around the decayed stakes and the fragments of machinery still clinging, appeared to leap forward more fiercely now that the vain impediments to its headlong rush were swept aside.\\n\\n\u201cIf a man\u2019s corpse were thrown in there, where would it be by morning?\u201d asked Monks, swinging the lantern in the dark shaft.\\n\\n\u201cTwelve miles downstream, and hacked to bits as well,\u201d replied Bumble, shuddering at the notion.\\n\\nMonks drew out the small packet he had hastily hidden in his breast, tied it to a lump of lead that had once belonged to a pulley lying on the floor, and dropped it into the torrent. It plunged cleanly, slicing the water with scarce a splash, and vanished.\\n\\nAs they gazed at one another, the three seemed finally able to draw easier breaths.\\n\\n\u201cThere!\u201d Monks exclaimed, letting the trap fall shut with a heavy clang that restored it to its place. \u201cIf the sea ever yields its dead, as the books promise, it will keep the gold, silver, and that rubbish among them. Our pleasant gathering is now concluded.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMost gladly,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, eager to be gone.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll keep that tongue of yours still, won\u2019t you?\u201d Monks warned, fixing him with a menacing stare.\"}"
