          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c7827da7f5bfbf0be41a52fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last we decided to live together during my stay in the city. Since my finances were a little better than his, I took on the cost of renting and furnishing, to match the strange dark mood we shared, a decrepit, grotesque house. It had long been abandoned because of superstitions we chose to ignore, and it leaned toward collapse in a lonely part of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad anyone known our routine there, they would have thought us mad\u2014though perhaps harmlessly so. We were completely shut away. No one visited us. Even my old acquaintances had no idea where I had gone, and Dupin had not had contact with anyone in Paris for many years. We lived entirely within our own world.\\n\\nMy friend had the odd fancy\u2014what else could I call it?\u2014of loving the night for its own sake, and I gave myself over to this eccentricity as I did to all his others. The dark goddess did not always stay with us, but we could recreate her. At the first light of day we closed all the dusty shutters of our ancient building and lit two heavily scented tapers that cast only the faintest, ghastliest glow. By that dim light we surrendered our minds to dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the real night. Then we went out into the streets, arm in arm, pursuing the conversations of the day or wandering far until late, searching through the wild lights and shadows of the crowded city for the endless mental stimulation that careful observation can bring.\\n\\nDuring these outings I could not avoid noticing, and admiring, Dupin\u2019s remarkable talent for analysis, though his vivid imagination had already led me to expect something of the sort. He seemed to take a sharp pleasure in exercising it, if not exactly in displaying it, and spoke openly of how much he enjoyed it. He told me, with a quiet chuckle, that most men, compared to him, carried windows in their chests, and he would back up that claim with direct and startling proof of how well he knew my own thoughts. At such moments he was cool and distant; his gaze seemed empty, and his voice, usually a rich tenor, rose into a thin treble that might have sounded peevish if not for the slow precision of his words.\"}"
