          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da74a08cec581c1e66f12a09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief conversation with Mr. Knightley afforded Emma much satisfaction. It ranked among the pleasant memories of the ball, which she revisited while strolling over the lawn the next morning. She was delighted that they had reached such a perfect understanding about the Eltons, their judgments of both husband and wife aligning so closely; and his commendation of Harriet, his willingness to yield in her favour, was especially pleasing. The Eltons\u2019 insolence, which had seemed on the point of spoiling the rest of her evening, had yielded some of its greatest pleasures; and she anticipated another agreeable consequence\u2014the ending of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From the way Harriet spoke of the matter before they left the ballroom, Emma was very hopeful. It was as if Harriet\u2019s eyes had suddenly opened, revealing that Mr. Elton was not the exalted being she had imagined. The fever had passed, and Emma could scarcely fear its returning through harmful politeness. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 ill temper to supply any further necessary discipline of pointed neglect. Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too much enamoured, and Mr. Knightley not inclined to quarrel with her\u2014what an exceedingly happy summer must lie ahead! She was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her that he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, needing to be at home by noon. She did not lament it. Having settled all these reflections, reviewed them, and set them to rights, she was just turning back to the house, spirits revived for the demands of the two little boys and their grandpapa alike, when the broad iron swing-gate opened, and two people entered whom she had never more expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! One instant convinced her that something out of the ordinary had occurred. Harriet appeared pale and terrified, and he was trying to reassure her. The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart; in another moment all three were in the hall, and Harriet, sinking instantly into a chair, swooned. A young lady who faints must be revived; questions need answering, and surprises explained. Such incidents are highly engaging, but their suspense cannot last long. In a few minutes Emma knew the whole. Miss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had likewise attended the ball, had gone out together, taking the Richmond road, which, though seemingly public enough for safety, had led them into alarm. About half a mile beyond Highbury, the road made a sharp turn and, thickly shaded by elms on both sides, grew very secluded for some distance; and when the young ladies had proceeded some way along it, they suddenly observed, a little ahead on a broader strip of turf beside the road, a group of gipsies.\"}"
