          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2e88253d4929ccfe6f3cdb16\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe deliberately missed her meaning when he hurried on, \u201cYes, customers. In the banking business we generally call the people we deal with our customers. He was a French gentleman, a scientific man, very accomplished\u2014a doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot from Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, this gentleman came from Beauvais. Like your father, he was respected in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our dealings were business, but confidential. I was working in our French branch then, and had been for twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask, when exactly, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m speaking of twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many French gentlemen and families, were entirely in Tellson\u2019s hands. In the same way, I am, or have been, trustee in one form or another for dozens of our customers. These are purely business ties, miss; there\u2019s no friendship in them, no special interest, no sentiment. I move from one customer to another in my business life just as I move from one appointment to another in my working day. In short, I have no feelings; I\u2019m a mere machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir, and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014her oddly furrowed forehead fixed on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan because my mother lived only two years after my father, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the hesitant little hand held out to him, put it ceremoniously to his lips, then led the young lady back to her chair. Holding the chair-back with his left hand and using his right by turns to rub his chin, tug at his wig, or stress his points, he stood looking down into her face while she sat looking up into his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will understand how truthful I was just now\u2014saying I have no feelings, that all my dealings with other people are mere business\u2014when you remember that I have never seen you since that day. No, you have been a ward of Tellson\u2019s since then, and I have been occupied with other Tellson business. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an enormous money mangle.\u201d\"}"
