          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ce2169e53c12e9ff83acbb39\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou must learn some of my philosophy. Think of the past only when remembering it brings you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot believe you have that kind of philosophy. Your memories must be so free from blame that your satisfaction comes not from wisdom but, even better, from ignorance. For me it is different. Painful memories intrude when they should not and cannot be pushed away. I have been selfish all my life in practice, though not in intention. As a child I was taught what was right, but no one taught me to control my temper. I was given good principles, yet allowed to follow them in pride and arrogance. As the only son, and for many years the only child, I was spoiled by parents who, though good and especially my benevolent father, allowed and almost taught me to be selfish and domineering, to care for no one beyond my own family, to think poorly of everyone else, to wish to undervalue their judgment and worth compared to mine. That was who I was from eight to twenty-eight, and I might still be the same if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! What do I not owe you? You taught me an important lesson, hard at first but most beneficial. You properly humbled me. I approached you without a doubt that you would accept me. You showed me how insufficient all my claims were to win a woman worthy of being won.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you truly convinced yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I had. What must you think of my vanity? I believed you were hoping for and expecting my proposal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manners must have misled you, though not on purpose, I assure you. I never meant to deceive you, but my spirits often carried me too far. You must have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHate you! I might have been angry at first, but my anger soon turned in the right direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hardly dare ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not at all. I felt only surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have been greater than mine at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no special politeness, and I admit I did not expect more than what was due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cI only wished to show you, by every courtesy I could offer, that I was not so petty as to resent the past. I hoped to win your forgiveness and lessen your poor opinion of me by proving that I had taken your reproofs to heart.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
