          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a6a5ad601cff80b859caf55f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you would have gone on if left alone. I wonder when you would have spoken if I hadn\u2019t asked you! My resolution to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had great effect. Too much, I\u2019m afraid; for what becomes of the moral if our happiness comes from breaking a promise? I shouldn\u2019t have brought up the subject. This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t need to worry. The moral will be perfectly intact. Lady Catherine\u2019s unjustified attempts to separate us removed all my doubts. I don\u2019t owe my present happiness to your eagerness to show gratitude. I wasn\u2019t in the mood to wait for you to broach anything. My aunt\u2019s information had given me hope, and I was determined to learn everything right away.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been tremendously useful, which ought to make her happy, since she loves to be useful. But tell me, why did you come down to Netherfield? Was it just to ride to Longbourn and feel awkward? Or did you intend something more serious?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true purpose was to see you and gauge, if possible, whether I could ever hope to make you love me. What I claimed, even to myself, was that I wanted to see if your sister still cared for Bingley, and, if she did, to confess everything to him as I later did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the courage to tell Lady Catherine what\u2019s about to happen to her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m more likely to lack time than courage, Elizabeth. But it must be done; give me a sheet of paper and I\u2019ll do it at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I didn\u2019t have a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire how even your handwriting is, as another young lady once did. But I have an aunt too, who mustn\u2019t be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she was unwilling to admit how much she had misjudged her relationship with Mr. Darcy, Elizabeth still hadn\u2019t answered Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter; but now, with something to share that she knew would be most welcome, she was almost ashamed to find her uncle and aunt had been deprived of three days of happiness, and she wrote immediately as follows:\\n\\n\u201cI would have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I should have done, for your long, kind, satisfying account; but to tell the truth, I was too out of sorts to write. You assumed more than truly existed. But now imagine as much as you like; let your fancy run loose, indulge your imagination in every possible flight the subject allows, and unless you picture me already married, you cannot be very wrong.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
      "role": "assistant"
    }
