          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6b6ed8a958e30a2e88c566fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhy, you don\u2019t mean\u2014\u201d my sister began.\\n\\n\u201cYes I do, Mum,\u201d said Pumblechook. \u201cBut hold on. Go on, Joseph. Good man! Keep going!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would everyone here say,\u201d Joe continued, \u201cto twenty pounds?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGenerous would be the word,\u201d my sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cWell then,\u201d Joe said, \u201cit\u2019s more than twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\nThat miserable hypocrite Pumblechook nodded again and said with a patronizing laugh, \u201cIt\u2019s more than that, Mum. Very good! Stay with it, Joseph!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo, to finish it,\u201d Joe said, happily handing the bag to my sister, \u201cit\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds, Mum,\u201d echoed that foul swindler Pumblechook, standing to shake her hand. \u201cIt\u2019s no more than you deserve (as I said when they asked my opinion), and I wish you joy of the money!\u201d\\n\\nIf the villain had stopped there, he would already have been bad enough, but he made it worse by seizing me like he had authority over me.\\n\\n\u201cNow you see, Joseph and wife,\u201d said Pumblechook, gripping my arm above the elbow, \u201cI\u2019m one of those who finish what they start. This boy must be bound this instant. That\u2019s my way. Bound at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHeaven knows, Uncle Pumblechook,\u201d my sister said, clutching the money, \u201cwe\u2019re deeply grateful to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind me, Mum,\u201d answered that evil corn dealer. \u201cA pleasure is a pleasure all over the world. But this boy, you know; we must have him bound. I promised I\u2019d handle it\u2014for truth\u2019s sake.\u201d\\n\\nThe Justices were sitting in the nearby Town Hall, and we went at once so I could be bound apprentice to Joe before them. I say we went, but Pumblechook pushed me there as if I had just picked a pocket or set fire to a stack; the Court seemed sure I\u2019d been caught in the act. As Pumblechook shoved me through the crowd, I heard some people ask, \u201cWhat\u2019s he done?\u201d and others say, \u201cHe\u2019s young, too, but he looks bad, doesn\u2019t he?\u201d One kindly looking person even gave me a tract decorated with a woodcut of a hateful young man decked out in chains like a sausage string, and titled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\\n\\nThe Hall struck me as odd, with higher pews than a church and people hanging over them to watch. Mighty Justices (one with powdered hair) leaned back in chairs with folded arms, or took snuff, or napped, or wrote, or read newspapers. There were also glossy black portraits on the walls, which my untrained eye thought looked like hard candy mixed with sticking plaster.\"}"
