          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"56c5d05227bddd7a3ead26b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No fancy could figure the sublimity that might have been shown had a similar spectacle occurred amid the night\u2019s darkness. Hell itself might have furnished an apt likeness. Even so, my hair bristled while I peered far down into the open gulfs, letting imagination descend, as it were, and pace the novel arched halls, the glowing pits, the crimson hideous fissures of the monstrous and measureless fire. I had indeed escaped by a hair. Had the balloon lingered even a very little longer within the cloud\u2014in other words, had not the simple discomfort of being drenched driven me to throw out the ballast, certain destruction must have followed. Such hazards, though rarely noted, are perhaps the gravest that aeronauts must encounter. By this time, however, I had reached too lofty an elevation to remain troubled on that score.\\n\\n\u201cI was now shooting upward fast, and by seven o\u2019clock the barometer registered an altitude of not less than nine miles and a half. I began to suffer great difficulty in drawing breath. My head, likewise, ached violently; and, having long felt a dampness on my cheeks, I finally perceived it was blood, oozing rather freely from the drums of my ears. My eyes also tormented me. When I passed a hand across them they seemed to have bulged from their sockets in no trifling degree; every object in the car, and even the balloon itself, appeared distorted to my sight. These indications exceeded what I had anticipated and gave me some alarm. At that moment, very rashly and without reflection, I flung from the car three five-pound parcels of ballast. The increased speed of ascent thereby gained carried me too abruptly, without sufficient gradation, into a highly rarefied belt of the air, and the consequence had nearly proved deadly to my adventure and to myself. I was suddenly seized with a convulsion lasting more than five minutes, and even when it subsided in part, I could snatch breath only at long intervals and with gasping efforts\u2014bleeding copiously the whole time from nose and ears, and even slightly from the eyes. The pigeons seemed distressed beyond measure and struggled to get free; while the cat mewed miserably, and, with tongue lolling from her mouth, reeled to and fro in the car as if poisoned. I now too late perceived the extreme recklessness of casting off the ballast, and my agitation was extreme. I expected nothing short of death\u2014and death within a few minutes.\u201d}\"}"
