          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"54e39072d33175c44b899ba6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was certainly diverting enough, and all that, yet I felt terribly let down; and when I inquired about the coat of mail, he merely chuckled and declared it was far too chilly to wear such things in England. Ah, here comes Mr. Podgers! Now, Mr. Podgers, I wish you to read the Duchess of Paisley\u2019s hand. Duchess, do remove your glove. No, not the left one, the other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Gladys, I truly doubt whether this is altogether proper,\u201d muttered the Duchess, weakly undoing a rather grubby kid glove.\\n\\n\u201cNothing that is interesting ever is,\u201d replied Lady Windermere; \u201con a fait le monde ainsi. However, I must present you. Duchess, this is Mr. Podgers, my tame cheiromantist. Mr. Podgers, the Duchess of Paisley; and if you pronounce her mountain of the moon larger than mine, I shall never trust you again.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am convinced, Gladys, nothing of that sort exists in my palm,\u201d the Duchess said, quite solemnly.\\n\\n\u201cYour Grace is entirely correct,\u201d observed Mr. Podgers, surveying the plump little hand with its short, square fingers. \u201cThe mountain of the moon is undeveloped. The life-line, however, is magnificent. Be so good as to bend the wrist. Thank you. Three separate lines on the rascette! You will reach a very advanced age, Duchess, and enjoy remarkable happiness. Ambition\u2014modest enough, the line of intellect not overdrawn, the line of heart\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo be indiscreet, Mr. Podgers,\u201d Lady Windermere interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cNothing would delight me more,\u201d Mr. Podgers said with a bow, \u201cif the Duchess had ever been indiscreet, but I regret I perceive a great stability of affection allied to a powerful sense of duty.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease continue, Mr. Podgers,\u201d said the Duchess, now distinctly gratified.\\n\\n\u201cEconomy ranks high among your Grace\u2019s qualities,\u201d Mr. Podgers went on, while Lady Windermere fell into peals of laughter.\\n\\n\u201cEconomy is an admirable virtue,\u201d the Duchess said serenely; \u201cwhen I married Paisley he possessed eleven castles and not one habitable house.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd at present he owns twelve houses and not one castle,\u201d Lady Windermere cried.\\n\\n\u201cWell, my dear,\u201d the Duchess began, \u201cI prefer\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cComfort,\u201d supplied Mr. Podgers, \u201cand the latest improvements, and hot water laid on in every bedroom. Your Grace is perfectly justified. Comfort is the sole benefit our civilisation provides.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have described the Duchess to perfection, Mr. Podgers, and now you must tell Lady Flora\u2019s,\u201d and, responding to the smiling hostess\u2019s nod, a tall girl with sandy Scottish hair and prominent shoulder-blades moved awkwardly from behind the sofa, extending a long, bony hand with spatulate fingers.\\n\\n\u201cAh, a pianist, I perceive,\u201d said Mr. Podgers, \u201ca splendid pianist, though perhaps scarcely a musician.\u201d\"}"
