          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dd0f43eb61df90e7ccf01a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last we arranged to share quarters during my visit in the city; and since my finances were a bit less straitened than his, I was allowed to shoulder the cost of leasing and fitting up\u2014according to the somewhat eccentric gloom of our shared temper\u2014a time-worn, grotesque mansion, long abandoned thanks to superstitions we never examined, and tottering on the verge of ruin in a secluded, desolate nook of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nHad the public been aware of the routine of our existence there, we would have been deemed lunatics\u2014though, perhaps, lunatics of a harmless strain. Our isolation was absolute. No guests were admitted. Indeed, the location of our retreat was carefully concealed even from my former acquaintances; and Dupin had for years ceased either to know or be known in Paris. We lived entirely within ourselves.\\n\\nIt was one of my friend\u2019s whims (for what else can it be called?) to love the night for her own sake; and into this bizarrerie, as into all his others, I quietly yielded, surrendering myself to his eccentric humors with perfect abandon. The sable goddess would not always remain with us in person; yet we could mimic her presence. At the first breath of dawn we shut up the dusty shutters of our antique pile, lighting a pair of tapers which, strongly scented, cast only the most ghastly, feeble glimmer. By their aid we devoted our spirits to daydreams\u2014reading, writing, or conversing\u2014until the clock gave notice of the coming of real Darkness. Then we would issue into the streets, arm in arm, carrying on the themes of the day, or roaming abroad till late, seeking in the wild lights and shadows of the teeming city that limitless mental excitement which quiet observation can provide.\\n\\nDuring these hours I could not help noticing and admiring\u2014though his lavish ideality had prepared me for it\u2014a singular analytic power in Dupin. He appeared, moreover, to take keen delight in exercising it\u2014if not precisely in displaying it\u2014and freely admitted the enjoyment he derived. With a low, chuckling laugh he would boast to me that most men, in relation to himself, wore windows in their chests, and he would be apt to follow such claims with direct, startling demonstrations of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. In those instants his demeanor was chill and abstract; his eyes held a vacant look; while his voice, ordinarily a rich tenor, climbed into a treble that might have seemed petulant were it not for the deliberate and perfectly distinct articulation.\"}"
        }
      ],
