{"request_id":"69a59a293e3e51e6b561f2fc","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. The people who did the deed were flesh and blood, and escaped in the same way. So how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason through this, and that method must lead us to a clear conclusion. Let us examine, one by one, the potential exits. It is obvious that the murderers were either in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or in the adjoining room, when the party climbed the stairs. Therefore, all avenues must be sought in these two rooms. The police stripped the floors, ceilings, and walls in every direction. No secret openings could have escaped them. Still, refusing to rely on their eyes alone, I examined everything myself. There were no hidden passages. Both doors from the rooms to the hallway were securely locked with the keys inside. Let us consider the fireplaces. Although of ordinary width for about eight or ten feet above the grates, they are too narrow higher up to admit even a large cat. Since escape by these routes is absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. From the windows in the front room no one could have escaped without being seen by the crowd in the street. The killers must have departed through the windows in the back room. Now that we have reached this conclusion so clearly, it is not our role, as thinkers, to reject it because of apparent impossibilities. We only have to prove that these “impossibilities” are not really so.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One is unobstructed and fully visible. The lower part of the other is hidden by the headboard of the heavy bed pushed against it. The first window was found bolted from within. It resisted even the strongest attempts to raise it. A large gimlet hole had been bored in the frame on the left, with a thick nail fitted tightly into it. When we examined the other window, we saw an identical nail set the same way, and even vigorous efforts could not lift that sash either. The police were now completely convinced that no one had escaped through these openings. And hence they thought it unnecessary to pull out the nails and open the windows.

“My own inspection was more meticulous, and for the reason I just stated—because it was here that every apparent impossibility had to be shown not to be impossible after all.”"}

