{"request_id":"f7441a5b08449ae7eecf2d97","rewritten_text":"You see, I had stashed my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things, but it was the kind of helmet you can’t take off by yourself. That detail hadn’t occurred to me when I put it there; in fact, I didn’t know it. I imagined it would be especially convenient. So now, knowing it was right there, so handy and close yet completely out of reach, only made everything worse. Yes, the thing you can’t reach is always the thing you want most; everyone knows that. Well, it kept my mind off everything else; it pulled my thoughts entirely into that helmet, mile after mile, picturing the handkerchief, imagining it. It was bitter and maddening to feel the salty sweat drip into my eyes and not be able to reach it. On paper it may seem minor, but it was nothing of the sort; it was real misery. I wouldn’t say so otherwise. I promised myself I’d carry a handbag next time, no matter how it looked or what people said. These iron-clad members of the Round Table might think it scandalous, maybe raise hell about it, but me—I’d take comfort first, style afterward. So we jogged along, and now and then we hit a stretch of dust and it would billow up in clouds, get into my nose, and make me sneeze and cry; and of course I said things I shouldn’t have, I won’t deny it. I’m no better than anyone else.

We couldn’t seem to meet anyone in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; in the mood I was in, that was a good thing for any ogre—at least, one with a handkerchief. Most knights would have focused on his armor, but if I’d gotten his bandanna, he could’ve kept his metal as far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile, it was getting hotter and hotter inside. The sun was beating down, heating the iron more and more. When you’re that hot, every little thing grates on you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that annoyed me; besides, I couldn’t stand the shield clanging and banging, first against my chest, then my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints squeaked and screeched like a worn-out wheelbarrow, and since we weren’t creating any breeze at that pace, I was close to frying in that stove. And the slower you went, the more the iron seemed to settle on you until you felt heavier with every minute."}

