          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"99037f9b0d4c4f106a700e5d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStop the starboard! Ting-a-ling-ling! Halt the larboard! Come on with the starboard! Stop her! Let the outside wheel turn slow! Ting-a-ling-ling! Chow-ow-ow! Run out that head-line! lively now! Come\u2014get that spring-line free\u2014what\u2019re you doing there! Take a turn round that stump with the bight! Stand by the stage\u2014let her drop! Engines finished, sir! Ting-a-ling-ling! SH\u2019T! S\u2019H\u2019T! SH\u2019T!\u201d (testing the gauge-cocks).\\n\\nTom kept whitewashing\u2014paid the steamboat no heed. Ben stared a second, then called: \u201cHi-Yi! You\u2019re up a tree, ain\u2019t you!\u201d\\n\\nNo response. Tom admired his latest stroke with an artist\u2019s eye, gave the brush another tender sweep, and inspected the outcome again. Ben sidled up alongside. Tom\u2019s mouth longed for the apple, but he clung to his task. Ben said:\\n\\n\u201cHallo, old fellow, you\u2019re made to labor, hey?\u201d\\n\\nTom whirled and answered:\\n\\n\u201cWhy, it\u2019s you, Ben! I wasn\u2019t paying attention.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSay\u2014I\u2019m off for a swim, I am. Don\u2019t you wish you could? But naturally you\u2019d prefer work\u2014wouldn\u2019t you? Sure you would!\u201d\\n\\nTom studied the boy a moment and asked:\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you call work?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, ain\u2019t that work?\u201d\\n\\nTom went back to the fence, replying casually:\\n\\n\u201cWell, maybe it is, maybe it ain\u2019t. All I know is it suits Tom Sawyer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh now, you don\u2019t pretend you like it?\u201d\\n\\nThe brush kept gliding.\\n\\n\u201cLike it? Well, I don\u2019t see why I shouldn\u2019t. Does a boy get a chance to whitewash a fence every day?\u201d\\n\\nThat shifted the perspective. Ben stopped chewing his apple. Tom swept the brush delicately back and forth\u2014stepped away to note the effect\u2014touched up a spot or two\u2014criticized the effect once more\u2014Ben watching each motion, growing steadily more engrossed, more fascinated. Soon he begged:\\n\\n\u201cSay, Tom, let me whitewash a bit.\u201d\\n\\nTom pondered, almost consented, then changed his mind:\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014no\u2014I reckon it wouldn\u2019t hardly answer, Ben. You see, Aunt Polly\u2019s dreadful particular about this fence\u2014right here on the street, you know\u2014but if it was the back fence I\u2019d not mind and she wouldn\u2019t either. Yes, she\u2019s mighty particular about this one; it\u2019s got to be finished real careful; I reckon there ain\u2019t one boy in a thousand, maybe two, that can do it the way it has to be done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014really? Oh come, now\u2014let me try. Just a little\u2014I\u2019d let you, if you were me, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBen, I\u2019d like to, honest injun; but Aunt Polly\u2014why, Jim wanted the job, and she said no; Sid wanted it, and she turned Sid down.\u201d\"}"
