          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8319d567c34078369355a72f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I wish to see you permanently well connected, and for that purpose it will be wise to keep as few peculiar acquaintances as possible; therefore, I tell you that if you remain in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope you will not be led, through your closeness with the sisters, into knowing the bride, who will likely be some mere farmer\u2019s daughter, untaught.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly. Yes. Not that I imagine Mr. Martin would marry anyone who had not received some education\u2014and been properly brought up. Still, I do not intend to set my opinion against yours\u2014and I am sure I shall not desire his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I shall always feel great regard for the Miss Martins, Elizabeth especially, and should be very sorry to give them up, for they are just as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, coarse woman, undoubtedly I had better avoid visiting her, if possible.\u201d\\n\\nEmma observed her through the variations of this speech, and detected no threatening sign of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted he had no other hold, and that there would be no serious hindrance, on Harriet\u2019s side, to any friendly plan of her own.\\n\\nThey encountered Mr. Martin the following day, while walking along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after gazing respectfully at Emma, he looked with unmistakable delight at Harriet. Emma did not regret such a chance for inspection; and moving a few steps ahead while they conversed, she soon allowed her sharp eye to grow sufficiently acquainted with Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was extremely neat, and he presented the air of a sensible young man, yet his person offered no further advantages; and when she compared him with gentlemen, she believed he must surrender all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s preference. Harriet was not indifferent to manners; she had voluntarily noticed her father\u2019s civility with admiration mixed with wonder. Mr. Martin looked as though he had no notion of what manners were.\\n\\nThey remained together only a few minutes, as Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and Harriet then came running back with a smiling face, full of fluttering excitement, which Miss Woodhouse hoped soon to calm.\\n\\n\u201cOnly think of our happening to meet him!\u2014How extraordinary! It was quite by chance, he said, that he had not gone round by Randalls. He never thought we walked this road. He imagined we took the way towards Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
