          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"24564be4f1d074b7dc3a99cc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We grew up side by side, less than a year apart. It goes without saying that we never quarrelled or drifted apart. Harmony was the heart of our friendship, and our contrasting personalities drew us closer. Elizabeth was calmer and more focused; yet for all my intensity, I could apply myself with deeper concentration and felt a stronger hunger for knowledge. She followed the airy inventions of poets, and in the majestic scenes around our Swiss home\u2014the towering mountains, the changing seasons, storms and calms, the quiet of winter, and the lively rush of our Alpine summers\u2014she found endless admiration and joy. While my companion gazed with serious satisfaction at these grand sights, I took delight in uncovering their causes. The world was a secret I longed to decipher. Curiosity, determined study to learn nature\u2019s hidden laws, and a joy bordering on ecstasy as those laws revealed themselves are among my earliest memories.\\n\\nWhen a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents gave up their wandering life and settled permanently in their homeland. We owned a house in Geneva and a country place at Belrive on the eastern shore of the lake, a bit more than a league from the city. We lived mostly at the latter, and my parents led a rather secluded life. My nature inclined me to avoid crowds and to give myself passionately to a few close attachments. I was therefore indifferent to most of my schoolmates, but I bound myself in the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Genevan merchant. He was a boy of striking talent and imagination. He loved daring, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He read deeply in tales of chivalry and romance. He composed heroic songs and began many stories of enchantment and knightly adventures. He tried to make us perform plays and hold masquerades featuring the heroes of Roncesvalles, the knights of King Arthur\u2019s Round Table, and the valiant bands who shed their blood to win back the holy sepulchre from the infidels.\\n\\nNo human being could have had a happier childhood than mine. My parents embodied kindness and indulgence. We knew they did not rule our fate with whim or tyranny, but acted as the very makers of all the delights we enjoyed.\"}"
