          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2d5c7705e5d18f120584a18\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent on ahead to Adrian, who had been chosen arbitrator; they had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and laid waste countryside, nourishing fierce animosity all the way, and now urged their respective claims with unabated factional zeal.\\n\\nBy questioning the envoys separately and after long inquiry, we discovered the actual condition of Paris. Since parliament had appointed him Ryland\u2019s substitute, every surviving English person had yielded obedience to Adrian. He was our commander to lead us from our homeland into unexplored territories, our legislator and guardian. When we first arranged our emigration project, continual division among our members had not been intended, and the chain of authority culminated with the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events reshaped our plans and caused the majority of our people to be severed for nearly two months from their supreme head. They had crossed in two distinct contingents, and upon meeting at Paris dissension broke out between them.\\n\\nThey discovered Paris abandoned. When the pestilence first arose, returning travellers and merchants, along with letters, had kept us constantly informed of the havoc wrought in Europe. Yet as mortality increased, this contact dwindled and stopped. Even within England communication from one end of the island to the other grew sluggish and scarce. No vessel crossed the channel between Calais and Dover; or if some sad wanderer, anxious to learn the fate of his kin, launched again for our coast, often the ravenous sea engulfed his tiny boat, or after a day or two he caught the contagion and expired before he could recount the ruin of France. Thus we remained in large part ignorant of continental affairs, still cherishing some obscure hope of discovering many fellow-sufferers along its breadth. But the very influences that had so dreadfully thinned England had worked with wider license in the neighboring realm. France was a blank; along the whole road from Calais to Paris no single living soul appeared. In Paris itself perhaps a hundred persons remained, who, resigned to their approaching doom, flitted through the capital\u2019s streets and gathered to talk of former days, showing that vivacity and even cheerfulness that rarely fails the sons and daughters of that nation.\\n\\nThe English took undisputed possession of Paris. Its towering houses and cramped streets were devoid of life. A few wan figures could be discerned at their usual haunt in the Tuileries; they marvelled why islanders should draw near their luckless city\u2014for in extremity of misery, sufferers invariably imagine their share of calamity the sharpest, as, when racked by intolerable agony, we would exchange the very torment we endure for any other that might seize a different part of the frame.\"}"
