          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8a8f7261a9f976cb7775e728\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI hope I\u2019ll soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,\u201d Mr. Weston said.\\n\\nMrs. Elton, very ready to imagine a special compliment meant for her, smiled graciously.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,\u201d he went on, \u201cand know that he is my son, though he doesn\u2019t carry my name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes, and I shall be delighted to meet him. I\u2019m sure Mr. Elton won\u2019t delay in calling on him; we\u2019ll both be very pleased to have him at the Vicarage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re very kind. Frank will be thrilled, I\u2019m sure. He\u2019s supposed to be in town next week, if not sooner. We were told so in a letter today. I met the post on my way this morning, and seeing my son\u2019s handwriting, I took the liberty of opening it\u2014even though it wasn\u2019t addressed to me; it was for Mrs. Weston. She\u2019s his main correspondent, I can tell you. I hardly ever get a letter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you really opened a letter addressed to her! Oh, Mr. Weston\u2014\u201d she laughed affectedly, \u201cI must protest. That sets a terribly dangerous example! I do hope you won\u2019t let the neighbours imitate you. Goodness, if this is what we\u2019re in for, we married women will have to start asserting ourselves. Oh, Mr. Weston, I never would have believed it of you!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, we men are rascals. You must watch out for yourself, Mrs. Elton. This letter tells us\u2014it\u2019s brief, written in haste just to let us know\u2014that they are all coming to town immediately because of Mrs. Churchill. She hasn\u2019t been well all winter and thinks Enscombe is too cold, so they\u2019re all to head south without delay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cReally? From Yorkshire, I think. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, about a hundred and ninety miles from London\u2014a long trip.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes indeed, very long. Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of great wealth? You would be astonished to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, dashes about. You may scarcely believe it, but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back with four horses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe problem with the distance from Enscombe,\u201d Mr. Weston said, \u201cis that Mrs. Churchill, as we hear it, hasn\u2019t been able to get off the sofa for a full week at a time. In Frank\u2019s last letter she complained, he said, that she was too weak to go into her conservatory unless she had both his arm and his uncle\u2019s.\u201d\"}"
