          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a783ad6a4fe3b589261e715b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, monsieur le mauvais sujet,\u201d the old lady exclaimed as he entered, \u201cwhy haven\u2019t you come to see me all this time?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lady Clem, I haven\u2019t had a moment to myself,\u201d Lord Arthur replied with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you mean you spend all day with Miss Sybil Merton, buying chiffons and talking nonsense? I can\u2019t understand why people make such a fuss about getting married. In my day we never dreamed of billing and cooing in public, or in private either.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you I haven\u2019t seen Sybil for twenty-four hours, Lady Clem. As far as I can tell, she belongs entirely to her milliners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; that\u2019s the only reason you visit an ugly old woman like me. I\u2019m surprised you men don\u2019t take warning. On a fait des folies pour moi, and here I am, a poor rheumatic creature, with a false front and a bad temper. If it weren\u2019t for dear Lady Jansen, who sends me all the worst French novels she can find, I don\u2019t think I could get through the day. Doctors are useless except for taking one\u2019s money. They can\u2019t even cure my heartburn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve brought you a cure for that, Lady Clem,\u201d said Lord Arthur solemnly. \u201cIt\u2019s an extraordinary invention by an American.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I like American inventions, Arthur. I\u2019m quite sure I don\u2019t. I read some American novels lately, and they were utter nonsense.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, but there\u2019s nothing nonsensical about this, Lady Clem! I promise you it\u2019s a perfect cure. You must promise to try it.\u201d Lord Arthur pulled a small box from his pocket and handed it to her.\\n\\n\u201cWell, the box is charming, Arthur. Is it really a present? How kind of you. And is this the marvelous medicine? It looks like a bonbon. I\u2019ll take it right now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, Lady Clem,\u201d Lord Arthur cried, grabbing her hand, \u201cyou mustn\u2019t do anything of the sort. It\u2019s a homeopathic medicine, and if you take it without having heartburn, it could do you terrible harm. Wait until you have an attack, and take it then. You\u2019ll be amazed by the result.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019d like to take it now,\u201d Lady Clementina said, lifting the little transparent capsule to the light, its bubble of liquid aconitine suspended inside. \u201cI\u2019m sure it\u2019s delightful. The truth is, although I hate doctors, I adore medicines. Still, I\u2019ll save it for my next attack.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd when will that be?\u201d Lord Arthur asked eagerly.\"}"
