          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"475d2d637213dca3e3c54a8a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be lifted to the skies, on flowery couches of ease, while others struggle to win the crown and drift through bloody seas?\\n\\nHe had the reputation of being a marvelous reader. At church \u201csociables\u201d he was invariably asked to recite poetry; and when he finished, the ladies would fling up their hands and let them drop limp into their laps, roll their eyes heavenward, and shake their heads as if to say, \u201cWords fail us; it surpasses all expression, FAR too lovely for this mortal realm.\u201d\\n\\nWhen the hymn ended, the Rev. Mr. Sprague transformed himself into a bulletin-board and rattled off \u201cnotices\u201d of gatherings and societies and whatnot until it seemed the list would run on to judgment day\u2014a peculiar custom still practiced in America, even in big cities, despite this age of abundant newspapers. Frequently the less reason there is for an inherited custom, the more stubbornly it resists extinction.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a generous, sweeping prayer that descended into particulars: it entreated blessings on the church, and the children of the church; on the neighboring churches; on the town; on the county; on the State; on the State\u2019s officials; on the United States; on the churches of the United States; on Congress; on the President; on the government officers; on poor sailors tossed upon stormy waters; on the oppressed millions groaning beneath European monarchs and Eastern tyrannies; on those who have the light and glad tidings yet lack eyes to see or ears to hear; on the heathen scattered through distant islands; and it ended by imploring that the words he was about to utter might win grace and favor, like seed falling into fertile soil and yielding in due season a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nA rustle of skirts followed, and the standing congregation settled into their seats. The boy whose tale this book tells found no delight in the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if that. He was restless throughout; he mentally kept count of the prayer\u2019s particulars without meaning to\u2014for he was not truly listening, but he knew the familiar ground and the preacher\u2019s well-worn path over it\u2014and whenever a scrap of fresh matter got mixed in, his ear caught it and his whole being objected to it; he deemed additions unfair and knavish. Midway through the prayer a fly alighted on the pew-back before him and tormented his soul by calmly rubbing its forelegs together, folding its arms around its head, and polishing it so zealously it seemed about to detach, exposing the slender thread of a neck; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them against its body as though they were coat-tails; completing its entire toilet as serenely as if certain it was in no danger at all.\"}"
