          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fb8013c95839411dcc7534af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew cast him a futile look while passing out to the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI anticipate the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Rest well! Escort Monsieur my nephew to his chamber there!\u2014And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you please,\u201d he muttered to himself before he rang his tiny bell once more and called his valet to his own room.\\n\\nAfter the valet came and went, Monsieur the Marquis paced back and forth in his loose robe to ready himself gently for sleep on that stifling still night. Rustling about the room with his soft slippers soundless on the floor, he moved like a polished tiger\u2014resembled some bewitched marquis of the incurably wicked stories, whose periodic transformation into a tiger was either fading or beginning.\\n\\nHe strode from one end of his sumptuous chamber to the other, revisiting the fragments of the day\u2019s journey that intruded on his thoughts: the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the sinking sun, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the small village nestled below, the peasants at the fountain, and the road-mender in his blue cap pointing at the chain beneath the carriage. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain, the small bundle lying on the steps, the women bending over it, and the tall man with upheld arms crying, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am calm now,\u201d observed Monsieur the Marquis, \u201cand may retire to bed.\u201d\\n\\nSo, leaving a single light burning on the broad hearth, he let the thin gauze curtains fall around him and heard the night shatter its silence with a long sigh as he settled to sleep.\\n\\nThe stone faces on the outer walls stared blankly at the black night for three weighty hours; for three weighty hours, the horses in the stables clattered at their mangers, the dogs barked, and the owl emitted a sound bearing little resemblance to the note poets traditionally credit it with. Yet such creatures obstinately refrain from uttering what is recorded for them.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the chateau\u2019s stone visages, leonine and human, gazed vacantly into the night. Dead darkness shrouded the entire landscape; dead darkness added its own hush to the hushed dust along the roads. The burial-ground reached a state where its small tufts of meager grass were indistinguishable from one another; the figure on the Cross might have stepped down for any trace there was to see. In the village, both taxers and taxed slept deeply. Dreaming perhaps of feasts, as the starved often do, and of ease and rest, as the beaten slave and the yoked ox may, its gaunt inhabitants slumbered soundly and were nourished and unburdened.\"}"
