          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6d4035ffeb1457a6fbf7aae1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThey know how sharp he is; they\u2019ll give Artful nothing less than life,\u201d said the first speaker. \u201cThey\u2019ll send the Artful away for good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by \u2018lagging\u2019 and \u2018a lifer\u2019?\u201d demanded Mr. Bolter. \u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking that way to me? Why don\u2019t you speak so I can follow you?\u201d\\n\\nFagin was about to explain these mysterious terms in plain language, and Mr. Bolter would have learned that they meant \u201ctransportation for life,\u201d when the conversation was cut short by the arrival of Master Bates. His hands were shoved in his breeches pockets, and his face twisted into a look of half comic, half miserable distress.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s all over, Fagin,\u201d said Charley once he and his new companion had been introduced.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ve found the gentleman who owns the box; two or three more are coming to identify him; and the Artful\u2019s booked for the voyage,\u201d replied Master Bates. \u201cI\u2019ll need a full suit of mourning, Fagin, and a hatband to visit him in before he sets off. To think of Jack Dawkins\u2014lummy Jack\u2014the Dodger\u2014the Artful Dodger\u2014being sent abroad for a miserable twopenny-halfpenny snuffbox! I never believed he\u2019d do it for less than a gold watch with chain and seals. Oh, why didn\u2019t he rob some rich old gentleman of all his valuables and leave like a gentleman, instead of going like a common prig without any honour or glory!\u201d\\n\\nWith this burst of feeling for his unlucky friend, Master Bates dropped onto the nearest chair, looking sour and hopeless.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by saying he has no honour or glory?\u201d cried Fagin, shooting an angry look at his pupil. \u201cWasn\u2019t he always the best hand among you all? Was there one of you who could match him on a job? Eh?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot one,\u201d answered Master Bates, his voice rough with grief. \u201cNot one.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen what are you talking about?\u201d demanded Fagin sharply. \u201cWhat are you bawling for?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBecause it\u2019s not on the record, is it?\u201d said Charley, stung into open defiance by his sorrow. \u201cBecause it won\u2019t show up in the indictment. Because nobody will ever know half of what he was. How will he be listed in the Newgate Calendar? Maybe he won\u2019t be there at all. Oh, my eye, my eye, what a blow it is!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d laughed Fagin, stretching out his right hand and turning to Mr. Bolter, shaking with chuckles as if he had the palsy. \u201cSee what pride they take in their trade, my dear.\u201d\"}"
