          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3bcefabdb17b203deed47bdf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To my mind that quaint fib had been told in the simplest, loveliest way; but I had only heard it once, and that does matter; when everything was new, it must have sounded delightful to the others. Sir Dinadan the Humorist woke first, and roused everyone else with a rather shabby practical joke. He fastened some metal mugs to a dog\u2019s tail and let him loose, and the poor beast tore madly around the place in panic, with the other dogs baying after him, smashing into anything in their path and producing absolute chaos and a perfect storm of racket; the entire crowd laughed till their eyes ran, and a few people toppled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in bliss. They were exactly like a pack of children. Sir Dinadan gloried in his prank so much that he kept repeating, till everyone was tired of it, how this immortal idea occurred to him; and, as with humorists of his sort, he was still laughing after everyone else had stopped. He was so puffed up he had to make a speech\u2014naturally a humorous one. I doubt I ever heard so many stale, worn-out gags strung together. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than any circus clown. It felt oddly mournful to sit here, thirteen centuries before my birth, and listen again to the same poor, moth-eaten jokes that had given me the dry gripes as a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly persuaded me no new joke can ever exist. People laughed at those antiquities, of course\u2014they always do; I had noticed that centuries afterwards. But the scoffer, that is, the boy, didn\u2019t laugh. No, he mocked; there was nothing he wouldn\u2019t scoff at. He declared most of Sir Dinadan\u2019s jokes were rotten and the remainder petrified. I said \u201cpetrified\u201d was fine; I myself thought the only proper method of classifying some of those jokes\u2019 majestic ages was by geologic periods. Yet that tidy idea went right past the boy, because geology hadn\u2019t been invented yet. I made a note of it, though, planning to educate the realm up to it if I survived. A good notion shouldn\u2019t be discarded merely because the market isn\u2019t ready. Then Sir Kay got up and started cranking his history-mill, with me as the stock for it.\"}"
