          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f3d2ef20eab585fdfe098f5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Year after year, Dorian Gray remained under the spell of that book, or perhaps it is truer to say he never wished to shake its hold. He obtained no fewer than nine large-paper copies from Paris, each one bound in a different hue to answer the shifting moods and wayward whims of a nature he seemed, at moments, almost to have surrendered. The hero, that marvellous young Parisian whose romantic and scientific temperaments were so curiously interwoven, became for him a prototype of his own being. Indeed, the entire volume appeared to recount the tale of his life, inscribed before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he stood more fortunate than the novel\u2019s extravagant protagonist. He never experienced\u2014nor had any reason to experience\u2014that grotesque terror of mirrors, shining metal, and still water that overwhelmed the young Parisian so early, prompted by the swift ruin of a beauty once apparently so extraordinary. With something like a cruel delight\u2014and perhaps in almost every delight, as surely in every pleasure, cruelty lies\u2014he would read the final portion, with its truly tragic, albeit somewhat overwrought, chronicle of the grief and anguish of a man who had forfeited what, in others and in the world, he had held most dear.\\n\\nThe exquisite loveliness that had so enthralled Basil Hallward, and so many others beside, seemed never to desert him. Even those who had heard the darkest whispers about him\u2014since odd rumours respecting his way of life from time to time stole through London and became club gossip\u2014could not accept any tale to his discredit when they beheld him. He bore always the aspect of one untainted by the world. Men who spoke coarsely fell silent upon Dorian Gray\u2019s entrance. There was a purity in his face that reproved them; his mere presence seemed to awaken in them recollections of innocence they had marred. They marvelled that one so gracious and winning could have escaped the stains of an age both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, returning home from those enigmatic and prolonged disappearances that provoked such conjecture among those who deemed themselves his friends, he would steal upstairs to the locked chamber, open the door with the key he now never relinquished, and stand, mirror in hand, before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted, shifting his gaze between the wicked, time-worn visage upon the canvas and the fair, youthful face that laughed back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
