          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e47e8b8c39b1f366905943e0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These envoys had been sent to Adrian, who had been chosen judge; they had traveled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and desolate countryside, seething with hatred for each other, and now they argued their cases with unchecked party spirit.\\n\\nBy questioning the deputies separately and investigating extensively, we discovered the real situation in Paris. Since Parliament had made him Ryland\u2019s deputy, every remaining English person had submitted to Adrian. He was our commander to lead us from our homeland to unknown regions, our lawgiver, and our protector. When the emigration plan was first organized, no one imagined that the group would be separated for long, and the chain of command ended with the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events altered our plans and caused most of us to be separated from the supreme leader for nearly two months. The people had crossed over in two distinct groups, and once they reached Paris, conflict broke out between them.\\n\\nThey had discovered Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, travelers, merchants, and letters regularly told us about the disease\u2019s progress on the continent. But as the death toll rose, those communications dwindled and finally stopped. Even within England, contact from one end of the island to the other became slow and rare. No ship crossed the channel between Calais and Dover; or if some grieving traveler, desperate to confirm the fate of his relatives, left the French shore to return to us, the hungry sea often swallowed his small boat, or after a day or two he caught the infection and died before he could describe France\u2019s ruin. We were therefore largely ignorant of conditions on the continent and clung to a faint hope of finding many companions along that broad route. But the same forces that had devastated England had worked even more violently in that neighboring land. France was empty; along the entire road from Calais to Paris not a single person was seen. In Paris there remained a handful, perhaps a hundred, who, accepting their approaching deaths, drifted through the streets of the capital and gathered to talk about earlier days, with that liveliness and even cheerfulness that hardly ever abandons individuals of that nation.\\n\\nThe English took undisputed possession of Paris. Its tall houses and narrow streets were lifeless. A few pale figures could be seen at their usual meeting place in the Tuileries; they wondered why these islanders would enter their doomed city\u2014for in extreme misery, sufferers always imagine their share of the calamity is the worst, just as, when in intense pain, we would trade the particular agony we endure for any other that might strike another part of the body.\"}"
