          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa105ace403c820744125e36\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I hurried along, and we soon reached my college. Then I remembered, with a shudder, that the creature I\u2019d left in my apartment might still be there, alive and roaming about. I dreaded seeing that monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. So I begged Henry to stay at the bottom of the stairs for a few minutes, and I rushed up to my room. My hand was already on the door latch before I realized what I was doing. I stopped, and a cold shiver ran through me. I flung the door open, as children do when they expect a ghost to be waiting on the other side; but nothing was there. I stepped inside cautiously. The room was empty, and my bedroom was free of its ghastly guest. I could hardly believe I\u2019d been granted such good fortune, but once I was certain my enemy had really fled, I clapped my hands in joy and ran downstairs to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went back up to my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast; but I couldn\u2019t control myself. It wasn\u2019t simply joy that gripped me; my skin tingled with hypersensitivity, and my pulse raced. I couldn\u2019t stay still for even a moment; I leapt over chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed out loud. At first Clerval thought my unusual liveliness came from being thrilled to see him, but when he studied me more closely, he noticed a wildness in my eyes he couldn\u2019t explain, and my loud, unrestrained, unnatural laughter frightened and astonished him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cfor God\u2019s sake, what\u2019s the matter? Don\u2019t laugh like that. You\u2019re so ill! What\u2019s causing all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes, because I imagined I saw the dreaded specter slipping into the room; \u201che can tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I thought the monster seized me; I fought wildly and collapsed in a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A meeting he had looked forward to with such joy turned to misery. But I didn\u2019t witness his sorrow, for I lay unconscious and didn\u2019t regain my senses for a very long time.\\n\\nThat was the start of a nervous fever that kept me bedridden for several months. During all that time, Henry was my only nurse. I later learned that since my father was too old and unfit for such a long trip, and my illness would have crushed Elizabeth, he spared them the grief by hiding how serious my condition was.\"}"
