          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d9fc837d259b374e6d845e9f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For a moment I was too stunned to think clearly. I knew my design differed in its details from this one, even though the general outline was similar. Soon I picked up a candle, sat at the far end of the room, and examined the parchment more closely. When I turned it over, I found my own sketch on the back exactly as I had drawn it. My first reaction was simple astonishment at the remarkable similarity of the outlines\u2014at the strange coincidence that, without my knowledge, a skull had been on the other side of the parchment directly beneath my drawing of the scarab, and that this skull matched my drawing so closely in both shape and size. That coincidence truly stunned me for a while. This is how such coincidences usually affect us. The mind tries to establish some link, a causal chain, and when it can\u2019t, it falls into a kind of temporary paralysis. But when I emerged from that stupor, another conviction gradually formed, one that alarmed me even more than the coincidence itself. I began to remember, clearly and without doubt, that there had been no drawing on the parchment when I made my sketch of the scarab. I became absolutely certain, because I recalled flipping first one side and then the other, searching for the cleanest spot. If the skull had been there then, I couldn\u2019t possibly have missed it. This mystery seemed beyond explanation; yet even then, a faint, firefly-like idea flickered in the darkest, most hidden corners of my mind\u2014a hint of the truth that last night\u2019s adventure would prove so magnificently. I stood up at once, put the parchment away safely, and put the matter out of my mind until I could be alone.\\n\\n\u201cWhen you had gone and Jupiter had fallen fast asleep, I began a more methodical investigation of the situation. First, I considered how the parchment had come into my possession. The place where we found the scarab was on the mainland coast, about a mile east of the island and just above the high-water mark. When I first picked it up, it bit me sharply, and I dropped it. Jupiter, ever cautious, refused to grab the insect as it flew toward him until he found a leaf or something similar he could use to handle it.\"}"
