          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"366c8e82fffd6921babde56e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity that always brings misery must have been broken, and the cruel fate that rules it would not allow this break in her eternal laws. But why should I regret that? Misery was my element, and only what was miserable could come near me; if Woodville had been happy I would never have known him. And can I, who for many years was fed by tears and raised under the dew of grief, stop to tell a tale of sorrow and death?\\n\\nWoodville had to travel into the country and was held there day after day, away from his lovely bride. He received a letter from her saying she was slightly ill, but urging him to hurry to her, that health would return from his eyes and his presence was her surest medicine. He was delayed three more days and then rushed to her. His heart, he knew not why, warned him of misfortune; he had heard nothing more from her; he feared she might be worse, and this fear made him restless to see her again standing before him in health and beauty. A sinister voice seemed always to whisper, \u201cYou will never again behold her as she was.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her home it was silent. He moved through several rooms; in one he saw a servant weeping bitterly. Weak with fear, he could hardly ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d and just heard the dreadful answer, \u201cNot yet.\u201d These shocking words felt less terrifying than those he had expected; learning that she was still alive and that he might still hope brought some relief. He remembered her letter and clung to the wild idea that his kisses, breathing warm love and life, would give her new spirit, and that with him near her she could not die, that his presence was the talisman of her life.\\n\\nHe hurried to her sickroom; she lay with cheeks burning from fever, yet her eyes were closed and she seemed unconscious. He wrapped her in his arms, pressed breathless kisses on her burning lips, and called to her in a voice of subdued anguish by the tenderest names: \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am with you; your life, your love. Return, dearest; you promised me this gift, that I would bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? To see you no more? To part from what is part of myself, without whom I have no memory and no future?\u201d\"}"
