          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dd134b348c9be40fc351447c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now hurry to the more stirring part of my tale, to events that stirred such feelings in me that they shaped who I have become. Spring moved in quickly; the weather grew mild and the sky clear. It astonished me that what had been barren and gloomy now bloomed with flowers and green plants. My senses rejoiced in countless delightful scents and sights. On one of those days, when the cottagers rested from their work\u2014the old man played his guitar while the children listened\u2014I noticed Felix looked indescribably sad. He sighed often, and once the old man stopped his playing. From his manner, I guessed he asked why his son was upset. Felix answered cheerfully, and the old man was about to resume his music when someone knocked at the door. A lady on horseback stood there, guided by a countryman. She wore a dark outfit and a thick black veil. Agatha asked her something, but the stranger only replied by softly saying Felix\u2019s name. Her voice was musical yet unlike any of my friends\u2019. Hearing it, Felix rushed to her, and she lifted her veil. I saw a face of angelic beauty. Her hair shone raven-black and was carefully braided; her eyes were dark yet gentle and lively; her features were perfectly proportioned, and her complexion was fair with a pink flush on each cheek. Felix was seized with joy at the sight of her, all traces of sorrow disappearing as his face shone with such ecstasy I could hardly believe it. His eyes sparkled and his cheeks reddened with happiness, and just then I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. She showed different emotions; wiping away a few tears, she offered her hand, which Felix kissed passionately, calling her, as best I could tell, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him but smiled. He helped her down from her horse, dismissed the guide, and brought her inside the cottage. There he spoke with his father, and the young strange