          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"74b0eb7ed83115d2eb02dc6a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking at him, one would have thought Nemesis had stolen Pallas\u2019s shield and shown him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He seemed turned to stone, his face marble with sadness. He had lived the refined and easy life of a young man with wealth and rank, a life free from common worries and full of carefree charm; and now, for the first time, he understood the terrible mystery of Destiny, the dreadful meaning of Doom.\\n\\nHow absurd and monstrous it all seemed! Could it be that on his hand, in letters he could not read but another could, there was some dreadful secret of guilt, some blood-red mark of crime? Was there no escape? Were we only chess pieces moved by an unseen power, vessels shaped by a potter\u2019s whim, set apart for honour or disgrace? His reason rebelled, yet he felt some tragedy hung over him and he had been suddenly forced to carry an unbearable burden. Actors are lucky. They can decide whether to appear in tragedy or in comedy, whether to suffer or to be merry, to laugh or to cry. But in real life it is different. Most men and women must play parts for which they are unsuited. Our Guildensterns play Hamlet for us, and our Hamlets must joke like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, but the play is poorly cast.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers entered the room. When he saw Lord Arthur he started, and his coarse, fat face turned a greenish yellow. The two men looked at each other, and for a moment there was silence.\\n\\n\u201cThe Duchess has left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and has asked me to bring it to her,\u201d said Mr. Podgers at last. \u201cAh, I see it on the sofa! Good evening.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Podgers, I insist on a straightforward answer to a question I am going to ask you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnother time, Lord Arthur, but the Duchess is waiting. I\u2019m afraid I must go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou shall not go. The Duchess is in no hurry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLadies