          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9cad8657980ac682d3af1042\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMen get married because they\u2019re exhausted; women do it out of curiosity, and both end up disappointed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I\u2019m likely to marry, Harry. I\u2019m too much in love. That\u2019s one of your aphorisms. I\u2019m applying it, just as I do everything you tell me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho are you in love with?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a pause.\\n\\n\u201cWith an actress,\u201d Dorian Gray said, blushing.\\n\\nLord Henry lifted his shoulders. \u201cThat\u2019s a rather ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t say that if you saw her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is she?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHer name is Sibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever heard of her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has. People will, though. She\u2019s a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are the decorative sex. They never have anything to say, but they say it beautifully. Women embody the triumph of matter over mind, just as men embody the triumph of mind over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it\u2019s perfectly true. I\u2019m analyzing women right now, so I should know. The subject isn\u2019t as complicated as I expected. I\u2019ve concluded there are only two kinds of women: plain and painted. Plain women are very useful. If you want a reputation for respectability, you only have to take them to supper. The others are very charming, but they make one mistake: they paint to look young. Our grandmothers painted to sound brilliant. Rouge and wit used to go together. That\u2019s finished now. As long as a woman can seem ten years younger than her own daughter, she\u2019s completely content. As for conversation, there are only five women in London worth talking to, and two of them can\u2019t be invited to decent gatherings. Anyway, tell me about your genius. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! Harry, your opinions frighten me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForget that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you come across her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you, Harry, but you mustn\u2019t be unsympathetic. After all, it never would have happened if I hadn\u2019t met you. You filled me with a wild desire to know everything about life. For days after meeting you, something seemed to pulse through my veins. When I lounged in the park or strolled down Piccadilly, I looked at everyone who passed and wondered, with a kind of mad curiosity, what lives they led. Some of them fascinated me. Others filled me with dread. There was a delicate poison in the air. I craved sensations...\u201d\"}"
