          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"314577e3400300898d257177\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while we\u2019d wake up and look around to see what made the noise, and maybe there\u2019d be a steamboat chugging upstream, way over by the far bank, so far away you couldn\u2019t tell anything about it except if it had a stern-wheel or a side-wheel. Then for about an hour there\u2019d be nothing to hear and nothing to see\u2014just thick loneness. Next a raft would slip by in the distance, and maybe some fellow on it would be chopping, because raft men are mostly at it. You\u2019d see the axe flash and swing down\u2014no sound yet; you\u2019d see the axe rise again, and only when it was above his head would the _k\u2019chunk!_ reach you\u2014it took that long to cross the water. That\u2019s how we spent the day, lazing around, listening to the quiet. Once a heavy fog rolled in, and the rafts and such rattled tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run over them. A scow or a raft went by so close we could hear them talking and cussing and laughing\u2014clear as could be\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see a sign of them. It gave you the crawls; sounded like spirits carrying on in the air. Jim said he figured they were spirits; but I says:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as night fell we shoved off; once we got her out near the middle we left her be and let the current take her wherever it pleased. Then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about everything\u2014we stayed naked day and night whenever the mosquitoes would let us. The new clothes Buck\u2019s family made me were too fine to feel easy in, and besides I never cared much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we\u2019d have that whole river to ourselves for ages. Over yonder were the banks and islands across the water, and maybe a spark in the distance\u2014which would be a candle in some cabin window; and sometimes you\u2019d catch a spark or two on the water\u2014on a raft or a scow, you know; and maybe a fiddle tune or a song would drift over from one of those crafts. It\u2019s grand living on a raft. We had the sky above, all dotted with stars, and we\u2019d lie on our backs, staring up, and argue about whether they were made or just happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they just happened; I reckoned it would\u2019ve taken too long to _make_ that many.\"}"
        }
