          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0da004a24345fe99410e62e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To examine a star in quick glances\u2014to look at it from the side by directing the outer parts of the retina toward it, those parts being more receptive to faint light than the center\u2014is to see the star clearly and truly appreciate its brilliance. That brilliance dims in proportion to how directly we look at it. More rays strike the eye when we stare straight at it, but the sidelong view has the finer capacity to grasp what we see. By being too intense we confuse and weaken our thoughts; we can even make Venus herself disappear from the sky by watching too long and too hard or too directly.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let\u2019s conduct an investigation ourselves before forming any opinion about them. An inquiry will amuse us\u201d\u2014I found that a strange choice of word but kept quiet\u2014\u201cand besides, Le Bon once did me a favor, and I\u2019m not ungrateful. We\u2019ll go and see the premises with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and I can easily obtain the necessary permission.\u201d\\n\\nHe did obtain it, and we went straight to the Rue Morgue. It is one of those wretched streets between Rue Richelieu and Rue Saint-Roch. We arrived late in the afternoon, since that part of the city is far from where we lived. The house was easy to find, as many people still stood across the street staring up at the closed shutters with pointless curiosity. It was an ordinary Parisian residence, with a gateway, and on one side a glassed-in watch box with a sliding window panel marking the concierge\u2019s lodge. Before entering we walked up the street, turned down an alley, and, turning again, passed behind the building\u2014Dupin all the while examining the entire neighborhood, as well as the house itself, with such painstaking attention that I couldn\u2019t see any purpose in it.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps to the front door, rang, and, after presenting our credentials, were admitted by the officers in charge. We went upstairs into the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, and where both bodies still lay. The disorder of the room had been left as it was. I noticed nothing beyond what had been reported in the Gazette des Tribunaux. Dupin inspected everything, including the bodies of the victims. Then we went through the other rooms and out into the yard, a gendarme accompanying us the entire time.\"}"
