          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d910b3b56de94d2f9804eea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those elderly, frail men were always seen by the public in the act of bowing, and people joked that after bowing a customer out, they kept bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in. The barrister was sharp enough to realize that the banker wouldn\u2019t speak so strongly without the kind of confidence that borders on certainty. Unprepared for the huge pill he had to swallow, he managed it. \u201cAnd now,\u201d Mr. Stryver said, wagging his forensic forefinger at the Temple at large once he had swallowed it, \u201cmy escape from this mess is to prove you all wrong.\u201d It was a trick from the repertoire of an Old Bailey tactician, and it always gave him great satisfaction. \u201cYou won\u2019t get the chance to make me look wrong, young lady,\u201d Mr. Stryver said; \u201cI\u2019ll take care of that myself.\u201d So when Mr. Lorry arrived that night at as late as ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver, with piles of books and papers strewn about for effect, acted as though nothing weighed on his mind less than the morning\u2019s subject. He even looked surprised to see Mr. Lorry, and appeared entirely distracted and preoccupied. \u201cWell!\u201d said the good-natured messenger after half an hour of fruitless attempts to steer him back to the subject. \u201cI\u2019ve been to Soho.\u201d \u201cTo Soho?\u201d Mr. Stryver echoed coolly. \u201cOh, of course! What am I thinking!\u201d \u201cAnd I\u2019m confident,\u201d Mr. Lorry said, \u201cthat my instincts during our conversation were correct. My view is confirmed, and I stand by my advice.\u201d \u201cBelieve me,\u201d Mr. Stryver replied with great friendliness, \u201cI regret it for your sake, and for the poor father\u2019s sake. I know it must be a painful topic for the family; let\u2019s drop it.\u201d \u201cI don\u2019t understand you,\u201d Mr. Lorry said. \u201cI\u2019m sure you don\u2019t,\u201d Stryver said, nodding in a smoothing, concluding way. \u201cNever mind, never mind.\u201d \u201cBut it does matter,\u201d Mr. Lorry insisted. \u201cNo it doesn\u2019t; I promise you it doesn\u2019t. I believed there was intelligence where there isn\u2019t, and noble ambition where there is none, and now I\u2019m relieved of my mistake, so no harm done. Young women have made such foolish choices many times before, and have regretted them in poverty and obscurity many times before. Unselfishly, I\u2019m sorry things ended, because it would have hurt me from a worldly perspective; selfishly, I\u2019m glad it ended, because it would have hurt me from a worldly perspective\u2014in other words, it\u2019s obvious there was nothing for me to gain.\u201d\"}"
