          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0125cf0d5a125755a00df20a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; all the tedious and unsatisfying correspondence with Elizabeth was left to Anne. Breakfast had barely finished when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them for their final walk around Lyme. They were supposed to leave for Uppercross by one o\u2019clock, and until then intended to stay together and outdoors as long as possible. As soon as they reached the street, Anne found Captain Benwick drawing near her. Their conversation the night before had not discouraged him, and they walked side by side for some time, once again discussing Mr. Scott and Lord Byron, still unable\u2014like any other two readers\u2014to agree completely on either\u2019s merits, until something caused a broad shift in the group and she suddenly had Captain Harville beside her instead. \u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said quietly, \u201cyou\u2019ve done a good deed by making that poor man talk so much. I wish he could have company like yours more often. It\u2019s bad for him, I know, to be shut up the way he is; but what can we do? We can\u2019t separate.\u201d \u201cNo,\u201d Anne replied, \u201cI can easily believe that to be impossible; but in time, perhaps\u2014we both know what time can do for every sorrow. Remember, Captain Harville, that your friend is still a young mourner\u2014only last summer, I\u2019ve heard.\u201d \u201cYes, indeed,\u201d he said with a deep sigh, \u201conly June.\u201d \u201cAnd he didn\u2019t learn of it right away, perhaps.\u201d \u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, newly placed on the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading news of him; he sent letters, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news had to reach him there, yet who could tell him? Not I. I\u2019d rather have been hauled up to the yardarm. No one could do it except that good man\u201d\u2014he pointed toward Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had reached Plymouth the week before; there was no danger she\u2019d be sent out again. He accepted the risk of the rest: requested leave, but without waiting for the answer, traveled day and night to Portsmouth, immediately rowed out to the Grappler, and stayed with our poor friend for a week. That\u2019s what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You can imagine, Miss Elliot, how dear he is to us!\u201d Anne considered the question with perfect conviction and said as much as her feelings allowed and as much as Captain Harville could bear, for he was too moved to continue and, when he spoke again, the subject was entirely different.\"}"
