          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"db8ac683d25bcc037c91e68e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond paced the room in anger. I stood ready to take whatever blame he threw at me. Adrian leaned against the wall. \u201cThis is absurd,\u201d he said. \u201cEven schoolboys couldn\u2019t behave more irrationally.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t understand,\u201d Raymond answered. \u201cThis is part of a whole system\u2014a plan of tyranny I will never accept. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the only slave in the realm? My privacy invaded, my actions judged, my friends insulted? I\u2019ll end it all at once. Bear witness.\u201d He removed the star that marked his office from his chest and tossed it onto the table. \u201cI renounce my office. I give up my power. Let anyone else take it!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet whoever claims it,\u201d cried Adrian, \u201cprove himself your superior, or be judged so by the world. No such man exists in England. Know yourself, Raymond, and the anger will fade and your calm return. Only months ago, whenever we prayed for the prosperity of our country and ourselves, we also prayed for the Protector\u2019s life, so tightly were our fortunes tied to yours. Your time was devoted to our welfare; your ambition was our praise. You adorned our towns with buildings, founded institutions, and made the land fertile. The powerful and unjust trembled before your tribunal, and the poor and oppressed rose like dawn-lit flowers under your protection.\\n\\n\u201cHow can you wonder that we are shocked and mourning when all this seems lost? But no, this bitter mood has already passed. Take up your duties again; your supporters will cheer you, your enemies will be quiet, and our love, respect, and loyalty will once more surround you. Control yourself, Raymond, and the world will yield.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this would make sense if you were addressing someone else,\u201d Raymond muttered. \u201cLearn the lesson yourself, and you, the first noble in the land, may become its sovereign. You, the good and wise and just, could rule every heart. But I have learned\u2014too soon for my own peace, too late for England\u2014that I took on a task beyond me. I cannot rule myself. My passions govern me; even my slightest impulse is a tyrant. Do you think I gave up the Protectorate\u2014yes, I have\u2014during a moment of sulkiness? By the living God, I swear I will never take up that trinket again. I will never again load myself with the weight of care and misery signified by that insignia.\u201d\"}"
