          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b68e89e136c01735e78911ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Country folk, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and vagabonds of every low sort merged into one mass; the whistling drovers, barking dogs, the bellowing and lunging oxen, the bleating sheep, the pigs grunting and squealing, the hawkers\u2019 calls, the shouts, curses, and fights on every side; the bells ringing and voices roaring out of every public house; the jostling, shoving, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling; the hideous, clashing noise echoing from every corner of the market; and the unwashed, unshaven, filthy, ragged figures constantly rushing in and out of the throng made it a deafening, dizzying scene that overwhelmed the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, hauling Oliver after him, shouldered his way through the thickest part of the crowd and paid very little attention to the countless sights and sounds that amazed the boy. He nodded a few times to passing friends, and, turning down the invitations to take an early drink, pushed straight ahead until they had left the uproar behind and made their way through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, youngster!\u201d said Sikes, glancing up at St. Andrew\u2019s Church clock, \u201cjust about seven! Step it up. Come on, don\u2019t start lagging already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nSikes jerked his young companion\u2019s wrist as he spoke. Oliver picked up his pace into a hesitant trot, somewhere between a fast walk and a run, and did his best to match the burglar\u2019s long strides.\\n\\nThey kept up this pace until they passed Hyde Park Corner and were heading for Kensington. Then Sikes eased off until an empty cart some distance behind caught up. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d painted on it, he asked the driver, as politely as he could manage, for a ride as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cHop in,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, he\u2019s mine,\u201d Sikes answered, staring hard at Oliver and slipping his hand into the pocket where the pistol was.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a bit too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, lad?\u201d the driver asked, noticing Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, hold my hand, Ned. Up you go!\u201d\\n\\nWith that, he helped Oliver into the cart. The driver pointed to a pile of sacks and told him to lie down there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed each mile marker, Oliver grew more and more puzzled about where his companion planned to take him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, Brentford\u2014all slipped by, yet they kept going just as steadily as if the journey had only begun.\"}"
