          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ccc405825c3059cd023fc172\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Back in my era, in my own land, the revenue was raised through imposts, and the citizen liked to imagine the foreign importer footed the bill, which comforted him; yet the truth was, the American people themselves paid it, shared so evenly that the yearly cost to the hundred-millionaire and to the day laborer\u2019s nursing infant was identical\u2014each shelled out six dollars. Nothing could be more equal than that, I suppose. Now, Scotland and Ireland paid tribute to Arthur, and the combined population of the British Isles totaled just under a million. A mechanic\u2019s daily wage was three cents when he had to meet his own living expenses. By that reckoning, the national government\u2019s budget came to ninety thousand dollars a year, or roughly two hundred and fifty a day. So by replacing gold with nickels during a king\u2019s-evil ceremony, I harmed nobody, displeased nobody, delighted everyone involved, and trimmed four-fifths off that day\u2019s government expenditure\u2014saving as much as eight hundred thousand dollars would have been in my American times. For that substitution I tapped wisdom from a very distant source\u2014the wisdom of my boyhood\u2014for the genuine statesman scorns no wisdom, however humble its origin: as a youngster, I always hoarded my pennies and donated buttons to the foreign missions. The buttons suited the ignorant savage as well as coins did, the coins suited me better than buttons; everybody was pleased, and nobody suffered.\\n\\nMarinel handled the sufferers one by one. He examined each applicant; if the fellow couldn\u2019t meet the standard he was sent away; if he could, he was passed on to the king. A priest intoned, \u201cThey shall lay their hands on the sick, and they shall recover.\u201d Then the king stroked the sores while the reading went on; at last, the patient graduated, received his nickel\u2014with the king slipping it on a cord about his neck\u2014and was dismissed. Would you suppose that would heal? It most certainly did. Any ritual will cure, provided the patient\u2019s faith in it is firm. Near Astolat stood a chapel where the Virgin had once appeared to a girl who pastured geese nearby\u2014the girl swore it herself\u2014and they raised the chapel there and hung a painting depicting the miracle\u2014a picture you\u2019d think dangerous for a sick person to approach; yet, on the contrary, thousands of the lame and the ill came annually, prayed before it, and departed hale and sound; and even the healthy could gaze upon it and live.\"}"
