          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"33055f131a9cf4fc56348d63\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental traits we describe as analytical are themselves difficult to dissect. We recognize them only through their results. What we do know, among other things, is that for someone who possesses them to excess, they become a source of intense enjoyment. Just as a strong man rejoices in his physical prowess, taking delight in exercises that set his muscles in motion, the analyst glories in the moral activity that untangles. He takes pleasure even in the smallest task that lets him exercise his talent. He loves riddles, puzzles, hieroglyphs, showing in each solution a level of sharpness that seems uncanny to the ordinary mind. His results, born from pure method, seem to be intuition itself.\\n\\nThe ability to solve things may be greatly strengthened by studying mathematics, especially that highest branch which, unfairly and merely because of its backward workings, has been called analysis, as if it deserves the title above all others. Yet to calculate is not the same as to analyze. A chess player, for example, calculates without needing to analyze. It follows that the influence of chess on mental character is greatly misunderstood. I am not writing a treatise now, but only prefacing a somewhat peculiar story with a few random observations; so I\u2019ll take the opportunity to say that the higher powers of the reflective mind are more thoroughly and more usefully exercised by the modest game of checkers than by all the elaborate fuss of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different, odd ways and have various values, complexity is mistaken (a common error) for depth. Attention is taxed to the utmost. If it lapses for a moment, an oversight occurs that leads to harm or defeat. Since the possible moves are not just numerous but intertwined, the chances of such mistakes multiply; in nine cases out of ten it is the more focused rather than the more perceptive player who wins. In checkers, on the other hand, where the moves are uniform and have little variation, the likelihood of a slip is reduced, and because attention is far less occupied, any advantage gained by either player comes from superior sharpness. To be less abstract, imagine a game of checkers reduced to four kings, where, of course, no blunder is expected. It\u2019s obvious that victory here (assuming both players are equally matched) can be decided only by some exquisite move, the result of a strong exertion of intellect.\"}"
