          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"711ab0b4eee965b2aae0b073\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The stupor and shock caused by this sudden change in my situation were, perhaps, the parts of the adventure hardest to explain. The upheaval itself was natural and bound to happen, and I had long expected it once I reached that exact point in my voyage where the planet\u2019s pull would give way to the satellite\u2019s\u2014in other words, where the balloon would feel less gravity from the earth than from the moon. Still, I had been fast asleep and woke with my senses in disorder to face a very startling sight, one I knew would come but not at that precise instant. The shift must have been smooth and gradual, and it is far from certain that, even if I had been awake, I would have felt any sign of upside-down change\u2014no discomfort, no disturbance of my body or equipment.\\n\\nIt almost goes without saying that once I truly grasped my situation and shook off the terror that had taken hold of my soul, my first thoughts turned entirely to the moon\u2019s general appearance. It stretched beneath me like a map, and though I believed it was still some distance away, the grooves and markings on its surface stood out to me with astonishing and inexplicable clarity. What struck me first was the total absence of ocean, sea, lake, river\u2014any water at all\u2014which seemed the strangest part of its geology. Yet, oddly enough, I saw vast level plains that looked distinctly alluvial, even though most of the visible hemisphere was packed with countless volcanic mountains, cone-shaped and more like artificial bulges than natural ones. The tallest rose no more than three and three-quarter miles straight up, but a map of the Campi Phlegraei volcanic regions would give your Excellencies a better idea of their layout than any weak description I could offer. Most of the mountains were clearly erupting, and they made me acutely aware of their rage and force through the repeated thunder of the so-called meteoric stones that now flew upward past the balloon with increasingly frightening speed.\"}"
