          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ee972bdfc14e60d41c557872\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Otis was greatly troubled by Lord Canterville\u2019s refusal and urged him to reconsider, but the genial peer remained resolute and ultimately persuaded the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift bestowed by the ghost. When, in the spring of 1890, the young Duchess of Cheshire appeared at the Queen\u2019s first drawing-room following her wedding, her jewels drew universal admiration. For Virginia was given the coronet\u2014the due of all exemplary little American girls\u2014and married her boy-lover as soon as he reached his majority. They were both so attractive and so devoted that everyone rejoiced in the match, save for the old Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had hoped to secure the Duke for one of her seven unmarried daughters and had even held three costly dinner-parties to snare him, and, oddly enough, Mr. Otis himself. Though Mr. Otis was very fond of the young Duke personally, in principle he disapproved of titles, confessing that he feared \u2018lest the true principles of republican simplicity might be lost amid the enervating influences of a pleasure-loving aristocracy.\u2019 His protests, however, were entirely overborne, and I daresay that, as he walked up the aisle of St. George\u2019s, Hanover Square, with his daughter on his arm, there was not a prouder man anywhere in England.\\n\\nAfter their honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess went down to Canterville Chase, and the next afternoon strolled to the lonely churchyard beside the pine-woods. Much difficulty had arisen at first regarding the inscription on Sir Simon\u2019s tomb, but it was finally settled to mark it simply with the old gentleman\u2019s initials and the verse from the library window. The Duchess had brought some exquisite roses, which she scattered upon the grave, and after they had lingered there awhile they wandered into the crumbling chancel of the ancient abbey. There the Duchess seated herself on 