          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82495d068e78b9bb09469755\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day the ghost felt very weak and tired. The terrible excitement of the last four weeks was finally catching up with him. His nerves were completely shattered, and he jumped at the slightest noise. For five days he stayed in his room and at last decided to give up on the bloodstain on the library floor. If the Otis family didn\u2019t want it, they clearly didn\u2019t deserve it. They were obviously people stuck on a low, material level of existence and unable to appreciate the symbolic value of sensory phenomena. The matter of ghostly apparitions and the growth of astral bodies was, of course, quite different and not really under his control. It was his solemn duty to appear in the corridor once a week and to gibber from the large oriel window on the first and third Wednesdays of every month, and he did not see how he could honorably escape these obligations. It was true his life had been very wicked, but on the other hand he was extremely conscientious in every matter relating to the supernatural. So for the next three Saturdays he went down the corridor as usual between midnight and three o\u2019clock, taking every possible precaution to avoid being heard or seen. He took off his boots, stepped as lightly as he could on the old worm-eaten boards, wore a huge black velvet cloak, and carefully used the Rising Sun Lubricator to oil his chains. I must admit it took a good deal of effort for him to consider this last form of protection. However, one night while the family were at dinner, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and carried off the bottle. He felt a little humiliated at first, but then was sensible enough to realize that much could be said in favor of the invention, and to a certain degree it served his purpose. Still, despite all his efforts, he was not left in peace. Strings were constantly stretched across the corridor, and he tripped over them in the dark. Once, while dressed for the role of \u201cBlack Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u201d he slipped badly after stepping onto a butter-slide the twins had built from the entrance of the Tapestry Chamber to the top of the oak staircase. This last insult enraged him so much that he resolved to make one final attempt to assert his dignity and social standing. He decided to visit the insolent young Etonians the following night in his famous character of \u201cReckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u201d\\n\\nHe had not appeared in this disguise for more than seventy years, not since he had frightened the charming Lady Barbara Modish so much with it that she immediately broke off her engagement to the current Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather and ran away to Gretna Green with the handsome Jack Castleton, saying nothing on earth would make her marry into a family that allowed such a horrible phantom to pace up and down the terrace at twilight.\"}"
        }
