          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"947c540ccb66857475b0ee4d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d said I, handing back the slip, \u201cI am as lost as before. Even if the treasure of Golconda lay waiting upon my solution of this riddle, I am convinced I could never win it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet,\u201d Legrand replied, \u201cthe answer is far less formidable than one might conclude from a hurried glance at the symbols. These marks, as any person could quickly suspect, compose a cipher\u2014in other words, they contain a meaning. Still, judging from what is known of Kidd, I could not imagine him equal to devising any very intricate cryptograph. I concluded, from the start, that it was of a simple kind\u2014one which, to the rough mind of a mariner, would appear utterly hopeless without a key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually unriddled it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWith ease; I have unraveled others ten thousand times more perplexing. Circumstances, along with a certain inclination of mind, have led me to take interest in such puzzles, and it is doubtful that human ingenuity can frame a cipher beyond the reach of human wit, properly applied. Once the characters were connected and legible, I scarcely gave a thought to the difficulty of interpreting them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn this instance\u2014indeed, in all cases of secret writing\u2014the first inquiry concerns the language of the cipher; for the methods of solution, particularly for the simpler kinds, depend upon, and vary with, the nature of the idiom. Generally, nothing remains but trial, guided by probability, of every tongue known to the solver, until the right one is hit upon. Yet, with the cipher before us, every difficulty vanished with the signature. The pun upon the name \u2018Kidd\u2019 can be appreciated in no language except English. Without that clue, I should have begun with Spanish and French, being the languages in which a pirate of the Spanish Main would most likely hide such a secret. As it turned out, I deemed the cryptograph English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou notice there are no spaces between words. Had there been, the task would have proved comparatively simple. I would then have started by collating and analyzing the shorter words, and, had a single-letter word appeared\u2014as is very likely\u2014(a or I, for instance,) I should have felt the solution assured. But lacking divisions, my first move was to determine the most common letters, and those least often appearing.\u201d\"}"
