          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"671603d258373ecadfb5cd79\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Though his character and disposition differed from hers, they would have satisfied all her hopes. Such a marriage must have proved advantageous to both: her lightness and vivacity might have softened his temper and refined his manners; while his discernment, experience, and worldly knowledge could have bestowed on her a more substantial good.\\n\\nBut the admiring public was not to be instructed by such an example of true marital felicity. Another alliance, incompatible with the former and of a very different nature, was on the point of taking place in their family.\\n\\nShe could not conceive how Wickham and Lydia would ever support themselves with anything like comfort. Yet she could readily predict how little real happiness could fall to a pair united only because desire had overpowered principle.\\n\\nMr. Gardiner soon wrote again to his brother. To Mr. Bennet\u2019s thanks he replied briefly, assuring him how eager he always felt to promote any member of his family; and he ended with entreating that the topic never be mentioned to him again. The chief aim of his letter was to notify them that Mr. Wickham had resolved to resign his post in the militia.\\n\\n\\\"It has long been my wish that he should do so,\\\" he continued, \\\"as soon as his marriage was determined. I trust you will agree with me that his removal from that corps is highly advisable both for him and my niece. Mr. Wickham intends entering the Regulars, and he still counts among his former companions some who are able and willing to aid his advancement. He has been promised an ensigncy in General ----'s regiment, now stationed in the north. It is advantageous that it should be so distant from this district. He gives reasonable assurances; and I hope that among different associates, where each must maintain a reputation, they will both show greater prudence. I have written to Colonel Forster to acquaint him with our arrangements and to request that he satisfy Mr. Wickham\u2019s various creditors in and around Brighton with promises of prompt payment, for which I have pledged myself. And will you undertake the trouble of giving the same assurances to his creditors in Meryton, whose names I shall attach according to his report? He has set down all his debts; I trust he has not misled us. Haggerston has our orders, and everything will be settled within a week. They will then join his regiment, unless they are first invited to Longbourn; and Mrs. Gardiner tells me my niece is ardently desirous of seeing you all before she leaves the south.\\\"\"}"
