          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ff4a0d0a006ac8f14479f27\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If not for me, he never would have been released. Morgan le Fay hated him with all her heart, and she never would have softened toward him. Still, his offense came from carelessness rather than true wickedness. He had said she had red hair. Well, she did, but that was no way to describe it. When red-haired people belong to a higher social class, their hair is called auburn.\\n\\nThink about this: among these forty-seven prisoners, there were five whose names, offenses, and imprisonment dates were no longer known. One woman and four men, all bent, wrinkled, and mentally wasted elders. They themselves had forgotten these facts long ago; at best they had vague guesses about them, nothing clear and nothing they would tell the same way twice. The line of priests whose duty it had been to pray daily with the prisoners and remind them that God had placed them there for some wise purpose, and to teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were what He loved to see in people of lower status, had traditions about these poor ruins of humanity, but nothing else. These stories went only so far, describing the length of their imprisonment but not the offenses. Even with tradition, the only thing certain was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer they had been kept in darkness could not even be guessed. The king and queen knew nothing about these poor creatures except that they were heirlooms, assets inherited, along with the throne, from the previous rulers. Nothing of their history had been passed along with their bodies, so the inheriting owners considered them worthless and felt no interest in them. I asked the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why in the world didn\u2019t you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question stumped her. She didn\u2019t know why she hadn\u2019t; the idea had never even entered her mind. So there she was, unknowingly predicting the real history of future prisoners of the Castle d\u2019If. It now seemed obvious to me that, with her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were simply property\u2014nothing more, nothing less. When we inherit property, it doesn\u2019t occur to us to throw it away, even when we don\u2019t value it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats into the open world and the glare of the afternoon sun\u2014first blindfolding them, out of kindness to eyes unused to light\u2014they were a striking sight. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pathetic horrors, every one of them; the truest possible children of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
        }
      ],
