          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bde0df7ba5bfe6ea834821cb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would be settled among those who loved her and had more sense than she did; secluded enough to be safe, yet busy enough to stay cheerful. She would never be drawn into temptation or left exposed to it. She would be respectable and content; and Emma admitted she was the luckiest creature alive to have inspired such steady, persistent affection in such a man\u2014or, if not quite the luckiest, then second only to herself.\\n\\nHarriet, inevitably occupied with the Martins, spent less and less time at Hartfield; which was not to be lamented. The closeness between her and Emma had to fade; their friendship had to change into a calmer goodwill; and luckily, what should happen and must happen seemed already beginning, in the most gradual, natural way.\\n\\nBefore September ended, Emma accompanied Harriet to church and saw her hand placed in Robert Martin\u2019s with such complete satisfaction that no memories, not even of Mr. Elton standing before them, could lessen it. Perhaps, indeed, at that moment she barely saw Mr. Elton except as the clergyman whose blessing at the altar might next fall on herself. Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, the last of the three couples to become engaged, were the first to marry.\\n\\nJane Fairfax had already left Highbury and returned to the comforts of her beloved home with the Campbells. The Mr. Churchills were also in town, waiting only for November.\\n\\nThe month between was the one Emma and Mr. Knightley chose, as far as they dared. They had decided their wedding should take place while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, so they could enjoy a fortnight\u2019s trip to the seaside, which was the plan. John and Isabella, and every other friend, agreed it was wise. But Mr. Woodhouse\u2014how could he be persuaded to consent? He had never mentioned their marriage except as something far off.\\n\\nWhen the subject was first raised, he was so miserable that they nearly lost hope. A second mention, though, caused less pain. He began to think it would happen and that he could not stop it\u2014an encouraging step toward acceptance. Still, he was not content. In fact, he seemed so unhappy that his daughter\u2019s courage faltered. She could not bear to see him suffer, to know he imagined himself neglected; and although her reason nearly accepted the assurance from both Mr. Knightleys that once the event was over his distress would soon fade, she hesitated\u2014she could not move forward.\"}"
