          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46d17a25ae7a43328cb2f47f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made sure she understood that Frank entertained the highest admiration of her\u2014considered her exceedingly lovely and altogether delightful; and with so much speaking in his favour, she felt she ought not to condemn him too severely. As Mrs. Weston remarked, \u201call young people will have their little fancies.\u201d\\n\\nAmong his new Surry acquaintances, there was one individual who showed far less indulgence. Generally, throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury, he was regarded with much fairness; generous allowances were granted for the small irregularities of so handsome a youth\u2014one who smiled so readily and bowed so gracefully. Yet there was one spirit not to be appeased, despite all the smiles and bows\u2014Mr. Knightley. The story was conveyed to him at Hartfield; he made no comment at first; but Emma soon heard him mutter to himself over the newspaper he held, \u201cHum! precisely the frivolous, foolish fellow I always imagined.\u201d She was nearly affronted; still, a moment\u2019s study convinced her he had spoken merely to ease his own feelings, not to provoke; thus she let it go.\\n\\nThough Mr. and Mrs. Weston brought unwelcome news in one regard, their visit that morning proved especially well-timed in another. Something occurred while they were at Hartfield that made Emma wish for their counsel; and, fortunately, it was exactly the counsel they offered.\\n\\nThis was the occurrence: the Coles had been settled in Highbury for several years, and were excellent people\u2014amiable, generous, and unassuming; but on the other hand, they were of humble stock, in trade, and only moderately genteel. When they first came into the neighbourhood, they lived within their means, quietly, entertaining little, and that little inexpensively; but within the last year or two, they had gained a substantial increase of fortune\u2014the house in London had yielded greater profits, and luck in general had favoured them. With their growing wealth, their ambitions expanded; they felt the want of a larger house, and a desire for more company. They enlarged their residence, increased their servants, and heightened every expense; by now, in fortune and manner of living, they stood second only to the family at Hartfield. Their delight in society and their new dining-room led everyone to expect they would host dinner parties; and a few gatherings, chiefly of single gentlemen, had already occurred. As for inviting the more established and best families, Emma hardly believed they would dare\u2014neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing could induce her to accept, even if they tried; and she lamented that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal carry less significance than she desired.\"}"
