{"request_id":"8b8b0fc9be67276c4aba8f99","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not annihilated by specters. The perpetrators were corporeal and made a tangible escape. By what means? Happily, only one line of reasoning lies open to us, and that line must bring us to a firm conclusion. Let us inspect, one by one, the possible outlets. It is evident that the killers were either in the chamber where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was discovered, or else in the adjoining room, when the rescuers climbed the stairs. We must search for exits only from these two quarters. The authorities stripped the floors, ceilings, and the brickwork of the walls in every direction. No hidden openings could have slipped their scrutiny. Still, unwilling to accept merely their eyesight, I made my own inspection. There were no secret passages. Both doors leading from the rooms into the corridor were fastened on the inside, and the keys lay within. Let us examine the chimneys. Although these were of common breadth for approximately eight or ten feet above the hearths, they would not admit, throughout their height, the body of even a large cat. Since escape by the paths already reviewed is absolutely impossible, we are forced back upon the windows. Through the front chamber’s windows, no one could have fled without being seen by the crowd in the street. The criminals must, therefore, have gone out by those of the rear room. Having been driven to this conclusion so unequivocally, we have no right, as reasoners, to dismiss it because it seems impossible. All that remains is to demonstrate that these seeming impossibilities are not such in truth.

“There are two windows in the chamber. One of them is unobstructed by furniture and wholly in view. The lower part of the other is concealed by the headboard of the ponderous bedstead thrust close against it. The first was discovered securely fastened inside. It resisted every bit of strength brought to bear by those attempting to lift it. A wide gimlet-hole was bored in the frame at the left, and an unusually stout nail was fitted in it nearly up to the head. When the second window was inspected, an identical nail was fitted in the same fashion; and a vigorous attempt to raise this sash failed likewise. The police were completely convinced that no one escaped by these avenues. Therefore, they thought it needless to remove the nails and open the windows.

“My own inspection went into finer detail, for precisely the reason already stated—because it was here, I knew, that all seeming impossibilities must be shown to be nothing of the sort.”}"}

