{"request_id":"129bf84e6801ea1d7ef6aa90","rewritten_text":"What sort of existence would be theirs while Fate still weighed this dreadful destiny? The wedding had to be deferred, no matter what. On this point he was utterly decided. Intensely as he adored the girl, and though the mere brush of her fingers when they sat side by side sent every nerve in him tingling with delight, he nevertheless perceived quite plainly where his duty lay, and fully realised that he held no right to wed until the murder was accomplished. Once that was done, he could stand beside Sybil Merton at the altar and surrender his life into her care without fear of wrongdoing. Once that was done, he could clasp her to him, secure in the knowledge that she need never blush for him nor droop her head in disgrace. Yet it had to be done beforehand, and the earlier the better for them both.

Many in his situation would have chosen the primrose way of dalliance rather than the steep ascent of obligation; but Lord Arthur was too scrupulous to prefer indulgence to principle. His affection held more than mere passion, and Sybil represented to him everything pure and noble. For a moment he felt a natural loathing for what was demanded of him, but that soon evaporated. His heart assured him it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him no other path remained. He faced a decision between living for himself and living for others, and however dreadful the task imposed upon him might be, he knew he must not let selfishness conquer love. Sooner or later all of us confront the same problem—the same question is posed to everyone. To Lord Arthur it arrived early—before his nature had been tainted by the calculating cynicism of middle age or his heart eaten away by the shallow, modish egotism of the age—and he felt no reluctance in doing his duty. Fortunately for him, he was no mere dreamer or idle dabbler. Had he been, he would have wavered, like Hamlet, and let vacillation destroy his intent. But he was practical at core. Life, for him, meant deed rather than thought. He possessed that rarest quality, common sense.

The wild, troubled emotions of the previous night had by now entirely ebbed, and he looked back on his frenzied wanderings from street to street, on his burning torment, almost with embarrassment. His very sincerity made those sufferings seem unreal to him now. He wondered how he could ever have been so foolish as to rant and rage against the unavoidable."}

