          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ab508893988d033687f831a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday nights in our village had a pleasant sense of tidying up and pausing quietly before starting life anew, and that feeling pushed Joe to stay out half an hour longer than usual. As that extra half hour and his rum and water ran out together, Joe stood up to leave and took my hand. \u201cHold on a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I have a shiny new shilling somewhere, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d He dug through a handful of small coins, wrapped the shilling in crumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cRemember, it\u2019s yours.\u201d I thanked him and stared far beyond what was polite, clinging to Joe. He wished Joe good night, he wished Mr. Wopsle good night (who left with us), and he gave me only a glance with his aiming eye\u2014no, not even a glance, because he closed it, yet you can do wonders with an eye by hiding it. On the way home, if I had been in the mood to talk, I would have had to do it all myself. Mr. Wopsle parted from us at the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe kept his mouth wide open all the way home to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was too stunned by this reminder of my old wrong and my old acquaintance, and I could think of nothing else. My sister was not in a very bad temper when we came into the kitchen, and Joe felt bold enough, that rare mood of hers, to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll be bound,\u201d Mrs. Joe said with triumph. \u201cOtherwise he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d I took it out of the paper, and it turned out to be good. \u201cBut what is this?\u201d Mrs. Joe cried, dropping the shilling and grabbing the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d Nothing less than two thick, sweaty one-pound notes that seemed to have kept close company with every cattle market in the county. Joe snatched up his hat again and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, sure the man would be gone. Soon Joe came back, saying the man had indeed disappeared, but that he had left a message at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
