          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8471ebabf9b97c06a9fff36a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 19th. Early this morning, around nine o\u2019clock, when the moon\u2019s surface loomed dangerously near and my anxiety was at its height, the pump of my condenser finally showed unmistakable signs that the atmosphere had changed. By ten I had good grounds to think the air had grown considerably denser. By eleven the apparatus required very little effort; and at noon, after some hesitation, I risked unscrewing the tourniquet. Finding no ill effects, I threw open the gum-elastic chamber and detached it from the car. As was predictable, cramps and a fierce headache immediately followed that rash, perilous experiment. However, since these and other breathing difficulties were not severe enough to endanger my life, I resolved to endure them as best I might, trusting that I was rapidly leaving them behind in my swift approach to the denser layers near the moon. Still, the descent remained violently rapid; and it soon became alarmingly evident that, while I had likely not erred in expecting an atmosphere dense in proportion to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had erred in assuming its density, even at the surface, would suffice to uphold the heavy load in my balloon\u2019s car. It ought to have done so, and to the same degree it does at Earth\u2019s surface, if we suppose true the ratio of gravity to atmospheric condensation on either world. That it failed was demonstrated by my headlong fall; the cause, I can only attribute to those possible geological upheavals previously mentioned. In any case, I was now almost upon the planet, plunging with dreadful speed. Not wasting an instant, I cast overboard first my ballast, then the water-kegs, then the condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every item inside the car. All in vain. I still plunged with ghastly velocity, now less than half a mile above the surface. As a last resort, after shedding coat, hat, and boots, I severed the car itself\u2014which was quite heavy\u2014from the balloon, and clung with both hands to the net-work. I scarcely had time to note that the entire landscape, as far as I could see, was thickly dotted with tiny dwellings before I pitched headlong into the heart of a bizarre-looking city and into the midst of a vast crowd of ugly little beings. Not one uttered a word or made the slightest move to help me; instead they stood like a troop of idiots, grinning absurdly, arms akimbo, and eyeing me and my balloon with sidelong glances.\"}"
