{"request_id":"19014bb836f0c910cfb4e363","rewritten_text":"Far down the way the line of flickering gas lamps curved, and outside a small walled house a lone hansom waited, the driver asleep inside. He hurried toward Portland Place, glancing back now and then as if afraid he was being followed. At the corner of Rich Street two men stood reading a small poster on a hoarding. An odd curiosity stirred him, and he crossed over. As he drew near, the word “Murder,” printed in bold black letters, caught his eye. He flinched, and a deep flush rose to his cheeks. It was an advertisement offering a reward for information leading to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, wearing a bowler hat, a black coat, checkered trousers, and bearing a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering if the unfortunate man would be caught and how he had been scarred. Perhaps one day his own name might be plastered on London’s walls. Perhaps someday a price would be set on his head too.

The thought sickened him with horror. He swung around and hurried off into the night.

He hardly knew where he went. He dimly remembered drifting through a maze of grimy houses, trapped in a giant web of dark streets, and it was bright dawn when he finally found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he wandered home toward Belgrave Square, he passed the big wagons heading to Covent Garden. The carters in their white smocks, with pleasant sunburned faces and rough curly hair, strode along, snapping their whips and calling to each other now and then; on the back of a huge gray horse, leading a jangling team, sat a chubby boy with a bunch of primroses stuck in his battered hat, clutching the mane with his little hands and laughing; the towering heaps of vegetables looked like masses of jade against the morning sky, like green stone set against the pink petals of some miraculous rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, though he could not say why. Something in the tender beauty of dawn struck him as unspeakably sad, and he thought of how many days break in splendor and end in storm. These countrymen, too, with their rough good-natured voices and casual ways—what a peculiar London they saw! A city free from the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale ghost-like place, a desolate town of tombs. He wondered what they made of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendor and its shame, its blazing, fiery pleasures and its terrible hunger, of all it creates and destroys from morning to night."}

