          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a3666d39a77e6ded0ad9b15\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou have not been in Bath long enough,\u201d he said, \u201cto enjoy the evening parties here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! no. They have nothing for me. I do not play cards.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou did not before, I know. You used to dislike cards; but time changes many things.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not yet so changed,\u201d Anne exclaimed, then stopped, worried about how her words might be taken. After a short pause he said, as if from sudden feeling, \u201cIt truly is a long time. Eight years and a half is a long time.\u201d\\n\\nWhether he meant to say more was left for Anne to imagine later in calmer moments; for while his words still echoed, she was interrupted by Henrietta, eager to use the spare time to go out, urging her companions to hurry before anyone else arrived.\\n\\nThey had to get ready. Anne talked as if she were perfectly willing, and tried to look so; but she felt that if Henrietta had known the regret and reluctance in her heart as she left that chair, as she prepared to leave the room, she would have felt, in all her own certainty about her cousin\u2019s affection, a reason to pity her.\\n\\nYet their preparations were suddenly stopped. Alarming sounds were heard; other visitors were arriving, and the door opened for Sir Walter and Miss Elliot, whose presence seemed to chill everyone. Anne felt an immediate heaviness, and wherever she looked she saw the same reaction. The warmth, ease, and cheerfulness of the room were over, replaced by stiff calm, forced silence, or flat conversation, to suit her father and sister\u2019s heartless elegance. How painful to feel it was so!\\n\\nHer jealous watchfulness was satisfied in one way. Captain Wentworth was acknowledged again by both, Elizabeth even more gracious than before. She spoke to him once, looked at him more than once. Elizabeth was, in fact, planning something important. Soon it became clear. After wasting a few minutes on polite nonsense, she began to give the invitation that was to cover all the remaining obligations to the Musgroves. \u201cTomorrow evening, to meet a few friends; nothing formal.\u201d It was all done very elegantly, and the cards she had ready, \u201cMiss Elliot at home,\u201d were laid on the table with a polite, inclusive smile to all, and one card and one smile more directly for Captain Wentworth. The truth was, Elizabeth had been in Bath long enough to understand the value of a man with his manner and appearance.\"}"
