          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a711f5a0f5e5f4d04771f35\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I uncovered the fire, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into flame. Seeing this, I made a fan from branches, which revived the embers when they were almost out. When night returned, I was pleased to find the fire gave light as well as heat, and that this discovery helped with my food. Some scraps the travelers had left were roasted, and they tasted far better than the berries I gathered from the trees. I tried to cook my food the same way, placing it on the live embers. The berries spoiled, but the nuts and roots were greatly improved.\\n\\nFood soon grew scarce, and I often spent the whole day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease my hunger. When this happened, I resolved to leave the place I had lived and look for somewhere my few needs could be more easily met. During this departure I deeply regretted losing the fire I had gained by accident, and I did not know how to make it again. I spent several hours seriously considering this problem, but I had to give up hope of finding a solution. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I walked through the woods toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days and finally reached open country. A heavy snowfall had fallen the night before, and the fields were one blank white; the scene was bleak, and my feet grew numb from the cold, wet ground.\\n\\nIt was about seven in the morning, and I was desperate for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a hill, likely built for a shepherd. This was a new sight to me, and I studied the structure with great curiosity. The door stood open, so I went in. An old man sat inside near a fire, cooking his breakfast. He turned at the sound, and when he saw me he shrieked loudly, left the hut, and ran across the fields faster than I thought possible for his frail body. His appearance, unlike anyone I had seen before, and his flight both surprised me. Yet I was delighted with the hut; snow and rain could not enter; the ground was dry; and it seemed to me as perfect and divine a refuge as Pand\u00e6monium appeared to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire.\"}"
