          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"53f2a893555e2fd74c1d3bb8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"PS.\u2014Do let me know about the bows. Jennings swears they are the latest thing.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s face grew so grave and woeful as he read the letter that the Duchess burst into fits of laughter.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Arthur,\u201d she exclaimed, \u201cI shall never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what reply am I to make about the clock? I consider it an admirable invention, and I should be delighted to own one myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t much care for them,\u201d Lord Arthur answered with a melancholy smile, and, after kissing his mother, he quitted the room.\\n\\nUpon reaching his chambers he threw himself on a sofa, and his eyes brimmed with tears. He had endeavoured with all his strength to commit this murder, yet twice he had failed, and through no fault of his own. He had tried to fulfil his duty, but it seemed Destiny herself had turned renegade. He felt oppressed by the sterility of good intentions, by the uselessness of striving to be noble. Perhaps it might be wisest to break off the marriage altogether. Sybil would suffer, admittedly, yet such suffering could hardly injure a nature so splendid as hers. As for him, what did it signify? There is always some war in which a man may fall, some cause to which he may devote his life, and since life held no delight for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny accomplish his fate; he would not raise a finger to aid her.\\n\\nAt half-past seven he dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and he was compelled to dine in their company. Their trifling chatter and empty witticisms left him bored, and once the coffee appeared he excused himself with an invented engagement to escape. As he was leaving the club, the hall-porter handed him a letter. It came from Herr Winckelkopf, requesting him to call next evening to inspect an explosive umbrella that discharged the moment it was opened. It was the newest contrivance, fresh from Geneva. He tore the letter into shreds. He had resolved to attempt no further experiments. Then he strolled down to the Thames Embankment and sat for hours beside the river. The moon looked through a mane of tawny clouds like the eye of a lion, and countless stars bespangled the hollow sky, like gold dust strewn upon a purple dome. Now and again a barge swung out upon the muddy current and drifted with the tide, while the railway signals shifted from green to crimson as the trains shrieked across the bridge.\"}"
