          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2c0452a0640bef78a699f0c7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis, at Lady Canterville\u2019s urgent request, had agreed to keep on. She gave them each a low curtsey as they stepped down and said in a quaint, old-fashioned tone, \u201cI bid you welcome to Canterville Chase.\u201d Following her, they walked through the grand Tudor hall into the library, a long, low room paneled in black oak, with a large stained-glass window at one end. Tea was laid out for them there, and after removing their wraps, they sat down and looked around while Mrs. Umney waited on them.\\n\\nSuddenly Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain on the floor near the fireplace and, not realizing what it meant, said to Mrs. Umney, \u201cI\u2019m afraid something has been spilled there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, madam,\u201d replied the old housekeeper quietly, \u201cblood has been spilled on that spot.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow awful,\u201d said Mrs. Otis. \u201cI do not like bloodstains in a sitting room. It must be removed at once.\u201d\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and, still speaking in that low, mysterious voice, said, \u201cIt is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, who was murdered on that very spot by her husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon lived nine more years and then vanished in very mysterious circumstances. His body has never been found, but his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. The bloodstain has been greatly admired by visitors and cannot be removed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is nonsense,\u201d cried Washington Otis. \u201cPinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will clean it up right away,\u201d and before the terrified housekeeper could stop him, he knelt down and began scrubbing the floor with a small stick that looked like a black cosmetic. In a few moments no trace of the bloodstain remained.\\n\\n\u201cI knew Pinkerton would do it,\u201d he said triumphantly, looking around at his admiring family, but as soon as he spoke a terrible flash of lightning lit the gloomy room, a violent peal of thunder made them all jump to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u201cWhat a monstrous climate!\u201d said the American Minister calmly, lighting a long cheroot. \u201cI suppose the old country is so crowded they don\u2019t have enough decent weather for everyone. I have always thought emigration is the only solution for England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Hiram,\u201d cried Mrs. Otis, \u201cwhat can we do with a woman who faints?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCharge it to her like breakages,\u201d answered the Minister. \u201cShe won\u2019t faint after that,\u201d and in a few moments Mrs. Umney indeed recovered.\"}"
