          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c4bc7314fe8e7375ee4de162\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the center of this fissure I noticed a pale speck, though at first I could not tell what it might be. After readjusting the telescope\u2019s focus, I looked once more and now discerned it to be a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cUpon this discovery I grew so confident that I considered the puzzle settled; for the phrase \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could point only to the skull\u2019s location upon the tree, and \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 permitted but a single meaning when it came to seeking buried treasure. I realized the plan was to drop a bullet from the skull\u2019s left eye, then draw a bee-line\u2014meaning a straight line\u2014from the nearest spot of the trunk through \u2018the shot\u2019 (the place where the bullet landed), and to extend that line for fifty feet. This would mark a fixed point\u2014and beneath that spot I thought it at least plausible that some valuable deposit might be hidden.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this,\u201d I observed, \u201cis extremely plain, and despite being clever, it remains straightforward and definite. After you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what occurred?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, having carefully noted the tree\u2019s bearings, I set off for home. The moment I stepped away from \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 though, the circular opening vanished; no matter how I turned, I never glimpsed it again. What strikes me as the principal ingenuity in the whole affair is the fact\u2014repeated trials assure me it is fact\u2014that the circular gap can be seen from no other accessible viewpoint than the narrow ledge on the rock\u2019s face.\\n\\n\u201cDuring this excursion to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel\u2019 I was accompanied by Jupiter, who, I have no doubt, had noticed my absent manner for some weeks past and took special care not to leave me unguarded. But the next morning I rose very early, managed to elude him, and went into the hills to search for the tree. After much labor I located it. When I returned that night my valet threatened to flog me. Of the remainder of the adventure I believe you know as much as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose,\u201d said I, \u201cyou missed the mark on the first digging attempt because Jupiter stupidly let the bug fall through the right instead of the left eye of the skull.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so. That error caused a difference of about two and a half inches in the \u2018shot\u2019\u2014meaning the peg nearest the tree; and had the treasure lain beneath the \u2018shot,\u2019 the mistake would have mattered little. But the \u2018shot\u2019 and the tree\u2019s nearest point served only to fix a line of direction; naturally, an error however slight at the start grew as we carried the line forward, and by the time we had reached fifty feet it had led us entirely astray.\u201d\"}"
