          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"44c8da1e704f73ba07e3dfad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d  \u201cA wire fence?\u201d  \u201cYes. You hinted at the idea yourself, two or three years ago.\u201d  \u201cOh, I remember\u2014when the Church first tested its strength against us, then decided to wait for a better season. So, how did you set up the fence?\u201d  \u201cI run twelve extremely strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014from a large dynamo in the cave, a dynamo with only a positive brush and a negative brush.\u201d  \u201cYes, that\u2019s correct.\u201d  \u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a circle of flat ground, a hundred yards across; they form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014meaning twelve concentric circles\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d  \u201cRight; continue.\u201d  \u201cThe fences are attached to heavy oak posts only three feet apart, and those posts are sunk five feet deep.\u201d  \u201cThat\u2019s sturdy.\u201d  \u201cYes. The wires have no ground connection outside the cave. They go out from the dynamo\u2019s positive brush; the negative brush is grounded; the other ends of the wires come back to the cave, and each one is grounded separately.\u201d  \u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t work!\u201d  \u201cWhy not?\u201d  \u201cToo expensive\u2014it wastes power. You need only one ground connection, through the negative brush. The other end of every wire has to be brought back into the cave and fastened separately, without any ground connection. Now note the efficiency. A cavalry charge storms the fence; you\u2019re using no power and spending no money, because there\u2019s only one ground connection until those horses hit the wire. The instant they touch it, they make contact with the negative brush through the ground and drop dead. Don\u2019t you see? You don\u2019t use energy until it\u2019s needed; your lightning is waiting there like a loaded gun, but it doesn\u2019t cost you a penny until you fire it. Ah yes, the single ground connection\u2014\u201d  \u201cOf course! I can\u2019t imagine how I missed that. It\u2019s cheaper and more effective than the other setup, because if wires snap or tangle, it doesn\u2019t matter.\u201d  \u201cNo, especially if we have a sensor in the cave to disconnect the broken wire. All right, proceed. The Gatlings?\u201d  \u201cYes\u2014that\u2019s taken care of. In the center of the innermost circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I\u2019ve arranged a battery of thirteen Gatling guns and stocked plenty of ammunition.\u201d  \u201cPerfect. They cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights show up, there\u2019ll be music. On the cliff edge above the cave\u2014\u201d  \u201cI\u2019ve put a wire fence there, too, and a Gatling gun.\u201d\"}"
