{"request_id":"1ca9eea7da0f159d669f5a84","rewritten_text":"We grew up side by side, less than a year apart. I hardly need to say we never quarreled. Harmony defined our friendship, and the differences in our personalities only drew us closer together. Elizabeth was calmer and more self-contained, while I, even with all my restless energy, could focus more intensely and was struck more deeply by the hunger for knowledge. She spent her time chasing the airy creations of the poets, and in the majestic, wondrous landscapes around our Swiss home—the grand outlines of the mountains, the shifting seasons, storms and calms, the quiet of winter, and the vibrant turbulence of our Alpine summers—she found endless room for admiration and delight. As my companion contemplated these magnificent sights with a serious, contented mind, I took joy in uncovering their causes. The world was a mystery I longed to decipher. Curiosity, the urgent drive to learn nature’s hidden laws, and a joy bordering on ecstasy whenever they were revealed to me, are among my earliest memories.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents gave up their roaming life completely and settled in their homeland. We owned a house in Geneva and a country place on Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, a little over a league from the city. We lived mostly at the latter, and my parents led lives of considerable seclusion. My temperament was to avoid crowds and devote myself intensely to a select few. I was indifferent to most of my classmates, but I formed a very close friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant, a boy of unusual talent and imagination. He loved adventure, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He read deeply in tales of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began many stories of enchantment and knightly quests. He urged us to act out plays and join in masquerades featuring the heroes of Roncesvalles, the knights of King Arthur’s Round Table, and the gallant company who spilled their blood to win back the Holy Sepulchre from the infidels.

No one could have enjoyed a happier childhood than mine. My parents embodied kindness and indulgence. We understood they were not tyrants imposing their whims on our fate, but the very source and creators of all the countless pleasures we enjoyed."}

