          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c6abf92fe12eef690013e13d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou won\u2019t, will you? Oh, you precious fool!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s naughty, isn\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d asked Charley Bates. \u201cHe\u2019ll end up strung up, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know what that means,\u201d Oliver answered.\\n\\n\u201cSomething like this, old fellow,\u201d said Charley. He grabbed the end of his neckerchief, held it straight up, dropped his head to his shoulder, and pushed a strangled sound through his teeth, giving a lively pantomime that made it plain that \u201cbeing scragged\u201d meant \u201cbeing hanged.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s what it means,\u201d said Charley. \u201cLook at him staring, Jack! I\u2019ve never seen such grand company as that boy; he\u2019ll kill me with laughter, I swear he will.\u201d Master Charley Bates, after another hearty laugh, picked up his pipe again, tears in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve been raised badly,\u201d said the Dodger, admiring his boots with great satisfaction once Oliver had finished polishing them. \u201cFagin will make something out of you, though, or you\u2019ll be the first one he\u2019s ever had that turned out useless. You\u2019d better start right away, because you\u2019ll take up the trade long before you imagine, and you\u2019re only wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates backed up this advice with various moral sayings of his own; and when those ran out, he and his friend Mr. Dawkins launched into a glowing account of the countless delights of the life they led, spiced with plenty of hints that the smartest thing Oliver could do was to win Fagin\u2019s favor without delay, using the same means they had used themselves.\\n\\n\u201cAnd always remember this, Nolly,\u201d said the Dodger, as they heard the Jew unlocking the upstairs door, \u201cif you don\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of saying it like that?\u201d interrupted Master Bates; \u201che doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take pocket handkerchiefs and watches,\u201d said the Dodger, lowering his speech to Oliver\u2019s level, \u201csome other chap will; so the folks who lose them will be just as bad off, and you\u2019ll be no better, and no one else will be a bit better either, except the fellows who grab them\u2014and you\u2019ve got just as much right to them as they do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course!\u201d said the Jew, who had slipped in without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt\u2019s all very simple, my dear; take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the catechism of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands with delight as he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s reasoning, and chuckled with pleasure at his pupil\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe talk went no farther then, because the Jew had returned with Miss Betsy and a gentleman Oliver had never seen before, whom the Dodger hailed as Tom Chitling. Tom, having lingered on the stairs to exchange a few gallant remarks with the lady, now stepped into the room.\"}"
