          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"089e281eafb41c9a808aa169\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Enough of that. I will begin my story: it is my final task, and I hope I still have the strength to complete it. I confess to no crimes; any faults of mine can easily be forgiven, for they arose not from malicious intent but from poor judgment; and I think few would claim that, with different choices and greater wisdom, they could have escaped the misfortunes that have claimed me. My fate has been ruled by necessity, a monstrous necessity. It would have taken hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger than any human power, I believe\u2014to shatter the thick, adamantine chain that bound me: once breathing only joy, filled with warm love and a delight in goodness, only to be consigned to misery that could end only in death, and is now about to end. But I forget myself; my tale remains untold. I will pause for a moment, wipe my dim eyes, and try to lose this vague but heavy sense of present unhappiness in the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank: he lost his own father early and was raised by a weak mother who gave him all the indulgence she thought due a wealthy nobleman. He was sent to Eton and later to university, and from childhood he was allowed free access to large sums of money, thus claiming from his earliest youth the independence that a boy with such advantages inevitably secures at a public school.\\n\\nUnder those conditions his passions found rich soil where they could take root and flourish, whether as flowers or as weeds, depending on their nature. Because he was always permitted to act for himself, his character quickly grew strong and distinct, displaying a varied surface on which any sharp-eyed observer might discern the seeds of both virtues and future misfortunes. His careless extravagance, which led him to squander vast sums to gratify fleeting whims\u2014whims he elevated to the status of passions because of their seeming intensity\u2014often manifested as boundless generosity. Yet even as he dedicated himself to meeting others\u2019 needs, his own desires were indulged to the fullest. He gave his money, but none of his own wishes were sacrificed to his gifts; he offered his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was glad to have stirred in any way.\\n\\nI do not claim that, had his desires been set against another\u2019s, he would necessarily have shown undue selfishness; but that test was never applied. He was nurtured in prosperity and surrounded by all its benefits; everyone loved him and sought to please him.\"}"
