{"request_id":"26dadf809e70ae508d042cce","rewritten_text":"By eight o’clock I had reached an elevation of seventeen miles above the earth. It was clear my rate of ascent was still increasing, and the change would have been noticeable even if I had not dropped any ballast. The pain in my head and ears returned in violent bursts, and I still bled now and then from the nose, yet overall I suffered less than I had expected. Breathing became harder with every moment, and each breath brought a troublesome spasm in my chest. I unpacked the condensing apparatus and prepared it for use. 

The view of the earth at this stage of my ascent was astonishing. To the west, north, and south stretched an endless surface of seemingly calm ocean, growing deeper blue by the moment and already taking on a slight curve. Far to the east, yet still distinct, lay the islands of Great Britain, the entire Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small strip of northern Africa. Not a single building could be seen, and the grandest cities of humankind had vanished from sight. From the rock of Gibraltar, now only a faint speck, the dark Mediterranean, studded with glittering islands like stars in the sky, extended to the east as far as I could see, until the waters seemed to plunge over the edge of the horizon. I listened, as if on tiptoe, for the echoes of that imagined waterfall. Overhead the sky was coal-black, and the stars shone brilliantly. 

By then the pigeons seemed to be suffering, so I decided to let them go. I untied one, a beautiful gray-mottled bird, and set him on the rim of the wicker frame. He looked very uneasy, glanced around anxiously, fluttered his wings, and made loud cooing sounds, yet he refused to leave the car. Finally I picked him up and tossed him about six yards from the balloon. Instead of descending as I expected, he struggled frantically to return, emitting sharp, piercing cries. He managed to reach the rim again, but almost at once his head fell onto his chest, and he died inside the car."}

