          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"deb688b60b637486c27867c0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of necessity that always brings misery must have snapped, and the malignant fate that governs it would never allow such a break in her eternal laws. But why should I complain? Misery was my natural element, and only what was miserable could come near me; if Woodville had been happy, I would never have known him. And I, who for years have been sustained by tears and raised under the dew of grief, can I hesitate to recount a tale of suffering and death? Woodville had to travel into the country and was kept away day after day in weary separation from his beautiful bride. He received a letter from her saying she was slightly ill, begging him to hurry to her, certain that his eyes would grant her health and his company would be her surest medicine. He was delayed three more days, and then he rushed to her. His heart, for reasons he could not name, foretold disaster; he had heard nothing further from her; he feared she might be worse, and this worry made him impatient and restless, desperate for the moment when he would see her again, glowing with health and beauty. A sinister voice seemed to whisper to him, \u201cYou will never again see her as she was.\u201d When he reached her home, everything within it was silent. He passed through several rooms; in one, he saw a servant weeping bitterly. He was weak with fear and could barely ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d and then he just listened to the dreadful reply, \u201cNot yet.\u201d Those shocking words struck him as less terrifying than what he had expected; to learn she was still alive and that hope remained was a relief. He remembered her letter and indulged the wild thought that his kisses, filled with warm love and life, would revive her, that with him near, she could not die; that his presence was the talisman of her life. He hurried to her sickroom; she lay with fever-flushed cheeks, yet her eyes were closed and she seemed senseless. He wrapped her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses on her burning lips; in a voice muted by anguish he called her by the tenderest names: \u201cCome back, Elinor; I am here with you, your life, your love. Come back, dearest; you promised me this gift, that I would bring you health. Let your sweet spirit revive; you cannot die near me. What is death? Never to see you again? To lose what is part of myself, without whom I have no past and no future?\u201d\"}"
