          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f9604f91a98655a55685ec76\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They went on past desolate brickfields. The fog thinned a little here, letting him see the odd kilns shaped like bottles, with orange tongues of flame spreading like fans. A dog barked as they passed, and somewhere far off in the murk a stray sea-gull screamed. The horse caught a rut, swerved, and suddenly broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road behind and clattered once more over rough-laid streets. Nearly every window lay in darkness, yet now and again grotesque shadows were cut out against some lamplit blind. He stared at them in fascination. They moved like gigantic puppets, gesturing as if alive. He loathed them. A sullen fury burned in his heart. When they took a corner, a woman shouted something after them from an open doorway, and two men dashed after the hansom for nearly a hundred yards. The cabman lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nPeople say passion makes the mind move in circles. Certainly, with ghastly repetition, Dorian Gray\u2019s bitten lips shaped those subtle phrases that dealt with soul and sense, until he had drawn from them, as it were, the full expression of his mood and, with intellectual sanction, excused passions that would have ruled him even without such excuse. From cell to cell of his brain ran the single thought, and the fierce craving to live, that most dreadful of appetites, nerved every trembling fibre. Ugliness, once hateful to him because it made things real, grew precious for that same reason. Ugliness became reality. The vulgar brawl, the foul den, the raw violence of chaos, the sheer vileness of thief and pariah, struck more vividly, by their intense actuality, than all the gracious forms of art, all the dreamy echoes of song. These were what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nAll at once the cabman jerked to a halt at the head of a dim lane. Above the low roofs and jagged chimneys rose the black spars of ships. White wreaths of mist clung to the yards like spectral sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere along here, sir, isn\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started, glanced around. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he said, and, springing out, pressed on the extra fare he had promised. He strode swiftly toward the quay. Here and there a lantern glimmered at the stern of some hulking merchantman, its light shaking and shattering in the puddles. A crimson glare poured from an outward-bound steamer taking on coal. The slimy paving looked like drenched rubber.\"}"
