          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"875cef49c27d45955a455d5c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had taken an excellent house in Camden Place, lofty and dignified, suited to a man of consequence. He and Elizabeth were settled there, quite satisfied with everything.\\n\\nAnne entered with a sinking heart, expecting to be imprisoned there for many months, silently wondering, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Yet the unexpected warmth of her welcome did her good. Her father and sister were pleased to show her the house and furniture and met her kindly. They even remarked that making a fourth at dinner was an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was pleasant and smiling, but her politeness felt routine. Anne had expected her to act properly upon arrival, but the friendliness from the others surprised her. They were clearly in high spirits, and soon she would hear why. They had no interest in listening to her. After waiting for compliments about being missed in their old neighborhood, which Anne could not give, they asked a few shallow questions before taking over the conversation. Uppercross meant nothing to them, Kellynch very little: everything was about Bath.\\n\\nThey delighted in telling her that Bath exceeded their hopes in every way. Their house was unquestionably the best in Camden Place; their drawing rooms had clear advantages over any others they had seen or heard of, and their superiority extended to the style of the decor and the taste of the furnishings. Their acquaintance was highly sought after. Everyone wanted to visit them. They had declined many introductions, yet still had cards left by people they did not know.\\n\\nThere was plenty to enjoy. Could Anne wonder they were happy? Perhaps not, but she had to sigh that her father felt no loss in this change, saw nothing to miss in the duties and dignity of a resident landholder, and found so much to be proud of in the small vanities of town life. She also had to sigh, and smile, and marvel as Elizabeth flung open the folding doors and walked with pride from one drawing room to the other, boasting of their size; at how the woman who had once been mistress of Kellynch Hall could find anything to brag about in space measured by two walls perhaps thirty feet apart.\\n\\nAnd there was more to make them happy. They had Mr Elliot as well.\"}"
