{"request_id":"61bcf1f34cb24758c8cb2f25","rewritten_text":"The stars still blazed when I jolted awake, and Taurus riding high in the southern sky showed it was midnight. I came out of troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s final feast; I arrived ravenous, the lids lifted, hot water sending up its empty steam, and then I ran from the host’s anger. He had Raymond’s face, and in my fevered mind the bowls he hurled after me were brimming with foul vapors. My friend’s form, twisted into countless distortions, swelled into a monstrous phantom marked with the sign of plague on its brow. The growing shadow kept rising until it filled, and then seemed ready to burst, the adamant dome that arched over and enclosed the world. The nightmare became torment; with a fierce effort I threw off sleep and called reason back to her usual duties. My first thought was Perdita; I had to return to her, to support her, to draw sustenance from despair itself to sustain her wounded heart, to recall her from her wild grief with the stern commands of duty and the gentle pull of sorrow.

Only the stars could guide me. I turned from the dreadful ruin of the Golden City and, after great effort, managed to free myself from its confines. Outside the walls I encountered a troop of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one and raced to my sister. Even in that short interval the plain had changed. The camp had broken up; the remains of the disbanded army were scattered in little groups across the field. Every face was shadowed, every move spoke disbelief and fear.

My heart heavy, I entered the palace and stood afraid to go on, to speak, to even look. Perdita sat in the middle of the hall on the marble floor, head on her chest, hair unbound, fingers weaving together nonstop; her face was marble-white, every feature knotted by anguish. She noticed me and looked up, searching. Her flicker of hope was agony; words died before I could form them, and a gruesome smile creased my lips. She read my expression; her head dropped again, her hands resumed their restless motion. At last I found my voice, but the sound terrified her. The poor girl had read my look; for all the world she did not want her misery shaped and sealed with harsh, final words. Instead she seemed eager to steer my thoughts away. She got to her feet. “Hush,” she whispered. “After much weeping, Clara is sleeping; we must not wake her.”"}

