          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1d4fbee9a7ddcea2598f50a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At least he believed so. But who could know for sure? No, there had been nothing more. From vanity he had spared her. Out of hypocrisy he had worn the mask of virtue. From curiosity he had tried self-denial. Now he understood that. But this murder\u2014would it haunt him for the rest of his life? Would he always carry his past like a weight? Would he actually confess? Never. There was only one piece of evidence left against him. The picture itself\u2014that was proof. He would destroy it. Why had he kept it so long? Once he had enjoyed watching it change and grow old. Recently there had been no such pleasure. It had kept him awake at night. Whenever he was away, terror gripped him that other eyes might see it. It had spread sadness over his passions. Its mere memory had spoiled many joys. It had been like a conscience to him. Yes, it had been his conscience. He would destroy it. He looked around and saw the knife that had killed Basil Hallward. He had cleaned it over and over until no stain remained. It shone, gray and gleaming. As it had killed the painter, so it would kill the painter\u2019s work and everything tied to it. It would kill the past, and once that was dead, he would be free. It would kill this monstrous secret life of the soul, and without its ugly warnings he would be at peace. He grabbed the knife and stabbed the picture with it. A cry rang out, followed by a crash. The cry was so full of pain that the startled servants woke and crept from their rooms. Two gentlemen walking past in the square below stopped and looked up at the large house. They walked on until they met a policeman and brought him back. The man rang the bell again and again, but no one answered. Apart from a light in one of the top windows, the house lay in darkness. After a while he stepped away and waited in a neighboring doorway. \u201cWhose house is that, Constable?\u201d asked the older gentleman. \u201cMr. Dorian Gray\u2019s, sir,\u201d said the policeman. The gentlemen looked at each other as they walked off and sneered. One was Sir Henry Ashton\u2019s uncle. Inside, in the servants\u2019 wing, the half-dressed staff whispered among themselves. Old Mrs. Leaf wept and twisted her hands. Francis was as pale as death. After about fifteen minutes he gathered the coachman and a footman and slowly climbed the stairs.\"}"
