          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0bfd3f5281e9b8d3a565a0d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 12. A remarkable shift occurred in the balloon\u2019s course, and although I had fully expected it, I was unmistakably delighted. After reaching about the twentieth parallel south, it abruptly veered sharply eastward and continued that way all day, remaining almost, if not entirely, in the precise plane of the lunar ellipse. What stood out was a noticeable rocking of the car caused by this change in direction\u2014a rocking that persisted, to varying degrees, for several hours.\\n\\nApril 13. Once again I was badly frightened by the return of the loud, crackling sound that had terrified me on the tenth. I thought about it for a long time but could reach no satisfying conclusion. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter shrank dramatically, now spanning an angle from the balloon of just over twenty-five degrees. The moon was invisible, nearly at my zenith. I remained in the plane of the ellipse but made little headway eastward.\\n\\nApril 14. The earth\u2019s diameter diminished at an extremely rapid rate. Today I became firmly convinced that the balloon was actually racing up the line of apsides to the point of perigee\u2014in other words, following the direct path that would bring it straight to the moon at the part of its orbit closest to Earth. The moon itself was directly overhead and thus out of sight. I had to work long and hard to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\nApril 15. By now even the outlines of the continents and seas on Earth were too blurred to make out. Around noon I noticed, for the third time, that dreadful noise that had so astonished me before. This time it lasted several moments and grew louder as it went on. Finally, while I stood stunned and terrified, expecting some monstrous disaster, the car shook violently, and a colossal, flaming mass of some unknown material streaked past the balloon with the roar of a thousand thunderclaps. When my fear and shock had eased a little, it was easy to assume it was a massive volcanic fragment flung from the world I was approaching\u2014most likely one of that peculiar class of objects occasionally found on Earth and called meteoric stones for lack of a better name.\\n\\nApril 16. Today, straining to look upward through each side window in turn, I was thrilled to see a tiny portion of the moon\u2019s disk jutting, so to speak, beyond the enormous circumference of the balloon.\"}"
