          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a64c31f92f851709c4bb234\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the center of the gap I noticed a white spot, but at first I could not tell what it was. I adjusted the telescope\u2019s focus, looked again, and realized it was a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cAt that discovery I was so confident I thought the puzzle solved. The words \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could only describe the skull\u2019s position on the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 could mean only one thing when searching for buried treasure. I understood that the plan was to drop a bullet through the skull\u2019s left eye. A straight line drawn from the nearest part of the trunk through the \u2018shot\u2019 (where the bullet landed) and out fifty feet would mark a specific spot\u2014and I figured something valuable might lie buried beneath it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll that,\u201d I said, \u201cis very clear\u2014clever, but still straightforward. After you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, after carefully taking the tree\u2019s bearings, I headed home. The moment I left the \u2018devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 though, the circular gap disappeared, and no matter how I turned, I could not see it again. The great ingenuity here is that the circular opening (and I am certain it is a fact) can be seen only from that narrow ledge on the rock face.\\n\\n\u201cJupiter had accompanied me on my trip to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel.\u2019 He had likely noticed my distracted manner for weeks and made sure I was never alone. But the next morning I got up very early, managed to slip away, and went into the hills to find the tree. After a lot of effort I located it. When I returned that night, my valet threatened to flog me. You know the rest of that episode as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI imagine,\u201d I said, \u201cthat you missed the spot on your first dig because Jupiter let the bug fall through the skull\u2019s right eye instead of the left.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. That mistake changed the \u2018shot\u2019\u2014the position of the stake closest to the tree\u2014by about two and a half inches. If the treasure had been right under the \u2018shot,\u2019 the error would have meant little. But the \u2018shot\u2019 and the nearest point of the tree were only anchor points to establish a line of direction. Naturally, the small error at the start grew as we extended the line, and by the time we went fifty feet, it had led us completely off course.\u201d\"}"
        }
