          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4da3c155639981040d5e1ad8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself to visit the Coles. The visit supplied her with many pleasant memories the next day, and whatever dignity she might have sacrificed by leaving her seclusion was more than repaid by the glow of popularity. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014good people who deserved happiness\u2014and left a name behind that would not fade quickly.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is rare, and there were two points that still troubled her. She wondered if she had broken the duty of woman to woman by sharing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings with Frank Churchill. It was scarcely right; but the thought had been so strong that it slipped out, and his willingness to believe everything she said was such a compliment to her insight that she could not be sure she ought to have kept quiet.\\n\\nThe other regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and there she had no doubt. She honestly and clearly regretted that her own playing and singing were inferior. She truly mourned the idleness of her childhood, sat down, and practised hard for an hour and a half.\\n\\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet\u2019s arrival; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, comfort would have come quickly.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t group us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh dear\u2014I think you play best. I think you play just as well as she does. I would much rather listen to you. Everyone said last night how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything must have felt the difference. The truth is, Harriet, my playing is only good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I will always think you play just as well as she does, or that if there is any difference nobody would ever notice it. Mr. Cole said you had so much taste; and Mr. Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, and that he valued taste much more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste. Nobody mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing\u2014there is no understanding a word of it. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it is simply her duty, because she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
