          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"53d3604174aa1fee59fbdb32\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She was right about the rats. Every so often one would poke its nose out of a hole in the corner. She said she needed things close by to throw at them when she was alone or they wouldn\u2019t leave her be. She showed me a bar of lead twisted into a knot and said she was usually a good shot with it, but she had wrenched her arm a day or two ago and wasn\u2019t sure she could throw straight. Still, she waited for a chance and soon hurled it at a rat; she missed by a mile and cried \u201cOuch!\u201d because it hurt her arm so much. Then she told me to try for the next one. I wanted to get gone before her husband came back, but of course I didn\u2019t let her see that. I took the lead, and when the first rat showed his nose I let fly; if he\u2019d stayed put, he would have been a mighty sick rat. She said that was fine, and she figured I\u2019d get the next one. She fetched the lump of lead again and also brought a hank of yarn she wanted help with. I held up my hands and she laid the hank over them, and she kept talking about her and her husband. Then she stopped to say:\\n\\n\u201cKeep your eye on the rats. You better keep the lead in your lap, ready.\u201d\\n\\nShe dropped the lump into my lap right then, and I clamped my knees on it while she kept talking. That lasted barely a minute. Then she lifted the yarn off, looked me straight in the face, smiling, and said:\\n\\n\u201cCome now, what\u2019s your real name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWh\u2014what, ma\u2019am?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s your real name? Is it Bill, or Tom, or Bob\u2014what is it?\u201d\\n\\nI reckon I shook like a leaf and didn\u2019t know what to do. But I said:\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t make fun of a poor girl like me, ma\u2019am. If I\u2019m in the way, I\u2019ll\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, you won\u2019t. Sit down and stay put. I\u2019m not going to hurt you, and I\u2019m not going to tell on you either. Just tell me your secret and trust me. I\u2019ll keep it, and what\u2019s more, I\u2019ll help you. So will my old man, if you want him to. You\u2019re a runaway apprentice, that\u2019s all. It\u2019s nothing. There\u2019s no harm in it. You\u2019ve been treated bad, so you made up your mind to leave. Bless you, child, I wouldn\u2019t tell on you.\u201d\"}"
        }
