          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4070a37702b834a289081637\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI swear,\u201d he cried, \u201cby the sun, by the blue sky, and by the fire of love burning in my heart, that if you grant my request, you shall never see me again while they live. Go home and begin your work; I will watch its progress with unbearable anxiety, and when you are ready I will appear.\u201d\\n\\nWith that, he abruptly left me, afraid, perhaps, that my resolve might change. I watched him run down the mountain faster than an eagle\u2019s flight, soon vanishing among the rolling waves of the icy sea.\\n\\nHis story had lasted the entire day, and the sun was sinking when he departed. I knew I should hurry down to the valley before darkness trapped me, yet my heart was heavy and my steps slow. The effort of threading the narrow mountain paths and keeping my footing wearied me, while my mind churned with the day\u2019s events. Night was well advanced when I reached the halfway rest and sat beside the fountain. The stars appeared when the clouds drifted away; dark pines stood before me, and broken trees lay scattered on the ground. The solemn scene stirred strange thoughts. I wept bitterly, and clasping my hands in agony, I cried, \u201cOh stars and clouds and winds, you are all ready to mock me; if you pity me, crush my feeling and memory\u2014let me become nothing. But if not, leave me\u2014leave me in darkness.\u201d\\n\\nThey were wild, miserable thoughts, yet I cannot explain how the endless sparkle of the stars oppressed me, or how each gust of wind sounded like a dull, hateful sirocco coming to destroy me.\\n\\nDawn broke before I reached the village of Chamounix. I rested nowhere but went straight back to Geneva. Even inside my own heart I could not express what I felt\u2014they weighed on me like a mountain, and their very excess dulled my anguish. Thus I returned home, and when I entered the house and stood before my family, my gaunt, frantic look filled them with alarm. I answered no questions, hardly spoke. I felt as if I were under a curse\u2014unworthy of their sympathy\u2014as if I could never again share their companionship.\"}"
