          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abcff132916a610ea6bcbc78\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the middle of this gap I spotted a white speck, though at first I couldn\u2019t tell what it was. I refocused the telescope, looked again, and now realized it was a human skull. \\n\\n\u201cAfter that, I was confident I\u2019d solved the riddle. The phrase \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could only describe where the skull sat in the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 could only mean one thing when hunting for buried treasure. I understood the plan was to drop a bullet from the skull\u2019s left eye, then draw a straight line\u2014what they call a bee-line\u2014from the closest spot on the trunk through the \u2018shot,\u2019 meaning where the bullet landed. Extending that line fifty feet would mark a precise point, and beneath that point I thought there might well be something valuable hidden.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cAll of this,\u201d I said, \u201cis very clear\u2014ingenious, yes, but still straightforward. Once you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened?\u201d \\n\\n\u201cWell, after carefully taking the tree\u2019s bearings, I headed home. The moment I stepped away from the \u2018devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 though, the circular slit vanished; no matter how I turned, I couldn\u2019t see it again. What strikes me as the cleverest part of this whole affair is that the circular opening can\u2019t be seen from any other accessible position than that narrow ledge on the rock face\u2014repeated tests have convinced me of it.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cOn that trip to the Bishop\u2019s Hotel I was accompanied by Jupiter, who had clearly noticed my distracted mood for weeks and made sure not to leave me alone. But the next morning I got up very early, managed to sneak away from him, and went into the hills to find the tree. After a lot of hard work I found it. When I came home that evening, my valet threatened to give me a whipping. The rest of the adventure you know as well as I do.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cI suppose,\u201d I said, \u201cthat on your first attempt at digging you missed the spot because Jupiter foolishly let the bug fall through the skull\u2019s right eye instead of the left.\u201d \\n\\n\u201cExactly. That mistake shifted the \u2018shot\u2019\u2014meaning the peg closest to the tree\u2014by about two and a half inches. If the treasure had been right under the shot, it wouldn\u2019t have mattered much; but the shot and the nearest point of the trunk were just two points to set a line. So even a small initial error grew worse along the line, and by the time we measured off fifty feet, we were completely off track.\u201d\"}"
