{"request_id":"8013639459a734f3d2507d3b","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s parents, who were rather old-fashioned and might object to the marriage if any hint of scandal arose, though he was certain that if he laid out the facts, they would be the first to understand his motives. Everything pointed to poison as the best solution. It was safe, reliable, discreet, and allowed him to avoid the sort of painful scene he detested, as did most Englishmen.

He knew nothing about poisons, and since the waiter couldn’t find anything in the library beyond Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine, he searched the shelves himself. Eventually he came across a finely bound Pharmacopoeia and a copy of Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members, elected by mistake in someone else’s place—a mishap that so angered the Committee that when the intended candidate appeared, they unanimously blackballed him. Lord Arthur was baffled by the technical jargon in both books and began to regret not paying more attention to his classics at Oxford, when he discovered in the second volume of Erskine a clear and thorough description of aconitine’s properties. It seemed exactly what he needed. The poison acted almost instantly, caused no pain, and when taken in a gelatin capsule, as Sir Mathew suggested, was not at all unpleasant. He noted the lethal dose on his shirt cuff, returned the books to their shelves, and strolled up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the renowned chemists. Mr. Pestle, who personally served aristocratic customers, was quite surprised by the order and murmured deferentially about needing a medical certificate. Once Lord Arthur explained it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to dispose of because it was showing signs of early rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf, Mr. Pestle declared himself satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s impressive knowledge of toxicology, and filled the prescription immediately.

Lord Arthur put the capsule into a charming little silver bonbonnière he noticed in a Bond Street shop window, discarded Pestle and Hambey’s unattractive pillbox, and drove straight to Lady Clementina’s."}

