          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"db2d977ad7f0f22108336ece\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a pleasant feeling of tidying up and taking a quiet breath before starting life anew in our village on Saturday nights, and it encouraged Joe to stay out half an hour later on Saturdays than on other evenings. When that extra half-hour ended and his rum and water was gone, Joe stood up to leave and took my hand. \u201cWait half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the stranger. \u201cI believe I have a bright new shilling in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d He fished it out of a handful of small coins, wrapped it in a scrunched-up piece of paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cRemember! Your own.\u201d I thanked him, staring at him far beyond what was polite, while clinging to Joe. He wished Joe good night, and he wished Mr. Wopsle good night (who left with us), and he gave me only a look from that aiming eye\u2014no, not even a look, because he shut it, yet astonishing things can be done with an eye by hiding it. On the way home, even if I had been in the mood to talk, any conversation would have had to come from me, because Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe walked all the way home with his mouth wide open to rinse out the rum with as much air as he could. But I was stunned by the reappearance of my old wrongdoing and old acquaintance, and I couldn\u2019t think of anything else. My sister was not in a particularly bad temper when we walked into the kitchen, and Joe took that rare chance to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d Mrs. Joe declared triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d I unwrapped the paper, and it turned out to be a good shilling. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d cried Mrs. Joe, tossing down the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d Nothing less than two thick, sweaty one-pound notes that looked as if they\u2019d been on the friendliest terms with every cattle market in the county. Joe grabbed his hat and ran back to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, fairly certain the man would be gone. Soon Joe returned, saying the man had indeed left, but he, Joe, had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
        }
      ],
