          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5de005673d5a320b172e86ca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Elizabeth was sorrowful and despondent; she no longer found pleasure in her usual activities, and every enjoyment seemed a sacrilege to the dead. She believed that endless mourning and tears were the only proper tribute to such innocence destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who had once wandered with me along the lake, talking with rapture about our future prospects. The first of those sorrows that come to separate us from earthly joys had touched her, and its dim shadow extinguished her brightest smiles.\\n\\n\u201cWhen I think about it, dear cousin,\u201d she said, \u201cthe wretched death of Justine Moritz has completely altered how I view the world and everything in it. Before, whenever I read or heard of vice and injustice, I treated them as stories from a distant past or as imagined evils; at least they felt far away and easier to reason about than to picture. But now misery has settled among us, and mankind appears to me like monsters thirsting for each other\u2019s blood. Yet I know I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl guilty; and had she actually committed the crime for which she died, she would have been the most depraved of human beings. To murder the son of her benefactor and friend, a child she had cared for from birth and seemed to love as her own, just for a few jewels! I could never approve the execution of anyone, yet I would have judged such a person unfit to live among people. But she was innocent. I know\u2014 I feel\u2014 she was innocent; and your sharing that belief reassures me. Oh, Victor, when lies can so closely resemble truth, how can anyone feel sure of their happiness? I feel as if I were walking along the edge of a precipice while crowds push and strive to hurl me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the killer has escaped; he walks the world free, perhaps even respected. But even if I were condemned to die on the scaffold for the same crimes, I would not exchange places with that wretch.\u201d\\n\\nI listened to her speech in the deepest agony. I was the true murderer, not by action, yet in effect. Elizabeth saw the torment in my face, and gently taking my hand, she said, \u201cMy dearest friend, you must calm yourself. These events have wounded me deeply, God knows, but even so, I am not as miserable as you seem.\u201d\"}"
