          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f24888b0d4c9216c595189b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Great God! Had I for one moment guessed the infernal design of my demonic enemy, I would sooner have exiled myself forever from my homeland and roamed the world a lonely exile than have consented to this wretched marriage. Yet, as though wielding some sorcery, the monster obscured his true purpose; and while I imagined I had secured only my own destruction, I hastened the doom of one infinitely dearer.\\n\\nAs the appointed time for our union approached, whether through cowardice or some prophetic instinct, my heart faltered. Still I hid my agitation beneath a semblance of mirth that drew smiles and delight to my father\u2019s face, though it scarcely escaped the vigilant, discerning eye of Elizabeth. She anticipated our joining with serene contentment, yet not untouched by the slight dread, born of former miseries, that the happiness now appearing so certain and palpable might dissolve into mere vapour, leaving nothing save deep, enduring regret.\\n\\nArrangements went forward for the celebration, congratulatory calls were exchanged, and all wore an aspect of cheer. I locked, as best I might, the gnawing anxiety in my heart and engaged with apparent zeal in my father\u2019s schemes, though they might prove but the adornments of my tragedy. Thanks to my father\u2019s efforts, a portion of Elizabeth\u2019s inheritance had been restored by the Austrian government. A small estate on the shores of Como was hers. It was settled that immediately after our wedding we would set out for Villa Lavenza and pass our first days of happiness beside the lovely lake near which it stood.\\n\\nMeanwhile I took every precaution to safeguard myself should the fiend attack openly. I bore pistols and a dagger at all times and kept ceaseless watch against any stratagem, and by such means won a measure of tranquillity. Indeed, as the day drew near, the menace seemed more an illusion, unworthy to trouble my peace, while the bliss I anticipated in my marriage assumed greater certainty as the date of its solemnization approached and I heard it constantly mentioned as an event chance itself could not prevent.\\n\\nElizabeth appeared content; my outward calm helped greatly to soothe her spirits. Yet on the day destined to fulfil both my wishes and my fate, she grew melancholy, oppressed by a presentiment of evil; perhaps, too, she remembered the terrible secret I had pledged to unveil to her on the morrow.\"}"
