{"request_id":"704625787e8bdbe8ac12fcd0","rewritten_text":"Many American women, once they leave home, affect a permanent air of frailty, thinking it a mark of European polish, but Mrs. Otis never made that mistake. She possessed a splendid constitution and an extraordinary amount of vigor. In many ways she was distinctly English, proving how much we now share with America, aside from language. Her oldest son, named Washington by patriotic parents—a choice he perpetually regretted—was a fair, rather handsome young man who prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino three seasons running. Even in London he was known as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only indulgences; otherwise he was remarkably practical. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, as lithe and lovely as a fawn, with a bold openness in her big blue eyes. She was a first-rate horsewoman and once raced old Lord Bilton twice around the park on her pony, winning by a length and a half right in front of the Achilles statue, to the wild joy of the young Duke of Cheshire, who proposed to her on the spot. His guardians sent him straight back to Eton that night, weeping all the way. After Virginia came the twins, commonly called “The Stars and Stripes” because they were always being spanked. They were delightful boys and, aside from the worthy Minister, the only real republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase lies seven miles from Ascot, the nearest train station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to collect them, and they set off in excellent spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was sweet with the scent of the pine woods. Now and then they heard a wood pigeon crooning over its own gentle song, or glimpsed, deep in the rustling ferns, the polished breast of a pheasant. Little squirrels watched them from the beeches, and rabbits darted away through the brush and over the mossy knolls, white tails held high. Yet as they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly clouded over, a strange stillness fell on the air, a great flock of rooks glided silently above them, and before they reached the house a few heavy raindrops had already fallen.

An elderly woman stood on the steps to welcome them, neatly dressed in black silk, wearing a white cap and apron."}

