          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed2229cc8ca1a5960cbfefdb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, once more resting his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm after a brief silence, \u201cto what do you attribute this seizure?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d Doctor Manette replied, \u201cthat there was a powerful and unusual resurgence of the chain of thoughts and memories that first produced the illness. Certain intense associations of a deeply distressing cast came back to him vividly, I think. It is likely that he had long harboured a fear that such associations would resurface\u2014perhaps under specific conditions\u2014perhaps on a certain occasion. He tried to brace himself in vain; perhaps the effort to steel himself left him less able to endure it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he retain any recollection of what happened during the relapse?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked, naturally hesitant.\\n\\nThe Doctor cast a desolate glance around the room, shook his head, and answered softly, \u201cNot in the least.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, concerning the future,\u201d Mr. Lorry hinted.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining firmness, \u201cI should feel great hope. Since it pleased Heaven in mercy to restore him so quickly, I should feel great hope. He, succumbing under the weight of some complicated thing\u2014long feared, long dimly foresighted and resisted\u2014and then recovering when the storm had broken and passed, gives me hope that the worst is done.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is good comfort. I am grateful!\u201d cried Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am grateful!\u201d echoed the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two further points,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201con which I wish to be informed. May I proceed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou cannot render your friend a better service.\u201d The Doctor extended his hand.\\n\\n\u201cTo the first, then. He is of studious habits, remarkably energetic; he devotes himself with great ardour to gaining professional knowledge, to conducting experiments, to many undertakings. Does he overdo it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think not. The nature of his mind may be to crave constant occupation. That might be partly inherent, partly the outcome of suffering. The less it engaged itself with healthy pursuits, the more risk there would be of its turning in an unhealthy direction. He may have watched himself and made that discovery.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are confident he is not under excessive strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I am quite confident.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked at present\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt that could readily occur. There has been a violent tension in one direction, and it requires a counterweight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPardon me, as a persistent man of business. Suppose for a moment that he _were_ overworked; would it manifest itself in some recurrence of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
