          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82e83234228323225b8bb5a4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fact, he was dressed as \u201cJonas the Graveless, or the Corpse-Snatcher of Chertsey Barn,\u201d one of his most striking roles, and one the Cantervilles had every reason to remember, since it was the true origin of their quarrel with their neighbor, Lord Rufford. It was about a quarter past two in the morning, and, as far as he could tell, no one was awake. As he wandered toward the library to see if any trace of the bloodstain remained, two figures suddenly sprang out at him from a dark corner, waving their arms wildly over their heads and screaming \u201cBOO!\u201d in his ear.\\n\\nSeized with a panic that was entirely understandable in the circumstances, he bolted for the staircase, only to find Washington Otis waiting there with the big garden syringe. Hemmed in by his enemies on every side and almost cornered, he vanished into the great iron stove, which was fortunately unlit, and had to make his way home through the flues and chimneys, reaching his room in a dreadful state of dirt, disarray, and despair.\\n\\nAfter that, he was never seen again on any nighttime excursion. The twins lay in ambush for him several times and scattered the passages with nutshells every night, to the great annoyance of their parents and the servants, but it did no good. It was obvious his feelings were so injured that he refused to show himself. Mr. Otis therefore returned to his monumental work on the history of the Democratic Party, which he had been pursuing for years; Mrs. Otis organized a spectacular clam bake that astonished the entire county; the boys devoted themselves to lacrosse, euchre, poker, and other American national games; and Virginia rode through the lanes on her pony, accompanied by the young Duke of Cheshire, who had come to spend the last week of his holiday at Canterville Chase. Everyone assumed the ghost had departed, and Mr. Otis even wrote to Lord Canterville to say so; in reply, Lord Canterville expressed great satisfaction at the news and sent his warmest congratulations to the Minister\u2019s admirable wife.\\n\\nThe Otises, however, were mistaken, for the ghost was still in the house, and though nearly an invalid now, he had no intention of letting matters drop, especially when he heard that the guest list included the young Duke of Cheshire, whose grand-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost. He was found the next morning lying on the card-room floor in such a helpless, paralyzed state that, although he lived to a great age, he was never able to say anything again except \u201cDouble Sixes.\u201d\"}"
