          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ec8bb0973973c041b0a4b2f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were thrilled to see their dear friend again, declared it felt like ages since they had met, and kept asking what she had been doing since they\u2019d parted. They barely acknowledged the rest of the family, avoided Mrs. Bennet whenever possible, spoke little to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. They left again soon, springing up with an energy that surprised their brother and hurrying off as if desperate to escape Mrs. Bennet\u2019s polite attentions.\\n\\nThe thought of the Netherfield ball delighted every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to see it as a tribute to her eldest daughter and felt especially flattered that Mr. Bingley delivered the invitation himself rather than sending a formal card. Jane imagined a joyful evening with her two friends and their brother\u2019s attentions, while Elizabeth looked forward to dancing often with Mr. Wickham and watching Mr. Darcy\u2019s expressions and manners confirm everything she believed. Kitty and Lydia\u2019s happiness depended less on any single event or person; though both intended, like Elizabeth, to dance half the night with Mr. Wickham, he was far from the only partner who could please them, and a ball was, after all, a ball. Even Mary assured her family she felt no reluctance about attending.\\n\\n\u201cAs long as my mornings remain my own,\u201d she said, \u201cthat\u2019s enough. I don\u2019t think it\u2019s any sacrifice to take part in evening engagements now and then. Society makes claims on all of us, and I count myself among those who believe that periods of recreation and amusement are desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so high that, though she rarely spoke to Mr. Collins without cause, she had to ask whether he meant to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation and, if so, whether he considered it proper to join the evening\u2019s entertainment. She was somewhat surprised to find he had no reservations and did not remotely fear a reprimand, from either the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh, for daring to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, I am far from thinking that a ball of this sort, hosted by a young man of character for respectable people, could have any harmful influence,\u201d he said, \u201cand I am so little opposed to dancing myself that I hope to be honored with the hands of all my fair cousins over the course of the evening. I will seize this chance to request yours, Miss Elizabeth, for the first two dances in particular\u2014a preference I trust my cousin Jane will attribute to the proper reason and not to any disrespect toward her.\u201d\"}"
