          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b44349d1e61fc894fe186e34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while midnight pealed from the tall Westminster tower, each resonant chime making the night quiver. Then the railway lamps faded, save for a lone lantern glowing like a great ruby atop its mast, and the city\u2019s roar subsided to a distant murmur.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and sauntered towards Blackfriars. How unreal the whole world appeared! How dreamlike! The houses across the river seemed shaped out of darkness itself. One might have sworn that silver and shadow had re-created everything. The massive dome of St. Paul\u2019s swelled like a bubble through the dim air.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle he caught sight of a man leaning over the parapet; drawing closer, he saw the fellow glance up, the gaslight flooding his features.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist! That pudgy, flaccid face, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the wan and feeble smile, the sensuous mouth\u2014no mistaking them.\\n\\nLord Arthur halted. A dazzling inspiration struck him, and he glided quietly forward. In an instant he grasped Mr. Podgers by the legs and hurled him into the Thames. There came a coarse curse, a heavy splash, then silence. Lord Arthur peered anxiously downward but saw only a tall hat, twirling in a pool of moonlit water. Soon that too sank, leaving no sign of the cheiromantist. For a moment he fancied he detected the bulky, twisted figure swimming towards the bridge staircase, and a dreadful sense of failure seized him, but it proved only a reflection, disappearing when the moon emerged from a cloud. At last he felt he had fulfilled destiny\u2019s command. He drew a deep breath of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u201cDropped something, sir?\u201d said a sudden voice behind him.\\n\\nHe spun around to face a policeman with a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u201cNothing worth speaking of, sergeant,\u201d he replied with a smile, and, hailing a passing hansom, he jumped in, directing the driver to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swayed between hope and apprehension. At times he half expected Mr. Podgers to step into the room; at others he felt Fate could not treat him so harshly. Twice he walked to the cheiromantist\u2019s lodgings in West Moon Street, yet he could not bring himself to ring. He craved certainty, and dreaded it.\\n\\nAt last it arrived. He was seated in the club smoking-room, sipping tea and listening listlessly to Surbiton\u2019s recounting of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
