          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d4903654dc5c33766a22d07f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a thorough gentleman. He was a gentleman clear through, and every member of his household the same. He came of good stock, as folks say, and that counts in a man just like it does in a horse, so Widow Douglas declared; and nobody ever disputed that she ranked among the very first aristocrats in our town. Pap was forever declaring it, too, though he himself didn\u2019t have any more breeding than a mudcat. Colonel Grangerford stood mighty tall and slender, with a darkish, pale complexion that didn\u2019t show a trace of red anywhere. He shaved clean every morning, all over that lean face; his lips were the thinnest I ever saw, and his nostrils were just as fine, while his nose rose high, topped by heavy brows, and his eyes were the darkest, sunk so far back they looked as if they peered at you out of caves, so to speak. His forehead was lofty, his hair jet black and straight, falling to his shoulders. His hands were long and narrow, and every day he put on a fresh shirt and a complete suit of linen so white it almost dazzled you; on Sundays he added a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane capped with silver. Not a speck of frivolity about him, not the slightest, and he was never loud. He was as kind as could be\u2014you just sensed it, and that gave you assurance. Once in a while he smiled, and it was pleasant to behold; but when he drew himself up like a liberty pole and the lightning started to dance under his eyebrows, you felt like climbing a tree first and finding out what was wrong afterward. He never needed to tell anyone to behave\u2014everyone was polite whenever he was around. Folks liked his company, too; he was nearly always like sunshine\u2014I mean he made everything feel fine. When he turned into a cloud bank it was terribly dark for half a minute, and that was plenty; nothing would dare go wrong again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and gave them good-day, and none of them sat again until the colonel and his lady had seated themselves. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard where the decanter stood, and mixed a glass of bitters to hand him. He took it and waited till Tom\u2019s and Bob\u2019s drinks were ready too, and then they bowed, saying, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam;\u201d and the colonel and his lady bowed the tiniest bit and said thank you, and all three drank. After that Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little splash of whiskey or apple brandy in the bottoms of their tumblers, and handed them to me and Buck, and we drank to the old folks as well.\"}"
