          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bb71c96f7a5a3e928d79a31b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have not the nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you feel at ease spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; his papa can, so why should not I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour how he was. I really think Charles might as well have told his father we would all come. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. Yesterday I was terribly frightened, but it is very different today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it too late to let them know, suppose you go as well as your husband. Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cDear me! that is an excellent idea, very excellent. I may as well go as stay, for I am no use at home\u2014am I?\u2014and it only tires me. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s feelings, are much better suited. You can make little Charles do anything; he always listens to you at once. It will be far better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh! I shall certainly go; I am sure I ought if I can, just as much as Charles, for they are desperate to have me meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. An excellent thought of yours, indeed, Anne. I will go tell Charles and get ready at once. You can send for us in a moment if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to worry you. I would not go, you may be sure, if I were not perfectly at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nShe was instantly knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and Anne, following her upstairs, arrived in time for the whole conversation, beginning with Mary\u2019s triumphant declaration\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, for I am no more use at home than you are. Even if I shut myself up forever with the child, I could not make him do anything he dislikes. Anne will stay; Anne agrees to stay home and take care of him. It is Anne\u2019s own suggestion, so I shall go with you, which will be much better, for I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I would be very glad to have you go; but it does seem rather hard that she should be left home by herself to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
        }
