          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ed8429762546ca12e262359\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Do not misread me; I was never truly mad. Even when my roving thoughts threatened to hurl me into mania, I still recognized my condition and never revealed it save through silence and solitude. Those about me perceived nothing of it. They observed only a poor girl with a broken spirit, speaking softly and sweetly, with tears sometimes escaping from beneath lowered eyelids, tears she tried to conceal. A girl who sought solitude and shrank from scrutiny; who never smiled\u2014indeed, I never smiled\u2014and that was the entire tale. \\n\\nYet I fled. I left my guardian\u2019s home and vanished; it was supposed from the letters I wrote and other circumstances that I had contrived to end my life. Thus they searched for me with less diligence than they would have otherwise; soon all trace and remembrance of me faded. I departed London in a small vessel bound for a northern English port. Having accomplished my purpose and finding myself wholly alone, tranquility returned. The sea lay smooth, the ship glided softly forward, and I sat on deck beneath the open vault of heaven, believing myself transformed. No longer the wild, raving, despairing Mathilda, but a youthful hermitess vowed to seclusion, striving to keep her breast free from tumult and accursed despair. The fanciful, nun-like garb I had assumed; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to me; the solitude to which I was henceforth consigned tended gentle feelings in my wounded heart. The breeze threading my hair revived me, and with calm eyes I watched the sunlight flickering on the waves and the birds pursuing one another over the waters, their wings skimming the surface. I slept as well, undisturbed by dreams, and woke renewed to savour once more my placid liberty.\\n\\nWithin four days we reached the harbour we sought. I refused to stay along the coast, proceeding instantly inland. I had already resolved upon the sort of retreat I required: a lone dwelling upon a broad plain, near no other habitation, where I might survey the whole horizon and roam far untroubled by the gaze of my fellow beings. I was no misanthrope, yet sensed that the gentle flow of my feelings relied on solitude. I settled amid a vast isolation\u2014a bleak heath scattered with stones, patches of short grass, and, here and there, a few rushes beside a small pool.\"}"
