          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"544719fb69affd0bbaf47707\",\"rewritten_text\":\"So two years went by. Day after day hundreds of dull hours wore on; they brought no visible change, yet slowly altered my mind as I drifted toward death. I began to study more, to feel the thoughts of others through books, to read history, and to lose myself among the multitudes who lived before me. Perhaps as the immediate pain faded, I became more human. Solitude also lost some of its charm: I started to long again for sympathy. I never felt drawn to crowds, but I did wish for one friend who would love me. You might say I was gradually becoming ready to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I wanted had to be so pure, so free from any influence of circumstance, that the world\u2019s coarse alloys\u2014present even in its noblest feelings\u2014could only deceive me. Believe me, I was even less prepared for human company than before. When I left them, they had tormented me like pain and sickness\u2014irritations outside the mind that I could cast off. But now I would have longed for sympathy; I would have wanted to bind my soul to someone else\u2019s and would have only stocked up on disappointment and suffering, for I was as tender as a sensitive plant, all raw nerve. I did not crave sympathy for ambition or wisdom, but for gentle, mutual affection: smiles to cheer me and soft words to comfort me. I wished for one heart where I could pour out my complaints without restraint, so that the heavenly nature of that soil might make blessed fruit spring from bad seed. Yet how could I find it? The love that animates friendship is a delicate spirit rarely found except when two kind souls grow together from youth, or when they are joined by shared suffering and purpose. It comes to some chosen few unbidden; it descends like gentle dew on selected spots which, though barren before, become fertile with sweet flowers under its benign influence. But when it is sought it flees; it mocks the prayers of its devotees; it gives freely, yet cannot be hunted down.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go looking for sympathy. But there on my lonely heath, under my humble roof surrounded by wilderness, it came to me like a winter sunbeam that adorns and melts the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone on blighted fruit; I did not revive beneath its light, for I was too utterly undone to feel its kindness.\"}"
