{"request_id":"4cb611a92477e00ba7305a0e","rewritten_text":"He declared he was languishing away his days uselessly where he stayed, that letters from his London acquaintances urged his return to finish the negotiation they had begun for his Indian venture. He could no longer postpone his departure; yet since his journey to London might be followed, even sooner than he imagined, by a longer voyage, he implored me to grant him as much of my company as I could afford. He begged me, therefore, to abandon my lone island and meet him at Perth, so we could travel south together. This letter partially roused me to life, and I resolved to leave my island at the end of two days.

Still, before I could depart, there stood a task at which I recoiled: I must pack away my chemical instruments, and to do so I had to enter the room where my detestable labor had been begun, handling the tools whose very sight made me ill. At dawn the next morning I mustered enough courage and unfastened the laboratory door. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed were strewn over the floor, and I almost believed I had hacked apart the living flesh of a human. I halted to steady myself and then stepped inside. With shaking hand I carried the apparatus out, yet I realized I ought not leave the remnants of my work there to arouse the peasants’ dread and mistrust; so I placed them into a basket with a heavy load of stones, intending to fling them into the sea that night, and meanwhile I sat on the shore, occupied with cleaning and arranging my chemical instruments.

No change could be more complete than that which had overtaken my mind since the night the dæmon appeared. I had previously regarded my promise with a dark despair, as a thing that, whatever the result, must be carried out; but now it seemed a veil had been stripped from my sight and I could see plainly for the first time. Not once did the thought of resuming my labors occur to me; the threat I had received burdened my mind, yet I never considered that some voluntary act of mine might avert it. I had concluded that to fashion another being resembling the fiend I had first made would be an offence of the basest and most monstrous selfishness, and I thrust aside every notion that might lead me elsewhere."}

