          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"77e8819577bfa24f5ab6cb4b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General was obliged to inform the jury that the accused, though youthful in appearance, was seasoned in treasonous dealings demanding his life. This correspondence with the public foe was no matter of today, yesterday, last year, or even the year before, but had gone on much longer. The prisoner, it was certain, had long been passing back and forth between France and England on secret missions for which he could offer no honest explanation. Had treasonous enterprises been of a nature to prosper\u2014which, happily, they never were\u2014the true wickedness and guilt of his work might have remained concealed. Providence, however, had inspired an individual beyond fear or reproach to ferret out the prisoner\u2019s designs and, horrified, reveal them to His Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. This patriot, Mr. Attorney-General assured them, would appear before the court. His stance and bearing were, on the whole, sublime. Once the prisoner\u2019s friend, he had, in a moment both auspicious and tragic, uncovered his infamy, and resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer shelter in his bosom upon the sacred altar of his country. Were statues in Britain decreed to benefactors as in ancient Greece and Rome, this illustrious citizen would certainly merit one; as they were not, he likely would go without. Virtue, as the poets\u2014whose verses the jury, of course, knew by heart, though their faces confessed they did not\u2014had observed, was contagious, especially that radiant quality called patriotism or love of country. The lofty example of this immaculate and unimpeachable witness for the Crown, to mention whom at all was an honour, had inspired the prisoner\u2019s servant to a holy resolve to inspect his master\u2019s table-drawers and pockets and to secrete his papers. Mr. Attorney-General anticipated attempts to disparage this admirable servant but, generally speaking, he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters and honoured him above his father and mother. He called upon the jury with confidence to share that sentiment. The testimony of these two witnesses, together with the documents they had discovered and which would be produced, would demonstrate that the prisoner had been supplied with lists of His Majesty\u2019s forces, their arrangement and readiness by sea and land, and would leave no doubt that he habitually transmitted such intelligence to a hostile power.\"}"
