          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"98205408536d7f2e15be293e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Perdita\u2019s name never arose; still I could not doubt that affection for her caused the wavering of purpose he displayed. And who merited love more than my high-souled sister? Who better deserved the hand of that self-elevated monarch than she, whose very look befitted a queen among nations? She cherished him even as he loved her; though frustration subdued her fervor, and ambition waged fierce war with his. We went together to the House that evening. Raymond, though aware that his projects and prospects must be debated and resolved in the coming discussion, wore an air of gaiety and unconcern. A hum like that of ten thousand swarming beehives deafened us as we entered the coffee-room. Clusters of politicians stood with harried faces and raised or hushed tones. The aristocratic faction\u2014the wealthiest and most influential men in England\u2014seemed less disturbed than the rest, for the issue would be argued without requiring their intervention. By the fire sat Ryland with his adherents. Ryland, of humble birth yet immense fortune, had inherited his riches from his manufacturer father. In youth he had witnessed the king\u2019s abdication and the merging of the two Houses of Lords and Commons; he had approved these popular advances, and his life\u2019s work had been to confirm and expand them. Since then the influence of landowners had grown; at first Ryland had not disapproved of Lord Raymond\u2019s plots, which drew away many supporters from his adversaries. But now matters were going too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the revival of sovereignty as a means to regain the authority and privileges they had forfeited. The half-slumbering spirit of royalty stirred again in men\u2019s minds; they, willing serfs, self-made subjects, stood ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few stalwart spirits endured, pillars of the state; yet the word republic had become trite to the common ear, and many\u2014an event soon to prove whether it was the majority\u2014yearned for the glitter and display of kingship. Ryland was aroused to defiance; he declared that only by his forbearance had this faction been allowed to thrive; the season for leniency had passed, and with a sweep of his arm he would brush away the cobwebs clouding his countrymen. When Raymond stepped into the coffee-room, his arrival drew near-shouts from his allies. They crowded about him, tallied their supporters, and laid out their reasons for expecting new recruits from members who had not yet announced themselves.\"}"
