{"request_id":"4c21a57d3423fade902e4919","rewritten_text":"We grew up together, with hardly a year between our ages. It is unnecessary to say that discord or conflict never came between us. Harmony animated our companionship, and the differences and contrasts in our characters bound us closer. Elizabeth possessed a calmer, more concentrated temperament; yet, though ardent, I could apply myself more intensely and was more deeply seized with a thirst for knowledge. She occupied herself with the airy visions created by poets; and in the majestic, wondrous scenery encircling our Swiss home—the sublime outlines of the mountains, the shifting seasons, tempest and stillness, the quiet of winter, and the vigour and turbulence of Alpine summers—she discovered boundless room for admiration and delight. While my companion regarded those grand spectacles with a grave, contented spirit, I took pleasure in probing their causes. The world seemed a mystery I longed to solve. Curiosity, earnest inquiry into the secret laws of nature, a joy bordering on rapture as they unfolded—these are among my earliest remembered sensations.

When a second son was born, seven years my junior, my parents entirely relinquished their wandering life and settled in their native land. We owned a house in Geneva and a campagne at Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, a little more than a league from the city. We lived chiefly at the latter, and my parents spent their days in substantial seclusion. My inclination was to shun crowds and devote myself fervently to a select few. I therefore cared little for my schoolmates as a group, yet entered the closest friendship with one among them. Henry Clerval, the son of a Genevese merchant, was a boy of unusual talent and fancy. He relished enterprise, hardship, and even danger purely for their own sake. Deeply versed in books of chivalry and romance, he composed heroic songs and began many a tale of enchantment and knightly escapade. He urged us to perform plays and to hold masquerades, drawing characters from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur’s Round Table, and the gallant host who shed their blood to wrest the holy sepulchre from the infidels.

No child could have enjoyed a happier youth than I did. My parents were animated by kindness itself. We knew they were not tyrants ruling our fate by whim, but the authors and creators of the countless pleasures we possessed."}

