          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c06e39517506ac2f4e3bd867\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February, and after a month in Bath, Anne was growing very anxious for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted to hear far more than Mary had shared. It had been three weeks since she had heard anything at all. She only knew that Henrietta was back home, and that Louisa, though said to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. She was thinking of all of them very intently one evening when a thicker-than-usual letter from Mary arrived\u2014and, adding to the pleasure and surprise, it came with Admiral and Mrs. Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was something to excite her. They were people her heart naturally turned toward.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d exclaimed Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have come to Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they brought you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Those letters are convenient passports. They ensure an introduction. Still, I should have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could not listen any longer; she could not even have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion fared under her father\u2019s remarks, because her letter absorbed her entirely. It had been started several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Anne,\\n\\n\u201cI make no apology for my silence, because I know how little people care about letters in a place like Bath. You must be much too happy to bother with Uppercross, which, as you well know, offers very little to write about. We have had a remarkably dull Christmas; Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove did not host a single dinner party the whole holiday. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. The holidays are over at last, though; I doubt any children ever had such long ones. They certainly were longer than mine. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear they still have not gone home. Mrs. Harville must be an odd mother to be separated from them so long. I do not understand it. They are not at all pleasant children, in my opinion; but Mrs. Musgrove seems to like them every bit as much as, if not more than, her own grandchildren. What dreadful weather we have had! Perhaps it is not felt in Bath with your nice pavements, but in the country it matters. I have not had a soul call on me since the second week of January, except for Charles Hayter, who has been visiting much more often than is welcome. Between ourselves, I think it was a great pity Henrietta did not stay in Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her out of his way a little.\u201d\"}"
