          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"617adaf861a6c5edba25d0ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Lay down your arms, fellow men, brothers! Forgiveness, help, and brotherly love await your repentance. You are dear to us because you wear the fragile form of humanity; each of you will find a friend and host among these ranks. Shall man remain the enemy of man while the plague, the enemy of all, hovers above us, rejoicing in our slaughter, more cruel even than itself?\u201d  Each army paused. On our side the soldiers gripped their weapons and fixed hard stares on the foe. The others still held their arms, more from fear than defiance; they looked at each other, each waiting for someone to lead, but they had no commander. Adrian leaped from his horse and approached one of the newly slain. \u201cHe was a man,\u201d he cried, \u201cand now he is dead. Quickly, bind the wounds of the fallen\u2014let none die; let no more souls escape through your merciless blows to recount before the throne of God the tale of fratricide. Bind their wounds\u2014restore them to their friends. Cast off the tiger hearts burning in your breasts; throw down those tools of cruelty and hate; in this brief pause granted by pitiless destiny, let every man be brother, guardian, and support to the other. Away with those blood-stained weapons, and hurry\u2014some of you\u2014bind up these wounds.\u201d  As he spoke, he knelt and lifted into his arms a man from whose side the warm tide of life poured out. The poor wretch gasped. Both armies were so still that his moans were clearly heard, and every heart, moments ago bent on total slaughter, now beat anxiously with hope and fear for this single man. Adrian tore off his military scarf and bound it around the sufferer. It was too late. The man breathed a deep sigh, his head dropped back, his limbs slackened. \u201cHe is dead,\u201d Adrian said, as the body slipped from his arms to the ground, and he bowed his head in grief and awe. The fate of the world seemed tied to the death of this one man. On both sides the forces threw down their arms; even the veterans wept, and our men extended their hands to their foes while a rush of love and deepest friendship filled every heart. The two armies mingled, unarmed and hand in hand, speaking only of how each might help the other. The adversaries joined, each repenting\u2014the one side for their former cruelties, the other for their recent violence\u2014and they obeyed the General\u2019s orders to march toward London.\"}"
