          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fd8db7a15820600e9b280ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI have not nerves for that kind of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really feel comfortable spending the entire evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuite. His papa can do it, so why may not I? Jemima is meticulous; she could send word every hour how he fares. In fact, I rather think Charles might have told his father we should all come. I am no more anxious about little Charles now than he is. Yesterday I was terribly frightened, but it is a very different case to-day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen if you do not consider it too late to give notice for yourself, why should you not go as well as your husband? Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot deem it improper while I remain with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cBless me! that is an excellent idea, really excellent. To be sure, I may just as well go as stay, for I am of no use at home\u2014am I?\u2014and it only worries me. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s feelings, are infinitely the fitter person. You can manage little Charles in anything; he obeys you instantly. It will be far preferable to leaving him solely with Jemima. Oh! I shall certainly go; I am sure I ought, if I have the chance, quite as much as Charles, for they are desperately anxious I should become acquainted with Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not object to being left alone. A capital idea of yours, indeed, Anne. I will go tell Charles and get ready at once. You can have us sent for, you know, at a moment\u2019s warning if anything is amiss; but I dare say nothing will alarm you. I would not go, you may rely on it, if I did not feel entirely at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next instant she was tapping at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and Anne, following her upstairs, arrived in time for the whole discussion, which opened with Mary\u2019s saying, in triumphant tones\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, for I am no more use at home than you. If I were to confine myself forever with the child, I could not induce him to do anything he disliked. Anne will remain; Anne pledges herself to stay at home and care for him. It is Anne\u2019s own scheme, and so I shall accompany you, which will be far better, for I have not dined at their house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very considerate of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be delighted to have you go; but it seems somewhat unfair that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
