          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3309894a9e9f860f17029d75\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I could still recall how matters stood during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. Up North a carpenter earned three dollars a day in gold value; down South he received fifty\u2014paid in Confederate shinplasters that were worth about a dollar a bushel. Up North a pair of overalls cost three dollars\u2014equal to a day\u2019s wages; in the South they cost seventy-five\u2014which meant two days\u2019 pay. Everything else kept the same ratio. So wages really stood twice as high in the North, because the northern wage bought just twice what the southern one could. \\n\\nSure enough, I made several acquaintances in the little village, and it pleased me immensely to see our fresh coins moving about\u2014plenty of milrays, plenty of mills, plenty of cents, a fair lot of nickels, and some silver; all in the hands of the artisans and common folk generally; yes, even a trace of gold\u2014but that lay in the bank, meaning the goldsmith\u2019s shop. I stepped in while Marco, son of Marco, was dickering with a shopman over a quarter pound of salt, and I asked for change for a twenty-dollar gold piece. They obliged me\u2014after first gnawing the coin a bit, ringing it on the counter, touching it with acid, and demanding where I had got it, who I was, whence I hailed, whither I was bound, when I expected to arrive, and perhaps a couple hundred more inquiries. And when their questions ran dry, I kept right on and volunteered all sorts of details; told them I owned a dog named Watch, that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, her grandfather a Prohibitionist, and that I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip who died hopeful of a glorious resurrection, and so forth, and so forth, and so forth, until even that famished village inquisitor began to look content\u2014and a trifle annoyed. Still he had to show due deference to a man of my financial standing, so he offered no impudence, though I noticed he vented it on his assistants, which was perfectly natural. Yes, they broke my twenty, yet I could tell it strained the bank a little; and that was no surprise, for it was like walking into a shabby country store back in the nineteenth century and suddenly asking the proprietor to break a two-thousand-dollar note. He could manage it, perhaps, but he would still marvel how a modest farmer came to be lugging such a sum in his pocket\u2014which was likely what this goldsmith wondered, too; for he followed me to the doorway and stood there staring after me with reverent admiration.\"}"
        }
