          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"27785ef5053f0beec69d01a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want you to stay well connected, and to do that it is best to have as few odd acquaintances as possible. So if you are still here when Mr. Martin marries, I hope you will not let your friendship with his sisters draw you into knowing his wife, who will likely be a farmer\u2019s daughter with no education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course. Yes. Not that I think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who had not had some schooling and been brought up well. Still, I am not going to argue with you, and I am sure I will not wish for his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I will always think highly of the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and I would be very sorry to give them up, for they are as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, coarse woman, of course I had better not visit her, if I can help it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her through this wavering speech and saw no worrying signs of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted he had no other hold on her and that Harriet would offer no serious resistance to any friendly plans she might make.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day while walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking very respectfully at Emma, he gazed with clear pleasure at her companion. Emma was glad to have the chance to inspect him, and by walking a few yards ahead while they talked, soon satisfied her sharp eye about Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was neat, and he looked like a sensible young man, but there was no other advantage, and she felt sure that when compared with real gentlemen he would lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s regard. Harriet was sensitive to manners; she had often admired her father\u2019s gentleness with both admiration and surprise. Mr. Martin looked as though he did not even know what manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting, and Harriet came running back with a smiling face and fluttering spirits, which Miss Woodhouse hoped to calm soon.\\n\\n\u201cOnly think of us happening to meet him! How very strange! He said it was pure chance that he had not gone round by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked this road. He thought we usually walked toward Randalls.\u201d\"}"
