{"request_id":"4f8c0fdb52d7ee2c839c703b","rewritten_text":"He had also to consider Sybil’s parents, rather old-fashioned people who might well oppose the marriage if any whiff of scandal arose, though he was convinced that once they knew the entire story they would be the first to understand the motives that guided him. Every reason, therefore, urged him towards poison. It was secure, certain, noiseless, and spared all those painful scenes which, like most Englishmen, he detested with a rooted aversion.

Yet about toxicology he was utterly ignorant, and as the waiter proved incapable of locating anything in the library beyond Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine, he explored the shelves himself, eventually finding a finely bound Pharmacopoeia and Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members, elected by mistake for someone else—a contretemps that so infuriated the Committee that when the proper candidate appeared they blackballed him to a man. Lord Arthur found the technical jargon in both volumes rather bewildering, and was beginning to lament not having taken more pains with his classics at Oxford when, in Erskine’s second volume, he came upon a particularly lucid and thorough account of aconitine’s properties, written in reasonably plain English. It struck him as precisely the poison he needed: rapid—almost instantaneous in its action—completely painless, and in the form of a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, by no means unpleasant. He therefore jotted on his shirt-cuff the quantity required for a fatal dose, returned the books to their shelves, and sauntered up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the renowned chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always attended personally to aristocratic customers, was greatly astonished by the request and murmured deferentially that a medical certificate would be necessary. However, once Lord Arthur explained that he needed it for a large Norwegian mastiff that had to be dispatched because it was showing early signs of rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice on the calf, Mr. Pestle declared himself quite satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur slipped the capsule into a dainty little silver bonbonnière he noticed in a Bond Street shop-window, tossed Pestle and Humbey’s unsightly pill-box away, and drove straight to Lady Clementina’s."}

