          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b9c0cec4802c39f8492dc244\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once we had seen our friend securely installed in his new post, our thoughts shifted toward Windsor. The place lay so near to London that parting from Raymond and Perdita hardly felt like a true separation. We bade them farewell within the Protectoral Palace. It delighted me to watch my sister seem to enter into the spirit of a performance, striving to sustain her rank with suitable grace. Her innate pride and humility of demeanour clashed now more than ever. Her nervousness was genuine, born from a fear of being undervalued, from that slight regard for the world\u2019s neglect which Raymond also possessed. Yet Perdita thought of others more than he ever did, and some of her shyness stemmed from wanting to ease any sense of inferiority in those around her\u2014an impression that never visited her own mind. Idris, by her birth and education, was better suited to ceremonial routine, although the same ease that made such actions instinctive for her also rendered them dull. Despite every drawback, Perdita plainly relished her role. Brimming with fresh ideas, she scarcely felt sorrow when we left. She embraced us warmly, promised to come soon, and did not lament the circumstances that separated us. Raymond\u2019s spirits were irrepressible; he scarcely knew how to employ his newly acquired power. His mind teemed with schemes, though none were settled, and he vowed that the era of his Protectorship would be marked by some deed of sublime renown. Thus we spoke of them, moralizing as we made our reduced way back to Windsor Castle. We rejoiced at our escape from political tempests and pursued solitude with heightened eagerness. Though our occupations were not lacking, my restless nature now strove only in the realm of intellectual effort; intense study proved a fine remedy to soothe the fever of spirit that idleness would surely have provoked. Perdita allowed Clara to accompany us to Windsor, and she, along with my two beautiful infants, became constant sources of fascination and delight.\\n\\nOnly Adrian\u2019s health troubled our serenity. It plainly declined without any symptom to suggest the cause, unless his luminous eyes, animated expression, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption; yet he felt neither pain nor dread. He threw himself ardently into books, finding respite from study in the company he cherished most\u2014his sister and myself.\"}"
