          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a6157446fbec168a4ea8571b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis had agreed to retain at Lady Canterville\u2019s earnest request. She made them each a deep curtsey as they stepped down and said in a quaint, old-fashioned tone, \u201cI welcome you to Canterville Chase.\u201d Following her lead, they walked through the grand Tudor hall into the library, a long, low room paneled in black oak with a large stained-glass window at the far end. There tea was already laid out for them, and after removing their wraps, they sat down and began to look around while Mrs. Umney served them.\\n\\nSuddenly Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain on the floor beside the fireplace and, unaware of its true meaning, said to Mrs. Umney, \u201cI\u2019m afraid something has been spilled there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, madam,\u201d replied the old housekeeper quietly, \u201cblood was spilled on that spot.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow dreadful,\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Otis. \u201cI dislike bloodstains in a sitting room. It must be removed at once.\u201d\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and answered in the same hushed, mysterious tone, \u201cIt is the blood of Lady Eleanor de Canterville, who was murdered on that very spot by her husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon outlived her by nine years and then vanished under very mysterious circumstances. His body was never found, but his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. The bloodstain has been greatly admired by tourists and cannot be removed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s nonsense,\u201d declared Washington Otis. \u201cPinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will clean it up in no time.\u201d Before the frightened housekeeper could stop him, he dropped to his knees and began vigorously scrubbing the floor with a small stick that looked like black cosmetic. In moments the bloodstain had vanished.\\n\\n\u201cI knew Pinkerton would handle it,\u201d he said triumphantly, glancing around at his admiring family\u2014but no sooner had he spoken than a terrible flash of lightning lit the gloomy room, a thunderclap made them all jump to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u201cWhat a monstrous climate,\u201d said the American Minister calmly as he lit a long cheroot. \u201cI suppose the old country is so crowded they don\u2019t have enough decent weather for everyone. I have always thought emigration is the only solution for England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Hiram,\u201d cried Mrs. Otis, \u201cwhat can we do with a woman who faints?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCharge it to her like breakages,\u201d answered the Minister. \u201cShe won\u2019t faint again after that.\u201d And in a few moments Mrs. Umney indeed revived.\"}"
