          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"079312abe279ab1a0487f3b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The stupor and astonishment this extraordinary turn of events produced in me was perhaps, when all is said and done, the least explainable part of the adventure. The sudden shift itself was natural and inevitable, and I had long expected it to occur the moment I reached the point in my voyage where the planet\u2019s pull would be replaced by that of the satellite\u2014or, more accurately, when the balloon\u2019s attraction toward Earth became weaker than its attraction toward the moon. Admittedly, I woke from a deep sleep with my senses in disorder to find a startling phenomenon that, although anticipated, caught me off guard at that immediate moment. The reversal must have happened gently and gradually, and it is far from certain that, had I been awake, I would have noticed any internal sign of the inversion\u2014no discomfort, no disruption either to myself or to my equipment. \\n\\nIt goes almost without saying that, once I fully grasped my situation and emerged from the terror that had gripped every faculty of my soul, my attention was first fixed entirely on the moon\u2019s general physical appearance. It lay beneath me like a map\u2014and although I judged it still to be at a considerable distance, the indentations of its surface appeared to my eyes with a striking and inexplicable clarity. The complete absence of ocean or sea\u2014or even lakes, rivers, or any body of water whatsoever\u2014stood out at first glance as the most extraordinary aspect of its geology. Yet, strangely enough, I saw vast flat regions unmistakably alluvial, although most of the hemisphere visible to me was covered with countless volcanic mountains, conical in shape, and looking more like artificial structures than natural formations. The highest of these rises no more than three and three-quarter miles vertically; but a chart of the volcanic districts of the Campi Phlegraei would give you a better sense of their general surface than any poor description I could attempt. Most of them were clearly erupting, making me dread their fury and power with the repeated thunder of the so-called meteoric stones, which now shot upward past the balloon with increasing, terrifying frequency.\"}"
