          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fc7437b957c7ca43768f836d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no laughter in the little town that same calm Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s household were being dressed in mourning, overwhelmed with grief and tears. A strange quiet settled over the village, though heaven knows it was usually quiet enough. The villagers went about their business with distant looks and spoke little, but sighed often. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children. They had no heart for their games and slowly abandoned them. \\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher found herself wandering gloomily around the empty schoolyard, feeling very sad. But nothing there eased her sorrow. She murmured to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if only I still had that brass andiron knob! But I don\u2019t have anything left to remember him by.\u201d She swallowed back a small sob. \\n\\nSoon she stopped and said quietly:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that again\u2014not for the whole world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d \\n\\nThat thought broke her, and she drifted away with tears streaming down her cheeks. Then a band of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014came by, leaned over the picket fence, and spoke in solemn tones about how Tom did this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe said this little thing or that (which now, they were sure, held dreadful meaning!)\u2014and each one pointed to the exact spot where the missing boys had stood, then added something like \u201cand I was standing right here\u2014just like this, and if you were him\u2014I was that close\u2014and he smiled, just like this\u2014and then something washed over me, like\u2014terrifying, you know\u2014and I never guessed what it meant, of course, but now I understand!\u201d \\n\\nThen they argued about who saw the dead boys last, and many laid claim to that gloomy honor and offered proof, somewhat embellished to favor the storyteller. When it was finally settled who truly had seen the departed last and exchanged their final words, the fortunate winners took on a sort of sacred importance, and the rest stared at them with envy. One poor fellow, having no other glory to brag about, said with very evident pride: \\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer beat me up once.\u201d \\n\\nBut that bid for fame was a failure. Most of the boys could say the same, so it cheapened the distinction too much. The group drifted away, still sharing memories of the lost heroes in hushed voices.\"}"
