          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12931bf6a5ace348f8222f51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Right in front of him, in the pale moonlight, stood an old man of terrifying appearance. His eyes glowed like burning coals; long gray hair hung over his shoulders in tangled ropes; his antique-cut clothes were filthy and torn, and heavy manacles and rusty shackles dangled from his wrists and ankles.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear sir,\u201d said Mr. Otis, \u201cI really must insist that you oil those chains, and I have brought you a small bottle of Tammany Rising Sun Lubricator for that purpose. They say one application works completely, and there are several testimonials on the label from our most distinguished homegrown divines. I\u2019ll leave it here by the bedroom candles, and I\u2019ll be happy to provide more if you need it.\u201d With that, the United States Minister set the bottle on a marble table, shut his door, and went to bed.\\n\\nFor a moment the Canterville ghost stood perfectly still in natural outrage; then he smashed the bottle against the polished floor and raced down the corridor, groaning hollowly and radiating a ghastly green glow. But just as he reached the top of the great oak staircase, a door flew open, two little white-robed figures appeared, and a large pillow whizzed past his head! There was clearly no time to waste, so he quickly slipped into the Fourth Dimension of Space as a way of escape, vanished through the wainscoting, and the house fell silent.\\n\\nWhen he reached a small hidden chamber in the left wing, he leaned against a moonbeam to catch his breath and tried to absorb what had happened. Never in a brilliant and uninterrupted three-hundred-year career had he been so grossly insulted. He thought of the Dowager Duchess, whom he had scared into a fit as she stood before her mirror in lace and diamonds; of the four housemaids who had fallen into hysterics when he merely grinned at them through the spare bedroom curtains; of the parish rector whose candle h