{"request_id":"148cf16ca4563b3ef984cc0e","rewritten_text":"It was only after some time had passed that I collected myself enough to see to the routine concerns of the balloon. When I did so, however, I inspected it carefully, and to my immense consolation discovered it unharmed. All my apparatus remained secure, and, luckily, neither ballast nor provisions were missing. In fact, I had fastened them so reliably in their places that such a mishap was never to be apprehended. Consulting my watch, I found the hour to be six. I was still climbing swiftly, and my barometer marked a current elevation of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me, far out on the ocean, lay a small black shape, slightly elongated, looking exactly like one of those children’s playthings termed dominoes. Turning my telescope toward it, I clearly saw it was a British ninety-four-gun ship, hauled close to the wind and plunging hard in the waves with her bow toward the west-southwest. Apart from this vessel, there was nothing in sight but sea, sky, and the sun, which had risen long since.

“It is the moment that I should lay before your Excellencies the aim of my hazardous journey. Your Excellencies will remember that straitened circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to a resolve of self-destruction. It was not that I found life itself utterly loathsome, but rather that I was plagued past endurance by the incidental miseries attached to my condition. In this frame of mind, desiring to live yet exhausted with living, the treatise I saw on the bookseller’s stall opened a new avenue to my fancy. I then came to a final decision. I resolved to go away yet endure—to leave the world yet continue conscious—in short, to cut through riddles, I made up my mind, whatever might follow, to force my way, if possible, to the moon. Now, lest you should think me more insane than I truly am, I shall set forth, as best I can, the arguments which persuaded me that such an enterprise, though undeniably arduous and full of peril, was not, for a daring spirit, utterly outside the bounds of the achievable.

“The actual remoteness of the moon from the earth was the point to be considered first. The mean or average span between the centers of the two planets is 59.9643 of Earth’s equatorial radii, or roughly 237,000 miles. I emphasize the mean interval, yet it must be remembered that the moon’s path, being an ellipse with an eccentricity as great as 0.05484 of the major semi-axis, and the earth’s center lying in one focus, if I could somehow contrive to encounter the moon, as it were, at its perigee, the distance just mentioned would be considerably reduced.”"}

