          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82dd230d9e4a3d71a78d0858\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat, then, has ensued? What impression have I made on your imagination?\u201d Dupin asked, and a chill ran through me. \u201cA lunatic,\u201d I replied, \u201ccommitted this crime\u2014some raving madman escaped from a nearby asylum.\u201d \u201cIn certain respects,\u201d he said, \u201cyour idea isn\u2019t irrelevant. Yet even the wildest ravings of the insane never match the peculiar voice heard on the stairs. Every madman belongs to some nation, and their language\u2014however incoherent\u2014still keeps the coherence of syllables. Besides, the hair of a madman isn\u2019t like the tuft I\u2019m holding. I disentangled this little tuft from the tightly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d \u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, thoroughly shaken. \u201cThis hair is extremely unusual\u2014it isn\u2019t human.\u201d \u201cI haven\u2019t claimed it is,\u201d he replied. \u201cBut before deciding, look at the sketch I\u2019ve drawn on this paper. It\u2019s an exact copy of what one witness described as \u2018dark bruises and deep indentations of finger nails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what Messrs. Dumas and Etienne called \u2018a series of livid spots, clearly the impression of fingers.\u2019 \u201cYou\u2019ll see,\u201d he continued, spreading the paper on the table, \u201cthat the drawing conveys the sense of a firm, locked grip. There\u2019s no sign of slipping. Each finger kept\u2014perhaps until the victim\u2019s death\u2014the terrible hold it first took. Now, try to place all your fingers at once in the impressions as they appear.\u201d I tried, but in vain. \u201cPerhaps we\u2019re not giving this a fair test,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies flat, while a human throat is cylindrical. Here\u2019s a billet of wood with about the same circumference. Wrap the drawing around it and try again.\u201d I did so, and the difficulty was even more obvious. \u201cThese,\u201d I said, \u201care not the marks of any human hand.\u201d \u201cRead this,\u201d Dupin answered, handing me a passage from Cuvier. It was a precise anatomical and descriptive account of the large tawny Orangutan of the East Indies. The enormous size, incredible strength and agility, savage ferocity, and imitative tendencies of these animals are known to everyone. I grasped the horror of the murders at once. \u201cThe description of the fingers,\u201d I said when I finished reading, \u201cmatches this drawing exactly.\u201d\"}"
        }
