          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c456ccb40c0efc47049e737\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I had tucked my handkerchief and a few other things into my helmet, which turned out to be the kind you can\u2019t take off by yourself. That hadn\u2019t occurred to me when I loaded it up; I didn\u2019t even know it at the time. I thought it would be especially handy there. So now the thought of the handkerchief sitting right there, within reach but impossible to touch, made everything worse and harder to endure. The thing you can\u2019t get is the thing you want most; everyone knows that. My mind locked onto that handkerchief, mile after mile, picturing it and longing for it, while the salty sweat trickled into my eyes and I couldn\u2019t wipe it away. It might sound minor on paper, but it was real misery. I wouldn\u2019t say it if it weren\u2019t so. I decided I\u2019d carry a handbag next time, no matter how it looked or what people said. Those iron-clad Round Table fellows would call it scandalous and raise a fuss, but I say comfort first, style later. We jogged along, and now and then we hit dusty patches that billowed into clouds, stinging my nose and making me sneeze and cry, and yes, I swore when I shouldn\u2019t have. I\u2019m no better than anyone else. We couldn\u2019t seem to meet a soul in this lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in, that was lucky for any ogre who had a handkerchief. Most knights would go for his armor, but if I could get his bandanna, he could keep his hardware for all I cared. Meanwhile, it kept getting hotter in there. The sun beat down, heating the iron more every minute. When you\u2019re hot like that, every little thing grates on you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, which annoyed me; and I couldn\u2019t bear the shield flapping and banging, first against my chest, then against my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints squeaked and screeched like a wheelbarrow, and without the breeze from moving faster, I was roasting in that stove. On top of that, the slower I went, the heavier the armor seemed, as if it were piling on more tons every minute.\"}"
