          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"40163624a860aab8a01a4fcc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But no, something even more dazzling came to him. He would be a pirate! That was it! Now his future lay open before him, blazing with unimaginable glory. His name would shake the world and make people shudder. He would sweep across the bright, choppy seas in his long, low, black-hulled racer, the Spirit of the Storm, with its grim flag flying at the mast. At the height of his fame he would suddenly appear in the old village and stride into church, sun-darkened and weather-beaten, in his black velvet doublet and trunks, huge jackboots, crimson sash, belt stuffed with pistols, crime-stained cutlass at his side, slouch hat with waving plumes, black flag spread wide with skull and crossbones, and he would listen in swelling delight to the whispers, \u201cIt\u2019s Tom Sawyer the Pirate\u2014the Black Avenger of the Spanish Main!\u201d\\n\\nYes, it was settled; his path was chosen. He would run away from home and begin at once. He would leave the very next morning. So he had to get ready now. He would gather his supplies. He went to a rotten log nearby and began digging under one end of it with his Barlow knife. Soon he hit wood that sounded hollow. He slipped his hand in and recited in a solemn voice:\\n\\n\u201cWhat hasn\u2019t come here, come! What\u2019s here, stay here!\u201d\\n\\nThen he scraped away the dirt and uncovered a pine shingle. He lifted it and revealed a neat little treasure-box whose bottom and sides were made of shingles. Inside lay a marble. Tom\u2019s astonishment had no bounds. He scratched his head in confusion and said:\\n\\n\u201cWell, that beats anything!\u201d\\n\\nThen he flung the marble away in annoyance and stood there thinking. The truth was that one of his superstitions had failed, a superstition he and all his comrades had considered foolproof. If you buried a marble with the required spell, left it alone for two weeks, and then used the incantation he had just spoken to open the spot, you were supposed to find every marble you had ever lost gathered there, no matter where they had been scattered. But now, the charm had plainly failed. Tom\u2019s whole belief system was rocked. He had often heard of the trick succeeding, never of it failing. It never crossed his mind that he himself had tried it several times before and then could not locate the hiding places afterward. He puzzled over it for a while and finally decided some witch had meddled and broken the spell.\"}"
