{"request_id":"056dc0daddaae41e006e5949","rewritten_text":"Among the public buildings in a certain town—which I will wisely leave unnamed and will not even disguise—there stands one that most cities large or small have in common: namely, a workhouse. In that workhouse there was born, on a day and date I need not repeat since it matters little to the reader at this stage, the tiny addition to mortality whose name heads this chapter.

For a long time after the parish surgeon ushered the child into this world of sorrow and trouble, there was significant doubt whether he would live long enough to receive any name at all. Had that happened, these memoirs would probably never have appeared; or, if they had, they might have fit onto two pages and earned the priceless distinction of being the shortest and most accurate biography in the literature of any era or nation.

While I won’t claim that being born in a workhouse is the luckiest or most enviable event that can befall a human being, I will say that in this particular case it was the best thing that could have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth is, it was extremely difficult to persuade Oliver to assume the duty of breathing—a troublesome habit, but one that custom has made necessary for our comfortable existence. For some time he lay gasping on a little flock mattress, hanging rather unevenly between this world and the next, with the scale decidedly tipped toward the latter. Now, if during that brief interval Oliver had been surrounded by attentive grandmothers, anxious aunts, seasoned nurses, and doctors of immense wisdom, he would undoubtedly have been killed in no time. But as there was no one present except a pauper old woman, made a bit hazy by an unusual ration of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such business on contract, Oliver and Nature worked the matter out between them. The outcome was that, after a few struggles, Oliver drew breath, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been laid upon the parish by raising as loud a cry as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who had owned that very useful appendage, a voice, for no longer than three minutes and fifteen seconds."}

