          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da83665cd387b745dda8d7e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so thoroughly content with this conversation that he never again troubled himself, or irked his dear sister Elizabeth, by referring to it; and she was gratified to learn she had said enough to keep him still. The day for his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived, and Mrs. Bennet had to yield to a parting which, as her husband would not hear of their all traveling to Newcastle, promised to last at least a twelvemonth. \u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she exclaimed, \u201cwhen shall we meet once more?\u201d \u201cOh, Lord! I cannot tell. Not these two or three years, perhaps.\u201d \u201cWrite to me often, my dear.\u201d \u201cAs often as I am able. But you know married women have little leisure for writing. My sisters may write to me. They will have nothing else to occupy them.\u201d Mr. Wickham\u2019s farewells proved far more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked charming, and uttered many graceful phrases. \u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, once they were out of the house, \u201cas ever I beheld. He simpers, he smirks, and he pays court to us all. I am prodigiously proud of him. I defy Sir William Lucas himself to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d The loss of her daughter rendered Mrs. Bennet very dull for several days. \u201cI often think,\u201d said she, \u201cthat parting with one\u2019s friends is the worst of all. One feels so forlorn without them.\u201d \u201cThis, you see, madam, is the result of marrying a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt should reconcile you to the single condition of your remaining four.\u201d \u201cNothing of the sort. Lydia is not leaving me because she is married; only because her husband\u2019s regiment lies so far away. Had it been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d Yet the low spirits into which this affair had cast her were soon relieved, and her mind reopened to the flutter of hope, by a report then beginning to circulate. The housekeeper at Netherfield had received orders to get ready for her master, who was coming down in a day or two, to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was quite in a fret. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head alternately. \u201cWell, well, and so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for Mrs. Philips first conveyed the tidings). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care in the least. He is nothing to us now, you know, and I am sure I never desire to see him again. Still, he is perfectly welcome to come to Netherfield, if it suits him.\u201d\"}"
        }
