          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c74fbbdadbee494bf1e6efd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my youth, discontent never touched my mind, and if I ever felt ennui, the sight of nature\u2019s beauty or the study of humanity\u2019s finest achievements always stirred my heart and revived my spirits. Now I am like a blasted tree; the bolt has struck my soul, and I felt even then that I would live on only to display what I shall soon stop being\u2014a miserable ruin of a person, pitied by others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe lingered at Oxford for quite a while, wandering through its surroundings and trying to find every place linked to the most inspiring era of English history. Our little voyages of discovery often stretched on because of the many sights that appeared. We visited the tomb of the great Hampden and the field where that patriot fell. For a moment my spirit rose above its degrading fears to consider the divine ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice these places honored and recalled. For an instant I dared to cast off my chains and look around with a free and noble spirit, but the iron had already eaten into my flesh, and I sank again, trembling and hopeless, into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford reluctantly and moved on to Matlock, our next resting place. The countryside near this village resembled the scenery of Switzerland more closely, but everything was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the distant white Alps that always rise around the pine-covered mountains of my homeland. We visited the miraculous cave and the little natural history cabinets, where the curiosities are arranged just as they are in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. Hearing the latter name spoken by Henry made me shudder, and I hurried to leave Matlock, which was now tied in my mind to that terrible experience.\\n\\nFrom Derby, still traveling north, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine myself back among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow clinging to the mountains\u2019 northern sides, the lakes, and the crashing rocky streams were all familiar and beloved sights. Here we also met a few acquaintances who nearly managed to trick me into happiness. Clerval\u2019s joy was far greater than mine; his mind grew in the company of talented men, and he discovered within himself far more abilities and resources than he had ever guessed he possessed while he kept company with those beneath him.\"}"
