          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dc4ec2241d1ac057048a7b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even reading about the luxuries of the dead was astonishing. Then he turned his attention to embroideries and to tapestries that served as frescoes in the chilly rooms of northern Europe. As he delved into the subject\u2014and he had an extraordinary talent for becoming completely absorbed in whatever he took up\u2014he was almost saddened by the thought of the ruin time inflicted on beautiful and marvelous things. He, at least, had escaped that. Summer followed summer, yellow jonquils bloomed and died again and again, nights of horror repeated their tale of shame, yet he remained unchanged. No winter scarred his face or dulled his flower-like complexion. How different it was with material things! Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus-colored robe on which the gods battled the giants, worked by brown-skinned girls for Athena\u2019s pleasure? Where was the massive velarium that Nero stretched across the Colosseum in Rome, that titanic purple sail representing the starry sky and Apollo driving a chariot drawn by white, gilt-reined horses? He longed to see the curious napkins crafted for the Priest of the Sun, showing every delicacy and dish a feast might require; the burial cloth of King Chilperic, adorned with three hundred golden bees; the fantastic robes that outraged the Bishop of Pontus, covered with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014everything, in fact, that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat Charles of Orleans once wore, with sleeves embroidered with a song that began \u201cMadame, je suis tout joyeux,\u201d the musical accompaniment wrought in gold thread and each square-shaped note formed with four pearls. He read about the room prepared in the palace at Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrots, made in embroidery and blazoned with the king\u2019s arms, and five hundred and sixty-one butterflies whose wings were likewise adorned with the queen\u2019s arms, all worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de\u2019 Medici had a mourning bed made of black velvet scattered with crescents and suns. Its curtains were damask, patterned with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold and silver ground, fringed along the edges with pearl embroidery, and it stood in a room hung with rows of the queen\u2019s emblems in cut black velvet on cloth of silver. Louis XIV had gold-embroidered caryatids fifteen feet high in his apartment.\"}"
