          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"559019709cef518b29534d22\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After dinner the whole gang went out to hunt turtle eggs on the sandbar. They jabbed sticks into the sand, and when they found a soft spot they dropped to their knees and dug with their hands. Sometimes they pulled fifty or sixty eggs from one hole. The eggs were round, white, and a little smaller than an English walnut. That night they had a famous fried-egg feast, and another on Friday morning.\\n\\nAfter breakfast they ran yelling and dancing onto the bar, chasing each other in circles, peeling off their clothes as they went until they were naked. Then they kept up the game far up the shallow water of the bar, fighting the strong current, which kept knocking their legs out from under them and made everything even funnier. Now and then they bent together and splashed water into each other\u2019s faces with their palms, edging closer with their faces turned away so they would not choke on the spray. Finally they grabbed each other and wrestled until the winner dunked his friend, and then they all went under in a knot of pale arms and legs and came up at once, blowing, sputtering, laughing, and gasping for air.\\n\\nWhen they were worn out, they ran ashore, sprawled on the dry hot sand, and covered themselves with it, only to bolt for the water again and repeat the whole show. At last it struck them that their bare skin looked like flesh-colored tights; so they traced a ring in the sand and staged a circus\u2014with three clowns, because none of them would surrender that proud role.\\n\\nNext they fetched their marbles and played knucks and ringtaw and keeps until they got bored. Then Joe and Huck took another swim, but Tom refused because when he had kicked off his trousers he had also kicked off his string of rattlesnake rattles, and he wondered how he had avoided cramps so long without that mysterious charm. He would not go back into the water until he found it, and by then the other boys were tired and ready to rest. They drifted apart, grew listless, and stared across the wide river toward the village dozing in the sun. Tom caught himself writing \u201cBECKY\u201d in the sand with his big toe; he erased it and scolded himself for being weak. But he wrote it again anyway; he could not stop himself. He wiped it out once more and then pulled himself away from temptation by herding the other boys together and joining them.\"}"
