{"request_id":"923ba932659c44274211c251","rewritten_text":"Spirits of the storm, take me in! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever—unless some gentler force has set another end for me, so that after long endurance I might earn my reward and once more feel my heart beat beside another heart like mine.

The Tiber, that road laid by nature’s own hand across her continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were tied along its banks. With a few books, provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and drift down the current to the sea; then, staying close to shore, I would follow the beautiful coasts and sunny promontories of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and Calabria, and dare the twin threats of Scylla and Charybdis. Then, aiming without fear—for what have I left to lose?—I would skim the ocean toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, because the sight of its familiar towers belongs to a different life than this. I would hug the coasts of Asia Minor and Syria, and, passing the seven mouths of the Nile, turn north again until, losing sight of forgotten Carthage and empty Libya, I reached the Pillars of Hercules. After that—anywhere. The slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean might become my home before I finish this long journey, or disease might strike my heart as I drift alone on the heaving Mediterranean. Or, in some harbor where I dock, I might find what I seek—a companion. If not—if time stretches on endlessly, me decrepit and gray while youth lies buried with those I love—the solitary wanderer will still set his sail and grip the tiller. Still obeying the winds of heaven, he will round promontory after promontory, anchor in bay after bay, still furrow the barren sea, leaving behind the green homeland of Europe, along the tawny shore of Africa; after surviving the fierce waters of the Cape, I might tie up my worn skiff in a creek shaded by spice groves on the fragrant isles of the far Indian Ocean.

These are wild fantasies. Yet ever since they struck me a week ago as I stood atop St. Peter’s, they have ruled my imagination. I have chosen my boat and stocked it with my meager supplies. I have selected a few books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare—though every library in the world is open to me, and in any port I can restock. I expect no change for the better, yet the monotonous present is unbearable to me."}

