          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fcea21d87a5fee3184f99e70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man, real pleasant and kind-looking when I roused him. I told him my brother and I had been hunting over on Spanish Island yesterday afternoon, camped on part of a raft we\u2019d found, and around midnight he must have kicked his gun in his sleep, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted the doctor to go over there and patch him up without saying anything to anyone, since we planned to come home this evening and surprise the family.\\n\\n\u201cWho is your family?\u201d he asks.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. After a moment he asks, \u201cHow\u2019d you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I say, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOdd dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nThen he lit his lantern, grabbed his saddle-bags, and we headed out. But when he saw the canoe he didn\u2019t like the look of it\u2014said it was big enough for one but didn\u2019t seem safe for two. I say:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you don\u2019t need to be scared, sir; it carried all three of us just fine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns; that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nStill, he put his foot on the gunwale and rocked it, shook his head, and said he figured he\u2019d look for a bigger one. But they were all locked and chained, so he took my canoe instead. He told me to wait till he got back, or I could look around more, or maybe I ought to go home and get everyone ready for the surprise if I wanted. But I said I didn\u2019t, so I told him exactly how to find the raft, and then he left.\\n\\nAn idea hit me pretty soon. I says to myself, suppose he can\u2019t fix that leg in a snap like folks say? suppose it takes him three or four days? What would we do\u2014stay there till he lets the secret out? No way; I knew what I\u2019d do. I\u2019d wait, and if he came back saying he had to go again, I\u2019d get down there too, even if I had to swim. We\u2019d tie him up, keep him, shove out down the river, and when Tom was done with him we\u2019d pay him what it was worth, or everything we had, and then let him make it to shore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a lumber pile to sleep, and the next time I woke the sun was already high overhead. I jumped up and ran to the doctor\u2019s house, but they said he\u2019d gone out sometime in the night and hadn\u2019t come back yet.\"}"
