          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f8977f231b325afd161f22c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If it hadn\u2019t been for me, he\u2019d never have gotten out. Morgan le Fay hated him with everything in her, and she never would have softened. Yet his offense was more careless than deliberately wicked. He had said she had red hair. Well, she did; but that wasn\u2019t the way to describe it. When red-haired people belong to a certain social level, their hair is called auburn.\\n\\nThink of it: among these forty-seven captives there were five whose names, crimes, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known. One woman and four men\u2014each stooped, wrinkled, their minds gone, like ancient relics. They themselves had long since forgotten those details; at best they nursed vague guesses about them, never the same twice. The succession of priests whose duty it was to pray daily with the captives and remind them that God had placed them there for some wise reason, teaching them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were the virtues He prized in people of lower rank, had traditions about these crumbling human ruins, but nothing more. Those traditions went only so far; they dealt with duration of imprisonment, not the nature of the offenses. Even with tradition, the only fact that could be proven was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer the darkness had lasted was beyond conjecture. The king and queen knew nothing about these poor souls except that they were heirlooms, assets inherited along with the throne from the previous regime. Nothing of their history had been handed down with their persons, so the inheriting rulers considered them worthless and felt no interest in them. I asked the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why on earth didn\u2019t you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question stumped her. She didn\u2019t know why she hadn\u2019t; the idea had simply never crossed her mind. So there she was, foreshadowing the true history of future prisoners of the Ch\u00e2teau d\u2019If without realizing it. It was now clear to me that, with her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were merely property\u2014nothing more, nothing less. And when we inherit property, it doesn\u2019t occur to us to throw it away, even when we don\u2019t value it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats into the open world and the harsh afternoon sun\u2014after blindfolding them out of pity for eyes so long unused to light\u2014they were a spectacle. Walking skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, sad terrors, every one of them; the truest possible offspring of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
        }
