          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"696690b86da8202a123e8fc3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI do not have the nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really feel comfortable spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; his father can do it, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour how he is. I honestly think Charles could have told his father we would all come. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly frightened yesterday, but today is very different.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it too late to let them know for yourself, suppose you go as well as your husband? Leave little Charles to my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d Mary cried, her eyes lighting up. \u201cGoodness, that is a very good idea, very good indeed. Of course I might as well go as not, because I am no use at home, am I? It only aggravates me. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s instincts, are much the better person. You can get little Charles to do anything; he always obeys you immediately. It will be much better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I will certainly go; I am sure I should, if I can, just as much as Charles, because they are desperate for me to get to know Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. A wonderful idea of yours, Anne. I will go tell Charles and get ready at once. You can send for us, you know, in a moment if anything is wrong; but I dare say nothing will upset you. I would not go, you may be sure, if I were not completely at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was knocking on her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she caught the entire conversation, which began with Mary saying, in a triumphant tone\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am going with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. Even if I shut myself up with the child forever, I could not make him do anything he dislikes. Anne will stay; Anne has offered to stay home and take care of him. It is Anne\u2019s own suggestion, so I will go with you, which is much better, because I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband answered, \u201cand I would be very glad to have you go; but it still feels rather unfair that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
