          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52837843cfa50948cd266597\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And if he asked why I wanted it, or why I thought I had any right to it, I would tell him\u2014though he cared little for such poor dreams\u2014that I had loved Estella dearly and for a long time, and that though I had lost her and must live a bereaved life, anything concerning her was still closer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. Seeing Mr. Jaggers stand silent and apparently unmoved by this plea, I turned to Wemmick and said, \u201cWemmick, I know you to be a man with a kind heart. I have seen your pleasant home, your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful, playful ways with which you lighten your workday. I beg you to speak for me to Mr. Jaggers and tell him that, considering all the circumstances, he ought to be more open with me.\u201d\\n\\nI had never seen two men look more strangely at each other than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this. At first, I feared Wemmick would be dismissed on the spot; but that fear faded as Mr. Jaggers relaxed into something like a smile, and Wemmick grew more confident.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s all this?\u201d asked Mr. Jaggers. \u201cYou with an old father, and you with pleasant and playful ways?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d answered Wemmick. \u201cIf I don\u2019t bring them here, what does it matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, placing his hand on my arm and smiling openly, \u201cthis man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d replied Wemmick, growing bolder. \u201cI think you\u2019re the one.\u201d\\n\\nAgain they traded their odd looks, each still suspicious that the other was deceiving him.\\n\\n\u201cYou with a pleasant home?\u201d said Mr. Jaggers.\\n\\n\u201cIf it doesn\u2019t interfere with business,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201clet it be so. Now, looking at you, sir, I wouldn\u2019t be surprised if you were planning to have a pleasant home of your own one day, when you\u2019re tired of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded thoughtfully two or three times and even sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe won\u2019t talk about \u2018poor dreams;\u2019 you know more about such things than I do, being fresher to that kind of experience. But now to this other matter. I\u2019ll put a case to you. Mind! I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited for me to say I understood he was admitting nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201cconsider this case. Suppose a woman, under the circumstances you mentioned, kept her child hidden and had to tell her lawyer about it when he said he needed to know, to understand how things stood regarding that child.\u201d\"}"
