          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1884652f06f085366ba4f28\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo now, let\u2019s hear the whole thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, you see, it was like this. Old missus\u2014that\u2019s Miss Watson\u2014picks on me all the time and treats me pretty rough, but she always said she wouldn\u2019t sell me down to Orleans. But I noticed there was a slave trader hanging around the place a lot lately, and I started getting uneasy. Well, one night I creep to the door pretty late, and it wasn\u2019t quite shut, and I hear old missus tell the widow she\u2019s going to sell me down to Orleans. She didn\u2019t want to, but she could get eight hundred dollars for me, and it was such a big pile of money she couldn\u2019t resist. The widow tried to get her to promise she wouldn\u2019t do it, but I didn\u2019t wait to hear the rest. I lit out mighty quick, I tell you.\\n\\n\u201cI took off and slid down the hill, planning to steal a skiff along the shore somewhere above the town, but there were still people up, so I hid in the old run-down cooper shop on the bank to wait for everyone to clear out. Well, I stayed there all night. There was somebody around all the time. Around six in the morning skiffs started going by, and by eight or nine every skiff that passed was talking about how your pap came over to town and said you were killed. The last skiffs were full of ladies and gentlemen going over to see the place. Sometimes they\u2019d pull up at the shore and rest before they started across, so from their talk I learned all about the killing. I was real sorry you were killed, Huck, but I sure ain\u2019t now.\\n\\n\u201cI lay there under the shavings all day. I was hungry, but I wasn\u2019t scared, because I knew old missus and the widow were going to start for the camp meeting right after breakfast and be gone all day, and they know I go off with the cattle about daylight, so they wouldn\u2019t expect to see me around, and wouldn\u2019t miss me until after dark in the evening. The other servants wouldn\u2019t miss me, because they\u2019d take off and have a holiday as soon as the old folks were out of the way.\\n\\n\u201cWell, when it got dark I headed up the river road and went about two miles or more to where there weren\u2019t any houses. I\u2019d made up my mind what I was going to do. You see, if I kept trying to get away on foot, the dogs would track me; if I stole a skiff to cross over, they\u2019d notice that skiff missing, see, and they\u2019d know about where I\u2019d land on the other side and where to pick up my trail.\u201d\"}"
        }
