          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"89955239416180acb9c78f00\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is truly curious that she always manages to catch what Jane says better than what I do. Jane\u2019s articulation is so precise! Still, she will find her grandmama no more deaf than she was two years ago; and that is saying much for my mother\u2019s age\u2014and it has really been a full two years, you know, since she was here. We had never gone so long without seeing her before, and as I was just remarking to Mrs. Cole, we shall scarcely know how to lavish sufficient attention on her now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you expect Miss Fairfax here soon?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh yes; next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u2014that must give you great pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you. How kind you are. Yes, next week. Everyone is astonished, and everyone repeats the same civil expressions. I am sure she will be as delighted to meet her friends at Highbury as they will be to greet her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot specify which, because Colonel Campbell wants the carriage one of those days. So extremely kind of them to send her all the way! But that is their custom, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is why she has written out of the usual order, as we term it; for ordinarily we should not have expected a letter before next Tuesday or Wednesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that was my supposition. I feared there would be little prospect of my hearing anything of Miss Fairfax to-day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo obliging of you to say so! No, we should not have heard, except for this special circumstance of her coming so soon. My mother is in raptures!\u2014for she is to stay with us at least three months. Three months, she says so expressly, as I am about to have the pleasure of reading to you. The situation, you see, is that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her parents to cross over and see her immediately. They had not planned to go before the summer, but she is so impatient to have them near her again\u2014for until she married, last October, she had never been away from them for even a week, which must make it feel very odd to be in separate kingdoms, I was going to say, but at any rate in different countries\u2014and so she wrote a pressing letter to her mother\u2014or to her father, I protest I cannot tell which it was, but we shall find out presently in Jane\u2019s letter\u2014she wrote in Mr. Dixon\u2019s name as well as her own, urging them to come straightaway, and they would meet them in Dublin and escort them back to their estate, Bally-craig, a place I hear is exquisitely beautiful.\u201d\"}"
