          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d083bc22ced6158742b95ebf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In fairness to the young lady, it must be noted that she did not flatly declare she would refuse, but only voiced with great emphasis and earnestness her wish to be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did; a courteous, tactful dodge revealing that innate refinement which cannot endure causing another the sting of an outright denial.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s expression sank. He turned from this young lady, who was cheerily\u2014one might even say resplendently\u2014arrayed in a scarlet gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other woman.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d asked the Jew softly, \u201cwhat is _your_ opinion?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it\u2019s no go; so there\u2019s no use trying it, Fagin,\u201d Nancy answered.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that supposed to mean?\u201d growled Mr. Sikes, glancing up.\\n\\n\u201cWhat I said, Bill,\u201d replied the lady calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re just the very one for it,\u201d argued Mr. Sikes. \u201cNo soul around here knows you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd seeing as I prefer they shouldn\u2019t,\u201d Nancy returned in the same steady tone, \u201cI\u2019d say it\u2019s more no than yes with me, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d insisted Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d retorted Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d Sikes repeated.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes proved correct. By turns of threats, promises, and bribes, the lady was at last induced to accept the errand. She was not hindered by the concerns that troubled her vibrant companion; having only lately shifted to the Field Lane quarter from the distant yet respectable suburb of Ratcliffe, she felt little fear of being recognised by any of her many acquaintances.\\n\\nThus, with a spotless white apron tied over her gown and her curl-papers tucked under a straw bonnet\u2014both items drawn from the Jew\u2019s limitless supplies\u2014Miss Nancy made ready to depart on the mission.\\n\\n\u201cHold a moment, my dear,\u201d said the Jew, producing a small lidded basket. \u201cCarry this in one hand. It lends you respectability, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door-key for the other hand, Fagin,\u201d suggested Sikes. \u201cMakes it look proper and reg\u2019lar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, you\u2019re right, my dear,\u201d the Jew agreed, suspending a hefty street-door key from the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere; excellent! Excellent, my dear!\u201d he said, rubbing his palms.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d cried Nancy, bursting into sobs and wringing the little basket and the key in anguish. \u201cWhat\u2019s become of him! Where\u2019ve they taken him! Oh, have mercy, and tell me what\u2019s been done with the dear child, gentlemen; do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
