          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2b2a212a7ac8405644a5feff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind except the memory that those days had existed.\\n\\nAnne had not set foot in Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. She had no reason to go, and on the few occasions when she might have gone to the Hall, she found ways to dodge and slip away. Her first return was to reclaim her place in the fashionable, elegant rooms of the Lodge and to please its mistress.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s delight at seeing her again carried a trace of worry. She knew exactly who had been spending time at Uppercross. Fortunately, either Anne truly looked fresher and rounder, or Lady Russell imagined it; and as Anne accepted the compliments, she couldn\u2019t help linking them to her cousin\u2019s quiet admiration and indulging the hope that she might be granted a second blooming in youth and beauty.\\n\\nOnce they began to talk, Anne quickly felt a shift in herself. The subjects that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, that she had felt slighted and forced to stifle among the Musgroves, now mattered less. Recently she had hardly thought about her father, her sister, or Bath. Their concerns had sunk beneath those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell brought up their old hopes and fears, expressing pleasure in the Camden Place house they had secured and regret that Mrs. Clay still stayed with them, Anne would have been mortified to reveal how much more her thoughts were occupied with Lyme and Louisa Musgrove, and everyone she knew there; how much more compelling she found the home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick than her father\u2019s residence in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs. Clay. She actually had to make an effort to meet Lady Russell with even the appearance of equal concern for matters that naturally should have been foremost.\\n\\nAnother topic brought a touch of awkwardness at first. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had been in the house less than five minutes the previous day when the full story was poured out to her; but even so, they had to talk it through\u2014she had to ask questions, deplore the rashness, mourn the outcome, and both of them had to say Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne knew she wasn\u2019t handling it as smoothly as Lady Russell. She couldn\u2019t speak his name and meet Lady Russell\u2019s eyes until she resorted to briefly explaining what she believed about the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
