          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a71ba99b82a0867a45cb238\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes\u2019s crime, fell into deep thought about whether an honest life wasn\u2019t ultimately the best. Convinced that it was, he turned away from his former life, determined to rebuild it somewhere new. He struggled and suffered for a long time; but with a contented spirit and steadfast purpose, he succeeded at last, and from being a farmer\u2019s drudge and a carrier\u2019s boy, he is now the happiest young grazier in all Northamptonshire.\\n\\nNow the hand writing these words trembles as it nears the end of its work and would keep weaving the thread of these adventures just a little longer.\\n\\nI would still like to stay awhile with a few of those whose lives I have shared for so long, and enjoy their happiness by trying to capture it. I would show Rose Maylie in all the bloom and grace of early womanhood, casting a soft, gentle light on her quiet path in life, a light that touched everyone who walked it with her, and warmed their hearts. I would depict her as the life and joy of the fireside circle and the lively summer group; I would follow her through the hot fields at noon and listen to the low tones of her sweet voice on moonlit evening walks; I would watch her practicing kindness and charity in the world, and tending to her domestic duties at home with a cheerful, tireless spirit. I would show her and her sister\u2019s orphaned child rejoicing in their love for each other, spending hours describing the friends they lost so tragically. I would summon again the joyful little faces that gathered around her knee and listen to their bright chatter; I would recall the sound of her clear laugh and summon the sympathetic tear that shone in her soft blue eye. These, and a thousand looks and smiles and turns of thought and speech\u2014I would gladly call them all back.\\n\\nHow Mr. Brownlow went on day by day filling his adopted child\u2019s mind with knowledge, growing more attached to him as his character unfolded and revealed the thriving seeds of everything Mr. Brownlow hoped he would become\u2014how he recognized in him new traits of his old friend, awakening in his own heart memories that were melancholy yet sweet and soothing\u2014how the two orphans, forged by hardship, remembered its lessons in mercy toward others, in mutual love, and in heartfelt gratitude to Him who had guarded and preserved them\u2014these things do not need to be told.\"}"
