          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8bc705e5b452060d6f5d1cb3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou need to learn a bit of my philosophy. Think of the past only when remembering it brings you happiness.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot credit you with possessing such philosophy. Your memories must be so entirely free from blame that the peace they give you is ignorance, which is far better than philosophy. With me it is different. Painful memories intrude and cannot, should not, be driven away. I have lived selfishly all my life\u2014my behavior has been selfish even if my principles were not. As a child I was taught what was right, but no one taught me to master my temper. I received good principles, but was allowed to follow them with pride and conceit. I was unfortunately an only son (for many years an only child), spoiled by my parents, who, though good themselves\u2014my father especially, the kindest, most amiable man\u2014still allowed, encouraged, even taught me to be selfish and domineering, to care for no one beyond my own family circle, to look down on everyone else, to want to believe that their sense and worth were inferior to mine. That is who I was from eight to twenty-eight; and that is who I might still be, were it not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! What do I not owe you! You taught me a lesson\u2014hard at first, but invaluable. Through you I was properly humbled. I came to you with no doubt of my welcome. You showed me how inadequate were all my pretensions to win a woman who deserved to be won.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you truly believe I would accept you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI did. What must you think of my vanity? I imagined you were wishing for and expecting my proposal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen my manners must have been at fault, though never intentionally. I never meant to mislead you, but my spirits often led me astray. How you must have despised me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDespise you? I was angry at first, perhaps, but my anger soon found its proper target.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not at all. I felt only surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not exceed mine at receiving any notice from you. My conscience told me I deserved no special politeness, and I certainly did not expect more than what was due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy aim then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, with every civility I could offer, that I was not so petty as to hold grudges; and I hoped to gain your forgiveness, to soften your poor opinion of me, by proving I had taken your rebukes to heart.\u201d\"}"
