          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d6dcc512fec5108a05ee1f60\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at last, \u201cit can\u2019t be denied that since the new feeding system started, the coffins have grown a bit narrower and shallower than they used to be; but we have to turn some profit, Mr. Bumble. Quality timber costs dearly, sir, and all the iron handles come by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery trade has its drawbacks. A reasonable profit is, of course, acceptable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course,\u201d the undertaker replied. \u201cAnd if I don\u2019t make money on one item, I make it up over time, you understand\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cYet I must say,\u201d the undertaker went on, picking up the train of thought the beadle had broken, \u201cI must say, Mr. Bumble, I face one big disadvantage: every stout person dies the quickest. The folks who\u2019ve been better off and paying rates for years are the first to sink when they enter the workhouse; and let me tell you, Mr. Bumble, three or four inches beyond the estimate cuts deep into one\u2019s profits\u2014especially with a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Sowerberry delivered this with the righteous annoyance of a man unfairly treated, and Mr. Bumble sensed the hint of a slur on the parish\u2019s honor, so he thought it wise to change the topic. Oliver Twist was foremost in his mind, so he made the boy his subject.\\n\\n\u201cBy the way,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cdo you know anyone who needs a boy? A parish apprentice who\u2019s presently a dead weight, a millstone around the parish\u2019s neck, you might say? Favorable terms, Mr. Sowerberry, favorable terms.\u201d As he spoke, Mr. Bumble lifted his cane toward the bill above him and gave three crisp taps on the words \u201cfive pounds,\u201d printed in enormous Roman capitals.\\n\\n\u201cGadso!\u201d exclaimed the undertaker, taking hold of the gilt-edged lapel of Mr. Bumble\u2019s official coat. \u201cThat\u2019s exactly what I wanted to talk to you about. You know\u2014dear me, what a remarkably elegant button this is, Mr. Bumble! I never noticed it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I think it rather handsome,\u201d said the beadle, glancing proudly down at the large brass buttons that adorned his coat. \u201cThe die matches the parish seal\u2014the Good Samaritan tending the sick and wounded man. The board presented it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I remember wearing it for the first time at the inquest on that fallen tradesman who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
