          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc94edd1547db5cda9c9dd4f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He stood up and left the room with my nurse, and a moment later my father came in.\\n\\nNothing could have pleased me more than his arrival. I reached out my hand and cried, \u201cAre you safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father calmed me by assuring me they were well and tried, by speaking about the people I loved, to lift my spirits; but he soon realized that cheerfulness cannot live in a prison. \u201cWhat a place this is for you, my son!\u201d he said, looking sadly at the barred windows and the wretched room. \u201cYou travelled to find happiness, yet misfortune seems to hunt you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHearing the name of my murdered friend upset me too much in my frail state, and I wept.\\n\\n\u201cAlas! Yes, Father,\u201d I replied. \u201cSome horrible fate is fixed on me, and I must live to see it done, or I would have died on Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not allowed to talk for long, because my fragile health required every effort to keep me calm. Mr. Kirwin entered and insisted I not waste my strength. Yet my father\u2019s presence felt like that of a good angel, and I slowly regained my health.\\n\\nAs the sickness left me, a dark and heavy sadness filled me. The image of Clerval, ghastly and slain, stood constantly before me. More than once the agitation of these thoughts made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why did they keep alive such a miserable, hated life? It must be so I could fulfil my fate, which was nearing its end. Soon, very soon, death would quiet my pounding heart and free me from the crushing weight of grief. In carrying out justice, I would also find rest. Then, though death still seemed far away, I wished for it without cease; I often sat for hours silent and still, longing for some great upheaval that would bury both me and my destroyer in its ruins.\\n\\nThe season of the assizes neared. I had already spent three months in prison, and though I was still weak and in constant danger of relapse, I had to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court met. Mr. Kirwin took on every task of gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
