{"request_id":"4ad610d73ef1cc598aef41db","rewritten_text":"I can hardly explain the joy I felt when I turned from the political fights at home, and the physical dangers of distant lands, back to my own dear house—the chosen place of goodness and love, of peace, and of every sacred sympathy. If I had never left Windsor, these emotions would not have risen so high; but in Greece I had fallen prey to fear and terrible change. In Greece, after long anxiety and grief, I watched two people depart whose names themselves meant greatness and virtue. Still, such miseries could not break into the family circle left to me while we lived quietly in our beloved forest. The years brought small changes, as they always do, and time set its mortal stamp on our joys and hopes. Idris, the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend, was also a tender, devoted mother. Motherhood was no pastime to her; it was a passion. We had three children; the second-born died while I was in Greece. That loss darkened her proud delight in motherhood with grief and fear. Before then, the young beings sprung from her, the heirs of her brief life, seemed to her secure in existence. Now she feared the pitiless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings as it had taken their brother. Any small illness brought waves of terror; she was wretched whenever she was away from them. She had stored all her happiness in their fragile lives and stayed on guard, lest the stealthy thief should steal these precious jewels as before. Thankfully, she had little cause for alarm. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly little fellow, his forehead bright, his eyes soft, his nature gentle yet independent. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheek glowed with health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed the stage whose mute ignorance fed Idris’s fears. She was dear to her mother and to us all. So much intelligence blended with innocence, sensibility with patience, seriousness with perfect good nature; beauty so transcendent joined to such endearing simplicity, that she shone like a pearl among our treasures, a marvel of excellence.

At the beginning of winter our Alfred, now nine years old, went to school at Eton for the first time. He saw it as the first step toward manhood and was delighted. Sharing lessons and play brought out the best parts of his character—his steady perseverance, his generosity, and his well-controlled firmness."}

