          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f152ae40aff7b68afd9596bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief talk with Mr. Knightley gave Emma great pleasure. It was one of the pleasant memories of the ball that she replayed while walking around the lawn the next morning. She was very glad they now understood each other about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so similar. His praise of Harriet, his concession in her favour, was especially gratifying. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which had threatened for a few minutes to ruin the rest of her evening, ended up producing some of its best moments; and she looked forward to another happy result\u2014the end of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From Harriet\u2019s way of speaking of the night before they left the ballroom, Emma had strong hopes. It seemed as if Harriet\u2019s eyes had suddenly opened and she now saw that Mr. Elton was not the superior creature she had imagined. The fever had broken, and Emma felt little fear that the pulse would race again from harmful politeness. She counted on the Eltons\u2019 spite to provide all the pointed neglect still needed. Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too much in love, and Mr. Knightley not wishing to quarrel with her\u2014what a happy summer she must have ahead! \\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he had to be home by midday. She did not regret it. \\n\\nHaving settled all these thoughts, reviewed them, and set them straight, she was turning to the house, spirits braced for the demands of the two little boys and their grandpapa, when the great iron sweep-gate opened and two people entered whom she had never less expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet on his arm\u2014actually Harriet! One moment was enough to show her something extraordinary had happened. Harriet looked pale and frightened, and he was trying to steady her. The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart; the three of them were soon in the hall, and Harriet at once sank into a chair and fainted. \\n\\nA young lady who faints must be revived; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such events are very interesting, but their suspense cannot last long. In a few minutes Emma knew the whole story. \\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also been at the ball, had walked out together and taken the Richmond road, which, though seemingly public enough for safety, had led them into alarm. About half a mile beyond Highbury, the road made a sudden turn and, shaded on both sides by elms, became for a good stretch very secluded; and when the young ladies had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead on a broader patch of greensward by the roadside, a group of gypsies.\"}"
