{"request_id":"1ea3065e9d4692070d0fa9ad","rewritten_text":"They were her life; dearer than any shrine of love, dearer than anything else on earth. The boys were excited like children when they heard we were moving. Clara asked if we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I answered, and her face lit up with joy. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. She had brooded over these scenes silently, without rest. Remembering them had turned her childhood playfulness into seriousness and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

We had many dear friends we could not leave, humble as they were. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; there was Alfred’s dog and a pet eagle whose sight was dim with age. Yet listing these favorites we must take brought grief for our heavy losses and a sigh for what we had to leave. Tears filled Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn carried first a favorite rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting both must come and regretting we could not take the castle and forest, the deer, birds, and every familiar, cherished object. “Fond and foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost forever treasures far more precious than these; we abandon them to save treasures to which these are nothing. Let us not forget our goal and our hope even for a moment; they will stand like a strong barrier against regret for trifles.”

The children were easily diverted and returned to their plans for future delight. Idris had vanished. She had gone to hide her weakness; slipping out of the castle, she descended to the little park, seeking solitude to indulge her tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing its rough trunk with her rosy lips as tears poured down, her sobs and broken words beyond control. With deep grief I saw this beloved of my heart lost in sorrow. I drew her to me, and as she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms around her, she remembered what still remained. “You are kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter agony tears my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children remain with me.”"}

