{"request_id":"db7db98ad0b171f9162a1f7f","rewritten_text":"He had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one. He had never harshly demanded his dues; instead, he had given them up by his own choice, cast himself onto an uncaring world, carved out his own place in it, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the poor, tangled estate under written orders to spare the people, to give them what little could be given—firewood that the harsh creditors allowed them in winter, and produce that could be saved from the same grip in summer—and no doubt he had stated this in his defense, so that it could not fail to show now.

This supported the desperate choice Charles Darnay had begun to make: he would go to Paris. Yes. Like the sailor in the old tale, winds and currents had carried him within range of the Loadstone Rock, and it drew him closer, forcing him to go. Everything rising in his thoughts pushed him on, faster and more steadily, toward that terrible pull. His hidden uneasiness was that evil plans were being pursued in his troubled country by evil men, and that he, who knew he was better than they, was absent instead of trying to stop the bloodshed and claim the cause of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness half smothered and half accusing him, he had compared himself with the brave old man whose sense of duty burned so fiercely; after that self-reproach came Monseigneur’s sneers, which hurt him deeply, and Stryver’s insults, which were especially crude and stinging for old reasons. Then came Gabelle’s letter: the plea of an innocent prisoner, threatened with death, appealing to his justice, honour, and good name.

His decision was made. He must go to Paris. Yes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to sail on until he collided with it. He knew of no rock; he saw almost no danger. The intent behind what he had done—even though he had left it unfinished—made it appear in his mind as something France would appreciate when he presented himself to support it. Then that splendid vision of doing good, often the hopeful mirage of many generous minds, rose before him, and he even imagined himself, in that illusion, wielding some influence to guide the raging Revolution that was running so wildly out of control."}

