          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a535a019d2466306006a565\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were thrilled to see their dear friend again, said it felt like ages since they last met, and kept asking what she had been up to while they were apart. They paid little attention to the rest of the family; they avoided Mrs. Bennet as much as they could, said little to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. They soon left again, springing from their seats with an energy that surprised their brother, and hurried out as if desperate to escape Mrs. Bennet\u2019s polite attentions.\\n\\nThe upcoming Netherfield ball delighted every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to see it as a compliment to her eldest daughter and felt especially flattered to receive the invitation directly from Mr. Bingley instead of through a formal card. Jane imagined a happy evening with her two friends and their brother\u2019s attentions; Elizabeth looked forward to dancing much with Mr. Wickham and to confirming everything through Mr. Darcy\u2019s expression and behaviour. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s excitement relied less on any single event or person; though each, like Elizabeth, planned to dance half the evening with Mr. Wickham, he was not the only partner who could satisfy them, and a ball was, after all, a ball. Even Mary assured the family she had no objection to attending.\\n\\n\u201cAs long as I have my mornings to myself,\u201d she said, \u201cthat is enough. I do not consider it a sacrifice to take part in evening engagements now and then. Society has claims on us all; and I count myself among those who think breaks for recreation and amusement are desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so high that, though she usually avoided speaking to Mr. Collins more than necessary, she could not resist asking if he intended to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation and whether he would think it proper to join the evening\u2019s entertainment. She was rather surprised to learn he had no doubts at all on that point and did not fear a rebuke from either the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh for daring to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, I do not believe,\u201d he said, \u201cthat a ball of this kind, hosted by a young man of good character for respectable people, can have any harmful effects; and far from objecting to dancing myself, I hope to be honoured with the hands of all my fair cousins over the course of the evening. I also take this opportunity to ask you, Miss Elizabeth, for the first two dances especially; a preference I trust my cousin Jane will attribute to the proper reason, and not to any lack of respect for her.\u201d\"}"
        }
