          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d9dfdf7f0cf787125fdc4ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"That mighty voice\u2014if such a thing could be termed inorganic, since no contrivance of pipe or key could be connected with it\u2014let fall its resonant note, and the girl, turning back to aid her aged companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nHe was her father; ever since childhood she had guided those obscured steps. Natives of Saxony, they had emigrated here only a few years earlier and woven new bonds with the neighboring villagers. Near the outbreak of the pestilence a young German student had joined them. Their simple tale was easily read: a nobleman loved the fair daughter of the impoverished musician, and followed them when he fled his friends\u2019 persecutions. Yet soon the remorseless leveler came, sweeping down the lofty flowers with the common grass. The youth fell among the earliest victims. She preserved herself for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness enabled her to maintain a deception, accidental at first; now, these solitary beings, sole survivors in the region, left him unaware of the transformation, nor did he know that when he listened to his child\u2019s playing, the mute mountains, unfeeling lake, and unconscious trees, save himself, composed her only audience.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, symptomatic illness seized her. She was petrified with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, sightless father solitary upon the void earth; yet she lacked courage to tell him the truth, and sheer desperation drove her to extraordinary efforts. At the usual vesper hour she led him to the chapel; though trembling and drenched in tears for his sake, she played faultlessly, neither missing time nor erring in note, the hymn composed to celebrate the creation of this beauteous globe, now to become her tomb.\\n\\nWe came to her like messengers from heaven; her overwrought courage, her scarcely sustained fortitude, vanished at the sight of rescue. With a cry she rushed towards us, clasped Adrian\u2019s knees, and, uttering only \u201cO save my father!\u201d loosened, with sobs and hysteric screams, the long-barred floodgates of her sorrow.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut that shadows her lover\u2019s grave\u2014she had indicated it to us as she died. Her father, at last aware of his daughter\u2019s peril, unable to behold the alteration of her beloved face, obstinately clasped her hand until it grew chill and rigid in death. Nor then did he stir or speak, until twelve hours afterward compassionate death bore him to unbroken rest. They sleep under the sod, the tree their monument\u2014a sacred spot etched upon my memory, girdled by craggy Jura and the far immeasurable Alps; the church spire they frequented still peers out of the embracing trees; and though her hand is icy, it seems to me that the heavenly music they cherished still wanders there, comforting their gentle spirits.\"}"
        }
