          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"402d09aceb457284abed336c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut I must forewarn you that I shall tell Sybil everything. She is coming to lunch with me to-morrow to discuss bonnets, and if Mr. Podgers discovers you have a foul temper, or a leaning toward gout, or perhaps a wife hidden away in Bayswater, I shall certainly inform her on the spot.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI have no fear,\u201d he replied. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! I am rather sorry to hear you say that. The soundest foundation for matrimony is a mutual misunderstanding. No, I am not cynical; I merely possess experience, which amounts to much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is eager to have his palm examined. Do not tell him he is engaged to one of the loveliest girls in London, for that was mentioned in the _Morning Post_ a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d exclaimed the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cplease let Mr. Podgers remain here a little longer. He has just informed me I ought to appear on the stage, and I am terribly interested.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I shall certainly remove him. Come over at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, pulling a slight _moue_ as she rose from the sofa, \u201cif I cannot be permitted to go on the stage, I must at least be allowed to be part of the audience.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; we shall all be part of the audience,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cAnd now, Mr. Podgers, be sure to tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is among my special favourites.\u201d\\n\\nHowever, when Mr. Podgers beheld Lord Arthur\u2019s hand he turned strangely pale and kept silent. A shudder seemed to pass through him, and his great shaggy eyebrows twitched convulsively in that odd, vexing fashion they assumed when he was bewildered. Then heavy beads of sweat sprang out across his yellow forehead like some poisonous dew, and his plump fingers grew cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur failed not to remark these singular signs of agitation, and, for the first time in his life, fear touched him. His impulse was to rush from the room, yet he restrained himself. Better to know the worst, whatever it might be, than remain in this ghastly suspense.\\n\\n\u201cI am waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWe are all waiting,\u201d cried Lady Windermere in her quick, impatient tone, yet the cheiromantist did not reply.\\n\\n\u201cI believe Arthur is going on the stage,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, \u201cand that, after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is nervous about telling him so.\u201d\"}"
