          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"834d75d60c0c764ac7260238\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He never tried again. The boys lay there counting the dragging moments until it felt like time itself had finished and eternity was turning gray; when they finally noticed the sun beginning to set they felt a rush of gratitude.\\n\\nThen one of the snores stopped. Injun Joe sat up, looked around, gave his companion\u2014whose head hung toward his knees\u2014a grim smile, nudged him with his foot, and said:\\n\\n\u201cHey! You call yourself a lookout, huh? All right, though\u2014nothing happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMan! Was I asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, partly, partly. It\u2019s nearly time to move, pard. What do we do with the little bit of loot we\u2019ve got left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here like always, I guess. No sense carrying it off till we head south. Six hundred and fifty in silver is a load.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014fine\u2014it won\u2019t hurt to come back here once more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I say we come at night like we used to\u2014it\u2019s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYeah; but listen, it might be a good while before I get the right chance at that job; things can happen; it ain\u2019t in such a safe spot; let\u2019s bury it proper\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood plan,\u201d said the partner, who walked across the room, knelt, lifted one of the rear hearthstones, and pulled out a bag that jingled nicely. He took twenty or thirty dollars for himself and the same for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to him. Joe was on his knees in the corner now, digging with his bowie knife.\\n\\nIn a heartbeat the boys forgot every fear, every misery. With hungry eyes they tracked each movement. Luck!\u2014its glory was beyond imagining! Six hundred dollars could make half a dozen boys rich! This was treasure-hunting under the best conditions\u2014no annoying doubts about where to dig. They nudged each other repeatedly\u2014speaking nudges, and easy to understand; they simply meant, \u201cOh, aren\u2019t you glad now we\u2019re here!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife hit something.\\n\\n\u201cHey!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d asked his partner.\\n\\n\u201cHalf-rotten plank\u2014no, it\u2019s a box, I think. Here\u2014lend a hand and let\u2019s see what it\u2019s doing here. Never mind, I\u2019ve busted a hole.\u201d\\n\\nHe reached in and pulled something out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men examined the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys above were just as excited and just as thrilled.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s partner said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll make short work of this. There\u2019s an old rusty pick over in the weeds on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I spotted it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
