          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"10810a5e83ecd13f5333e532\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In this manner two years slipped away. Day after day hundreds passed, bringing little outward alteration, though a few gradually worked upon my mind as I drifted slowly towards death. I took to study more earnestly, grew more responsive to the thoughts of others as set down in books, read history, and lost my individuality among the throng who had lived before me. Perhaps as the immediate pangs faded, I became more human. Solitude relinquished some of its charms: I again longed for sympathy, not that I ever felt tempted to seek the multitude, but I yearned for a single friend who would love me. You may say I was gradually fitted to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I craved had to be so pure, so freed from outward influences, that in the world I must have been thwarted by the coarse materials mingled even with its best emotions. Believe me, at that time I was less suited for fellowship with my kind than ever. When I quitted them they had tormented me, yet it was as pain and sickness torment\u2014something extraneous to the mind that chafed it and that I wished to cast off. Now I should have longed for sympathy; I should have tried to wreathe my soul to another\u2019s, and prepared for myself abundant draughts of disappointment and sorrow; for I was tender as the sensitive plant, all quivering nerves. I desired no sympathy in ambition or wisdom, but in sweet, mutual affection\u2014smiles to gladden me and gentle words to console. I yearned for one heart into which I could pour my complaints unrestrained, that from the heavenly nature of the soil blessed fruit might spring from such poor seed. Yet how could I discover this? The love that forms the soul of friendship is a soft spirit rarely found except when two amiable natures are knit from early youth, or united by common suffering and pursuits; it comes to some of the elect unbidden and unaware, it descends like gentle dew on chosen places which, however barren before, become under its kind influence fertile in all sweet plants; but when sought it flees, it scorns the prayers of its worshipers; it will grant itself, but not be wooed.\\n\\nI understood all this and went not forth to seek sympathy; yet there on my solitary heath, beneath my lowly roof, while all around lay desert, it came to me like a winter sunbeam lending beauty even as it melts the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone on blasted fruit; I did not revive beneath its brightness, for I was too utterly ruined to feel its beneficent warmth.\"}"
        }
