{"request_id":"12fe29101705f88530eb2fa0","rewritten_text":"To me, that quaint lie had been told simply and beautifully; but I had heard it only once, and that made a difference. It had surely delighted the others when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the humorist woke first, and soon roused the rest with a miserably cheap prank. He tied metal mugs to a dog’s tail and set him loose; the dog tore around in terror, the other dogs chasing him, crashing into everything, raising a deafening racket. Every man and woman howled with laughter until tears ran down, some tumbling from their chairs and rolling on the floor in bliss. They were like a crowd of children. Sir Dinadan felt so triumphant he kept repeating, until it grew tiresome, how the immortal idea had struck him; and like humorists of his sort, he stayed laughing after everyone else stopped. Feeling important, he decided to give a speech—naturally a comic one. I never heard so many ancient, used-up jokes strung together. He was worse than traveling minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It felt especially pitiful to sit here, thirteen hundred years before my birth, listening again to stale, worm-eaten jokes that had given me stomach pains when I was a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me no new joke is possible. Everyone laughed at those antiques—just as they always do; I had noticed the same centuries after. Of course the scoffer didn’t laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there was nothing he wouldn’t mock. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten and the rest petrified. I said “petrified” was good; I had already thought the only proper way to classify some of those venerable jokes was by geologic ages. But that clever line bounced off the boy, because geology hadn’t been invented yet. Still, I made a note of it and planned to educate the kingdom up to it if I survived. A good idea shouldn’t be wasted just because the audience isn’t ready.

Now Sir Kay stood up and began cranking his history-mill with me as the fuel."}

