          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2432ecc3934d40e3cd4b970a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we possessed a donkey. Imagine the three of us arriving on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and myself\u2014with my caro sposo walking alongside. I must truly discuss purchasing one with him. In country living, I consider it almost indispensable; for, no matter how many diversions a woman may have, she cannot remain confined at home forever, and long walks, as you know\u2014there is dust in summer, and in winter nothing but mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will encounter neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and now it is completely dry. Still, come on a donkey if that is your preference. You may borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I would have everything arranged precisely to your liking.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I have no doubt of. I do you complete justice, my dear friend. Beneath that particular dry, blunt exterior, I am aware you possess the warmest of hearts. As I often tell Mr. E., you are a consummate humourist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I thoroughly appreciate your attentiveness to me throughout this plan. You have discovered exactly what delights me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another motive for avoiding a shaded table. He wished to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, and Emma as well, to join the outing; and he understood that if any of them were to sit down to eat outdoors, the result would surely be his illness. Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the seductive excuse of a morning drive and an hour or two spent at Donwell, be lured away to suffering.\\n\\nHe had been invited in all sincerity. No hidden terrors were to rebuke him for his trusting acceptance. He did agree. Two years had passed since he last visited Donwell. \u201cSome splendid morning, he, Emma, and Harriet might go perfectly well; he could remain quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls roamed the gardens. He had no reason to suppose they would be damp at midday. He would greatly enjoy seeing the old house again and would be most happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, and any other neighbours. He perceived not the least objection to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there some particularly fine morning. It seemed to him very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014extremely kind and judicious\u2014far superior to dining out. He was not partial to dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in everyone\u2019s immediate assent. The invitation met such warm reception everywhere that, much like Mrs. Elton, all seemed to treat the arrangement as though it were a special compliment directed at themselves. Emma and Harriet declared their expectations of enjoyment to be exceedingly high; and Mr. Weston, unsolicited, promised to bring Frank over to join them if it could be arranged\u2014a display of approval and gratitude that they might easily have excused. Mr. Knightley then found himself obligated to state that he would indeed be pleased to see him; whereupon Mr. Weston pledged to write without delay and to spare no persuasive arguments to secure Frank\u2019s attendance.\"}"
        }
