          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"05905f3f01f5b9fffbb37b97\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you doing?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and tried to figure out where I was. It was after sunrise, and I had been out cold. Pap was standing over me looking sour and sickly. He said, \u201cWhat are you doing with that gun?\u201d\\n\\nI guessed he didn\u2019t remember anything about what he\u2019d been up to, so I said, \u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me up?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t. I couldn\u2019t budge you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFine. Don\u2019t stand there yammering all day\u2014get outside and see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I headed up the riverbank. I noticed bits of branches and that sort of thing floating past, and pieces of bark sprinkled here and there; that let me know the river was starting to rise. I figured I\u2019d be having a grand time if I were over in town. The June rise always brought me luck, because as soon as the water starts swelling, cordwood floats down, and pieces of log rafts too\u2014maybe a dozen logs together. All you have to do is snag them and sell them to the woodyards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI walked along the bank with one eye out for Pap and the other for whatever the rising river might bring. Suddenly comes a canoe, a real beauty about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I dove headfirst off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam for it. I half expected someone to be lying down in it, since people did that to fool passersby, and when some guy pulled his skiff up close they\u2019d pop up and laugh at him. But not this time. It was a real drift canoe. I climbed in and paddled it to shore. I figured the old man would be pleased when he saw it\u2014it was worth ten dollars.\\n\\nBut when I got to land, Pap still hadn\u2019t shown up, and as I was pushing the canoe into a little creek like a gully, all covered over with vines and willows, another idea hit me. I decided I\u2019d hide it well, and then, instead of disappearing into the woods when I ran off, I\u2019d head down the river about fifty miles, make camp in one spot for good, and avoid the rough foot travel.\\n\\nIt was pretty close to the shack, and I kept thinking I heard the old man coming, but I got the canoe hidden. Then I slipped out and looked around a cluster of willows, and there he was, down the path a bit, taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
