          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"727d3f92be25deb26bde6d36\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Cut off from usual means, the analyst immerses himself in his adversary\u2019s spirit, identifies with it, and often perceives instantly the single measures (sometimes laughably simple) by which he might lure the other into blunder or hasty miscalculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been renowned for its effect on what people term the calculating faculty; and thinkers of the highest rank have been known to take a seemingly inexplicable pleasure in it, while dismissing chess as trifling. Beyond question, nothing comparable more severely taxes analytical power. The greatest chess-player in Christendom may remain only the foremost expert at chess; but mastery of whist implies aptitude for triumph in those more vital enterprises where mind contends with mind. By proficiency I mean that faultlessness in the game which embraces a grasp of every source from which legitimate advantage may arise. These are not merely numerous but diverse, and often lie within recesses of thought entirely closed to ordinary intellects. To observe closely is to retain distinctly; and, to that extent, the intensely focused chess-player performs well at whist, while Hoyle\u2019s rules (themselves resting on the bare mechanics of the game) are generally intelligible. Hence to possess a strong memory and to play \u201cby the book\u201d are usually accounted the entirety of sound practice. Yet it is beyond mere rule that the analyst\u2019s art shines forth. He silently amasses a multitude of observations and deductions. So, perhaps, do his associates; but the variance in the volume of information secured depends less on the soundness of the inference than on the quality of the observation. Essential knowledge lies in what to observe. Our player imposes no limits on himself; nor, because the game is central, does he discard inferences drawn from matters outside of it. He scrutinizes his partner\u2019s face, setting it against each opponent\u2019s. He studies how the cards are sorted in every hand; often counting trump after trump, honor after honor, by the glances their holders cast upon each. He registers every change of expression as the play goes on, deriving a rich store of reflection from shifting signs of certainty, surprise, exultation, or vexation. From the way a trick is gathered he judges whether the taker can secure another in that suit. He detects a feint by the manner in which a card is flung upon the table. A stray or unintentional word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, with the accompanying eagerness or indifference about hiding it; the tallying of tricks, with the order in which they are laid down; awkwardness, wavering, keenness, or agitation\u2014all offer, to his seemingly instinctive insight, clues to the genuine posture of affairs.\"}"
        }
      ],
