          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b45269ba684b40874592148\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years Dorian Gray couldn\u2019t loosen himself from the influence of that book. Or perhaps it\u2019s more accurate to say he never tried to free himself from it. He obtained no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition from Paris and had them bound in different colors so they would suit his varying moods and the shifting whims of a nature he sometimes seemed to have almost entirely lost control over. The hero\u2014the extraordinary young Parisian in whom romantic and scientific temperaments were curiously intertwined\u2014became for him a sort of preview of himself. Indeed, the entire book seemed to him to contain the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic hero. He never knew\u2014never had any reason to know\u2014that grotesque fear of mirrors, polished metal surfaces, and still water that struck the young Parisian so early in life, triggered by the sudden decay of a beauty that had once apparently been remarkable. With almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps in nearly every delight, as surely in every pleasure, cruelty has its place\u2014he would read the latter part of the book, with its genuinely tragic, if somewhat overstated, account of the sorrow and despair of someone who had lost in himself what he prized most in others and in the world.\\n\\nFor the remarkable beauty that had so mesmerized Basil Hallward, and many others besides him, seemed never to abandon him. Even those who had heard the most sinister things about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumors about his lifestyle filtered through London and became club gossip\u2014couldn\u2019t believe anything dishonorable when they saw him. He always looked like someone who had kept himself untainted by the world. Men who talked crudely fell silent when Dorian Gray entered the room. There was something in the innocence of his face that reproached them. His mere presence seemed to revive in them the memory of the purity they had stained. They wondered how someone so charming and graceful could have avoided the corruption of an age that was both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, returning home from those mysterious and prolonged absences that prompted such odd speculation among the people who were his friends\u2014or thought they were\u2014he himself would creep upstairs to the locked room, open the door with the key he now never parted with, and stand with a mirror in front of the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him, looking first at the wicked, aging face on the canvas and then at the fair young face that laughed back at him from the polished glass.\"}"
        }
      ],
