          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63974d0a03623b8204ff060c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said nothing about it until their visitor left for the night. As soon as he was gone, Mr. Bennet turned to his daughter. \u201cJane, I congratulate you. You will be a very happy woman.\u201d Jane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness. \u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he said, \u201cand I am delighted you will be so well settled. I am sure you will do splendidly together. Your tempers are alike. You are both so compliant that nothing will ever be decided; so easygoing that every servant will cheat you; and so generous that you will always spend more than you earn.\u201d \u201cI hope not. Carelessness with money would be inexcusable in me.\u201d \u201cSpend more than they earn! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d exclaimed his wife, \u201cwhat nonsense is this? He has four or five thousand a year, and likely more.\u201d Then she turned to her daughter. \u201cOh, my dear Jane, I am so happy! I am sure I won\u2019t sleep a wink tonight. I knew it would happen. I always said it must, in the end. I was sure you could not be so beautiful for nothing! I remember, the moment I first saw him when he came into Hertfordshire last year, I thought how likely it was you would end up together. Oh, he is the handsomest young man ever seen!\u201d Wickham and Lydia were entirely forgotten. Jane was now her unquestioned favorite child; for the moment, she cared for no one else. Her younger sisters quickly tried to secure favors from her future happiness. Mary asked for the use of the Netherfield library, and Kitty begged earnestly for a few dances there every winter. From then on Bingley became a daily visitor at Longbourn, often arriving before breakfast and never leaving until after supper, except when some barbarous neighbor, who could never be detested enough, invited him to dinner and he felt obliged to accept. Elizabeth now had little time to talk with her sister, for while Bingley was there, Jane paid attention to no one else; yet Elizabeth found herself useful to both during the necessary hours of separation. When Jane was absent, he always attached himself to Elizabeth for the pleasure of talking about her, and when Bingley left, Jane sought the same comfort. \u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d she said one evening, \u201cby telling me he had no idea I was in town last spring!\u201d\"}"
