{"request_id":"5aaede305c48f7946aacd1e9","rewritten_text":"I do not understand at all why she wanted to be close to me; but if the same situation happened again, I know I would be fooled again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and I had not a note or a line from her in the meantime. When she finally came, it was clear she took no pleasure in it; she made a brief, formal apology for not coming sooner, said nothing about wanting to see me again, and was changed in every way. When she left, I resolved to end the acquaintance. I pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out; I can honestly say every attempt at intimacy came from her. Yet I pity her because she must feel she acted improperly, and because I am sure anxiety for her brother is the reason. I need not say more; and though we know such anxiety is needless, if she feels it, it explains her behavior. He is so deservedly dear to his sister that any concern she feels for him is natural and charming. Still, I cannot help wondering that she has such fears now, because if he cared for me at all, we would have met long ago. I know he is aware that I am in town, from something she herself said; yet by her manner it seemed as though she wanted to convince herself that he favors Miss Darcy. I cannot make sense of it. If I were not afraid of harsh judgment, I would almost say there seems a great deal of deceit in all this. I will try to put aside every painful thought and think only of what will make me happy—your affection and the constant kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he might never return to Netherfield, that he might give up the house, but she was not certain. We had better not discuss it. I am extremely pleased that you have such good news from our friends at Hunsford. Please go to see them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits revived when she thought that Jane would no longer be misled, at least by the sister. Any hope regarding the brother was now completely gone."}

