          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"45fa66065756f08e8668e069\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou\u2019re not suggesting\u2014\u201d began my sister. \u201cYes I am, Mum,\u201d said Pumblechook. \u201cBut hold on a moment. Go on, Joseph. Good for you! Go on!\u201d \u201cWhat would everyone here say,\u201d Joe continued, \u201cto twenty pounds?\u201d \u201cGenerous would be the word,\u201d my sister replied. \u201cWell, then,\u201d said Joe, \u201cit\u2019s more than twenty pounds.\u201d That wretched hypocrite Pumblechook nodded again and said, with a patronizing laugh, \u201cIt\u2019s more than that, Mum. Very good! Keep it going, Joseph!\u201d \u201cThen let\u2019s finish it,\u201d said Joe, happily handing the bag to my sister; \u201cit\u2019s twenty-five pounds.\u201d \u201cIt\u2019s twenty-five pounds, Mum,\u201d echoed that lowest of swindlers, Pumblechook, getting up to shake her hand; \u201cand it\u2019s no more than you deserve (as I said when they asked my opinion), and I wish you joy of the money!\u201d If the scoundrel had stopped there, it would have been bad enough, but he made matters worse by proceeding to take me into custody with a show of authority that surpassed all his previous offenses. \u201cNow look here, Joseph and wife,\u201d said Pumblechook, gripping my arm above the elbow, \u201cI\u2019m one of those who always see things through. This boy has to be bound, immediately. That\u2019s my way. Bound without delay.\u201d \u201cHeaven knows, Uncle Pumblechook,\u201d said my sister, clutching the money, \u201cwe\u2019re deeply indebted to you.\u201d \u201cDon\u2019t mention it, Mum,\u201d replied that diabolical corn dealer. \u201cA pleasure\u2019s a pleasure, everywhere. But this boy, you understand\u2014we must have him bound. I said I\u2019d take care of it, to tell you the truth.\u201d The Justices were sitting in the Town Hall nearby, and we went straight over to have me bound as Joe\u2019s apprentice in their presence. I say we went, but Pumblechook pushed me over there as if I\u2019d just picked a pocket or set fire to a haystack; in fact, the general impression in court was that I\u2019d been caught in the act. As Pumblechook shoved me through the crowd, I heard some people say, \u201cWhat\u2019s he done?\u201d and others, \u201cHe\u2019s young, too, but doesn\u2019t he look bad?\u201d A kindly-looking man even handed me a tract decorated with a woodcut of a sinister young man loaded with chains like sausages, titled TO BE READ IN MY CELL. The Hall struck me as a strange place, with pews taller than any church\u2019s, people leaning over them to stare, powerful Justices (one with a powdered wig) reclining in chairs, arms folded, or taking snuff, dozing, writing, or reading newspapers, and shiny dark portraits on the walls that my untrained eye thought looked like a mix of hard candy and plaster patches.\"}"
