          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"79f7e1fe00772a77aff9cc9d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often pondered the old idea of the two-part soul and entertained myself by imagining a double Dupin\u2014one creative, one analytical.\\n\\nDo not think, from what I have just said, that I am describing any mystery or writing a romance. What I have shown in the Frenchman was only the effect of a highly active, perhaps unhealthy mind. But an example of his remarks in these moments will best explain.\\n\\nOne night we were walking down a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Both of us seemed deep in thought, and neither had spoken for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin burst out with these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small fellow, that\u2019s true, and would suit the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt about that,\u201d I answered without thinking, not immediately noticing (so lost was I in my own thoughts) how perfectly he had matched my reflections. A second later, realization struck me, and my astonishment was immense.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said seriously, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I admit I\u2019m amazed, and hardly trust my senses. How could you know I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d Here I paused to make sure he really knew the person in my mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy stop? You were noting that his tiny figure makes him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been thinking. Chantilly was a former cobbler from the Rue St. Denis who, crazed with the stage, had tried to play Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy and had been publicly mocked for it.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is one\u2014by which you have managed to read my mind in this matter.\u201d In truth I was more shaken than I cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruit seller,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the cobbler-turned-actor was too short for Xerxes and the like.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruit seller! You astonish me\u2014I know no fruit seller.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who ran into you as we entered the street\u2014perhaps fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now remembered that a fruit seller, balancing a large basket of apples on his head, had nearly knocked me down by accident as we left the Rue C\u2014\u2014 for the street we were now in; but what that had to do with Chantilly, I could not imagine.\"}"
        }
