{"request_id":"79939793a9d438b294512cf9","rewritten_text":"By eight o’clock I had actually risen to an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It seemed clear to me that my ascent was not merely continuing but increasing in speed, and that the advantage would have been noticeable, in some small degree, even without the discharge of ballast I had undertaken. Pain shot through my head and ears at violent intervals, and I was still subject to occasional bleeding at the nose; yet overall I endured far less suffering than one might have anticipated. My breathing, however, grew more labored with every moment, each inhalation accompanied by a vexing spasmodic contraction of the chest. I therefore unpacked the condensing apparatus and made it ready for immediate use.

The sight of the earth from that stage in my rise was indeed magnificent. Toward the west, north, and south, as far as my vision could extend, stretched a limitless expanse of seemingly unruffled sea, deepening every instant into a richer blue and already betraying a faint hint of convexity. At an immense distance to the east, yet still clearly discernible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic littoral of France and Spain, and a small portion of the northern African continent. Not a trace of individual structures remained, and the proudest of cities had melted completely from the earth’s face. From the Rock of Gibraltar, now dwindled to a mere dim speck, the dark Mediterranean—studded with shining islands as heaven with stars—rolled eastward as far as my eye could follow, until its entire body of waters seemed at last to plunge headlong beyond the edge of the horizon; I found myself poised, as it were, listening for the thunder of that colossal cataract. Overhead, the sky was pitch black, and the stars shone out with brilliant clarity.

The pigeons appeared at this moment to be suffering greatly, and I resolved to grant them their freedom. I first unfastened a beautiful gray-mottled bird and set him upon the wicker rim. He displayed intense agitation, glancing uneasily about, fluttering his wings, and giving out loud coos, yet he could not muster the courage to leave the car. Finally I seized him and flung him some six yards away from the balloon. Contrary to my expectation, he made no attempt to descend, but fought violently to return, all the while emitting very shrill and piercing cries. He at last managed to regain his old perch on the rim, yet scarcely had he done so when his head drooped upon his breast, and he fell dead inside the car."}

