          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df62f495fffe16c111da876d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse, beautiful, intelligent, and wealthy, blessed with a comfortable home and cheerful disposition, seemed to possess some of life\u2019s greatest advantages, and had spent nearly twenty-one years with hardly anything to trouble or upset her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters of a deeply loving, indulgent father, and after her sister married, she had been the mistress of his house from an early age. Her mother had died so long ago that Emma could only dimly remember her embrace, and her place had been filled by an excellent governess who came close to being a mother in affection.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had been in Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s household for sixteen years, serving more as a friend than a governess, very fond of both daughters but especially Emma. Between them it was more like the closeness of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor stopped formally holding the role of governess, her gentle temperament had hardly allowed her to impose any restraint; and since that slight authority had long faded, they had lived together as friends who were mutually devoted, with Emma doing exactly as she pleased, highly valuing Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment but mostly guided by her own.\\n\\nThe real problems in Emma\u2019s situation were that she could too easily have her own way, and she had a tendency to think a little too highly of herself; these were the drawbacks that could tarnish her many pleasures. Yet the danger was so unnoticed at present that she did not consider them misfortunes at all.\\n\\nThen sorrow came\u2014a gentle sorrow\u2014but not from any unpleasant self-awareness. Miss Taylor married. It was the loss of Miss Taylor that first brought grief. On the wedding day of this beloved friend, Emma first sat in sustained sadness. Once the wedding was over and the bridal party gone, she and her father were left to dine together, with no expectation of a third person to enliven the long evening. After dinner her father, as usual, settled himself to sleep, leaving her only to sit and reflect on what she had lost.\\n\\nThe event offered every promise of happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of impeccable character, comfortable fortune, suitable age, and agreeable manners; and there was some satisfaction in thinking how selflessly and generously she had desired and encouraged the match. Still, it felt like a bleak day\u2019s work for her. She knew she would feel the absence of Miss Taylor every hour of every day. She thought of the past kindness\u2014the affection of sixteen years\u2014how Miss Taylor had taught and played with her since she was five, how she had dedicated all her efforts to engaging and entertaining her when she was well, and how she had nursed her through every childhood illness.\"}"
