          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a338f008671d7c7deb29c4c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cCome now, Tom,\u201d says I, \u201cseems like you could tell a body. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, _I_ ain\u2019t certain. But he\u2019s bound to have it. All nobility does.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just his way. If it didn\u2019t hit him to explain something, he wouldn\u2019t, no matter how long you worked at him.\\n\\nHe\u2019d settled all that coat-of-arms notion, so then he set in to finish the rest of that part\u2014which was to lay out a sorrowful inscription\u2014said Jim needed one, same as the lot of them. He cooked up a bunch, wrote them on a sheet, and read them off, this way:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart busted._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, deserted by the world and friends, fretted out his mourning life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit took to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, died a noble stranger, natural son to Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice shook while he read them, and he nearly went to pieces. After he was done he couldn\u2019t for the world decide which one Jim ought to scratch on the wall, they were all so fine; but in the end he concluded Jim should scratch every one of them. Jim said it would take a whole year to scratch that heap of stuff onto the logs with a nail, and he couldn\u2019t form letters anyway; but Tom promised to mark them out for him, so all Jim had to do was trace the lines. Then pretty soon he says:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think, the logs won\u2019t answer; they don\u2019t have log walls in a dungeon. We got to dig those words into rock. We\u2019ll fetch a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim claimed a rock was worse than logs; said scraping them into stone would take so cussed long he\u2019d never get loose. But Tom said he\u2019d let me help him. Then he glanced over to see how me and Jim were doing with the pens. It was downright aggravating slow, didn\u2019t give my hands any chance to heal from the sores, and we wasn\u2019t gaining any ground hardly; so Tom says:\\n\\n\u201cI know the way to manage it. We have to have one rock for the coat of arms and the mourning inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that very rock.\u201d\"}"
