          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6574a1aaa8d2bd794a35108c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He rose, left the room with my nurse, and my father entered almost immediately.\\n\\nNothing could have delighted me more in that instant than my father\u2019s arrival. I extended my hand to him and exclaimed,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nHe soothed me with assurances about their wellbeing and, lingering on those subjects dearest to me, tried to lift my sinking spirits; yet he soon perceived that cheerfulness cannot dwell in a prison. \u201cWhat a place for you to inhabit, my son!\u201d he said, casting a sorrowful glance at the barred windows and miserable surroundings. \u201cYou set out in search of happiness, yet a cruel fate seems to dog your steps. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe mere mention of my unfortunate, murdered friend was more agitation than my feeble state could bear; tears burst forth.\\n\\n\u201cAlas, yes, my father,\u201d I answered. \u201cSome most dreadful destiny hangs over me, and I must live to accomplish it, or else I should have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to talk long, for my fragile health demanded every precaution that might secure calm. Mr. Kirwin entered and insisted my strength not be spent by excessive effort. Yet my father\u2019s presence felt like that of a guardian angel, and little by little my health returned.\\n\\nAs the illness receded, a dark, oppressive melancholy took possession of me, and nothing could dispel it. Clerval\u2019s image appeared incessantly before my eyes, ghastly and slain. More than once the agitation these thoughts provoked made my friends fear a serious relapse. Alas! Why had they preserved so wretched and hateful a life? Surely it was that I might fulfil my fate, which was now nearing its conclusion. Soon\u2014very soon\u2014death would still these pulsings and free me from the crushing weight of anguish that drove me to the dust; and, in satisfying justice, I too would find rest. Then, though death seemed distant, the longing for it never left my mind; for hours I would sit motionless and mute, yearning for some vast upheaval that might entomb both me and my destroyer in its ruin.\\n\\nThe assizes season drew near. I had been three months imprisoned, and though still weak and ever in danger of relapse, I had to journey nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court convened. Mr. Kirwin himself undertook every duty of gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
