          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a63dd65c9dad621b756a4b11\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At least he thought so. But who could know? No, there had been nothing else. Out of vanity he had spared her. Out of hypocrisy he had worn a mask of goodness. For curiosity\u2019s sake he had tried to renounce himself. He saw that now.\\n\\nBut this murder\u2014was it going to haunt him forever? Was he always to be weighed down by his past? Was he truly going to confess? Never. Only one piece of evidence remained against him. The portrait itself\u2014that was the evidence. He would destroy it. Why had he kept it so long? Once it had pleased him to watch it change and grow old. Lately, he had felt no such pleasure. It had kept him awake at night. When he was away, he had been terrified that other eyes would look at it. It had thrown gloom over his passions. The mere memory of it had spoiled many moments of joy. It had been like a conscience to him. Yes, it had been his conscience. He would destroy it.\\n\\nHe looked around and saw the knife that had stabbed Basil Hallward. He had cleaned it many times until no stain remained. It was bright and gleaming. Just as it had killed the painter, so it would kill the painter\u2019s work and all it stood for. It would kill the past, and once that was dead, he would be free. It would kill this monstrous soul-life, and without its hideous warnings he would be at peace. He grabbed the blade and plunged it into the portrait.\\n\\nA scream rang out, followed by a crash. The scream was so full of agony that the startled servants woke and crept out of their rooms. Two gentlemen passing below in the square stopped and looked up at the grand house. They walked on until they met a policeman and brought him back. The man rang the bell several times, but there was no answer. Except for a light in one of the top windows, the house was completely dark. After a while he withdrew to a neighboring portico and kept watch.\\n\\n\u201cWhose house is that, Constable?\u201d asked the older of the two gentlemen.\\n\\n\u201cMr. Dorian Gray\u2019s, sir,\u201d the policeman replied.\\n\\nThey exchanged a look as they walked away and sneered. One of them was Sir Henry Ashton\u2019s uncle.\\n\\nInside, in the servants\u2019 quarters, the half-dressed staff were whispering to each other. Old Mrs. Leaf was crying and wringing her hands. Francis was pale as death.\\n\\nAfter about fifteen minutes, he gathered the coachman and one of the footmen, and they crept upstairs.\"}"
