          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"051dcb904a50b9e411d9b9b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIt must have been during that very period of Mr. Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cthat has always stirred my particular curiosity. It must have been when he first became acquainted with my father and sister. I never met him myself; I only heard about him; but there was something in how he behaved toward my father and sister then, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that never quite matched what I know of him now. It hinted at a different sort of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know everything, I know it all,\u201d cried Mrs. Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I ever met him, but he talked about them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I can probably satisfy you on points you wouldn\u2019t expect, and as for his marriage, I knew all about it at the time. I was part of every for and against; I was the friend he trusted with his hopes and plans; and though I didn\u2019t know his wife beforehand\u2014her lower social standing made that impossible\u2014I knew her throughout her life afterward, or at least until the last two years, and I can answer any question you might ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular question about her. I always understood they weren\u2019t a happy couple. But I would like to know why, at that time in his life, he snubbed my father\u2019s acquaintance the way he did. My father certainly meant to treat him kindly and properly. Why did Mr. Elliot pull away?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs. Smith, \u201cat that point in his life had one aim: to make his fortune, and by a faster route than the law. He was determined to make it through marriage. At least he was determined not to ruin it through an imprudent marriage; and I know he believed (whether rightly or not, I cannot say) that your father and sister, with their politeness and invitations, were plotting a match between the heir and the young lady, and such a match could never satisfy his idea of wealth and independence. That was why he withdrew, I assure you. He told me the entire story. He hid nothing from me. It was odd that, after leaving you behind in Bath, my first and closest acquaintance upon marrying should be your cousin, and that through him I should be continually hearing about your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
