          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37ab694ac6177c43f9f5a913\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances for observing them could not but arise. Anne was soon among all four together often enough to form an opinion, though too prudent to confess it at home, where she knew it would please neither husband nor wife; for while she suspected Louisa held the chief place in favour, she could not help thinking, as far as recollection and experience allowed her to judge, that Captain Wentworth was enamoured of neither. They were the more besotted with him; yet even that was hardly love. It was merely a brief fever of admiration; though it might, likely must, ripen into love with some. Charles Hayter seemed conscious of being undervalued, and still Henrietta occasionally looked torn between them. Anne yearned for the ability to show them all what they were doing, to point out the harms they were courting. She never imputed deceit to any. Her greatest comfort was to think Captain Wentworth entirely unaware of the suffering he caused. There was no triumph, no contemptible triumph, in his demeanour. He had likely never heard, and never considered, any claim Charles Hayter might have. His only fault lay in accepting attentions (for accept he must be said to do) from two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to withdraw. Three days went by without his appearing once at Uppercross; a very marked alteration. He even declined a standing dinner invitation; and when Mr Musgrove found him then with some large volumes before him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were certain all was not well, and discussed, with solemn faces, his studying himself into the grave. Mary hoped and believed he had been expressly dismissed by Henrietta, while her husband lived in constant expectation of seeing him the next day. Anne could only regard Charles Hayter as acting sensibly.\\n\\nOne morning, near this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth having gone shooting together, the sisters at the Cottage, seated quietly over their work, received a visit at the window from their counterparts at the Mansion-house.\\n\\nIt was a splendid November day, and the Miss Musgroves crossed the little grounds to stop merely for the purpose of announcing that they meant to take a long walk, and therefore concluded Mary could not wish to accompany them; yet when Mary, feeling some jealousy at not being thought a good walker, instantly answered, \u201cOh yes, I should like to join you very much, I quite enjoy a long walk,\u201d Anne felt convinced, by the sisters\u2019 looks, that it was exactly what they did not desire, and she once more admired that odd necessity produced by family habits, of everything needing to be told, and everything undertaken together, however unwelcome or inconvenient.\"}"
        }
