          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e658aca159864bcd0f10eae\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAllow me to point out, Sir Walter,\u201d Mr Shepherd said one morning at Kellynch Hall as he set down the newspaper, \u201cthat the current situation is working in our favour. This peace will send all our wealthy naval officers ashore, and they will all be wanting homes. There could not be a better moment, Sir Walter, to choose among tenants\u2014very responsible tenants. Many a fine fortune has been made during the war. If a rich admiral were to appear in our path, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very fortunate man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter replied. \u201cThat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would be quite a prize for him\u2014perhaps the greatest prize of all, even if he has already claimed any number of others. Wouldn\u2019t you agree, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he must at such wit, and continued, \u201cMay I point out, Sir Walter, that when it comes to business, gentlemen of the navy are easy to deal with. I have some experience of their methods, and I freely admit that they think generously and are just as likely to make excellent tenants as any group one might meet. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would venture to suggest is this: if rumours arise about your intention\u2014which must be expected, because we know how hard it is to keep one part of the world from prying into the actions of another. Consequence has its cost. I, John Shepherd, could hide any family matters I chose, for no one would bother to watch me. But Sir Walter Elliot has many eyes upon him, which may be quite difficult to avoid. Therefore, I take the liberty of saying I would not be greatly surprised if, despite all our caution, some whisper of the truth should leak out. If that happens, and applications inevitably follow, I believe any coming from our wealthy naval officers would be especially worthy of consideration. And let me add that I can be here in two hours at any time to spare you the trouble of replying.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter only nodded. But soon after, as he rose and paced the room, he remarked with sarcasm, \u201cI imagine there are few naval gentlemen who would not be astonished to find themselves in a house like this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would certainly look around and bless their good fortune,\u201d said Mrs Clay, for she was present; her father had driven her over\u2014nothing was so good for Mrs Clay\u2019s health as a drive to Kellynch. \u201cBut I entirely agree with my father that a sailor could be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
        }
      ],
