          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d44eed6ac731dd50b9863612\",\"rewritten_text\":\"How long I stayed in that stupor I cannot tell. It must have been a fair length of time, for when awareness slowly returned, dawn was breaking, the balloon hung at a tremendous height over a vast wilderness of ocean, and not the faintest strip of land showed anywhere on the wide horizon. Yet my sensations on recovering were not nearly as agonizing as one might expect. In truth, there was an early edge of madness in the calm way I began to examine my situation. I raised each hand to my eyes in turn and wondered what could have caused the swollen veins and the dreadful blackness of my fingernails. Then I carefully felt my head, shaking it again and again and touching it with anxious care, until at last I convinced myself it was not, as I had half suspected, larger than the balloon itself. After that, with a knowing air, I searched both breeches pockets, and, missing a set of tablets and a toothpick case, tried to understand how they could have vanished. Failing to explain it, I felt intensely annoyed. A new sensation of discomfort in the joint of my left ankle occurred to me, and a dim awareness of my situation began to grow. But, strangely, I was neither amazed nor horrified. If I felt anything, it was a kind of smug satisfaction at the cleverness I was about to show in freeing myself from this dilemma; not for a moment did I doubt my final safety. For a few minutes I stayed wrapped in deep thought. I clearly remember pressing my lips together, touching the side of my nose with my forefinger, and making the various gestures and expressions common to men who, settled in their armchairs, ponder complicated or weighty questions. Once I believed I had gathered my thoughts enough, I carefully, step by step, put my hands behind my back and unfastened the large iron buckle on the waistband of my trousers. The buckle had three teeth that, being rusty, turned very hard on their hinges. After 