{"request_id":"276ed2a7f3f04a585964246a","rewritten_text":"Summer rolled on, and, crowned by the sun’s burning rays, the pestilence loosed her unfailing arrows across the world. Peoples beneath her sway bowed their heads and perished. The corn that had sprung up abundantly lay in autumnal rot, while the mournful labourer, who ventured out to glean bread for his children, lay rigid and plague-stricken in the furrow. Majestic boughs of the green forests waved above the dying, who answered their solemn music with discordant cries. Painted birds darted through the shade; heedless deer rested unscathed on the fern—the oxen and horses wandered from their unattended stalls and fed among the wheat, for death singled out mankind alone.

With the progress of summer and mortality our dread increased. My poor beloved and I looked at one another and at our young ones. “We will save them, Idris,” I exclaimed, “I will save them. Years hence we shall recount to them these terrors, then past with their cause. Should they be the only survivors on earth, still they shall live, nor shall their cheeks grow pale, nor their sweet voices falter.” Our eldest boy in some measure comprehended the scene around him, and would at times, with grave looks, inquire of me the cause of such wide ruin. Yet he was only ten, and the buoyancy of youth soon dispelled unseasonable care from his brow. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful infant ignorant of pain or sorrow, would toss back his fair curls from his eyes, make the halls ring with his mirth, and contrive a thousand artless ways to draw our notice to his sport. Clara, our gentle, lovely Clara, was our support, our comfort, our joy. She made it her task to attend the sick, console the mournful, aid the aged, and share the diversions and kindle the gaiety of the young. She glided through the chambers like a benignant spirit sent from the celestial realm to brighten our dark hour with unearthly splendour. Gratitude and blessing marked the path of her presence. Yet, when she stood before us in unpretending simplicity, playing with our children or with girlish diligence performing small kind services for Idris, one marveled by what fair trait of her pure beauty, by what gentle tone of her thrilling voice, so much heroism, wisdom, and active benevolence could reside.

The summer dragged painfully, for we trusted that winter might at least check the plague. To expect its utter extinction was a hope too precious—too heartfelt—to be spoken. When such a thought escaped unawares, the listeners, in a tempest of tears and sobs, revealed how deep their fears and how scant their expectations."}

