          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4bcaaa51fab67a7e0920802\",\"rewritten_text\":\"That little conversation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma a great deal of pleasure. As she strolled around the lawn the next morning, it was one of the best memories she savored from the ball. She was delighted they had reached such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that they felt so similarly about both husband and wife; and his praise of Harriet, his admission in her favor, was especially satisfying. The Eltons\u2019 rudeness, which had threatened for a few minutes to ruin the rest of her evening, had actually produced some of its greatest delights; and she anticipated another happy outcome\u2014the end of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From the way Harriet spoke of the incident before they left the ballroom, Emma felt strong hope. It seemed as if Harriet\u2019s eyes had suddenly been opened and she now saw that Mr. Elton was not the superior man she had believed him to be. The fever had broken, and Emma could scarcely fear that the pulse would quicken again because of dangerous kindness. She was counting on the Eltons\u2019 ill will to provide all the pointed neglect still needed. With Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too much in love, and Mr. Knightley not wanting to quarrel with her, how wonderfully happy the summer ahead must be! \\n\\nShe was not going to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, as he needed to be home by noon. She felt no regret. \\n\\nAfter sorting through everything and setting it to rights, she was just turning toward the house, spirits refreshed for whatever the two little boys and their grandfather might demand, when the heavy iron gate swung open and two people walked in\u2014people she could scarcely have expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! One instant was enough to convince her that something remarkable had happened. Harriet was pale and frightened, and he was trying to comfort her. The iron gates and the front door were less than twenty yards apart; in a moment all three were in the hall, and Harriet dropped into a chair and fainted. \\n\\nA young lady who faints must be revived; questions must be asked, and surprises explained. Such events are fascinating, but their suspense cannot last long. In a few minutes Emma knew everything. \\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone out for a walk together and taken a route\u2014the Richmond road\u2014which, though apparently public enough to seem safe, led them into danger. About half a mile beyond Highbury, the road took a sudden turn and, shaded by elms on both sides, became quite secluded for some distance; and when the young women had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead, on a broader patch of grass to the side, a group of gypsies.\"}"
