          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b295d75394fe8281368403ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was somewhat taken aback by the match, yet she spoke more of her heartfelt wish for their happiness than of her surprise, nor could Elizabeth convince her to see it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia were never tempted to envy Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins remained merely a clergyman, and the news affected them only as something to circulate about Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not help feeling triumphant at being able to remind Mrs. Bennet that she now had a daughter advantageously settled, and she visited Longbourn more frequently than usual to proclaim her delight, though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour expressions and spiteful comments might have driven delight away.\\n\\nA constraint between Elizabeth and Charlotte kept both silent on the subject; Elizabeth believed that true confidence could never revive between them. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her cling more tenderly to her sister, whose integrity and sensibility she felt could never be doubted, and whose happiness she watched over with mounting concern, as Bingley had been absent a week and no word of his return had arrived.\\n\\nJane had answered Caroline\u2019s letter promptly and now counted the days until she might expect another. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks reached them on Tuesday, directed to their father and written with all the solemn gratitude a year\u2019s residence might have inspired. Having relieved his conscience, he exulted with exuberant phrases over his felicity in winning the affection of their amiable neighbour, Miss Lucas, and declared it was solely to enjoy her company that he so readily accepted their invitation to Longbourn, where he hoped to arrive on Monday fortnight; for Lady Catherine, he added, so warmly endorsed his marriage that she wished it to occur as soon as possible, which he trusted would compel his amiable Charlotte to fix an early day for making him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMrs. Bennet could no longer view Mr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire with pleasure. Rather, she was now as inclined as her husband to complain of it. How odd that he should come to Longbourn instead of Lucas Lodge; it was likewise highly inconvenient and extremely troublesome. She detested having guests in the house when her health was so delicate, and lovers were, of all people, the most irksome.\"}"
