{"request_id":"8d4dc859f049ce625278469c","rewritten_text":"He wrote that he was wasting his time where he was and that letters from London friends urged him to return and finish the talks about his Indian venture. He could not put off leaving any longer, and since his trip to London might soon be followed by his longer voyage, he asked me to give him as much of my company as I could. He begged me to leave my lonely island and meet him in Perth so we could travel south together. The letter stirred me back to life a little, and I decided to leave the island in two days.

Before departing, I had a task that filled me with dread: I had to pack my chemical instruments, which meant entering the room where I had done my hateful work and handling the tools whose very sight sickened me. At daybreak the next morning I gathered my courage and unlocked the laboratory door. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered on the floor, and I almost felt as if I had torn apart living human flesh. I paused to steady myself before stepping inside. With shaking hands I carried the instruments out, but I realized I could not leave the remains to stir horror and suspicion among the villagers. I therefore placed them in a basket with many stones, planning to throw it into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could match the change in my feelings since the night the demon appeared. I had once viewed my promise with dark despair, as something that must be completed no matter the cost; but now it was as if a film had been lifted from my eyes and I could finally see. The thought of resuming my work never crossed my mind. The threat I had heard still weighed on me, yet it did not occur to me that any choice of mine could prevent it. I was convinced that creating another like the fiend I had already made would be the worst, most selfish act imaginable, and I pushed away any thought that might lead me to another conclusion."}

