          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"538b42f3f35ddb01475e7af6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Cut off from ordinary resources, the analyst immerses himself in his opponent\u2019s mindset, identifies with it, and often sees in an instant the only tactics\u2014even ridiculously simple ones\u2014by which he can lure that opponent into error or rush him into miscalculation. Whist has long been recognized for its impact on what we call the calculating power, and men of the highest intellect have been known to take an apparently inexplicable pleasure in it while dismissing chess as frivolous. There is unquestionably nothing of a similar kind that tests the faculty of analysis so severely. The best chess player in Christendom may be little more than the best chess player, but mastery of whist implies the ability to succeed in all those more important ventures where mind struggles with mind. By mastery I mean that perfection in the game which includes grasping every source from which legitimate advantage can be drawn. These sources are not only numerous but diverse, often hidden in corners of thought completely inaccessible to the ordinary understanding. To observe closely is to remember clearly, and the chess player with strong concentration will do well enough at whist, while Hoyle\u2019s rules\u2014which rest on the mechanics of the game\u2014are broadly understandable. Thus a good memory and a habit of playing \u201cby the book\u201d are usually seen as the entire secret of good play. But true skill is shown in matters that lie beyond rule. The analyst silently makes a multitude of observations and deductions. His partners may do the same, yet the difference in the scope of information obtained lies not so much in the soundness of the reasoning as in the quality of the observation. The essential knowledge is what to observe. Our player places no limits on himself; nor, simply because the game is everything, does he refuse conclusions drawn from things outside the game. He studies his partner\u2019s face, comparing it carefully with the expression of each opponent. He considers how every player arranges the cards in hand, often counting trump after trump and honor after honor by the glances the holders give them. He notices every change of expression as play advances, gathering insights from the shifts that signal certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way a trick is gathered, he judges whether the taker can secure another in that suit. He recognizes a feint by the way a card is laid on the table. A casual word, the accidental dropping or turning of a card and the anxiety or nonchalance shown in covering it, the counting of tricks and the order in which they are stacked, embarrassment, hesitation, excitement, or agitation\u2014all of these give his seemingly intuitive mind clues to the true state of play.\"}"
