{"request_id":"9f1bc19407ce43f2b15265c5","rewritten_text":"I encountered several funerals; they were sparsely attended and the onlookers treated them as omens of the worst to come. Some watched the processions with fierce curiosity—others ran away in fear—some cried out loud.

After immediately helping the sick, Adrian’s main effort had been to hide the symptoms and progress of the plague from London’s residents. He knew that fear and gloomy expectations helped the disease; that despair and constant worry made people physically easier to infect. So no disturbing sights were visible: most shops stayed open, and there was still a reasonable crowd of passersby. Yet, though the appearance of an infected city was avoided, to me—who had not seen it since the plague began—London looked changed enough. No carriages passed, weeds grew tall in the streets; the houses seemed deserted; most shutters were closed; and the people I met had a pale, frightened stare, far from the usual brisk manner of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as it clattered toward the Protectoral Palace—and the fashionable streets leading there looked even more dreary and empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded—it was his hour for audiences. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited, watching people go in and out. They were mainly from the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had vanished with the stoppage of trade and the country’s usual drive for profit in every field. There was anxiety, sometimes terror, in the newcomers, sharply contrasting with the resigned, even satisfied faces of those who had been admitted. I could see my friend’s influence in their brisker steps and brighter expressions. When the clock struck two, no more were let in; the disappointed left in sullen or sorrowful silence, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was struck by how much healthier Adrian looked. He no longer drooped like a spring flower grown too tall, bowed down by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were bright, his face calm, and an air of focused energy infused his whole person, so different from his former lethargy. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting petitions or recording notes from that day’s hearings. Two or three petitioners still remained. I admired his fairness and patience. Those who could afford to live outside London, he urged to leave at once, providing the means to do so."}

