          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"774bb17d43b071307ee48826\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He always pretended Peter had written him those letters, but that was a lie; he got every single one out of that young fool we paddled to the steamboat. Mary Jane brought in the letter her father left, and the king read it aloud and cried over it. It gave the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls, and it gave the tanyard (which was doing fine business), along with some other houses and land (worth about seven thousand), plus three thousand dollars in gold to Harvey and William, and it told exactly where the six thousand in cash was hidden in the cellar. These two crooks said they\u2019d bring it up and keep everything honest and aboveboard, and they told me to come with a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag they poured it out on the floor. It was a beautiful sight, all those gold coins. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said, \u201cOh, this is nothing great, oh no, I don\u2019t think so! Why, Bilge, it beats the Nonesuch, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d The duke said it surely did. They pawed at the gold coins, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle on the floor; then the king said, \u201cThere\u2019s no use talking; being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs who got left is the right line for you and me, Bilge. This is what comes from trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there ain\u2019t any better way.\u201d Most people would have been satisfied with the pile and taken it on faith, but no, they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred fifteen dollars short. The king said, \u201cDamn him, I wonder what he did with that four hundred fifteen?\u201d They worried over it for a while and searched all around. Then the duke said, \u201cWell, he was a pretty sick man, and he probably made a mistake. That must be it. Best to let it go and keep quiet about it. We can spare it.\u201d \u201cOh, sure, we can spare it. I don\u2019t care about that\u2014it\u2019s the count I\u2019m thinking about. We want to be completely square and open and aboveboard here, you know. We want to haul this money upstairs and count it in front of everybody\u2014so there\u2019s nothing suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
