          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"910885f01ac1f34b967b15b2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They passed silent brickfields. The fog thinned a little, and he could see the odd, bottle-shaped kilns with orange tongues of flame fanning out. A dog barked as they went by, and somewhere far off in the dark a stray sea gull screamed. The horse stumbled in a rut, veered aside, and broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled once more over rough-paved streets. Most windows were dark, but now and then bizarre silhouettes appeared against a lit blind. He watched them with curiosity. They moved like grotesque marionettes and gestured like living things. He loathed them. A dull fury filled his heart. As they turned a corner, a woman shouted something at them from an open door, and two men chased the hansom for about a hundred yards. The driver lashed at them with his whip.\\n\\nPeople say passion makes thought run in circles. Certainly, with an awful repetition, Dorian Gray\u2019s bitten lips shaped and reshaped those subtle sentences about soul and sense, until he found in them the complete expression of his mood and, by intellectual approval, justified passions that even without such justification would still have ruled him. From cell to cell of his brain crept the same thought, and the fierce desire to live, the most terrible of all human hungers, sharpened every trembling nerve and fiber. Ugliness, once hateful to him because it made things real, became precious to him now for that very reason. Ugliness was the only reality. The coarse fight, the filth-stained den, the raw violence of chaotic life, the sheer vileness of thief and outcast were more vivid, with their intense immediacy, than all the graceful forms of art, the dreamy shadows of song. They were what he needed for oblivion. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the driver jerked the carriage to a stop at the top of a dark lane. Over the low roofs and jagged chimney stacks of the houses rose black ship masts. Wreaths of white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere around here, sir, isn\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian jolted, looked around, and replied, \u201cThis will do.\u201d He jumped out, thrust the driver the extra fare he had promised, and hurried toward the quay. Here and there a lantern glowed at the stern of a huge merchantman. Its light quivered and fractured in the puddles. A red glare spilled from an outbound steamer taking on coal. The slimy pavement gleamed like a soaked raincoat.\"}"
