          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a5d2d19cd333023f2af3a50\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I met the curious stranger I am going to describe while touring Warwick Castle. Three things drew me to him: his open simplicity, his astonishing knowledge of old armor, and the peaceful ease of his company\u2014since he did all the talking. We fell together, as quiet people do, at the back of the guided group, and he immediately began saying things that caught my interest. As he spoke, soft and pleasant and smooth, it was as if he slipped out of this world and into some far-off era, some forgotten country, and he cast such a spell that I seemed to walk among ghosts and shadows and dust and mold of gray antiquity, conversing with a relic of it. He talked of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and the rest of the Round Table exactly as I would talk about my closest friends or enemies or neighbors\u2014and he grew so old, so incredibly old and faded and dry and musty, as he went on. Suddenly he turned to me and said, as calmly as if mentioning the weather or any common topic\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know about transmigration of souls; do you know about changing epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI said I had not heard of it. He was so uninterested\u2014like people mentioning the weather\u2014that he did not notice whether I answered or not. There was half a moment of silence, broken by the droning voice of the paid guide:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, sixth century, time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; note the round hole through the chain mail in the left breast; can\u2019t explain it; supposed to have been made by a bullet since firearms were invented\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy acquaintance smiled\u2014not a modern smile, but one that must have vanished from use many centuries ago\u2014and muttered to himself:\\n\\n\u201cKnow you well, I saw it done.\u201d Then, after a pause, added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBy the time I recovered from the electric shock of that remark, he had disappeared.\\n\\nAll that evening I sat by my fire at the Warwick Arms, lost in a dream of the old days, while rain beat on the windows and wind roared along the eaves and corners. From time to time I dipped into old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s enchanting book, fed on its rich feast of wonders and adventures, breathed in the scent of its obsolete names, and dreamed again.\"}"
