          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c3281a6f95233abfd60f4ed9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Smallpox! I rushed to the king and whispered in his ear, \u201cOut the door this instant, sire! The woman is dying of the disease that ravaged Camelot two years ago.\u201d He did not move. \u201cTruly I will stay\u2014and lend aid.\u201d I whispered again, \u201cKing, you must not. You have to go.\u201d \u201cYou mean well, and your words are not unwise. But it would be a disgrace for a king to show fear, and a shame for a knight to hold back his hand when someone needs help. Peace, I will not go. You must go. The Church\u2019s ban spares me but forbids you to be here, and she will deal harshly with you if word reaches her of your trespass.\u201d His position was perilous and might cost him his life, yet arguing was useless. If his knightly honor was engaged, that ended the matter; he would stay no matter what, and I knew it. So I let it go. The woman spoke: \u201cGood sir, in your kindness will you climb the ladder there and tell me what you find? Do not fear to speak plainly, for there are times when even a mother\u2019s heart cannot break any further\u2014having already broken.\u201d \u201cWait,\u201d said the king, \u201cand give the woman food. I will go.\u201d He set down the knapsack. I turned to head out, but he had already moved. He paused, looking down at a man lying in dim light who had not yet noticed us or spoken. \u201cIs this your husband?\u201d the king asked. \u201cYes.\u201d \u201cIs he sleeping?\u201d \u201cGod be thanked for that mercy, yes\u2014these three hours. Where can I pay the full price of my gratitude, for my heart is overflowing with it for this sleep he sleeps now.\u201d I said, \u201cWe will be careful. We will not wake him.\u201d \u201cAh no, you won\u2019t, for he is dead.\u201d \u201cDead?\u201d \u201cYes, and it is a triumph to know it! No one can hurt him or insult him any longer. He is in heaven now and happy\u2014or if not there, then he remains in hell and is content, for there he will find neither abbot nor bishop. We were children together; we have been man and wife these five and twenty years and never apart until today. Think how long that is to love and suffer side by side. This morning he lost his senses, and in his imagining we were boy and girl again wandering through joyful fields; he kept wandering, lightheartedly chatting, and entered those other fields we know nothing of, shut away from mortal sight.\u201d\"}"
