          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1e68e55c99b501f7975da040\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Poor Jack was later killed in a duel with Lord Canterville on Wandsworth Common, and Lady Barbara succumbed to a broken heart at Tunbridge Wells before the year ended, so in every respect it had been a tremendous triumph. It was, nonetheless, an exceedingly intricate disguise\u2014if one may use such stage jargon for a mystery among the higher-natural world\u2014and required him a full three hours to ready himself. At last all was in order, and he took great pride in his appearance. The heavy leather riding-boots belonging to the costume were a trifle too big for him, and only one of the pair of horse-pistols could be found, yet overall he was content, and at a quarter past one he slipped out of the wainscot and stole down the corridor. Reaching the chamber occupied by the twins\u2014called the Blue Bed Chamber owing to its blue hangings\u2014he noticed the door slightly open. Desiring a dramatic entrance, he flung it wide, whereupon a hefty jug of water crashed down on him, drenching him through and narrowly missing his left shoulder. At the same instant, he heard muffled peals of laughter coming from the four-poster. The shock to his nerves proved so strong that he bolted back to his own room at top speed, and by the following day he was confined to bed with a violent cold. His sole consolation in the whole misadventure was that he had not taken his head with him, for had he done so the outcome might have been extremely grave.\\n\\nHe now abandoned the hope of frightening this insolent American clan, and as a rule contented himself with padding through the halls in felt slippers, a thick red muffler round his throat against the draughts, and a small arquebuse in case the twins attacked. The final insult reached him on the nineteenth of September. He had descended to the great entrance hall, convinced he would be safe there, and passed the time by making sardonic comments about the large Saroni photographs of the United States Minister and his wife, which had replaced the Canterville portraits. He wore only a simple long shroud sprinkled with churchyard mould, had bound his jaw with a strip of yellow linen, and carried a small lantern along with a sexton\u2019s spade.\"}"
