          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d7c0b2a3ba4ddc1e812f73bc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These memories weighed on Felix\u2019s heart and made him the most miserable of his family when I first saw him. He could have endured poverty, and as long as it was the price of his virtue he even took pride in it, but the Turk\u2019s ingratitude and the loss of his beloved Safie were bitter wounds he could not heal. The Arabian\u2019s arrival now brought new life to him.\\n\\nWhen word spread in Leghorn that Felix had lost his wealth and status, the merchant ordered his daughter to forget her lover and prepare to return to her homeland. Safie\u2019s generous spirit was angered by this command. She tried to reason with her father, but he stalked away, repeating his harsh order.\\n\\nA few days later, the Turk hurried into his daughter\u2019s room and told her he had reason to believe his stay in Leghorn had been exposed and that the French government would soon seize him. He had therefore hired a ship to take him to Constantinople and would sail in a few hours. He planned to leave his daughter with a trusted servant, who would follow later with most of his wealth, which had not yet arrived in Leghorn.\\n\\nOnce alone, Safie considered her next steps in this emergency. Living in Turkey repelled her; both her religion and her feelings turned her against it. Some papers of her father\u2019s, which fell into her hands, told her of her lover\u2019s exile and the name of his current refuge. She hesitated for some time, but finally made her decision. Taking a few of her own jewels and some money, she left Italy with an attendant, a native of Leghorn who spoke the common language of Turkey, and set out for Germany.\\n\\nShe reached a town about twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage without trouble, but there her attendant fell gravely ill. Safie nursed her with devoted care, yet the poor woman died, leaving Safie alone, unable to speak the language of the country and completely ignorant of the world\u2019s ways. Even so, she found help. The Italian had given the name of their destination, and after her death the mistress of the house where they had stayed made sure Safie reached her lover\u2019s cottage safely.\"}"
