          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d3c93c111753624f8e554ead\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must point out, Sir Walter,\u201d Mr Shepherd said one morning at Kellynch Hall as he set down the newspaper, \u201cthat the current situation works in our favour. This peace is sending all our wealthy naval officers home. They will all want houses. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, to have a selection of tenants, very reliable tenants. Many great fortunes have been made during the war. If a rich admiral crossed our path, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very lucky man, Shepherd,\u201d Sir Walter answered. \u201cThat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would be a prize for him, the greatest prize of all, no matter how many others he has taken before; wouldn\u2019t it, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he should at such wit, and continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI would note, Sir Walter, that in business, naval gentlemen are easy to deal with. I have some experience with how they conduct business, and I must admit they have generous ideas and are as likely as any group to make good tenants. Therefore, Sir Walter, I suggest that if rumours start spreading about your intention\u2014which we must consider possible, since we all know how hard it is to keep one half of the world from learning what the other half is doing, and fame must pay its price\u2014I, John Shepherd, might hide any family matters I wished, for no one would think it worth watching me; but Sir Walter Elliot has too many eyes upon him to escape notice. So I will say this much: I will not be surprised if, despite our caution, some rumour of the truth leaks out. Assuming it does, as I was about to say, and applications inevitably follow, I think those from our wealthy naval commanders would be especially worth considering. I also beg leave to add that I can be here within two hours at any time, so you need not trouble yourself with replies.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter only nodded. But soon afterward, rising and pacing the room, he said with sarcasm\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are few gentlemen of the navy, I imagine, who would not find themselves astonished to be living in such a house.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would look around, certainly, and bless their good luck,\u201d said Mrs Clay, who was present. Her father had brought her over, as a drive to Kellynch was the best thing for her health. \u201cBut I entirely agree with my father in thinking a sailor could be a very suitable tenant.\u201d\"}"
