          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"efb7aa57a0e257277621e5c8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I call on you, spirits of the dead, and on you, wandering agents of vengeance, to help and guide me in my work. Let the cursed, hellish monster drink deeply of agony; let him feel the despair that now tortures me.\\n\\nI began my plea with solemn awe, almost sure the shades of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion, but fury seized me as I finished, and rage choked my voice.\\n\\nA loud, fiendish laugh broke through the still night. It rang in my ears, echoed by the mountains, and I felt as if all hell surrounded me with mockery. In that moment I might have gone mad and ended my miserable life, but my vow was heard, and I was kept for vengeance. The laughter faded, and a voice I knew and hated, close to my ear, whispered clearly, \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have chosen to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward the source of the sound, but the devil slipped from my grasp. Suddenly the broad moon rose and shone on his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI chased him, and for months this has been my duty. With only the faintest clues, I followed the twists of the Rhone, but in vain. The blue Mediterranean came into view, and by strange luck I saw the fiend board a ship at night bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same vessel, yet he escaped; I know not how.\\n\\nThrough the wilds of Tartary and Russia, though he still evaded me, I kept to his trail. Sometimes the peasants, terrified by his hideous appearance, told me where he had gone; sometimes he himself, afraid I might lose his track, despair, and die, left signs to lead me. Snow fell on my head, and I saw the mark of his huge steps on the white plain. You who are just beginning life, who have yet to know care or agony, how can you grasp what I have felt and still feel? Cold, hunger, and exhaustion were the least of my pains; I was cursed by some devil and carried my eternal hell with me. Yet a spirit of good still followed me, directing my steps and, when I complained most, suddenly freeing me from seemingly impossible dangers. Sometimes, when hunger overcame me and nature gave way, a meal awaited me in the wilderness, restoring my strength and hope.\"}"
