          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"01da9e4a37c4299a81eb52f7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He always pretended Peter told him what to write, but that was a lie; he pulled every last bit of it out of that young fool we paddled up to the steamboat. Mary Jane brought the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud and cried over it. The letter left the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls, and it left the tanyard\u2014which was doing good business\u2014plus some other buildings and land worth about seven thousand, and three thousand dollars in gold to Harvey and William, and it told where six thousand in cash was hidden down in the cellar. So those two frauds said they\u2019d go fetch it and make everything honest and aboveboard, and told me to come along with a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag they spilled it on the floor. It was a grand sight, all those gold pieces. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said: \u201cOh, this sure isn\u2019t fine, not one bit! No, I reckon not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d The duke said it did. They pawed the gold pieces, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle down on the floor; and the king said: \u201cNo use arguing; being brothers to a rich dead man and speaking for foreign heirs who got left out\u2014that\u2019s the life for you and me, Bilge. This comes of trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the end. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there\u2019s no better way.\u201d Most folks would have been satisfied with the pile and taken it on faith; but no, they had to count it. So they counted, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king said: \u201cBlast him, I wonder what he did with that four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d They fretted over it awhile and searched all around. Then the duke said: \u201cWell, he was mighty sick, and likely he made a mistake\u2014I figure that\u2019s it. Best to let it go and keep quiet. We can spare it.\u201d \u201cOh, sure, we can spare it. I don\u2019t care about that\u2014it\u2019s the count that bothers me. We want to be mighty square and open and aboveboard here, you know. We need to carry this money upstairs and count it in front of everybody\u2014then nothing looks suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
