          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8ef59e1944888aac6262e7f6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind her, except the memory that it had ever been. Anne had not stepped inside Kellynch since she left Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need, and the few chances she had of going to the Hall she managed to avoid. Her first return was to take up her place again in the modern, elegant rooms of the Lodge, and to cheer its mistress. Lady Russell\u2019s happiness at seeing her held a bit of worry. She knew who had been staying at Uppercross. Still, whether Anne truly looked a little fuller and fresher or Lady Russell only imagined it, Anne enjoyed the congratulations. She amused herself by linking them with her cousin\u2019s silent admiration and by hoping she might enjoy a second spring of youth and beauty. Once they began talking, Anne quickly felt a shift in her own mind. The subjects that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, that she thought dismissed and had had to hide among the Musgroves, now mattered less. Lately she had even lost sight of her father and sister and of Bath itself. Their concerns had been pushed below those of Uppercross. So when Lady Russell returned to their old hopes and fears, praising the house taken in Camden Place and regretting that Mrs. Clay still lived with them, Anne would have been ashamed for anyone to know how much more she was thinking of Lyme and Louisa Musgrove and all the people there. The home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick interested her far more than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness with Mrs. Clay. She actually had to work to meet Lady Russell with even the appearance of equal concern for topics that should by nature be her first duty. Their talk on another matter began with a little unease. They had to speak about the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had heard the whole account within five minutes of arriving the day before, yet there was still more to say: she had to ask, to regret the rashness, to mourn the outcome, and Captain Wentworth\u2019s name had to pass both their lips. Anne felt she did not handle it as well as Lady Russell. She could not speak his name and look straight at Lady Russell until she decided to explain briefly what she believed about the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
