{"request_id":"ca1f7d6ab4cc9e0f4426cae0","rewritten_text":"Yet this reflection, which once upheld me at the outset of my endeavors, now only grinds me deeper into the dust. All my calculations and expectations have vanished, and like the archangel who dared omnipotence, I am fettered in unending torment. My imagination burned brightly, and my powers of reasoning and persistence were equally strong; through their conjunction I conceived and achieved the making of a man. Even now I cannot recall without agitation the daydreams that fed me while the task remained unfinished. In thought I walked amid the heavens, now rejoicing in my abilities, now blazing with anticipation of their consequences. From childhood I was saturated with exalted hopes and proud designs; how low have I fallen! My friend, could you have known me formerly, you would not recognize the ruin before you. Despondency rarely entered my heart; some lofty fate seemed to carry me forward until I fell, never, never to rise again.

Am I destined to lose this splendid spirit? I yearned for a companion; I searched for one who might sympathize with and cherish me. Here, upon these bleak seas, I have discovered such a soul, yet I dread that I have secured him only to appreciate his worth and watch him vanish. I would reconcile him to life, but he rejects the notion.

"I thank you, Walton," he replied, "for your benevolent designs toward so wretched a being; but when you speak of new bonds and fresh attachments, do you imagine any can replace the lost? Could any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman another Elizabeth? Even where the affections are not deeply stirred by superior excellence, the companions of our childhood retain a peculiar power over the mind which few later friends can claim. They know our childish tendencies which, though they may be altered, are never eradicated; they can interpret our actions with surer judgments concerning the purity of our motives. A sister or brother will never, unless early signs exist, suspect each other of treachery, while another friend, however devoted, may still be viewed with doubt despite himself. Yet my friends were precious not merely through habit and association but by their own worth; wherever I go, the gentle voice of Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval whisper ceaselessly in my ear. They are dead, and in such loneliness only one feeling persuades me to continue breathing. Were I engaged in some lofty enterprise or scheme vast in benefit to mankind, then I could endure to see it finished.""}

