          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d29cc4f017bb7a9dbd86be6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted a fiery horse, the same one that had carried him to victory in Greece. He wore himself out with exhausting exercise, trying to drown the torment of his mind in sheer physical effort.\\n\\nSlowly he came back to himself. At last, as someone might recover from poison, he raised his head above the feverish haze of passion into the calm air of clear thought. He considered what he ought to do. He was shocked by how much time had passed while madness, not reason, had guided his actions. A month had gone by, and in all that time he had not seen Evadne. Her hold, once bound to the lasting feelings of his heart, had greatly weakened. He was no longer her slave, no longer her lover; he would never see her again, and by this complete return he would earn Perdita\u2019s trust.\\n\\nYet when he tried to decide, his imagination summoned the miserable home of the Greek girl\u2014 the place she had chosen, from noble and upright motives, instead of a more comfortable one. He thought of her splendour when he first met her, of her life in Constantinople surrounded by every form of eastern luxury; then of her current poverty, her daily labour, her desolate loneliness, her thin face marked by hunger. Compassion rose within him. He would visit her once more; he would find some way to restore her to society and to the privileges of her rank. After that, their parting would come naturally.\\n\\nAgain he remembered how, during this long month, he had avoided Perdita, fleeing her as if she were the rebuke of his own conscience. But he was alert now. All this would be corrected, and future devotion would erase the memory of the single stain on their peaceful life. Thinking of this made him cheerful, and he carefully set out the course he would follow. He recalled promising Perdita that he would attend that very evening\u2014the nineteenth of October, the anniversary of his election as Protector\u2014a festival held in his honour. Let this celebration be a good omen for future happiness. First he would stop by Evadne. He would not stay long, but he owed her some explanation and compensation for his sudden absence. Then he would return to Perdita, to the world he had neglected, to social duties, to the splendour of rank, to the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the events described earlier, Perdita had expected a complete change in Raymond\u2019s behaviour and habits. She looked for open communication and a return to the affectionate companionship that had been the joy of her life.\"}"
