          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"94a552ae380c6add542505e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Otis was quite upset by Lord Canterville\u2019s refusal and urged him to reconsider, but the good-natured peer stood firm. He eventually persuaded the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift the ghost had given her, and when, in the spring of 1890, the young Duchess of Cheshire appeared at the Queen\u2019s first drawing-room after her marriage, her jewels were the talk of everyone. Virginia received the coronet, which is the reward of all well-behaved little American girls, and married her sweetheart as soon as he came of age. They were both so charming and loved each other so dearly that everyone was delighted with the match, except for the old Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had tried to snare the Duke for one of her seven unmarried daughters and had thrown no fewer than three lavish dinner parties to that end, and, strangely enough, Mr. Otis himself. Mr. Otis liked the young Duke very much personally, but in principle he disapproved of titles and, as he said, \u201cwas not without fear that, amid the softening influences of a pleasure-loving aristocracy, the true ideals of republican simplicity might be forgotten.\u201d His objections, however, were completely brushed aside, and I believe that when he walked up the aisle of St. George\u2019s, Hanover Square, with his daughter on his arm, there was not a prouder man in all of England.\\n\\nAfter their honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess went down to Canterville Chase, and the day after they arrived they took an afternoon walk to the lonely churchyard near the pine woods. There had been considerable debate at first about the inscription on Sir Simon\u2019s tombstone, but it was finally decided to engrave only the old gentleman\u2019s initials and the verse from the library window. The Duchess brought some lovely roses, which she scattered on the grave, and after they had stood there for a while they wandered into the ruined chancel of the old abbey. The Duchess sat down on a fallen pillar while her husband lay at her feet, smoking a cigarette and gazing up into her beautiful eyes. Suddenly he flicked away the cigarette, took her hand, and said, \u201cVirginia, a wife shouldn\u2019t keep secrets from her husband.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Cecil! I have no secrets from you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, you do,\u201d he replied with a smile. \u201cYou never told me what happened when you were shut up with the ghost.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have never told anyone, Cecil,\u201d Virginia said gravely.\"}"
