{"request_id":"bef037769b03a88895604e45","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to know that no disaster has marked the start of the enterprise you feared would end badly. I arrived here yesterday, and my first task is to assure my dear sister of my good health and rising confidence in my venture’s success.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, a cold northern breeze touches my cheeks, steadying my nerves and filling me with joy. Do you feel that, too? This wind, coming from the regions I am heading toward, gives me a preview of those icy lands. Inspired by this promising wind, my dreams grow more intense and clear. I cannot make myself believe that the pole is only frost and desolation; in my imagination it is a place of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun never disappears, its wide disk skimming the horizon and spreading constant light. There—if you will allow me to trust the reports of earlier navigators—snow and frost retreat, and while sailing over a calm sea, we might be carried to a land that surpasses in wonders and beauty any other known region. Its plants and landscapes may be unlike anything else on earth, just as the movements of the heavens are surely unique in those still-unknown solitudes. What can we not expect in a country of endless light? I might discover the power that draws the compass needle or make celestial observations that only this journey can turn from seeming oddities into stable truths. I will satisfy my fierce curiosity by seeing a part of the world no one has visited and may walk on land never touched by human feet. These promises are enough to conquer any fear of danger or death and make me begin this demanding voyage with the joy a child feels when he sets off in a small boat with his holiday friends, exploring up his home river. Yet even if these hopes prove false, you cannot deny the priceless benefit I will give to humanity by finding a passage near the pole to countries that now take months to reach, or by uncovering the secret of the magnet, which, if it can be solved at all, requires an enterprise like mine.

These thoughts have calmed the nervousness with which I began my letter, and my heart now burns with an enthusiasm that lifts me to the heavens, for nothing soothes the mind like a steady purpose—a point on which the soul can fix its intellectual gaze."}

