          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"df14902a90e8299f3cc00ba6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He told me he was wasting his time where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and finish the negotiations for his Indian venture. He could no longer delay his departure. Since his journey to London might be followed, even sooner than he expected, by the longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He implored me to leave my solitary island and meet him in Perth so we could travel south together. His letter stirred me back to life, and I resolved to leave my island in two days\u2019 time.\\n\\nBefore I went, however, there was a task I dreaded: I had to pack up my chemical instruments, which meant entering the room where my hateful work had taken place and handling the tools whose very sight sickened me. At dawn the next morning I mustered enough courage to unlock the laboratory door. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered on the floor, and I felt as if I had mutilated the living flesh of another human being. I paused to regain control before stepping inside. With a trembling hand I carried the instruments out, but I realized I should not leave those remnants behind to provoke horror and suspicion among the peasants. I placed them in a basket with a large load of stones and set them aside, determined to throw them into the sea that night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach cleaning and arranging my chemical equipment.\\n\\nNothing could be more complete than the change in my feelings since the night the demon appeared. I had once regarded my promise with dark despair, as something that must be kept no matter the consequences. Now it was as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes and I could see clearly for the first time. The idea of resuming my work never once entered my mind. The threat I had heard still weighed on me, yet I never considered that any voluntary act of mine could avert it. I had decided that creating another creature like the fiend I first made would be an act of the basest, most monstrous selfishness, and I shut out every thought that might lead me to a different decision.\"}"
