          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8b8ace8513c658deebb84ac0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Should I ask them? Or Miss Bates? She\u2019s even closer\u2014and I\u2019m not sure she isn\u2019t as likely as anyone to guess what everyone else wants. I think we need a larger council. How about I go invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you like,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, a little unsure. \u201cIf you think she can help.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ll get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe\u2019ll be full of delight and gratitude, but she won\u2019t tell you anything. She won\u2019t even hear your questions. I see no point in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she\u2019s so amusing, so very amusing! I love listening to Miss Bates talk. And I don\u2019t have to bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Weston joined them, and when he heard the plan, he approved immediately.\\n\\n\u201cYes, do, Frank\u2014go get Miss Bates, and let\u2019s settle this at once. She\u2019ll enjoy the idea, I\u2019m sure, and I can\u2019t think of anyone better at removing obstacles. Go fetch Miss Bates. We\u2019re getting too particular. She\u2019s a constant lesson in how to be cheerful. But bring both of them. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady\u2026\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I\u2019ll think you a fool, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I\u2019m sorry, sir. I didn\u2019t immediately remember. Naturally, if you wish it, I\u2019ll try to persuade them both.\u201d And off he ran.\\n\\nLong before he came back with the short, neat, quick-moving aunt and her graceful niece, Mrs. Weston\u2014being sweet-tempered and a good wife\u2014had already gone over the passage again and found the problems far less serious than she had first thought, really quite minor; and thus the dilemma ended. Everything else, at least in theory, went smoothly. All the small details of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper arranged themselves or were left as trifles for Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes to settle any time. Everyone invited was certainly coming; Frank had already written to Enscombe to request staying a few days past his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused. And it was bound to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she enthusiastically agreed that it must be. They did not need her as an adviser, but as an enthusiastic supporter (a much safer role) she was truly welcome. Her approval, both broad and detailed, warm and constant, couldn\u2019t fail to please; and for another half hour they walked back and forth through the different rooms, some offering ideas, some following along, all happily enjoying their future plans.\"}"
