          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"33b8feba3deb6737fea1519f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have no way of knowing how long I remained like that. It must have been quite a while, though, because when I partly regained awareness, dawn was breaking, the balloon was soaring at a tremendous height over a vast emptiness of ocean, and nowhere within the enormous horizon was there even the slightest hint of land. Still, my feelings as consciousness returned were not nearly as full of agony as one might expect. There was, in fact, a touch of budding madness in the calm way I started to survey my situation. I held each of my hands up to my eyes, one after the other, and wondered what could have caused the swollen veins and the dreadful blackness of my fingernails. Then I carefully examined my head, shaking it again and again and feeling it with painstaking attention, until I finally convinced myself it was not, as I had half suspected, actually larger than my balloon. Next, with an air of expertise, I patted both of my breeches pockets, and, finding that a notebook and a toothpick case were missing, tried to figure out what had become of them. Unable to do so, I felt indescribably vexed. It then struck me that the joint of my left ankle was causing considerable discomfort, and a dim awareness of my predicament began to glow in my mind. But, strangely enough, I felt neither shock nor terror. If I felt anything at all, it was a sort of sly delight at the ingenuity I was about to display in getting myself out of this fix; and I never once considered my ultimate survival to be in any real doubt. For several minutes I sat immersed in the deepest thought. I clearly remember repeatedly pressing my lips together, touching my forefinger to the side of my nose, and making all those gestures and grimaces common to men who, comfortably seated in their armchairs, ponder matters of complexity or consequence. Believing I had now gathered my thoughts sufficiently, I carefully and deliberately put my hands behind my back and unhooked the large iron buckle attached to the waistband of my trousers. The buckle had three prongs which, being rather rusty, turned with great difficulty on their pivots. After considerable effort, I managed to bring them to a right angle with the body of the buckle and was pleased to find they stayed firmly in that position. Holding the improvised tool between my teeth, I began to untie the knot of my cravat. I had to pause to rest several times before I could finish this maneuver, but, eventually, it was done.\"}"
