          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3b0bbd108e414cb88f5241fe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His expression was relentlessly determined, and \u201cthat cursed bug\u201d were the only words he let slip during the whole trip. I was responsible for a pair of dark lanterns, while Legrand contented himself with the scarab, which he had tied to the tip of a whipcord; he swung it back and forth as he walked, like a magician showing off a trick. When I noticed this latest, obvious sign of my friend\u2019s mental unhinging, I could hardly hold back tears. Still, I decided it was wiser to indulge his obsession, at least for now, until I could try some more forceful tactic with a chance of success. In the meantime I tried, unsuccessfully, to probe him about the purpose of the expedition. Having persuaded me to come with him, he seemed unwilling to discuss any matter of lesser importance, and to all my questions he returned nothing but \u201cwe\u2019ll see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the head of the island in a skiff and, climbing the bluffs on the mainland shore, headed northwest through terrain ferociously wild and barren, where not the slightest sign of a human footprint appeared. Legrand marched in front with firm confidence, pausing only for a moment here and there to note what seemed to be landmarks of his own devising on some earlier visit.\\n\\nWe traveled this way for roughly two hours, and the sun was just setting when we entered a region far more desolate than anything we had yet encountered. It was a kind of plateau near the summit of an almost unscalable hill, thickly forested from the base to the crest, and punctuated by huge boulders that looked as if they were resting loosely on the earth, in many cases prevented from tumbling into the valleys below only by the trees against which they leaned. Deep ravines, branching in every direction, lent the scene an even sterner, more solemn air.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed was choked with brambles, through which we quickly realized we would never have forced a way without a scythe; and Jupiter, at his master\u2019s order, began cutting a path for us to the base of an extraordinarily tall tulip tree. It stood on the level ground with eight or ten oaks, towering over them all\u2014and over every other tree I had ever seen\u2014in the grace of its foliage and shape, the vast spread of its branches, and the general majesty of its bearing.\"}"
        }
