          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c8157ef4408685c08bbf7cf3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 7. I rose early and, to my great joy, finally saw what I could only conclude with certainty was the North Pole itself. It was there without question, directly beneath my feet; but sadly I had risen to such an immense height that nothing could be observed with precision. Judging by the sequence of numbers recording my various altitudes at different times between six in the morning on April 2 and twenty minutes before nine on the same day (when the barometer dropped), it could be fairly assumed that by four in the morning on April 7 the balloon had reached an altitude certainly no less than 7,254 miles above sea level. This height may seem enormous, yet the calculation producing it probably fell well short of the truth. In any case, I clearly saw the entire major diameter of the earth; the whole northern hemisphere lay beneath me like an orthographic chart, and the equator itself marked the horizon\u2019s edge. Still, you can easily imagine that the narrow regions of the Arctic circle, though directly below and therefore appearing without foreshortening, were too small and too far from my viewpoint for any very precise examination. Even so, what I could see was strange and exciting. Northward from that massive rim previously described, which with slight qualification may be called the limit of human discovery in these parts, stretched one continuous\u2014or nearly continuous\u2014sheet of ice. In its early degrees its surface visibly flattened, farther on it settled into a plane, and eventually, becoming distinctly concave, it ended at the Pole itself in a sharply defined circular center whose apparent diameter from the balloon formed an angle of about sixty-five seconds. Its dark color, shifting in intensity, was always deeper than any other point on the visible hemisphere and sometimes sank into absolute, impenetrable blackness. Beyond this little could be determined. By noon the circular center had greatly shrunk, and by seven in the evening it disappeared completely; the balloon passed over the western edge of the ice and drifted swiftly toward the equator.\\n\\nApril 8. I noted a perceptible decrease in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a significant change in its overall color and appearance.\"}"
