          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a22bea607009d547ae9bd1af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Lacking his usual tools, the analyst throws himself into his opponent\u2019s mindset, sees through that lens, and often spots at once the only ways\u2014sometimes surprisingly simple\u2014to mislead him into error or rush him into miscalculation. Whist has long been known for sharpening what people call the calculating power, and men of the highest intellect have often found a mysterious pleasure in it while dismissing chess as trivial. Nothing else so similar demands such analysis. The best chess player in Christendom might be little more than that, but real skill in whist shows a mind fit for the more important struggles where minds clash. By proficiency, I mean mastery of the game that understands every source from which legitimate advantage can spring. These sources are many and varied, often hidden in parts of thought that ordinary minds cannot reach. Careful observation leads to precise memory, so a focused chess player will do fairly well at whist, and Hoyle\u2019s rules, based on the mechanics of the game, are clear enough. Having a good memory and playing \u201cby the book\u201d are usually considered the sum of good play. Yet the analyst\u2019s skill shows itself beyond mere rule-following. He silently makes countless observations and deductions. His companions may do so too, but the difference lies less in the logic of their conclusions than in what they observe. The key is knowing what to notice. Our player limits himself in nothing; even though the game is the point, he does not ignore clues from outside the game. He studies his partner\u2019s face, comparing it with each opponent\u2019s. He watches how each hand arranges its cards, often counting trump after trump and honor after honor through the glances of their holders. He tracks every change of expression as the game advances, building a store of insight from looks of certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way someone gathers a trick he judges whether that player can win another in the same suit. He recognizes feints by the way a card is laid down. A careless word, an accidentally dropped or turned card, and the owner\u2019s anxiety or ease about hiding it; the counting of tricks and how they are stacked; every sign of embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or nervousness\u2014all feed his seemingly intuitive sense of the real situation.\"}"
