          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2e276923220e67f0ec1b5f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The powerful voice\u2014more elemental than mechanical, for we could not imagine pipes or keys producing it\u2014fell silent, and the girl, turning to help her aged companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nIt was her father, and since childhood she had guided his blinded steps. They were Germans from Saxony who had emigrated here only a few years earlier and forged new bonds with the surrounding villagers. Around the onset of the plague, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to guess: he, a nobleman, loved the beautiful daughter of the poor musician and followed them when his own friends persecuted him. But soon the great equalizer came with an undulled scythe, mowing down the tall flowers of the field along with the grass. The young man was among the first to fall. She preserved herself for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to maintain a deception that had begun by accident, and now, with no one else left alive, he remained unaware of what had happened. When he listened to his daughter\u2019s music, the mute mountains, the senseless lake, and uncomprehending trees were, aside from him, her only audience.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived she had been stricken with the plague\u2019s symptoms. She was paralyzed with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, sightless father alone on this empty earth, yet she lacked the courage to tell him the truth. The intensity of her desperation drove her to extraordinary effort. At the usual hour of vespers she led him to the chapel, and, though trembling and weeping for his sake, she played\u2014without a misplaced beat or wrong note\u2014the hymn written to celebrate the creation of the adorned world, which was soon to become her tomb.\\n\\nWe appeared to her like messengers from heaven. The courage she had strained to sustain vanished at the sight of help. With a shriek she rushed toward us, threw her arms around Adrian\u2019s knees, and, uttering only \u201cOh, save my father!\u201d dissolved into sobs and hysterical cries, releasing the sorrow she had suppressed for so long.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the great walnut tree that shades her lover\u2019s grave, the spot she indicated to us with her last breath. Her father, at last aware of her peril, could not see the changes in her beloved face, yet he stubbornly held her hand until it grew cold and rigid in death. Even then he neither moved nor spoke, until twelve hours later merciful death granted him endless rest. They sleep beneath the earth, the tree serving as their monument. I still see the sacred spot clearly in my mind, framed by craggy Jura and the far, immeasurable Alps. The spire of the church they once attended still rises from the sheltering trees, and though her hand is cold, I could swear the divine music they cherished still drifts through the air, comforting their tender spirits.\"}"
