          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"33434aaf633defb68da341bd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe deliberately misheard the word she repeated and rushed on, \u201cYes, customers; in banking we usually call our clients our customers. He was a French gentleman, a scientific gentleman, a man of great accomplishments\u2014a doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot from Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman came from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honor of knowing him there. Our dealings were business, yet confidential. I was then in our French branch, and had been\u2014oh! twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask, when, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI speak, miss, of twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many French gentlemen and French families, were entirely in Tellson\u2019s hands. In the same way I am, or have been, trustee of one kind or another for scores of our customers. These are purely business relations, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special interest, nothing sentimental. I have moved from one to another in the course of my business life, just as I move from one of our customers to another in the course of my business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am a mere machine. To go on\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I am starting to believe\u201d\u2014the curiously roughened forehead focused on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan after my mother survived my father only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost sure it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the hesitant little hand that trustingly reached for his, raised it to his lips with some ceremony, then led the young lady straight back to her chair. Holding the chair-back with his left hand and using his right to rub his chin, tug his wig at the ears, or emphasize his words, he stood looking down into her face while she looked up at his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truthful I was just now, in saying I had no feelings and that all my dealings with others are mere business relations, when you realize that I have never seen you since. No; you have been in Tellson\u2019s care since then, and I have been occupied with the other business of Tellson\u2019s. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning a vast financial mangle.\u201d\"}"
