          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"864cdd726e5b05195a06a8cf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Master Charles Bates, horrified by Sikes\u2019s crime, began to reflect on whether an honest life was best after all. Convinced that it was, he left his old haunts behind and decided to start fresh in some new kind of work. He struggled hard and suffered for a while, but with his cheerful nature and good intentions he eventually succeeded; from being a farmhand and a carrier\u2019s boy, he has become the happiest young grazier in all Northamptonshire. The hand that writes these lines trembles as it nears the end of its task, wishing to spin out the story a little longer. I long to stay with a few of those with whom I have spent so much time, and share their happiness by trying to describe it. I would show Rose Maylie in all the bloom and grace of young womanhood, casting gentle light on her quiet path in life, which touched everyone who walked it with her and warmed their hearts. I would paint her as the life and joy of the fireside circle and the lively summer group; follow her through the hot fields at noon, and listen to the soft tones of her sweet voice during the moonlit evening walk; watch her kindness and charity abroad, and her calm, tireless care of household duties at home. I would depict her and her dead sister\u2019s child happy in their love for each other, spending long hours together imagining the friends they had so sadly lost. I would call up again the bright little faces that clustered around her knee, and hear their cheerful chatter; I would bring back the sound of that clear laugh, and imagine again the sympathetic tear that shone in her gentle blue eye. These, along with countless looks and smiles, and thoughts and words\u2014I would remember them all if I could. How Mr. Brownlow continued each day to fill his adopted child\u2019s mind with knowledge, growing more attached to him as he saw the seeds of all he had hoped taking root\u2014how he traced in him fresh traits of his old friend, awakening in himself memories that were sad yet sweet and comforting\u2014how the two orphans, tested by hardship, kept its lessons in mercy to others, in mutual love, and in grateful thanks to Him who safeguarded and preserved them\u2014these are things that need not be told.\"}"
