          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d8e7ff42c07b0dbe0340e03\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew glanced back at him without understanding as he went toward the door. \u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI look forward to seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Light Monsieur my nephew to his room there!\u2014And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you wish,\u201d he muttered to himself before he rang his small bell again and summoned his valet to his own chamber. When the valet had come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis paced in his loose robe to calm himself for sleep on that hot, still night. Moving around the room, his soft slippers made no sound on the floor; he glided like a refined tiger\u2014like some enchanted marquis of the unrepentant wicked kind in stories, whose regular change into tiger form was either ending or beginning. He crossed his lush bedroom from end to end, thinking again of the scraps of that day\u2019s journey that came uninvited into his mind: the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the setting sun itself, the descent, the mill, the prison perched on the crag, the small village in the hollow, the peasants by the fountain, and the mender of roads with his blue cap pointing out the chain under the carriage. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain, the little bundle lying on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms raised, crying, \u201cDead!\u201d \u201cI am calm now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis, \u201cand can go to bed.\u201d Leaving only one light burning on the wide hearth, he let his thin gauze curtains drop around him and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he settled to sleep. The stone faces on the outer walls stared blankly into the black night for three heavy hours; for three heavy hours the horses in the stable rattled their harness, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound that little resembled the call that poets assign to owls. Yet such creatures are stubborn in rarely saying what is written for them. For three heavy hours the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human alike, stared blindly at the darkness. Dead blackness lay over the whole landscape, and dead blackness added its hush to the hushed dust on the roads. The burial ground was so far gone that its small heaps of poor grass could not be distinguished from one another; the figure on the Cross might have stepped down for all anyone could see. In the village, tax collectors and the taxed slept soundly. Perhaps they dreamed of feasts, as the starving usually do, and of ease and rest, as the driven slave and the yoked ox may, while its lean inhabitants slept deep, fed and freed in their dreams.\"}"
