          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cc7aa1554941c50c582126e0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that all? That isn\u2019t much! Plenty are out of favor with the Republic and have to stare through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m he. Naturally, since I\u2019m the last.\u201d\\n\\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has answered every previous question. Jarvis Lorry has climbed down and stands with his hand on the coach door, responding to a group of officials. They stroll around the carriage and slowly climb up to the box to inspect the small pile of luggage on the roof; the country people loitering nearby press closer to the doors and stare greedily inside. A mother holds out her little child\u2019s short arm so it can touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay we leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou may leave. Forward, my postilions! Safe travels!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.\u2014And the first danger is behind us!\u201d\\n\\nAgain, these words come from Jarvis Lorry, as he clasps his hands and looks up. There is terror in the carriage, there is weeping, there is the heavy breathing of the unconscious traveler.\\n\\n\u201cAren\u2019t we going too slowly? Can\u2019t they be urged to go faster?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would look like fleeing, my darling. I mustn\u2019t press them too hard; it would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see if we\u2019re being chased!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, no one is chasing us.\u201d\\n\\nHouses in pairs and trios pass by, solitary farms, crumbling buildings, dye works, tanneries, and the like, open fields, avenues of leafless trees. Hard, uneven pavement lies beneath us; soft, deep mud borders either side. Sometimes we veer into the bordering mud to avoid the stones that rattle and jolt us; sometimes we are stuck in ruts and bogs there. The pain of our impatience becomes so great that, in our frantic fear, we consider jumping out and running\u2014hiding\u2014doing anything but staying still.\\n\\nOut of the open country and back again into crumbling buildings, solitary farms, dye works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in pairs and trios, avenues of leafless trees. Have these men tricked us and taken us back by another road? Isn\u2019t this the same place twice over? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see if we\u2019re being pursued! Quiet! The posting-house.\\n\\nLeisurely, our four horses are taken out; leisurely, the coach sits in the small street, without horses and unlikely to move again; leisurely, the new horses appear one by one; leisurely, the new postilions follow, snapping and braiding the lashes of their whips; leisurely, the old postilions count their coins, make wrong totals, and reach unhappy conclusions.\"}"
