          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"376b4d49c4c4f117f8018bb7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted she was somewhat surprised at the match, yet she spoke more of her genuine wish for their happiness than of her astonishment, and Elizabeth could not make her see it as unlikely. Kitty and Lydia did not envy Miss Lucas at all, since Mr. Collins was only a clergyman, and the matter interested them solely as news to share in Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not help feeling triumphant at having a daughter well married and took every chance to visit Longbourn and declare her happiness, even though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour looks and unkind remarks should have chased happiness away.\\n\\nBetween Elizabeth and Charlotte there lay a restraint that kept them silent on the subject, and Elizabeth was convinced they could never again share real trust. Disappointed in Charlotte, she clung more tenderly to her sister, whose sense and sensitivity she believed unshakable, and for whose happiness she worried more each day, since Bingley had been gone a week and there was no word of his return.\\n\\nJane had quickly replied to Caroline\u2019s letter and now counted the days until she could reasonably expect an answer. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks arrived on Tuesday, addressed to their father and full of solemn gratitude that might have been expected after living with them for a year. With that duty done, he went on, in many rapturous phrases, about his joy in gaining the affection of their amiable neighbor, Miss Lucas. He then explained that he had so eagerly accepted their kind invitation to Longbourn solely to enjoy her company, and he hoped to return on Monday fortnight; for Lady Catherine, he added, so warmly approved his marriage that she wished it to take place at once, which he hoped would convince his dear Charlotte to set an early date and make him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer any pleasure to Mrs. Bennet. On the contrary, she was as ready as her husband to complain. It was most odd that he should come to Longbourn instead of Lucas Lodge; it was also highly inconvenient and extremely troublesome. She detested having visitors while her health was so uncertain, and lovers were, of all people, the most disagreeable.\"}"
