          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"99fe9216ddf5760eab126ccd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday at noon I went to the well and watched for a bit. Merlin was still burning smoke powders, clawing at the air, and muttering gibberish just as hard as ever, though he looked pretty discouraged, since he hadn\u2019t even managed to bring up a bead of sweat in that well yet. Finally I said, \u201cHow are things looking now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am right now laboring with the most powerful enchantment known to the princes of the occult arts in the Eastern lands; if it fails me, nothing can help. Silence, until I finish.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that darkened the whole region and must have made trouble for the hermits, because the wind was blowing their way and it rolled down over their caves in a dense, billowing fog. He poured out torrents of speech to match and twisted his body and slashed the air with his hands in the most extraordinary way. After twenty minutes he dropped down panting, nearly exhausted. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns, and behind them a crowd of pilgrims and two acres\u2019 worth of foundlings, all drawn by the huge smoke and all in a fever of excitement. The abbot anxiously asked for results. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any labor of mortal power could break the spell binding these waters, the one I just tried would have done it. It has failed, which proves what I feared is true. The sign of this failure is that the most potent spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name no one may utter and live, has set his spell on this well. No mortal breathes now, nor ever will, who can uncover the secret of that spell, and without that secret no one can break it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done all a man could. Allow me to go.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally this threw the abbot into quite a panic. He turned to me, the alarm plain on his face, and said, \u201cYou\u2019ve heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then, not all! What part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has cast his spell on the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTherefore, you also mean that when he says none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he\u2019s not necessarily telling the truth. There are conditions under which an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014small, very small, but still a chance\u2014of success.\u201d\"}"
        }
