          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f5610ffd4176e2c5e269ec3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cListen, then! You!\u201d Monks replied. \u201cWhen his father fell ill in Rome, his wife\u2014my mother\u2014went to join him. They had long been separated, but she left Paris and took me along\u2014maybe to secure his property, for she didn\u2019t care much for him, and he didn\u2019t care for her. He never knew we were there, because his senses had failed. He slept on until the next day and then died. In his desk we found two papers, written the night his illness began, addressed to you\u201d\u2014he turned to Mr. Brownlow\u2014\u201cwith a note saying they were not to be sent until after his death. One was a letter to Agnes; the other was a will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat was in the letter?\u201d asked Mr. Brownlow.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter? A sheet covered again and again with lines\u2014a confession full of remorse, begging God for help. He had tricked her with a story about some secret to be revealed one day that kept him from marrying her just then. She trusted him patiently until it was too late and she lost what could never be restored. She was only a few months from giving birth. He explained everything he had planned to do to hide her shame if he lived, and begged her, if he died, not to curse his memory or think the consequences of their sin would fall on her or their child. He said the guilt was all his. He reminded her of the day he gave her the little locket and the ring with her Christian name engraved on it, with a blank for the name he hoped one day to give her. He begged her to keep it and wear it over her heart as before. Then he rambled wildly, repeating the same words over and over, as if he had gone mad. I believe he had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, while Oliver\u2019s tears fell fast.\\n\\nMonks kept silent.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cwas in the same tone as the letter. He wrote of the misery his wife had caused him, of the rebellious nature, vice, malice, and early wicked passions of you, his only son, who had been taught to hate him. He left you and your mother each an annuity of eight hundred pounds. The rest of his property he split into two equal shares\u2014one for Agnes Fleming, and one for their child, if it were born alive and ever reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
