          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64f98984931267948eb176af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut I must warn you in advance that I will tell Sybil everything. She is coming to lunch with me tomorrow to talk about bonnets, and if Mr. Podgers discovers you have a bad temper, or gout, or a wife living in Bayswater, I will certainly tell her all of it.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI am not afraid,\u201d he replied. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! I am a little sorry to hear you say that. The best foundation for marriage is mutual misunderstanding. No, I am not cynical at all; I simply have experience, which amounts to much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is desperate to have his hand read. Don\u2019t tell him he is engaged to one of the most beautiful girls in London, because that was in the Morning Post a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d cried the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cplease let Mr. Podgers stay here a little longer. He just told me I should go on the stage, and I am so interested.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I will certainly take him away. Come over at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, making a little moue as she rose from the sofa, \u201cif I am not allowed to go on the stage, I must at least be in the audience.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; we are all going to be in the audience,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cAnd now, Mr. Podgers, be sure to tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is one of my special favorites.\u201d\\n\\nBut when Mr. Podgers saw Lord Arthur\u2019s hand he turned oddly pale and stayed silent. A shiver seemed to go through him, and his great bushy eyebrows twitched in that irritating way they did when he was confused. Then huge beads of sweat broke out on his yellow forehead like poisonous dew, and his fat fingers grew cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur noticed these strange signs of agitation, and for the first time in his life he felt fear. His impulse was to flee the room, but he held himself back. It was better to know the worst, whatever it might be, than to stay in this horrible uncertainty.\\n\\n\u201cI am waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWe are all waiting,\u201d cried Lady Windermere in her quick, impatient way, but the chiromantist gave no answer.\\n\\n\u201cI believe Arthur is going on the stage,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, \u201cand after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is afraid to say so.\u201d\"}"
