          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"766732f5d485bb3bc3d5f5a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said that as he left his home and passed through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke coming from the inn chimney. Thinking travelers might be gathered there, he knocked and was let inside. Only Lucy and her mother were there. Her mother had lost the use of her limbs after a bout of rheumatism, and everyone else in the area had departed in turn, leaving them alone. Lucy begged the man to stay with her; in a week or two her mother would be better, and then they could set out. Without help they would die, left so helpless and abandoned. The man answered that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, so he felt he could not stay. As a last attempt, Lucy gave him a letter addressed to Idris, to be delivered wherever he met us. At least this charge he fulfilled, and Idris read with emotion the letter that followed: \\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI know you will remember and pity me, and I dare to hope you will help me; what other hope have I? Forgive the way I write; I am so confused. A month ago my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already better, and in another month I am sure she will be able to travel, as you kindly said you would arrange for us. But now everyone has gone\u2014everyone. As they left, each person said my mother might be better before we were entirely deserted. But three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who stayed behind because of his newborn child, and as theirs is a large family, I hoped to persuade them to wait a little longer for us; yet I found the house empty. I have seen no one since, until this good man came. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have hidden it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we will die miserably if nothing changes. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road; and if, when she gets better, I could somehow find the road and travel all those miles to the sea, you would all be in France, and the vast ocean would lie between us, dreadful even to sailors.\u201d\"}"
        }
