          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ff5dc25663d1f2e4bac331e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou ought to be concerned, Dorian. Any gentleman cares for his good reputation. You wouldn\u2019t wish to be spoken of as something foul and debased. Certainly, you possess position and wealth and all that, but rank and riches are not the whole of life. Understand, I do not place any faith in these stories. At least, I cannot accept them when I look at you. Sin stamps itself upon a man\u2019s features; it cannot be hidden. People sometimes speak of secret vices, but no such things exist. If a miserable fellow has some vice, it shows in the cut of his mouth, the drop of his eyelids, even in the shaping of his hands. Someone\u2014I won\u2019t give his name, though you know him\u2014approached me last year for a portrait. I had not seen him before and knew nothing of him then, though I have heard plenty since. He offered an extravagant sum. I declined. There was a twist in his fingers that repelled me. Now I know I guessed rightly. His life is appalling. But you, Dorian, with that pure, luminous, innocent face, with your marvellous, untroubled youth\u2014I cannot credit anything evil of you. And yet I rarely see you; you never come down to the studio now, and when I am away from you and hear all these ghastly things whispered in society, I am at a loss. Why is it that the Duke of Berwick rises and quits a club room when you enter? Why is it that so many London gentlemen will neither cross your threshold nor welcome you under theirs? You were once close to Lord Staveley. I met him at dinner last week. Your name came up, linked with the miniatures you have lent to the Dudley exhibition. Staveley curled his lip and remarked that you might possess the most artistic tastes, but that you were a man no pure-minded girl should be allowed to know, and no chaste woman should sit with. I reminded him I was your friend and asked his meaning. He told me\u2014plainly, before everyone present. It was dreadful! Why does your friendship prove fatal to young men? There was that unfortunate Guards officer who shot himself\u2014he was your intimate. There was Sir Henry Ashton, forced out of England with a stained name.\u201d\"}"
