          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f1bf33c8a64167bdbd031803\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She suddenly felt a strong urge to go to the front door to see if it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of another motive? Captain Wentworth must be out of sight. She left her seat\u2014she would go. One half of her should not always seem wiser than the other half, nor always suspect the other of worse motives. She would check the weather. But she was stopped at once by Captain Wentworth himself coming in, surrounded by several men and women who were clearly his acquaintances and whom he must have joined just below Milsom Street. He was more visibly startled and flustered on seeing her than she had ever noticed before; he even flushed red. For the first time since they had met again, she felt she showed more feeling than he did. She had the advantage of having had a moment to prepare. The first overpowering shock and confusion were past for her. Still, she had enough to feel\u2014agitation, pain, pleasure\u2014a blend of delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her and then turned away. His manner showed embarrassment. She could not have called it either cold or friendly, only embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short pause he came back and spoke again. They exchanged the usual questions about ordinary matters; neither of them seemed much wiser for what they learned, and Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than before. By spending so much time together they had managed to speak with an air of indifference and calm, but he could not manage it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. There was some kind of self-consciousness. He looked well, not as if he had suffered in health or spirits, and he talked about Uppercross, the Musgroves, even Louisa, with a brief flash of his own mischievous expression when he said her name. Still, it was Captain Wentworth ill at ease, uncomfortable, unable to pretend otherwise.\\n\\nAnne was not surprised but was saddened to see that Elizabeth refused to acknowledge him. She saw that he saw Elizabeth, that Elizabeth saw him, and that each fully recognized the other. She was convinced he was ready to be greeted, expecting it, and she felt the pain of seeing her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot had grown very impatient, now pulled up, and the servant entered to announce it.\"}"
