{"request_id":"6ccfc57774b0160b3b148350","rewritten_text":"I thought that quaint lie was told with the most straightforward and graceful touch; but then I had only heard it once, and that makes a difference. It was probably delightful to the others when it was new.

Sir Dinadan the humorist woke up first and soon roused the rest with a practical joke of decidedly low quality. He tied metal cups to a dog’s tail and let him loose, and the animal tore around the place in a panic, while all the other dogs howled after him, crashing into anything in their way and turning the whole scene into a chaotic uproar. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran, and some toppled from their chairs and rolled on the floor in bliss. They were just like children. Sir Dinadan was so impressed with his stunt that he kept repeating, to the point of exhaustion, how the immortal idea had struck him. As humorists of his type do, he was still laughing long after everyone else had stopped. He felt so pleased with himself that he decided to make a speech—naturally, a funny one. I don’t think I had ever heard so many tired, worn-out jokes strung together in my life. He was worse than the street singers, worse than the circus clown. It felt especially dreary to sit there, thirteen centuries before I was born, and listen yet again to those stale, moth-eaten jokes that had given me stomach cramps when I was a boy thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me that a new joke might be impossible. Everyone laughed at these antiques—well, they always do; I had noticed the same thing centuries afterward. The boy, the skeptic, didn’t laugh, of course. He mocked; there was nothing he wouldn’t mock. He declared most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten and the rest petrified. I said “petrified” was excellent; I thought the only proper way to classify those venerable jokes was by geological eras. But that clever thought didn’t land with the boy, because geology hadn’t been invented yet. Still, I made a note of the remark and planned to educate the kingdom up to it if I survived. There’s no point wasting a good thing just because the market isn’t ready.

Then Sir Kay stood up and began cranking his history machine, with me as the fuel."}

