          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c6d024c0bcad9e950fdb8a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On my return, I found this letter from my father:\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably waited impatiently for a letter setting the date of your return; at first I thought of writing only a brief note telling you when to come. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I dare not do it. How shocked you would be, expecting a joyful welcome, to meet instead with tears and misery. And how, Victor, can I tell you our misfortune? Being away cannot have made you indifferent to our joys and sorrows; still, how can I hurt my long-absent son? I want to prepare you for the dreadful news, yet I know it is impossible; even now your eyes race ahead, looking for the words that carry the terrible message.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that sweet child whose smile warmed my heart, so gentle yet so merry! Victor, he was murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you; I will simply tell you what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th) your two brothers, my niece, and I went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, so we stayed out longer than usual. Dusk had fallen before we thought of going home, and then we realized that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were nowhere to be seen. We sat on a bench to wait for them. Soon Ernest returned and asked if we had seen his brother. He said they had been playing together; William had run off to hide, and Ernest had looked for him in vain, waited a long time, and still he did not come back.\\n\\n\u201cThis story alarmed us, and we searched for him until night settled in, when Elizabeth guessed he might have gone back to the house. He was not there. We went out again with torches, for I could not rest while I imagined my sweet boy lost and exposed to the damp night air; Elizabeth too was in extreme anguish. Around five in the morning I found my lovely boy, who only the evening before had been blooming with health, lying on the grass, pale and still; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cWe brought him home, and the anguish on my face revealed the truth to Elizabeth. She begged to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she insisted, hurried into the room where it lay, examined the victim\u2019s neck, and clasping her hands cried, \u2018O God!\u2019\u201d\"}"
