{"request_id":"f5edb240e21ae1a5a7f7d8fc","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove seized the first chance of being alone with Anne to say, “Oh, Miss Anne, I can’t help wishing Mrs Charles possessed a bit of your way with those children. They become different creatures with you! But ordinarily they are so spoiled! It’s a pity you cannot put your sister on the right course for managing them. They are as fine, healthy little ones as ever lived, poor dears, without any favoritism; but Mrs Charles does not have the least idea how they ought to be handled—! Bless me, how troublesome they are sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them at our house as often as I otherwise might. I believe Mrs Charles is not quite satisfied that I do not invite them more frequently; but you know it is very disagreeable to have children about one whom one is forever obliged to check: ‘don’t do this’ and ‘don’t do that,’ or who can only be kept tolerably in order by more cake than is at all good for them.”

From Mary came this report. “Mrs Musgrove considers all her servants so steady that it would be high treason to doubt it; but I am convinced, without exaggeration, that her upper housemaid and her laundry-maid, instead of minding their business, are gadding through the village all day long. I encounter them wherever I go; and I declare I never go twice into my nursery without catching sight of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature alive, it would be enough to ruin her; for she tells me they are forever coaxing her to take a walk with them.” On Mrs Musgrove’s part, it ran, “I make a point of never interfering in any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs, for I know it would not answer; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, for you may manage to set matters right, that I have no high opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear odd stories of her; she is always gallivanting, and from my own observation I can affirm she dresses so finically that she is enough to spoil any servant she approaches. Mrs Charles quite swears by her, I am aware; but I just give you the hint, that you may keep a lookout, because if you detect anything wrong, you need not hesitate to mention it.”

Again Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was very apt not to give her the precedence that was her right when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she saw no reason why she should be treated as so entirely at home as to lose her place."}

