          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5db73be757125a3cd7aaa28a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to watch them were bound to come. Anne was soon among all four often enough to form an opinion, though she was too prudent to admit it at home, knowing neither husband nor wife would be satisfied; for while she thought Louisa somewhat preferred, she could not help believing\u2014from memory and experience\u2014that Captain Wentworth was not in love with either. They were more in love with him, though even that was not true love. It was a brief fever of admiration that might, and likely must, become love for some of them. Charles Hayter seemed conscious of being overlooked, yet Henrietta sometimes appeared torn between them. Anne longed to warn them all about what they were doing, and to point out the dangers they were courting. She suspected no deceit. Her greatest relief was believing Captain Wentworth had no idea he was causing pain. There was no triumph, no mean triumph, in his manner. He had probably never heard of, or even considered, Charles Hayter\u2019s claims. His only fault was accepting\u2014accepting was the right word\u2014the attentions of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to withdraw. Three days had passed without his visiting Uppercross\u2014a clear change. He even declined a formal dinner invitation; and when Mr Musgrove found him surrounded by large books, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were convinced something was wrong, speaking gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed Henrietta had dismissed him outright, while her husband expected to see him the next day. Anne could only think Charles Hayter showed sense.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, with Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth off shooting together, the sisters at the Cottage sat quietly at their work when the sisters from the Mansion-house stopped by the window.\\n\\nIt was a beautiful November day, and the Miss Musgroves crossed the small grounds solely to announce that they were going for a long walk and therefore assumed Mary would not want to join them. When Mary, bristling at the suggestion she was not a good walker, immediately answered, \u201cOh yes, I should like to join you very much, I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne read in the girls\u2019 expressions that this was exactly what they did not want. She again noted how the family habit seemed to require that everything be announced and everything be done together, no matter how unwelcome or inconvenient.\"}"
