          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"404d626683aae4d57f951b89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner Mr. Bennet hardly spoke; but once the servants left, he decided it was time to talk with his guest. He chose a topic that he expected would please him by noting how fortunate he seemed in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to Mr. Collins\u2019s wishes and her concern for his comfort appeared most unusual. Mr. Bennet could not have picked better. Mr. Collins passed at once into eager praise. The subject made him even more solemn than usual, and with an air of great importance he declared he had never seen behavior like hers in a woman of rank\u2014such friendliness and kindness as he had received from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both the sermons he had been honored to preach before her. She had also invited him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the previous Saturday to complete her evening card game of quadrille. Many people considered Lady Catherine proud, he knew, but he himself had seen only graciousness. She always spoke to him as she would to any other gentleman; she made no objection to his joining the neighborhood society, nor to his leaving his parish now and then for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as he could, provided he chose wisely; and she had once paid him a visit in his modest parsonage, where she perfectly approved all the alterations he had made, and even took the trouble to suggest a few herself\u2014some shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is all very proper and polite, I am sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a very pleasant woman. It is a pity that great ladies in general are not more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden in which my humble residence stands is separated from Rosings Park, her Ladyship\u2019s home, only by a lane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you said she was a widow, sir? Has she any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has only one daughter, the heiress of Rosings and of very great property.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better off than many girls. And what sort of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a most delightful young lady indeed. Lady Catherine herself says that, in true beauty, Miss de Bourgh far surpasses the most beautiful women; because there is something in her features that marks the young lady of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
