          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"24975ad647b5532a90170cbb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The studio swam with the lush perfume of roses, and when the mild summer breeze stirred the garden trees, the heavy fragrance of lilac or the more delicate scent of the pink-blossoming thorn drifted through the open doorway. Reclining on the corner of the divan made from Persian saddle-bags, Lord Henry Wotton lay smoking innumerable cigarettes, as he always did, and from there he could glimpse the honey-sweet, honey-gold sprays of a laburnum, whose quivering branches seemed scarcely capable of supporting such flame-like loveliness. From time to time the fanciful shadows of birds in flight glided across the long curtains of tussore silk that hung before the vast window, creating a fleeting Japanese impression and reminding him of those jade-faced, wan painters of Tokyo who strive, through an art that must remain motionless, to express speed and movement. The sullen drone of bees thrusting their way through the long, uncut grass, or circling with tireless monotony around the dusty gilded horns of the wandering woodbine, only heightened the oppressive stillness. The muffled rumble of London sounded like the bourdon note of a far-off organ. In the middle of the room, fixed to an upright easel, stood the life-size portrait of a young man of exceptional physical beauty, and a short distance before it sat the painter himself, Basil Hallward, whose sudden disappearance several years ago had created such a sensation and sparked countless odd conjectures. As he gazed at the graceful and handsome figure he had captured so deftly in his art, a smile of satisfaction crossed his face and seemed ready to remain. Yet he abruptly sprang up, and shutting his eyes, pressed his fingers against the lids, as if wishing to trap inside his mind some peculiar dream he dreaded might dissolve. \u201cIt is your finest work, Basil, the best thing you have ever produced,\u201d Lord Henry remarked languidly. \u201cYou ought to send it to the Grosvenor next year. The Academy is much too vast and too common. Every time I have been there, either the crush of people prevented me from seeing the paintings, which was dreadful, or the multitude of paintings kept me from seeing the people, which was worse. The Grosvenor is truly the only suitable place.\u201d \u201cI don\u2019t believe I shall send it anywhere,\u201d he replied, tossing his head back in that queer manner that used to make his friends at Oxford laugh.\"}"
