          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"97b6834e6e005ab7d79f3463\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her colour changed and she fell silent.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner stood a little behind; when Elizabeth stopped, he asked Mrs. Gardiner to introduce him to her friends. This request was so polite that she was caught off guard; she almost smiled at his now seeking the acquaintance of the very people whose company his pride had rejected when he proposed to her. \u201cWhat a surprise it will be,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he learns who they are! He thinks they are fashionable people.\u201d\\n\\nThe introduction went on at once; as Elizabeth explained their relation to her, she gave him a sly glance to see how he took it, expecting he might run off as fast as possible from such low companions. His surprise at the connection was clear: still, he bore it bravely, and instead of leaving, he turned back with them and began talking to Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth could not help feeling pleased, even triumphant. It comforted her that he now knew she had relations she need not be ashamed of. She listened closely to their talk and delighted in every word from her uncle that showed his intelligence, taste, or good manners.\\n\\nThe conversation soon shifted to fishing; she heard Mr. Darcy invite Mr. Gardiner, with great politeness, to fish there as often as he wished while he stayed in the neighbourhood, offering to provide fishing tackle and pointing out the parts of the stream where sport was usually best. Mrs. Gardiner, walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, gave her a look full of wonder. Elizabeth said nothing, yet she was deeply gratified; the compliment must be for her sake. She was, however, extremely astonished, and kept repeating, \u201cWhy is he so changed? What could cause it? It cannot be for me, it cannot be for my sake that his manners have softened. My reproofs at Hunsford could not have produced such a change. It is impossible that he still loves me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter walking for some time wi