          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"096875967bdfee1dcd142d20\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologized. He answered, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, and you can imagine she was pursued as a great prize. Her half brother had regained plenty of money, but through debts and new foolishness squandered it again in the worst way. The rift between him and her was even deeper than the one he had had with their father, and it\u2019s suspected he nursed a bitter, deadly grudge against her for stirring up their father\u2019s anger. Now, I reach the cruel part of the story\u2014only pausing, my dear Handel, to point out that a dinner napkin will not fit into a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was trying to stuff mine into my glass, I have no idea. I only know that with determination worthy of a better cause, I exerted myself to force it in. Again I thanked him and apologized, and again he said in the brightest tone, \u201cNot at all, I\u2019m sure!\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014whether at the races, the public balls, or anywhere else you like\u2014a certain man who courted Miss Havisham. I never saw him (this was twenty-five years ago, before you and I, Handel), but my father used to say he was flashy, just the type for the role. Yet he could not, without ignorance or prejudice, be mistaken for a gentleman, my father emphatically insists, because he holds that no man who isn\u2019t a gentleman at heart has ever been, since the world began, a gentleman in manner. He says no polish can hide the grain of the wood, and the more polish you apply, the more the grain shows. Well! This man pursued Miss Havisham relentlessly and claimed to be devoted to her. I believe she hadn\u2019t shown much susceptibility before, but every bit she had came out then, and she loved him passionately. She clearly idolized him. He exploited her affection so methodically that he extracted large sums of money from her,