          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f600706f6dbc5ff026a6d2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He chose Friday, August 17th for his appearance and spent most of the day examining his wardrobe, ultimately settling on a large slouch hat with a red feather, a burial shroud frilled at the wrists and neck, and a rusty dagger. Toward evening a violent storm broke out, and the wind blew so hard that every window and door in the old house shook and rattled. It was exactly the kind of weather he loved. His plan was simple. He would quietly make his way to Washington Otis\u2019s room, gibber from the foot of the bed, and stab himself three times in the throat to the sound of slow music. He held a special grudge against Washington, fully aware that he used Pinkerton\u2019s Paragon Detergent to scrub out the famous Canterville bloodstain. After reducing the reckless, foolhardy boy to utter terror, he would go to the room of the United States Minister and his wife, press a clammy hand to Mrs. Otis\u2019s forehead, and hiss into her shaking husband\u2019s ear the awful secrets of the charnel house. As for little Virginia, he was undecided. She had never insulted him and was pretty and gentle. He felt a few hollow groans from the wardrobe would be more than enough, and if that failed to wake her, he might claw at the bedspread with trembling, palsied fingers. He was determined to teach the twins a lesson. First, he would sit on their chests, to give them the suffocating sensation of a nightmare. Then, since their beds were close together, he would stand between them as a green, icy corpse until they were paralyzed with fear, and finally he would throw off the shroud and crawl around the room with bleached bones and one rolling eyeball as \u201cDumb Daniel, or the Suicide\u2019s Skeleton,\u201d a role in which he had repeatedly made a tremendous impression, and which he thought as good as his celebrated part, \u201cMartin the Maniac, or the Masked Mystery.\u201d\\n\\nAt half-past ten he heard the family going to bed. For a while wild bursts of laughter from the twins disturbed him, as they were clearly amusing themselves with schoolboy cheerfulness before turning in, but by a quarter past eleven everything was quiet, and when midnight struck, he set out. An owl beat against the window panes, a raven croaked from the old yew tree, and the wind moaned around the house like a lost soul, but the Otis family slept unaware of their fate, and above the rain and storm he could clearly hear the steady snoring of the United States Minister.\"}"
