{"request_id":"e3a0137bebe56a698eb8f025","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I felt no uneasiness regarding her. She was what you might call a notable woman, capable of steering through the world without my help. To be honest, I think she always considered me an idle lad, a mere encumbrance fit only for weaving dreams, and she was rather pleased to see me gone. It was a gloomy night when I took my leave; with me, as aides-de-camp, came the three creditors who had plagued me so long, and together we carried the balloon with its car and fittings by a circuitous route to the station where the rest of the apparatus lay. We found everything undisturbed and I at once set about the task.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I noted, was black; not a single star was visible; and a light, intermittent drizzle made us decidedly uncomfortable. My chief concern, though, was for the balloon, which, despite its layer of varnish, was beginning to weigh down with damp; the powder too was in danger of spoiling. I therefore kept my three duns steadily employed, pounding ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the other vessels. They nonetheless kept pestering me with questions about the purpose of all this equipment, grumbling loudly over the dreadful toil I forced on them. They declared they could not see any good in being soaked to the skin merely to take part in such ghastly rituals. I began to grow uneasy and worked with redoubled vigor, for I honestly believe the fools imagined I had struck some bargain with the devil and that whatever I was up to amounted to something little short of wicked. I feared every moment they might abandon me. I managed, however, to placate them by promising payment in full of all debts as soon as I could bring the business to a conclusion. They, of course, put their own construction upon my words; they assumed, no doubt, that I would soon possess a great heap of ready cash, and so long as I settled my accounts and threw in a little extra for their trouble, I dare say they cared not a straw what became of my soul or my body.

“In about four hours and a half I judged the balloon sufficiently filled. I therefore fastened on the car and stowed all my apparatus in it—not overlooking the condensing gear, a generous supply of water, and a great store of provisions, such as pemmican, which holds much nourishment in comparatively small space.”"}

