{"request_id":"4ce11c9fca5b8b76e5bb8a48","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, yet it is winter and the sun has already set. No clouds hang in the clear, frosty sky to catch its slanting rays, but the air itself holds a faint rosy glow, which reflects off the snow covering the ground. I live in a lonely cottage on a wide, empty heath, and no sound of life reaches me. I look out over the barren plain blanketed in white, broken only by a few black patches the noon sun scraped from the sharp hilltops where the snow slid off as it fell and settled thinner than on the flat ground. A few birds peck at the hard ice covering the pools, since the frost has lasted so long.

I am in a strange state of mind. I am alone—completely alone—in the world. Misfortune has swept over me and left me withered. I know I am about to die, and I feel happy—almost joyful. I take my pulse; it beats fast. I lay my thin hand on my cheek; it burns. A quick, slight spirit within me is releasing its final sparks. I will never see another winter’s snow. I truly believe I will never again feel the life-giving warmth of summer. With this conviction I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps such a story should die with me, but a feeling I cannot name propels me, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist the faintest impulse. While life burned strong in me, I believed my tale held a sacred horror that made it unfit to speak aloud, and now, on the brink of death, I profane its hidden terrors. It is like the grove of the Furies, where only the dying may enter, and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know if anyone but you, my friend, will read these pages when I am gone. I do not write to you alone, because it would please me to dwell on our friendship in a way that would be needless if you were the only reader. I will tell my tale as if meant for strangers. You have often asked why I live so solitary, why I weep, and why I cling to my impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I could not answer; in death I unveil the mystery. Others will toss these pages aside, but to you, Woodville—kind, affectionate friend—they will be dear, the precious memorials of a heartbroken girl who, dying, is still warmed by gratitude for you. Your tears will fall on the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will. While I breathe, I thank you for your sympathy."}

