          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f80a8342ba133a90a331d52a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All the young people were eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth talked of going again himself, since it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross; and though it was November, the weather was not truly bad. Louisa, the most excited of them all, decided they must go. Along with the pleasure of acting on her own wishes, she now believed there was merit in insisting on her way, so she overruled her parents\u2019 desire to wait until summer. Thus Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth were all to go to Lyme. \\n\\nTheir first careless plan was to leave in the morning and return that night. Mr Musgrove refused for the sake of his horses; and when they considered it more sensibly, they saw that a day in mid-November would leave little time to explore a new place, after the seven hours the countryside required for travelling there and back. They therefore decided to stay the night and not return until dinner the following day. This felt like a good change; and though they gathered at the Great House for an early breakfast and set out on time, it was past noon before both carriages\u2014Mr Musgrove\u2019s coach containing the four ladies, and Charles\u2019s curricle carrying Charles and Captain Wentworth\u2014were rolling down the long hill into Lyme and entering its even steeper main street. It was clear that they would have no more than enough time to look around before daylight and warmth were gone. \\n\\nAfter finding rooms and ordering dinner at an inn, the next thing was to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any amusements or variety Lyme might offer as a resort. The assembly rooms were closed, nearly all the lodgers gone, hardly any families left but the residents; and, as the buildings themselves are not worth admiring, the town\u2019s remarkable setting is what draws the visitor\u2019s eye\u2014the main street almost rushing into the water, the walk along the Cobb curving around the pleasant little bay that in season bustles with bathing machines and company; the Cobb itself, with its old marvels and recent improvements, and the very beautiful line of cliffs stretching east of the town. It would take a very odd stranger indeed not to find enough charm in Lyme\u2019s immediate surroundings to make him wish to know the place better.\"}"
