          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fee64b4df13ba46a81315dc4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m not eating at home, so I won\u2019t need you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, sir,\u201d the man replied, leaving the room.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, we can\u2019t waste a minute. This chest is heavy\u2014I\u2019ll take it for you. You bring the rest.\u201d His words came fast, commanding. Campbell felt overpowered by him. They left together.\\n\\nAt the top landing, Dorian took out the key and turned it in the lock. Then he stopped, a troubled look in his eyes. He shivered. \u201cI don\u2019t think I can go in, Alan,\u201d he murmured.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s nothing to me. I don\u2019t need you,\u201d Campbell said coldly.\\n\\nDorian pushed the door halfway open. As he did, the face in his portrait leered at him in the sunlight. The torn curtain lay on the floor before it. He remembered that last night, for the first time in his life, he had forgotten to hide the cursed canvas and was about to rush forward when he drew back with a shudder.\\n\\nWhat was that disgusting red moisture shining wet and slick on one of the hands, as if the canvas had sweated blood? How terrible it looked!\u2014for a moment worse, he thought, than the silent thing lying stretched out on the table, the thing whose grotesque warped shadow on the stained carpet showed it hadn\u2019t moved, but was still there just as he had left it.\\n\\nHe took a deep breath, opened the door wider, and with half-closed eyes and his head turned away, walked quickly in, determined not to look even once at the dead man. Then he bent down, snatched up the gold-and-purple hanging, and threw it over the picture.\\n\\nHe stopped there, too afraid to turn around, his eyes fixed on the complex patterns before him. He heard Campbell carrying in the heavy chest, the irons, and the other items needed for his gruesome task. He began to wonder if he and Basil Hallward had ever met and, if they had, what they would have thought of each other.\\n\\n\u201cLeave me now,\u201d said a stern voice behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and hurried out, only aware that the dead man had been shoved back into the chair and that Campbell was staring into a gleaming yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the key turn in the lock.\\n\\nIt was long past seven when Campbell returned to the library. He was pale but icy calm. \u201cI did what you asked,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
