          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2b4e4c0639664e8a1e5759ab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"WHEN Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, purchased Canterville Chase, everyone declared he was acting very foolishly, for there was no question that the place was haunted. In fact, Lord Canterville himself, a man of precise honour, considered it his obligation to point this out to Mr. Otis when they began negotiating. \\n\\n\u201cWe have not wished to occupy the place ourselves,\u201d said Lord Canterville, \u201cever since my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a seizure, from which she never truly recovered, by two skeleton hands laid upon her shoulders as she dressed for dinner. I feel compelled to inform you, Mr. Otis, that several living members of my family, as well as the parish rector, the Rev. Augustus Dampier, a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge, have also seen the ghost. After the Duchess\u2019s unhappy mishap, none of our younger servants would stay on, and Lady Canterville often had very little sleep at night because of the strange noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy Lord,\u201d replied the Minister, \u201cI\u2019ll take the furniture and the ghost at the price. I hail from a modern nation where we can buy anything money will procure; with our lively young men painting the Old World red and carting off your best actresses and prima donnas, I suppose if there were a ghost anywhere in Europe, we\u2019d have it back home soon enough in a public museum or touring as an attraction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid the ghost is real,\u201d said Lord Canterville with a smile, \u201ceven if it has ignored the proposals of your eager impresarios. It has been known for three hundred years, since 1584, in fact, and invariably appears before the death of any family member.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so does the family doctor, for that matter, Lord Canterville. But there is nothing like a ghost, sir, and I suspect the laws of Nature will not be suspended for the British nobility.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou Americans are certainly very natural,\u201d answered Lord Canterville, who did not quite grasp Mr. Otis\u2019s final remark, \u201cand if you do not mind sharing the house with a ghost, that is fine. Only remember I warned you.\u201d\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was finalized, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis\u2014once Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street, a famed belle of New York\u2014was now a striking, middle-aged woman with fine eyes and an excellent profile.\"}"
