          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c6a9464e80eec5509f294f42\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her complexion shifted, and she fell silent.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner was standing slightly behind, and when Elizabeth paused, he asked her if she would introduce him to her friends. This was a courtesy she had never expected from him, and she could barely hide a smile at the thought of his now chasing acquaintance with the very people he had looked down on while proposing to her. \u201cWhat a shock he\u2019ll have,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he learns who they are. He thinks they\u2019re fashionable society.\u201d\\n\\nThe introductions were made immediately, and as she explained their family connection, she glanced at him to see how he took it. She almost expected him to flee from such humiliating relations. He was clearly surprised, yet he bore it well; instead of leaving, he turned back with them and started talking with Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth couldn\u2019t help being pleased, couldn\u2019t help feeling triumphant. It comforted her that he should know she had relatives no one needed to be ashamed of. She listened closely to every exchange, proud of each word from her uncle that revealed his intelligence, taste, or good manners.\\n\\nThe conversation soon shifted to fishing, and she heard Mr. Darcy, with utmost politeness, invite him to fish there whenever he liked while he remained nearby, offering to lend him tackle and pointing out the stretches of the river where the fish were most plentiful. Mrs. Gardiner, walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, shot her a look full of astonishment. Elizabeth said nothing, but she was deeply gratified; the compliment had to be meant for her. Still, her amazement was intense, and she kept repeating to herself, \u201cWhy has he changed so much? What could it come from? It cannot be because of me; it cannot be for my sake that his manners have softened like this. My reproaches at Hunsford could not have produced such a change. It\u2019s impossible he could still love me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter walking this way for some time\u2014two ladies in front, two gentlemen behind\u2014they rearranged themselves when they climbed back from the riverbank, where they had gone to examine an unusual water plant. The change came from Mrs. Gardiner, who, tired from the morning\u2019s exertion, found Elizabeth\u2019s arm insufficient and therefore took her husband\u2019s instead.\"}"
