{"request_id":"4d14db3e501f9bed65d6494a","rewritten_text":"It was past two o’clock when a sudden turn brought me beside the lake near a cove where a small skiff was tied. It was close to our house, and I could see my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat, and, used to such exploits, pushed it off and rowed as fast as I could. As I came across, dressed in white with only my tartan rachan over me, my hair streaming down, and moving faster than anyone thought possible, my father later said I looked more like a spirit than a girl. I reached the shore, my father held the boat, I leapt out, and in a moment I was in his arms.

Only then did life begin for me. Everything changed from dull sameness to the brightest joy. The happiness I found with my father was far beyond what I had dared to hope. We were always together, and our conversations never ran out. He had spent sixteen years among nations barely known in Europe; he had wandered through Persia, Arabia, the north of India, and had entered the dwellings of the natives with a freedom few Europeans enjoyed. His stories of their customs, his anecdotes, and his descriptions of scenery filled delightful hours when we grew tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

The sound of affection was so new to me that I listened with delight when he told me what he had felt for me during those long years when everyone thought he had forgotten me. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl; but later, as grief faded and hope returned, I could only turn to her, and in cities and deserts her fairy form, as I imagined it, was always before me. The northern breeze that refreshed me was sweeter, as if it carried part of your spirit. Often I thought of coming back at once to take you to some fertile island where we could live in peace forever. On my return my hopes were crushed by fears; my impatience became painful beyond measure. I hardly dared think that the sun might shine and the moon rise, not on your living form but on your grave. But no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my comfort, and my hope.”"}

