          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"06823ad9dbcc8d361d07c0ca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond paced the room in anger. I waited, prepared for his reproaches, while Adrian leaned against the wall. \u201cThis is utterly absurd,\u201d he exclaimed. \u201cIf you were schoolboys, you could not behave more unreasonably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou do not understand,\u201d Raymond answered. \u201cThis is merely part of a system\u2014a scheme of tyranny to which I will never submit. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the sole slave in the nation? My privacy invaded, my actions judged, my friends insulted? I will rid myself of it all. Be witnesses,\u201d he said, removing the star, badge of office, from his breast and flinging it on the table. \u201cI renounce my post; I abdicate my power\u2014let anyone else take it!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet it be taken,\u201d cried Adrian, \u201cby one who can declare himself, or whom the nation will call, your superior. There is no man in England presumptuous enough. Know yourself, Raymond, and your anger will subside; your satisfaction return. Only a few months ago, whenever we prayed for our country or ourselves, we also prayed for the Protector\u2019s life and welfare, so closely were they linked. Your hours were devoted to our good, your ambition to earn our praise. You adorned our towns with buildings, established useful institutions, made the soil abundantly fertile. The powerful and unjust shrank from your tribunal, and the poor and oppressed lifted their heads like flowers awakened at dawn by your protection.\\n\\n\u201cCan you marvel that we are in shock and lament when all seems altered? Yet this splenetic mood is already ending; resume your duties. Your supporters will greet you, your enemies fall silent, our love, honour, and duty will again surround you. Command yourself, Raymond, and the world lies under your sway.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this might be good advice for someone else,\u201d Raymond answered gloomily. \u201cLearn your own lesson, and you, the first peer of the realm, might become its sovereign. You, the good, the wise, the just, might command every heart. But I see now\u2014too soon for my own peace, too late for England\u2019s benefit\u2014that I took on a task beyond me. I cannot command myself. My passions rule me; my slightest impulse tyrannizes me. Do you believe I abandoned the Protectorate, as I have done, in a fit of spleen? By the living God, I swear I will never take up that bauble again, never again burden myself with the weight of care and misery it represents.\"}"
