          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"481a938ea13e7a18a1091f69\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With these shrieks, many of the women, whipped into sightless fury, spun around, striking and clawing at their own companions until they collapsed in a passionate faint, saved only by their men from being crushed beneath the feet around them.\\n\\nEven so, not an instant was squandered; not a single heartbeat! That Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville and might be freed. Never, if Saint Antoine remembered his own torments, insults, and grievances! Men and women under arms poured out of the Quarter with such speed, dragging even these last dregs behind them with a suction so strong, that in fifteen minutes there remained in Saint Antoine\u2019s bosom only a few ancient crones and the crying little ones.\\n\\nNo, by then they packed the Hall of Examination where this old, ugly, wicked man stood, overflowing into the nearby open space and streets. The Defarges, husband and wife, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three pressed forward in the front ranks, not far from him in the Hall.\\n\\n\u201cLook!\u201d cried madame, pointing with her knife. \u201cLook at the old villain bound with ropes. Well done to tie a bunch of grass on his back. Ha, ha! Well done indeed. Let him feast on it now!\u201d Madame tucked her knife beneath her arm and applauded as though at a spectacle.\\n\\nThe people directly behind Madame Defarge explained the reason for her delight to those behind them, and these to others, and they to more, until the neighbouring streets echoed with the clatter of clapping hands. Likewise, during the two or three hours of tedious drawl, and the winnowing of many bushels of words, Madame Defarge\u2019s frequent bursts of impatience were caught up, with astonishing rapidity, at a distance: all the more because certain men who by some remarkable agility had climbed the exterior of the building to look in through the windows, knew Madame Defarge well and served as a telegraph between her and the throng outside.\\n\\nAt last the sun climbed so high it cast a gentle beam, like hope or protection, straight down upon the old prisoner\u2019s head. That favour was too great to endure; in a heartbeat the barrier of dust and chaff that had lasted so improbably long shattered, and Saint Antoine possessed him!\\n\\nThe news flashed at once to the farthest edges of the multitude. Defarge had merely leapt over a railing and a table, enclosing the wretched creature in a lethal embrace\u2014Madame Defarge had just followed, twining her hand into one of the ropes binding him\u2014The Vengeance and Jacques Three had not yet arrived, and the men at the windows had not yet swooped into the Hall like birds of prey from their lofty roosts\u2014when the shout seemed to rise across the whole city, \u201cBring him out!\u201d\"}"
        }
