          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ffbb680c1ab79e9c07ec15e3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now hurry to the more moving part of my story. I will describe the events that impressed me with feelings that have transformed me from what I once was into what I am now.\\n\\nSpring advanced quickly; the weather turned fair and the sky remained clear. I was startled that what had seemed barren and gloomy now blossomed with the most beautiful flowers and greenery. My senses were delighted and refreshed by countless scents and countless sights of beauty.\\n\\nOne of those days, when my cottagers took their regular rest from labor\u2014the old man played his guitar while the children listened\u2014I noticed that Felix\u2019s face was indescribably melancholy. He sighed often, and when his father paused in his playing, I guessed from his manner that he was asking the reason for his son\u2019s sadness. Felix answered in a cheerful tone, and the old man was about to resume his music when someone knocked at the door.\\n\\nIt was a lady on horseback, accompanied by a countryman serving as a guide. She wore a dark outfit and was covered with a thick black veil. Agatha asked a question, to which the stranger only replied by speaking Felix\u2019s name in a sweet voice. Her voice was musical but unlike any of my friends\u2019. Hearing this name, Felix hurried to the lady, who, once she saw him, lifted her veil. I saw a face of angelic beauty and expression. Her hair was glossy raven black, intricately braided; her eyes were dark but gentle and lively; her features were perfectly proportioned; her complexion was wondrously fair, each cheek tinted with a lovely pink.\\n\\nFelix seemed overwhelmed with delight at her sight. Every trace of sorrow vanished from his face, replaced instantly by such ecstatic joy I could hardly believe it possible. His eyes sparkled and his cheeks flushed with pleasure, and in that moment I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. She was moved by different feelings; wiping a few tears from her lovely eyes, she held out her hand to Felix, who kissed it rapturously and called her, as best I could make out, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him but smiled. He helped her dismount, dismissed her guide, and led her into the cottage. A conversation followed between him and his father, and the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet, ready to kiss his hand, but he lifted her and embraced her warmly.\\n\\nI soon realized that although the stranger spoke clearly and had a language of her own, neither she understood the cottagers nor did they understand her.\"}"
