{"request_id":"f25deb3fee77cfc365713c7e","rewritten_text":"Among the public edifices in a certain town—which, for several reasons, prudence forbids me to name or even disguise under an invented title—stands one ancient institution common to nearly every place, grand or humble: a workhouse. Within that establishment there came into being, on a day and date so unimportant to the reader at this juncture that I need not repeat them, the morsel of mortality whose appellation heads this chapter.

For a considerable period after the parish surgeon ushered it into this vale of tears, it remained doubtful whether the child would live to bear any name at all; in which case these memoirs, if they appeared at all, would have been squeezed into a couple of pages, furnishing the most succinct and accurate specimen of biography in any literature.

Though I will not assert that birth within a workhouse is the happiest or most enviable fate that can befall a human creature, yet I maintain that, in Oliver Twist’s case, nothing better could possibly have occurred. The truth is, they had some difficulty prevailing on Oliver to undertake the troublesome task of breathing—a habit which custom has rendered indispensable to our comfortable existence—and for some time he lay gasping on a little flock mattress, perched precariously between this world and the next, with the scales decidedly leaning toward the latter. Had Oliver, during that interval, been surrounded by attentive grandmothers, concerned aunts, skilled nurses, and learned physicians, he would, beyond all question, have been dispatched in no time. But as there was no one there except a pauper crone, somewhat befogged by an unusual ration of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such cases on contract, the contest was left to Oliver and Nature. The upshot was that, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and proceeded to inform the inmates of the workhouse of the new burden laid upon the parish by raising as mighty a cry as could be expected from an infant lad who had been in possession of that useful appendage, a voice, for no more than three minutes and a quarter."}

