          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c546c7fe4def641c795563d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"What kind of life could they expect while Fate kept this terrible destiny in balance? The wedding had to be delayed at all costs. He was completely sure of that. He loved the girl deeply, and even the brush of her fingers when they sat together sent joy through every nerve, yet he clearly saw his duty. He knew he had no right to marry until he had carried out the murder. Once it was done, he could stand at the altar with Sybil Merton, place his life in her hands, and feel no fear of wrongdoing. Once it was done, he could hold her, certain she would never have to blush for him or bow her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner the better for them both.\\n\\nMany in his situation would have chosen the easy pleasures of delay over the steep climb of duty, but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure before principle. His love was more than passion; to him, Sybil represented all that was good and noble. At first he felt natural disgust at what he was asked to do, but that feeling soon faded. His heart assured him it was not a sin but a sacrifice, and his mind told him there was no other way. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others, and though the task was terrible, he knew he could not let selfishness defeat love. Sooner or later everyone faces the same question; all of us must answer it. For Lord Arthur it came early, before the cynicism of middle age could corrupt him or the shallow vanity of fashionable life could dull his heart, so he felt no doubt about his duty. Fortunately he was not a dreamer or idle dilettante. Had he been, he might have wavered like Hamlet and let indecision wreck his plans. Instead he was essentially practical. To him, life was action, not thought. He possessed that rare treasure: common sense.\\n\\nThe wild, muddy emotions of the previous night had completely vanished, and he now felt almost ashamed when he recalled his mad rush from street to street, his fierce emotional torment. The very honesty of his suffering now made it seem unreal. He wondered how he could have been so foolish as to storm and rage against what was bound to happen.\"}"
