          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f2fddb4a2ce33e0ad632988\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered, then returned and closed the window behind him. Once at the door, he turned the key, opened it, and did not even glance toward the murdered man. He maintained that the secret was to refuse to acknowledge the reality. The friend whose fatal portrait had caused all his misery had vanished from his life. That sufficed. Suddenly he remembered the lamp\u2014a curious Moorish piece of dull silver worked with arabesques of burnished steel and dotted with rough turquoises. Perhaps his servant might notice its absence and start asking questions. He hesitated briefly, then went back to take it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the lifeless figure. How motionless it lay! How ghastly white the long hands appeared! It resembled some hideous wax effigy. After locking the door again, he crept quietly down the stairs. The woodwork creaked, complaining as though in torment. He paused repeatedly to listen. No, all was still. It was nothing but the echo of his own steps. Reaching the library, he spotted the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden. He opened a secret press built into the wainscoting, the same place where he kept his odd disguises, and placed them inside. They could easily be burned later. He took out his watch: twenty minutes to two. He sat and began to think. Year after year\u2014almost month by month\u2014men in England were hanged for what he had just done. Murder seemed within the air, as though some red star had drawn too near the earth.... Yet what proof existed against him? Basil Hallward had left at eleven; no one had witnessed his return. Most of the servants were away at Selby Royal. His valet was asleep.... Paris! Of course. That was where Basil had gone\u2014by the midnight train, just as he had planned. With his strange reserve, suspicions would not rise for months. Months! Everything could be destroyed well before that. A sudden idea jolted 