          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b84c3ac3f0396fec96aa484c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday nights brought our village a delightful feeling of tidying up and pausing quietly before beginning life anew, and that feeling emboldened Joe to linger half an hour later than usual. When the extra half-hour and the rum-and-water ran dry together, Joe rose to leave and took my hand. \u201cHold a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the odd man. \u201cI\u2019m sure there\u2019s a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if there is, the lad shall have it.\u201d He fished it out from a fistful of small change, wrapped it in wrinkled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he declared. \u201cRemember! All your own.\u201d I thanked him, staring so long that good manners were forgotten, and clung tight to Joe. He bade Joe good-night, wished Mr. Wopsle the same (for he went out with us), and bestowed on me nothing but the intention of his aiming eye\u2014not even that, for he shut it; yet wonders can be worked with an eye by hiding it. Walking home, had I been inclined to chatter, the conversation would have been entirely mine, as Mr. Wopsle left us at the Jolly Bargemen\u2019s door, and Joe trudged home with his mouth gaping, to let as much air as possible wash the rum away. But I was heavy-headed, overwhelmed by the resurfacing of my old misdeed and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else. My sister was not particularly cross when we appeared in the kitchen, and Joe took that rare mood as license to report the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d cried Mrs. Joe triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see.\u201d I unfolded the paper, and the coin was sound. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d exclaimed Mrs. Joe, tossing the shilling aside and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo One-Pound notes?\u201d Nothing less than two plump, sweating pound notes, as though they had long fraternized with every cattle market in the county. Joe snatched his hat and ran to the Jolly Bargemen to return them. While he was away, I settled on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, fairly certain we would not find the man there. Soon Joe came back to say the man had gone, but he had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
