          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ee012da4c83fdd4b328cb520\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI should warn you that I\u2019m going to tell Sybil everything. She\u2019s coming to lunch with me tomorrow to talk about hats, and if Mr. Podgers discovers that you have a bad temper, or a tendency toward gout, or a wife stashed away in Bayswater, I will certainly pass all of it on to her.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u201cI\u2019m not worried,\u201d he said. \u201cSybil knows me as well as I know her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I\u2019m a bit sorry you said that. The best foundation for marriage is mutual misunderstanding. No, I\u2019m not being cynical, I\u2019ve simply lived a little, which amounts to the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is dying to have his palm read. Don\u2019t bother telling him he\u2019s engaged to one of the most beautiful girls in London, because that was in the Morning Post a month ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear Lady Windermere,\u201d cried the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u201cplease let Mr. Podgers stay with me a little longer. He\u2019s just told me I ought to go on the stage, and I\u2019m fascinated.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf he\u2019s told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I\u2019ll certainly take him away. Come here at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, making a small pout as she rose from the sofa, \u201cif I\u2019m not allowed to go on the stage, I should at least be allowed to watch.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course; we\u2019re all going to watch,\u201d said Lady Windermere. \u201cAnd now, Mr. Podgers, do tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is one of my special favorites.\u201d\\n\\nBut when Mr. Podgers saw Lord Arthur\u2019s palm he turned strangely pale and kept silent. A shudder ran through him, and his thick eyebrows twitched convulsively, in that odd, irritating way they did when he was confused. Huge beads of sweat broke out on his sallow forehead like some poisonous dew, and his plump fingers turned cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur noticed these unsettling signs, and for the first time in his life, he felt afraid. He wanted to bolt from the room, but he held himself back. Better to know the worst, whatever it might be, than endure this horrible uncertainty.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019re all waiting,\u201d snapped Lady Windermere, but the palm reader still said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m convinced Arthur is destined for the stage,\u201d said Lady Jedburgh, \u201cand after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him.\u201d\"}"
