          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9522d6c3e0359f3e742e1f68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was energized by the intense sensation of regained health; he felt that serving as general of the Athenians gave him a role worthy of his ambition, and in his hope of conquering Constantinople he counted on creating an event that would stand as a landmark in the ages, an exploit unmatched in human history. A city rich with historic association, its beautiful location celebrated throughout the world, had been the stronghold of the Moslems for centuries; he believed it would soon be rescued from slavery and barbarism and returned to a people distinguished for genius, civilization, and the spirit of liberty. Perdita rested on his restored companionship, on his love, his hopes, and his fame, as comfortably as a Sybarite on a luxurious couch; every thought brought delight, each emotion seemed bathed in a congenial and soft element.\\n\\nWe arrived at Kishan on the seventh of July. The weather during our journey had been clear. Each day, before dawn, we left our night camp and watched the shadows retreat from hill and valley while the sun\u2019s golden approach spread. The soldiers with us, full of national vivacity, took enthusiastic pleasure in the beauty of nature. The rising of the sun was greeted with triumphant music, while birds, heard in brief intervals, filled the spaces between the tunes. At noon we pitched our tents in some shady valley or wooded hollow among the mountains, while a stream babbling over pebbles lulled us into grateful sleep. Our evening march, calmer, was even more delightful than the restless energy of the morning. When the band played, they instinctively chose melodies of moderated passion; the farewell of love or the lament for absence would be followed and ended by a solemn hymn that suited the gentle loveliness of evening and raised our minds to grand, religious thoughts. Often all sounds ceased so we could listen to the nightingale, while fireflies danced in bright patterns and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travelers. If we passed a valley, gentle shadows surrounded us, and rocks were tinted with beautiful colors. If we crossed a mountain, Greece lay below like a living map, its famous peaks cutting the sky, its rivers threading silver lines through fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travelers gazed in ecstasy at this splendid landscape, so different from the muted tones and melancholy graces of our homeland. When we left Macedonia, the fertile yet low plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties, yet the journey stayed interesting. An advanced guard announced our approach, and the country people quickly moved to honor Lord Raymond.\"}"
