          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c3f85a0fb3d16a0192ac8702\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without pausing to consider, and hardly aware of her own feelings, Elizabeth, upon finishing this letter, immediately snatched up the other and, opening it with extreme impatience, read the following: it was dated a day later than the end of the first. \\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received the hurried note I sent before. I hope this one is clearer, yet though I am not pressed for time, my head is so confused that I cannot promise coherence. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what to say, but I have unpleasant news and cannot delay it. Foolish as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now eager to know it has actually happened, for there is too much reason to fear they never went to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day prior, only a few hours after the express. Although Lydia\u2019s brief note to Mrs. F. led them to believe they were headed to Gretna Green, Denny let slip that he believed W. never meant to go there or to marry Lydia at all, and this was repeated to Colonel F., who, instantly alarmed, set out from B., intending to follow their trail. He easily traced them to Clapham, but no farther, for upon entering that town they transferred to a hackney-coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known after that is that they were seen continuing on the London road. I am at a loss what to believe. After making every possible inquiry on that side of London, Colonel F. came through into Hertfordshire, diligently repeating his questions at every turnpike, and at the inns at Barnet and Hatfield, yet to no avail\u2014no such persons had passed. With the warmest concern he proceeded to Longbourn and shared his fears with us in a way that does him great credit. I truly pity him and Mrs. F., though no blame can be laid on them. Our distress, dear Lizzy, is extreme. My father and mother imagine the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make a private wedding in town more suitable than continuing with their first plan; and even if he could conceive such a scheme against a girl of Lydia\u2019s connections, which seems unlikely, could she be so lost to all propriety? Impossible! I am pained, however, to find that Colonel F. is not inclined to trust in their marriage: he shook his head when I voiced my hopes and said he feared W. was not a man to be depended on.\u201d\"}"
