          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46caa002e0bfdcd465ff068d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking at him, you would have said Nemesis had snatched Pallas\u2019s shield and shown him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He seemed turned to stone, his face marble in its sorrow. He had lived the delicate, luxurious life of a young man with rank and money, a life exquisite in its freedom from sordid worry, its beautiful boyish carelessness; and now, for the first time, he became aware of Destiny\u2019s terrible mystery, of Doom\u2019s awful meaning.\\n\\nHow insane and monstrous it all seemed! Could it be that written on his hand, in characters he himself could not read but another could decipher, was some terrible secret of sin, some blood-red mark of crime? Was there truly no escape? Were we nothing more than chess pieces moved by an unseen power, vessels the potter shapes at his whim, for honor or for shame? His reason rebelled against it, yet he felt some tragedy hanging over him, that he had been suddenly summoned to bear an unbearable burden. Actors are so fortunate. They can choose whether to appear in tragedy or in comedy, whether to suffer or to revel, to laugh or to weep. But real life is different. Most men and women are forced to play parts for which they have no talent. Our Guildensterns play Hamlet for us, and our Hamlets have to jest like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, but the play is badly cast.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers entered the room. When he saw Lord Arthur he started, and his coarse, fleshy face turned a sort of greenish yellow. The two men locked eyes, and for a moment there was silence.\\n\\n\u201cThe Duchess left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and asked me to bring it to her,\u201d said Mr. Podgers at last. \u201cAh, I see it on the sofa! Good evening.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Podgers, I insist on your giving me a direct answer to a question I\u2019m about to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnother time, Lord Arthur. The Duchess is anxious. I\u2019m afraid I must go.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re not leaving. The Duchess isn\u2019t in any hurry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLadies should not be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u201d said Mr. Podgers with his sickly smile. \u201cThe fair sex tends to lose patience.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s finely cut lips curled in irritated scorn. Just then, the poor Duchess seemed to him of no importance at all. He crossed the room to where Mr. Podgers stood and held out his hand.\\n\\n\u201cTell me what you saw there,\u201d he said. \u201cTell me the truth. I must know. I\u2019m not a child.\u201d\"}"
