          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d60fb291b954106e9e14db0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Because of this, I decided to get out as soon as we reached the town and remove myself from his hearing. I carried out this plan without trouble. My small portmanteau was in the boot under my feet; I only had to turn a hinge to pull it out. I dropped it in front of me, followed it down, and found myself standing under the first lamp on the first stones of the town pavement. The convicts stayed with the coach, and I knew where they would be taken to the river. In my mind, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting at the slime-covered steps, heard again the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d like an order to dogs, and saw once more the evil-looking Noah\u2019s Ark resting on the black water.\\n\\nI could not have said what I feared, because my fear was undefined and vague, but it held me strongly. As I walked toward the hotel, I felt a dread far beyond simply meeting someone unpleasant that made me shake. I am sure it never took a clear shape and that it revived for a few minutes the terror of my childhood.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I not only ordered dinner there but had started eating before the waiter recognized me. Once he apologized for forgetting me, he asked if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014the same one who had delivered the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was apprenticed\u2014looked surprised and soon slid a dirty old copy of the local paper directly in front of me. I picked it up and read this paragraph:\\n\\n\u201cOur readers will hear with some interest, concerning the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young ironworker from this area (what a subject, by the way, for the magical pen of our not yet fully appreciated townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!), that the youth\u2019s earliest patron, companion, and friend was a highly respected person not entirely disconnected from the corn and seed trade, and whose very convenient and spacious business premises lie within one hundred miles of the High Street. It is not without personal pride that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is good to know that our town produced the founder of the latter\u2019s fortunes.\u201d\"}"
