          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6420b95c69dd734039c63957\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For a moment I was too amazed to think clearly. I knew my planned design differed in detail from this one, even though the general shapes were somewhat alike. Soon I took a candle, sat at the other end of the room, and examined the parchment more closely. When I turned it over, I saw my own sketch on the back, exactly as I had drawn it. At first I was only astonished by the striking similarity in outline\u2014the odd coincidence that, without my knowledge, a skull would be on the other side of the parchment, right beneath my picture of the scarab, and that this skull, in both outline and size, would match my drawing so closely. The strangeness of the coincidence entirely stunned me for a time. Coincidences often act this way. The mind tries to find a connection, a cause and effect, and, unable to do so, falls into a kind of temporary paralysis. Yet when I recovered from this stupor, a new conviction slowly emerged, one that startled me even more than the coincidence itself. I began to remember clearly\u2014positively\u2014that there had been no drawing on the parchment when I sketched the scarab. I was absolutely sure of it; I recalled turning first one side and then the other, hunting for the cleanest spot. Had the skull been there, I certainly would have noticed it. Here was a mystery I could not explain; but even then, there was a faint glow\u2014like a glow-worm\u2014in the deepest corners of my mind, an early hint of the truth that last night\u2019s adventure displayed so brilliantly. I stood up at once, put the parchment away safely, and put off further thought until I could be alone.\\n\\n\u201cWhen you had gone, and Jupiter was fast asleep, I began a more methodical study of the matter. First I considered how the parchment came into my hands. The spot where we found the scarab was on the mainland shore, about a mile east of the island, and only a short distance above the high tide line. When I picked it up, it bit me sharply, making me drop it. Jupiter, being his cautious self, before seizing the insect\u2014which had flown toward him\u2014looked around for a leaf or something similar to use to handle it.\"}"
