          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cbc361fd4bf6554f7e4bf8bd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped furtively out of the wainscoting, a wicked smile twisting his cruel, lined mouth, and the moon veiled herself in a cloud as he crept past the great oriel window where his own arms and those of his murdered wife shone in azure and gold. Still he glided onward, like some baleful shadow, the very darkness seeming to recoil from him as he went. Once he fancied he heard a cry and halted, but it proved only the hound from the Red Farm baying, so he continued, muttering queer sixteenth-century imprecations and now and then flourishing the rusty dagger through the midnight air. At last he reached the corner of the passage leading to unlucky Washington\u2019s room. He paused there for an instant, the wind tossing his long grey locks about his head and twisting into grotesque folds the indescribable horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he knew the hour had come. He chuckled softly and turned the corner; yet scarcely had he done so when, with a piteous cry of dread, he recoiled and hid his blanched face in his lean, bony hands. Directly before him stood a ghastly apparition, motionless as carved stone, monstrous as some lunatic\u2019s dream. Its head was bald and burnished; its face round, bloated, and white; hideous laughter seemed to have contorted its features into an everlasting grin. From its eyes poured rays of crimson light, its mouth gaped like a fiery well, and a dreadful garment, akin to his own, wrapped its Titan frame in voiceless snows. Across its breast hung a placard inscribed with strange letters of antique fashion\u2014a scroll of disgrace, a record of wild transgressions, some awful register of crime\u2014and in its right hand it upheld a falchion of gleaming steel.\\n\\nHaving never beheld a ghost before, he was naturally seized with profound terror, and after a second hurried glance at the dreadful apparition, he bolted back to his room, tangling himself in his long winding-sheet as he raced down the corridor, and finally dropping the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jack-boots, where the butler discovered it next morning. Once within his own chamber, he flung himself onto a narrow pallet-bed and buried his face beneath the coverings. After a time, however, the stout old Canterville spirit reasserted itself, and he resolved to speak with the other ghost as soon as daylight came. Thus, just as dawn was silvering the hills, he made his way once more to the spot where he had first seen the grisly phantom, convinced that, after all, two ghosts were better than one, and that with his newfound ally he might safely tackle the twins.\"}"
