          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"404dcbcaa281281caf4cb020\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, bought Canterville Chase, everyone said he was making a foolish choice because the place was definitely haunted. Lord Canterville himself, a man of strict honour, felt obliged to mention this to Mr. Otis when they discussed the purchase.\\n\\n\u201cWe have not cared to live there,\u201d said Lord Canterville, \u201csince my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a seizure\u2014one from which she never truly recovered\u2014when two skeletal hands were laid on her shoulders as she dressed for dinner. I must tell you, Mr. Otis, that several living members of my family have seen the ghost, as has the rector of the parish, the Reverend Augustus Dampier, a fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the poor Duchess\u2019s accident, none of our younger servants would stay, and Lady Canterville lost much sleep because of the strange noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy lord,\u201d answered the Minister, \u201cI will take the furniture and the ghost at their price. I come from a modern country where we have everything money can buy, and with our lively young men painting the Old World red and running away with your best actresses and prima donnas, I\u2019m sure that if there were a ghost in Europe, we would have it at home very soon, either in one of our public museums or travelling as a show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid the ghost is real,\u201d said Lord Canterville, smiling, \u201ceven if it has ignored the offers of your daring impresarios. It has been known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always appears before a member of our family dies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so does the family doctor, Lord Canterville. But there is no such thing as a ghost, and I don\u2019t believe the laws of nature will be suspended for the British aristocracy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certainly very matter-of-fact in America,\u201d replied Lord Canterville, who did not quite understand Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark. \u201cIf you do not mind a ghost in the house, that is fine. Just remember I warned you.\u201d\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was completed, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family moved into Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis\u2014formerly Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street, a celebrated New York beauty\u2014was now a very handsome middle-aged woman with fine eyes and a striking profile.\"}"
        }
      ],
