          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9eacd2f939f7443a1c19b592\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning they found her lifeless in bed, having apparently slipped away without suffering. Sir Mathew Reid had been summoned at once, yet naturally nothing could be done, and her burial was arranged for the twenty-second at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death she had drawn up her will, leaving Lord Arthur her little house in Curzon Street along with all her furniture, personal possessions, and paintings, except for her miniature collection, which was destined for her sister, Lady Margaret Rufford, and her amethyst necklace, which she bequeathed to Sybil Merton. The estate was not worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the lawyer, was particularly anxious that Lord Arthur return immediately, if possible, since a great number of bills awaited settlement, and Lady Clementina had never kept proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s thoughtful remembrance, and felt Mr. Podgers had much to answer for. Nevertheless his devotion to Sybil eclipsed every other feeling, and the awareness that he had fulfilled his duty filled him with serenity and solace. Reaching Charing Cross, he felt entirely content.\\n\\nThe Mertons welcomed him with genuine warmth. Sybil exacted a promise that nothing should ever again come between them, and their wedding was fixed for the seventh of June. Life once more appeared bright and splendid to him, and all his former happiness returned.\\n\\nYet one afternoon, while he was inspecting the Curzon Street house with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil, burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers of miscellaneous trifles, the young woman suddenly gave a little cry of pleasure.\\n\\n\u201cWhat have you discovered, Sybil?\u201d asked Lord Arthur, glancing up from his task and smiling.\\n\\n\u201cThis charming little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it quaint and Dutch? Do let me keep it! I\u2019m sure amethysts won\u2019t suit me until I\u2019m well past eighty.\u201d\\n\\nIt was the box that had once contained the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, a faint flush rising to his face. He had almost forgotten what he had done, and it struck him as oddly fitting that Sybil, for whom he had endured such dreadful tension, should be the one to remind him of it first.\\n\\n\u201cOf course you may have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, thank you, Arthur; and might I have the bonbon as well? I never dreamed Lady Clementina cared for sweets. I imagined she was far too intellectual.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur turned deathly pale, and a ghastly thought flashed through his mind.\"}"
