          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a3b18df41b9e79fe6ea00628\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After sharing in the general lament over the dreadful result Elizabeth now regarded as nearly inevitable, and which Miss Bennet could not declare entirely impossible, the former resumed the topic: \u201cBut tell me every detail I have not yet heard. I want more particulars. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no suspicion before the flight occurred? They must have been constantly together.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted he had often suspected some preference, particularly on Lydia\u2019s part, yet nothing alarming. I am truly sorry for him. He behaved with the utmost attention and kindness. He _was_ coming to us to express his concern before he had the least notion they had not gone to Scotland; once that fear spread, it sped his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Denny remain convinced Wickham would not marry? Did he know they meant to run off? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; yet when questioned by _him_, Denny denied any knowledge of their scheme and refused his true opinion. He did not repeat his assurance they would never wed, and from _that_ I am inclined to hope he might have been misunderstood earlier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster arrived, none of you suspected they were not truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea occur to us? I was a little uneasy\u2014slightly fearful for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in wedlock, because I knew his behaviour had not always been entirely correct. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only perceived the imprudence of the match. Kitty then confessed, with very natural triumph at knowing more than we did, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had been prepared for such a measure. She had known, it seems, of their being enamoured for many weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before going to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I think not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think ill of Wickham himself? Is he aware of Wickham\u2019s true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit he did not speak as favourably of Wickham as formerly. He considered him imprudent and extravagant; and since this wretched business occurred, it is reported he quitted Meryton heavily in debt\u2014but I hope that is untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, had we been less silent\u2014had we told what we knew of him\u2014this would never have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it might have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut to expose any person\u2019s past faults without knowing their current sentiments appeared unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
