          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ef58b442699868d19115c58a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was well past midday before he awoke. His valet had glided in several times on tiptoe to see if he stirred, puzzled at what kept his young master abed so late. At last the bell sounded, and Victor entered quietly with a cup of tea and a stack of letters, all arranged on a small tray of old S\u00e8vres porcelain, and drew back the olive satin curtains with their shimmering blue lining before the three tall windows.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur has slept admirably this morning,\u201d he remarked with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is the time, Victor?\u201d Dorian Gray asked, still heavy with sleep.\\n\\n\u201cOne hour and a quarter after noon, Monsieur.\u201d\\n\\nHow late indeed! He sat upright, took a sip of tea, and began sorting through his correspondence. One note, delivered by hand that morning, was from Lord Henry. He hesitated briefly, then set it aside. The remaining letters he opened without interest. They contained the usual mix of visiting cards, dinner invitations, tickets to private viewings, programmes for charity concerts, and other such things showered upon young men of fashion every season morning. There was a rather hefty bill for a chased silver Louis Quinze dressing set he had yet to pluck up courage to forward to his guardians, who were so staunchly old-fashioned they did not understand that we live in an era when unnecessary objects are our sole necessities; and there were several very politely phrased notes from Jermyn Street money-lenders, offering instant advances of any amount at the most moderate interest.\\n\\nAfter some ten minutes he rose, wrapped himself in an elaborate dressing gown of silk-embroidered cashmere, and moved into the onyx-floored bathroom. The cool water revived him after his prolonged sleep. He seemed to have forgotten all he had endured. Once or twice a faint sense of having been involved in some strange tragedy crossed his mind, yet it all possessed the unreality of a dream.\\n\\nFully dressed, he went into the library and sat down to a light French breakfast set for him on a small round table near the open window. The day was exquisite. The warm air seemed saturated with spices. A bee flew in and circled the blue-dragon bowl filled with sulphur-yellow roses before him. He felt entirely content.\\n\\nSuddenly his gaze fell on the screen he had positioned in front of the portrait, and he gave a start.\\n\\n\u201cToo cold for Monsieur?\u201d the valet asked, placing an omelette on the table. \u201cShall I close the window?\u201d\\n\\nDorian shook his head. \u201cI am not cold,\u201d he murmured.\"}"
