          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e3368261c22abbd86afecc7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During my younger years discontent never crossed my mind, and whenever boredom threatened, the beauty of nature or the study of humanity\u2019s finest and most sublime works always stirred my heart and lifted my spirits. Now I\u2019m a blasted tree; the lightning has struck my soul, and I felt then that I would live on only to display what I would soon stop being\u2014a miserable example of ruined humanity, pitied by others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent quite some time at Oxford, wandering its surroundings and trying to identify every place connected to the most inspiring period of English history. Our little expeditions often stretched on as new sights appeared. We visited the tomb of the illustrious Hampden and the field where that patriot fell. For a moment my soul rose above its degrading, miserable fears to consider the sacred ideals of liberty and self-sacrifice these monuments commemorated. For an instant I dared to shake off my chains and look around with a free, noble spirit, but the iron had bitten deep, and I sank back, trembling and hopeless, into my wretched self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford reluctantly and headed to Matlock, our next stop. The countryside around the village resembled Swiss scenery more than anything else, though everything was on a smaller scale, and the green hills lacked the distant white Alpine crowns that always accompany the pine-clad mountains of my homeland. We toured the astonishing cave and the little natural history cabinets, where the curiosities were arranged just as they are in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. Hearing that last name spoken by Henry made me shudder, and I hurried to leave Matlock, which now seemed tied to that terrible memory.\\n\\nFrom Derby we continued north and spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine myself among the Swiss mountains again. The small patches of snow that still clung to the northern slopes, the lakes, and the crashing rocky streams were all familiar and beloved sights. We made acquaintances there who nearly persuaded me I was happy. Clerval\u2019s joy was even greater than mine; his mind opened up in the company of men of talent, and he discovered in himself more abilities and resources than he\u2019d ever suspected while keeping to his inferiors.\"}"
