          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9c948e95ae75eaf22a006a1b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Such were the happenings that gnawed at Felix\u2019s heart and made him, when I first met him, the most desolate member of his family. Poverty alone he could have borne\u2014indeed, while want had been the reward of his virtue, he took pride in it\u2014but the Turk\u2019s ingratitude and the loss of beloved Safie were afflictions sharper and beyond remedy. Now, however, the Arabian\u2019s arrival breathed new vigor into his spirit.\\n\\nOnce the tidings reached Leghorn that Felix had been stripped of fortune and rank, the merchant ordered his daughter to dismiss every thought of her lover and get ready to return to her native land. Safie\u2019s generous soul rebelled against such commands; she tried to reason with her father, but he angrily departed, repeating his despotic decree.\\n\\nA few days later the Turk entered his daughter\u2019s rooms and abruptly informed her that he had grounds to think his presence in Leghorn had been exposed and that he would soon be seized by the French authorities; therefore he had secured a vessel bound for Constantinople, on which he intended to sail within hours. He planned to leave his daughter with a trusted servant, who was to follow when she pleased, bringing most of his property, which had not yet been transported to Leghorn.\\n\\nLeft alone, Safie pondered what course of action would best suit this crisis. The idea of living in Turkey repelled her; both her faith and her feelings were opposed to it. Among some papers of her father\u2019s that fell into her possession she learned of her lover\u2019s banishment and discovered the name of the place where he now lived. She wavered for a time, but in the end fixed her resolve. Taking certain jewels that were hers and a sum of money, she left Italy accompanied by an attendant native to Leghorn, who spoke the common tongue of Turkey, and set out for Germany.\\n\\nShe arrived safely at a town some twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage, when her companion was seized with a dangerous illness. Safie tended her with devoted care, yet the unfortunate girl died, leaving the Arabian alone, unable to speak the country\u2019s language and wholly unversed in the customs of the world. She nevertheless found herself among kind people. The Italian had revealed the name of their destination, and after her death the mistress of the house where they had boarded ensured that Safie reached her lover\u2019s cottage unharmed.\"}"
