          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a21d74176a2f0e4b7e09a728\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Three or four months went by, and winter had set in for real. I\u2019d been going to school almost every day and could spell, read, and write a little. I could recite the multiplication table up to six times seven is thirty-five, and I doubt I\u2019d ever get any farther even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t care much for math anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I learned to put up with it. Whenever I got too bored I\u2019d skip, and the beating I got the next day actually did me good and perked me up. The longer I went to school, the easier it got. I was getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, too, and they didn\u2019t chafe me so much anymore. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed still pulled on me pretty hard, but before the cold weather hit I used to sneak out to sleep in the woods sometimes, which felt like a relief. I liked the old ways best, but I was starting to like the new ones a bit too. The widow said I was coming along slow but steady, and doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast I accidentally knocked over the salt cellar. I grabbed for the salt as fast as I could to toss some over my left shoulder and ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson beat me to it and slapped my hand away. She said, \u201cTake your hands off that, Huckleberry; what a mess you\u2019re always making!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but I knew that wouldn\u2019t keep the bad luck away. After breakfast I went out feeling nervous and shaky, wondering when it was going to hit me and what it would be. There are ways to fend off some kinds of bad luck, but not this kind, so I didn\u2019t do anything except shuffle along gloomy and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile where you pass through the tall board fence. There was about an inch of fresh snow, and I saw some tracks. They had come up from the quarry, lingered around the stile for a while, and then moved on around the garden fence. It was strange that whoever it was hadn\u2019t come in after hanging around like that. I couldn\u2019t make sense of it. It was really curious somehow. I was about to follow the tracks around, but I bent down to take a closer look at them first.\"}"
