          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a2fb3dd74e9eeee476f8097c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at last, \u201csince the new feeding system began, the coffins are indeed narrower and shallower than before; but we must make some profit, Mr. Bumble. Well-seasoned timber is costly, sir, and all the iron handles come by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery trade has its drawbacks. A fair profit is certainly allowed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course,\u201d the undertaker replied. \u201cAnd if I don\u2019t make money on one item, I make it up later, you see\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cThough I must say,\u201d continued the undertaker, picking up the thread Mr. Bumble had broken, \u201cI must say, Mr. Bumble, I face one great disadvantage: all the stout people die the fastest. Those who have been better off and paid rates for years are the first to sink when they come into the house. And let me tell you, Mr. Bumble, that three or four inches over one\u2019s estimate cuts deeply into one\u2019s profits, especially when one has a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nAs Mr. Sowerberry spoke with the rightful anger of a man ill-used, and as Mr. Bumble felt it hinted at the parish\u2019s honor, the latter thought it wise to change the subject. With Oliver Twist on his mind, he turned to him.\\n\\n\u201cBy the bye,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cyou don\u2019t know anyone who wants a boy, do you? A parish apprentice who\u2019s currently a dead weight, a millstone, so to speak, around the parish\u2019s neck? Liberal terms, Mr. Sowerberry, liberal terms?\u201d As he spoke, Mr. Bumble lifted his cane and tapped three times on the words \u201cfive pounds,\u201d printed above him in huge Roman letters.\\n\\n\u201cGadso!\u201d exclaimed the undertaker, taking Mr. Bumble by the gilt-edged lapel of his official coat. \u201cThat\u2019s exactly what I wanted to discuss with you. You know\u2014dear me, what an extremely fine button this is, Mr. Bumble! I never noticed it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I think it rather attractive,\u201d said the beadle, looking proudly at the large brass buttons on his coat. \u201cThe design is the same as the parish seal\u2014the Good Samaritan healing the injured man. The board gave it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I remember wearing it for the first time when I attended the inquest on that ruined tradesman who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
