          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"34be6060f67164d00b3b4cd1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I command you, spirits of the departed, and you, wandering agents of vengeance, to strengthen and guide me in my task. Let the accursed, infernal creature quaff the bitterness of agony; let him endure the despair that now preys on me.\u201d\\n\\nI began this curse with such solemn reverence that it seemed my slain companions heard and approved my dedication, yet by the end the furies mastered me, and rage strangled my speech.\\n\\nOut of the night\u2019s stillness came an answering peal\u2014a loud, demonic laugh. It lingered upon my ears, the mountains gave it back, and I felt as if all hell enclosed me with scorn and mirth. In that instant I should have yielded to madness and ended my wretched life, had not my vow been acknowledged and my fate preserved for revenge. The laughter faded, and then a voice I knew and loathed, seeming close beside me, murmured distinctly, \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have resolved to live, and I am content.\u201d\\n\\nI sprang toward the spot from which the sound issued, but the fiend slipped from my grasp. Suddenly the full orb of the moon rose and revealed his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI pursued him, and for many months this has consumed me. With the faintest clue, I traced the windings of the Rhone in vain. The blue Mediterranean spread before me, and by a strange turn I saw the demon board by night and conceal himself in a vessel bound for the Black Sea. I secured passage on the same ship, yet he escaped me\u2014I know not how.\\n\\nThrough the wilderness of Tartary and Russia, though he eluded capture, I followed his trail. Sometimes peasants, terrified by his abhorrent presence, indicated his route; sometimes he himself, fearing that I might lose him, despair, and die, left markers for my pursuit. Snow settled upon my head, and I saw the impression of his enormous stride across the white expanse. You who are just entering life, unfamiliar with care and untouched by anguish, how can you imagine what I have endured and still endure? Cold, hunger, and weariness were the lightest of my torments; a demon\u2019s curse clung to me, and I bore my private inferno wherever I wandered; yet some benevolent spirit also accompanied and steered my course, and when I complained most bitterly, it would suddenly free me from seemingly insuperable perils. At times, when the body, overcome by starvation, faltered from exhaustion, a meal awaited me in the desolation that revived and emboldened me.\"}"
