          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"baa118783689a67fb431caaa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events unfolded in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old lair\u2014the very one from which the girl had whisked Oliver away\u2014musing over a dim, smoky fire. He balanced a pair of bellows on his knee, with which he had apparently tried to coax the coals into livelier blaze; yet he had sunk into profound thought, and, folding his arms atop it with his chin resting on his thumbs, stared absently at the corroded bars.\\n\\nBehind him, the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling sat at a table, wholly absorbed in a whist match; the Dodger taking dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The face of the first-named gentleman, always keen, assumed fresh interest through his close watch of the cards and his careful reading of Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand; upon which, whenever opportunity arose, he cast a succession of intent glances, prudently directing his own play by what he perceived in his neighbor\u2019s cards. As the night was cold, the Dodger kept his hat on, as he often did indoors. He also gripped a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he felt compelled to reach for the quart pot on the table, standing ready with gin-and-water for the party\u2019s refreshment.\\n\\nMaster Bates likewise paid heed to the game; yet, being more high-strung than his accomplished associate, he resorted more often to the gin-and-water, and additionally indulged in numerous jokes and off-hand remarks, all sorely unfit for a serious rubber. Indeed, the Artful, relying on their close friendship, more than once seized the chance to lecture his companion soberly on such improprieties; every admonition Master Bates accepted with excellent temper, merely begging his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to thrust his head in a sack, or replying with some other neatly crafted quip of the same order, whose apt delivery stirred great admiration in Mr. Chitling. It was noteworthy that the latter gentleman and his partner unfailingly lost; and that this fact, far from irritating Master Bates, seemed to give him the highest delight, for he roared with laughter at the end of each hand and declared he had never witnessed so merry a game in all his born days.\\n\\n\u201cThat makes two doubles and the rubber,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, with a face miles long, as he pulled half-a-crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never met such a chap as you, Jack; you scoop it all.\u201d\"}"
