          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aabf7fc4c78af05dbdeedbc9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was early February, and after a month in Bath, Anne grew very eager for word from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted much more than Mary had told her. Three weeks had passed since any news had come. All she knew was that Henrietta was back home, and that Louisa, though said to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. She was thinking of them all one evening when a thicker-than-usual letter from Mary arrived, delivered with Admiral and Mrs. Croft\u2019s compliments, making the surprise and pleasure even greater. The Crofts must be in Bath! That interested her; her heart naturally turned toward them. \u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have arrived in Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d \u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d \u201cOh! Those letters serve nicely as passports. They guarantee an introduction. I would have called on Admiral Croft anyway. I know what is due to my tenant.\u201d Anne could listen no longer; she could not have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion survived. The letter absorbed her, and she saw it had been started several days earlier. \u201cFebruary 1st. \u201cMY DEAR ANNE, I make no apology for being silent, because I know how little people care about letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to mind Uppercross, which, as you know, offers little to write about. We had a very dull Christmas; Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove did not give a single dinner party during the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anyone. The holidays are over at last: I believe no children ever had such long ones. I certainly did not. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear they have never gone home. Mrs. Harville must be an odd mother to part with them for so long. I do not understand it. They are not pleasant children, in my opinion; but Mrs. Musgrove seems to like them as much as, if not more than, her own grandchildren. What dreadful weather we have had! Perhaps it is not noticed in Bath, with your neat pavements, but in the country it matters. No one has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, and he has been calling far more often than welcome. Between ourselves, I think it is a great pity Henrietta did not stay in Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little out of his way.\u201d\"}"
