          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9495a199965cb07da226bb9b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last we lightened the chest by taking out two thirds of what it held, and then, though it cost us effort, we managed to hoist it from the pit. The items removed were stacked among the brambles, and the dog was stationed there to guard them, with Jupiter\u2019s strict instructions neither to move an inch nor to utter a sound until we came back. We hurried home with the chest and reached the hut safely, though thoroughly exhausted, at one in the morning. Utterly spent, we could do no more for the moment. We rested until two, ate supper, and immediately set out again for the hills, carrying three sturdy sacks we were fortunate enough to have on hand. Shortly before four we reached the pit once more, divided the rest of the spoil as evenly as possible, left the holes unfilled, and again trudged back to the hut, where we deposited our golden burdens a second time just as the faintest streaks of dawn brightened the eastern treetops.\\n\\nWe were now completely worn out, yet the fever of excitement would not let us sleep. After a fitful doze of three or four hours, we rose almost as if we had arranged it beforehand, eager to inspect our treasure.\\n\\nThe chest had been filled to the lid, and we devoted the entire day and most of the following night to examining what lay inside. Nothing resembled order or method; everything had been flung in pell-mell. Once we had carefully sorted the lot, we realized we owned even more riches than we had first imagined. In coin there was slightly more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars\u2014so far as we could judge by the tables of the day. Not a scrap of silver appeared. It was all gold of ancient minting, in many varieties\u2014French, Spanish, German pieces, a handful of English guineas, and several counters whose like we had never seen. Some coins were extremely large and heavy, their inscriptions so worn we could not decipher them. None were American. Estimating the jewels proved harder. Among them were diamonds\u2014a hundred and ten altogether, all large and exceptionally fine; eighteen rubies of striking brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, each of them beautiful; twenty-one sapphires, and a single opal. Every stone had been torn from its setting and tossed loose into the chest. The settings themselves\u2014fished out from the rest of the gold\u2014looked as though they had been hammered flat, seemingly to make identification impossible.\"}"
