          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e68149af025049f096b70fc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to consider more calmly what had happened and what I ought to do. Only a few hours had passed, yet it felt as though years of change had occurred since morning. My father was as good as dead to me, and for a moment I imagined his white head laid in a coffin while I, youth fading into age, wept for his timely death. But it was not so. I was still young, far too young, and he was not dead to anyone else. Yet I, most miserable, must never see or speak to him again. I had to flee from him more earnestly than from my worst enemy; whether in solitude or among crowds, I must never look on him again. That thought left me breathless with anguish, and when it fixed itself in my mind I could not follow any further train of thought. From then on, I decided, I would live in the bleakest seclusion. I would go to the Continent and become a nun\u2014not for religion, since I was no Catholic, but so I could be shut out from the world forever. There I would find solitude for my tears, and the sounds of life would never reach me.\\n\\nBut my father\u2014my beloved, miserable father? Would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Many years from now, when age had cooled the burning feelings he now endured, might he not become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my forehead, and I felt (and wept to feel) a half-melancholy smile soften the pain on my lips. I dared to allow kinder hopes for my future: years must pass, but they would move swiftly, carried by hope\u2014or if they moved slowly, still they would pass, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years as a lonely wanderer; let him again pour out his wild complaints to the vast forests and roaring waterfalls of another land; let him once more meet dreadful dangers and spirit-crushing hardships; let the southern sun scorch his passion-worn cheeks and the cold night rains fall on him and chill his blood.\\n\\nTo this life, miserable father, I devote you! Go! Let your days be spent with savages and your nights under the open sky! Let your limbs grow weary, your heart grow cold, and your youth vanish entirely within you!\"}"
