          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"72489608ecb2a3490e5f3ebf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He slipped silently out of the wainscoting, an evil smile twisting his cruel, wrinkled mouth, and the moon hid behind a cloud as he crept past the great oriel window where his own arms and those of his murdered wife were blazoned in azure and gold. On and on he glided like a malevolent shadow, even the darkness seeming to recoil as he went. Once he thought he heard someone call and stopped, but it was only the baying of a dog from the Red Farm, so he moved on, muttering strange sixteenth-century curses and now and then brandishing the rusty dagger in the midnight air. At last he reached the corner of the passage leading to unlucky Washington\u2019s room. For a moment he paused there, the wind whipping his long gray locks around his head and twisting into grotesque folds the nameless horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud. Then the clock struck the quarter hour, and he knew the moment had come. He chuckled to himself and rounded the corner\u2014but no sooner had he turned than, with a pitiful wail of terror, he stumbled back and buried his blanched face in his long, bony hands. Right in front of him stood a ghastly apparition, motionless as a carved statue and monstrous as a madman\u2019s dream. Its head was bald and gleaming, its face round and fat and white, and hideous laughter seemed to have twisted its features into an eternal grin. From its eyes streamed rays of scarlet light, its mouth was a wide pit of fire, and a ghastly robe, like his own, sheathed in silent whiteness that titanic form. On its chest hung a placard with strange writing in ancient characters\u2014some scroll of disgrace, it seemed, some record of wild sins, some dreadful ledger of crime\u2014and in its right hand it held aloft a falchion of gleaming steel.\\n\\nNever having seen another ghost before, he was naturally terrified, and after one more quick glance at the fearful phantom he fled back to his room, tripping over his long winding-sheet as he rushed down the corridor and finally dropping the rusty dagger into the Minister\u2019s jackboots, where the butler found it in the morning. Once in the privacy of his chamber, he flung himself onto a narrow pallet bed and hid his face under the covers. After a while, however, the brave old Canterville spirit reasserted itself, and he resolved to go speak to the other ghost as soon as daylight came. So, just as dawn was silvering the hills, he returned to the spot where he had first seen the grisly phantom, feeling that, after all, two ghosts were better than one and that, with the help of his new ally, he might finally deal with the twins.\"}"
