          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c8de1f99fc9ad38f1987d718\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet my father remained beside me, consoling and chasing away the bitter recollections with radiant hopes: it seemed a tender pleasure to weep merely that he might brush away my tears.\\n\\nAgain he diverted my mourning by setting it beside the despair he had felt when my mother died. Even then I quailed at the portrait he sketched of his own passions: endowed with a poet\u2019s fancy, he so animated his description of the tempest that had rent his feelings that his speech became living truth, and I believed while I shivered. I wondered how he could ever have resumed the ordinary duties of existence after thoughts so wild had allied him with something unearthly; while he spoke, the conceptions he communicated were so tremendous that the human heart seemed too narrow to contain them. His emotions appeared more fitting for a spirit whose dwelling is the earthquake and the volcano than for one bound to a mortal frame and human features. Yet these belonged to the past; he was altered now. He had become all affection and gentleness; and when I lifted my eyes in astonishment as he spoke, the smile on his lips showed me his heart was filled with the mildest feelings.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death we moved to London, where my father led me to engage in deeper studies than I had ever pursued. My progress was his joy; he remained with me through every lesson, assisting or sharing in each. We mingled much with society, and there was no day that he did not try to adorn with some fresh enjoyment. The tender devotion he bore me, together with the love and reverence I returned, cast an enchantment over every instant. The hours seemed slow because each minute was occupied; we lived more in a single week than many do in several months, and the variety and novelty of our pleasures added flavour to each.\\n\\nWe were forever taking excursions together. Whether it was to behold lovely landscapes, to view fine paintings, or simply to wander without plan in search of whatever diversion might arise, I was always content near my father. It grieved me whenever a third person joined us; yet if I turned toward him with a troubled expression, his gaze, fixed on me and shining with tenderness, instantly restored joy to my heart. O hours of fervent delight! Brief as ye were, ye stretch to the length of an entire life when I look back through the mist of sorrow that rose immediately afterward as though to veil you from my sight.\"}"
