{"request_id":"2c04fcf8dc6cf6cf21987edf","rewritten_text":"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth talked about going there again himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and though it was November, the weather was not bad. In short, Louisa, the most enthusiastic of the group, had decided they should go. Besides the pleasure of doing as she pleased, she felt principled in insisting on her own way, and she overruled her parents’ wishes to postpone the trip until summer. So it was settled that Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth would set off for Lyme.

Their first careless plan had been to go in the morning and return that night. Mr. Musgrove, however, refused for the sake of his horses; and once they thought it through, they realized a day in mid-November, after subtracting the seven hours needed for travel, would leave little time to explore a new place. They therefore decided to stay overnight and not be expected back until dinner the following day. This was felt to be a significant improvement, and although they gathered at the Great House for an unusually early breakfast and set off promptly, it was well past noon by the time the two carriages—Mr. Musgrove’s coach carrying the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle with Charles driving Captain Wentworth—came down the long hill into Lyme and entered the even steeper main street. It was obvious they would have barely enough time to look around before daylight and warmth were gone.

After securing rooms and ordering dinner at one of the inns, the next necessary step was to walk straight down to the sea. They had come too late in the year for any entertainment or social life that Lyme, as a resort, might offer. The assembly rooms were closed, nearly all the lodgers had left, and hardly any families remained except the permanent residents. With nothing particularly notable about the buildings themselves, the distinctive setting of the town—the main street almost tumbling into the water, the walk to the Cobb curving around the pleasant little bay that, in season, bustles with bathing machines and visitors; the Cobb itself, with its old marvels and new improvements, and the beautiful line of cliffs stretching east of the town—is what any newcomer’s eye is drawn to. And anyone who fails to see the charms of Lyme’s immediate surroundings must be a very unusual visitor indeed, for most are immediately tempted to know it better."}

