          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c9e75a889a91750f15f3f7cc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was greatly troubled by the marriage being delayed a second time, and Lady Julia, having already ordered what she meant to wear, used every means to persuade Sybil to abandon the engagement. Yet Sybil, devoted as she was to her mother, had committed her whole life to Lord Arthur, and nothing Lady Julia urged could shake her trust. As for Lord Arthur, it was several days before he recovered from the crushing setback, and his nerves went quite to pieces for a while. His excellent common sense, however, soon reasserted itself, and his sound, practical mind left him in no doubt as to his next step. Poison having proved a thorough failure, dynamite or some other explosive was clearly the proper course.\\n\\nHe therefore ran through the list of his acquaintances again, and, after careful deliberation, resolved to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of wide culture and erudition, was particularly fond of clocks and had amassed an extraordinary collection of timepieces, from the fifteenth century down to the modern day, and Lord Arthur felt this passion of the worthy Dean\u2019s provided an ideal opening for his plan. Where to obtain an explosive device was, of course, another matter entirely. The London Directory afforded him no guidance, and he considered it of little use to appeal to Scotland Yard, as they never appeared to know anything about the activities of the dynamitards until after a blast had occurred, and not much even then.\\n\\nAll at once he remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with very revolutionary leanings, whom he had encountered at Lady Windermere\u2019s during the winter. Count Rouvaloff was reputed to be writing a biography of Peter the Great, and to have come to England to examine the papers concerning the Tsar\u2019s stay in this country as a shipwright; but it was widely believed he was actually a Nihilist agent, and the Russian Embassy certainly did not regard his presence in London with favour. Lord Arthur felt he was precisely the person he needed, and one morning drove down to his Bloomsbury lodgings to ask for counsel and aid.\\n\\n\u2018So you have gone in for politics in earnest?\u2019 said Count Rouvaloff, when Lord Arthur had explained why he had come; but Lord Arthur, who loathed any sort of swagger, was bound to confess that he took no interest in social problems, and wanted an explosive machine solely for a strictly family affair in which no one but himself was involved.\"}"
