          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ed54c2d4b648b977ed6e727\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was of low stature yet strikingly upright, and his voice was the most melodious I had ever heard. He opened his discourse with a review of the history of chemistry and the advancements brought about by various learned men, uttering with fervent emphasis the names of the foremost discoverers. He next gave a brief survey of the science in its present condition and clarified many of its fundamental terms. After conducting a few introductory experiments, he closed by delivering a eulogy on modern chemistry, the expressions of which are unforgettable to me:\\n\\n\u201cThe old instructors of this discipline,\u201d he declared, \u201cvowed impossibilities and achieved nothing. The modern adepts promise scarcely anything; they are aware that metals cannot be changed into other metals and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands appear created only to plunge into dirt and whose eyes seem destined to peer through microscopes or into crucibles, have in truth wrought wonders. They pierce the hidden retreats of nature and reveal her operations in their secret haunts. They climb into the skies; they have worked out the circulation of the blood and the constitution of the air we inhale. They have gained fresh and nearly boundless powers; they can summon the thunderbolts of heaven, counterfeit the earthquake, and confront the unseen world with its own phantoms.\u201d\\n\\nSuch were the professor\u2019s utterances\u2014rather, such were the words of fate, spoken for my ruin. While he continued, I felt as though my soul contended with a tangible foe; each key that composed the mechanism of my being was pressed in turn; note after note resounded, and swiftly my mind filled with a single idea, a single notion, a single aim. So much has already been accomplished, cried the spirit of Frankenstein\u2014still more, immeasurably more, shall I accomplish; keeping to the footsteps that have been traced, I will blaze a new trail, probe uncharted forces, and unveil for mankind the most profound secrets of creation.\\n\\nNo sleep visited me that night. My inner self was in revolt and agitation; I sensed that order must spring from it, yet I lacked the strength to effect it. Gradually, after daybreak, slumber came. When I awoke, my thoughts of the previous night seemed like a dream. Only a determination remained\u2014to return to my former studies and to consecrate myself to a science for which I believed I had an innate aptitude. That same day I called upon M. Waldman. In private his demeanour was even gentler and more engaging than before the public, for the dignity that marked him during his lectures was in his own home replaced by the utmost friendliness and warmth.\"}"
