          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c53acb915eb0ad5fb3663273\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Ye spirits of the gale, accept me! ye forces of ruin, fling wide your arms and hold me fast for ever, unless some gentler power has already destined a different fate, so that after long endurance I may earn my recompense, and once more feel my heart beating beside that of another akin to myself.  \\n\\nTiber, nature\u2019s self-spread thoroughfare winding through her continent, lay at my feet, its banks lined with many moored boats. With a handful of books, stores of food, and my dog, I would step into one, drift with the current to the sea, and then, hugging the coast, glide along the lovely shores and glowing headlands of the blue Mediterranean, rounding Naples and Calabria, daring the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis; afterward, with undaunted aim\u2014for what had I left to lose?\u2014skim the ocean\u2019s surface toward Malta and the distant Cyclades. I would shun Constantinople, whose familiar domes and inlets belonged to a past life that no longer touched mine; I would skirt Asia Minor and Syria, and after passing the seven-mouthed Nile, turn north again until, leaving behind half-forgotten Carthage and forsaken Libya, I reached the Pillars of Hercules. Beyond that\u2014no matter where\u2014the slimy caverns and noiseless abysses of ocean might become my home, before I finished this drawn-out journey, or the dart of disease pierced my heart while I floated alone on the heaving Mediterranean; or, in some harbor I entered, I might discover what I craved\u2014a companion; or if not\u2014through unending years, bent with age and silver-haired\u2014youth already entombed with those I adored\u2014the solitary voyager would still hoist his sail and grip the tiller, and, still answering the call of heaven\u2019s winds, forever round yet another promontory, drop anchor in yet another bay, ceaselessly furrowing the seedless ocean, leaving the emerald homeland of Europe behind, down the tawny coastline of Africa, and, after braving the fierce seas of the Cape, perhaps moor my weathered skiff in some cove shaded by the spicy groves of the fragrant isles in the far Indian Ocean.  \\n\\nThese are wild imaginings. Yet, ever since they seized me a week ago, as I stood atop St. Peter\u2019s, they have dominated my fancy. I have chosen my boat and packed my meager supplies. I have set aside a few volumes; foremost among them are Homer and Shakespeare\u2014though the libraries of the earth lie open to me, and in any harbor I can replenish my shelf. I expect no change for the better; only the unvarying present has become unendurable.\"}"
