          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8794f9b4788410d034312907\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet he kept one secret from his dear friends, a secret he had guarded since childhood. Although he loved his fellow collegians, he would not trust their delicate judgment or sympathy. He was in love. He feared they would mock the intensity of his passion, and he could not bear to have them profane it by calling it a brief or trivial fancy when to him it was the very core of his life.\\n\\nA gentleman of modest means lived near his family estate and had three lovely daughters. The eldest was by far the most beautiful, yet her beauty only enhanced her other qualities: her mind was clear and strong, and her disposition angelically gentle. She and my father had been playmates since infancy. Diana had been a favorite of his mother even as a child; this partiality grew with the years of the lively and lovely girl, and thus my father spent every school and college vacation constantly in her company. Novels and other means by which young people in civilized society learn about passions before they truly feel them had a powerful effect on him, as he was unusually receptive to every impression. At eleven Diana was his favorite playmate, yet he already spoke the language of love. Although she was nearly two years older, her education made her more childish, at least in knowledge and expression of feeling. She met his fervent declarations with innocence and returned them without understanding their meaning. She had read no novels and kept company only with her younger sisters. What chance had she to know the difference between love and friendship? When her maturing mind finally revealed the true nature of their relationship, her affection was already given to her friend, and her only fear was that other charms or his own fickleness might make him break his childish vows.\\n\\nBut each day their attachment grew more ardent and tender. It was a passion that had grown with his growth, entwined with every faculty and feeling, and could only end with his life. No one knew of their love except themselves. Yet although he dreaded the disapproval of his companions, who might scorn him for loving someone beneath him in fortune, nothing ever shook his resolve to marry her as soon as he could find the courage to face the obstacles he was determined to conquer.\\n\\nDiana was fully worthy of his deepest affection.\"}"
