{"request_id":"481dd622e2bea578242e8642","rewritten_text":"During his brief stay this time, Emma had hardly seen him; just enough, however, to feel that the first meeting was behind her and to be struck by the mix of irritation and pretension now clinging to his manner. She was actually beginning to wonder how she had ever thought him agreeable at all; his very appearance was inseparably tied to some miserably unpleasant memories. Except as a moral exercise—a penance, a lesson, a source of useful humility for her own mind—she would gladly have been assured she need never see him again. She wished him well, but he caused her pain, and his well-being at a distance of twenty miles would have pleased her most.

The discomfort of his lingering in Highbury, however, must surely be eased by his marriage. Many foolish concerns would be prevented—many awkward moments avoided because of it. A Mrs. Elton would justify any change in the way they interacted; their former intimacy could fade quietly. It would be nearly like beginning their polite acquaintance all over again.

As for the lady herself, Emma thought very little of her individually. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, no doubt; accomplished enough for Highbury—handsome enough, though likely to look plain beside Harriet. Concerning her connections, Emma felt perfectly at ease; persuaded that after all his proud boasts and contempt for Harriet, he had accomplished nothing. On that point, the truth seemed reachable. What she was might remain uncertain; but who she was could be discovered. Setting aside the ten thousand pounds, she did not appear in any way superior to Harriet. She brought no notable name, no lineage, no alliances. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant—he must be called that; but since the profits of his business life appeared so very modest, it was reasonable to guess the dignity of his trade had been equally modest. She had been accustomed to spend part of each winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home—the very heart of Bristol—for though her parents had died years before, there remained an uncle “in the law line.” Nothing more specifically respectable was claimed for him beyond that phrase, and she had lived with him. Emma suspected he was the drudge of some attorney, too dense to advance. All the grandeur of the connection seemed to rest on the elder sister, who was very well married to a gentleman prospering near Bristol, one who kept two carriages. That was the conclusion of the story; that was Miss Hawkins’s distinction."}

