          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"984d484e4f746f2936a22c76\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you\u2019re calling Miss Havisham a man. And I doubt even you would go that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, up in town?\u201d Joe said.\\n\\n\u201cIs there a Miss Havisham down in town?\u201d my sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go play there. And of course he is going. And he had better go play there,\u201d my sister said, shaking her head at me as if urging me to be very light and playful, \u201cor I\u2019ll deal with him.\u201d\\n\\nI had heard of Miss Havisham in town\u2014everyone for miles around had heard of her\u2014as an immensely rich, severe woman who lived in a large, gloomy house locked up against thieves and who lived in seclusion.\\n\\n\u201cWell, imagine that!\u201d Joe said, astonished. \u201cI wonder how she came to know Pip!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIdiot!\u201d my sister cried. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Because some person,\u201d Joe politely suggested again, \u201cmentioned she wanted him to go there to play.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd couldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go there and play? Isn\u2019t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook is one of her tenants, and that he sometimes\u2014we won\u2019t say every quarter or half-year, because that would be asking too much of you\u2014but sometimes\u2014goes there to pay his rent? And couldn\u2019t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, being always considerate and thoughtful for us\u2014though you may not think so, Joseph,\u201d she added in the most reproachful tone, as if he were the most heartless of nephews, \u201cthen recommend this boy, standing prancing here\u201d\u2014which I solemnly swear I was not doing\u2014\u201cto whom I\u2019ve always been a willing slave?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d Uncle Pumblechook cried. \u201cWell said! Nicely put! Excellent! Now Joseph, you know the situation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d my sister continued reproachfully, while Joe apologized by rubbing the back of his hand across his nose over and over, \u201cyou do not yet\u2014though you may not realize it\u2014know the situation. You might think you do, but you do not, Joseph. Because you don\u2019t know that Uncle Pumblechook, sensible that for all we know this boy\u2019s fortune might be made by going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, keep him overnight, and take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lord have mercy on me!\u201d my sister cried, flinging off her bonnet in sudden despair, \u201chere I am talking to a pair of Mooncalves, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, the mare catching cold at the door, and the boy filthy with soot and dirt from the hair on his head to the soles of his feet!\u201d\"}"
