          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fcd7976aafca93c39172b2b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Attorney-General had to tell the jury that the prisoner before them, though young, had long been involved in treason, which now put his life at risk. This correspondence with the public enemy was not a matter of today, yesterday, last year, or even the year before. It was certain that for longer than that, the prisoner had been traveling back and forth between France and England on secret business he could not honestly explain. If treason ever prospered\u2014which fortunately it never did\u2014the true wickedness of his work might have stayed hidden. But Providence had moved a person beyond fear or reproach to discover the prisoner\u2019s schemes and, horrified, report them to his Majesty\u2019s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. This patriot would be presented to them. His position and bearing were, overall, sublime. He had once been the prisoner\u2019s friend, but when he discovered his infamy in a moment both fortunate and terrible, he resolved to sacrifice the traitor he could no longer hold close on the sacred altar of his country. If statues were awarded in Britain as in ancient Greece and Rome to public benefactors, this shining citizen would surely have one. Since they were not, he probably would not. Virtue\u2014so the poets had observed in many passages the jury surely had at their fingertips (though their guilty expressions revealed they knew nothing of them)\u2014was in some way contagious, especially the bright virtue called patriotism, or love of country. The noble example of this flawless and unassailable witness for the Crown, whom it was an honour even to mention, inspired the prisoner\u2019s servant. That example planted in him a holy resolve to search his master\u2019s desk drawers and pockets and hide his papers. Mr. Attorney-General expected to hear attempts to discredit this admirable servant, but as a rule he preferred him to his own brothers and sisters and honoured him more than his own father and mother. He confidently urged the jury to follow suit. The evidence of these two witnesses, along with the documents they would produce, would show that the prisoner had been supplied with lists of his Majesty\u2019s forces and their locations and readiness by sea and land, leaving no doubt that he had routinely passed such information to an enemy power.\"}"
