          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ac9762f5261dc1370eeaa362\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI won\u2019t be dining at home tonight, so I shall have no need of you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cVery good, sir,\u201d replied the servant, withdrawing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, we can\u2019t waste a second. This chest weighs a ton! Let me carry it. You gather the rest.\u201d His orders came fast, sharp; Campbell felt mastered by him. Together they left the room.\\n\\nUpon reaching the upper landing, Dorian drew out the key, turned it in the lock, then paused, a cloud of distress crossing his expression. A tremor ran through him. \u201cI don\u2019t know that I can go in, Alan,\u201d he whispered.\\n\\n\u201cThat is of no consequence to me. I have no need of you,\u201d Campbell answered icily.\\n\\nDorian pushed the door half open. Instantly he caught sight of his portrait\u2019s face grinning in the sunlight. The torn curtain lay across the floor before it. He suddenly remembered that, for the first time in his life, he had neglected the previous night to conceal the cursed canvas and was about to dash forward when a shiver drove him back.\\n\\nWhat was that disgusting crimson moisture beading, slick and gleaming, upon one of the painted hands, as though the canvas itself sweated blood? How awful it looked\u2014more awful, it struck him for the instant, than the mute object he knew lay stretched upon the table, the object whose grotesquely distorted shadow on the mottled carpet told him it had not shifted, but remained precisely where he had left it.\\n\\nDrawing a long breath, he forced the door wider and, eyes narrowed and head turned aside, stepped in swiftly, determined not to send even a single glance toward the corpse. Bending to snatch up the gold-and-purple drapery, he flung it completely over the picture.\\n\\nHe halted there, dreading to turn, his gaze fastening on the complex pattern before him. He heard Campbell hauling in the weighty chest, the irons, and the other apparatus demanded for his terrible task. A strange curiosity took him\u2014had he and Basil Hallward ever encountered each other, and if they had, what impressions had passed between them?\\n\\n\u201cLeave me,\u201d came the harsh voice behind him.\\n\\nHe wheeled and rushed out, just aware that the dead body had been shoved back into the chair and that Campbell stared into a slick yellow face. Descending the stairs, he caught the sound of the key turning again in the lock.\\n\\nIt was well after seven when Campbell returned to the library. His complexion was ashen, yet his composure perfect. \u201cI have done what you asked,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
