          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a66ddcf585daadd6eaf2bb9c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe would make such a charming companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse concurred in everything, but added, \u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith, for that matter, is another equally lovely young creature. You will like Harriet. Emma could not wish for a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am delighted to hear it\u2014but only Jane Fairfax is known to be so accomplished and superior!\u2014and precisely Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThis subject was talked over very cheerfully, and others of equal weight followed and faded away with the same concord; yet the evening did not end without a slight recurrence of agitation. The gruel arrived and offered abundant material for conversation\u2014copious commendations and observations\u2014decided assurances of its suitableness for every constitution, and some rather sharp invectives against the many households where it was rarely produced tolerably;\u2014but, unfortunately, among the failures the daughter had to cite, the latest, and therefore the most vivid, was her own cook at South End, a young woman temporarily engaged, who never could apprehend what she meant by a basin of fine smooth gruel, thin, but not excessively so. However often she had wished for it and sent orders, nothing decent had ever been achieved. It was a perilous entry.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and fixing his eyes on her with tender concern.\u2014The exclamation in Emma\u2019s ear conveyed, \u201cAh! there is no end to the melancholy consequences of your going to South End. It is quite unfit to dwell upon.\u201d And for a little time she hoped he would avoid speaking of it, that a silent pondering might restore his enjoyment of his own smooth gruel. After a pause of several minutes, though, he began:\\n\\n\u201cI shall always regret that you chose the sea this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why must you regret it, sir?\u2014I assure you, it benefited the children greatly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if the sea was necessary, it ought not to have been South End. South End is unhealthy. Perry was astonished you had selected South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people entertain that notion, but truly it is entirely a mistake, sir.\u2014We were all perfectly healthy there, never experienced the slightest inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield insists it is quite wrong to suppose the place unhealthy; and I am sure he is reliable, for he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if anywhere.\u2014Perry spent a week at Cromer once, and he considers it the finest of all the sea-bathing places.\u201d\"}"
