{"request_id":"7dd5b3b20685753b36a9ff92","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a mild breath soon coaxed it into flame. I noticed this as well and devised a fan of boughs, which stirred the embers whenever they threatened to go out. When night returned, I was pleased to discover that the blaze provided light in addition to warmth and that this new mastery over the element benefited my diet, for several scraps abandoned by the travellers had been roasted and tasted far more savoury than the berries I plucked from the branches. I endeavoured, therefore, to cook my food the same way, setting it upon the glowing coals. The berries were ruined by this process, yet the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, nevertheless, grew scarce, and I often spent entire days roaming without success for a few acorns to dull my hunger. Upon realizing this, I resolved to depart the place I had inhabited thus far and search for another where my few desires might be more readily supplied. In this departure I keenly regretted the loss of the fire I had gained by accident and could not reproduce. I devoted several hours to pondering that difficulty, but I was forced to abandon all efforts to recover it, and cloaking myself, I set off through the forest toward the west. For three days I wandered and finally reached the open plains. Heavy snow had fallen during the preceding night, coating the fields in a uniform whiteness; the sight was melancholy, and the cold, damp covering chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I yearned for food and refuge; at last I noticed a small hut atop a rise, doubtless erected for some shepherd’s use. The sight was entirely new to me, and I inspected its construction with deep curiosity. Discovering the door ajar, I stepped inside. An elderly man sat by a fire, preparing his breakfast. He turned at the sound, and seeing me, gave a piercing cry before fleeing the hut and racing across the fields with a swiftness his frail frame scarcely seemed capable of. His visage, unlike any I had ever encountered, and his swift escape somewhat astonished me. Yet I was delighted by the hut itself; here snow and rain could not intrude, the earth stayed dry, and it seemed as exquisite and divine a sanctuary to me as Pandæmonium to the demons of hell after their torment in the burning lake."}

