{"request_id":"37230891e9816d560ea2fb1b","rewritten_text":"The line of flickering gas-lamps curved far into the distance, and outside a small walled house stood a lone hansom cab, its driver asleep inside. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing back now and then as if afraid someone followed him. At the corner of Rich Street two men were reading a small notice on a hoarding. A strange curiosity seized him, and he crossed the street. As he drew close, the word “Murder,” printed in bold black letters, caught his eye. He flinched, and a deep flush rose to his cheek. The notice offered a reward for any information that might lead to the arrest of a man of medium height, somewhere between thirty and forty, wearing a billycock hat, a black coat, and check trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering if the unfortunate man would be captured, and how he had received that scar. Perhaps, one day, his own name might be posted on London’s walls. Perhaps a price would be put on his head, too.

The thought filled him with horror. He turned sharply and hurried off into the night.

He barely knew where he went. He had a vague memory of wandering through a maze of shabby houses, of losing himself in a vast web of dark streets, and it was full daylight when he finally reached Piccadilly Circus. As he strolled home toward Belgrave Square, he passed the heavy wagons bound for Covent Garden. The carters, in their white smocks, with sunburned faces and coarse curly hair, marched sturdily along, cracking their whips, calling to each other now and then. On the back of a huge gray horse, the leader of a clanging team, sat a chubby boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat, clutching the mane with his small hands and laughing. The towering heaps of vegetables looked like masses of green jade against the morning sky, like green jade set against the rosy glow of some marvellous flower. Lord Arthur felt a curious emotion and could not explain it. There was something in the dawn’s fragile beauty that seemed unbearably sad, and he thought of the days that begin in loveliness and end in storms. These countrymen, too, with their rough cheerful voices and their casual ways—what a strange London they witnessed! A London without the sin of night or the smoke of day, a pallid, ghostly city, a deserted town of tombs. He wondered what they made of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendour and its shame, of its fierce, flaming pleasures and its terrible hunger, of all it creates and ruins between sunrise and nightfall."}

