          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"209440b93e16765ce541db94\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologized. He replied, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, and you may guess she was watched as a great prize. Her half-brother had plenty of money again, but between debts and renewed wildness he squandered it terribly once more. He and she clashed even more fiercely than he had with their father, and it is suspected he nursed a deep grudge against her for provoking their father\u2019s anger. Now comes the cruel part of the story\u2014only stopping, my dear Handel, to point out that a dinner napkin does not fit in a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was trying to cram mine into my tumbler, I cannot explain. I only know I was, with a determination worthy of a better cause, working hard to squeeze it in. Again I thanked him and apologized, and again he cheerfully replied, \u201cNot at all, I\u2019m sure!\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014say at the races, or the public balls, or wherever you please\u2014a certain man who courted Miss Havisham. I never saw him (for this was five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but my father often mentioned that he was a flashy man, suited to his purpose. Yet my father insists he could never be mistaken for a gentleman, except by ignorance or prejudice, because he holds that no man lacking a true gentleman\u2019s heart was ever, since the world began, a true gentleman in manner. He says no varnish can hide the grain of the wood, and the more varnish you apply, the more the grain shows. Well! This man chased Miss Havisham closely and claimed to adore her. I believe she had not shown much tenderness before then; but whatever capacity she had broke out, and she loved him passionately. There is no doubt she worshiped him. He played upon her affection so methodically that he drew large sums of money from her, and persuaded her to buy her brother\u2019s share in the brewery (weakly left to him by their father) at a huge price, arguing that when he became her husband he had to hold and run the whole concern. Your guardian was not in Miss Havisham\u2019s confidence then, and she was too proud and too infatuated to take advice from anyone.\u201d\"}"
        }
