          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8690d07d304df90e0a5a3628\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After a while we\u2019d wake up and figure out what roused us, maybe spotting a steamboat chugging upstream so far away on the other side we couldn\u2019t tell much about her except whether she was a stern-wheeler or a side-wheeler. Then for about an hour there\u2019d be nothing to hear and nothing to see\u2014just steady loneliness. Next you\u2019d notice a raft slipping by way off yonder, maybe with some fellow chopping, because they\u2019re almost always chopping on a raft; you\u2019d see the axe flash and swing down\u2014you don\u2019t hear a thing; you see that axe rise again, and by the time it\u2019s over the man\u2019s head you finally hear the k\u2019chunk\u2014it took all that time to travel over the water. So we spent the day lazing around, listening to the silence. Once the fog got so thick the rafts and other craft going past were banging tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run them down. A scow or a raft came so close we could hear the men talking and cussing and laughing\u2014clear as anything\u2014but we couldn\u2019t see a sign of them; it gave you the creeps; it was like spirits carrying on in midair. Jim said he figured it was spirits, but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Darn the darn fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as it got dark we shoved off; when we reached the middle we left the raft alone and let the current take her wherever it pleased; then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all sorts of things\u2014we were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it\u2014the new clothes Buck\u2019s folks made for me were too fine to feel comfortable, and besides I never cared much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we had that whole river to ourselves for the longest stretch. Over yonder were the banks and islands across the water, and maybe a spark or two\u2014which meant a candle in a cabin window; and sometimes you\u2019d see a spark or two on the water\u2014on a raft or a scow, you know; and maybe you\u2019d catch a fiddle tune or a song drifting over from one of those craft. Living on a raft is wonderful. We had the sky above us, sprinkled with stars, and we\u2019d lie on our backs and stare up at them and argue whether they were made or just happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they simply happened; I figured it would take too long to make that many.\"}"
