          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2bc89fb7e7260cf687ecd66e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thanks for the information. I like to know everything about new friends, and nothing about the old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray. He\u2019s her newest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha to stop pestering me with charity requests. I\u2019m fed up. The good woman thinks I have nothing better to do than write cheques for her silly whims.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFine, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, but it will do no good. Philanthropic people lose all sense of real kindness. That\u2019s how you spot them.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman growled in approval and rang for his servant. Lord Henry walked through the low arcade onto Burlington Street and headed toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s family. Even told clumsily, it had stirred him with its hint of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a mad passion. A few wild weeks of happiness cut off by a hideous, treacherous crime. Months of silent agony, and then a child born in suffering. The mother taken by death, the boy left alone under the rule of an old, loveless man. Yes, it made an interesting background. It framed the lad, made him seem more perfect. Behind every exquisite thing lurked tragedy. Worlds had to labor so that even the humblest flower could bloom....\\n\\nAnd how charming he had been at dinner the night before, with wide, startled eyes and lips parted in frightened pleasure as he sat across from him at the club, the red candle shades tinting the awakening wonder of his face a deeper rose. Talking to him was like playing a fine violin. He reacted to every touch of the bow.... There was something dangerously absorbing in wielding influence. Nothing else was like it. To project one\u2019s soul into some graceful form and let it linger there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own ideas echoed back with the added music of passion and youth; to pour one\u2019s temperament into another as if it were a subtle liquid or strange perfume\u2014there was real joy in that, perhaps the most satisfying joy left in an age as narrow and vulgar as theirs, gross in its pleasures and common in its goals....\\n\\nHe was a marvellous type, this lad whom he had met by chance in Basil\u2019s studio, or could be shaped into one, at least.\"}"
