          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a7ce2452ec62161ecbb0108e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t rush him all at once and risk botching it halfway; no, I move off to one side and work my way toward him gradually so he never suspects I\u2019m going to hit him at all. Then, in a flash, he\u2019s flat on his back and hasn\u2019t the faintest idea how it happened. That\u2019s how I went after Brother Dowley. I started talking slow and easy, as if I were just passing time; the oldest man alive couldn\u2019t have figured out where I was starting from and guessed where I\u2019d end up. \u201cBoys, there are a lot of curious things about law, and custom, and usage, and all that, if you take a close look, and about the drift and progress of human opinion and movement, too. There are written laws\u2014they die; but there are unwritten laws\u2014those are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says they have to rise, little by little, straight through the centuries. And look how it works. We know what wages are now, here and there and all around; we take an average and call that today\u2019s wage. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and two hundred; that\u2019s as far back as we can reach, but it\u2019s enough to tell us the law of progress, the measure and rate of periodic increases. So without any documents to help us, we can come very close to figuring out what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Good so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking back; we turn around and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be at any future date you want to name, for hundreds of years.\u201d \u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d \u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will have risen to six times what they are now in your region, with farmhands earning three cents a day and mechanics six.\u201d \u201cI wish I could die now and live then!\u201d cried Smug the wheelwright, his eyes glowing with greed. \u201cAnd that\u2019s not all; they\u2019ll get their board besides\u2014such as it is, it won\u2019t make them swell. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014listen now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wages will be\u2014remember, this is law, not guesswork\u2014twenty cents a day!\u201d There was a collective gasp of awed amazement. Dickon the mason whispered, eyes and hands raised:\"}"
