          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3f9aaed5d5bc475228ad33b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short but impressively upright, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He opened his lecture with a recap of chemistry\u2019s history and the many improvements made by different learned men, passionately sounding the names of its most distinguished discoverers. He then gave a brief overview of the current state of the science and clarified several of its basic terms. After performing a few introductory experiments, he finished with praise for modern chemistry, in words I will never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe old teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised the impossible and achieved nothing. The scientists of today promise very little; they know metals cannot be transmuted and the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem destined only to dabble in dirt and whose eyes pore over microscopes or crucibles, have truly performed miracles. They penetrate nature\u2019s recesses and show how she works in her hidden places. They rise into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and almost limitless powers; they can command heaven\u2019s thunder, imitate the earthquake, and even tease the invisible world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nSuch were the professor\u2019s words\u2014or rather, let me say, the words of fate\u2014pronounced to destroy me. As he spoke I felt as if my soul was struggling with a tangible enemy; one by one the different keys of my being were struck, chord after chord resonated, and soon my mind held only one thought, one idea, one goal. So much has been done, cried the soul of Frankenstein\u2014more, far more, will I accomplish; following the paths already laid down, I will break new ground, discover unknown forces, and reveal to the world the deepest secrets of creation.\\n\\nI did not close my eyes that night. My inner self was in rebellion and turmoil; I sensed order would arise from it, yet I could not bring it about. Little by little, after dawn, sleep came. When I awoke, my thoughts of the previous night seemed like a dream. All that remained was a resolve to return to my former studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural gift. That same day I visited M. Waldman. In private his manners were even more gentle and appealing than in public, for the certain dignity he showed during his lecture was replaced in his own home by the greatest friendliness and kindness.\"}"
