          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"595904e5aa61efbb05926aa4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. The perfect scene would be all of us arriving on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014with my dear husband walking beside us. I really must talk to him about buying one. In country life a donkey seems almost essential; no matter how many diversions a woman has, she cannot stay shut up indoors forever, and long walks are impractical\u2014dust in summer, mud in winter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will not meet either between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and right now it is perfectly dry. Still, come on a donkey if you like. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I want everything arranged exactly to your taste.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I am sure you do. Indeed, I give you full credit, my good friend. Beneath that dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are a real humorist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I am very aware of your attentiveness to me in this whole plan. You have found exactly what will please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to convince Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the outing, and he knew that any of them sitting outside to eat would certainly make Mr. Woodhouse ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured under the harmless excuse of a morning drive and a few hours at Donwell only to suffer for it afterward.\\n\\nHe was invited in good faith. No hidden dangers would reproach him for his easy trust. He agreed to go. He had not visited Donwell for two years. \u201cSome fine morning he, Emma, and Harriet could go quite well; he could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked through the gardens. He did not think they could be damp at midday. He would greatly enjoy seeing the old house again and would be very glad to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton and any other neighbors. He saw no objection at all to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there on some fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014far better than dining out. He did not care for dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was lucky that everyone accepted at once. The invitation was so well received that it seemed, like Mrs. Elton, they each took the plan as a personal compliment. Emma and Harriet declared they expected great pleasure from it, and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to bring Frank over if possible\u2014proof of appreciation and gratitude that could have been spared. Mr. Knightley then had to say he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston promised to write immediately and urge Frank to come with every argument he could muster.\"}"
