{"request_id":"4d802333e56bd028190be117","rewritten_text":"Don’t misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. Even when wild thoughts seemed to push me toward insanity, I always knew my own mind, and I betrayed them only to silence and solitude. Those around me saw none of this. They saw only a broken-spirited girl who spoke softly and in a low voice, whose eyes were cast down and sometimes let slip tears she tried to hide. A girl who loved being alone, who shrank from notice, who never smiled—oh no, I never smiled—and that was the whole of it.

Still, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house and disappeared; from the letters I left behind and other hints, it was believed that I had taken my own life. I was searched for with less urgency than might have been, and soon all trace and memory of me vanished. I left London on a small vessel bound for a northern English port. Having succeeded in my plan and finding myself alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm and the ship moved on gently. I sat on deck under the open sky, believing myself changed: no longer the wild, raving, wretched Mathilda but a young Hermitess devoted to solitude, who must keep her heart free from upheaval and godless despair. My fanciful, nun-like clothes; the knowledge that my very existence was known only to me; and the solitude that would define all my days ahead fostered gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze playing through my hair revived me, and I quietly watched the sunbeams glitter on the waves and the birds chasing one another over the water, barely touching it with their wings. I slept without dreams and woke refreshed, ready to enjoy my calm freedom again.

In four days we reached the harbor we were bound for. I refused to stay on the coast and immediately headed inland. I had already chosen the kind of place where I would live: a solitary house on a wide plain, far from any other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander far without suffering the gaze of other people. I was not misanthropic, but the gentle flow of my feelings depended on being alone. I settled in a vast solitude, on a dreary heath scattered with stones, patched with short grass, and here and there a clutch of rushes beside a small pool."}

