          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aaa07b0d37a87d2d0698bf0a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day the ghost was exhausted and frail. The awful excitement of the past four weeks was finally taking its toll. His nerves were completely shot, and he jumped at the slightest sound. For five days he stayed in his room, and eventually decided to give up on the bloodstain on the library floor. If the Otis family didn\u2019t want it, they clearly didn\u2019t deserve it. They obviously lived on a dull, material level of existence and were incapable of appreciating the symbolic value of sensuous phenomena. The issue of ghostly apparitions and the growth of astral bodies, however, was quite different and really outside his control. It was his solemn duty to appear in the corridor once a week, and to gibber from the large oriel window on the first and third Wednesday of each month, and he didn\u2019t see how he could honorably get out of those duties. It was quite true that he had lived a wicked life, but on the other hand he was very conscientious in everything connected to the supernatural. For the next three Saturdays, therefore, he walked the corridor as usual between midnight and three in the morning, taking every possible precaution not to be heard or seen. He removed his boots, stepped as lightly as he could on the old worm-eaten boards, wore a large black velvet cloak, and made sure to use the Rising Sun Lubricator to oil his chains. I must admit it took him considerable effort to bring himself to adopt this last protective measure. Still, one night while the family was at dinner, he slipped into Mr. Otis\u2019s bedroom and took the bottle. At first he felt a bit humiliated, but afterward he was sensible enough to realize that the invention had a lot to recommend it, and to some extent it served his purpose. Even so, he was not left in peace. Wires were constantly being stretched across the corridor, tripping him in the dark, and on one occasion, while dressed as \u201cBlack Isaac, or the Huntsman of Hogley Woods,\u201d he suffered a serious fall after stepping on a butter-slide the twins had built from the entrance of the Tapestry Chamber to the top of the oak staircase. This final insult infuriated him so much that he resolved to make one last attempt to assert his dignity and social standing, and decided to visit the insolent young Etonians the next night in his famous role of \u201cReckless Rupert, or the Headless Earl.\u201d He had not appeared in that disguise for more than seventy years, in fact not since he had so terrified pretty Lady Barbara Modish with it that she abruptly broke off her engagement to the present Lord Canterville\u2019s grandfather and ran away to Gretna Green with handsome Jack Castleton, insisting that nothing on earth would make her marry into a family that allowed such a ghastly ghost to parade up and down the terrace at dusk.\"}"
