          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c4f5e3234e9fb142f50794fb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou won\u2019t, won\u2019t you? Oh, you precious fool!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s being naughty, isn\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d asked Charley Bates. \u201cHe\u2019ll end up scragged, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know what that means,\u201d Oliver replied.\\n\\n\u201cSomething like this, old fellow,\u201d said Charley. He grabbed the end of his neckerchief, held it up, dropped his head to his shoulder, and made a strange noise through his teeth to show that scragging and hanging were the same thing.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s what it means,\u201d Charley said. \u201cLook how he stares, Jack! I never saw such fun as this boy; he\u2019ll be the death of me, I swear.\u201d Master Charley Bates laughed hard again and returned to his pipe with tears in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve been brought up badly,\u201d said the Dodger, admiring his boots after Oliver polished them. \u201cFagin will make something of you, though, or you\u2019ll be the first he ever had who turned out useless. You\u2019d better start right away; you\u2019ll take to the trade before you expect it, and you\u2019re just wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates backed this up with various moral lectures of his own; and when those ran out, he and his friend Mr. Dawkins launched into a glowing description of all the pleasures in their line of work, adding plenty of hints that Oliver\u2019s best move was to secure Fagin\u2019s favor quickly by doing what they themselves had done.\\n\\n\u201cAnd always keep this in mind, Nolly,\u201d said the Dodger, as the Jew\u2019s key turned in the door above, \u201cif you don\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking like that?\u201d Master Bates cut in. \u201cHe doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take handkerchiefs and watches,\u201d the Dodger explained in simpler terms, \u201csome other guy will; so the people who lose them will still be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off, too, and nobody will be a bit better except the chaps who grab them\u2014and you\u2019ve got just as much right to them as they do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course!\u201d said the Jew, who had come in without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt\u2019s all in a nutshell, my dear; a nutshell\u2014take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the catechism of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands with pleasure as he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s logic, and chuckled with delight at his student\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe conversation went no further then, because the Jew had returned home with Miss Betsy and a gentleman Oliver had never seen, whom the Dodger greeted as Tom Chitling. After Tom lingered on the stairs to exchange a few flirtatious words with the lady, he came in at last.\"}"
