          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3b872f80533f5b25a2a83f68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Why, we could probably spin a very dramatic superstition from this idea. I assume you\u2019ll call the bug scarabaeus caput hominis, or something along that line\u2014Natural History books are full of such names. But where are the antennae you mentioned?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe antennae!\u201d said Legrand, who seemed to be getting inexplicably heated about the matter. \u201cI\u2019m sure you must see the antennae. I made them as clear as they are on the actual insect, and I think that should be enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, all right,\u201d I said. \u201cMaybe you did\u2014still, I don\u2019t see them.\u201d I handed him the paper without further comment, not wanting to ruffle his temper, though I was amazed at how the situation had shifted. His bad mood confused me, and the drawing itself showed absolutely no antennae; in fact, the whole thing really did look a lot like the standard illustrations of a death\u2019s-head.\\n\\nHe took the paper with clear irritation and was about to crumple it up, apparently to toss it in the fire, when a casual glance at the design seemed to seize his attention. In an instant his face flushed a deep red, and in another it went ghostly pale. For a few minutes he stayed where he was, studying the drawing closely. Finally he got up, took a candle from the table, and sat on a sea chest in the far corner of the room. There he examined the paper again, anxiously, turning it this way and that. He said nothing, and his behavior astonished me; but I thought it wiser not to aggravate his growing moodiness with any remarks. Soon he took a wallet from his coat pocket, placed the paper carefully inside, and put both into a desk, which he locked. He seemed calmer now, but his earlier excitement had vanished. He didn\u2019t seem sulky so much as preoccupied. As the evening wore on he sank deeper into his reverie, and none of my attempts at conversation could break it. I had planned to spend the night in the hut, as I often did, but seeing my host in this state, I decided I ought to leave. He didn\u2019t urge me to stay, yet when I left he shook my hand with even more warmth than usual.\\n\\nAbout a month later (and during that time I hadn\u2019t seen Legrand once), I was visited in Charleston by his man, Jupiter. I\u2019d never seen the good old man look so downhearted, and I feared some serious misfortune had struck my friend.\"}"
