          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82796fb4b1c8677c435e7bc3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of consolation had ever been dispatched to Ireland. The omission had recoiled upon the head of the offender; when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no note of condolence arrived at Kellynch, thus giving full cause to believe the Dalrymples deemed the kinship at an end. How to mend this uneasy affair and be received once more as cousins was the problem, and it was not one which, in a judicious sense, either Lady Russell or Mr Elliot considered insignificant. \u201cFamily ties were always worth preserving; good society always worth courting; Lady Dalrymple had taken a house for three months in Laura Place, and would be living in proper style. She had been in Bath the preceding year, and Lady Russell had heard her praised as a delightful woman. It was highly desirable that the connection be renewed, if it might be achieved without any sacrifice of propriety on the Elliot side.\u201d Sir Walter, nevertheless, must choose his own method, and ultimately penned a most polished letter of ample explanation, regret, and appeal to his right honourable cousin. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot could admire this letter, but it secured everything necessary by drawing three scribbled lines from the Dowager Viscountess: \u201cShe felt greatly honoured, and should be happy in their acquaintance.\u201d The labour of the affair was ended; the pleasures began. They called in Laura Place, they displayed the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret wherever they would be most conspicuous; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place,\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret,\u201d were topics for everyone. Anne was mortified. Even had Lady Dalrymple and her daughter been exceedingly agreeable, she would have been mortified by the commotion they raised; but they were nothing. There was no distinction of manners, accomplishments, or intellect. Lady Dalrymple bore the title of \u201ca charming woman\u201d simply because she had a smile and civil reply for each person. Miss Carteret, with even less to contribute, was so plain and so ungainly that only her birth gained her admittance at Camden Place. Lady Russell admitted she had anticipated something better; yet \u201cthe acquaintance was worth maintaining,\u201d and when Anne dared voice her opinion of them to Mr Elliot, he agreed they amounted to nothing individually, but still insisted that, as relatives, as reputable society, as people who would draw other reputable society about them, they had their usefulness. Anne smiled and replied, \u201cMy notion of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of intelligent, well-informed people, who have plenty to talk about; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
