          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9216f76833f8bbff67c27f55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Biddy cried; the garden growing dark, the lane, and the coming stars all blurred before me. \u201cNothing was ever discovered, Biddy?\u201d \u201cNothing.\u201d \u201cDo you know what has become of Orlick?\u201d \u201cFrom his clothes, I would guess he works in the quarries.\u201d \u201cThen of course you have seen him? Why are you looking at that dark tree in the lane?\u201d \u201cI saw him there, the night she died.\u201d \u201cThat wasn\u2019t the last time either, Biddy?\u201d \u201cNo; I have seen him there while we have been walking. It\u2019s no use,\u201d Biddy said, laying her hand on my arm as I was about to run out. \u201cYou know I wouldn\u2019t deceive you. He wasn\u2019t there a minute, and now he\u2019s gone.\u201d My anger flared at the thought that he still followed her, and I felt a deep hatred for him. I told her so, and that I would spend whatever it took to drive him from the area. She gradually calmed me, and told me how Joe loved me, how he never complained\u2014she didn\u2019t have to mention of whom, I knew\u2014and always did his duty in his life, with a strong hand, quiet tongue, and gentle heart. \u201cIndeed, it would be hard to praise him too much,\u201d I said. \u201cAnd Biddy, we must speak of these things often, because I shall be down here often now. I won\u2019t leave poor Joe alone.\u201d Biddy said not a word. \u201cBiddy, don\u2019t you hear me?\u201d \u201cYes, Mr. Pip.\u201d \u201cBesides your calling me Mr. Pip, which I find poor manners, Biddy, what do you mean?\u201d \u201cWhat do I mean?\u201d Biddy asked timidly. \u201cBiddy,\u201d I said, putting on a virtuous tone, \u201cI must insist on knowing what you mean by this.\u201d \u201cBy this?\u201d said Biddy. \u201cDon\u2019t echo,\u201d I retorted. \u201cYou didn\u2019t use to echo, Biddy.\u201d \u201cDidn\u2019t use to!\u201d cried Biddy. \u201cOh, Mr. Pip! Didn\u2019t!\u201d Well! I thought I might give up that point as well. After another silent turn in the garden, I returned to the main subject. \u201cBiddy,\u201d I said, \u201cI mentioned coming down here often to see Joe, and you answered with a very pointed silence. Please, Biddy, tell me why.\u201d \u201cAre you quite sure that you will come to see him often?\u201d Biddy asked, stopping in the narrow garden path and looking at me with clear, honest eyes under the stars.\"}"
