{"request_id":"c7785e6dfeaf8270110da5ab","rewritten_text":"At last we lightened the box by removing two thirds of what was inside, and with some effort lifted it out of the hole. We stashed the items we pulled out among the brambles and left the dog to guard them, with Jupiter ordering him not to move or make a sound until we came back. We hurried home with the chest and reached the hut safely, though exhausted, at one in the morning. We were too worn out to do anything more right away. We rested until two, ate supper, and immediately set out for the hills again with three sturdy sacks we luckily had on hand. Just before four we arrived at the pit, divided the remaining loot as evenly as we could, and, leaving the holes unfilled, headed back to the hut, where for the second time we dropped off our golden loads just as the first pale streaks of dawn showed over the treetops in the east.

We were completely spent, yet the excitement kept us from real rest. After a fitful sleep of three or four hours, we got up as if we had agreed beforehand and began examining our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the top, and we spent the entire day and most of the next night examining its contents. Nothing was arranged; everything had been tossed in at random. After sorting everything carefully, we realized we were richer than we first thought. In coin there was a little over four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, as nearly as we could tell by the period’s tables. There wasn’t a speck of silver. All the coins were gold, old, and varied—French, Spanish, and German money, with a few English guineas and some counters we had never seen before. Several very large, heavy coins were so worn we couldn’t read their inscriptions. There was no American money. Estimating the value of the jewels was harder. We counted a hundred and ten diamonds—some extremely large and fine, none of them small; eighteen brilliantly colored rubies; three hundred and ten beautiful emeralds; and twenty-one sapphires, plus one opal. All the stones had been broken from their settings and tossed loose in the chest. The settings themselves, which we found among the other gold, seemed to have been smashed with hammers, apparently to prevent identification."}

