          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4a4ba7df0123f22e4532c430\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At one point, though, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far over the ice in such an unusual vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately fell into profound darkness, and he answered, \u201cTo find someone who ran away from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you were chasing travel the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we saw him, because the day before we picked you up we spotted some dogs pulling a sledge with a man in it across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked countless questions about the route the demon, as he called him, had taken. Soon after, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI\u2019ve undoubtedly stirred up your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you\u2019re too considerate to pry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; it would be very rude and inhumane of me to trouble you with my questions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and deadly situation; you kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon after this he asked whether I thought the breakup of the ice had destroyed the other sledge. I answered that I couldn\u2019t say for sure, because the ice didn\u2019t break until nearly midnight, and the traveler might have reached safety before then; but I couldn\u2019t judge.\\n\\nFrom then on a new vigor seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed the greatest urgency to be on deck to watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I\u2019ve convinced him to stay in the cabin, since he\u2019s far too weak to endure the harsh air. I\u2019ve promised that someone will keep watch for him and alert him immediately if anything new comes into view.\\n\\nSuch is my journal of this strange episode up to today. The stranger\u2019s health has slowly improved, but he is very quiet and seems uneasy when anyone but me enters his cabin. Yet his manners are so gracious and gentle that the sailors are all interested in him, even though they\u2019ve had little conversation with him. As for myself, I\u2019m beginning to love him like a brother, and his constant deep sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble soul in his better days, being so attractive and kind even now in ruin.\\n\\nI told you in one of my letters, dear Margaret, that I expected to find no friend on the vast ocean; yet I have found a man who, before suffering crushed his spirit, I would have been delighted to call the brother of my heart.\"}"
        }
