          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed64f8f2677d5a32724f9429\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He bowed and left. Saw no one in the street at that time. It is a side street\u2014very lonely.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam Bird, tailor, testifies that he was one of the group who entered the house. He is English. Has lived in Paris two years. Was among the first to go up the stairs. Heard the argument. The gruff voice belonged to a Frenchman. Managed to catch several words but cannot recall them all now. Clearly heard \u2018sacr\u00e9\u2019 and \u2018mon Dieu.\u2019 At that moment there was a noise like several people struggling\u2014a scraping and scuffling. The shrill voice was very loud\u2014louder than the gruff one. He is sure it was not an Englishman\u2019s voice. It seemed to be German. Might have been a woman\u2019s voice. Does not understand German.\\n\\n\u201cFour of the previously named witnesses, when recalled, testified that the door of the room where Mademoiselle L.\u2019s body was found was locked from inside when they reached it. Everything was completely silent\u2014no groans or any other sounds. When they forced the door, no one was inside. The windows, in both the front and back rooms, were shut and firmly fastened from within. A door between the two rooms was closed but not locked. The door leading from the front room into the hallway was locked, with the key inside. A small front room on the fourth floor at the head of the hallway was open, the door ajar. This room was packed with old beds, boxes, and similar items. These were carefully moved and searched. Not an inch of the house was left unchecked. Chimney sweeps were sent up and down the chimneys. The house had four stories with garrets (mansardes). A trapdoor on the roof was nailed down tightly\u2014seemed not to have been opened in years. The witnesses gave varying estimates of the time between hearing the argument and breaking open the door\u2014some as short as three minutes, some as long as five. The door was difficult to open.\\n\\n\u201cAlfonzo Garcio, undertaker, testifies that he lives on the Rue Morgue. He is Spanish. Was one of the party who entered the house. Did not go upstairs. Is nervous and feared the effects of agitation. Heard the argument. The gruff voice was that of a Frenchman. Could not make out the words. The shrill voice was that of an Englishman\u2014he is certain of it.\u201d\"}"
