          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b9621fa28612766e3549007b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so entirely satisfied with that conversation that he never again troubled himself, or annoyed his dear sister Elizabeth, by bringing it up, and she was pleased to see she had said enough to keep him quiet.\\n\\nThe day for his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived, and Mrs. Bennet had no choice but to accept the separation. Since her husband had no intention of following her scheme of the whole family going to Newcastle, the separation was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen will we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I have no idea. Not for two or three years, perhaps.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time for writing. My sisters can write to me. They\u2019ll have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s farewells were far more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said a great many pretty things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, as soon as they left the house, \u201cas ever I saw. He simpers and smirks and charms us all. I am exceedingly proud of him. I challenge even Sir William Lucas to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nLosing her daughter made Mrs. Bennet very low for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthat there is nothing so dreadful as parting from one\u2019s friends. One feels so abandoned without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the result, you see, madam, of marrying off a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt should make you more content that your other four remain single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s not the case at all. Lydia isn\u2019t leaving me because she is married; it is only because her husband\u2019s regiment is so far away. If it had been closer, she wouldn\u2019t have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nBut the listless state this event had thrown her into was soon eased, and her mind revived with new hope, by a piece of news then beginning to spread. The housekeeper at Netherfield had been ordered to prepare for her master, who was arriving in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet became wholly agitated. She looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head by turns.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for it was Mrs. Philips who brought her the news). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, of course. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never wish to see him again. However, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield if he likes.\u201d\"}"
