          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d7289305cad4017e0b4b0758\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the sight of him, the Ourang-Outang instantly bounded through the chamber door, darted down the staircase, and then, through an unfortunately open window, leaped into the street.\\n\\nThe Frenchman chased it in desperation; the ape, still clutching the razor, paused now and again to look back and make grim gestures at its pursuer, until the man nearly overtook it, when it darted off once more. Thus the pursuit lasted for a considerable time. The streets lay utterly silent, for it was near three in the morning. While slipping into an alley behind the Rue Morgue, the runaway\u2019s attention was caught by a light shining from the open window of Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s room on the fourth floor. Dashing to the house, it spotted a lightning rod, scaled it with astounding speed, gripped the window shutter, which was flung wide against the wall, and used it to swing itself straight onto the bed\u2019s headboard. The entire maneuver took less than a minute. As it entered, the Ourang-Outang kicked the shutter open once more.\\n\\nMeanwhile, the sailor felt both relief and dread. He hoped to recapture the creature now, for it could scarcely escape the trap it had entered except by the rod, where he might intercept it on the descent. Yet he was deeply uneasy about what havoc it might wreak inside. This anxiety drove him to keep up the chase. A lightning rod is not difficult to climb, especially for a seaman; but once he reached the height of the window, which was far to his left, he could go no further, managing only to lean over enough to gain a glimpse into the room. At that sight he almost lost his grip from sheer horror. Now burst forth those dreadful shrieks in the night that had roused the sleepers of the Rue Morgue. Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, dressed in their nightclothes, seemed to have been arranging some papers in the iron chest already mentioned, which had been rolled to the center of the chamber. It stood open, with its contents strewn on the floor beside it. The victims must have sat with their backs to the window, and from the interval between the creature\u2019s entrance and the screams, it appears they did not notice it at once. The clapping of the shutter would naturally have been taken for the wind.\\n\\nAs the sailor peered in, the massive brute had gripped Madame L\u2019Espanaye by the hair (which hung loose from recent combing) and was brandishing the razor about her face, mimicking a barber\u2019s motions.\"}"
