          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6aeff31f7c8821cfe9b1ca23\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No imagination could capture how sublime this spectacle would have been if it had occurred in the darkness of night. Hell itself might have served as an apt comparison. Even as it was, my hair stood on end while I stared into the gaping depths, letting my mind descend, as if wandering through those strange vaulted halls, fiery gulfs, and red, gruesome chasms of hideous, unfathomable flame. I had truly escaped by a hair\u2019s breadth. If the balloon had stayed in the cloud just a little longer\u2014if I had not decided to dump ballast simply to avoid getting wet\u2014I would have been ruined. Such dangers, though rarely considered, may be the greatest faced in ballooning. By this point, however, I was too high to worry about that anymore.\\n\\nI was rising fast, and by seven o\u2019clock the barometer showed an altitude of nine and a half miles. Breathing became extremely difficult. My head throbbed, and after feeling dampness on my cheeks for some time, I realized it was blood pouring from my eardrums. My eyes troubled me as well; when I ran my hands over them, they felt distinctly swollen and pushed out of their sockets, and everything in the car\u2014and even the balloon itself\u2014looked warped. These symptoms were worse than I expected and frightened me. At that moment, rashly and without thinking, I tossed three five-pound bags of ballast out of the car. The sudden surge upward carried me too quickly, without gradual adjustment, into a highly rarefied layer of the atmosphere, and it nearly cost me my expedition and my life. A violent spasm seized me for more than five minutes, and even after it eased somewhat, I could draw breath only in short, gasping intervals, while blood streamed from my nose and ears and even trickled slightly from my eyes. The pigeons were in visible distress and fought to break free, while the cat cried miserably and staggered across the car with her tongue hanging out, as though poisoned. I realized too late how reckless I had been in dumping the ballast, and my panic was overwhelming. I expected nothing less than death\u2014and death within minutes.\"}"
