          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1cd0f2a89992a96ed969be27\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even reading about the luxury of the dead seemed amazing. He turned his attention to embroideries and to tapestries that served as frescoes in the cold rooms of the northern nations of Europe. As he studied the subject\u2014he always had a remarkable ability to become completely absorbed in whatever he took up\u2014he felt almost saddened by how time ruined beautiful, extraordinary things. He, at least, had escaped that. Summer followed summer, yellow jonquils bloomed and died again and again, nights of horror repeated their shame, yet he remained unchanged. No winter marked his face or stained his flowerlike bloom. How different it was with objects! Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus-colored robe, showing gods fighting giants, worked by brown-skinned girls for Athena\u2019s pleasure? Where was the huge velarium Nero stretched across the Colosseum, that massive purple sail showing the starry sky and Apollo driving a chariot drawn by white, gold-reined steeds? He longed to see the curious table-napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, which displayed every dish needed for a feast; the mortuary cloth of King Chilperic, with its three hundred golden bees; the fantastical robes that angered the Bishop of Pontus and were decorated with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014all that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat Charles of Orleans once wore, whose sleeves bore the verses of a song beginning \u201cMadame, je suis tout joyeux,\u201d with a musical accompaniment wrought in gold thread and each square note formed with four pearls. He read of the room prepared at the palace at Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrots, embroidered with the king\u2019s arms, and five hundred and sixty-one butterflies, whose wings carried the queen\u2019s arms, all worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de\u2019 Medici had a mourning bed made of black velvet sprinkled with crescents and suns. Its curtains, of damask with leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold and silver ground, were edged with pearl embroidery, and the room was hung with rows of the queen\u2019s emblems in cut black velvet on cloth of silver. Louis XIV had gold-embroidered caryatids fifteen feet high in his apartment.\"}"
