          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b3e862244f503a3eaf0adadb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mighty voice\u2014if we can call it that, for we could not connect it with any organ or keyboard\u2014fell silent, and the girl, turning to help her elderly companion, at last saw us. It was her father; ever since childhood she had guided his blind steps. They were Germans from Saxony who had emigrated a few years earlier and formed new ties with the surrounding villagers. Around the time the plague began, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to guess. He, a nobleman, loved the fair daughter of the poor musician and followed them into exile from his family\u2019s disapproval. Soon the great leveller came, its scythe cutting both the grass and the tall flowers. The youth died early. She kept herself alive for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness let her preserve a fiction born by accident, and now these solitary survivors in the land let him remain unaware of the change; he did not know that when he listened to his child\u2019s music, the mute mountains, senseless lake, and unfeeling trees were, besides himself, her only audience. The very day we arrived she had been struck by the first symptoms of illness. She was frozen with dread at the thought of leaving her old, sightless father alone on the empty earth; yet she had not the courage to tell him, and the very height of her desperation drove her to extraordinary effort. At the usual evening hour she guided him to the chapel, and though she trembled and wept for his sake, she played without missing a beat or note the hymn celebrating the creation of the adorned earth, soon to be her tomb. We seemed to her like visitors from heaven. Her strained courage and barely sustained strength vanished at the sight of help. With a shriek she rushed toward us, embraced Adrian\u2019s knees, and with the single cry, \u201cO save my father!\u201d she broke down in sobs and hysterical cries, opening the long-sealed floodgates of her grief. Poor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, the place she pointed out to us moments before her death. Her father, finally aware of his daughter\u2019s danger yet unable to see the changes in her dear face, clung stubbornly to her hand until it grew cold and stiff in death. Even then he did not move or speak, until twelve hours later when merciful death took him to unbroken rest. They sleep beneath that sod, the tree their monument. The blessed spot is clear in my memory, ringed by craggy Jura and the far immeasurable Alps. The spire of the church they once frequented still rises from the sheltering trees; and though her hand is cold, I still imagine the holy music they loved drifting through the air, soothing their gentle spirits.\"}"
        }
      ],
