          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aa0752e47a441f502c2a90e6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how unfathomable they remained! The soul harbored animal impulses, and the body, in turn, experienced spells of spirituality. The senses could become refined, while the intellect might descend. Who could identify where the bodily urge ended or the psychic impulse began? How superficial were the rigid definitions of typical psychologists! Yet how hard it was to adjudicate the claims of each competing school! Was the soul merely a shade dwelling within the house of sin? Or did the body actually reside within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The divorce of spirit from matter was mysterious, and their union proved equally mysterious.\\n\\nHe began to muse whether psychology could ever become such an exact science that each tiny spring of existence would be laid bare. As things stood, we endlessly misread ourselves and seldom grasped others. Experience held no ethical worth. It was simply the label men affixed to their errors. Moralists generally treated it as a warning mechanism, endowed it with a certain moral efficacy in shaping character, extolled it as something that instructed us what to pursue and what to shun. But experience provided no driving force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly showed was that our future would mimic our past, and that the wrongdoing we once committed reluctantly we would commit repeatedly, even joyfully.\\n\\nIt was evident to him that only the experimental method could yield any scientific dissection of the passions; and Dorian Gray was certainly a subject ready to hand, offering promise of abundant and rewarding results. His sudden, frenzied love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological curiosity of considerable import. There could be no question that curiosity played a large part in it, curiosity paired with the thirst for novel experiences, yet it was not a straightforward, but a highly intricate passion. Whatever portion of it sprang from the purely sensuous instinct of youth had been reshaped by imagination, altered into something that seemed to the boy himself removed from sense, and for that very reason all the more perilous. It was those passions about whose origins we misled ourselves that most tyrannized over us. Our weakest motives were the ones whose nature we recognized. Frequently, while we imagined we were experimenting on others, we were in fact experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat brooding over these reflections, a knock sounded at the door, and his valet entered to inform him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
