          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2906f29e89eb1a8d329d921\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not every one would have so kindly persisted as she did, refusing to take Jane\u2019s answer; she flatly insisted she would not write such a refusal yesterday, as Jane wanted; she meant to wait\u2014and sure enough, by yesterday evening everything was arranged for Jane to go. Quite a surprise to me! I had not the faintest suspicion!\u2014Jane drew Mrs. Elton aside and told her at once that, on reconsidering the benefits of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s position, she had resolved to accept it.\u2014I never heard a syllable until all was concluded.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening at Mrs. Elton\u2019s?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, every one of us; Mrs. Elton insisted on our coming. It was decided on the hill, while we were strolling with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You must all spend your evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I positively must have every one of you come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was present too, was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Mr. Knightley was not; he excused himself at once; and though I imagined he would appear, because Mrs. Elton vowed she would not let him escape, he never did;\u2014but my mother, Jane, and I were there, and we passed a very agreeable evening. Such considerate friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, must always be pleasant company, though everyone looked rather worn after the morning\u2019s party. Even enjoyment, as you know, is exhausting\u2014and I cannot say that any of them seemed to have derived much delight from it. Still, I shall always remember it as a charming party, and feel deeply grateful to the considerate friends who included me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I assume, though you did not perceive it, had spent the whole day deciding?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time arrives, it must be unwelcome to her and to all her friends\u2014but I trust her appointment will have every relief possible\u2014I mean with regard to the family\u2019s character and manners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Indeed, there is everything imaginable to make her content. Besides the Sucklings and Bragges, there is not another nursery establishment so generous and refined within Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge is the most delightful woman!\u2014Her style of living is nearly on par with Maple Grove\u2014and regarding the children, excepting the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are no other youngsters so elegant and sweet. Jane will meet such respect and kindness!\u2014It will be nothing but delight, a life of delight.\u2014And her salary!\u2014I truly dare not mention her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse.\"}"
