          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b495e7d87909222dc4f0da5c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have already remarked that he was a poet: at three-and-twenty he put forth his first poem, and the whole nation greeted it with fervour and delight. Fortune attended him without fail; never had a reputation risen so swiftly, nor spread so completely. The crowd praised the very verses that amazed the scholar secluded in his study: not a single voice dissented.\\n\\nIt was at this zenith of his fame that he met Elinor. She was a youthful heiress of rare loveliness, living under her guardian\u2019s charge: from the first sight of them together it was plain they were created for one another. Elinor lacked Woodville\u2019s genius, yet she was generous and noble, exalted by her youth and the admiration she everywhere inspired above knowledge of anything except virtue and goodness. She was fair; her manners candid and unaffected; her dark-blue eyes shone with a radiance bestowed only by sensibility wedded to wisdom.\\n\\nDestined for each other, they soon loved. Woodville tasted love\u2019s happiness for the first time; Elinor was transported by winning the heart of one so splendid and honoured among men. Could aught but pure joy spring from such a union?\\n\\nWoodville, the Poet, was sought by every circle, all eyes fixed upon him when he entered; yet he was merely the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor possessed wealth. Her guardian did not oppose their attachment: Woodville\u2019s merit was too great for anyone to cavil at his lack of fortune; but her father\u2019s dying injunction forbade her to wed before reaching her majority, and her inheritance depended on strict obedience. She had just completed her twentieth year, and both lovers were compelled to endure the delay. Nevertheless they were always together, their bliss like that of Eden: they studied side by side, contrived plans for future pursuits, and, drinking love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and lips, they scarcely lamented the pause before full union. Woodville\u2019s glory ever increased, while under his accomplished guidance Elinor grew yet more lovely and wise.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would reach one-and-twenty; every preparation for their wedding was complete. How can I tell the catastrophe that befell such joy? Yet this earth, shrouded in blight and sorrow, would cease to be itself if two such angelic beings had been permitted to exist solely for one another. Search the world over and you will not find the faultless felicity their marriage promised; only a revolution in the order appointed for us wretched earth-dwellers could have allowed such perfect happiness to endure.\"}"
