          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4b5028760402fc8a4ad1d9a9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I question how long you would have persisted without interference. When would you have spoken if I had not forced the moment? My resolve to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had tremendous effect. Too much, perhaps, for what becomes of morality if our comfort arises from a broken promise, since I should not have touched on the topic? This cannot stand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere is no need for you to be troubled. The moral remains quite intact. Lady Catherine\u2019s unwarrantable attempts to divide us swept away all my uncertainties. I do not owe my current happiness to your impatient wish to show gratitude. I was in no temper to wait for the opening you might offer. My aunt\u2019s report gave me hope, and I resolved to learn everything immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been infinitely beneficial, which should delight her, given she adores being useful. But tell me, why did you come down to Netherfield? Simply to ride to Longbourn and feel awkward? or did you plan something more decisive?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true intention was to see you, and to judge, if possible, whether I might ever hope to inspire your affection. My announced motive, or what I told myself it was, involved discovering if your sister still cared for Bingley, and, if she did, making to him the admission I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you ever expect to muster the courage to inform Lady Catherine of what awaits her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more apt to lack time than courage, Elizabeth. Yet it must be done; give me a sheet of paper, and it shall be done at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd were I not obliged to write a letter myself, I might sit beside you and admire the steadiness of your hand, as another young lady once did. But I too have an aunt who must no longer be neglected.\u201d\\n\\nFrom a reluctance to confess how much she had overrated her intimacy with Mr. Darcy, Elizabeth had still not replied to Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s lengthy letter; yet now, possessing news she knew would please most, she felt almost ashamed to realize her uncle and aunt had already been deprived of three days of joy, and she immediately penned the following:--\\n\\n\u201cI would have thanked you earlier, my dear aunt, as duty required, for your long, kind, and satisfying collection of particulars; but, truthfully, I was too vexed to write. You imagined more than truly existed. Yet now imagine whatever you please; give your fancy free rein, let your imagination soar along every possible path the subject permits, and unless you deem me already married, you cannot be much mistaken.\u201d\"}"
