          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4561cd97f4a8f538a383f699\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe and the children might very well stay.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, papa\u2014you have never been able to make that happen, and I do not think you ever will. Isabella cannot stand being away from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThat was too true to argue. Though the idea displeased him, Mr. Woodhouse only gave a resigned sigh, and as Emma saw how the thought of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband weighed on him, she quickly turned the talk to a part of the subject that would cheer him.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must give us as much of her company as she can while my brother and sister are here. I know she will be delighted with the children. We are very proud of the children, are we not, papa? I wonder which one she will find the handsomest, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I wonder which she will. Poor little things, how happy they will be to come. They are very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I do not know who is not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John looks very much like his mother. Henry is the eldest; he was named after me, not after his father. John, the second, was named after his father. Some people think it odd that the eldest was not named for his father, but Isabella insisted on Henry, which I thought very sweet of her. And he is a very smart boy indeed. They are all remarkably clever, and they have so many charming ways. They will come and stand by my chair and say, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?\u2019 and once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were meant only for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them quite often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are so gentle yourself; but if you could compare him with other fathers, you would not think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and strong; and if they misbehave, he gives them a sharp word now and then, but he is a loving father\u2014certainly Mr. John Knightley is a loving father. The children are all fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and throws them up to the ceiling in a very frightening way!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they enjoy it, papa; there is nothing they like more. It is such a delight for them that if their uncle did not insist on taking turns, whoever started would never let the other have a turn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I cannot understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is how it is with all of us, papa. Half the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other half.\u201d\"}"
        }
