          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"febb89726bec605fdd105379\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With that finished, I released a few additional loops from the rim, slid more of the cloth between hoop and net, and refastened the loosened loops to their respective buttons. Proceeding thus, I could slip the entire upper portion of the bag into the space between the network and the hoop. It was evident the hoop would now drop inside the car, while the car and everything in it would hang solely by the buttons\u2019 strength. At first glance this might appear an unreliable support, yet it was not so, since the buttons were not only very stout individually, but arranged so close together that each bore only a minute fraction of the total weight. Even if the car with its load had weighed thrice as much, I should have felt no apprehension. I lifted the hoop once more within the gum-elastic envelope and held it nearly at its original height using three slender poles prepared for this purpose. This, naturally, kept the bag expanded above and maintained the netting below in the proper place. All that remained was to close the mouth of the covering, which I managed by gathering the folds of material and twisting them very tightly on the inside with a fixed tourniquet-like device.\\n\\n\u201cIn the sides of the cover thus adjusted about the car were set three round panes of thick, clear glass that let me see without effort in every horizontal direction. In the section of cloth forming the bottom there was likewise a fourth window of the same sort, aligning with a small opening in the car\u2019s floor. Through this I could look straight downward; but I could not contrive any similar arrangement overhead, owing to the peculiar method of sealing the opening there and the wrinkles it caused in the fabric, so I had no view of objects directly overhead. This was no great loss, since even had I placed a pane at the top, the balloon itself would have rendered it useless.\\n\\n\u201cAbout a foot beneath one of the side panes was a circular aperture eight inches across, edged with a brass rim whose inner lip was cut for a screw. Into this rim I secured the large tube of the condenser, the main body of the apparatus standing, naturally, inside the gum-elastic chamber.\u201d\"}"
