          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0226ac5b3432829a8c0f893d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you\u2019re about to call Miss Havisham a he. And I doubt even you would stretch it that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, up town?\u201d Joe repeated.\\n\\n\u201cIs there a Miss Havisham down town?\u201d snapped my sister.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go there and play. Naturally he\u2019s going. And he\u2019d better play there,\u201d my sister declared, wagging her head at me as a cue to be airy and lively, \u201cor I\u2019ll put him to work.\u201d\\n\\nI already knew of Miss Havisham in town\u2014every soul for miles knew of Miss Havisham in town\u2014as a monstrously wealthy, forbidding lady living in a vast, cheerless house fortified against thieves, leading a reclusive existence.\\n\\n\u201cWell, to be sure!\u201d cried Joe, amazed. \u201cHow ever did she come to know Pip?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNoodle!\u201d my sister shrilled. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Which a certain person,\u201d Joe ventured again with courtesy, \u201cremarked that she desired him to go and play there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd couldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook whether he knew of a boy to go and play there? Isn\u2019t it remotely possible that Uncle Pumblechook is one of her tenants, and that sometimes\u2014we won\u2019t impose quarterly or even half-yearly, that would overtax you\u2014but sometimes goes there to pay his rent? And couldn\u2019t she then inquire of Uncle Pumblechook if he knew a boy to go and play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, ever mindful and considerate toward us\u2014though you may fail to recognize it, Joseph,\u201d she added in her deepest tone of reproach, as though he were the most unfeeling nephew alive, \u201cthen suggest this boy, standing here Prancing\u201d\u2014which I do solemnly affirm I was not\u2014\u201cfor whom I have eternally been an obedient drudge?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d exclaimed Uncle Pumblechook. \u201cNicely stated! Sweetly put! Very good! Now, Joseph, you\u2019re aware of the situation.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d my sister insisted, still reproachful, while Joe sheepishly passed the back of his hand back and forth across his nose, \u201cyou do not yet\u2014despite what you may suppose\u2014understand the situation. You may think you do, but you do _not_, Joseph. Because you don\u2019t know that Uncle Pumblechook, perceiving that for aught we can say this boy\u2019s fortune could be sealed by his visit to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him to town this very night in his own chaise-cart, to lodge him tonight, and to deliver him with his own hands to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And Lor-a-mussy me!\u201d cried my sister, flinging off her bonnet in a flash of despair, \u201chere I am chattering to a pair of Mooncalfs, while Uncle Pumblechook waits, the mare catches her death at the door, and the boy stands smeared with soot and grime from the crown of his head to the sole of his foot!\u201d\"}"
