{"request_id":"0b649114dc1d5ca8804ed9ac","rewritten_text":"When my tears stopped, reason and memory returned, and I began to think more calmly about what had happened and how I should act. Only a few hours had passed, yet a tremendous change had overtaken me—what usually takes years had happened since morning. My father was now as good as dead to me, and for a moment I felt as if his white hair were already on the coffin pillow and I—my youth vanished in premature age—were mourning his timely passing. But it was not so; I was still young, oh, far too young, and he was not dead to others. But I, most wretched of beings, must never see or speak to him again. I must flee from him more resolutely than from my worst enemy. Whether in solitude or in crowded cities, I must never look on him again. The thought suffocated me with anguish and filled my imagination so fully that for a while I could pursue no other line of thought. From then on, I decided, I would live in the bleakest seclusion. I would go to the Continent and become a nun—not out of religion, for I was no Catholic, but so I might be cut off from the world forever. There I would find solitude in which I could weep, and the noise of life would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved, most miserable father? Would he die? Would he never conquer the fierce passion that now ruled him without mercy? Might it not be that many, many years hence, when age had cooled the burning sensations he now felt, he might again be a father to me? This thought smoothed my brow, and I could feel—and wept to feel it—a half-melancholy smile soften my suffering lips. I dared to embrace better hopes for my future. Years must pass, but they would slip by lightly, carried by hope, or even if they dragged, still they would pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering alone. Let him once more pour out his wild complaints to the vast forests and thunderous waterfalls of another country. Let him face fearsome dangers and soul-crushing hardships. Let the scorching southern sun burn his passion-worn cheeks and the cold night rains chill his blood.

To that life, miserable father, I consign you! Go! Spend your days among savages and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs be worn, your heart grow cold, and all youth die within you!"}

