          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bb151c55f216ce8b0cae67eb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAfter that, we put up a wire barrier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWire barrier?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You suggested it yourself two or three years back.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I recall\u2014the first time the Church tested its power against us and soon judged it wiser to wait for a more promising season. Well, how have you set up the barrier?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI run twelve very sturdy wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014out of a large dynamo in the cave\u2014a dynamo with nothing but a positive and a negative brush.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, correct.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a level circle of ground a hundred yards across; they form twelve separate barriers, each ten feet apart\u2014meaning twelve concentric circles\u2014and their ends reenter the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight; proceed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe barriers are lashed to massive oaken posts only three feet apart, and the posts are sunk five feet deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExcellent strength.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. The wires have no ground-contact outside the cave. They leave the positive brush of the dynamo; the negative brush provides the ground-connection; the other ends come back to the cave, and each one is grounded independently.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that will never do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s too costly\u2014wastes power for nothing. You need only one ground-connection, through the negative brush. Each opposite end must return into the cave and be fastened separately, without any ground-contact. Now see the thrift of it. Suppose a cavalry charge flings itself against the barrier; you are consuming no power, spending no coin, because there\u2019s just one ground-connection until those horses strike the wire; the instant they touch it they complete a circuit to the negative brush through the earth, and fall dead. Don\u2019t you see? You don\u2019t expend energy till it is required; your lightning waits like a loaded gun, but it costs you nothing until you fire it. Oh yes, the single ground-connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly! I can\u2019t imagine how I missed it. It\u2019s not only cheaper, but more effective than the other plan, since if wires snap or tangle, no damage follows.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNone, particularly if we have a telltale in the cave and cut off the broken wire. Now, proceed. What about the gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014set up. In the middle of the innermost circle, on a wide platform six feet high, I\u2019ve assembled a battery of thirteen gatling guns with ample ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerfect. They command every path, and when the Church\u2019s knights show up, there\u2019ll be lively music. The brow of the cliff above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve mounted a wire barrier there too, and a gatling gun.\u201d\"}"
