{"request_id":"881a4f41c304f9282770a61e","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have while Fate still weighed this dreadful outcome on its scales? The wedding had to be delayed, no matter the cost. He was absolutely certain of that. As passionately as he loved the girl, as wildly as his nerves trembled with joy at the slightest brush of her hand when they sat together, he still saw his duty with perfect clarity, and fully understood that he had no right to marry until he had carried out the murder. Once that was done, he could stand before the altar with Sybil Merton and surrender his life to her without fear of wrongdoing. Once that was done, he could hold her and know she would never need to blush for him, never need to lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner it was done, the better for them both.

Many men in his position would have chosen the easy, indulgent path over the hard climb to duty, but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure ahead of principle. His love went beyond simple desire, and Sybil represented everything good and noble in the world to him. For a moment he naturally recoiled from what was being asked of him, but the feeling soon faded. His heart told him it was not a sin, but a sacrifice; his mind reminded him there was no other option. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others, and even though the task before him was undeniably terrible, he knew he could not allow selfishness to defeat love. Sooner or later each of us must face the same decision; the same question is posed to all. Lord Arthur faced it early in life, before cynicism had warped his nature or shallow fashion had corroded his heart, and he felt no hesitation about doing his duty. Luckily for him, he was no mere dreamer or idle dilettante. Had he been, he might have hesitated like Hamlet and let indecision ruin his purpose. But he was essentially practical. To him, life meant action rather than contemplation. He possessed that rarest of qualities: common sense.

By now the wild, stormy emotions of the previous night had completely faded, and he almost felt ashamed when he remembered his frantic roaming from street to street, the fierce anguish that had gripped him. The very sincerity of his suffering made it seem unreal to him now. He wondered how he could have been so foolish as to rant and rave over what could not be avoided."}

