          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"392e61461ebd1bb6f6125d51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"April 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction which, though fully expected, filled me with unalloyed delight. After following its previous path as far as roughly the twentieth parallel south, it suddenly veered sharply eastward and spent the entire day moving in that course, maintaining nearly, if not precisely, the plane of the lunar ellipse. Noteworthy, this shift produced a distinct rocking of the car, a swaying that endured for many hours in varying strength.\\n\\nApril 13th. Was again greatly disturbed by a renewal of the loud, crackling sound that frightened me on the tenth. I meditated long on it without reaching any satisfactory interpretation. The apparent diameter of the earth diminished greatly, now spanning scarcely more than twenty-five degrees beneath the balloon. The moon remained invisible, almost directly over my zenith. I still kept to the plane of the ellipse, though with little advancement eastward.\\n\\nApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter shrank with extreme rapidity. Today I felt strongly persuaded that the balloon was ascending along the line of apsides toward perigee\u2014in plain terms, taking the exact route that would bring it straight to the moon at the nearest point of its orbit to the earth. The moon itself was vertically above me and therefore out of sight. Condensing the air required prolonged and arduous labor.\\n\\nApril 15th. No outlines of continents or oceans could now be distinguished on the earth with any clarity. Around noon I detected, for the third time, that horrifying noise which had so completely alarmed me earlier. This time it persisted, intensifying as it went on. Finally, while I stood numb with terror, expecting some unimaginable catastrophe, the car shook with tremendous force, and an enormous blazing mass of unknown material swept past the balloon with the clamorous bellowing of a thousand thunders. Once my fear and astonishment had somewhat abated, I readily concluded it must be a colossal volcanic fragment hurled from the very world I was approaching, likely one of those peculiar substances occasionally discovered on earth and labeled meteoric stones for want of a better term.\\n\\nApril 16th. Today, straining to look upward through the side windows in turn, I saw, to my great joy, a tiny portion of the moon\u2019s disk projecting, as if encircling, beyond the vast circumference of the balloon.\"}"
