          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d7a12ca2c0ec860afe1d41a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGit up! What you doin\u2019 there?\u201d\\n\\nI blinked awake and peered around, trying to figure out my whereabouts. Sun was well up, and I\u2019d been dead asleep. Pap loomed over me looking sour and sickly, too. He says:\\n\\n\u201cWhat you meddlin\u2019 with this gun for?\u201d\\n\\nI reckoned he didn\u2019t remember a lick of what he\u2019d been up to, so I says:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody aimed to break in, so I was laying for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you fetch me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried; but I couldn\u2019t stir you\u2014couldn\u2019t move you a hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, fine. Quit standing there palaverin\u2019 all day; hustle out and see if there\u2019s a fish on them lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along directly.\u201d\\n\\nHe slipped the bolt, and I scooted off up the river-bank. I noticed bits of limbs and such drifting down, with specks of bark, so I knowed the river was starting to swell. I allowed I\u2019d be having big times now if I was over at the town. The June rise always used to bring me luck; soon as it commenced here\u2019d come cordwood floating along, and hunks off log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs bunched up; all you had to do was snag them and sell to the woodyards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI kept on up the bank with one eye peeled for pap and the other watching what the rise might fetch. All at once here come a canoe; a real dandy, too, thirteen or fourteen foot, sitting high like a duck. I dove head-first off the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and struck out for the canoe. I half expected somebody was stretched out in it, because folks often played that trick, and when a chap had dragged a skiff pretty near they\u2019d pop up and laugh at him. But it warn\u2019t that way now. It was a drift-canoe sure enough, and I clambered in and paddled her to shore. I thought, the old man\u2019ll be tickled when he spies this\u2014she\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I made land pap wasn\u2019t in sight yet, and as I was running her into a little creek like a gully, all wrapped in vines and willows, another notion struck me: I figured I\u2019d stash her fine, and then, instead of lighting out for the woods when I made my break, I\u2019d head down the river about fifty miles and fix a camp in one place for good, and not have so rough a time hoofing it.\\n\\nIt was nigh the shanty, and I fancied I heard the old man coming every minute; but I got her stowed, and then I stepped out and peeked around a clump of willows, and there was the old man down the trail a spell, already drawing a bead on a bird with his gun.\"}"
