          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"117f9933561312f739c825a6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-headed cavalier went riding over Brockley meadows, where she tore her riding habit so badly squeezing through a hedge that, upon returning home, she resolved to slip up the back staircase to avoid notice. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, its door chanced to stand open, and she imagined she saw someone inside. Assuming it was her mother\u2019s maid, who occasionally came there with her work, she stepped in to ask for help mending the damage. To her utter astonishment, it was the Canterville Ghost himself! He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees whirl through the air and the red leaves caper wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, his entire posture one of deep dejection. Indeed, he looked so forlorn and so worn-out that little Virginia, whose initial impulse had been to flee and fasten herself in her room, felt a sudden compassion and resolved to console him. Her tread was so light, and his melancholy so absorbing, that he did not realize she was there until she spoke.\\n\\n\u201cI am very sorry for you,\u201d she said, \u201cbut my brothers return to Eton to-morrow, and then, if you behave properly, no one will bother you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is ridiculous to ask me to behave,\u201d he replied, turning in amazement toward the pretty child who had dared address him, \u201cquite ridiculous. I must clank my chains, groan through keyholes, and pace about by night, if that is what you mean. It is my sole justification for existing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no justification for existing at all, and you know you have been dreadfully wicked. Mrs. Umney told us the day we arrived that you had murdered your wife.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I fully admit it,\u201d said the Ghost peevishly, \u201cbut it was entirely a domestic affair and concerned no one else.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is very wrong to kill anybody,\u201d said Virginia, who sometimes had a sweet Puritan gravity inherited from some old New England forebear.\\n\\n\u201cOh, I detest the cheap severity of abstract morals! My wife was exceedingly plain, never starched my ruffs properly, and understood nothing of cookery. Why, there was a buck I shot in Hogley Woods, a magnificent pricket, and do you know the way she had it served? However, it is of no consequence now\u2014it is all past\u2014and I must say it was hardly considerate of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u201d\"}"
