          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f60b8567fbbfa8d5c9c2f454\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nothing Emma learned later made her take back her bad opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her first impression had been right. However often they met, Mrs. Elton always appeared the same\u2014full of herself, presumptuous, overly familiar, ignorant, and ill-bred. She had a little beauty and some skill, yet so little good sense that she believed she came armed with superior worldly knowledge to brighten and improve a country neighbourhood, and she imagined Miss Hawkins had held a social standing that only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance could surpass.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton saw her differently. He seemed not only happy but proud, as if congratulating himself on bringing to Highbury a woman even Miss Woodhouse could not match. Most of her new acquaintances, either inclined to approve or unused to judging for themselves, followed Miss Bates\u2019s goodwill or assumed the bride must be as clever and charming as she claimed, and were easily satisfied. Mrs. Elton\u2019s praise thus spread from mouth to mouth as it should, unhindered by Miss Woodhouse, who continued her initial polite contribution and spoke graciously of Mrs. Elton as \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one respect, Mrs. Elton grew even worse. Her feelings toward Emma cooled. Likely offended by Emma\u2019s lukewarm response to her offers of friendship, she pulled back and became noticeably colder and more distant. The change suited Emma, but the malice behind it only deepened her dislike. Both Mrs. Elton\u2019s and Mr. Elton\u2019s manners toward Harriet were also unpleasant\u2014sneering and careless. Emma hoped this would quickly cure Harriet, yet the mentality that caused such conduct lowered both Eltons in her eyes. It was clear poor Harriet\u2019s feelings had been offered up to marital confidence, and Emma\u2019s part in the story had probably been coloured to make Harriet look worst and Mr. Elton feel best. Harriet had become the focus of their shared dislike. Whenever they ran out of conversation, it was easy for them to start criticizing Miss Woodhouse; any hostility they dared not show Emma directly spilled out in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nFrom the very start, Mrs. Elton took a strong liking to Jane Fairfax\u2014not just when conflict with one young lady might make the other more appealing, but immediately. Mere natural admiration did not satisfy her. Unasked, without any right or invitation, she insisted on helping and befriending Jane. Before Emma lost Mrs. Elton\u2019s confidence, and by about their third meeting, she heard all about Mrs. Elton\u2019s knight-errantry on the subject.\"}"
