          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"928f06bcb7980193e5761c21\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a point in every well-made boy\u2019s life when he suddenly burns to go dig for hidden treasure. That urge came over Tom one day. He marched out to find Joe Harper, but couldn\u2019t. Then he tried Ben Rogers; Ben had gone fishing. Soon Tom ran into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a private spot and quietly explained the plan. Huck was willing. Huck was always willing to join any scheme that promised fun and didn\u2019t need money, because he had too much of the kind of time that isn\u2019t money. \u201cWhere\u2019ll we dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is treasure buried everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not a bit. It\u2019s hidden in very particular spots, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests under the tip of a branch on an old dead tree right where the shadow falls at midnight; but mostly under the floor of haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho hides it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, of course\u2014who else? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it was mine I wouldn\u2019t hide it; I\u2019d spend it and have a good time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t. They always hide it and leave it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo. They mean to, but they usually forget the signs, or they die. Anyway, it sits there a long time and gets rusty. After a while somebody finds an old yellow paper that tells how to find the signs\u2014a paper that takes about a week to work out because it\u2019s mostly symbols and hieroglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro-what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics\u2014pictures and such that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell then, how are you going to find the signs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need signs. They always bury treasure under a haunted house or on an island or under a dead tree with one limb sticking out. We\u2019ve tried Jackson\u2019s Island some already, and we can try it again later. There\u2019s that old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and there are plenty of dead-limb trees\u2014loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a question! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how do you know which one to dig at?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDig at them all!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that\u2019ll take all summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so what? Suppose you find a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and dull, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. How about that?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes lit up.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s great. Plenty great for me. Just give me the hundred dollars and I won\u2019t need any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
