{"request_id":"dddf80c0aa96d077558bd497","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s parents, who were rather old-fashioned and might object to the marriage if there was any hint of scandal, though he was sure that if he told them the full story, they would be the first to understand his motives. He had every reason, then, to choose poison. It was safe, sure, quiet, and spared him the painful scenes he detested, as most Englishmen did.

Yet he knew nothing about poisons, and the waiter could find nothing in the library except Ruff’s Guide and Bailey’s Magazine. He searched the shelves himself and finally found a handsome edition of the Pharmacopoeia and a copy of Erskine’s Toxicology, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the oldest members of the Buckingham, having been elected there by mistake; the Committee were so furious over the blunder that they blackballed the proper candidate when he appeared. Lord Arthur struggled with the technical terms in both books and regretted not paying more attention to his classics at Oxford, but in the second volume of Erskine he discovered a clear, detailed account of aconitine. It seemed exactly the poison he needed. Its effect was swift—almost immediate—completely painless, and, taken in a gelatine capsule as Sir Mathew recommended, not unpleasant at all. He noted the fatal dose on his shirt cuff, returned the books to their places, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the famous chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always served the aristocracy himself, was surprised by the order and respectfully mentioned that a medical certificate might be required. However, once Lord Arthur explained that he needed to dispose of a large Norwegian mastiff showing signs of rabies, which had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf, Mr. Pestle was fully satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s knowledge of toxicology, and filled the prescription at once.

Lord Arthur slipped the capsule into a pretty silver bonbonnière he had seen in a Bond Street shop window, threw away Pestle and Humbey’s ugly pill-box, and drove straight to Lady Clementina’s."}

