          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1cd67545de6e102b669718fa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, gravity would keep weakening in proportion to the square of the distance, and with a wildly increasing speed I would eventually reach those distant regions where the earth\u2019s pull gives way to the moon\u2019s. With this in mind, I decided it was pointless to burden myself with more provisions than would last forty days.\\n\\n\u201cAnother difficulty still troubled me. It has been noted that during balloon ascents to great altitudes, besides the pain involved in breathing, people suffer intense discomfort in the head and body, often with nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms, which grow worse the higher one goes. This was a disturbing thought. Was it not likely these symptoms would continue to intensify indefinitely, or at least until death ended them? I eventually concluded otherwise. Their origin lay in the gradual removal of normal atmospheric pressure on the body\u2019s surface and the resulting swelling of the superficial blood vessels\u2014not in any actual breakdown of the organism, as happens with respiratory distress when the air is too thin to renew the blood in a heart\u2019s ventricle. Unless that renewal failed, I saw no reason life could not continue even in a vacuum; the expansion and contraction of the chest\u2014what we call breathing\u2014is purely muscular action, the cause rather than the result of respiration. In short, I believed that as the body adapted to the lack of atmospheric pressure, the pain would gradually lessen, and while it lasted I trusted my iron constitution to bear it.\\n\\n\u201cThus, honorable sirs, I have described some, though not all, of the considerations that led me to plan a journey to the moon. I will now present the outcome of an attempt so apparently bold in theory and, in any case, so completely without precedent in human history.\\n\\n\u201cAfter reaching the height previously mentioned\u2014three and three-quarter miles\u2014I tossed a quantity of feathers from the gondola and saw that I was still rising quickly enough. There was therefore no need to throw out ballast. I was pleased, for I wanted to keep as much weight aboard as possible, for reasons that will become clear later.\u201d\"}"
        }
