          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"33a9f6052b201b22ecb658b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m not dining at home, so I won\u2019t need you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, sir,\u201d the man said as he left the room.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, we can\u2019t waste a moment. This chest is heavy\u2014I\u2019ll carry it. You take the other things.\u201d His words came fast and commanding. Campbell felt overpowered by him. They left the room together.\\n\\nAt the top landing, Dorian took out the key, turned it in the lock, and then stopped. A worried look crossed his face, and he shuddered. \u201cI don\u2019t think I can go in, Alan,\u201d he murmured.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s your concern. I don\u2019t need you,\u201d Campbell replied coldly.\\n\\nDorian pushed the door half open. His portrait\u2019s face sneered in the sunlight. The torn curtain lay on the floor in front of it. He remembered that last night, for the first time ever, he had forgotten to cover the cursed canvas, and he started to rush forward, then recoiled with another shudder.\\n\\nWhat was that vile red dew gleaming wet on one of the painted hands, as if the canvas had sweated blood? How dreadful it looked\u2014more dreadful, he felt for a moment, than the silent thing stretched across the table, the thing whose twisted shadow on the stained carpet told him it hadn\u2019t moved and still lay as he had left it.\\n\\nHe drew a deep breath, opened the door wider, and with half-closed eyes and his head turned away, walked quickly inside, determined not to look even once at the dead man. Stooping, he lifted the gold and purple drape and flung it over the picture.\\n\\nHe stopped there, afraid to turn around, staring at the tangle of patterns before him. Behind him he heard Campbell bringing in the heavy chest, the irons, and the other tools he had demanded for the grim task. He wondered if Campbell and Basil Hallward had ever met, and what they had thought of each other if they had.\\n\\n\u201cLeave me now,\u201d said a harsh voice behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and hurried out, barely aware that the corpse had been pushed back into the chair and that Campbell was studying a gleaming yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the key turn in the lock.\\n\\nIt was well after seven when Campbell returned to the library. He was pale but completely composed. \u201cI\u2019ve done what you asked,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
        }
      ],
