          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1a69396b62219d378c1deb0b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I hastened along and soon reached my college. Then the recollection came over me, with a chill, that the creature I had abandoned in my chamber might still be roaming there. I trembled at the idea of confronting the monster, yet dreaded even more that Henry might encounter him. So I begged him to stay a few minutes at the foot of the stairs, and I flew up to my own quarters. My hand was already on the latch before I regained my senses; then I halted, seized with a cold shudder. I flung the door wide open, as children do when they fancy a spectre waits behind it; yet nothing met my sight. I entered on tiptoe: the room was empty, and my bedroom likewise cleared of its odious inmate. I could scarcely credit so extraordinary a piece of good fortune; but once convinced my adversary had escaped, I clapped my hands with delight and ran back to Clerval.\\n\\nWe mounted to my chamber, and the servant soon served breakfast; yet I could not master myself. It was not mere happiness that seized me; my skin prickled with overpowering nerves, and my pulse raced. I could not stay still for a moment; I leaped over chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed unstoppably. At first Clerval attributed my unwonted gaiety to the joy of seeing him again, but as he watched me more carefully, he detected a frenzy in my eyes he could not explain, and my loud, uninhibited, unfeeling laughter both startled and alarmed him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cwhat on earth is the matter? Do not laugh so. How wretched you look! What has caused this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAsk me nothing,\u201d I exclaimed, covering my eyes, for I imagined the dreaded spectre gliding into the room; \u201c_he_ could tell. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I thought the monster clutched me; I struggled madly and dropped down in a convulsion.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must his emotions have been? A reunion he had expected with such happiness so horribly changed into sorrow. Yet I did not witness his anguish, for I was insensible and remained without consciousness for a very long time.\\n\\nThus began the nervous fever that held me prisoner for many months. Throughout that period Henry was my only caretaker. I later learned that, aware of my father\u2019s advanced years and unfitness for so lengthy a journey, and how my illness would torment Elizabeth, he spared them distress by concealing the true severity of my malady.\"}"
