{"request_id":"3138a8be966ec50516b99c64","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly coaxed it into flames. I took note of this and made a fan out of branches to stir the embers when they were close to dying out. When night returned I was pleased to find that the fire provided light as well as heat, and that discovering this element helped with my food, because some scraps left by the travelers had been roasted and tasted far better than the berries I gathered from the trees. I tried to cook my own food the same way, laying it on the coals. The berries were ruined by this experiment, but the nuts and roots were much improved.

“Food, however, became scarce, and I often spent an entire day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease my hunger. Realizing this, I decided to leave the place I had lived in so far and hunt for one where my few needs would be easier to meet. As I left I greatly mourned the loss of the fire I had gained by chance and had no idea how to recreate. I spent several hours thinking seriously about this challenge, but had to give up on replacing it, and wrapping myself in my cloak, I headed through the woods toward the setting sun. I wandered like this for three days and finally reached open country. Heavy snow had fallen the night before, covering the fields in unbroken white; it looked bleak, and the cold, wet covering chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I desperately wanted food and shelter; at last I spotted a small hut on a rise, clearly built for a shepherd’s comfort. This was completely new to me, and I examined the structure with great curiosity. Finding the door open, I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, cooking his breakfast. He turned at the sound of my steps, saw me, screamed loudly, and fled the hut, running across the fields with a speed surprising for his frail frame. His look, unlike any I had seen before, and his panicked escape startled me. Yet I was enchanted by the hut itself; snow and rain could not get in, the ground was dry, and to me it seemed as exquisite and divine a refuge as Pandæmonium did to the demons of hell after their torment in the lake of fire.”"}

