          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e53b20a98a35d8ca2e1168e8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cPlease think about it, ma\u2019am,\u201d Oliver pleaded, tears forcing their way out despite his efforts to hold them back. \u201cPlease think how young and good she is, and how much happiness and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014completely certain\u2014that for your sake, because you are so kind; for her sake; and for everyone she fills with joy; she will not die. Heaven cannot let her die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush,\u201d Mrs. Maylie said, laying her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou think like a child, poor boy. Yet, even so, you remind me of my duty. I forgot it for a moment, Oliver, and I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old and have seen enough illness and death to know the agony of being parted from those we love. I have also seen enough to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them; but that knowledge should comfort us in our grief. Heaven is just, and such things teach us powerfully that there is a brighter world than this, and that the passage to it can be swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how dearly!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke, she silenced her sobs at once, straightened herself, and became composed and resolute. He was even more astonished that this steadiness endured, and that throughout the care and constant watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie remained calm and prepared: carrying out all her new duties steadily and, outwardly at least, even cheerfully. But he was young and did not yet understand what strong minds can endure under trial. How could he, when even such minds rarely know their own strength?\\n\\nA tense night followed. By morning, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears proved sadly accurate. Rose was entering the first stage of a severe, dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not sink into useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, pressing a finger to her lips while fixing her eyes on him. \u201cThis letter must reach Mr. Losberne as quickly as possible. It has to be taken to the market town, only four miles away by the footpath across the fields, and from there sent by express rider straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will send it, and I know I can rely on you to see it done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his face showed how eager he was to set out immediately.\"}"
        }
