          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"89b77a7be10fa8fcb0d0a602\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStop the starboard! Ting-a-ling-ling! Stop the port! Come ahead on the starboard! Stop her! Let your outside turn slow! Ting-a-ling-ling! Chow-ow-ow! Get out that head-line! lively now! Come\u2014out with your spring-line\u2014what\u2019re you doing there! Take a turn around that stump with the bight! Stand by that stage now\u2014let her go! Done with the engines, sir! Ting-a-ling-ling! SH\u2019T! S\u2019H\u2019T! SH\u2019T!\u201d (testing the gauge-cocks). Tom kept whitewashing and ignored the steamboat. Ben stared for a moment and then said, \u201cHi-yi! You\u2019re stuck, aren\u2019t you!\u201d Tom didn\u2019t reply. He studied his last stroke like an artist, gave his brush another light sweep, and inspected it again. Ben moved in beside him. Tom\u2019s mouth watered for the apple, but he kept working. Ben said, \u201cHello, old fellow, you\u2019ve got to work, huh?\u201d Tom spun around and said, \u201cOh, it\u2019s you, Ben! I didn\u2019t notice.\u201d \u201cSay\u2014I\u2019m going swimming. Don\u2019t you wish you could? But of course you\u2019d rather work\u2014wouldn\u2019t you? Sure you would!\u201d Tom looked at him for a moment and said, \u201cWhat do you call work?\u201d \u201cWhy, isn\u2019t that work?\u201d Tom went back to whitewashing and answered casually, \u201cWell, maybe it is and maybe it isn\u2019t. All I know is it suits Tom Sawyer.\u201d \u201cOh come on, you don\u2019t mean you like it?\u201d The brush kept moving. \u201cLike it? Well, I don\u2019t see why I shouldn\u2019t. Does a boy get a chance to whitewash a fence every day?\u201d That changed things. Ben stopped nibbling his apple. Tom swept his brush carefully back and forth, stepped back to judge the effect, added a touch here and there, checked it again\u2014Ben watching every move, more interested every minute. Soon he said, \u201cSay, Tom, let me whitewash a bit.\u201d Tom thought, almost agreed, then changed his mind. \u201cNo\u2014no\u2014I don\u2019t think that\u2019d do, Ben. You see, Aunt Polly\u2019s mighty picky about this fence\u2014right here on the street, you know\u2014but if it were the back fence I wouldn\u2019t mind and neither would she. Yes, she\u2019s awful particular about this fence; it has to be done real careful; I guess there isn\u2019t one boy in a thousand, maybe two thousand, who can do it the way it needs to be done.\u201d \u201cNo\u2014is that so? Oh come on\u2014let me just try. Only a little\u2014I\u2019d let you, if you were me, Tom.\u201d \u201cBen, I\u2019d like to, honest; but Aunt Polly\u2014well, Jim wanted to do it, but she wouldn\u2019t let him; Sid wanted to do it, and she wouldn\u2019t let Sid.\u201d\"}"
