{"request_id":"210f761abe74f932f7147892","rewritten_text":"Summer moved on, and crowned by the sun’s fierce rays, plague loosed its fatal arrows across the world. Nations under its sway bowed their heads and died. Corn that had sprung up plentifully lay rotting on the ground in autumn, while the despairing parent who had gone to gather food for his children lay rigid and plague-struck in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches in stately calm, while dying people lay beneath their shade, answering the solemn music with harsh cries. Painted birds darted through the leaves; carefree deer rested unharmed on the fern—oxen and horses wandered from their unattended stables and grazed among the wheat, for death fell on humans alone.

With summer’s advance and mortality grew our fears. My poor love and I looked at each other and at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we will tell them of our fears, which will have vanished along with their cause. Even if they alone remain on earth, they shall live; their cheeks shall not grow pale nor their sweet voices fade.” Our eldest understood a little of what was happening around us, and sometimes he asked me with solemn looks why such vast destruction had come. But he was only ten years old; the cheerfulness of youth soon chased that heavy care from his brow. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful infant without any notion of pain or sorrow, would toss back his fair curls from his eyes and fill the halls with his laughter, drawing our attention in a hundred artless ways to his games. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, our delight. She made it her task to tend the sick, soothe the grieving, help the aged, and share the play and spark the joy of the young. She glided through the rooms like a good spirit sent from the heavenly realm to cast a foreign radiance on our dark hour. Gratitude and praise marked every place her feet had touched. Yet when she stood before us in unassuming simplicity, playing with our children or performing small kind tasks for Idris with girlish dedication, we wondered in which fair feature of her pure loveliness, in which soft note of her thrilling voice, so much heroism, wisdom, and active goodness could reside.

The summer dragged on tediously because we hoped winter would at least slow the disease. To believe it might vanish altogether was too dear, too deeply felt, to be voiced. When such a wish escaped someone’s lips, those who heard it answered with a burst of tears and sobs, showing how deep their fears were and how slight their hopes."}

