          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a4e54946ee81328471745b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It now recorded an altitude for me of 132,000 feet, or five-and-twenty miles, so at that moment I surveyed no less than the three-hundred-and-twentieth part of the earth\u2019s total surface. By nine o\u2019clock land had again vanished to the east, though not before I noticed the balloon was being borne swiftly toward the N. N. W. The sea\u2019s curvature below was strikingly plain, even though drifting banks of cloud frequently broke my view. I observed, too, that not even the most delicate vapor rose higher than ten miles above the sea. \\n\\n\u201cAt half past nine I attempted an experiment by tossing a handful of feathers through the valve. Contrary to my expectations, they did not float; instead they plunged straight downward like a bullet, together, with extreme speed\u2014gone from sight in mere seconds. At first I was perplexed by this singular sight, unwilling to believe my ascent had suddenly received such tremendous acceleration. Soon, however, I realized the air was now too attenuated to buoy even feathers; they truly fell, as they seemed, with astonishing quickness, and the combined swiftness of their fall and my upward motion had startled me. \\n\\n\u201cBy ten o\u2019clock very little demanded my immediate attention. Everything proceeded admirably, and I felt sure the balloon climbed with a speed growing each moment, though I lacked any method to measure the rate of increase. I suffered no discomfort whatsoever and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam, sometimes inspecting my various devices, at other times refreshing the chamber\u2019s air. I decided to tend to this latter task every forty minutes, more to guard my health than because such frequent renewal was truly needed. Meanwhile, I could not refrain from indulging in visions. Fancy disported itself in the dreamy, untamed realms of the moon. Imagination, feeling for once unbound, wandered freely among the ever-shifting marvels of a dim and mutable world. Now I saw gray, ancient forests, jagged cliffs, and cataracts plunging with loud roar into bottomless chasms. Then I drifted all at once into motionless noonday solitudes, untouched by any breeze of heaven, where endless fields of poppies and delicate, lily-like blooms stretched out for weary distances, all soundless and still forever.\u201d\"}"
