          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7ac4f6e69670243a09b41a05\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We were united in our affection for Adrian, though she, still scarcely past childhood, lacked my capacity to gauge the width of his virtues or to echo his pursuits and judgments as I could. I was ever in his company. His nature held a tenderness and ethereal sweetness that lent our conversations a gentle, almost otherworldly tone. Yet he could also be as mirthful as a skylark singing aloft, his thoughts soaring like an eagle\u2019s, pure as the gentle dove. He could lighten Perdita\u2019s gravity and blunt the sharp spur of my own restless activity. I looked back on my impatient desires and vexed struggles with others as on a stormy dream, feeling as altered as if I had migrated into a new form whose fresh senses and nerve-work changed how the seeming universe was mirrored in my mind. Yet in truth I remained the same in strength, in that deep hunger for sympathy, and in my longing for vigorous action. My manly virtues did not abandon me, for the enchantress Urania spared Samson\u2019s locks even as he rested at her feet; but everything grew softened and humanized. Nor was Adrian limited to instructing me in the chilly certainties of history and philosophy. As he used these to teach me to curb my wild and unrefined spirit, he also revealed the living manuscript of his own heart, granting me insight into its marvelous nature.\\n\\nEven in his earliest years, the former queen of England had tried to plant bold and ambitious schemes in her son\u2019s mind. She discerned that he possessed genius and extraordinary gifts; she nurtured these with the aim of later turning them to the advantage of her own designs. She fostered his thirst for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even tolerated his untamed devotion to liberty, trusting that such a passion would, as so often happens, ripen into a craving for dominion. She sought to raise him to harbor resentment against those who had forced his father to abdicate, urging him to seek retribution. In this she failed. Reports\u2014however warped\u2014of a great and wise people asserting its right to self-rule stirred his admiration; from early youth he became a republican by conviction. Yet his mother did not relinquish hope. To her love of authority and proud birth she added determined ambition, patience, and restraint. She dedicated herself to studying her son\u2019s character. Through praise, rebuke, and exhortation she endeavored to discover and strike the responsive chords; and though the music that flowed at her touch sounded discordant to her ear, she grounded her expectations in his talents and felt assured that eventually she would prevail over him.\"}"
