          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3cd4215d1f309cc5688eb32a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His stubbornness was extreme, and \u201cdat deuced bug\u201d were the only words he let fall the whole way. I myself was entrusted with two dark lanterns, while Legrand contented himself with the scarab\u00e6us, dangling it from the end of a whip-cord; he swung it back and forth like some prestidigitator as he walked. Seeing this latest, undeniable sign of my friend\u2019s disturbed mind, I could hardly keep from weeping. Yet I judged it prudent to indulge his whim for the time being, or at least until some more forceful remedy might promise success. Meanwhile I tried, though fruitlessly, to draw him out concerning the object of the excursion. After securing my company, he showed no inclination to discuss any matter of lesser moment, and to all my inquiries he answered only, \u201cwe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the island\u2019s head in a skiff, then climbed the high ground on the mainland shore and moved northwestward through a tract desolately wild, where not the faintest trace of human passage appeared. Legrand marched ahead resolutely, stopping only for a moment now and again to consult certain landmarks, apparently of his own devising on some former visit.\\n\\nThus we went for nearly two hours, and the sun was just setting when we entered a region even more cheerless than any before. It was a kind of table-land near the crest of an almost inaccessible hill, thickly forested from base to summit, broken by huge crags that seemed to rest loosely on the earth and were, in many instances, kept from plunging into the valleys below only by the trees against which they leaned. Deep ravines running in various directions lent the scene a still more solemn austerity.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed was choked with brambles, through which we soon found passage would have been impossible without a scythe; and Jupiter, acting under his master\u2019s orders, set about cutting a path for us to the foot of an extraordinarily tall tulip-tree. This tree stood with eight or ten oaks on the level and towered above them all\u2014and above every other tree I had ever seen\u2014in the elegance of its leaves and shape, the spread of its branches, and its overall majestic presence.\"}"
