          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d0044591bffc09c7b5536752\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities we call analytical are themselves hard to analyze. We recognize them only through their effects. We do know that when someone possesses them in excess, they become a source of deep enjoyment. Just as a strong man delights in exercises that test his muscles, so the analyst rejoices in the moral effort of untangling. He takes pleasure even in small tasks that let him use his gift. He loves riddles, conundrums, and hieroglyphics, and his solutions show a sharpness that to ordinary minds seems almost supernatural. His results, driven by pure method, seem intuitive.\\n\\nStudying mathematics, especially its highest branch that ironically bears the name analysis because of its reverse steps, may greatly strengthen the faculty of resolving. Yet calculating is not the same as analyzing. For instance, a chess-player may calculate without truly analyzing. This means people misjudge the game\u2019s effect on the mind. I am not writing a treatise but merely introducing an unusual tale with random observations; I will therefore claim that the higher powers of reflection are more fully and usefully engaged by the modest game of checkers than by all the elaborate fuss of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different and odd ways and have different values, people often mistake mere complexity for depth. Here the attention is the quality most demanded. If it lapses for even a moment, a mistake happens that brings harm or defeat. Because the possible moves are numerous and tangled, the chances of such mistakes multiply, and nine times out of ten the more concentrated, rather than the more acute, player wins. In checkers, however, where moves are uniform and have little variation, the chance of errors drops, and since mere attention is barely employed, whatever advantage a player gains comes from superior sharpness. To make this less abstract, imagine a checkers match reduced to four kings, with no errors expected. There, if both players are equal, victory can come only from some refined move born of a powerful intellectual effort.\"}"
