          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0b72fda5a349e1a92549ac35\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall, Somersetshire, amused himself with no other book than the Baronetage; it filled any idle interval and comforted him in moments of anxiety. While he pored over the scant surviving earliest patents his thoughts were lifted to admiration and respect; whenever domestic vexations intruded they melted into disdain or pity as he perused the near endless creations of the last century; and when nothing else on the page served, he could always turn to his own history with unfailing interest. The favourite volume regularly opened to this entry:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq., of South Park, Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nExactly thus had the paragraph issued from the press; but Sir Walter had embellished it for the benefit of himself and his household by adding after Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq., of Uppercross, Somerset,\u201d and by noting with precision the day of his wife\u2019s death.\\n\\nNext came the chronicle and advancement of the ancient and reputable line, in customary language: its original settlement in Cheshire; its mention in Dugdale; the service as high sheriff; representation of a borough in three successive parliaments; displays of loyalty; and the creation of the baronetcy in the first year of Charles II, together with all the Marys and Elizabeths they wed; altogether composing two neat duodecimo pages, closing with the arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed again by Sir Walter\u2019s script\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity encompassed every facet of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s nature\u2014vanity of appearance and of station. He had been singularly handsome in early life, and at fifty-four retained the traits of a very fine man. Scarcely any woman could be more devoted to her looks; nor could the valet of a newly ennobled peer cherish more satisfaction in his master\u2019s rank than Sir Walter did in his own. He regarded beauty as second only to the privilege of a baronetcy, and the Sir Walter Elliot who combined these blessings commanded the deepest homage of his heart.\\n\\nHis good looks and title had at least this fair justification for his affection: through them he had secured a wife far superior in character to what he could ever have merited on his own account.\"}"
        }
