          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b0d54a36efa68ec76fa2f02a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Recent intelligence from Rotterdam indicates that the city is in a state of unusual philosophical agitation. Events have taken place there so wholly unlooked for, so strikingly new, so absolutely opposed to every former conception, that I have no hesitation in believing that by this time all Europe is in commotion, physics is boiling over, and reason and astronomy are locked in noisy debate.\\n\\nIt seems that upon the \u2014\u2014 day of \u2014\u2014 (the exact date escapes me), a vast concourse of people, for purposes not particularly specified, had gathered in the great square of the Exchange of that well-ordered city. The weather was hot\u2014exceptionally so for the season\u2014scarcely a stirring of wind; and the multitude felt no annoyance at being now and then gently refreshed by brief sprinklings which descended from large white cloud-masses that fitfully checkered the blue dome overhead. Yet, about midday, a slight yet singular movement ran through the assembly: after that came the clatter of ten thousand tongues, and instantly ten thousand faces turned skyward, ten thousand pipes simultaneously dropped from ten thousand mouths, and a shout, comparable only to Niagara\u2019s thunder, rang long and loud, raging through all the quarters of Rotterdam.\\n\\nThe cause of this commotion soon became plain enough. From beyond the huge bulk of one of those sharply defined cloud-masses already mentioned, there slowly slid into an open tract of blue a curious, jumbled, but seemingly solid object, so strangely formed, so fantastically assembled, that no amount of staring would suffice for the hardy burghers standing open-mouthed below. What might it be? In the name of all the vrouws and devils of Rotterdam, what omen was this? No one knew, no one could conjecture; not even the burgomaster, Mynheer Superbus Von Underduk, possessed the slightest clew to the enigma. Consequently\u2014as nothing more sensible suggested itself\u2014each man replaced his pipe with care in the accustomed corner of his mouth, and, cocking his right eye toward the prodigy, puffed, waited, waddled, and grunted meaningly\u2014then waddled away, grunted, paused, and once more\u2014puffed.\\n\\nMeanwhile the object of such wonder, and occasion of so much smoke, sank lower and lower toward the worthy city. Within a very few minutes it came near enough for precise observation. It seemed\u2014indeed, it undeniably was\u2014a kind of balloon; yet surely no balloon of that description had ever before been witnessed in Rotterdam.\"}"
