          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"115c3d5e63282178cf4e1f70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"All the young people were quite set on seeing Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going again himself, for it was merely seventeen miles from Uppercross. Though it was November, the weather was far from unpleasant; and, in short, Louisa, being the keenest of them all and having decided on it, now fortified by the notion that insisting on her will showed commendable firmness, overruled her parents\u2019 wish to defer the trip until summer. So to Lyme they would go\u2014Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth. The first heedless plan had been to depart in the morning and return at night; but Mr. Musgrove, for his horses\u2019 sake, objected. Once considered sensibly, a November day would hardly allow much time for exploring a new place, after taking away the necessary seven hours for travel. They were therefore to stay overnight and not be looked for until dinner the following day. This was deemed a considerable improvement; and though they all gathered at the Great House to breakfast rather early and started very punctually, it was well past noon before the two carriages\u2014Mr. Musgrove\u2019s coach with the four ladies, and Charles\u2019s curricle driven by him with Captain Wentworth\u2014were descending the long hill into Lyme, entering its even steeper main street, and making it quite certain that they would barely have time to look around before the light and warmth disappeared. After securing rooms and ordering a meal at an inn, their next obvious step was to walk straight down to the shore. They had arrived too late in the year for any diversion or variety Lyme might offer as a resort. The public rooms were closed, nearly all lodgers gone, scarcely any families but the residents remaining; and as the buildings themselves hold little to admire, it is the town\u2019s striking situation\u2014the main street seeming to rush into the sea, the walk to the Cobb tracing the cheerful little bay, busy in season with bathing machines and visitors\u2014the Cobb itself, with its ancient curiosities and recent improvements, and the beautiful ridge of cliffs extending eastward\u2014that attracts a stranger\u2019s eye. A person would have to be very singular indeed not to find charms in Lyme\u2019s immediate surroundings that make him wish to know the place better.\"}"
