          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5dbd0aefac9d62257a07368a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShe would make such a wonderful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed wholeheartedly, but added, \u201cOur dear little friend Harriet Smith is another lovely young woman. You\u2019ll like Harriet. Emma couldn\u2019t have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m delighted to hear it\u2014but Jane Fairfax, everyone knows, is utterly accomplished and exceptional! And she\u2019s exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThey continued happily on that topic, then moved on to others of similar importance, all discussed in the same harmony. But the evening did not end without a slight return of agitation. The gruel arrived and gave them plenty to say\u2014lavish praise, numerous comments, unquestioned declarations of its healthfulness for every constitution, and rather sharp criticism of all the households where it was never prepared properly. Unfortunately, among the failures the daughter mentioned, the most recent and therefore the most vivid was with her own cook at South End, a temporary hire who never could grasp what she meant by a properly smooth bowl of gruel, thin but not too thin. No matter how often she requested and ordered it, she never got anything acceptable. This was a dangerous topic.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. The exclamation in Emma\u2019s ear meant, \u201cAh! there\u2019s no end to the sad consequences of your going to South End. It ought not to be spoken of.\u201d For a short time she hoped he would hold his tongue, that silent reflection would be enough to restore his appreciation of his own smooth gruel. After a few minutes, however, he began, \u201cI\u2019ll always regret that you went to the sea this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you regret it, sir? I assure you, it did the children a world of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you had to go to the sea, it shouldn\u2019t have been South End. South End is unhealthy. Perry was surprised you chose South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think that, but it\u2019s quite a mistake, sir. We were all perfectly well there, never had the least discomfort from the mud, and Mr. Wingfield says it\u2019s entirely wrong to believe the place unhealthy. I\u2019m sure he can be trusted; he understands the air and his own brother\u2019s family has gone there several times.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry spent a week at Cromer once and says it\u2019s the best of all the seaside bathing places.\u201d\"}"
