          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d26b590a17b6aa7f359faeb1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In the morning she was found dead in her bed, apparently without pain. Sir Mathew Reid had been called at once, but there was nothing he could do, and she was to be buried on the 22nd at Beauchamp Chalcote. A few days before her death she made her will, leaving Lord Arthur her little house in Curzon Street and all her furniture, personal effects, and pictures, except for her miniature collection, which went to her sister Lady Margaret Rufford, and the amethyst necklace, which Sybil Merton was to have. The estate was not worth much, but Mr. Mansfield, the solicitor, was very eager for Lord Arthur to return immediately, if possible, because there were many bills to settle and Lady Clementina had never kept proper accounts.\\n\\nLord Arthur was deeply moved by Lady Clementina\u2019s thoughtful remembrance of him and felt that Mr. Podgers had much to answer for. Still, his love for Sybil outweighed every other feeling, and knowing he had done his duty gave him peace and comfort. When he arrived at Charing Cross, he felt completely happy.\\n\\nThe Mertons greeted him warmly. Sybil made him promise never again to let anything come between them, and the wedding was set for the 7th of June. Life seemed bright and beautiful once more, and his old joy returned.\\n\\nOne day, however, while he was going through the house in Curzon Street with Lady Clementina\u2019s solicitor and Sybil, burning bundles of faded letters and emptying drawers full of odds and ends, the young woman suddenly gave a little cry of delight.\\n\\n\u201cWhat have you found, Sybil?\u201d said Lord Arthur, looking up from his work with a smile.\\n\\n\u201cThis lovely little silver bonbonni\u00e8re, Arthur. Isn\u2019t it charming and Dutch? Do give it to me! I know amethysts won\u2019t suit me until I\u2019m over eighty.\u201d\\n\\nIt was the box that had held the aconitine.\\n\\nLord Arthur started, and a faint blush touched his cheek. He had almost forgotten what he had done, and it struck him as a strange coincidence that Sybil, for whom he had endured all that terrible tension, should be the first to remind him of it.\\n\\n\u201cOf course you can have it, Sybil. I gave it to poor Lady Clem myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, thank you, Arthur; and may I have the bonbon too? I never guessed Lady Clementina liked sweets. I thought she was far too intellectual.\u201d\\n\\nLord Arthur turned deathly pale, and a horrible idea crossed his mind.\"}"
