{"request_id":"cf53e1e10013c4bdcd05d6e1","rewritten_text":"As I reached through the valve with a cup of water for the old cat, my shirt sleeve snagged on the loop that held the basket, and in an instant it freed the basket from the base. It vanished so abruptly it might as well have dissolved into thin air. There was not even a tenth of a second between the basket breaking loose and it totally disappearing with everything inside. I wished it well on its way down, though I had no hope that either cat or kittens would survive to recount their bad luck.

“At six o’clock, I saw a vast stretch of the earth’s eastern horizon fall under a thick shadow, which swept forward rapidly until, by five minutes to seven, the entire surface I could see lay in night. The sun’s rays, however, still lit the balloon long afterward, and this detail, though expected, filled me with immense pleasure. It was clear that in the morning I would see the sunrise hours before the people of Rotterdam, despite their being so much farther east, and that every day, the higher I rose, the longer I would enjoy daylight. I resolved to keep a journal of my journey, counting the days straight through twenty-four hours without regard to periods of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling sleepy, I decided to lie down for the rest of the night; yet a problem arose, one that, obvious as it now seems, had never crossed my mind until that moment. If I slept as planned, how would the cabin’s air be refreshed meanwhile? Breathing it for more than an hour was impossible, or if it could be stretched to an hour and a quarter, the results might be disastrous. This dilemma caused me serious worry; and it will seem astonishing that after all the risks I had taken, I viewed it so gravely that I nearly abandoned any hope of reaching my final goal and almost resigned myself to making a descent. But that pause lasted only a moment."}

