          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ddf55c41daef2e0dbf1354da\",\"rewritten_text\":\"P.S.\u2014Please tell me about the bows. Jennings swears they\u2019re the latest style.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so grave and miserable over the letter that the Duchess burst into fits of laughter.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Arthur,\u201d she exclaimed, \u201cI\u2019ll never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what shall I say about the clock? I think it\u2019s a brilliant invention, and I should like one myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think much of them,\u201d Lord Arthur replied with a wistful smile, and after kissing his mother he left the room.\\n\\nOnce upstairs, he threw himself on a sofa, eyes brimming with tears. He had done everything possible to commit this murder, yet both attempts had failed through no fault of his own. He had tried to do his duty, but Destiny herself seemed to have betrayed him. He was weighed down by the emptiness of good intentions, the futility of trying to be noble. Perhaps it would be better to break off the engagement altogether. Sybil would suffer, of course, but suffering could never truly damage such a noble nature. As for himself, what did it matter? There is always some war in which a man can die, some cause to which he can give his life, and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny carry out her sentence. He would not lift a finger to help her.\\n\\nAt seven-thirty he got dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and he was obliged to dine with them. Their shallow talk and mindless jokes left him cold, and as soon as coffee arrived he made up an excuse and left. As he was walking out, the hall porter handed him a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, inviting him to come by the next evening to look at an explosive umbrella that detonated the moment it was opened. It was the newest invention and had just arrived from Geneva. Arthur tore the letter to pieces. He had resolved to attempt no more experiments. Then he wandered down to the Thames Embankment and sat for hours by the river. The moon glared through a mane of tawny clouds, like a lion\u2019s eye, and countless stars studded the hollow vault of sky, like gold dust scattered on a purple dome. Every so often a barge swung out into the muddy current and drifted away on the tide, while the railway signals flicked from green to red as trains screamed across the bridge.\"}"
