          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"019b508e5e49627b31ec7920\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The chain of doom that forever drags misery must have snapped, and the malignant destiny that rules it would never suffer such a rupture of her eternal decrees. Yet why should I grieve at this? Misery was my native air, and only what was wretched could draw near me; had Woodville been content, I never would have met him. And can I, who for years lived on tears and was reared beneath the showers of grief, hesitate to recount a story steeped in woe and death?\\n\\nWoodville was compelled to travel into the country and lingered there day after day in intolerable separation from his lovely bride. A letter reached him from her, saying she was somewhat ailing, yet urging him to hasten to her, declaring that health would flow from his eyes and that his presence would prove her surest medicine. Three more days held him away, and then he hurried toward her. His heart, he knew not why, foreshadowed disaster; no further letters had come; he dreaded that her illness had worsened, and this fear made him feverish and impatient for the instant when she would again stand before him, clothed in bloom and grace; for a sinister voice kept whispering, \u201cYou shall never behold her as she was again.\u201d\\n\\nWhen he reached her dwelling, it lay silent: he passed through several rooms; in one a servant sobbed violently. He grew faint with terror and could hardly ask, \u201cIs she dead?\u201d barely hearing the dreadful answer, \u201cNot yet.\u201d Those astounding words fell on him with less horror than what he had dreaded; to find she still lived, that hope remained, was a bittersweet relief. He recalled her letter, and indulged the wild belief that his kisses, warm with love and life, would breathe new energy into her, that she could not perish while he was near; that his presence was the talisman sustaining her existence.\\n\\nHe sped to her sick chamber; she lay with flaming cheeks and closed eyes, apparently insensible. He enfolded her in his arms; he pressed breathless kisses upon her burning lips; in a voice of hushed anguish he summoned her by the tenderest names: \u201cReturn, Elinor; I am here\u2014your life, your love. Return; dearest, you granted me this boon, that I should restore you. Let your gentle spirit rekindle; you cannot die beside me. What is death? To see you no more? To be sundered from what is part of myself; without whom I possess neither recollection nor future?\u201d\"}"
        }
